Fasih.add("reading",[{"id":"r-a2-01","l":"A2","title":"A Student's Day","topic":"daily","paras":["Sara is a student at a university in Oran. She studies computer science. Every morning she wakes up at six o'clock and has a quick shower.","She leaves home at seven. She takes the tram to the university because it is cheap and fast. On the tram she reads her notes and listens to a podcast.","Her classes start at eight. She has lectures until twelve, then she eats lunch in the cafeteria with her friends. The cafeteria is big and always full of students. She usually has rice with chicken and a glass of water.","In the afternoon she works in the library. She never studies at home because her little brother is very noisy. She goes home at five and helps her mother cook dinner.","In the evening Sara relaxes. She watches a film or calls her grandmother. She goes to bed at ten, because she must wake up early again tomorrow. On Fridays she sleeps late and visits her aunt."],"gloss":[{"w":"lecture","ar":"محاضرة"},{"w":"cafeteria","ar":"مقصف، كافيتيريا"},{"w":"tram","ar":"ترام، قطار المدينة"},{"w":"notes","ar":"ملاحظات، مذكرات"},{"w":"noisy","ar":"مزعج، كثير الضجيج"},{"w":"relax","ar":"يسترخي، يرتاح"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How does Sara go to the university?","o":["By bus","By tram","On foot"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: تركب الترام لأنه رخيص وسريع."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does Sara never study at home?","o":["She has no desk","Her home is far away","Her little brother is very noisy"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: أخوها الصغير مزعج جدا."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Sara usually eat for lunch?","o":["Rice with chicken","Fish and bread","Soup and salad"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: عادة تأكل الأرز مع الدجاج."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Sara studies medicine.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: تدرس علوم الحاسوب وليس الطب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Sara's classes start at eight o'clock.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: تبدأ حصصها في الثامنة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Sara has a sister.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكر أخا صغيرا فقط ولا يذكر أختا."},{"k":"gap","q":"Sara wakes up at ___ o'clock every morning.","a":["six"],"why":"الفقرة 1: تستيقظ في الساعة السادسة."},{"k":"gap","q":"In the evening she calls her ___.","a":["grandmother"],"why":"الفقرة 5: تتصل بجدتها مساء."}],"sumAr":"سارة طالبة حاسوب في وهران، تستيقظ مبكرا وتركب الترام إلى الجامعة. تدرس في المكتبة بعد الغداء وتساعد أمها مساء ثم تنام في العاشرة."},{"id":"r-a2-02","l":"A2","title":"The Saturday Market","topic":"food","paras":["Every Saturday morning, the main square in our small town becomes a busy market. The sellers arrive before sunrise and put up their tables and colourful umbrellas.","On the left you can buy fresh vegetables. Mr Karim sells tomatoes, onions and big green peppers. His oranges are sweet and cost only one hundred dinars a kilo.","In the middle there is a cheese stall and a bread stall. The baker starts work at three in the morning, so the bread is always warm. Children love the small sweet cakes next to it.","At the back of the square, people sell clothes, pots and old books. You must walk slowly, because it is very crowded. Many customers ask for a lower price, and the sellers usually smile and agree.","I go to the market with my grandfather every week. He always buys honey from the same old woman. Last Saturday we stayed for two hours and carried home five heavy bags."],"gloss":[{"w":"stall","ar":"كشك، طاولة بيع"},{"w":"sunrise","ar":"شروق الشمس"},{"w":"crowded","ar":"مزدحم"},{"w":"customer","ar":"زبون"},{"w":"baker","ar":"خباز"},{"w":"price","ar":"سعر"},{"w":"umbrella","ar":"مظلة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Who starts work at three in the morning?","o":["The vegetable seller","The cheese seller","The baker"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: الخباز يبدأ العمل في الثالثة صباحا."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Mr Karim sell?","o":["Clothes and books","Tomatoes, onions and peppers","Bread and cakes"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: يبيع الطماطم والبصل والفلفل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why must you walk slowly at the back of the square?","o":["It is very crowded","The ground is wet","It is very hot"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: المكان مزدحم جدا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The market is open every day.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: السوق يقام كل يوم سبت فقط."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Children love the small sweet cakes.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: الأطفال يحبون الكعك الحلو الصغير."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The old woman sells honey at a low price.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 5: تُذكر العجوز والعسل دون ذكر السعر."},{"k":"gap","q":"The sellers arrive before ___.","a":["sunrise"],"why":"الفقرة 1: يصل البائعون قبل شروق الشمس."},{"k":"gap","q":"Last Saturday they carried home five heavy ___.","a":["bags"],"why":"الفقرة 5: حملا إلى البيت خمس حقائب ثقيلة."}],"sumAr":"في كل يوم سبت تتحول ساحة البلدة الصغيرة إلى سوق مزدحم فيه خضار وخبز وجبن وملابس. يذهب الكاتب مع جده كل أسبوع ويشتريان العسل ويعودان بحقائب ثقيلة."},{"id":"r-a2-03","l":"A2","title":"An Email from Canada","topic":"communication","paras":["Hi Omar, how are you? I am writing from my new flat in Montreal, Canada. I moved here three weeks ago with my family, and I miss you all very much.","The city is beautiful, but it is very cold! Yesterday it was minus twelve degrees. I wear a big coat, a hat and gloves every day. I can't go out without them.","My new school is big and modern. The students are friendly, but they speak English and French very fast. I understand about half of the words. My teacher says I must practise every day, so I am going to watch films in French.","I have one problem: the food. I can't find our spices in the shops, and my mother is sad about that. Next month we are going to visit an Arabic shop on the other side of the city.","Please write soon and tell me about the football team. Are you still the best player? Say hello to your parents. Your friend, Hamza"],"gloss":[{"w":"flat","ar":"شقة"},{"w":"gloves","ar":"قفازات"},{"w":"spices","ar":"توابل، بهارات"},{"w":"practise","ar":"يتمرّن، يتدرّب"},{"w":"modern","ar":"عصري، حديث"},{"w":"friendly","ar":"ودود"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where does Hamza live now?","o":["In Algiers","In Paris","In Montreal"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 1: يكتب من شقته الجديدة في مونتريال."},{"k":"mc","q":"When did Hamza move?","o":["Three weeks ago","Three months ago","Yesterday"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 1: انتقل قبل ثلاثة أسابيع."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is Hamza's mother sad?","o":["She misses her friends","She can't find their spices","She has no job"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: لا تجد التوابل التي اعتادوها في المحلات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Hamza's new school is small.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: مدرسته كبيرة وحديثة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Hamza is going to watch films in French.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: ينوي مشاهدة أفلام بالفرنسية ليتمرن."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Hamza has a new best friend in Canada.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص صديقا مقربا جديدا."},{"k":"gap","q":"Yesterday it was minus ___ degrees.","a":["twelve"],"why":"الفقرة 2: كانت الحرارة تحت الصفر باثني عشر درجة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The students speak English and ___ very fast.","a":["French"],"why":"الفقرة 3: الطلاب يتكلمون الإنجليزية والفرنسية بسرعة."}],"sumAr":"يكتب حمزة إلى صديقه عمر من مونتريال بعد انتقاله إليها. يصف البرد الشديد ومدرسته الجديدة ومشكلة التوابل، ويسأل عن فريق كرة القدم."},{"id":"r-a2-04","l":"A2","title":"A Healthy Breakfast","topic":"food","paras":["Many people skip breakfast, but doctors say it is an important meal. After a night without food, your body needs energy to start the day.","A good breakfast has three parts. First, you need protein, for example eggs, yogurt or a little cheese. Protein keeps you full for a long time.","Second, eat some fibre. Whole bread, oats and fruit are good choices. A banana with a bowl of oats is cheap, quick and tasty.","Third, drink water, milk or fresh orange juice. Try not to drink too much sugary juice or fizzy drinks in the morning. They give you energy for a short time, and then you feel tired again.","Do you have no time in the morning? Don't worry. You can prepare breakfast the night before. Put oats and milk in a jar and leave it in the fridge. In the morning it is ready. Students who eat breakfast often concentrate better in class."],"gloss":[{"w":"skip","ar":"يتخطى، يتجاهل"},{"w":"protein","ar":"بروتين"},{"w":"fibre","ar":"ألياف"},{"w":"oats","ar":"شوفان"},{"w":"sugary","ar":"محلّى بالسكر"},{"w":"fizzy","ar":"غازي"},{"w":"concentrate","ar":"يركّز"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does protein do?","o":["It makes you tired","It keeps you full for a long time","It gives you fibre"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: البروتين يبقيك شبعانا مدة طويلة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which breakfast is cheap, quick and tasty?","o":["Eggs and cheese","Orange juice and cake","A banana with a bowl of oats"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: الموز مع الشوفان رخيص وسريع ولذيذ."},{"k":"mc","q":"What should you try not to drink too much?","o":["Sugary juice","Water","Milk"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: تجنب الإكثار من العصير المحلّى."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Doctors say breakfast is not important.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: يقول الأطباء إنها وجبة مهمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"You can prepare oats the night before.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: ضع الشوفان والحليب في وعاء في الثلاجة ليلا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Eggs are cheaper than yogurt.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص بين سعر البيض والزبادي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Whole bread, oats and ___ are good choices.","a":["fruit"],"why":"الفقرة 3: الخبز الكامل والشوفان والفاكهة خيارات جيدة."},{"k":"gap","q":"You can leave the jar in the ___ overnight.","a":["fridge"],"why":"الفقرة 5: اترك الوعاء في الثلاجة."}],"sumAr":"الفطور وجبة مهمة لأن الجسم يحتاج طاقة بعد الليل. الفطور الجيد يضم بروتينا وألياف وشرابا صحيا، ويمكن تحضيره في الليلة السابقة لمن لا وقت لديه."},{"id":"r-a2-05","l":"A2","title":"A Long Train Journey","topic":"travel","paras":["Last month I took a long train journey from the capital to a town in the south. It takes about twelve hours, so I bought my ticket two weeks before. I chose a seat next to the window.","The train left at seven in the evening. At first I looked at the lights of the city. Then it was dark, and I could only see my own face in the glass.","I had a small bag with sandwiches, water and a book, but I did not read much. A friendly old man sat opposite me. He told me stories about his work as a train driver for thirty years.","At midnight, the train stopped at a small station for twenty minutes. Everyone got off to stretch their legs and buy hot tea. The air was cool and full of stars.","I slept badly, but I woke up at dawn and saw the desert for the first time. The sand was orange and the sky was pink. I forgot that I was tired. Next time I am going to take a blanket."],"gloss":[{"w":"journey","ar":"رحلة، سفرة"},{"w":"ticket","ar":"تذكرة"},{"w":"opposite","ar":"مقابل، قبالة"},{"w":"station","ar":"محطة"},{"w":"stretch","ar":"يمدّ، يتمدد"},{"w":"dawn","ar":"فجر"},{"w":"blanket","ar":"بطانية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"When did the train leave?","o":["At seven in the evening","At midnight","At dawn"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: غادر القطار في السابعة مساء."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was the old man's job?","o":["Doctor","Teacher","Train driver"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: عمل سائق قطار ثلاثين سنة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did everyone get off the train at midnight?","o":["To buy tickets","To stretch their legs and buy tea","To change trains"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: نزلوا ليتمددوا ويشتروا الشاي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The journey takes about twelve hours.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: الرحلة تستغرق نحو اثنتي عشرة ساعة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer read his book for many hours.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: لم يقرأ كثيرا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer travelled with a friend.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر مرافقا له في السفر."},{"k":"gap","q":"He bought his ticket two ___ before.","a":["weeks"],"why":"الفقرة 1: اشترى التذكرة قبل أسبوعين."},{"k":"gap","q":"At dawn he saw the ___ for the first time.","a":["desert"],"why":"الفقرة 5: رأى الصحراء لأول مرة عند الفجر."}],"sumAr":"يروي الكاتب رحلة قطار طويلة من العاصمة إلى بلدة في الجنوب. التقى رجلا مسنا كان سائق قطار، وتوقف القطار منتصف الليل، ثم رأى الصحراء عند الفجر."},{"id":"r-a2-06","l":"A2","title":"The First Day at Work","topic":"work","paras":["Nadia started her first job on Monday. She is now an assistant in a small engineering office. She was nervous, so she woke up early and checked her bag three times.","At eight thirty she arrived at the office. The manager, Mr Hadi, met her at the door and shook her hand. He showed her the kitchen, the meeting room and her desk. It was next to a big window.","Then she met the team. There are six people, and they were all very kind. A colleague named Lina gave her a notebook, a pen and a password for the computer. Lina said that she could ask anything.","In the morning Nadia learned how to use the filing system. After lunch she wrote two emails and answered the phone twice. She made one small mistake, but nobody was angry.","At five o'clock she was tired but happy. On the bus home she made a list of things to learn. Tomorrow she must arrive on time, and she is going to bring a plant for her desk."],"gloss":[{"w":"nervous","ar":"متوتر، قلق"},{"w":"assistant","ar":"مساعد"},{"w":"manager","ar":"مدير"},{"w":"colleague","ar":"زميل عمل"},{"w":"password","ar":"كلمة مرور"},{"w":"filing system","ar":"نظام الأرشفة"},{"w":"mistake","ar":"خطأ"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is Nadia's job?","o":["Assistant in an engineering office","Manager of a shop","Teacher in a school"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 1: هي مساعدة في مكتب هندسي صغير."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who gave Nadia a notebook and a pen?","o":["Mr Hadi","Her mother","Lina"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: الزميلة لينا أعطتها دفترا وقلما."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did Nadia do after lunch?","o":["She learned the filing system","She wrote two emails and answered the phone","She went home"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: بعد الغداء كتبت رسالتين وردت على الهاتف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Nadia felt nervous before work.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: كانت متوترة فاستيقظت مبكرا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The office has twenty people.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: في الفريق ستة أشخاص."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Nadia has worked in an office before.","a":"NG","why":"النص يقول إنها وظيفتها الأولى فقط، ولا يذكر خبرة سابقة بالمكاتب."},{"k":"gap","q":"Her desk was next to a big ___.","a":["window"],"why":"الفقرة 2: مكتبها بجانب نافذة كبيرة."},{"k":"gap","q":"On the bus she made a list of things to ___.","a":["learn"],"why":"الفقرة 5: كتبت قائمة بأشياء تتعلمها."}],"sumAr":"بدأت نادية عملها الأول مساعدة في مكتب هندسي. استقبلها المدير وتعرفت إلى الفريق وتعلمت نظام الأرشفة، وعادت متعبة لكنها سعيدة."},{"id":"r-a2-07","l":"A2","title":"Four Seasons in the Mountains","topic":"nature","paras":["Aziz lives in a small village high in the mountains. There are only eighty houses, one school and one shop. The village is beautiful in every season.","In spring the snow melts and the rivers become loud. The farmers plant potatoes and beans, and wild flowers cover the hills. The children walk to school in their rain boots.","Summer is warm and dry. Tourists come to walk in the forest and sleep in tents. The villagers sell honey, cheese and wool to them. The evenings are cool, so everybody sits outside.","In autumn the leaves turn red and yellow. This is the time to pick apples and walnuts. People also cut wood and store it for winter, because the cold season is long.","Winter is hard. Snow can be one metre deep, and sometimes the road is closed for a week. Then the school closes too, and the children play in the snow. Aziz loves it, and he never wants to leave."],"gloss":[{"w":"melt","ar":"يذوب"},{"w":"wool","ar":"صوف"},{"w":"tent","ar":"خيمة"},{"w":"villager","ar":"قروي"},{"w":"store","ar":"يخزّن"},{"w":"deep","ar":"عميق"},{"w":"wild","ar":"بري"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How many houses are there in the village?","o":["Eighty","Eight hundred","Eighteen"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 1: في القرية ثمانون بيتا فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do the farmers plant in spring?","o":["Apples and walnuts","Wool and honey","Potatoes and beans"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 2: يزرع الفلاحون البطاطا والفول."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do people cut wood in autumn?","o":["To build tents","To store it for winter","To sell it to tourists"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: يخزنونه لفصل الشتاء الطويل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Tourists visit the village in summer.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: يأتي السياح صيفا للمشي والتخييم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The village has two shops.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: في القرية دكان واحد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"It snows every day in winter.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 5: يذكر عمق الثلج دون أن يقول إنه يسقط كل يوم."},{"k":"gap","q":"In autumn people pick apples and ___.","a":["walnuts"],"why":"الفقرة 4: يقطفون التفاح والجوز."},{"k":"gap","q":"In winter the road is sometimes closed for a ___.","a":["week"],"why":"الفقرة 5: قد يُغلق الطريق أسبوعا."}],"sumAr":"يعيش عزيز في قرية جبلية صغيرة جميلة في كل الفصول. تزرع القرية في الربيع وتستقبل السياح في الصيف وتقطف الثمار في الخريف، ويغطي الثلج طرقها في الشتاء."},{"id":"r-a2-08","l":"A2","title":"The Story of the Bicycle","topic":"history","paras":["The bicycle is a simple machine, but its story is long. The first bicycle was made in Germany in 1817. It had two wheels and a wooden frame, but it had no pedals.","The rider sat on it and pushed the ground with his feet. It was fun, but it was also slow and tiring. People called it a running machine.","In the 1860s, pedals were added to the front wheel. Later, makers built bicycles with a very big front wheel. They were fast, but they were also dangerous, because the rider sat very high.","The real change came in the 1880s. Makers put the pedals in the middle and used a chain to move the back wheel. Both wheels were now the same size, and the new bicycle was safe and easy to ride. Soon rubber tyres made it more comfortable.","Today millions of people use bicycles. They are cheap, they do not make pollution, and they keep us healthy. Many cities now have special roads for cyclists."],"gloss":[{"w":"wheel","ar":"عجلة"},{"w":"frame","ar":"هيكل"},{"w":"pedal","ar":"دوّاسة"},{"w":"chain","ar":"سلسلة"},{"w":"tyre","ar":"إطار العجلة"},{"w":"dangerous","ar":"خطير"},{"w":"pollution","ar":"تلوث"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"When was the first bicycle made?","o":["In 1860","In 1817","In 1880"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: صُنعت أول دراجة عام 1817."},{"k":"mc","q":"How did the rider move the first bicycle?","o":["With pedals","With a chain","By pushing the ground with his feet"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 2: كان الراكب يدفع الأرض بقدميه."},{"k":"mc","q":"What moved the back wheel on the new bicycle?","o":["A chain","A rubber tyre","A big front wheel"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: استُعملت سلسلة لتحريك العجلة الخلفية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The first bicycle was made in Germany.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: صُنعت في ألمانيا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Bicycles with a very big front wheel were safe.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: كانت سريعة لكنها خطيرة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The first bicycle was expensive.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص سعر الدراجة الأولى."},{"k":"gap","q":"The first bicycle had no ___.","a":["pedals"],"why":"الفقرة 1: لم تكن للدراجة الأولى دواسات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Rubber ___ made bicycles more comfortable.","a":["tyres"],"why":"الفقرة 4: الإطارات المطاطية جعلتها أكثر راحة."}],"sumAr":"بدأت الدراجة عام 1817 في ألمانيا بلا دواسات، ثم أضيفت الدواسات وصُنعت دراجات بعجلة أمامية كبيرة خطيرة. في ثمانينيات القرن التاسع عشر ظهرت دراجة آمنة بسلسلة وعجلتين متساويتين."},{"id":"r-a2-09","l":"A2","title":"A Visit to the Doctor","topic":"health","paras":["Last Tuesday I felt terrible. I had a high temperature, a sore throat and a bad cough. I called the clinic and the receptionist gave me an appointment for ten o'clock.","In the waiting room there were four other patients. A little girl was holding a toy rabbit, and an old man was reading a newspaper. After twenty minutes, the doctor called my name.","The doctor was a kind young woman. She asked me some questions. How long did the cough last? Did I have any allergies? Then she looked in my throat and listened to my chest with a stethoscope.","She said I had a bad cold, not a serious illness. She gave me a prescription for some tablets and told me to rest. She told me that I must drink a lot of water and stay at home for three days.","I took the prescription to the pharmacy and bought the medicine. Then I went home, made a hot lemon tea and went to bed. By Friday I felt much better."],"gloss":[{"w":"temperature","ar":"حمّى، ارتفاع الحرارة"},{"w":"sore throat","ar":"التهاب الحلق"},{"w":"appointment","ar":"موعد"},{"w":"patient","ar":"مريض"},{"w":"prescription","ar":"وصفة طبية"},{"w":"pharmacy","ar":"صيدلية"},{"w":"allergy","ar":"حساسية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What time was the appointment?","o":["Nine o'clock","Ten o'clock","Eleven o'clock"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: كان الموعد في العاشرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did the doctor use to listen to the writer's chest?","o":["A thermometer","A prescription","A stethoscope"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: استعملت السماعة الطبية."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long must the writer stay at home?","o":["Three days","One week","One day"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: عليه البقاء في البيت ثلاثة أيام."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The doctor was a young woman.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: الطبيبة امرأة شابة لطيفة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The doctor said the writer had a serious illness.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: قالت إنه نزلة برد قوية وليس مرضا خطيرا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer took the tablets for a week.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص مدة تناول الأقراص."},{"k":"gap","q":"The writer had a high temperature, a sore throat and a bad ___.","a":["cough"],"why":"الفقرة 1: كان يعاني من سعال شديد."},{"k":"gap","q":"The writer bought the medicine at the ___.","a":["pharmacy"],"why":"الفقرة 5: اشترى الدواء من الصيدلية."}],"sumAr":"مرض الكاتب بحمّى والتهاب في الحلق وسعال، فزار العيادة. فحصته طبيبة وأعطته وصفة وطلبت منه الراحة وشرب الماء، فتحسن بحلول يوم الجمعة."},{"id":"r-a2-10","l":"A2","title":"How to Save Money","topic":"money","paras":["Yassine is twenty-three years old. He works in a phone shop and earns about fifty thousand dinars a month. He wants to buy a small car, so he is saving money.","First, he writes down everything he spends. Every Sunday he checks his notebook and sees where the money goes. Last month he found that he spent too much on snacks and coffee.","Second, he makes a plan. On the first day of the month he puts ten thousand dinars into a savings account. After that he never touches it. He lives with his parents, so he does not pay rent. The rest is for food, transport and small things.","Third, he is careful with big purchases. Before he buys something expensive, he waits for one week. Often he decides that he does not really need it. He also looks for discounts and compares prices in three shops.","Yassine says saving is not easy, but it is possible. In about two years he is going to have enough money for his car. He believes that a small habit every day is better than a big dream."],"gloss":[{"w":"earn","ar":"يكسب"},{"w":"spend","ar":"ينفق"},{"w":"savings account","ar":"حساب توفير"},{"w":"purchase","ar":"عملية شراء"},{"w":"discount","ar":"تخفيض"},{"w":"habit","ar":"عادة"},{"w":"rent","ar":"إيجار"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why is Yassine saving money?","o":["To pay his rent","To buy a small car","To open a shop"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: يريد شراء سيارة صغيرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Yassine do every Sunday?","o":["He checks his notebook","He visits the bank","He goes shopping"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: يراجع دفتره كل أحد."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does he do before he buys something expensive?","o":["He asks his father","He borrows money","He waits for one week"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: ينتظر أسبوعا قبل الشراء."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Yassine works in a phone shop.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: يعمل في محل للهواتف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"He puts money into his savings account at the end of the month.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: يودع المبلغ في اليوم الأول من الشهر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Yassine's parents give him money every month.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكر أنه يسكن مع والديه فقط، ولا يذكر أنهما يعطيانه مالا."},{"k":"gap","q":"He compares prices in three ___.","a":["shops"],"why":"الفقرة 4: يقارن الأسعار في ثلاثة محلات."},{"k":"gap","q":"He is going to have enough money in about two ___.","a":["years"],"why":"الفقرة 5: سيجمع المال الكافي خلال نحو سنتين."}],"sumAr":"يدّخر ياسين لشراء سيارة صغيرة، فيسجل مصاريفه ويضع جزءا ثابتا في حساب توفير أول كل شهر. كما ينتظر أسبوعا قبل أي شراء كبير ويقارن الأسعار."},{"id":"r-a2-11","l":"A2","title":"The Match on Saturday","topic":"sport","paras":["On Saturday afternoon our town team, the Eagles, played against a team from the next village. The match was at the old stadium near the river. About five hundred people came to watch.","The weather was sunny and the pitch was green and dry. Children sat on the steps with flags, and a man sold hot nuts at the gate. When the players ran onto the pitch, everybody stood up and shouted.","For thirty minutes nobody scored. Then the captain, a tall defender, passed the ball to the striker. The striker ran past two players and kicked the ball into the net. It was one-nil!","Just before half time, the other team scored too. After the break the game was fast and exciting. The Eagles' goalkeeper made two great saves, and the referee gave a yellow card to one player for a hard tackle.","In the last minute, the Eagles got a corner. The ball flew into the box and a young midfielder headed it in. The final score was two-one. After the match the players shook hands and the fans sang in the streets."],"gloss":[{"w":"stadium","ar":"ملعب"},{"w":"pitch","ar":"أرضية الملعب"},{"w":"striker","ar":"مهاجم"},{"w":"goalkeeper","ar":"حارس مرمى"},{"w":"referee","ar":"حكم"},{"w":"captain","ar":"قائد الفريق"},{"w":"corner","ar":"ركنية"},{"w":"tackle","ar":"تدخّل، افتكاك الكرة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where was the match?","o":["In a school yard","At the old stadium near the river","In the main square"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: أقيمت المباراة في الملعب القديم قرب النهر."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who scored the first goal for the Eagles?","o":["The goalkeeper","The captain","The striker"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: المهاجم سجّل الهدف الأول."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who scored the winning goal?","o":["A young midfielder","The striker","A tall defender"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: لاعب وسط شاب سجّل برأسه."},{"k":"tfng","q":"About five hundred people watched the match.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: حضر نحو خمسمئة شخص."},{"k":"tfng","q":"It rained during the match.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: كان الجو مشمسا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The Eagles have won every match this year.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص نتائج الفريق في بقية الموسم."},{"k":"gap","q":"The final score was ___.","a":["two-one","2-1"],"why":"الفقرة 5: انتهت المباراة بنتيجة اثنين مقابل واحد."},{"k":"gap","q":"The goalkeeper made two great ___.","a":["saves"],"why":"الفقرة 4: تصدّى حارس المرمى لكرتين رائعتين."}],"sumAr":"لعب فريق البلدة مباراة أمام فريق القرية المجاورة أمام نحو خمسمئة متفرج. سجّل المهاجم أولا ثم تعادل الخصم، وفي الدقيقة الأخيرة فاز الفريق بهدف من ركنية."},{"id":"r-a2-12","l":"A2","title":"The Town Library","topic":"education","paras":["The town library is in a beautiful old building next to the post office. It opened in 1965, and today more than three thousand people are members. Membership is free for everyone.","On the ground floor there are novels, newspapers and a big section for children. Every Wednesday a librarian reads stories to the little ones. The children sit on soft cushions and listen carefully.","Upstairs there is a quiet room for students. It has twenty desks, free wifi and plugs for laptops. During exams the room is full from eight in the morning, so you must arrive early to find a seat.","You can borrow up to five books for three weeks. If you forget to return them, you must pay a small fine. You can also renew a book on the internet if nobody is waiting for it.","The library offers more than books. It has computer classes for older people and a small film club on Fridays. Many members say it is the most friendly place in town."],"gloss":[{"w":"librarian","ar":"أمين مكتبة"},{"w":"member","ar":"عضو، منتسب"},{"w":"borrow","ar":"يستعير"},{"w":"renew","ar":"يجدّد"},{"w":"fine","ar":"غرامة"},{"w":"section","ar":"قسم"},{"w":"cushion","ar":"وسادة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where is the library?","o":["Next to the post office","Next to the school","Near the river"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 1: بجانب مكتب البريد."},{"k":"mc","q":"How many books can you borrow?","o":["Three","Five","Ten"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: يمكنك استعارة حتى خمسة كتب."},{"k":"mc","q":"When does the librarian read stories to children?","o":["On Fridays","Every day","Every Wednesday"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 2: كل أربعاء تقرأ أمينة المكتبة القصص."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Membership costs a small fee.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: الاشتراك مجاني للجميع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The quiet room has twenty desks.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: في الغرفة الهادئة عشرون مكتبا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The library has a café.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص وجود مقهى في المكتبة."},{"k":"gap","q":"If you return a book late, you must pay a ___.","a":["small fine","fine"],"why":"الفقرة 4: عند التأخر تدفع غرامة صغيرة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The film club meets on ___.","a":["Fridays"],"why":"الفقرة 5: نادي الأفلام يجتمع أيام الجمعة."}],"sumAr":"مكتبة البلدة مبنى قديم جميل، اشتراكها مجاني وفيها قسم للأطفال وقاعة هادئة للطلبة. تعير الكتب لثلاثة أسابيع وتنظم دروس حاسوب ونادي أفلام."},{"id":"r-a2-13","l":"A2","title":"Life Before Mobile Phones","topic":"society","paras":["Thirty years ago, most people did not have a mobile phone. Life was different, and in some ways it was slower. If you wanted to talk to a friend, you had to call his house.","Families had one telephone, and it stood in the hall. Everybody could hear your conversation, so it was difficult to say private things. Many people also used public phones in the street, with coins or special cards.","There was no GPS either. Before a long trip, drivers bought a paper map and planned the road. If they were lost, they stopped and asked a stranger for help. Sometimes this was a good way to meet new people.","People took photographs with a camera and a roll of film. A roll had only twenty-four pictures, and you waited a week to see the results. So people thought carefully before they pressed the button.","Of course, today's phones are very useful. But some people say that in the past we listened more, waited more and looked at each other more. Perhaps we can learn something from those days."],"gloss":[{"w":"conversation","ar":"محادثة"},{"w":"private","ar":"خاص، سري"},{"w":"public phone","ar":"هاتف عمومي"},{"w":"stranger","ar":"غريب، شخص لا نعرفه"},{"w":"roll of film","ar":"شريط تصوير"},{"w":"result","ar":"نتيجة"},{"w":"useful","ar":"مفيد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where did families keep their telephone?","o":["In the kitchen","In the hall","In the garden"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: كان الهاتف في الردهة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did drivers buy before a long trip?","o":["A paper map","A GPS","A camera"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: كان السائقون يشترون خريطة ورقية."},{"k":"mc","q":"How many pictures did a roll of film have?","o":["Twelve","Thirty-six","Twenty-four"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: في الشريط أربع وعشرون صورة فقط."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Everybody in the house could hear a conversation on the family phone.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: الجميع يسمع المحادثة في الردهة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Public phones were free.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: كانت تعمل بالقطع النقدية أو بطاقات خاصة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most people had a computer at home.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتحدث عن الحواسيب المنزلية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Drivers bought a paper ___ before a long trip.","a":["map"],"why":"الفقرة 3: يشترون خريطة ورقية قبل السفر."},{"k":"gap","q":"Public phones used coins or special ___.","a":["cards"],"why":"الفقرة 2: بقطع نقدية أو بطاقات خاصة."}],"sumAr":"قبل ثلاثين سنة لم تكن الهواتف المحمولة موجودة، وكان للأسرة هاتف واحد في الردهة. كان الناس يستعملون الخرائط الورقية والكاميرات بالأفلام، ويرى بعضهم أن الحياة آنذاك كانت أبطأ وأكثر إصغاء."},{"id":"r-a2-14","l":"A2","title":"A Lawyer at Our School","topic":"law","paras":["Last week a lawyer called Mrs Amina visited our school. She was wearing a black robe and she carried a big folder. She talked to our class for forty minutes.","First she explained her job. A lawyer helps people with problems about the law. For example, she helps families who want to buy a house, workers who lose their jobs, and people who go to court.","Then she told us about a normal day. In the morning she reads documents and writes letters. Sometimes she meets clients in her office and listens to their stories. In the afternoon she often goes to court and speaks to the judge.","A student asked if the job was difficult. Mrs Amina smiled. She said that a good lawyer must read a lot, listen carefully and always tell the truth. She studied for five years at university before she started work.","At the end, she explained why laws are important. Laws protect everybody, and they are the same for rich and poor people. Now many of us want to be lawyers, and we are going to visit a real court next month."],"gloss":[{"w":"lawyer","ar":"محامية، محامٍ"},{"w":"robe","ar":"رداء، بذلة القضاة والمحامين"},{"w":"court","ar":"محكمة"},{"w":"judge","ar":"قاضٍ"},{"w":"client","ar":"موكّل، زبون"},{"w":"document","ar":"وثيقة، مستند"},{"w":"protect","ar":"يحمي"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What was Mrs Amina wearing?","o":["A white coat","A black robe","A blue suit"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: كانت ترتدي رداء أسود."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does she often do in the afternoon?","o":["She writes letters","She teaches children","She goes to court"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: بعد الظهر تذهب غالبا إلى المحكمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long did she study at university?","o":["Five years","Two years","Ten years"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: درست خمس سنوات في الجامعة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Mrs Amina talked to the class for forty minutes.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: تحدثت أربعين دقيقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Mrs Amina only helps rich people.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: القوانين واحدة للغني والفقير، وهي تساعد أنواعا كثيرة من الناس."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Mrs Amina works with her brother.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص أي شيء عن أخيها."},{"k":"gap","q":"She meets clients in her ___.","a":["office"],"why":"الفقرة 3: تقابل موكليها في مكتبها."},{"k":"gap","q":"The students are going to visit a real ___ next month.","a":["court"],"why":"الفقرة 5: سيزورون محكمة حقيقية الشهر القادم."}],"sumAr":"زارت محامية تدعى السيدة أمينة مدرستنا وشرحت عملها في مساعدة الناس في مشكلاتهم القانونية. قالت إن المحامي الجيد يقرأ كثيرا ويصغي ويصدق، وإن القوانين تحمي الجميع."},{"id":"r-b1-01","l":"B1","title":"Recycling in a Modern City","topic":"environment","paras":["In many modern cities, rubbish is no longer simply thrown away. Every week, thousands of tonnes of paper, glass, plastic and metal are collected, sorted and sent to factories to be used again. City councils say that recycling saves energy, protects the countryside and creates jobs, but it only works when ordinary residents take part.","Most households now have several bins in different colours. Blue is for paper, green is for glass, and yellow is for plastic bottles and cans. Food waste goes into a small brown bin, and everything else goes into a grey one. At first, many people found the system confusing, so the council sent leaflets to every home and put clear pictures on each lid.","After collection, the waste travels to a large sorting centre. There, magnets pull steel cans out of the stream, and machines blow light paper away from heavy bottles. Workers stand beside the moving belts and remove anything that should not be there, such as plastic bags or dirty food boxes. Even one wrong item can spoil a whole load of clean material.","Food waste is treated in a different way. It is taken to a special plant, where it is turned into compost and a gas that can heat buildings. Some schools now use this compost in their gardens, and pupils grow vegetables for the canteen. Teachers say that this helps children understand where waste ends up.","Recycling is not perfect, however. Some materials, such as greasy pizza boxes or broken mirrors, cannot be processed. Experts therefore advise people to reduce what they buy and to reuse things whenever possible. A glass jar, for example, can hold pasta for years. Recycling, they say, should be the last step, not the first."],"gloss":[{"w":"recycling","ar":"إعادة التدوير"},{"w":"sorted","ar":"مفروزة / مصنّفة"},{"w":"leaflet","ar":"منشور / كتيّب"},{"w":"compost","ar":"سماد عضوي"},{"w":"canteen","ar":"مقصف"},{"w":"processed","ar":"تتم معالجتها"},{"w":"reuse","ar":"إعادة الاستخدام"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why did the council send leaflets to every home?","o":["To ask residents for money","To help people understand the bin system","To announce a new factory"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: كان النظام محيّرًا فأُرسلت منشورات وصور للتوضيح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do magnets do at the sorting centre?","o":["They separate light paper from bottles","They carry the waste to the factory","They pull steel cans out of the stream"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: المغناطيس يسحب علب الصلب من التيار."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Yellow bins are used for glass.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: الأخضر للزجاج والأصفر للبلاستيك والعلب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Workers at the sorting centre remove wrong items from the belts.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: العمال يزيلون كل ما لا ينبغي وجوده."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The sorting centre employs more than five hundred people.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر عدد العاملين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Food waste is turned into compost and a ___ that can heat buildings.","a":["gas"],"why":"الفقرة 4: السماد وغاز يمكنه تدفئة المباني."},{"k":"gap","q":"Experts advise people to ___ what they buy.","a":["reduce"],"why":"الفقرة 5: ينصح الخبراء بتقليل ما نشتريه."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Greasy pizza boxes can be recycled easily.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: لا يمكن معالجة علب البيتزا الدهنية."}],"sumAr":"تعمل المدن الحديثة على جمع النفايات وفرزها وإعادة استخدامها بمساعدة السكان وصناديق ملوّنة ومراكز فرز. ومع ذلك لا تُعالَج كل المواد، لذا ينصح الخبراء بالتقليل وإعادة الاستخدام قبل التدوير."},{"id":"r-b1-02","l":"B1","title":"Learning a Language as an Adult","topic":"education","paras":["Many adults believe they are too old to learn a new language. They remember difficult school lessons and think that children have a magical advantage. Research, however, suggests a more balanced picture. Children often reach a natural accent more easily, but adults have strengths of their own that can make them faster learners in the first months.","One strength is experience. Adults already know how language works, so they understand ideas such as verbs, tenses and polite requests. They can also use reading skills and dictionaries, and they can explain to themselves why a sentence is wrong. A ten-year-old rarely does this. Adults also have clear reasons to learn, such as a better job, a university place or a move abroad.","Of course, adults face problems that children do not. They are usually tired after work, they have family duties, and they feel embarrassed when they make mistakes. Many stop after a few weeks because progress seems slow. Teachers often say that the biggest enemy is not age but irregular practice.","Successful learners usually follow a few simple habits. They study for twenty minutes every day instead of three hours once a week. They listen to podcasts on the bus, read short news stories, and write a few lines in a notebook before sleep. They also speak as early as possible, even if their sentences are simple. A language exchange partner, who speaks your target language and wants to learn yours, can be very helpful.","Finally, it helps to measure progress in a kind way. Instead of asking, \"Am I fluent yet?\", ask, \"What can I do now that I could not do last month?\" Ordering a meal, understanding a short film or writing a polite email are real achievements. Small wins keep motivation high, and motivation is what carries an adult learner to the end."],"gloss":[{"w":"advantage","ar":"ميزة"},{"w":"accent","ar":"لكنة"},{"w":"embarrassed","ar":"محرَج"},{"w":"irregular","ar":"غير منتظم"},{"w":"habit","ar":"عادة"},{"w":"fluent","ar":"طليق في الكلام"},{"w":"motivation","ar":"الدافعية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which strength do adults have, according to the text?","o":["They understand how language works","They have more free time","They hear sounds more clearly"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: يعرف الكبار كيف تعمل اللغة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do teachers say is the biggest enemy of adult learners?","o":["Old age","Expensive books","Irregular practice"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: العدو الأكبر هو الممارسة غير المنتظمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Children often find it easier to get a natural accent.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: الأطفال غالبًا يكتسبون اللكنة الطبيعية بسهولة أكبر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The text says that children are better than adults in every way.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: للكبار نقاط قوة خاصة بهم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most adult learners prefer online lessons to classes.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين الدروس الإلكترونية والصفوف."},{"k":"gap","q":"Successful learners study for ___ every day.","a":["twenty minutes","20 minutes"],"why":"الفقرة 4: يدرسون عشرين دقيقة يوميًا."},{"k":"gap","q":"A language ___ partner wants to learn your language.","a":["exchange"],"why":"الفقرة 4: شريك تبادل اللغة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer advises waiting until your sentences are perfect before you speak.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: ينصح بالكلام مبكرًا حتى لو كانت الجمل بسيطة."}],"sumAr":"للكبار نقاط قوة في تعلّم اللغات، مثل الخبرة وفهم القواعد ووضوح الهدف، لكن التعب وقلة الانتظام يعيقانهم. النجاح يأتي من عادات يومية بسيطة والكلام المبكر وقياس التقدم بلطف."},{"id":"r-b1-03","l":"B1","title":"A Day in a Courtroom","topic":"law","paras":["Courtroom 4 opens at half past eight, long before the public arrives. A clerk checks the microphones, places files on the judge's desk and makes sure that there is enough water for everyone. Today there are five cases on the list, from a minor traffic offence to a dispute between a tenant and a landlord.","At nine o'clock, everyone stands as the judge enters. The first case is a man accused of driving without insurance. His lawyer explains that the insurance ended only two days earlier and that he was already arranging a new policy. The prosecutor shows a document from the insurance company. The judge listens carefully, asks two short questions, and then announces a fine.","The second case takes longer. A tenant says that his landlord kept his deposit without a good reason. The landlord says that the flat was left damaged. Both sides bring photographs, and a witness, the tenant's neighbour, describes the state of the flat when he moved out. The landlord's lawyer questions the witness closely, but the witness stays calm and gives the same answers every time.","After lunch, the judge hears a request to delay a trial because an important witness is ill. The lawyers discuss dates, and a new hearing is set for next month. Meanwhile, in the public seats, a group of law students takes notes. One of them whispers that real courts are much quieter than those on television. There is no shouting, and decisions are made by reading rules and weighing evidence.","By half past four, the list is finished. The judge writes the final decisions, and the clerk files the documents so that every party can receive a copy. Behind the calm routine is a simple principle: everyone has the right to be heard, and the decision must be based on facts and law, not on opinion. The room is then cleaned for the next day."],"gloss":[{"w":"clerk","ar":"كاتب المحكمة"},{"w":"dispute","ar":"نزاع"},{"w":"tenant","ar":"مستأجر"},{"w":"deposit","ar":"وديعة / مبلغ تأمين"},{"w":"witness","ar":"شاهد"},{"w":"prosecutor","ar":"المدّعي العام"},{"w":"hearing","ar":"جلسة استماع"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does the clerk do first in the morning?","o":["Questions a witness","Prepares the room for the judge","Announces a fine"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: يفحص الميكروفونات ويجهز المكتب والماء."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the landlord keep the deposit?","o":["He says the flat was damaged","He says the rent was late","He says the tenant left early"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: يقول المالك إن الشقة تركت متضررة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The man accused of driving without insurance receives a fine.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: القاضي يعلن غرامة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The neighbour changes his answers during questioning.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: الشاهد يبقى هادئًا ويعطي الإجابات نفسها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The tenant wins his case against the landlord.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر نتيجة القضية الثانية."},{"k":"gap","q":"A new hearing is set for next ___.","a":["month"],"why":"الفقرة 4: الجلسة الجديدة الشهر القادم."},{"k":"gap","q":"In the public seats, law students take ___.","a":["notes"],"why":"الفقرة 4: الطلاب يدوّنون ملاحظات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What must a decision be based on?","o":["Opinion and feeling","Facts and law","Speed and cost"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: القرار يبنى على الوقائع والقانون."}],"sumAr":"يصف النص يومًا في قاعة محكمة: تحضير القاعة، قضية مخالفة مرورية، ونزاع بين مستأجر ومالك، وتأجيل محاكمة. ويؤكد أن لكل شخص حقّ الاستماع إليه وأن القرارات تُبنى على الوقائع والقانون."},{"id":"r-b1-04","l":"B1","title":"The History of Paper","topic":"history","paras":["Paper is so common today that we rarely think about it. Yet for most of history, people wrote on much more difficult materials. In Egypt, writers used papyrus, made from a tall water plant. In other regions, they used animal skins, wood or clay. These materials were heavy, expensive or hard to make, so writing stayed in the hands of a few people.","According to historical tradition, paper was developed in China about two thousand years ago. Workers mixed old cloth, plant fibres and water into a soft pulp. They spread the pulp on a fine screen, pressed out the water and left the thin sheet to dry. The result was light, cheap and smooth, and it took ink well.","For several centuries, the method stayed mostly inside China. Then it travelled along trade routes to Central Asia and the Middle East. Cities such as Samarkand and Baghdad became famous for their paper mills, and scholars wrote and copied thousands of books. Libraries grew, and knowledge of medicine, mathematics and geography spread more widely than before.","Paper reached Europe much later, in the Middle Ages. At first, each sheet was made by hand, so books remained costly. The great change came with the printing press in the fifteenth century. Printers needed large amounts of cheap paper, and mills multiplied. Later, in the nineteenth century, machines began to make paper from wood pulp, and prices fell again.","Today we use paper for books, packaging, money and tickets, even though screens are everywhere. Modern factories also recycle huge amounts of old paper, which saves trees and water. Many people predicted that computers would end paper, yet we still print and write on it every day. The story of paper shows how a simple, cheap invention can change the way humans share ideas."],"gloss":[{"w":"papyrus","ar":"البردي"},{"w":"fibre","ar":"ألياف"},{"w":"pulp","ar":"عجينة / لبّ"},{"w":"screen","ar":"شبكة / غربال"},{"w":"mill","ar":"مصنع / معمل"},{"w":"scholar","ar":"عالِم / باحث"},{"w":"costly","ar":"باهظ الثمن"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why did writing stay in the hands of a few people for a long time?","o":["Few people wanted to learn","Early materials were hard to get or make","Ink was not invented"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: المواد ثقيلة أو غالية أو صعبة الصنع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was early Chinese paper made from?","o":["Wood and clay","Animal skins","Old cloth and plant fibres"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 2: خُلطت الأقمشة القديمة وألياف النبات والماء."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Papyrus was made from a tall water plant.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: البردي يُصنع من نبات مائي طويل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Paper was first made in Europe.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: ظهر الورق في الصين أولًا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The Chinese kept the method secret by law.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر أي قانون للسرية."},{"k":"gap","q":"In the nineteenth century, machines began to make paper from wood ___.","a":["pulp"],"why":"الفقرة 4: من لب الخشب."},{"k":"gap","q":"Samarkand and Baghdad became famous for paper ___.","a":["mills"],"why":"الفقرة 3: اشتهرتا بمصانع الورق."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The printing press increased the demand for paper.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: احتاج الطباعون كميات كبيرة من الورق."}],"sumAr":"نشأ الورق في الصين من الأقمشة والألياف ثم انتقل عبر طرق التجارة إلى آسيا الوسطى والشرق الأوسط ثم أوروبا. ساعدت المطبعة والآلات على خفض سعره، وما زلنا نستعمله رغم الشاشات."},{"id":"r-b1-05","l":"B1","title":"Sleep and Memory","topic":"science","paras":["Most people know that a poor night makes them feel tired, but fewer realise that sleep also protects memory. While the body rests, the brain is busy. Scientists who study sleep say that it works like a night shift in a library: the day's new information is sorted, stored and connected to older knowledge.","A night has several stages, which repeat about every ninety minutes. In deep sleep, the brain seems to move facts and events from a short-term store to a long-term one. In the lighter stage, when we dream most, it appears to link ideas and practise skills. Both stages matter, and cutting sleep short removes part of them.","Experiments support this idea. In a typical study, two groups of students learn a list of words. One group sleeps for eight hours, and the other stays awake all night. Two days later, both groups sleep normally and are tested. The students who slept after learning usually remember more. Lost sleep, it seems, cannot be fully repaid later.","This has practical lessons for students. Studying until dawn before an exam may feel serious, but it often backfires. A tired mind finds it harder to focus, so less information goes in, and less of it stays. It is wiser to review the material in the evening, go to bed on time, and look at the key points again in the morning.","Experts also suggest some simple habits. Go to bed and wake up at similar times every day. Keep the bedroom dark, cool and quiet. Avoid heavy meals and strong coffee late in the day. Switch off phones about an hour before sleep, because bright screens can make it harder to fall asleep. A short nap of twenty minutes after lunch can help, but long naps may disturb the night."],"gloss":[{"w":"memory","ar":"الذاكرة"},{"w":"stage","ar":"مرحلة"},{"w":"store","ar":"مخزن / تخزين"},{"w":"backfire","ar":"يأتي بنتيجة عكسية"},{"w":"focus","ar":"يركّز"},{"w":"disturb","ar":"يُزعج / يُخلّ بـ"},{"w":"nap","ar":"قيلولة قصيرة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How do scientists compare sleep to a library?","o":["A quiet reading room","A night shift that sorts and stores information","A place that closes early"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: النوم كنوبة عمل ليلية تفرز المعلومات وتخزنها."},{"k":"mc","q":"What seems to happen in deep sleep?","o":["The brain stops working","The brain practises dreams","The brain moves facts to long-term storage"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 2: تنتقل المعلومات من الذاكرة القصيرة إلى الطويلة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The stages of a night repeat about every ninety minutes.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: تتكرر المراحل كل تسعين دقيقة تقريبًا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Students who stay awake all night remember more than those who sleep.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: الذين ناموا يتذكرون أكثر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The study mentioned involved more than one hundred students.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر عدد الطلاب."},{"k":"gap","q":"Review the material in the evening and look at key points again in the ___.","a":["morning"],"why":"الفقرة 4: المراجعة مرة أخرى في الصباح."},{"k":"gap","q":"Keep the bedroom dark, cool and ___.","a":["quiet"],"why":"الفقرة 5: غرفة النوم مظلمة وباردة وهادئة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A twenty-minute nap after lunch can help.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: قيلولة قصيرة بعد الغداء قد تفيد."}],"sumAr":"النوم يحمي الذاكرة لأن الدماغ يرتب المعلومات ويخزنها ليلًا. لذلك لا يفيد السهر قبل الامتحان، وتساعد عادات بسيطة مثل النوم في وقت منتظم وغرفة هادئة وإبعاد الهاتف."},{"id":"r-b1-06","l":"B1","title":"Working from Home","topic":"work","paras":["Since the arrival of fast internet and video calls, millions of employees have started to work from home, at least for part of the week. For some, it is a dream: no crowded train, no early alarm, and a quiet space to think. For others, it brings surprising difficulties. As with most changes, the truth lies somewhere in the middle.","The advantages are easy to see. Workers save time and money on travel, and many say that they can concentrate better without noise from colleagues. Parents can be present when children return from school. Companies also benefit, because they can hire people who live far away and sometimes need smaller offices.","However, there are real disadvantages. Some people feel lonely because they miss casual conversations at the coffee machine. Others find it hard to stop working, since the laptop is always on the kitchen table. Without a clear end to the day, they answer emails late at night and feel tired in the morning. Managers, meanwhile, sometimes worry about how to check that work is done.","Good habits can solve many of these problems. It helps to have a fixed place for work, even a small desk in the corner of a room. A regular timetable, with breaks and a clear finishing time, protects free time. Short walks outside improve mood, and a quick video call with a colleague can replace the office chat. Employers can help by agreeing on clear goals instead of counting hours.","Many companies now prefer a hybrid model, in which staff work at home on some days and in the office on others. This allows teams to meet face to face for planning and creative work, while keeping quiet days for tasks that need focus. Whether it is the future for everyone is still debated, but it has clearly changed how we think about work."],"gloss":[{"w":"employee","ar":"موظف"},{"w":"concentrate","ar":"يركّز"},{"w":"colleague","ar":"زميل عمل"},{"w":"casual","ar":"عفوي / غير رسمي"},{"w":"timetable","ar":"جدول زمني"},{"w":"hybrid","ar":"هجين"},{"w":"debated","ar":"موضع نقاش"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why do many workers concentrate better at home?","o":["They have no tasks","There is less noise from colleagues","They work fewer hours"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: بلا ضجيج الزملاء."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do some people find it hard to stop working?","o":["Their manager calls them","They have no holidays","The laptop is always on the kitchen table"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: الحاسوب دائمًا على طاولة المطبخ."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Companies can hire people who live far away.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: يمكن توظيف من يسكنون بعيدًا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Working at home is better for all employees.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1 و3: له مزايا وعيوب ولا يناسب الجميع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most employees would like to work at home permanently.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر رأي أغلبية الموظفين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Employers can agree on clear ___ instead of counting hours.","a":["goals"],"why":"الفقرة 4: أهداف واضحة بدل عدّ الساعات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Many companies prefer a ___ model.","a":["hybrid"],"why":"الفقرة 5: النموذج الهجين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that a fixed place for work can help.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: من المفيد تخصيص مكان ثابت للعمل."}],"sumAr":"للعمل من المنزل مزايا مثل توفير الوقت والمال وهدوء التركيز، وله عيوب مثل الشعور بالوحدة وصعوبة إنهاء اليوم. تساعد عادات واضحة، وكثير من الشركات تفضل النموذج الهجين."},{"id":"r-b1-07","l":"B1","title":"A Young Entrepreneur's First Shop","topic":"business","paras":["Salma was twenty-three when she opened her first shop, a small bakery near a university. She had worked in a café for three years and saved money every month. Her family thought the idea was risky, but she had a clear plan, and she was determined to try. \"If it fails,\" she told them, \"at least I will have learned something.\"","The first problem was money. Her savings covered only half of the costs, so she asked a small-business fund for a loan. She prepared a simple plan showing the cost of ovens, rent and flour, and how many loaves she needed to sell each day. The fund approved a smaller amount than she hoped, so she bought one good oven instead of two and found a second-hand display counter.","Choosing the location was the next challenge. Rents in the city centre were too high, but a street near the university had thousands of students walking past every day. She spent a week counting people outside the empty shop and noting the busiest hours. This simple research convinced her that the place was right.","The opening week was difficult. On the second day, the oven broke, and she had to throw away a whole batch. A friend lent her a smaller one, and she worked until midnight. Customers were patient, and some even offered advice. By the end of the month, she realised that students loved her cheap breakfast offer, while office workers wanted bread after five o'clock, so she changed her opening hours.","A year later, Salma has two part-time employees and a loyal group of regular customers. She says that she learned three lessons: keep costs low at the start, listen to customers, and never be afraid to change plans. She is now thinking about selling her bread online, but she wants to be sure that the quality stays high."],"gloss":[{"w":"risky","ar":"محفوف بالمخاطر"},{"w":"determined","ar":"عازمة / مصمّمة"},{"w":"loan","ar":"قرض"},{"w":"loaf","ar":"رغيف"},{"w":"batch","ar":"دفعة (من الخبز)"},{"w":"location","ar":"موقع"},{"w":"loyal","ar":"وفيّ"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How did Salma choose the location of her shop?","o":["She asked her family","She counted people passing the empty shop","She copied another bakery"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: قضت أسبوعًا تعدّ المارّة وتسجل ساعات الذروة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did she buy only one oven?","o":["The fund approved less money than she hoped","Ovens were not available","She did not need two"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: وافق الصندوق على مبلغ أقل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Salma's family was sure that her idea was safe.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: رأت عائلتها أن الفكرة محفوفة بالمخاطر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The oven broke on the second day.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: تعطل الفرن في اليوم الثاني."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Her bakery makes cakes for weddings.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر كعك الأعراس."},{"k":"gap","q":"Rents in the city centre were too ___.","a":["high"],"why":"الفقرة 3: الإيجارات مرتفعة جدًا."},{"k":"gap","q":"Students loved her cheap ___ offer.","a":["breakfast"],"why":"الفقرة 4: عرض الفطور الرخيص."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Salma now has two full-time employees.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: لديها موظفان بدوام جزئي."}],"sumAr":"افتتحت سلمى مخبزًا صغيرًا قرب جامعة بعد خطة واضحة وقرض محدود. واجهت أعطالًا وتحديات، لكنها تعلمت أن تُبقي التكاليف منخفضة وتصغي إلى الزبائن وتغيّر خطتها عند الحاجة."},{"id":"r-b1-08","l":"B1","title":"Life at the Edge of a Great Desert","topic":"nature","paras":["The town of Tamar, an imaginary place, lies where green farmland ends and the great sand desert begins. From the roof of the old school, you can see both worlds: palm trees and small gardens on one side, and endless red dunes on the other. About twenty thousand people live here, and their lives are shaped by a simple fact: water is precious.","Summers are extremely hot, with daytime temperatures above forty-five degrees. People wake before dawn to do heavy work, rest indoors during the afternoon, and come out again in the evening, when the air cools. Houses are built with thick walls and narrow streets, which give shade. Many families also sleep on the roof on the hottest nights.","Water comes from deep wells and from an old system of underground channels that carries water from the hills to the gardens. Farmers share it by a strict timetable, and each family knows the hours when it can open its channel. They grow dates, wheat and vegetables under the tall palms, which protect the lower plants from sun and wind.","The desert is also a danger. Strong winds can lift sand into the air, and sometimes a storm covers roads and fields in a few hours. After a storm, neighbours work together to clear the paths. Young people learn early how to protect their eyes and faces with a long scarf, and how to read the sky for signs of coming wind.","In recent years, tourism has brought new work. Visitors come to ride camels, sleep in tents and watch the stars, which are extremely bright because there are no city lights. Local guides explain the history of the desert routes and the plants that survive there. Residents welcome the income, but they also worry that too many visitors could use too much water and leave rubbish. They hope to find a balance between old traditions and new opportunities."],"gloss":[{"w":"dune","ar":"كثيب رملي"},{"w":"precious","ar":"ثمين"},{"w":"shade","ar":"ظل"},{"w":"channel","ar":"قناة"},{"w":"strict","ar":"صارم"},{"w":"scarf","ar":"وشاح"},{"w":"income","ar":"دخل"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What shapes the lives of people in Tamar?","o":["The fact that water is precious","The number of tourists","The cold winters"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 1: حياتهم تتشكل بندرة الماء."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are the houses built with thick walls and narrow streets?","o":["To keep animals out","To stop the wind","To give shade"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 2: لتوفير الظل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"About twenty thousand people live in Tamar.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: نحو عشرين ألف نسمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Farmers can open their water channel whenever they like.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: الري وفق جدول صارم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"There is a hospital in the town.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر مستشفى."},{"k":"gap","q":"Farmers grow dates, wheat and vegetables under tall ___.","a":["palms"],"why":"الفقرة 3: تحت النخيل العالي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Young people protect their faces with a long ___.","a":["scarf"],"why":"الفقرة 4: وشاح طويل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Stars look very bright because there are no city lights.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: لا توجد أضواء مدينة."}],"sumAr":"تعيش بلدة خيالية على حافة صحراء كبرى حيث الماء ثمين والحر شديد. يتكيف السكان بالبيوت الظليلة وتنظيم الري ومواجهة العواصف، وتجلب السياحة دخلًا جديدًا مع قلق على الماء والنظافة."},{"id":"r-b1-09","l":"B1","title":"Why Cities Invest in Public Transport","topic":"city","paras":["Every morning, thousands of cars enter big cities, and many of them carry only one person. The result is traffic jams, noise and polluted air. In response, more and more city governments are spending large sums of money on buses, trams and metro lines. At first sight, this may seem expensive, but the reasons behind the investment are strong.","The first reason is space. A single bus can carry as many passengers as forty cars, yet it takes up far less road. A metro line under the ground uses no street space at all. Since land in city centres is very costly, moving more people in less space is a clear gain.","The second reason is health and the environment. Fewer cars mean cleaner air and fewer accidents. Passengers who use public transport also walk to and from stations, so they get a little exercise every day. Electric trams and buses reduce greenhouse gases even further, which helps cities meet their climate targets.","The third reason is fairness. Not everybody can afford a car or is able to drive. Students, older people and people with low incomes depend on buses and trains to reach schools, hospitals and jobs. When the service is reliable, they can take part fully in city life. Businesses also benefit, because workers arrive on time and shoppers can reach the high street easily.","Of course, a good system must be well designed. Passengers need frequent services, clear maps, reasonable prices and safe stations. If buses are late or dirty, people will return to their cars. Many cities therefore use one ticket for all services and show live arrival times on screens and phones. Experts agree that investing in public transport is not only about moving people; it is about making the whole city work better."],"gloss":[{"w":"traffic jam","ar":"ازدحام مروري"},{"w":"invest","ar":"يستثمر"},{"w":"passenger","ar":"راكب"},{"w":"greenhouse gases","ar":"غازات الدفيئة"},{"w":"income","ar":"دخل"},{"w":"reliable","ar":"موثوق"},{"w":"frequent","ar":"متكرّر"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why is a bus good for saving space?","o":["It is smaller than a car","It carries as many people as forty cars on less road","It uses the metro tunnel"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: حافلة واحدة تنقل ركاب أربعين سيارة بمساحة أقل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which group depends on public transport, according to the text?","o":["Students and people with low incomes","Only tourists","Only office managers"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: الطلاب وكبار السن وذوو الدخل المحدود."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A metro line under the ground uses no street space.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: المترو تحت الأرض لا يشغل مساحة شارع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Electric buses increase greenhouse gases.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: الحافلات الكهربائية تقلل الغازات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Public transport is free in most large cities.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر مجانية النقل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Fewer cars mean cleaner ___ and fewer accidents.","a":["air"],"why":"الفقرة 3: هواء أنظف وحوادث أقل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Many cities use one ___ for all services.","a":["ticket"],"why":"الفقرة 5: تذكرة واحدة لكل الخدمات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that bad service may send people back to their cars.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: إذا تأخرت الحافلات يعود الناس لسياراتهم."}],"sumAr":"تستثمر المدن في النقل العام لأنه يوفر المساحة ويحسّن الصحة والبيئة ويحقق العدالة لمن لا يملكون سيارة. وينجح ذلك فقط إذا كانت الخدمة منتظمة ونظيفة وسهلة الاستعمال."},{"id":"r-b1-10","l":"B1","title":"Volunteering","topic":"society","paras":["Every year, millions of people give their time to help others without being paid. They clean beaches, teach children to read, deliver food to elderly neighbours or help at sports events. This is called volunteering, and it is one of the most valuable forms of public service. Communities depend on it, but volunteers often say that they gain at least as much as they give.","Volunteering helps others in obvious ways. A local library can stay open longer if volunteers help to organise books. A shelter can feed more people if students prepare meals on Saturdays. In emergencies, such as floods, volunteers often arrive before official help and carry out simple but vital tasks, such as filling sandbags or giving information.","The benefits for volunteers are also real. Many learn new skills, such as teamwork, communication and planning, which employers value highly. Young people can add this experience to their CVs and discover which kind of job they enjoy. Retired people meet new friends and feel useful. Several studies suggest that volunteers feel happier and less lonely, although the exact reasons are still being discussed.","Starting is easier than many people think. First, choose a cause that you really care about, such as animals, education or the environment. Then contact a local organisation and ask what help is needed. Be honest about how many hours you can offer. Two hours every week is better than a big promise that you cannot keep. Many groups provide short training before you begin.","Volunteering can also have difficulties. Some tasks are tiring or emotional, and new volunteers may feel unsure. Good organisations therefore give support and clear instructions, and they thank people regularly. If you do not have time for regular work, you can still help by joining a one-day event, sharing skills online, or giving things you no longer need. Small actions, repeated by many people, can change a neighbourhood."],"gloss":[{"w":"volunteer","ar":"متطوّع"},{"w":"valuable","ar":"قيّم"},{"w":"shelter","ar":"مأوى"},{"w":"emergency","ar":"حالة طارئة"},{"w":"vital","ar":"حيوي"},{"w":"cause","ar":"قضية / غاية"},{"w":"organisation","ar":"منظمة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What do volunteers often say about their work?","o":["It is too tiring","They gain at least as much as they give","It should be paid"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: يقولون إنهم يكسبون بقدر ما يعطون."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is two hours every week better than a big promise?","o":["It is easier to learn","It costs less","It is a promise you can keep"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: وعد صغير تستطيع الوفاء به أفضل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In emergencies, volunteers sometimes arrive before official help.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: يصلون غالبًا قبل المساعدة الرسمية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"All volunteers receive a small payment.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: يتطوعون دون أجر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most volunteers in the world are under thirty years old.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر أعمار المتطوعين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Volunteers learn teamwork, communication and ___.","a":["planning"],"why":"الفقرة 3: العمل الجماعي والتواصل والتخطيط."},{"k":"gap","q":"First, choose a ___ that you really care about.","a":["cause"],"why":"الفقرة 4: اختر قضية تهمّك."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Scientists have fully proved why volunteers feel happier.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: الأسباب الدقيقة ما زالت قيد النقاش."}],"sumAr":"التطوع عمل مجاني يفيد المجتمع والمتطوع معًا، إذ يمنحه مهارات وأصدقاء وشعورًا بالفائدة. يبدأ المرء بقضية يحبها ووقت واقعي، وحتى المساهمات الصغيرة تغيّر الحي."},{"id":"r-b1-11","l":"B1","title":"How Vaccines Train the Body","topic":"health","paras":["Every day, the human body meets germs such as bacteria and viruses. Most of the time, the immune system, the body's defence team, deals with them quickly. However, some germs are so dangerous that the body needs help. This is the job of a vaccine: it teaches the immune system to recognise a germ before the real infection arrives.","A vaccine contains a harmless piece or a weak form of a germ. It cannot cause the full disease, but the body does not know that. White blood cells treat it as an enemy and make special proteins called antibodies, which attach themselves to the invader. Think of it as a training exercise for a football team: the match is not real, but the players learn the other side's tactics.","The most important part is memory. After the training, some immune cells stay in the body for years. If the real germ ever appears, these memory cells recognise it at once and react quickly. The infection is then stopped early, or it becomes much milder. Some vaccines need extra doses, called boosters, to remind the body.","Vaccines protect more than the person who receives them. When most people in a community are protected, the germ finds it hard to move from one person to another. This is called community protection. It is especially important for babies, elderly people and those who cannot be vaccinated for medical reasons, because they depend on the people around them.","Before a vaccine is approved, it is tested in several stages, first on small groups and then on thousands of volunteers. Doctors check that it works and that side effects are small and short, such as a sore arm or a mild fever. After approval, experts continue to watch for rare problems. Overall, vaccines are one of the most effective tools of modern medicine, and they have helped to save millions of lives."],"gloss":[{"w":"germ","ar":"جرثومة"},{"w":"immune system","ar":"جهاز المناعة"},{"w":"antibody","ar":"جسم مضاد"},{"w":"invader","ar":"غازٍ / دخيل"},{"w":"booster","ar":"جرعة تنشيطية"},{"w":"side effect","ar":"أثر جانبي"},{"w":"approved","ar":"مُعتمَد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does a vaccine contain?","o":["A strong form of the germ","A harmless piece or weak form of a germ","Only water and sugar"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: جزء غير ضار أو شكل ضعيف من الجرثومة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What are antibodies?","o":["Special proteins that attach to the invader","Tiny machines in the vaccine","Medicines for fever"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: بروتينات خاصة تلتصق بالغازي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A vaccine can cause the full disease.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: لا يسبب المرض الكامل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Some immune cells stay in the body for years after the training.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: خلايا الذاكرة تبقى سنوات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Boosters are cheaper than the first dose.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر أسعار الجرعات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Vaccines are tested on thousands of ___.","a":["volunteers"],"why":"الفقرة 5: على آلاف المتطوعين."},{"k":"gap","q":"This is called ___ protection.","a":["community"],"why":"الفقرة 4: الحماية المجتمعية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Side effects are usually small and short.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: آثار صغيرة وقصيرة كألم الذراع."}],"sumAr":"يدرّب اللقاح جهاز المناعة بجزء غير ضار من الجرثومة فتتكوّن أجسام مضادة وخلايا ذاكرة. يحمي اللقاح الفرد والمجتمع، ويُختبر على مراحل قبل اعتماده."},{"id":"r-b1-12","l":"B1","title":"The First Computers","topic":"technology","paras":["Today a phone in your pocket is more powerful than the huge machines that filled whole rooms seventy years ago. The word \"computer\" originally described a person, someone whose job was to do long calculations by hand. Only later did it come to mean a machine.","Long before electricity, inventors dreamed of calculating machines. In the nineteenth century, a British mathematician designed an \"engine\" with gears that could, in theory, follow a set of instructions. It was never fully built in his lifetime, but his idea of a programmable machine was ahead of its time. A friend of his wrote notes on how such a machine might be used, and many people regard her as an early programmer.","The first electronic computers appeared in the 1940s. They used thousands of glass tubes to switch signals on and off, and they were enormous. Some machines weighed more than twenty tonnes and needed a large hall, a team of operators and a powerful cooling system. They could do in seconds what took people days. Governments and universities used these machines for science, weather forecasts and military calculations.","The glass tubes were soon replaced by transistors, which were smaller, cheaper and more reliable. Later, engineers placed thousands, then millions, of transistors on a single small chip. Computers shrank from room size to desk size. In the 1970s and 1980s, the first personal computers appeared, and ordinary families, schools and small offices could buy one.","Early programmers used punched cards, and they often waited hours to see whether their program worked. A single mistake could ruin everything. Today we expect instant answers, but the basic idea has not changed: a computer follows instructions very quickly and exactly. Understanding its history reminds us that progress usually comes in small steps, from many people over many years."],"gloss":[{"w":"calculation","ar":"عملية حسابية"},{"w":"gear","ar":"ترس"},{"w":"programmable","ar":"قابل للبرمجة"},{"w":"enormous","ar":"ضخم"},{"w":"transistor","ar":"ترانزستور"},{"w":"reliable","ar":"موثوق"},{"w":"punched card","ar":"بطاقة مثقّبة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What did the word computer originally mean?","o":["A type of calculator","A person who did calculations","A room full of machines"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: كان يصف شخصًا يقوم بحسابات طويلة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What replaced the glass tubes?","o":["Gears","Punched cards","Transistors"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: حلت الترانزستورات محلها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The nineteenth-century engine was completed in its designer's lifetime.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: لم تُبنَ بالكامل في حياته."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The first electronic computers appeared in the 1940s.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: ظهرت في الأربعينيات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Early computers were cheaper than doing calculations by hand.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن التكلفة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Early electronic computers used thousands of glass ___.","a":["tubes"],"why":"الفقرة 3: آلاف الأنابيب الزجاجية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Early programmers used punched ___.","a":["cards"],"why":"الفقرة 5: البطاقات المثقبة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Personal computers appeared in the 1970s and 1980s.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: ظهرت الحواسيب الشخصية حينها."}],"sumAr":"كانت الحواسيب الأولى آلات ضخمة تعمل بالأنابيب الزجاجية، ثم صغّرتها الترانزستورات والرقائق حتى وصلت إلى البيوت. وبقيت الفكرة الأساسية واحدة: تنفيذ التعليمات بسرعة ودقة."},{"id":"r-b1-13","l":"B1","title":"Why Honesty Matters in Public Service","topic":"government","paras":["Public servants work for everyone. They collect taxes, run hospitals, issue licences and build roads. Because they look after public money and make decisions that change people's lives, honesty is not an extra quality in their job. It is the foundation of it. When a public servant is honest, citizens can trust that the rules are the same for every person, rich or poor.","Imagine a clerk who asks for a small gift before he signs a building permit. One person pays, and his project moves forward. Another person refuses, and his papers stay on the desk for months. This is unfair, and it also makes the whole system slow and expensive. Over time, people stop believing that the office exists to help them, and they start to look for shortcuts instead.","Dishonesty also wastes money. If a manager chooses a company because it is owned by a friend, and not because it offers the best price, the government pays more for a worse road or a weaker bridge. The extra cost comes from the pockets of ordinary taxpayers. In the end, schools, clinics and public transport receive less money than they need.","Fortunately, there are ways to protect honesty. Clear rules make it easier to see what is allowed. Public contracts can be published online, so that journalists and citizens can check them. Workers who report wrongdoing should be protected from punishment, and officials should explain their decisions in simple language. Training also helps, but the most important factor is the example set by leaders. When a director follows the rules every day, the staff understand that honesty is expected, not optional. Trust takes years to build, but it can be lost in a single scandal."],"gloss":[{"w":"licence","ar":"رخصة"},{"w":"permit","ar":"تصريح، ترخيص"},{"w":"shortcut","ar":"طريق مختصر"},{"w":"taxpayer","ar":"دافع الضرائب"},{"w":"wrongdoing","ar":"مخالفة، سلوك خاطئ"},{"w":"scandal","ar":"فضيحة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"According to the text, why is honesty so important for public servants?","o":["They earn more money than other workers.","They look after public money and affect people's lives.","They work only with foreign companies."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: يديرون المال العام وقراراتهم تؤثر في حياة الناس."},{"k":"mc","q":"What happens when one person pays for a permit and another refuses?","o":["Both projects move forward.","Both people get a refund.","One project moves and the other is delayed."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: من يدفع تتقدم معاملته ومن يرفض تتأخر أوراقه."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which of these helps to protect honesty?","o":["Keeping contracts secret","Protecting workers who report wrongdoing","Giving officials larger gifts"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: يجب حماية من يبلّغ عن المخالفات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Dishonest choices can make the government pay more for public works.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: اختيار شركة صديق قد يرفع التكلفة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Honest rules are the same in every country in the world.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتحدث عن القواعد في كل دول العالم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that training is more important than the example of leaders.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الرابعة: أهم عامل هو قدوة القادة وليس التدريب."},{"k":"gap","q":"Public contracts can be published ___ so that citizens can check them.","a":["online"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: تُنشر العقود على الإنترنت."},{"k":"gap","q":"Trust takes years to build, but it can be lost in a single ___.","a":["scandal"],"why":"الجملة الأخيرة من الفقرة الرابعة."}],"sumAr":"النزاهة أساس العمل في الخدمة العامة لأن الموظفين يديرون المال العام ويؤثرون في حياة الناس. الفساد يسبب الظلم وهدر المال، ويمكن حمايتها بقواعد واضحة وشفافية وحماية المبلّغين وقدوة القادة."},{"id":"r-b1-14","l":"B1","title":"What Does a Defence Lawyer Do?","topic":"law","paras":["When a person is accused of a crime, he or she does not have to face the court alone. A defence lawyer is a professional who protects the rights of the accused. Many people think that a defence lawyer simply tries to free guilty people, but this is a wrong idea. The real aim is to make sure that the trial is fair and that the law is followed at every step.","The work begins long before the hearing. First, the lawyer meets the client and listens carefully to his or her version of events. Then the lawyer reads the police reports, studies the evidence and talks to witnesses. Sometimes he finds mistakes, for example a document that is missing or a statement that does not match other facts. These details can change the result of a case.","In court, the lawyer speaks for the client. She asks questions to the witnesses of the other side and tests whether their stories are reliable. She also explains the law to the judge and argues that the evidence is not strong enough. If the client is found guilty, the lawyer can ask for a lighter punishment or help to prepare an appeal to a higher court.","A good defence lawyer needs several skills. She must be calm under pressure, write clearly and speak with confidence. She must also keep everything the client says secret, because trust is essential in this job. Society needs defence lawyers because an innocent person can be accused by mistake. Without proper defence, courts could make serious errors, and nobody would feel safe. For this reason, a fair legal system gives every accused person the right to a lawyer, even if he cannot pay for one."],"gloss":[{"w":"accused","ar":"المتَّهم"},{"w":"trial","ar":"محاكمة"},{"w":"witness","ar":"شاهد"},{"w":"reliable","ar":"موثوق"},{"w":"appeal","ar":"استئناف"},{"w":"innocent","ar":"بريء"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the real aim of a defence lawyer, according to the text?","o":["To free every client","To make sure the trial is fair","To punish the witnesses"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: الهدف ضمان محاكمة عادلة واتباع القانون."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the lawyer do first?","o":["Meets the client and listens to his or her story","Asks the judge for a lighter punishment","Prepares an appeal"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: يبدأ العمل بلقاء الموكّل والاستماع إليه."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why must a defence lawyer keep the client's words secret?","o":["Because the law is difficult","Because the police ask for it","Because trust is essential"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: الثقة أساسية في هذه المهنة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A lawyer may find a mistake in the police documents.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثانية: قد يجد المحامي أخطاء في المستندات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Defence lawyers earn more money than other lawyers.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر دخل المحامين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"If the client is found guilty, the lawyer can do nothing more.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: يمكنه طلب عقوبة أخف أو تحضير استئناف."},{"k":"gap","q":"The lawyer reads the police reports and talks to ___.","a":["witnesses"],"why":"الفقرة الثانية: يقرأ التقارير ويتحدث إلى الشهود."},{"k":"gap","q":"A lawyer can prepare an appeal to a ___ court.","a":["higher"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: استئناف أمام محكمة أعلى."}],"sumAr":"محامي الدفاع يحمي حقوق المتهم ويضمن عدالة المحاكمة، فيدرس الأدلة ويستجوب الشهود ويشرح القانون للقاضي. يحتاج إلى الهدوء والسرية، ووجوده يحمي الأبرياء من الأخطاء القضائية."},{"id":"r-b1-15","l":"B1","title":"Staying Safe Online","topic":"technology","paras":["We use the internet to study, work, shop and talk to our families. It is a wonderful tool, but it also brings risks. Criminals know that many people are careless online, and they use simple tricks to steal money and personal information. The good news is that a few easy habits can protect you from most of these problems.","The first habit concerns passwords. A short password like a birthday or a name is easy to guess. A strong password is long and mixes letters, numbers and symbols. You should also use a different password for each account. If one website is attacked, the thieves cannot open your other accounts. A password manager can remember all of them for you, so you only need to remember one.","The second habit is to be careful with messages. A common trick is called phishing. You receive an email that looks like it comes from your bank. It says that your account has a problem and asks you to click a link. The page you see looks real, but it is false, and anything you type goes straight to the criminal. Before you click, check the address of the sender and look for spelling mistakes. If you are not sure, go to the official website yourself.","Finally, keep your devices up to date. Updates often repair weaknesses that hackers use. Do not connect to public Wi-Fi when you send private information, because other users of the network may be able to see it. It is also wise to turn on two-step login, which sends a code to your phone. With these simple steps, you can enjoy the internet with much less worry. Remember that no company will ever ask you for your password by email."],"gloss":[{"w":"careless","ar":"مهمِل، غير حذر"},{"w":"password","ar":"كلمة مرور"},{"w":"phishing","ar":"تصيّد احتيالي"},{"w":"link","ar":"رابط"},{"w":"weakness","ar":"ثغرة، نقطة ضعف"},{"w":"device","ar":"جهاز"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What makes a password strong?","o":["It is long and mixes letters, numbers and symbols.","It is your birthday.","It is the same for all accounts."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: كلمة المرور القوية طويلة ومتنوعة الرموز."},{"k":"mc","q":"In a phishing message, the page you see is usually","o":["sent by your bank","false","very slow"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: الصفحة تبدو حقيقية لكنها مزيفة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why should you avoid public Wi-Fi for private information?","o":["It is always expensive.","It makes your phone slower.","Other users may see what you send."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: قد يرى مستخدمون آخرون بياناتك."},{"k":"tfng","q":"You should use a different password for each account.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثانية: كلمة مرور مختلفة لكل حساب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Phishing emails never contain spelling mistakes.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: ابحث عن الأخطاء الإملائية، فهي قد تظهر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most people in the world use a password manager.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر نسبة المستخدمين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Updates often repair ___ that hackers use.","a":["weaknesses"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: التحديثات تصلح الثغرات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Two-step login sends a ___ to your phone.","a":["code"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: يرسل رمزاً إلى هاتفك."}],"sumAr":"للإنترنت مخاطر، لكن عادات بسيطة تحمي المستخدم: كلمات مرور قوية ومختلفة، والحذر من رسائل التصيّد، وتحديث الأجهزة، وتجنب الشبكات العامة، وتفعيل الدخول بخطوتين."},{"id":"r-b1-16","l":"B1","title":"AI Assistants in Daily Life","topic":"ai","paras":["A few years ago, talking to a computer sounded like science fiction. Today, millions of people do it every morning. AI assistants on phones and in homes can answer questions, set alarms, translate sentences and write short messages. They learn from huge amounts of text, so they can understand ordinary language and reply in a natural way.","Students are among the most active users. An assistant can explain a difficult idea in simpler words, suggest a plan for revision or check the grammar of an essay. Workers use it, too. A manager can ask for a summary of a long report, and a shop owner can prepare a friendly answer for a customer in a few seconds. In this way, the assistant saves time on boring tasks.","However, these tools are not perfect. Sometimes an assistant gives an answer that sounds confident but is wrong. For this reason, important facts, such as dates, laws or medical advice, should always be checked in a reliable source. There is also the question of privacy. Anything you type may be stored, so it is better not to share passwords or private documents.","The best way to use an assistant is to treat it as a helper, not as a replacement for your own thinking. If a student asks it to write a whole essay, he learns very little. If he asks it to give feedback on his own draft, he learns a lot. People who understand both the strengths and the limits of AI will gain the most from it. In the future, these assistants will probably become even more common, so it is wise to learn how to use them carefully today."],"gloss":[{"w":"assistant","ar":"مساعد"},{"w":"alarm","ar":"منبّه"},{"w":"revision","ar":"مراجعة (للدراسة)"},{"w":"confident","ar":"واثق"},{"w":"privacy","ar":"الخصوصية"},{"w":"feedback","ar":"ملاحظات تقييمية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How do AI assistants understand ordinary language?","o":["They learn from huge amounts of text.","They copy the user's voice.","They ask a teacher."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: يتعلمون من كميات ضخمة من النصوص."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is one use of an assistant for a manager?","o":["Driving to work","Summarising a long report","Repairing a computer"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: يمكن للمدير طلب ملخص لتقرير طويل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why should important facts be checked?","o":["Assistants never answer questions.","Assistants are always slow.","An answer can sound confident but be wrong."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: قد تبدو الإجابة واثقة لكنها خاطئة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"AI assistants can translate sentences.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الأولى: من وظائفهم ترجمة الجمل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"It is safe to share passwords with an assistant.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: يُفضَّل ألا تشارك كلمات المرور."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most students in the world use AI assistants every day.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يعطي نسبة للطلاب."},{"k":"gap","q":"A student can ask an assistant for ___ on his own draft.","a":["feedback"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: يطلب ملاحظات على مسودته."},{"k":"gap","q":"Anything you type may be ___, so privacy is important.","a":["stored"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: قد يُخزَّن ما تكتبه."}],"sumAr":"تُستعمل مساعدات الذكاء الاصطناعي يومياً للإجابة والترجمة والتلخيص والدراسة، لكنها قد تخطئ وتثير مسألة الخصوصية. الأفضل اعتبارها مساعداً لا بديلاً عن تفكيرنا."},{"id":"r-b1-17","l":"B1","title":"Why Bees Matter","topic":"nature","paras":["Bees are small, but their work is enormous. When a bee visits a flower to drink nectar, grains of pollen stick to its body. At the next flower, some of this pollen falls off. This process is called pollination, and many plants need it to produce fruit and seeds. Without it, a flower can finish its life without making anything new.","Much of the food on our tables depends on this simple service. Apples, almonds, tomatoes, pumpkins and many other crops grow better when bees visit them. Farmers know this very well. Some of them even rent boxes of bees and place them in their fields during the flowering season. Scientists say that a large part of the world's food crops benefit from animal pollinators, and bees are the most important group.","Unfortunately, bees are in danger in many places. Farmers sometimes spray strong chemicals to kill insects that damage crops, and these chemicals can also harm bees. Cities and large farms have replaced meadows full of wild flowers with concrete and a single kind of plant, so bees find less food. Changes in the climate can also confuse them, because flowers may open earlier or later than usual.","The good news is that ordinary people can help. You can plant flowers on a balcony or in a garden, choosing different kinds that bloom in different months. You can leave a small corner of grass uncut and avoid chemical sprays. Another idea is to buy honey from local beekeepers, who look after their bees with care. If a bee lands on you, stay calm, because it only stings when it feels threatened. Protecting bees means protecting our own food. Even a small garden can become a safe home for many bees."],"gloss":[{"w":"nectar","ar":"رحيق"},{"w":"pollen","ar":"حبوب اللقاح"},{"w":"pollination","ar":"التلقيح"},{"w":"crop","ar":"محصول"},{"w":"meadow","ar":"مرج"},{"w":"beekeeper","ar":"مربّي نحل"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is pollination?","o":["Pollen moving from one flower to another","Bees making honey","Flowers closing at night"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: انتقال حبوب اللقاح من زهرة إلى أخرى."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do some farmers rent boxes of bees?","o":["To sell honey in the market","To help crops during the flowering season","To keep insects away"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: يضعونها في الحقول وقت الإزهار."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which of these is a danger to bees?","o":["Too many beekeepers","Cold water","Strong chemical sprays"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: المواد الكيميائية تضر بالنحل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Bees are the most important group of animal pollinators.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثانية: النحل هو المجموعة الأهم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A bee always stings when a person comes near.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الرابعة: تلدغ فقط عندما تشعر بالتهديد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Bees live longer in cities than in the countryside.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن أعمار النحل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Wild flower meadows have been replaced by ___ and a single kind of plant.","a":["concrete"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: حلّ الإسمنت محل المروج."},{"k":"gap","q":"You can buy honey from local ___ who look after their bees.","a":["beekeepers"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: اشترِ العسل من مربّي النحل المحليين."}],"sumAr":"يلقّح النحل الأزهار فيساعد على إنتاج كثير من الغذاء، لكنه مهدد بالمبيدات وفقدان المروج وتغيّر المناخ. يمكننا حمايته بزراعة الأزهار وتجنب الرش وشراء العسل المحلي."},{"id":"r-b1-18","l":"B1","title":"The Deep Ocean","topic":"nature","paras":["The ocean covers more than two thirds of our planet, yet we know less about its deepest parts than about the surface of the Moon. The deep ocean begins about two hundred metres under the water, where sunlight slowly disappears. Below one thousand metres, there is no light at all. It is a world of darkness, great pressure and very cold water.","The pressure in the deep sea is so strong that it would crush a human body. To visit these places, scientists use special submarines with thick walls, or robots controlled from a ship. These machines carry lights and cameras, and they often bring back surprising pictures. Each journey shows new animals that nobody has seen before.","Animals in the deep have found clever ways to live without sunlight. Some fish make their own light, which is called bioluminescence. They use it to attract food, to find a partner or to scare enemies. Others have huge mouths and sharp teeth, because food is rare and they must catch whatever passes by. Many creatures are soft, slow and pale, and they save energy by moving as little as possible.","The deep ocean is also important for people. It stores a large amount of carbon and helps to control the climate. Unfortunately, it is under threat. Rubbish sinks to the bottom, and some companies want to dig the seabed for metals. Because deep animals grow slowly, damaged areas may need hundreds of years to recover. Many scientists therefore ask governments to protect the deep sea before it is too late. Every new discovery reminds us how much there still is to learn. Although the journey is difficult, it is one of the most exciting areas of science today."],"gloss":[{"w":"pressure","ar":"ضغط"},{"w":"submarine","ar":"غوّاصة"},{"w":"bioluminescence","ar":"التوهج الحيوي"},{"w":"creature","ar":"كائن حي"},{"w":"seabed","ar":"قاع البحر"},{"w":"recover","ar":"يتعافى"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where does the deep ocean begin?","o":["About two hundred metres under the water","At the beach","One thousand metres under the water"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: يبدأ المحيط العميق نحو مئتي متر."},{"k":"mc","q":"How do scientists visit the deep sea?","o":["They swim with simple masks.","They use submarines or robots.","They use aeroplanes."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: غواصات خاصة أو روبوتات."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do many deep sea animals move very little?","o":["They like the cold.","They are afraid of light.","They need to save energy."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: يوفّرون الطاقة بقلة الحركة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"There is no light below one thousand metres.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الأولى: لا ضوء تحت ألف متر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"All deep sea fish have huge mouths.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: بعضها فقط له أفواه ضخمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The deepest fish in the ocean lives near the North Pole.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر مكان أعمق سمكة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Some fish make their own light, which is called ___.","a":["bioluminescence"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: التوهج الحيوي."},{"k":"gap","q":"The deep ocean stores a large amount of ___ and helps to control the climate.","a":["carbon"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: يخزّن كمية كبيرة من الكربون."}],"sumAr":"المحيط العميق عالم مظلم شديد الضغط والبرودة تعيش فيه كائنات غريبة تنتج ضوءها. يزور العلماء أعماقه بغواصات وروبوتات، وهو مهم للمناخ لكنه مهدد بالنفايات والتعدين."},{"id":"r-b1-19","l":"B1","title":"A Night at the Museum","topic":"arts","paras":["When the visitors go home and the lights go down, a museum does not go to sleep. At night, a small team of workers walks quietly through the halls. They check the rooms, look at the thermometers and make sure that nothing has changed. Their job is to protect objects that may be hundreds or even thousands of years old.","Old objects are very delicate, and their worst enemies are light, heat and damp air. Strong light slowly changes the colour of paper, cloth and paintings. Heat can make wood crack, and too much moisture helps mould to grow. For this reason, many rooms are kept dim, and the temperature and humidity stay almost the same all day and all night. Machines measure them every minute and send a warning if the numbers change.","Some objects are kept in special glass cases with their own air. A very old book, for example, may lie open on a soft support so that its pages are not bent. Staff are not allowed to touch most items with bare hands, because the natural oils on the skin can leave marks. They wear thin gloves and carry objects with great care. Insects such as moths can also eat cloth and paper, so the museum checks traps every week.","Another important job is repair work, which is called conservation. A conservator cleans a statue gently, fills a small crack or removes old glue. The aim is not to make the object look new, but to stop it from becoming worse. Every action is written in a report, so that future experts know what was done. Thanks to this patient work, children today can look at the same objects that people admired long ago, and so can their grandchildren."],"gloss":[{"w":"delicate","ar":"هشّ، دقيق"},{"w":"damp","ar":"رطب"},{"w":"humidity","ar":"الرطوبة"},{"w":"mould","ar":"عفن"},{"w":"conservation","ar":"الترميم والحفظ"},{"w":"glue","ar":"غراء"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What do night workers do in a museum?","o":["They clean the street.","They check the rooms and protect the objects.","They sell tickets."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: يتفقدون القاعات ويحمون القطع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What can strong light do to paper and paintings?","o":["It makes them stronger.","It makes them grow mould.","It slowly changes their colour."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: الضوء القوي يغيّر اللون ببطء."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do staff wear thin gloves?","o":["Skin oils can leave marks on objects.","The objects are very hot.","The gloves are a uniform."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: زيوت الجلد قد تترك آثاراً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Machines measure the temperature and humidity every minute.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثانية: الأجهزة تقيس كل دقيقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Conservators try to make old objects look new.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الرابعة: الهدف منع التدهور لا جعلها تبدو جديدة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The largest museum in the world has a thousand night workers.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر هذه المعلومة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Moths can eat cloth and ___.","a":["paper"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: العث يأكل القماش والورق."},{"k":"gap","q":"Every action is written in a ___ so that future experts know what was done.","a":["report"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: يُكتب كل إجراء في تقرير."}],"sumAr":"في الليل يراقب موظفو المتحف القاعات ويحمون القطع القديمة من الضوء والحرارة والرطوبة والحشرات. ويقوم المرمّمون بإصلاحها بعناية وتوثيق كل خطوة لتبقى للأجيال القادمة."},{"id":"r-b1-20","l":"B1","title":"Healthy Habits for Students","topic":"health","paras":["Student life is busy. There are lectures, homework, exams and often a part-time job. Many students think that they have no time to look after their health, but small daily habits can make a big difference. A healthy body helps the brain to learn, to remember and to stay calm during difficult weeks.","The first habit is sleep. Most young adults need between seven and nine hours every night. During sleep, the brain organises what you studied during the day. Students who stay awake all night before an exam often forget more than they learn. It is better to finish studying early and go to bed at a regular time. Switching off the phone an hour before bed also helps you to fall asleep.","The second habit is eating well. Breakfast gives energy for the morning, and fruit, vegetables, eggs and whole grains are good choices. Sugary drinks and fast food may give quick energy, but you soon feel tired again. Drinking enough water is just as important, because even a little dryness in the body can make it hard to concentrate.","Finally, move your body and rest your mind. A thirty-minute walk, a short game of football or a few exercises in your room reduce stress and improve your mood. When you study, take a five-minute break every hour and look away from the screen. Talk to friends and family when you feel under pressure, because sharing a problem makes it lighter. You do not need to change everything at once. Choose one habit this week, and add another next week. These habits cost little money, and they will help you to feel stronger and more confident during the whole year, not only before exams."],"gloss":[{"w":"lecture","ar":"محاضرة"},{"w":"organise","ar":"ينظّم"},{"w":"whole grains","ar":"حبوب كاملة"},{"w":"concentrate","ar":"يركّز"},{"w":"stress","ar":"توتر، ضغط نفسي"},{"w":"mood","ar":"مزاج"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How much sleep do most young adults need?","o":["Four to five hours","Seven to nine hours","Ten to twelve hours"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: من سبع إلى تسع ساعات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What happens during sleep, according to the text?","o":["The brain organises what you studied.","The body stops using energy.","The phone charges your memory."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: الدماغ ينظّم ما درسته."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is drinking water important for students?","o":["It makes food cheaper.","It replaces sleep.","It helps them to concentrate."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: قلة الماء تصعّب التركيز."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Sugary drinks can make you feel tired again soon.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: طاقة سريعة ثم تعب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer advises students to change all their habits at once.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الرابعة: اختر عادة واحدة هذا الأسبوع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Students who exercise get better marks than other students.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن الدرجات."},{"k":"gap","q":"A thirty-minute walk can reduce ___ and improve your mood.","a":["stress"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: المشي يخفف التوتر."},{"k":"gap","q":"Take a five-minute ___ every hour when you study.","a":["break"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: خذ استراحة كل ساعة."}],"sumAr":"العادات الصحية تساعد الطالب على التعلّم: نوم كافٍ بانتظام، وطعام جيد وماء كثير، وحركة يومية، واستراحات أثناء الدراسة، والحديث مع الأصدقاء. ويُنصح بتغيير عادة واحدة كل أسبوع."},{"id":"r-b1-21","l":"B1","title":"City Life and Country Life","topic":"city","paras":["People have argued for a long time about where it is better to live. Some love the energy of a big city, while others dream of a quiet house in the countryside. In fact, both ways of life have strong points and weak points, and the best choice depends on the person.","Cities offer many opportunities. There are more jobs, more universities and more hospitals, and public transport can take you almost anywhere. You can meet people from different cultures and enjoy libraries, cinemas and restaurants at any hour. On the other hand, cities are crowded and noisy. Rent is expensive, traffic is heavy and the air is often dirty. Many city workers spend hours every day travelling to the office.","Life in the country is slower and calmer. The air is cleaner, houses are cheaper and people usually know their neighbours. Children can play outside in safe places, and nature is always close. However, there are fewer jobs, and young people often have to leave to study. Villages may have only a small clinic, and the nearest hospital can be far away. Without a car, it can be difficult to shop or visit friends.","Today, technology is changing this old choice. Fast internet allows some people to work from a village for a company in a distant city. Online lessons bring education to places without a university. At the same time, many cities are building parks and cycle paths to make life healthier. Perhaps the future will bring the best parts of both worlds closer together. Before you decide, think about what matters most to you, such as a career, family, quiet or low costs, and then choose the place that fits your life."],"gloss":[{"w":"countryside","ar":"الريف"},{"w":"opportunity","ar":"فرصة"},{"w":"crowded","ar":"مزدحم"},{"w":"rent","ar":"إيجار"},{"w":"neighbour","ar":"جار"},{"w":"distant","ar":"بعيد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which is a strong point of city life?","o":["Cheaper houses","More jobs and universities","Cleaner air"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: وظائف وجامعات ومستشفيات أكثر."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is a problem of life in the country?","o":["The hospital can be far away.","The traffic is heavy.","Rent is very high."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: قد يكون أقرب مستشفى بعيداً."},{"k":"mc","q":"How does fast internet change the choice?","o":["It closes villages.","It makes cities smaller.","It lets some people work from a village."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: العمل من قرية لشركة بعيدة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In the countryside, people usually know their neighbours.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: الناس يعرفون جيرانهم عادة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that everybody should live in a city.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الأولى: الاختيار الأفضل يعتمد على الشخص."},{"k":"tfng","q":"More than half of the world's people live in cities.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر نسبة سكان المدن."},{"k":"gap","q":"Many cities are building parks and ___ paths to make life healthier.","a":["cycle"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: حدائق ومسارات للدراجات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Without a ___, it can be difficult to shop or visit friends in the country.","a":["car"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: دون سيارة يصعب التسوق وزيارة الأصدقاء."}],"sumAr":"للمدينة فرص وخدمات لكنها مزدحمة ومكلفة، وللريف هدوء وهواء نقي لكن فرصه أقل. التكنولوجيا تقرّب بين العالمين، والاختيار الأفضل يعتمد على أولويات كل شخص."},{"id":"r-b1-22","l":"B1","title":"Saving Water","topic":"environment","paras":["Water seems to be everywhere, but only a small part of it is fresh and ready to drink. The rest is salty seawater or frozen at the poles. In many countries, rivers and wells are getting lower every year, because more people need water and the weather is becoming drier. Saving water is therefore a task for everyone.","At home, small changes bring big results. A tap that drips can waste thousands of litres in a year, so repairing it quickly is a good idea. Turning off the tap while you brush your teeth saves several litres each time. A short shower uses much less water than a bath. Families can also wash fruit in a bowl and then use that water for the plants, and they can run the washing machine only when it is full.","Farms use most of the world's fresh water, so smart farming is very important. In traditional systems, much water evaporates in the sun before the plants can drink it. Drip irrigation sends small amounts directly to the roots, and farmers can water at night or early in the morning when it is cooler. Some choose crops that need less water, which is wise in dry regions.","Governments and cities also have a role. They must repair old pipes, because in some places a large part of the water is lost through leaks before it reaches homes. They can collect rainwater, clean used water for a second use and teach children about saving it. Prices can also encourage careful use. Water is not just a resource for today. If we use it wisely, our children will still have enough clean water to drink, to grow food and to live healthy lives."],"gloss":[{"w":"fresh water","ar":"مياه عذبة"},{"w":"drip","ar":"يتقطّر"},{"w":"evaporate","ar":"يتبخّر"},{"w":"irrigation","ar":"ري"},{"w":"leak","ar":"تسرّب"},{"w":"resource","ar":"مورد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why is saving water a task for everyone?","o":["Because seawater is free","Because more people need water and the weather is drier","Because rivers are always full"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: الحاجة تزداد والطقس أكثر جفافاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does drip irrigation do?","o":["It sends small amounts of water to the roots.","It collects rainwater.","It cleans the pipes."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: يوصل كميات صغيرة إلى الجذور."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is a problem with old pipes?","o":["They make water salty.","They make water too hot.","Water is lost through leaks."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: يضيع جزء كبير بالتسرب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A short shower uses less water than a bath.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثانية: الاستحمام السريع أقل استهلاكاً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most of the world's fresh water is used in homes.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: المزارع تستهلك معظم المياه العذبة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Desalination plants are the cheapest way to get water.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتحدث عن تحلية المياه."},{"k":"gap","q":"A tap that ___ can waste thousands of litres in a year.","a":["drips"],"why":"الفقرة الثانية: الصنبور المتقطّر يهدر آلاف اللترات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Farmers can water at night or early in the morning when it is ___.","a":["cooler"],"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: الري في وقت أبرد."}],"sumAr":"قلة المياه العذبة تجعل توفيرها واجباً على الجميع: في البيت بإصلاح التسرب وترشيد الاستعمال، وفي الزراعة بالري بالتنقيط، وعلى الحكومات بإصلاح الأنابيب وإعادة استعمال المياه."},{"id":"r-b1-23","l":"B1","title":"The Invention of the Telephone","topic":"technology","paras":["Before the telephone, a message could travel only as fast as a person, a horse or a train. In the nineteenth century, the telegraph made communication quicker, because it sent short electric signals along wires. However, it needed trained operators and could send only coded dots and dashes, not a human voice.","In the 1870s, inventors in several places tried to send speech through wires. The idea was to change sound into an electric signal and then change it back into sound at the other end. After many experiments, a Scottish-born teacher of the deaf named Alexander Graham Bell built a working device. In 1876 he made the first successful call, and the words he spoke to his assistant in the next room were clearly heard.","The new invention spread quickly. At first, only wealthy people and businesses had telephones, and every call had to pass through an operator who connected the wires by hand. Later, automatic exchanges replaced these operators, and telephone lines were laid across countries and under the sea. Doctors could call for help, shops could take orders, and families could hear each other's voices over long distances.","The telephone kept changing during the twentieth century. Phones became smaller, cheaper and available in nearly every home. Then mobile phones appeared, so people could talk while walking in the street. Today, a smartphone is a camera, a map, a bank and a computer as well as a telephone. Yet its main purpose is still the one that Bell imagined, which is to carry the human voice across a distance. A small experiment in a workshop therefore changed the way the whole world communicates. Today, we hardly notice this miracle, because it has become part of everyday life."],"gloss":[{"w":"telegraph","ar":"التلغراف"},{"w":"signal","ar":"إشارة"},{"w":"operator","ar":"عامل مقسم"},{"w":"device","ar":"جهاز"},{"w":"exchange","ar":"مقسم هاتفي"},{"w":"distance","ar":"مسافة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What was a limit of the telegraph?","o":["It could not use electricity.","It could only send coded dots and dashes, not a voice.","It was cheaper than a telephone."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: يرسل رموزاً مشفّرة فقط لا صوتاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was the basic idea of the telephone?","o":["To turn sound into an electric signal and back into sound","To send letters faster","To record voices on paper"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: تحويل الصوت إلى إشارة ثم إلى صوت."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who replaced the telephone operators?","o":["Doctors","Shop owners","Automatic exchanges"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: المقاسم الآلية حلّت محلهم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Bell made the first successful call in 1876.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثانية: أول مكالمة ناجحة عام 1876."},{"k":"tfng","q":"At first, nearly every home had a telephone.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: في البداية الأغنياء والشركات فقط."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Bell was married with three children.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر حياة بيل العائلية."},{"k":"gap","q":"The telegraph sent short electric signals along ___.","a":["wires"],"why":"الفقرة الأولى: عبر الأسلاك."},{"k":"gap","q":"Later, ___ phones appeared, so people could talk while walking in the street.","a":["mobile"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: ظهرت الهواتف المحمولة."}],"sumAr":"قبل الهاتف كان التلغراف يرسل رموزاً فقط. في عام 1876 نجح بيل في نقل الصوت عبر الأسلاك، ثم انتشر الهاتف وتطور حتى صار الهاتف الذكي الذي ما زال يحمل الصوت عبر المسافات."},{"id":"r-b1-24","l":"B1","title":"How Courts Use Evidence","topic":"law","paras":["In a court case, a judge cannot simply guess who is right. Decisions must be based on evidence, which means facts and objects that help to show what really happened. A trial is like putting together a puzzle. Each piece of evidence is small, but together the pieces can show a clear picture.","There are several kinds of evidence. Witness evidence comes from people who saw or heard something and who answer questions in court. Documents, such as contracts, bank records or emails, can show what was agreed. Physical evidence includes objects, for example a tool, a photograph or a fingerprint. Experts, such as doctors or accountants, can also explain technical matters that ordinary people do not understand.","Not everything can be used. Courts ask whether evidence is relevant and reliable. Relevant means that it is connected to the question that the court must decide. Reliable means that it can be trusted. A witness who was far away in the dark may remember wrongly. Evidence that was collected illegally, for example by breaking the rules during a search, may be rejected by the judge. Both sides have the right to challenge what the other side presents.","Finally, the court must decide how much weight to give each piece of evidence. In a criminal case, the prosecution must prove its claim beyond reasonable doubt. This is a high standard, because a mistake may send an innocent person to prison. In a civil case, such as a dispute about money, the judge usually asks which story is more likely to be true. Careful rules about evidence protect fairness, and they help the public to trust the decisions of the courts. In this way, justice depends not only on judges, but also on careful and honest work with facts."],"gloss":[{"w":"evidence","ar":"أدلة، بيّنات"},{"w":"fingerprint","ar":"بصمة إصبع"},{"w":"relevant","ar":"ذو صلة"},{"w":"challenge","ar":"يطعن في، يعترض على"},{"w":"prosecution","ar":"النيابة، الادعاء"},{"w":"dispute","ar":"نزاع"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer compare a trial to?","o":["A race","A puzzle","A journey"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة الأولى: المحاكمة كتركيب أحجية."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which is an example of physical evidence?","o":["A bank record","An expert's opinion","A fingerprint"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثانية: الأشياء مثل البصمة والصورة والأداة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does reliable evidence mean?","o":["It can be trusted.","It is very old.","It is connected to the question."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة: الموثوق هو ما يمكن الوثوق به."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Evidence collected illegally may be rejected by the judge.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة الثالثة: قد يرفض القاضي الأدلة المجموعة بطريقة غير قانونية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In a criminal case, the standard of proof is lower than in a civil case.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة الرابعة: الجنائي يتطلب معياراً أعلى."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Judges in all countries must be lawyers for at least ten years.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر شروط تعيين القضاة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The prosecution must prove its claim beyond ___ doubt.","a":["reasonable"],"why":"الفقرة الرابعة: إثبات يتجاوز الشك المعقول."},{"k":"gap","q":"Documents such as contracts, bank records or ___ can show what was agreed.","a":["emails"],"why":"الفقرة الثانية: العقود والسجلات والبريد الإلكتروني."}],"sumAr":"تعتمد المحاكم على الأدلة من شهود ووثائق وأشياء مادية وخبراء، ويجب أن تكون ذات صلة وموثوقة ومجموعة قانونياً. في القضايا الجنائية يجب الإثبات بما يتجاوز الشك المعقول حمايةً للأبرياء."},{"id":"r-b2-01","l":"B2","title":"The Science of Memory and Forgetting","topic":"science","paras":["Memory is often compared to a recording device, but scientists now regard it as something far more active. Every time we recall an event, the brain rebuilds it from scattered fragments: a smell here, a face there, a feeling of fear or pleasure. Because this process is a reconstruction rather than a replay, memories can change slightly each time they are used. This helps to explain why two people who witnessed the same accident often describe it in different ways.","Researchers usually divide memory into three stages. First, sensory memory holds an impression of what we see or hear for only a second or two. Information that receives attention then moves into short-term, or working, memory, where an adult can keep about seven items, such as the digits of a telephone number, for roughly half a minute. Only a small part of this material is passed on to long-term memory, which has no known limit and can last for decades.","The transfer depends heavily on how the material is processed. In a well-known type of experiment, volunteers who were asked to think about the meaning of words remembered far more of them than volunteers who simply counted the letters. Linking new facts to things we already know, or creating a vivid mental picture, strengthens the connection. Sleep also plays a part, because the brain appears to replay the day's experiences at night and to store the important ones more securely.","Forgetting, however, is not simply a failure. A nineteenth-century German researcher tested himself on lists of meaningless syllables and found that most of the material vanished within a day, after which the loss slowed down. The resulting forgetting curve has been confirmed many times. It shows that the fastest loss occurs soon after learning, which is why a short review within twenty-four hours is more effective than a long one a week later.","Some forgetting is actually useful. If we remembered every parking space we had ever used, finding today's car would be much harder. Many scientists therefore argue that the brain deliberately allows unimportant details to fade so that valuable information is easier to reach. Others believe that most lost memories are not erased but merely hard to retrieve, because the cues that once pointed to them have disappeared. A familiar song may suddenly bring back a school trip that had been forgotten for years.","Practical studies suggest several ways to improve retention. Spreading study sessions over several days produces better results than cramming in a single night. Testing yourself, instead of rereading notes, forces the brain to retrieve information and makes the memory stronger. Regular exercise and adequate sleep also help, while heavy stress can interfere with the process. No technique makes memory perfect, but understanding how it works allows students to use their time wisely."],"gloss":[{"w":"reconstruction","ar":"إعادة بناء"},{"w":"witness","ar":"شاهد عيان / يشهد"},{"w":"retention","ar":"الاحتفاظ بالمعلومات"},{"w":"cramming","ar":"الحشو (الدراسة المكثفة في وقت قصير)"},{"w":"retrieve","ar":"يسترجع"},{"w":"cue","ar":"مؤشر تذكيري / إشارة"},{"w":"vanish","ar":"يختفي"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["How information is stored in stages","Why witnesses disagree about accidents","The limits of long-term storage","Tricks for remembering telephone numbers"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["The benefits of forgetting nothing","How quickly learned material is lost","Why sleep improves exam results","A new method of testing intelligence"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"The brain may alter a memory slightly each time it is recalled.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 0: الذاكرة إعادة بناء وقد تتغير قليلاً مع كل استرجاع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Information can stay in working memory for several minutes.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: تبقى المعلومات في الذاكرة العاملة نحو نصف دقيقة فقط."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Older adults forget names more often than young people do.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين كبار السن والشباب إطلاقاً."},{"k":"gap","q":"Long-term memory has no known ___.","a":["limit"],"why":"الفقرة 1: ذاكرة المدى الطويل ليس لها حد معروف."},{"k":"gap","q":"Testing yourself forces the brain to ___ information.","a":["retrieve"],"why":"الفقرة 5: الاختبار الذاتي يجبر الدماغ على استرجاع المعلومات."},{"k":"mc","q":"The volunteers who thought about the meaning of words","o":["remembered fewer words than the others","remembered more words than the others","finished the task more quickly","preferred counting letters"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: من فكروا في المعنى تذكروا كلمات أكثر."},{"k":"mc","q":"Some scientists believe that lost memories are often","o":["permanently deleted","caused by stress","hard to reach rather than erased","stored only during sleep"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: كثير من الذكريات ليست ممحوّة بل يصعب استرجاعها."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the forgetting curve, the best time to review new material is","o":["one week later","just before an exam","soon after learning it","after a full night of sleep"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 3: الفقدان الأسرع يقع بعد التعلم مباشرة، فالمراجعة المبكرة أفضل."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص أن الذاكرة عملية إعادة بناء نشطة تمر بثلاث مراحل، وأن النسيان طبيعي وقد يفيد الدماغ. ويقدم طرقاً عملية لتحسين الحفظ مثل المراجعة الموزعة والاختبار الذاتي والنوم الكافي."},{"id":"r-b2-02","l":"B2","title":"Growing Food in the City","topic":"environment","paras":["By 2050 about two out of every three people are expected to live in cities, which raises an old question: where will their food come from? Traditionally, farms have been far from the places where food is eaten, and vegetables have travelled hundreds of kilometres by lorry, ship or plane. Urban farming tries to shorten that journey by growing crops inside the city itself, on rooftops, in empty car parks, in disused buildings and even in shipping containers.","The simplest form is the community garden. Residents of an apartment block rent a small plot on vacant land, or share a roof, and grow tomatoes, herbs and beans for their families. These gardens produce modest harvests, but they bring neighbours together, offer exercise outdoors and teach children where food comes from. In several cities, schools now run their own plots and use the produce in their lunch menus.","More technical methods have appeared in recent years. In hydroponics, plants grow in water containing dissolved nutrients rather than in soil. Vertical farms stack trays of lettuce or herbs in layers inside a warehouse, with LED lamps replacing sunlight. Because the environment is carefully controlled, such farms can harvest many times a year, and they use far less water than open fields, since the water is recycled.","The benefits go beyond fresh produce. Food grown close to consumers needs little transport, so fewer emissions are produced, and it can be picked ripe instead of early. Green roofs also absorb rainwater, which reduces flooding, and they insulate buildings, keeping them cooler in summer. Some projects employ people who have struggled to find work, providing training in horticulture and basic business skills.","Nevertheless, urban farming faces real obstacles. Land in city centres is expensive, and a farm must compete with housing and offices for space. Indoor systems depend on electricity, and the lamps and pumps can cost more to run than the crops are worth unless the energy is cheap or renewable. For this reason, most commercial indoor farms grow only leafy vegetables and herbs, which are light, quick to grow and sell at a good price. Staple foods such as wheat and rice need far too much space to be produced this way.","Experts therefore describe urban farming as a supplement to conventional agriculture, not a replacement for it. It is unlikely to feed a whole city, but it can improve the supply of fresh vegetables, reduce waste and make residents more aware of the food system. Planners increasingly include space for growing in new developments, treating it as part of ordinary urban infrastructure, like parks or cycle lanes. Even a modest roof garden, in this view, is a small step towards a city that depends less on distant farms."],"gloss":[{"w":"rooftop","ar":"سطح المبنى"},{"w":"vacant","ar":"شاغر / فارغ"},{"w":"hydroponics","ar":"الزراعة المائية"},{"w":"nutrient","ar":"مادة مغذية"},{"w":"emissions","ar":"انبعاثات"},{"w":"obstacle","ar":"عقبة"},{"w":"staple","ar":"غذاء أساسي"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Why plants no longer need water","Modern growing techniques without soil or sunlight","The history of community gardens","How schools teach gardening"],"a":1},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["The difficulties facing city farms","Cheap electricity for every home","Why wheat is expensive","Competition between schools"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Vertical farms rely on artificial light rather than the sun.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: المصابيح تحل محل ضوء الشمس في المزارع العمودية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Community gardens normally produce very large harvests.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: محاصيل الحدائق المجتمعية متواضعة وليست كبيرة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Workers on urban farms are paid more than workers on traditional farms.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر شيئاً عن مقارنة الأجور."},{"k":"gap","q":"Hydroponic plants grow in water that contains dissolved ___.","a":["nutrients"],"why":"الفقرة 2: تنمو النباتات في ماء يحوي مغذيات مذابة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Most commercial indoor farms grow leafy vegetables and ___.","a":["herbs"],"why":"الفقرة 4: تزرع المزارع الداخلية الخضر الورقية والأعشاب."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are wheat and rice rarely grown in indoor farms?","o":["They need too much space","They do not grow without soil at all","They sell at a very high price","They grow too quickly"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: القمح والأرز يحتاجان مساحة كبيرة جداً."},{"k":"mc","q":"One benefit of green roofs mentioned in the text is that they","o":["produce electricity","help to prevent flooding","make food cheaper","create new housing"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: الأسطح الخضراء تمتص مياه المطر فتقلل الفيضانات."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's overall view of urban farming is that it","o":["will soon replace traditional farms","is too expensive to be useful","is a helpful addition to existing agriculture","only works in small villages"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: الزراعة الحضرية مكمّل للزراعة التقليدية لا بديل عنها."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص أشكال الزراعة داخل المدن من الحدائق المجتمعية إلى المزارع العمودية، وفوائدها البيئية والاجتماعية. ويبيّن أن تكلفة الأرض والطاقة تحدّ منها، لذلك تبقى مكمّلاً للزراعة التقليدية."},{"id":"r-b2-03","l":"B2","title":"A Short History of Maps","topic":"history","paras":["Long before written alphabets existed, people drew maps. Scratches on stone and bone, some of them tens of thousands of years old, may show hunting grounds, rivers or camp sites. Early maps were practical tools, made to answer a simple question: how do I reach the place I need? They were not drawn to scale, and they often showed only what mattered to the person who made them.","The first maps that attempted accuracy appeared in ancient civilisations that needed to measure land. In river valleys, floods washed away boundaries every year, so surveyors recorded fields on clay tablets or papyrus so that taxes and ownership could be settled. Greek scholars later added mathematics. They argued that the Earth is a sphere, calculated its size with surprising accuracy, and introduced lines of latitude and longitude, which allowed any place to be described by two numbers.","In medieval Europe, many maps became religious or symbolic. Large circular maps placed a holy city at the centre and filled the edges with imaginary creatures. Their purpose was to express a view of the world rather than to guide travellers. Meanwhile, sailors used a different kind of chart, drawn from experience of coastlines, which showed harbours and straight lines pointing towards the main compass directions. Arab geographers combined travellers' reports with astronomy to produce detailed maps of trade routes across Asia, Africa and Europe.","The age of exploration from the fifteenth century transformed mapmaking. Voyages to new continents brought a flood of information, and the printing press allowed maps to be copied cheaply. In the sixteenth century a new projection was designed that kept compass bearings as straight lines, which made it ideal for navigation. However, it enlarged regions near the poles, so that northern countries looked much bigger than they really were. This distortion still affects how many people imagine the world.","Accuracy improved again when governments began to use triangulation, measuring the angles between distant hills to calculate exact distances. National survey agencies then produced detailed maps of entire countries, which were used for taxes, roads, railways and armies. In the twentieth century aeroplanes and then satellites replaced walking surveyors, since a single photograph taken from the sky could capture an area that would take months to measure on foot.","Today most people use maps on their phones, which rely on satellite signals to show their position in real time. These digital maps combine traffic information, photographs and reviews, and they update constantly. Yet the basic problem has not changed: a flat map can never show a round planet without some distortion. Choosing what to show, and what to leave out, remains a decision made by people, so every map still reflects the priorities of those who draw it."],"gloss":[{"w":"survey","ar":"مسح (الأراضي)"},{"w":"boundary","ar":"حدّ فاصل"},{"w":"latitude","ar":"خط العرض"},{"w":"projection","ar":"إسقاط خرائطي"},{"w":"distortion","ar":"تشويه"},{"w":"triangulation","ar":"التثليث (قياس بالمثلثات)"},{"w":"chart","ar":"خريطة ملاحية"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Maps that served belief and maps that served sailors","The invention of the compass","Why Arab geographers stopped drawing maps","The end of religious maps"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["The decline of printing","Governments and new methods of measuring land","How satellites were launched","Why mountains are hard to map"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"Greek scholars introduced a system that describes a place using two numbers.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: خطوط العرض والطول تحدد أي مكان برقمين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Medieval religious maps were mainly designed to help travellers find their way.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: غرضها التعبير عن نظرة للعالم لا إرشاد المسافرين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Maps drawn on clay lasted longer than maps drawn on papyrus.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين متانة الطين والبردي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Ancient surveyors recorded fields on clay tablets or ___.","a":["papyrus"],"why":"الفقرة 1: سجّل المساحون الحقول على ألواح الطين أو البردي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Governments used ___ to calculate exact distances between distant hills.","a":["triangulation"],"why":"الفقرة 4: استخدمت الحكومات التثليث لحساب المسافات."},{"k":"mc","q":"The sixteenth-century projection was useful for sailors because it","o":["showed countries at their true size","kept compass directions as straight lines","included pictures of imaginary creatures","was cheaper than other maps"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: حافظ على الاتجاهات كخطوط مستقيمة فصلح للملاحة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was one result of the new projection?","o":["Southern countries appeared too large","Northern countries appeared larger than reality","Oceans disappeared from maps","Maps became less popular"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: تضخمت المناطق القريبة من القطبين."},{"k":"mc","q":"In the final paragraph, the writer suggests that every map","o":["is perfectly accurate today","depends on the choices of its makers","is made by satellites alone","will soon become unnecessary"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: كل خريطة تعكس أولويات من يرسمها."}],"sumAr":"يتتبع النص تاريخ الخرائط من الرسوم البدائية إلى الخرائط الرقمية، مروراً بإسهامات اليونان والعرب وعصر الاستكشاف والمسح بالأقمار الصناعية. ويخلص إلى أن الخريطة المسطحة تظل تعكس اختيارات من يرسمها."},{"id":"r-b2-04","l":"B2","title":"Why Languages Disappear","topic":"society","paras":["Linguists estimate that around seven thousand languages are spoken in the world today, but nearly half of them may disappear before the end of this century. Many have fewer than a thousand speakers, and a large number are used only by elderly people. When the last fluent speaker of a language dies, an entire way of describing the world disappears with them, often without anyone outside the community noticing. Most of the languages at risk are found in remote mountain areas, islands and forests, where small groups have lived apart for centuries.","Languages rarely vanish because they are banned; far more often they fade through gradual pressure. When a community moves from villages to cities, parents discover that jobs, schools and government services all use a dominant language. They begin to speak it at home so that their children will succeed, and within two or three generations the young people understand the old language but no longer speak it. Linguists call this process language shift.","Economic forces are therefore powerful, but history also matters. Colonial administrations and national education systems have often punished pupils for using local languages in the classroom, teaching them that these languages were backward. Such attitudes leave lasting shame, and some families still hesitate to pass on their mother tongue. Modern media add to the pressure, since films, websites and software are produced mainly in a small number of widely spoken languages.","Why does this matter? A language stores knowledge built up over centuries, including the names of local plants, methods of farming and stories about the landscape. Some communities have words for weather patterns that scientists have only recently begun to study. Linguists also value endangered languages because their grammar can challenge assumptions about how human language works. For the speakers themselves, the language is a central part of their identity and sense of belonging.","Reviving a language is difficult, but it has succeeded in some places. Successful programmes tend to share several features: young children are taught in the language at nursery school, older speakers are paid to teach, and the community itself decides the goals. In several regions, so-called language nests, in which elders look after small children and speak only the heritage language, have produced a new generation of fluent speakers within about ten years. Such results are encouraging, because they show that a language with very few speakers can still recover when the next generation is given the chance to learn it.","Technology can help as well as harm. Speakers now record dictionaries on smartphones, publish stories online and use messaging apps to write in their own language. Researchers are also building speech-recognition tools for languages that have little written material. Yet experts agree that no recording can replace daily use. A language survives only when people choose to speak it with their children."],"gloss":[{"w":"endangered","ar":"مهدَّد بالانقراض"},{"w":"dominant","ar":"مهيمن / سائد"},{"w":"colonial","ar":"استعماري"},{"w":"heritage","ar":"تراث / موروث"},{"w":"revive","ar":"يُحيي من جديد"},{"w":"fluent","ar":"بطلاقة"},{"w":"identity","ar":"هوية"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["How governments forbid minority languages","The slow shift from one language to another","The best schools for young children","Why cities have too many languages"],"a":1},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["The value of endangered languages","How grammar is taught","The weather in remote regions","Why elderly people stay silent"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"A community may lose its old language within two or three generations.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: قد يفقد الجيل الثالث القدرة على التحدث باللغة القديمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most endangered languages die out because they are officially banned.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: اللغات نادراً ما تختفي بسبب الحظر، بل بالضغط التدريجي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Languages with a written alphabet survive longer than spoken-only languages.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين اللغات المكتوبة وغير المكتوبة في البقاء."},{"k":"gap","q":"The gradual move from a minority language to a dominant one is called language ___.","a":["shift"],"why":"الفقرة 1: يسمي اللغويون هذه العملية التحول اللغوي."},{"k":"gap","q":"A language can store knowledge such as the names of local ___.","a":["plants"],"why":"الفقرة 3: اللغة تحفظ أسماء النباتات المحلية وأساليب الزراعة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do many parents start using the dominant language at home?","o":["They have forgotten the old language","They want their children to do well","The government pays them to do so","Their grandparents ask them to"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: يريدون لأبنائهم النجاح في الدراسة والعمل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Language nests are successful because","o":["children hear the heritage language from elders","they use modern software","they replace nursery schools","they are run by governments"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: كبار السن يتحدثون اللغة التراثية فقط مع الصغار."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's conclusion about technology?","o":["It has no effect on languages","It can replace daily speaking","It is useful but cannot save a language alone","It is the main cause of language loss"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: التقنية تساعد لكن لا شيء يغني عن الاستخدام اليومي."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص أن اللغات تختفي غالباً بالتحول التدريجي بسبب الاقتصاد والتعليم والإعلام لا بالحظر. ويبيّن قيمتها المعرفية والهوياتية، ونجاح برامج الإحياء، ودور التقنية المحدود إذا غاب الاستخدام اليومي."},{"id":"r-b2-05","l":"B2","title":"Storing Renewable Energy","topic":"environment","paras":["Solar panels produce electricity only when the sun shines, and wind turbines only when the wind blows. Demand for power, however, rises and falls according to the habits of people: it is often highest in the early evening, when homes are lit and cooked in but the sun has already set. Unless surplus energy can be stored and released later, electricity from renewable sources cannot fully replace power stations that burn fuel on demand.","The oldest and most widespread solution is pumped hydropower. When there is more electricity than needed, water is pumped uphill into a reservoir; when demand rises, the water is released through turbines and generates power again. The method is reliable and can supply a city for many hours. Its weakness is geography, because it requires suitable mountains and large amounts of water, and building a new reservoir can damage local ecosystems.","Batteries are the best-known alternative. Lithium-ion batteries, the type found in phones and electric cars, are now installed in large containers next to solar farms. They respond within fractions of a second, so they are excellent for stabilising the grid. Yet they usually store energy for only a few hours, and mining the metals needed to make them raises environmental and social concerns. Researchers are therefore testing cheaper designs that use more common materials, such as sodium or iron.","Another approach is to store energy as heat. In one design, surplus electricity heats a tank of molten salt or a pile of hot rock, and the heat is later used to boil water and drive a turbine. The materials are cheap and last for decades. Heat storage is particularly attractive for factories, which need high temperatures anyway, and for district heating networks that supply hot water to entire neighbourhoods.","Hydrogen offers a way to store energy for weeks or even months. Electricity splits water into hydrogen and oxygen, the hydrogen is kept in tanks or underground caverns, and it can later be burnt or used in a fuel cell. The disadvantage is inefficiency: a large share of the original energy is lost during the process, so hydrogen is expensive. Specialists therefore expect it to be used mainly for industries that are hard to electrify, such as steel production and shipping.","No single technology will solve the problem. Most experts expect future power systems to combine several kinds of storage, together with smarter ways of managing demand. For example, electric cars can be charged when electricity is plentiful and cheap, and washing machines or water heaters can be programmed to run at the same times. Better storage will not make renewable energy free, but it will make it dependable enough to power daily life. The challenge is now less about invention than about building enough capacity quickly and at a reasonable price."],"gloss":[{"w":"surplus","ar":"فائض"},{"w":"reservoir","ar":"خزّان مائي"},{"w":"grid","ar":"شبكة الكهرباء"},{"w":"molten","ar":"مصهور"},{"w":"inefficiency","ar":"قلة الكفاءة"},{"w":"electrify","ar":"يعمل بالكهرباء"},{"w":"dependable","ar":"موثوق"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["Using water and mountains to store power","The dangers of mining metals","How to build a wind turbine","Why reservoirs are always harmful"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["A cheap fuel for domestic cooking","Storage for long periods, with a high cost","The history of steel production","How oxygen is used in shipping"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"Lithium-ion batteries can react to changes in the grid almost instantly.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: تستجيب البطاريات خلال أجزاء من الثانية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Pumped hydropower can be built successfully in any part of the world.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: تحتاج إلى جبال مناسبة وكميات كبيرة من الماء."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Sodium batteries are already cheaper than lithium-ion batteries in most countries.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكر اختبار تصاميم أرخص فقط، دون مقارنة الأسعار الحالية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Lithium-ion batteries are excellent for stabilising the ___.","a":["grid"],"why":"الفقرة 2: البطاريات ممتازة لتثبيت شبكة الكهرباء."},{"k":"gap","q":"Hydrogen can be kept in tanks or underground ___.","a":["caverns"],"why":"الفقرة 4: يخزَّن الهيدروجين في خزانات أو كهوف تحت الأرض."},{"k":"mc","q":"The main problem with renewable power described in paragraph 0 is that","o":["it cannot be produced at all in winter","supply does not always match demand","it is more expensive than fuel","it harms power stations"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 0: الإنتاج مرتبط بالشمس والرياح بينما يتغير الطلب."},{"k":"mc","q":"Heat storage is particularly suitable for factories because","o":["they already need high temperatures","they use less electricity","they are close to mountains","they produce hydrogen"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: المصانع تحتاج أصلاً إلى درجات حرارة عالية."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer concludes that future energy systems will","o":["depend on one type of battery","use a mixture of storage methods and demand management","no longer need storage","rely mainly on hydrogen"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: يتوقع الخبراء الجمع بين عدة تقنيات وإدارة ذكية للطلب."}],"sumAr":"يوضح النص أن الطاقة المتجددة متقطعة لذلك تحتاج إلى تخزين، ويستعرض الضخ المائي والبطاريات والحرارة والهيدروجين بمزايا وعيوب كل منها. ويرجح أن المستقبل سيجمع بين عدة طرق مع إدارة أذكى للطلب."},{"id":"r-b2-06","l":"B2","title":"The Psychology of Decision-Making","topic":"mind","paras":["We like to believe that our choices are the result of careful reasoning, yet psychologists have shown that many decisions are made quickly and almost automatically. Researchers often describe two systems of thinking. One is fast, intuitive and effortless; the other is slow, deliberate and tiring. In everyday life we rely on the fast system for most things, from choosing a sandwich to judging whether a stranger looks friendly, and we call on the slow system only when a problem seems difficult or important.","The fast system uses mental shortcuts, known as heuristics. They are usually helpful, because they save time and energy, but they can also lead to predictable mistakes. One example is the availability heuristic: people judge how common something is by how easily examples come to mind. After reading several news reports about an aeroplane accident, many people feel that flying is dangerous, even though statistics show that it is among the safest forms of travel.","Another well-documented pattern is anchoring. When people are given a number before they make an estimate, that number influences their answer, even if it is clearly irrelevant. In one type of experiment, volunteers who first saw a high random number guessed higher prices for ordinary objects than those who saw a low one. Sellers use the same effect when they display a high original price beside a reduced one, making the discount appear generous.","Emotion also shapes decisions. People generally dislike losses more than they enjoy equal gains, a tendency called loss aversion. As a result, an investor may keep a falling share for too long, hoping to avoid admitting a loss, and a shopper may buy an unnecessary extra item because it is presented as a last chance. Tiredness and hunger make matters worse; studies suggest that decisions made late in a long day are more likely to be cautious or careless.","Understanding these tendencies does not remove them, but it allows people and organisations to design better choices. Some governments, for instance, enrol workers automatically in pension schemes, while still allowing them to leave. Because most people accept the default option, participation rises sharply without anyone being forced. Hospitals use checklists for similar reasons: a simple list prevents experienced doctors from forgetting steps when they are under pressure.","Individuals can also improve their own decisions. Waiting a day before a major purchase, asking someone with no stake in the outcome for advice, and writing down reasons for and against an option all give the slow system time to work. Psychologists do not claim that people should analyse every choice; that would be exhausting and impractical. The aim is to recognise the few situations where a little extra thought is worth the effort."],"gloss":[{"w":"intuitive","ar":"حدسي / بديهي"},{"w":"deliberate","ar":"متأنٍّ / متعمَّد"},{"w":"heuristic","ar":"قاعدة ذهنية مختصرة"},{"w":"anchoring","ar":"التثبيت (التأثر برقم أولي)"},{"w":"loss aversion","ar":"النفور من الخسارة"},{"w":"default","ar":"الخيار الافتراضي"},{"w":"stake","ar":"مصلحة / نصيب"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["Mental shortcuts and the mistakes they cause","How to avoid air travel","The safety of aeroplanes","Why news reports are always wrong"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Why pensions are unpopular","Designing choices to help people decide better","Forcing workers to leave their jobs","How doctors choose their patients"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"People tend to be more upset by a loss than pleased by a gain of the same size.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: الناس يكرهون الخسارة أكثر مما يفرحون بمكسب مساوٍ."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Anchoring only affects people who are tired.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: الرقم الأولي يؤثر في التقدير بصفة عامة لا بسبب التعب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Younger people are better at slow, deliberate thinking than older people.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتحدث عن اختلاف الأعمار في التفكير."},{"k":"gap","q":"Mental shortcuts are known as ___.","a":["heuristics"],"why":"الفقرة 1: الاختصارات الذهنية تسمى heuristics."},{"k":"gap","q":"Hospitals use ___ so that busy doctors do not forget steps.","a":["checklists"],"why":"الفقرة 4: تستخدم المستشفيات قوائم التحقق لتفادي نسيان الخطوات."},{"k":"mc","q":"After reading reports of an aeroplane accident, many people feel that flying is dangerous because","o":["statistics prove it","examples come to mind easily","the slow system is active","they are tired"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: يحكمون بسهولة استحضار الأمثلة في الذهن."},{"k":"mc","q":"Automatic enrolment in pension schemes works because","o":["workers are forced to join","most people keep the default option","pensions are free","employers choose for them"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: معظم الناس يقبلون الخيار الافتراضي فترتفع المشاركة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do psychologists recommend in the final paragraph?","o":["Analysing every decision in detail","Never trusting advice","Spending extra thought on a few important decisions","Making all choices quickly"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: يكفي التفكير الإضافي في المواقف القليلة المهمة."}],"sumAr":"يبيّن النص أن معظم قراراتنا سريعة وتعتمد على اختصارات ذهنية قد تقود إلى أخطاء مثل التثبيت والنفور من الخسارة. ويعرض كيف يمكن للمؤسسات والأفراد تحسين القرارات بتصميم الخيارات والتمهل."},{"id":"r-b2-07","l":"B2","title":"How Open Data Changes Government","topic":"government","paras":["Governments collect enormous amounts of information: budgets, contracts, school results, air quality readings and bus timetables. For most of history, this material stayed inside offices, available only to officials or to citizens who submitted formal requests. Open data changes that approach. It means publishing information online, in a format that computers can read, with a licence that allows anyone to use it freely and without payment.","The first benefit is transparency. When a city publishes every payment above a small amount, journalists and residents can search for unusual patterns, such as the same company winning many contracts without any competition. Knowing that spending can be checked tends to discourage waste and corruption. In several countries, citizen groups have built websites that turn long spreadsheets into simple charts showing where each unit of public money goes.","Open data also supports better services. Transport authorities that release real-time bus locations allow private developers to create journey-planning apps, which the authority itself might not have had the budget to build. Hospitals that share anonymous waiting-time statistics help patients choose the nearest clinic with the shortest queue. Because such apps are often free, the public benefits while the government saves money.","Inside government, open data can also improve decisions. Departments frequently hold information that other departments need but cannot easily obtain. When data is published in a standard format, officials can compare results across regions, identify which schools are improving fastest and direct funds accordingly. Researchers outside government add expertise; for instance, university teams have used published crime figures to test whether new street lighting reduces night-time offences. Findings of this kind can then be tested in other cities, so that good ideas spread quickly and weak ones are dropped before much money has been spent.","Yet open data is not a perfect solution. Publishing a file does not guarantee that anyone can understand it, because many datasets are incomplete, out of date or full of technical codes. Privacy is another concern, since combining several apparently harmless datasets can reveal who a person is. Governments must therefore remove personal details carefully, and some critics argue that publication is sometimes used to look transparent while the most sensitive information stays hidden.","The most successful projects treat users as partners. Officials meet developers, journalists and teachers to ask which datasets they actually need, and they train staff to publish clean, regularly updated files. Over time, the culture of a department can change: instead of asking why information should be released, employees begin to ask why it should not. Some cities even invite residents to vote on which datasets to release first, which gives the public a direct voice in the process and helps officials to focus on the information people truly value. That shift, more than any website, is the real measure of open government."],"gloss":[{"w":"licence","ar":"ترخيص"},{"w":"transparency","ar":"شفافية"},{"w":"contract","ar":"عقد"},{"w":"anonymous","ar":"مجهول الهوية"},{"w":"dataset","ar":"مجموعة بيانات"},{"w":"sensitive","ar":"حسّاس"},{"w":"queue","ar":"طابور"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["How publishing information helps to expose waste","Why journalists dislike spreadsheets","The cost of building websites","How contracts are written"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["The success of every open data project","The limits and risks of publishing data","Why privacy is no longer needed","How to remove old datasets"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"Publishing payments can help people notice companies that win many contracts without competition.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: النشر يكشف أنماطاً غير عادية مثل فوز شركة بعقود بلا منافسة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Transport authorities build all journey-planning apps themselves.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: مطورون خاصون ينشئون هذه التطبيقات من بيانات الحافلات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Open data portals are used more often by journalists than by students.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر من يستخدم البوابات أكثر."},{"k":"gap","q":"Open data is published in a format that ___ can read.","a":["computers"],"why":"الفقرة 0: تُنشر البيانات بصيغة تستطيع الحواسيب قراءتها."},{"k":"gap","q":"Combining harmless datasets can reveal who a ___ is.","a":["person"],"why":"الفقرة 4: دمج مجموعات بيانات بسيطة قد يكشف هوية الشخص."},{"k":"mc","q":"How do hospitals benefit patients through open data?","o":["They reduce the price of treatment","They show which clinic currently has the shortest wait","They allow patients to choose doctors by age","They publish patients' names"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: إحصاءات أوقات الانتظار تساعد المرضى على اختيار أقصر طابور."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do officials benefit from data published in a standard format?","o":["It is cheaper to print","They can compare results between regions","It prevents any criticism","It replaces all researchers"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: المعيار الموحد يتيح مقارنة النتائج بين المناطق."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the writer, the real sign of open government is","o":["a new website","a change in the attitude of employees","the number of datasets","the size of the budget"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: تحوّل ثقافة الموظفين هو المقياس الحقيقي."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص كيف تعزز البيانات المفتوحة الشفافية وتحسّن الخدمات والقرارات الحكومية، مع التنبيه إلى مشكلات جودة البيانات والخصوصية. ويؤكد أن النجاح الحقيقي يكمن في تغيير ثقافة الإدارات وإشراك المستخدمين."},{"id":"r-b2-08","l":"B2","title":"The Changing Legal Profession","topic":"law","paras":["For centuries, the image of a lawyer was a man in a dark suit sitting in a dusty office, surrounded by shelves of heavy books. Today the profession looks very different. Lawyers work in teams, use software, advise businesses in several countries at once, and increasingly come from backgrounds that were once excluded. These changes have been gradual, but together they have altered who practises law, how they do it and whom they serve.","The first major change concerns who becomes a lawyer. In many countries, law was historically open mainly to wealthy men, because training required long unpaid apprenticeships and expensive fees. Over the twentieth century, universities widened access, scholarships were introduced and women entered the profession in large numbers. In some countries women now make up the majority of new law graduates, although they remain under-represented among senior partners and judges.","Technology has also transformed daily work. Research that once required days in a library can now be completed in minutes using searchable databases. Programs scan thousands of contracts to find unusual clauses, and some tools draft simple documents such as leases or wills. Junior lawyers used to spend their first years checking papers line by line; much of this work is now automated, so firms are rethinking how to train new staff.","Globalisation has shaped the profession as well. Companies that trade across borders need advice on several legal systems at once, so many law firms have merged and opened offices abroad. Specialisation has increased: instead of being general practitioners, many lawyers now concentrate on fields such as data protection, environmental regulation or international arbitration. A client with a dispute may even choose to settle it outside court, through mediation, which is usually faster and cheaper.","Access to justice remains a serious problem. Legal advice is expensive, and many people who face eviction, unpaid wages or family disputes cannot afford it. Governments, charities and universities have responded with free advice centres, student clinics in which supervised students help real clients, and online guides written in plain language. Some courts have also introduced video hearings, which save travel time, especially for people living in remote regions.","Looking ahead, the profession faces difficult questions about ethics and trust. If software can predict how a case is likely to end, who is responsible when its advice is wrong? Regulators are updating codes of conduct to require lawyers to understand the tools they use and to protect client confidentiality. Many observers believe that the core of the work will survive, since judgement, empathy and the ability to persuade cannot easily be automated. What is changing is the way those skills are combined with technology. Lawyers who can explain complex rules clearly to ordinary people are likely to remain especially valuable."],"gloss":[{"w":"apprenticeship","ar":"تدريب مهني"},{"w":"under-represented","ar":"ناقص التمثيل"},{"w":"clause","ar":"بند (في عقد)"},{"w":"arbitration","ar":"تحكيم"},{"w":"mediation","ar":"وساطة"},{"w":"eviction","ar":"إخلاء من السكن"},{"w":"confidentiality","ar":"السرية"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["Wider access to legal careers","The end of university courses","Why judges are always men","The cost of legal fees today"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Lawyers who refuse to help the poor","Efforts to make legal help available to everyone","The history of video games in court","Why evictions have stopped"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"Legal research that used to take days can now often be done in minutes.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: قواعد البيانات الرقمية تختصر أياماً من البحث إلى دقائق."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Women are now equally represented among senior partners and judges everywhere.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: لا تزال النساء أقل تمثيلاً في المناصب العليا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most lawyers say they prefer working for large international firms.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر تفضيلات المحامين في العمل."},{"k":"gap","q":"In student ___, supervised students help real clients.","a":["clinics"],"why":"الفقرة 4: عيادات الطلاب يساعد فيها الطلاب المشرَّف عليهم عملاء حقيقيين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Some lawyers specialise in data protection or international ___.","a":["arbitration"],"why":"الفقرة 3: من التخصصات حماية البيانات والتحكيم الدولي."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why was law once mainly open to wealthy men?","o":["Universities refused to teach others","Training involved long unpaid periods and high fees","There were very few courts","Women were not interested"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: التدريب كان طويلاً بلا أجر وبرسوم مرتفعة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is an advantage of mediation mentioned in the text?","o":["It is usually quicker and less expensive","It always gives a better result","It replaces all lawyers","It is only used abroad"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: الوساطة أسرع وأرخص عادة من المحكمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer thinks the core of legal work will","o":["disappear within a few years","remain because of human skills","be done only by software","become less important"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: الحكم والتعاطف والإقناع لا يمكن أتمتتها بسهولة."}],"sumAr":"يتتبع النص تغيّر مهنة المحاماة من حيث من يمارسها، وأثر التقنية والعولمة والتخصص، ومساعي توسيع الوصول إلى العدالة. ويخلص إلى أن المهارات الإنسانية ستبقى جوهر المهنة مع إدخال التقنية بأخلاقيات واضحة."},{"id":"r-b2-09","l":"B2","title":"Plastic in the Oceans","topic":"environment","paras":["Every year millions of tonnes of plastic enter the sea. Bottles, bags, fishing nets and food packaging are carried there by rivers and wind, or are dropped directly from ships and beaches. Because plastic is light, durable and cheap, it became one of the most widely used materials of the last century. These same qualities now make it a lasting problem, since most types take hundreds of years to break down completely.","Plastic in the ocean does not stay in one place. Currents gather floating waste into vast areas known as garbage patches, where the density of debris is higher than elsewhere. Contrary to popular images, these are not solid islands that one could walk on; they are more like a thin soup of fragments, many of them too small to see from a ship. A large share of waste, however, sinks to the seabed or washes ashore, even on remote islands far from any city.","Sunlight and waves slowly break plastic into pieces smaller than five millimetres, called microplastics. Tiny fibres are also released every time synthetic clothes are washed, and small beads were once added to cosmetics. Microplastics have been found in the deepest ocean trenches, in sea ice and in the digestive systems of fish, shellfish and sea birds. Scientists are still investigating how they affect human health, and so far the evidence is incomplete.","The harm to wildlife is easier to observe. Turtles mistake floating bags for jellyfish, and seabirds feed pieces of plastic to their chicks. Animals can also be trapped in abandoned fishing nets, a problem known as ghost fishing, because the nets continue to catch animals long after their owners have lost them. Such injuries are a cause of death for many marine animals, and they also damage coastal economies that depend on fishing and tourism.","Cleaning the ocean is difficult. Floating barriers and ships equipped with nets can remove some waste, but they cover only a small area and cannot collect microplastics. Many experts therefore argue that the most effective action is to stop plastic from entering the water in the first place. Rivers are a priority: placing barriers or collection systems in rivers near large cities can capture rubbish before it reaches the sea.","Prevention depends on policy and habits. Several countries have banned thin plastic bags or charged shoppers a small fee, and consumption fell quickly. Others require manufacturers to pay for collecting and recycling the packaging they sell. Better waste collection in fast-growing cities, where rubbish services have not kept pace with population, would make an even greater difference. Individuals can contribute by choosing reusable bottles and bags, but specialists stress that personal action is not enough without well-designed systems."],"gloss":[{"w":"durable","ar":"متين / يدوم طويلاً"},{"w":"debris","ar":"حطام / مخلفات"},{"w":"microplastics","ar":"لدائن دقيقة"},{"w":"trench","ar":"أخدود (في قاع البحر)"},{"w":"abandoned","ar":"مهجور"},{"w":"barrier","ar":"حاجز"},{"w":"consumption","ar":"استهلاك"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Tiny fragments found almost everywhere in the sea","The cost of cosmetic products","How clothes are made","The disappearance of ocean trenches"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Why ocean cleaning is easy","The problems of removing waste once it is in the sea","How ships are built","Why rivers are dirty"],"a":1},{"k":"tfng","q":"Garbage patches are better described as thin mixtures of fragments than as solid islands.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: هي أشبه بحساء رقيق من القطع الصغيرة لا جزر صلبة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Microplastics have been found only in surface waters.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: وُجدت في أعمق الأخاديد وفي جليد البحر وأجسام الكائنات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most of the plastic in the oceans comes from one single country.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يحدد مصدر النفايات بدولة واحدة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Nets that keep catching animals after being lost cause what is called ghost ___.","a":["fishing"],"why":"الفقرة 3: المشكلة تسمى الصيد الشبحي (ghost fishing)."},{"k":"gap","q":"Microplastics are pieces smaller than five ___.","a":["millimetres"],"why":"الفقرة 2: اللدائن الدقيقة أصغر من خمسة مليمترات."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is plastic a lasting problem?","o":["It is very expensive","It takes hundreds of years to break down","It is only made in a few countries","It floats only in rivers"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 0: معظم أنواع البلاستيك تحتاج مئات السنين لتتحلل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Many experts think the best action is to","o":["collect rubbish from the open sea","stop plastic from reaching the water","ban all fishing","add beads to cosmetics"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: الأفضل منع دخول البلاستيك إلى الماء أصلاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What happened in countries that charged a fee for plastic bags?","o":["Consumption decreased quickly","Shops stopped selling bags","Prices of food rose","Rubbish services improved"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: انخفض الاستهلاك بسرعة بعد فرض رسم بسيط."}],"sumAr":"يوضح النص كيف يدخل البلاستيك إلى البحار ويتفتت إلى لدائن دقيقة تضر بالكائنات الحية. ويبيّن صعوبة التنظيف بعد وقوع التلوث، ويؤكد أن الوقاية بالسياسات والعادات الأفضل هي الحل الأنجع."},{"id":"r-b2-10","l":"B2","title":"The Rise of Online Education","topic":"education","paras":["Twenty years ago, studying at a university meant moving to a campus, attending lectures in person and borrowing books from a library. Today a learner in a small town can follow a course from a leading university, watch lectures on a phone and submit assignments from a kitchen table. Online education began as a small experiment, but it has grown into one of the most significant changes in the way people learn.","The early stages were modest. Universities started by placing lecture notes and recorded talks on websites, and distance-learning institutions had sent course materials by post long before the internet existed. The turning point came when fast broadband became widely available and platforms appeared that offered free courses to anyone. Within a few years, millions of people had enrolled, attracted by the low cost and the freedom to choose their subject.","The advantages are clear. Online courses allow people with jobs or family duties to study at their own pace, pausing a video or repeating a section as often as necessary. They also remove geographical barriers: a student in a remote village can learn from teachers in another country. Employers increasingly accept short online certificates as evidence of skills, and many workers use them to change careers without returning to full-time study.","However, completion rates are a persistent problem. In many free courses, only a small minority of those who register actually finish. Without a fixed timetable, classmates and a teacher who notices absence, motivation is easy to lose. Researchers also point out that early users were often already well educated, so online learning did not automatically help the groups that most needed it. Poor internet connections and the lack of a quiet place to study remain serious obstacles.","Educators have therefore experimented with ways to make online study more engaging. Short videos of about six minutes hold attention better than an hour-long lecture. Quizzes during the video, discussion forums and group projects create a sense of community. Some institutions use a blended model, in which students learn the content online and meet teachers in person for discussion, laboratory work and practice. Evidence suggests that this combination often produces results at least as good as traditional teaching. It also reduces cost, because fewer classrooms are needed, and it allows teachers to spend face-to-face time on the tasks where their presence matters most.","Looking forward, artificial intelligence may personalise learning further, because software can notice which topics a student finds difficult and offer extra exercises. Yet many teachers warn that technology cannot replace human encouragement, and that universities will remain valuable as places for debate and friendship. Most probably, online and traditional education will continue to merge, giving learners more choice about where, when and how they study."],"gloss":[{"w":"campus","ar":"الحرم الجامعي"},{"w":"enrol","ar":"يسجّل (في دورة)"},{"w":"certificate","ar":"شهادة"},{"w":"persistent","ar":"مستمر / مزمن"},{"w":"engaging","ar":"جذّاب / مشوّق"},{"w":"blended","ar":"مدمج / هجين"},{"w":"personalise","ar":"يخصّص حسب الفرد"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Why many learners do not finish their courses","The best internet connections","How teachers choose subjects","Why employers dislike certificates"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Ways of making online study more interesting","The decline of group projects","Why short videos are expensive","How laboratories were closed"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Distance learning existed before the internet.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: كانت مؤسسات التعليم عن بعد ترسل المواد بالبريد قبل الإنترنت."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The first users of online courses were mostly people with little education.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: كان المستخدمون الأوائل في الغالب متعلمين جيداً أصلاً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most online students prefer to study in the evening.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر وقت الدراسة المفضل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Employers increasingly accept short online ___ as evidence of skills.","a":["certificates"],"why":"الفقرة 2: يقبل أصحاب العمل الشهادات القصيرة دليلاً على المهارات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Videos of about ___ minutes hold attention better than long lectures.","a":["six"],"why":"الفقرة 4: المقاطع القصيرة نحو ست دقائق تشدّ الانتباه أكثر."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was the turning point for online education?","o":["The invention of the postal service","Widespread fast broadband and free course platforms","The closing of libraries","The end of university fees"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: انتشار النطاق العريض ومنصات الدورات المجانية."},{"k":"mc","q":"Motivation is easy to lose in online courses because","o":["the videos are too short","there is no fixed timetable or teacher to notice absence","the subjects are too easy","there are no certificates"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: غياب الجدول والزملاء ومعلم يلاحظ الغياب."},{"k":"mc","q":"A blended model combines","o":["free and paid courses","online learning with meetings in person","several universities","lectures and holidays"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: محتوى عبر الإنترنت مع لقاءات شخصية مع المعلمين."}],"sumAr":"يتناول النص نشأة التعليم الإلكتروني ومزاياه كالمرونة وإزالة الحواجز الجغرافية، ومشكلة ضعف الإكمال وعدم المساواة. ويعرض أساليب تحسينه ومنها النموذج المدمج، ويتوقع تقارب التعليم الإلكتروني والتقليدي."},{"id":"r-b2-11","l":"B2","title":"What Sleep Research Has Discovered","topic":"health","paras":["Until the middle of the twentieth century, many people believed that sleep was simply a passive state in which the brain switched off. Research using devices that record electrical activity has shown the opposite. During sleep the brain is highly active, moving through repeated cycles that last about ninety minutes, and each cycle contains different stages that serve different functions.","Scientists generally distinguish between light sleep, deep sleep and rapid eye movement, or REM, sleep. In light sleep, the heartbeat and breathing slow down and the body relaxes. Deep sleep, which is more common in the first half of the night, is when the body repairs tissue and releases hormones that support growth. REM sleep, when most vivid dreams occur, becomes longer towards morning. The eyes move quickly beneath closed lids while the muscles are largely paralysed, which prevents us from acting out our dreams.","One of the most important discoveries concerns memory. Studies show that people who sleep after learning new material remember it better than those who stay awake for the same period. During deep sleep the brain appears to transfer fresh information from temporary storage to long-term storage, while REM sleep may help to connect new facts with existing knowledge, which can lead to creative solutions. This is why an all-night study session before an examination is often counterproductive.","Researchers have also identified two systems that control when we sleep. The first is the body clock, which follows a rhythm of roughly twenty-four hours and is strongly influenced by light. The second is sleep pressure, a chemical build-up that grows the longer we stay awake. When both systems agree, we fall asleep easily. Bright screens in the evening can confuse the clock by suggesting that it is still daytime, which is why many specialists advise reducing screen use an hour before bed.","Lack of sleep carries real costs. After a night of short sleep, people react more slowly, find it harder to concentrate and are more likely to make errors; very tired drivers are involved in a large number of road accidents. Over the longer term, chronic sleep shortage has been linked with a higher risk of heart disease, weight gain and low mood, although researchers caution that these connections are complex and not fully understood.","Needs vary, but most adults function best with seven to nine hours. The body clocks of teenagers shift later, so early school start times can leave them exhausted, and some schools that moved to a later start have reported better attendance. Practical advice is simple: keep regular hours, avoid caffeine late in the day, keep the bedroom dark and quiet, and do not use the bed for work. Sleep is not wasted time but an active process on which both body and mind depend."],"gloss":[{"w":"passive","ar":"سلبي / خامل"},{"w":"hormone","ar":"هرمون"},{"w":"paralysed","ar":"مشلول"},{"w":"counterproductive","ar":"يأتي بنتيجة عكسية"},{"w":"chronic","ar":"مزمن"},{"w":"caffeine","ar":"كافيين"},{"w":"tissue","ar":"نسيج"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["The link between sleep and remembering","How to prepare for exams quickly","The dangers of dreams","Why scientists stopped studying memory"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Two natural systems that control sleeping","The invention of the alarm clock","Why light is harmful at all times","How to build a bedroom"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"REM sleep tends to last longer as the night goes on.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: تطول مرحلة REM نحو الصباح."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Deep sleep is more common in the second half of the night.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: النوم العميق أكثر في النصف الأول من الليل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"People who sleep for more than nine hours live longer.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يربط بين النوم الطويل وطول العمر."},{"k":"gap","q":"During REM sleep the muscles are largely paralysed, so we cannot act out our ___.","a":["dreams"],"why":"الفقرة 1: شلل العضلات يمنعنا من تمثيل أحلامنا."},{"k":"gap","q":"A chemical build-up that grows while we stay awake is called sleep ___.","a":["pressure"],"why":"الفقرة 3: ضغط النوم تراكم كيميائي يزداد مع طول اليقظة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is an all-night study session often a poor idea?","o":["Sleep helps to store what has been learned","The brain stops working at night","Studying at night is forbidden","Sleep makes exams easier"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: النوم ينقل المعلومات إلى الذاكرة طويلة المدى."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are bright screens in the evening a problem?","o":["They make the room too warm","They confuse the body clock","They cause chemical pressure","They stop dreams"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: الشاشات توحي للساعة البيولوجية بأن النهار ما زال قائماً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What has happened in some schools that started lessons later?","o":["Attendance improved","Teenagers slept less","Exams were cancelled","Teachers were tired"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: أفادت بعض المدارس بتحسن الحضور."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص ما كشفته أبحاث النوم: أنه نشاط دماغي مرحلي يعزز الذاكرة، وتتحكم فيه الساعة البيولوجية وضغط النوم. ويبيّن أضرار قلة النوم ويقدم نصائح عملية للحصول على نوم كافٍ."},{"id":"r-b2-12","l":"B2","title":"How Recommendation Algorithms Work","topic":"technology","paras":["When a video platform suggests what to watch next, or an online shop displays a line saying that other customers also bought a certain item, a recommendation algorithm is at work. These systems sort enormous catalogues, which may contain millions of items, so that each user sees only a handful that are likely to be of interest. Without them, finding a film or a book would be like searching for one particular grain of sand on a beach.","The simplest method is content-based filtering. The system describes each item by its features, such as the genre, director and length of a film, and then looks for items similar to those the user has already enjoyed. If you have watched several documentaries about wildlife, the algorithm will offer more of the same. This approach works even for new users with a short history, but it tends to repeat the same type of content and rarely surprises anyone.","A second method, collaborative filtering, ignores the features of the item and focuses on people. It finds users whose past behaviour resembles yours and suggests what they liked and you have not yet seen. The logic is that taste can be predicted from the habits of similar people, even if you cannot explain why you like something. This method is powerful when a platform has many users, but it struggles with new items that nobody has rated, which is known as the cold-start problem.","Modern systems usually combine several methods and use machine learning to improve them. They learn from many signals: what you click, how long you watch, whether you finish a video and what you skip. Importantly, they often learn more from behaviour than from what users say they like, because people's actions reveal their real interests. The system is also tested constantly: different users are shown different versions, and the one that keeps people more satisfied is adopted.","This design raises concerns. If a platform is optimised for attention, it may promote sensational content that is more likely to be watched, rather than content that is accurate or useful. Critics also describe filter bubbles, in which users see mainly material that matches their existing views and therefore meet fewer different opinions. Researchers disagree about how strong this effect is, and some studies suggest that people's own choices matter more than the algorithm.","Regulators and companies are therefore exploring remedies. Some platforms let users reset their history or switch off personalised suggestions, and some laws require companies to explain in plain language why an item was recommended. Independent researchers ask for access to data so that they can audit the systems. For ordinary users, the best protection is awareness: recommendations are not neutral advice but predictions designed to serve the goals of the platform."],"gloss":[{"w":"catalogue","ar":"فهرس / كتالوج"},{"w":"filtering","ar":"تصفية"},{"w":"resemble","ar":"يشبه"},{"w":"optimise","ar":"يحسّن إلى أقصى حد"},{"w":"sensational","ar":"مثير (للإثارة)"},{"w":"remedy","ar":"علاج / حل"},{"w":"audit","ar":"يدقّق / يراجع"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Predicting taste from similar people","How films are rated by critics","The features of a new product","Why users dislike sharing data"],"a":0},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Worries about attention and narrow viewpoints","The best way to watch documentaries","How catalogues are built","Why algorithms never make errors"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Content-based filtering can give suggestions to a user who has little viewing history.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: تعمل هذه الطريقة حتى مع مستخدمين جدد بسجل قصير."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Collaborative filtering works best when a platform has very few users.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: الطريقة قوية عندما يكون للمنصة مستخدمون كثيرون."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most platforms test new versions of their systems on their own employees first.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكر الاختبار على مستخدمين مختلفين فقط، لا على الموظفين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Collaborative filtering struggles with new items that nobody has ___.","a":["rated"],"why":"الفقرة 2: مشكلة البداية الباردة مع عناصر جديدة لم يقيّمها أحد."},{"k":"gap","q":"Users who mainly see material matching their views are said to be in a filter ___.","a":["bubble"],"why":"الفقرة 4: النقاد يسمون ذلك فقاعة الترشيح (filter bubble)."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do systems often learn more from behaviour than from stated preferences?","o":["Users never answer questions","Actions show people's real interests","Behaviour is easier to delete","Stated preferences are illegal to use"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: أفعال الناس تكشف اهتماماتهم الحقيقية."},{"k":"mc","q":"A concern about attention-based platforms is that they may","o":["promote sensational rather than useful content","stop users from watching videos","charge higher prices","remove all documentaries"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: قد تروّج لمحتوى مثير بدل المحتوى الدقيق المفيد."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer suggests that the best protection for users is to","o":["avoid every platform","understand that recommendations are predictions with a purpose","delete their accounts monthly","trust the algorithm completely"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: التوصيات تنبؤات تخدم أهداف المنصة وليست نصيحة محايدة."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص كيف تعمل أنظمة التوصية بالتصفية حسب المحتوى والتصفية التعاونية والتعلم الآلي. ويناقش مخاوف مثل فقاعات الترشيح والمحتوى المثير، ويختم بأن الوعي هو أفضل حماية للمستخدم."},{"id":"r-b2-13","l":"B2","title":"Whistleblowers and the Public Interest","topic":"law","paras":["A whistleblower is a person who reveals wrongdoing inside an organisation, usually a company or a government department, to people who can stop it. The wrongdoing may be fraud, dangerous working conditions, or the hiding of safety risks. Because whistleblowers often know about problems before anyone outside does, they can prevent harm that would otherwise stay hidden for years.","Yet speaking out carries heavy costs. Employees who report concerns frequently face dismissal, threats, or exclusion by colleagues. Some find that future employers are reluctant to hire them, since they are seen as disloyal. A number of studies of workplaces suggest that fear of such treatment is the most common reason why staff stay silent about misconduct they have witnessed.","For this reason, many countries have passed laws to protect people who report. These laws usually contain three main elements. First, they allow a person to report anonymously or in confidence. Second, they forbid an employer from punishing someone who has reported in good faith. Third, they offer a route to compensation if punishment occurs anyway. However, protection on paper does not always translate into protection in practice, as workers may not know that the law exists or may doubt that officials will enforce it.","Reports can travel along different channels. Internal channels, such as a manager or an ethics hotline, are generally encouraged first because they let an organisation correct its own mistakes quickly. External channels include regulators, the police and elected representatives. A third option is public disclosure through the media, which most laws allow only in limited circumstances, for example when there is an immediate danger to life or when earlier reports have been ignored. Critics of this last route argue that unverified information can damage innocent people's reputations, while supporters reply that publicity is sometimes the only force strong enough to make powerful institutions act.","The question of motive also divides opinion. Some commentators suggest that a whistleblower who acts out of anger or hope of personal gain is less trustworthy. Legal experts, however, tend to say that motive matters far less than accuracy. If the information is true and concerns a serious risk, the public benefits regardless of the reporter's feelings. Courts therefore usually ask whether the person reasonably believed the information to be true, not whether he or she was a saint.","Technology has changed the practice in recent years. Secure online platforms allow people to send documents without revealing their names, and some organisations now run such systems on behalf of the public. At the same time, digital records make it easier for employers to trace the source of a leak, so careful reporters must think hard about how they send material. Whatever the method, the central idea remains the same: societies are safer and fairer when those who see wrongdoing can speak about it without fear. A culture that rewards honesty, rather than silence, is likely to detect problems sooner and at lower cost."],"gloss":[{"w":"whistleblower","ar":"مُبلِّغ عن المخالفات"},{"w":"misconduct","ar":"سوء سلوك مهني"},{"w":"dismissal","ar":"فصل من العمل"},{"w":"anonymously","ar":"دون الكشف عن الاسم"},{"w":"compensation","ar":"تعويض"},{"w":"disclosure","ar":"إفصاح، كشف"},{"w":"motive","ar":"دافع"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["The personal risks of speaking out","Why companies hire honest staff","How courts punish dishonest employers","The history of workplace laws"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Whistleblowers may be aware of a problem before people outside the organisation are.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: يعرفون المشكلات قبل غيرهم من الخارج."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most laws require a person who reports wrongdoing to give his or her name.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: القوانين تسمح بالتبليغ دون كشف الهوية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Whistleblowers who go to the media are more successful than those who use internal channels.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 4: لا يقارن النص بين نجاح القناتين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Legal protection can fail if workers doubt that the law will be applied.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: قد لا يثق العمال بتطبيق القانون."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are internal channels usually suggested first?","o":["They are the only legal route.","They allow an organisation to fix its errors fast.","They protect the reporter's identity completely.","They avoid involving managers."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: تتيح للمؤسسة تصحيح أخطائها بسرعة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do courts mainly consider when judging a whistleblower's motive?","o":["Whether the reporter was angry","Whether the reporter gained money","Whether the reporter reasonably thought the information true","Whether the reporter used the media"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: المحاكم تسأل هل اعتقد المبلّغ بشكل معقول صحة المعلومات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Most laws allow public disclosure through the media only in ___ circumstances.","a":["limited"],"why":"الفقرة 4: في ظروف محدودة فقط."},{"k":"gap","q":"Digital records make it easier for employers to trace the ___ of a leak.","a":["source"],"why":"الفقرة 6: تتبع مصدر التسريب."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main idea of the final paragraph?","o":["Technology has made reporting impossible.","Honest reporting is helped by safe systems, but risks remain.","Employers should read all digital records.","Anonymous platforms are illegal in most countries."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: التقنية تساعد، لكن تتبع المصدر ما زال ممكناً."}],"sumAr":"النص يشرح دور المبلّغين عن المخالفات في حماية المصلحة العامة وما يواجهونه من مخاطر. ويعرض قوانين الحماية وقنوات التبليغ وأثر التقنية الحديثة."},{"id":"r-b2-14","l":"B2","title":"The Economic Cost of Corruption","topic":"anticorruption","paras":["Corruption is often described as a moral failure, but it is also an economic one. When public officials accept bribes or steal public money, the effects spread far beyond the individuals involved. Economists have spent decades trying to measure those effects, and although exact figures are disputed, most agree that corruption slows growth and makes societies poorer. The difficulty is that bribes are secret by nature, so researchers must rely on surveys and indirect evidence rather than on complete records.","The most direct cost is the loss of public money. When a contract for a road or a hospital is awarded to the company that pays the largest bribe rather than the one that offers the best price, the government pays more for lower quality. Some of the extra cost is simply pocketed, and some is spent on poor materials, so the building may need repairs within a few years. Taxpayers therefore pay twice: once for the original project and again for its replacement.","Corruption also discourages investment. Businesses, especially foreign ones, prefer countries where rules are predictable. If a firm must pay unofficial fees to obtain a licence, connect to electricity, or clear goods through customs, these payments act like an extra tax, and one that is impossible to plan for. Faced with such uncertainty, many companies decide to invest elsewhere, which means fewer jobs and less technology transfer for the country concerned.","A third problem concerns the way resources are distributed. Where officials can profit from certain projects, they tend to favour those that are easy to hide, such as large construction schemes, over less visible but more useful ones, such as teacher training or routine maintenance. Public spending on education and health may fall as a result, even when the total budget stays the same. Over time, a workforce that is less healthy and less educated is less productive.","Small businesses and poorer households are often hit hardest. A large company can afford lawyers and can sometimes negotiate with officials, whereas a market trader or a young entrepreneur may be forced to pay or close down. Surveys in several regions have found that the poor spend a larger share of their income on informal payments for basic services than the wealthy do. Corruption therefore widens inequality, which in turn can reduce social trust and political stability.","Fortunately, evidence shows that reform can work. Publishing government contracts online, rotating officials between posts, and allowing citizens to track spending have all been associated with lower levels of bribery in some places. Independent audit bodies that are free from political pressure also help. Yet no single measure is enough on its own. Lasting improvement usually requires a combination of clear laws, honest enforcement and a public that is willing to demand accountability."],"gloss":[{"w":"bribe","ar":"رشوة"},{"w":"pocketed","ar":"اختُلس، وُضع في الجيب"},{"w":"predictable","ar":"يمكن التنبؤ به"},{"w":"customs","ar":"الجمارك"},{"w":"maintenance","ar":"صيانة"},{"w":"inequality","ar":"تفاوت، عدم مساواة"},{"w":"audit","ar":"تدقيق، مراجعة حسابات"},{"w":"accountability","ar":"مساءلة"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["How corruption changes where money is spent","Why large projects are popular with voters","The training of teachers in poor countries","How budgets are approved each year"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most economists believe that corruption harms economic growth.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: معظم الاقتصاديين متفقون على أنها تبطئ النمو."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Poorer families spend a smaller share of their income on informal payments than richer families do.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: الفقراء ينفقون حصة أكبر لا أصغر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Corruption is more common in large countries than in small ones.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص بين الدول الكبيرة والصغيرة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Foreign companies pay larger bribes than local companies.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص مقدار ما تدفعه الشركات الأجنبية مقارنة بالمحلية."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the writer say that taxpayers 'pay twice'?","o":["They pay taxes and also private fees.","Poorly built projects must be repaid or rebuilt soon.","Bribes are added to their bills.","They must fund two governments."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: المواد الرديئة تستدعي إصلاحاً أو استبدالاً مبكراً."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to paragraph 3, why do some companies avoid corrupt countries?","o":["Unofficial payments are like a tax that cannot be planned.","Their workers dislike the climate.","Official taxes there are too high.","Foreign firms are banned from licences."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: الرسوم غير الرسمية كضريبة لا يمكن التخطيط لها."},{"k":"gap","q":"Officials favour large construction schemes because they are easy to ___.","a":["hide"],"why":"الفقرة 4: المشاريع السهلة الإخفاء."},{"k":"gap","q":"Corruption widens ___, which can reduce social trust.","a":["inequality"],"why":"الفقرة 5: يوسّع التفاوت بين الناس."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which reform is mentioned in the final paragraph?","o":["Raising officials' salaries","Banning foreign investment","Letting citizens follow public spending","Closing small businesses"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 6: السماح للمواطنين بتتبع الإنفاق."}],"sumAr":"يبيّن النص أن الفساد يضر الاقتصاد عبر هدر المال العام وطرد الاستثمار وسوء توزيع الموارد. كما يوضح أن الفقراء والمشاريع الصغيرة هم الأكثر تضرراً وأن الإصلاح ممكن."},{"id":"r-b2-15","l":"B2","title":"Artificial Intelligence in the Courtroom","topic":"ai","paras":["Courts have always depended on human judgement, but artificial intelligence is now entering the courtroom in several ways. Some uses are modest, such as searching thousands of documents in minutes. Others are more controversial, such as software that predicts whether a defendant is likely to commit another offence. Understanding the difference between these uses is essential for anyone who cares about fair trials.","The least controversial applications save time. In large cases, lawyers may have to read millions of emails and contracts. Programs trained to recognise relevant words and patterns can sort these files far faster than a team of junior staff, and at a lower cost. Similar tools help judges find earlier decisions on a legal point, and chatbots can guide members of the public through court procedures. These systems extend access to justice because they reduce expenses that many people cannot afford.","A more sensitive use is risk assessment. In some jurisdictions, officials use algorithms to estimate the chance that a person released on bail will fail to return to court or will reoffend. The software compares the individual with data on thousands of earlier cases. Supporters argue that this approach is more consistent than the instincts of a tired judge at the end of a long day. Opponents point out that the data reflect past decisions, and if those decisions were biased against a certain group, the program may repeat the bias while appearing neutral.","This problem is made worse by a lack of transparency. Many risk tools are produced by private companies that treat their methods as trade secrets. A defendant who is told that a score has influenced his sentence may therefore have no practical way to challenge how it was calculated. Lawyers have argued that this undermines the right to a fair hearing, which depends on the ability to examine the evidence used against you.","Another worry involves errors. Generative AI systems can produce text that sounds confident but contains invented details, including cases that never existed. Lawyers in several countries have been criticised for submitting such material to courts without checking it. Judges have responded by requiring lawyers to confirm that every source has been verified by a human being.","In response, many legal bodies are drafting guidelines. Common principles include human oversight of every important decision, clear explanations of how a tool works, regular testing for bias, and the right to object to the use of automated analysis. Most experts agree that AI should assist judges rather than replace them. The reason is not only technical. Passing sentence is an act of public authority, and many people believe that such authority must remain accountable to a person who can explain and defend the decision."],"gloss":[{"w":"defendant","ar":"متهم"},{"w":"algorithm","ar":"خوارزمية"},{"w":"bail","ar":"كفالة، إفراج مؤقت"},{"w":"reoffend","ar":"يعود إلى ارتكاب الجريمة"},{"w":"jurisdiction","ar":"اختصاص قضائي، ولاية قضائية"},{"w":"bias","ar":"تحيّز"},{"w":"oversight","ar":"إشراف، رقابة"},{"w":"verified","ar":"تم التحقق منه"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Secret methods that are hard to challenge","The low cost of legal software","Why lawyers dislike private companies","How judges choose their staff"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Chatbots can help ordinary people understand how court procedures work.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: روبوتات المحادثة ترشد الجمهور في الإجراءات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Risk assessment software compares a person with information from a single earlier case.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: تقارن بيانات آلاف القضايا لا قضية واحدة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Risk assessment tools are more accurate than human judges.","a":"NG","why":"النص يقول إنها أكثر اتساقاً ولا يذكر الدقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Generative AI is used more by prosecutors than by defence lawyers.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص من يستخدمها أكثر."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do supporters favour risk assessment programs?","o":["They are cheaper than lawyers.","They treat cases more consistently than a tired judge may.","They never use past data.","They are written by courts."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: أكثر اتساقاً من حدس قاضٍ مرهق."},{"k":"mc","q":"How have judges reacted to false material produced by AI?","o":["They banned all computers.","They required lawyers to confirm that humans checked sources.","They stopped accepting written evidence.","They punished the software companies."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: طلبوا تأكيداً أن إنساناً تحقق من كل مصدر."},{"k":"gap","q":"Programs can sort files faster than a team of ___ staff.","a":["junior"],"why":"الفقرة 2: موظفين مبتدئين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Many risk tools are treated as trade ___ by private companies.","a":["secrets"],"why":"الفقرة 4: أسرار تجارية."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do most experts think about AI in courts?","o":["It should replace judges.","It should help judges but not replace them.","It should only be used in civil cases.","It should be tested once only."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: يجب أن تساعد القضاة لا أن تحل محلهم."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص استخدامات الذكاء الاصطناعي في المحاكم من فرز الوثائق إلى تقدير المخاطر. ويناقش مشكلات التحيز وغياب الشفافية والأخطاء، ويؤكد ضرورة بقاء القرار بيد القاضي."},{"id":"r-b2-16","l":"B2","title":"How Birds Find Their Way on Long Journeys","topic":"nature","paras":["Every year, billions of birds travel thousands of kilometres between their breeding and wintering grounds. Some Arctic terns fly from one polar region to the other and back, covering distances that exceed those of any other animal. Even small songbirds that weigh less than a pencil can cross deserts and seas. How they find their way without maps has puzzled scientists for centuries. The answer, it turns out, involves a remarkable combination of inherited instinct, physical senses and learning.","Research suggests that birds do not rely on a single sense but on several, which they combine as conditions change. The most important of these is probably the Earth's magnetic field. Experiments in which captive birds were placed in artificial magnetic fields showed that they shifted the direction in which they tried to fly. Scientists believe that certain light-sensitive molecules in the bird's eye may react to the field, allowing the animal to \"see\" its direction, although the exact mechanism is still debated.","The sun provides a second source of information. Because the sun moves across the sky at a steady rate, a bird with an internal clock can work out compass direction from its position at a given time of day. Birds that migrate at night appear to use the stars instead. Young birds raised in a planetarium where the pattern of stars was rotated chose a different direction from normal when released, which suggests that they learn the night sky's pattern early in life.","Smell and sound may also play roles, especially near the end of a journey. Some seabirds seem able to recognise the odours of familiar coastlines, and pigeons that were deprived of their sense of smell often struggled to return home. Low-frequency sounds from ocean waves or mountains might also act as landmarks over long distances, although evidence for this idea is limited.","Not all knowledge is inherited. Many young birds migrate for the first time on their own and follow an inborn programme: they fly in a particular direction for a particular length of time. Experienced adults, however, build detailed mental maps of landmarks such as rivers, coastlines and mountain ranges. In species that travel in family groups, such as geese and cranes, the young learn the route by following their parents, which means that knowledge can be passed from generation to generation.","Understanding migration has practical value. Tracking devices small enough for light birds now reveal where they rest and feed, and this information helps conservationists to protect the wetlands and forests on which they depend. Artificial light from cities, however, can confuse night migrants, and tall buildings kill many birds each year. Some cities now switch off lights during the busiest weeks of the migration season, a simple measure that has saved large numbers of lives."],"gloss":[{"w":"migrate","ar":"يهاجر (موسمياً)"},{"w":"breeding","ar":"تكاثر"},{"w":"magnetic field","ar":"مجال مغناطيسي"},{"w":"mechanism","ar":"آلية"},{"w":"inborn","ar":"فطري"},{"w":"landmark","ar":"معلم بارز"},{"w":"wetland","ar":"أرض رطبة"},{"w":"conservationist","ar":"ناشط في حماية البيئة"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Using the sun and stars as a compass","Why birds sleep during the day","The danger of tall buildings","How planetariums are built"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Arctic terns travel greater distances than any other animal.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: مسافاتها تتجاوز مسافات أي حيوان آخر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Scientists fully understand how birds sense the magnetic field.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: الآلية الدقيقة ما زالت موضع نقاش."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Birds that migrate in groups fly faster than birds that travel alone.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص سرعة الطيور."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Small songbirds are more likely to die on migration than large birds.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص معدلات الموت بين الأنواع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did experiments with artificial magnetic fields show?","o":["Birds stopped flying completely.","Birds changed the direction they attempted to fly.","Birds became unable to eat.","Birds flew faster."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: غيّرت الطيور اتجاه طيرانها."},{"k":"mc","q":"How do young geese and cranes learn their route?","o":["By reading the stars","By smelling the coast","By following their parents","By using tracking devices"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: بتتبع آبائها."},{"k":"gap","q":"Birds that migrate at night appear to use the ___ instead.","a":["stars"],"why":"الفقرة 3: تستخدم النجوم ليلاً."},{"k":"gap","q":"Some cities switch off ___ during the busiest weeks of the migration season.","a":["lights"],"why":"الفقرة 6: تطفئ الأضواء."},{"k":"mc","q":"What can be said about the evidence for sound as a landmark?","o":["It is limited.","It is complete.","It was proved by pigeons.","It was rejected by all scientists."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: الأدلة على الأصوات محدودة."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص كيف تستعين الطيور المهاجرة بالمجال المغناطيسي والشمس والنجوم والروائح والخرائط الذهنية. كما يبيّن فائدة دراسة الهجرة في حماية الطيور."},{"id":"r-b2-17","l":"B2","title":"The Printing Press and the Spread of Knowledge","topic":"arts","paras":["Before the middle of the fifteenth century, books in Europe were copied by hand, usually by monks or professional scribes. A single volume could take months to complete, and its price put it beyond the reach of most families. Only churches, universities and wealthy individuals owned more than a handful of books. Around 1450, a goldsmith in the German city of Mainz developed a printing press that used movable metal type, and the world of information began to change. Within a single lifetime, the idea would reach almost every corner of the continent.","The idea behind movable type was simple but powerful. Each letter was cast as a separate piece of metal, arranged into lines and pages, inked and pressed onto paper. After printing, the letters could be rearranged and used again. A workshop could now produce hundreds of identical copies in the time a scribe needed for one, and the cost per book fell sharply. For the first time, a student could dream of owning a personal library.","The technology spread with remarkable speed. Within fifty years, printing workshops had opened in hundreds of European towns, and millions of books had been produced. Many early printed works were religious texts, but printers soon found that there was also a market for calendars, grammar books, maps and works of science. Because printers needed to sell their stock, they chose titles that ordinary readers wanted to buy, which gradually shifted publishing away from the interests of the Church.","Printing also made knowledge more reliable. Hand copying inevitably introduced mistakes, and each new copy could add more. With printing, scholars in different cities could refer to exactly the same page and number. Scientists could publish tables of measurements and diagrams that others could check and improve. Many historians consider this shared, accurate record to be a foundation of the modern scientific method.","The social effects were equally significant. As books became cheaper, more people learned to read, and literacy rates rose, first in cities and later in the countryside. Pamphlets allowed new ideas to circulate faster than authorities could control them, and governments in several countries responded by introducing licensing systems and lists of banned books. Such attempts at censorship show how powerful the new medium was perceived to be.","Comparisons with the internet are common today. Both technologies lowered the cost of copying and sharing information, and both caused anxiety about false or harmful material. There are differences, however. Printing took generations to change society, whereas digital media have done so within a few decades. Whether this faster change will bring similar benefits remains an open question, but the history of print suggests that cheap access to information, combined with the skill to read critically, can transform whole societies."],"gloss":[{"w":"scribe","ar":"ناسخ، كاتب مخطوطات"},{"w":"movable type","ar":"حروف طباعة متحركة"},{"w":"cast","ar":"سُبك، صُبّ"},{"w":"identical","ar":"مطابق"},{"w":"literacy","ar":"القدرة على القراءة والكتابة"},{"w":"pamphlet","ar":"كُتيّب، نشرة"},{"w":"censorship","ar":"رقابة، منع النشر"},{"w":"circulate","ar":"يتداول، ينتشر"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Printing made information more accurate and shareable","The religious books of the Church","Why scribes were paid so little","How paper was invented"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Before printing, most families could not afford to buy books.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: سعر الكتاب فوق قدرة معظم الأسر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"After printing, the metal letters could not be used again.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: يمكن إعادة ترتيب الحروف واستخدامها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The first book produced by a printing press was a religious text.","a":"NG","why":"النص يقول إن كثيراً من الكتب الأولى دينية ولا يحدد أولها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Printing workshops reached the Middle East later than they reached Europe.","a":"NG","why":"لا يتناول النص الشرق الأوسط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did printers choose titles that ordinary people wanted?","o":["The Church ordered it.","They needed to sell their stock.","Scholars paid for them.","Paper was cheap."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: كانوا بحاجة إلى بيع ما يطبعونه."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did some governments introduce licensing systems?","o":["To teach people to read","To control the movement of new ideas","To reduce the price of paper","To help printers abroad"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: لضبط انتشار الأفكار الجديدة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Each letter was cast as a separate piece of ___.","a":["metal"],"why":"الفقرة 2: قطعة معدن."},{"k":"gap","q":"Hand copying inevitably introduced ___.","a":["mistakes"],"why":"الفقرة 4: أخطاء."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer suggest in the last paragraph?","o":["The internet has changed society more slowly than print.","Cheap information and critical reading can transform societies.","Digital media are less harmful than books.","Print is no longer important."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: المعلومات الرخيصة مع القراءة الناقدة تغيّر المجتمعات."}],"sumAr":"يوضح النص كيف جعلت المطبعة الكتب رخيصة وكثيرة ودقيقة، فانتشرت القراءة والعلم. ويقارن بين أثر الطباعة وأثر الإنترنت في المجتمعات."},{"id":"r-b2-18","l":"B2","title":"Fighting Desertification","topic":"environment","paras":["Desertification is the process by which fertile land becomes dry and unproductive. It does not mean that existing deserts are spreading like a tide; rather, it describes the degradation of drylands through a combination of climate change and human activity. Millions of people in Africa, Asia and other regions depend on land that is at risk, which makes the problem a matter of food, health and security. Because the damage builds up slowly over many years, it is easy to ignore until the land has already lost most of its value.","Several causes are usually involved. Overgrazing removes the plants that bind the soil, and cutting trees for firewood exposes the ground to wind. Farming methods that use the same field year after year without rest exhaust its nutrients, while poorly managed irrigation can leave salt on the surface, making the soil toxic to crops. When prolonged droughts arrive, already weakened land cannot recover.","The consequences extend beyond farmers. Dust storms damage health and reduce visibility, and lost harvests push up food prices. When families can no longer earn a living from the land, many migrate to cities, which can place pressure on housing and services. In some regions, competition for scarce pastures and water has also increased tension between communities. Governments in affected countries must then spend scarce money on emergency food and housing instead of long-term development.","One of the best-known responses is the \"green barrier\", a belt of trees planted along the edge of a desert to slow the wind and protect fields behind it. Several countries have attempted such projects. Results have been mixed: where local people were involved in choosing and caring for the trees, many survived, but where seedlings were planted without such cooperation, large numbers died from lack of water.","Smaller-scale techniques have often proved more effective. Farmers in parts of West Africa dig shallow pits or build low stone lines along the slope of a field to catch rainwater and let it soak into the ground. Others protect young trees that sprout naturally from old roots in their fields, rather than clearing them. These trees add nutrients, offer shade and reduce wind damage, and the cost is little more than patience. Because the methods are cheap and use local knowledge, neighbours copy them readily.","Experts increasingly stress prevention over repair. Rotating crops, limiting the number of animals on a pasture, and using drip irrigation, which delivers water directly to roots, all reduce the pressure on the land. Satellite images now allow governments to monitor vegetation cover and detect early warning signs. Restoring degraded land is slow and expensive; keeping healthy land healthy is almost always cheaper. International agreements encourage richer countries to fund such work, since the effects of lost farmland, including migration and higher food prices, rarely stay within national borders. Success, in the end, depends on giving farmers both knowledge and a secure right to use their land, so that they have a reason to protect it for the future."],"gloss":[{"w":"desertification","ar":"التصحّر"},{"w":"degradation","ar":"تدهور"},{"w":"overgrazing","ar":"الرعي الجائر"},{"w":"irrigation","ar":"ريّ"},{"w":"toxic","ar":"سام"},{"w":"seedling","ar":"شتلة"},{"w":"nutrient","ar":"مادة مغذية"},{"w":"pasture","ar":"مرعى"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Cheap local methods that protect the soil","Why trees are dangerous in deserts","The history of West African farming","How satellites replaced farmers"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Desertification is caused by a mixture of climate change and human activity.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: مزيج من تغيّر المناخ والنشاط البشري."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Desertification means that existing deserts move forward like a tide.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: النص ينفي هذا المعنى صراحة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Dust storms are more frequent today than they were fifty years ago.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقدم النص مقارنة زمنية لتكرار العواصف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Drip irrigation is more expensive than flood irrigation.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص تكلفة الريّ بالتنقيط بغيره."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why were the results of green barrier projects mixed?","o":["Trees survived only where local people helped care for them.","The trees grew too fast.","Winds were too weak.","Farmers refused to plant anything."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: نجحت حيث شارك السكان المحليون."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do neighbours readily copy the small-scale methods?","o":["They are cheap and rely on local knowledge.","They are paid by governments.","They need no water at all.","They were invented abroad."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: رخيصة وتعتمد على المعرفة المحلية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Overgrazing removes the plants that ___ the soil.","a":["bind"],"why":"الفقرة 2: النباتات التي تربط التربة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Poorly managed ___ can leave salt on the surface.","a":["irrigation"],"why":"الفقرة 2: الري السيئ يترك الملح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do experts now emphasise?","o":["Restoring land after damage","Preventing damage in the first place","Moving people to cities","Planting only trees"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: الوقاية أهم من الإصلاح."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص أسباب التصحر ونتائجه على الناس والغذاء. ويعرض الحلول كالأحزمة الخضراء والتقنيات المحلية البسيطة، ويؤكد أن الوقاية أرخص من الإصلاح."},{"id":"r-b2-19","l":"B2","title":"The Placebo Effect","topic":"health","paras":["A placebo is a treatment that contains no active medicine, such as a sugar pill or an injection of salt water. Yet patients who receive one often report real improvement. This \"placebo effect\" has fascinated doctors for centuries, and it is now studied seriously as a window into how expectation and the brain influence health. Understanding it matters because it affects every medical treatment, whether or not the treatment contains an active ingredient.","The placebo effect is most obvious in conditions where symptoms depend partly on perception, such as pain, nausea, fatigue and low mood. Brain scans show that when people expect pain relief, the brain releases natural chemicals that reduce pain signals. In other words, the improvement is not imaginary; it has a measurable biological basis. Some patients even report that their symptoms eased within minutes of taking a pill that contained nothing but sugar. However, there is little evidence that placebos can shrink tumours or cure infections.","The way in which a placebo is given matters considerably. Studies have found that large pills work better than small ones, that capsules are seen as stronger than tablets, and that injections often produce a stronger response than pills. Even colour and brand names can influence results. A doctor who is warm, listens carefully and expresses confidence also seems to increase the effect. These findings suggest that the ritual of care is itself part of the treatment.","Placebos are essential in medical research. To test a new drug, researchers usually divide volunteers into two groups; one receives the drug and the other a placebo. Neither the patients nor, ideally, the doctors know who has received which, a design called a double-blind trial. If the drug group improves significantly more than the placebo group, the drug is judged to have a real effect. Without such comparisons, doctors might credit a drug for improvements that would have happened anyway. Many conditions improve naturally with time, which is another reason why the control group is so valuable.","The opposite phenomenon also exists. In the \"nocebo\" effect, patients who expect harm experience unpleasant symptoms. Volunteers in trials who are warned about possible side effects sometimes report them even when taking a harmless pill. This raises an ethical question, since doctors are obliged to inform patients of risks, yet that very information may cause some of the discomfort.","Another ethical issue is honesty. Traditionally, giving a placebo meant deceiving the patient. Yet some recent small studies have suggested that patients may still feel better when they are told openly that a pill contains no medicine, provided that the doctor explains the possible benefit. If larger studies confirm this, placebos might become a legitimate supplementary tool for conditions such as chronic pain. For now, most doctors say placebos should never replace proven treatments, but understanding them can help to make real treatments work better."],"gloss":[{"w":"placebo","ar":"دواء وهمي"},{"w":"nausea","ar":"غثيان"},{"w":"perception","ar":"إدراك"},{"w":"tumour","ar":"ورم"},{"w":"double-blind","ar":"مزدوج التعمية"},{"w":"side effect","ar":"أثر جانبي"},{"w":"deceive","ar":"يخدع"},{"w":"chronic","ar":"مزمن"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["How the delivery of a placebo changes its power","The dangers of large pills","Why doctors dislike capsules","The cost of sugar pills"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"The brain releases natural chemicals that reduce pain when a person expects relief.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: الدماغ يفرز مواد طبيعية تخفف الألم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In a double-blind trial, the doctors always know which patients received the drug.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: لا المرضى ولا الأطباء يعرفون عادةً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The placebo effect is stronger in women than in men.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص أي فرق بين الجنسين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Placebos work better on children than on adults.","a":"NG","why":"لا يتناول النص أعمار المرضى."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do studies suggest about how placebos are given?","o":["Injections may work better than pills.","Tablets work better than capsules.","Small pills work best.","Colour has no influence."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: الحقن غالباً أقوى استجابة من الأقراص."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are placebo groups used in drug trials?","o":["To save money","To show whether the drug has a real effect","To make doctors feel confident","To avoid side effects"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: للمقارنة وإثبات التأثير الحقيقي للدواء."},{"k":"gap","q":"There is little evidence that placebos can shrink ___ or cure infections.","a":["tumours"],"why":"الفقرة 2: لا دليل على تقليص الأورام."},{"k":"gap","q":"In the ___ effect, patients who expect harm experience unpleasant symptoms.","a":["nocebo"],"why":"الفقرة 5: تأثير النوسيبو."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did some recent small studies suggest?","o":["Patients may improve even when told the pill is a placebo.","Placebos cure chronic pain completely.","Doctors should never explain treatments.","Honest explanations make placebos useless."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 6: قد يتحسن المرضى حتى لو عرفوا أنه دواء وهمي."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص أن التأثير الوهمي له أساس بيولوجي حقيقي ويتأثر بطريقة تقديم العلاج. ويبيّن أهميته في التجارب الطبية والمسائل الأخلاقية المرتبطة به."},{"id":"r-b2-20","l":"B2","title":"Smart Cities","topic":"technology","paras":["A smart city uses digital technology to collect information about how it operates and uses that information to improve services. Sensors in roads, lamp posts and buildings send data to central systems, where software looks for patterns. The aim is to make cities cleaner, safer and cheaper to run at a time when more than half of the world's population already lives in urban areas.","Transport is the most common starting point. Traffic lights linked to sensors can adjust their timing to the number of vehicles waiting, which reduces queues and exhaust fumes. Screens at bus stops show exact arrival times based on the GPS position of each vehicle, and parking apps guide drivers directly to empty spaces rather than leaving them to circle the block. Some cities also use data to plan new bus routes where demand is greatest. In the best cases, these changes save residents many hours each year.","Resource management is another area. Sensors on water pipes can detect the small drop in pressure caused by a leak long before water appears on the surface, allowing repairs to be made before large amounts are wasted. Streetlights that dim when no one is nearby save electricity, and bins fitted with sensors tell collection crews when they are full, so lorries visit only those that need emptying.","Smart technology also supports public safety and emergency response. Flood sensors in rivers can warn residents hours before water reaches homes, and traffic data can help ambulances choose the fastest route. Some cities publish \"open data\" about budgets, air quality or public transport so that citizens and small companies can build their own apps. This openness may also increase transparency, since residents can check how money is spent. Residents may also report problems, such as broken pavements, through simple apps, and officials can then see which neighbourhoods need attention most.","These advantages come with risks. A system that tracks vehicles, phones and cameras may build a detailed picture of how individuals move around, and without clear rules that information could be misused or sold. Privacy specialists therefore recommend collecting only what is needed, removing names from data wherever possible, and telling residents what is gathered and why. Security is another concern, because a city that depends on connected systems may be vulnerable to cyber attacks. A single failure in a central system could, in theory, affect lighting, traffic and water supply at once.","Finally, there is the question of fairness. Technology tends to reach wealthy districts first, and residents without smartphones or internet access may be unable to use digital services. Experts argue that successful smart cities begin with real problems, such as flooding or long commutes, rather than with the desire to install the newest gadgets, and that they involve residents in the planning. Technology is a tool; its value depends on the goals it serves."],"gloss":[{"w":"sensor","ar":"مستشعر"},{"w":"exhaust fumes","ar":"عوادم، غازات العادم"},{"w":"demand","ar":"طلب"},{"w":"pressure","ar":"ضغط"},{"w":"open data","ar":"بيانات مفتوحة"},{"w":"transparency","ar":"شفافية"},{"w":"vulnerable","ar":"عرضة للخطر"},{"w":"commute","ar":"التنقل اليومي إلى العمل"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["The privacy and security risks of connected systems","How sensors are made","Why ambulances use traffic data","The cost of new bus routes"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"More than half of the world's people already live in cities.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: أكثر من نصف سكان العالم في المدن."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In smart cities, streetlights always stay at full brightness during the night.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: تخفت الإضاءة عندما لا يكون أحد قريباً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Smart city projects are cheaper to run in Asia than in Europe.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص بين القارات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most residents support the collection of data by their city.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص رأي السكان."},{"k":"mc","q":"How can sensors on water pipes help a city?","o":["They detect leaks early.","They clean the water.","They reduce rainfall.","They lower the price of pipes."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: يكشفون انخفاض الضغط الناتج عن تسرب مبكراً."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to experts, where should a smart city project begin?","o":["With the newest gadgets","With real problems that residents face","With wealthy districts","With private companies"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: تبدأ بمشكلات حقيقية كالفيضانات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Traffic lights linked to sensors can adjust their ___ to the number of vehicles waiting.","a":["timing"],"why":"الفقرة 2: يعدّلون توقيتها."},{"k":"gap","q":"Bins fitted with sensors tell collection crews when they are ___.","a":["full"],"why":"الفقرة 3: عندما تمتلئ."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why might open data increase transparency?","o":["Residents can check how public money is spent.","Companies must hide their budgets.","Sensors replace officials.","Apps are free."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: يستطيع السكان معرفة أوجه الإنفاق."}],"sumAr":"يعرّف النص المدن الذكية وفوائدها في النقل وإدارة الموارد والسلامة العامة. ثم يحذّر من مخاطر الخصوصية والأمن وعدم العدالة ويؤكد البدء بمشكلات حقيقية."},{"id":"r-b2-21","l":"B2","title":"Water Scarcity","topic":"environment","paras":["Water covers most of the planet, yet only a tiny fraction is fresh water that people can drink or use to grow crops, and much of that is locked in glaciers and ice caps. Water scarcity occurs when demand for usable water exceeds the supply in a region. It already affects millions of people, and many experts expect the problem to grow. Because water is needed for food, health and industry, shortages can affect almost every part of life.","Scarcity has two main forms. Physical scarcity arises where there is simply not enough water, as in deserts and dry regions with large populations. Economic scarcity arises where water exists but communities lack the pipes, wells or money needed to bring it to people. A country with large rivers may still have villages without safe drinking water for this reason. In such places, women and children often spend hours each day carrying water from distant sources, time that could be used for school or paid work.","Agriculture is the largest user of fresh water, taking far more than households or industry. Crops such as rice and cotton need large amounts, and irrigation systems that spray or flood fields lose much of the water to evaporation. Growing populations increase the demand for food, and richer diets with more meat increase it further, because producing meat requires more water than producing grain.","Climate change adds pressure. Higher temperatures increase evaporation, and rainfall patterns are shifting, making droughts longer in some areas and floods more severe in others. Mountain glaciers, which supply rivers during dry seasons, are shrinking in several regions. In addition, many cities draw water from underground reserves faster than rain can refill them, so water tables keep falling and wells must be drilled ever deeper. This is especially worrying because underground water can take thousands of years to collect, and once it is used up it cannot easily be replaced.","Solutions exist at every scale. Drip irrigation delivers water drop by drop to plant roots and can cut agricultural use significantly. Cities can repair leaking pipes, which in some places lose a third of the water that enters them, and can recycle treated wastewater for industry and gardens. In coastal regions, desalination plants remove salt from seawater, although the process requires a great deal of energy and is expensive. Households can help by fixing dripping taps and choosing efficient appliances.","Because rivers and aquifers often cross borders, water is also an issue of cooperation. Countries that share a river need agreements about how much each may use, and shared data on flow and rainfall can prevent misunderstandings. History shows that most disputes over water have been settled through negotiation. Experts believe the challenge in the coming decades will be to use water wisely, price it fairly, and ensure that the poorest are not left behind."],"gloss":[{"w":"scarcity","ar":"ندرة"},{"w":"glacier","ar":"نهر جليدي"},{"w":"evaporation","ar":"تبخّر"},{"w":"water table","ar":"منسوب المياه الجوفية"},{"w":"wastewater","ar":"مياه الصرف"},{"w":"desalination","ar":"تحلية المياه"},{"w":"aquifer","ar":"طبقة مياه جوفية"},{"w":"appliance","ar":"جهاز منزلي"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":1,"o":["Two different kinds of water shortage","How glaciers form","The cost of drilling wells","Rivers that cross borders"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Agriculture uses more fresh water than households or industry.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: الزراعة أكبر مستهلك للمياه العذبة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Desalination is described as a cheap process that needs little energy.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: تتطلب طاقة كبيرة وهي مكلفة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Rice farming uses more water than cotton farming.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكر المحصولين معاً دون مقارنة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most countries that share rivers have already signed water agreements.","a":"NG","why":"النص يقول إن الدول بحاجة إلى اتفاقات ولا يذكر عددها."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is economic scarcity?","o":["Water exists but people lack the means to deliver it.","Rain never falls in the region.","Water is too expensive to export.","Rivers are owned by companies."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: الماء موجود لكن تنقص الأنابيب أو المال."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does a meat-rich diet increase water demand?","o":["Animals drink only seawater.","Meat needs more water to produce than grain.","Meat must be washed often.","Farmers use less irrigation."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: إنتاج اللحم يتطلب ماء أكثر من الحبوب."},{"k":"gap","q":"Irrigation systems that spray or flood fields lose much of the water to ___.","a":["evaporation"],"why":"الفقرة 3: يتبخر جزء كبير من الماء."},{"k":"gap","q":"Desalination plants remove ___ from seawater.","a":["salt"],"why":"الفقرة 5: تزيل الملح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is said about most disputes over water?","o":["They led to wars.","They were solved by negotiation.","They were ignored.","They ended in court."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: معظمها حُلّ بالتفاوض."}],"sumAr":"يبيّن النص أن ندرة المياه قد تكون طبيعية أو اقتصادية، وأن الزراعة وتغير المناخ يزيدان الضغط. ويعرض حلولاً كالري بالتنقيط والتحلية والتعاون بين الدول."},{"id":"r-b2-22","l":"B2","title":"The Gig Economy","topic":"business","paras":["The gig economy describes a labour market in which people earn money through short-term tasks or projects rather than permanent jobs. Drivers who accept rides through an app, designers who take on freelance commissions, and couriers who deliver meals are all part of it. Digital platforms match workers with customers within minutes, and this convenience has helped the sector expand rapidly across many countries. For many people, a smartphone has become both a workplace and a source of income, and the number of platforms continues to grow every year.","For workers, the main attraction is flexibility. A student can work evenings only, a parent can choose hours around school, and someone with a regular job can earn extra income at weekends. Platforms also lower the barriers to entry: there is often no interview, and starting may require little more than a phone and an identity document. For people who struggle to find traditional employment, this can be a valuable first step, and some workers use gig tasks to build a reputation before moving to a more stable career.","Consumers and businesses gain as well. Customers enjoy low prices and quick service, while small companies can hire a specialist for a single project instead of paying a full-time salary. This allows start-ups to grow with fewer fixed costs. Some economists argue that the model also creates competition that encourages established companies to improve their own services and to respond faster to what customers want.","However, the model has serious drawbacks. Most gig workers are classified as independent contractors rather than employees, so they usually receive no paid holiday, sick pay or pension contribution. Income can be unpredictable, because the number of tasks depends on demand and on how many other workers are online. Workers also pay their own expenses, such as fuel, vehicle repairs and insurance, so the amount left after costs may be lower than it first appears.","Another concern is the power of algorithms. Platforms often use ratings and automated systems to decide who receives work, and a few poor reviews can reduce a worker's access to tasks. Because decisions are made by software, workers may find it hard to understand why their earnings have fallen or to appeal against a suspension. Researchers describe this as a form of management without a manager.","Governments are now trying to respond. Some courts have ruled that certain drivers should be treated as employees, while some countries have introduced minimum earnings or insurance requirements for platform workers. Others prefer a middle category between employee and contractor. Whatever approach is taken, the challenge is to preserve the flexibility that many workers value while ensuring that they are not left without basic protection. Workers themselves are also forming groups and online communities to share information and negotiate better terms."],"gloss":[{"w":"freelance","ar":"عمل حر، مستقل"},{"w":"courier","ar":"موصّل طلبات"},{"w":"flexibility","ar":"مرونة"},{"w":"contractor","ar":"متعاقد مستقل"},{"w":"pension","ar":"معاش تقاعدي"},{"w":"algorithm","ar":"خوارزمية"},{"w":"suspension","ar":"إيقاف عن العمل"},{"w":"negotiate","ar":"يفاوض"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Decisions made by software without human explanation","The pleasure of working at night","How to start a small company","Why customers like low prices"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Gig workers who are classed as contractors usually do not receive paid holiday.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: لا إجازة مدفوعة غالباً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Small companies using gig workers must still pay a full-time salary for each specialist.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: يمكنهم توظيف متخصص لمشروع واحد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most gig workers would prefer a permanent job if one were available.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص تفضيلات العمال."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Couriers earn more money than drivers on average.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص بين دخل الفئتين."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main attraction of gig work for workers?","o":["Free insurance","Flexible hours","High pensions","Guaranteed income"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: الميزة الرئيسية هي المرونة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why can gig income be unpredictable?","o":["It depends on demand and the number of workers online.","Workers are paid yearly.","Platforms hide all prices.","Customers pay in cash only."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 4: يعتمد على الطلب وعدد العاملين."},{"k":"gap","q":"Starting may require little more than a phone and an identity ___.","a":["document"],"why":"الفقرة 2: وثيقة هوية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Researchers describe this as a form of management without a ___.","a":["manager"],"why":"الفقرة 5: إدارة بلا مدير."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do some countries do about platform work?","o":["Ban it completely","Create rules such as minimum earnings or insurance","Stop courts from ruling on it","Replace it with permanent jobs"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: حدّ أدنى للدخل أو تأمين."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص مزايا اقتصاد العمل الحر عبر المنصات للعمال والزبائن، ثم عيوبه كغياب الحماية وعدم استقرار الدخل وسلطة الخوارزميات. ويشير إلى محاولات الحكومات تنظيمه."},{"id":"r-b2-23","l":"B2","title":"How Judges and Juries Make Decisions","topic":"law","paras":["When a person is accused of a serious crime, someone must decide what really happened and what should follow. In some legal systems, this is the work of a professional judge. In others, it is shared with a jury, a group of ordinary citizens chosen at random. Researchers who study these decision-makers have found that the process is more complex, and sometimes less rational, than we might hope.","Judges and juries follow different logic. A judge is trained in law and is expected to give written reasons for his or her decision, which can later be examined by a higher court. A jury usually delivers only a verdict, and members discuss the case in private. Supporters of juries argue that ordinary people bring common sense and community values, and that involving citizens increases public trust in the courts. Critics worry that jurors may struggle with technical evidence.","Psychologists have identified several influences on decisions that have nothing to do with the facts. One is the order of information: people tend to give extra weight to evidence they hear first or last. Another is the vividness of testimony; a witness who speaks confidently is often believed even when his or her memory is shaky. Studies also suggest that attractive or well-dressed defendants may sometimes be treated more leniently, although findings vary.","Judges are not immune from such effects. One much-discussed study of parole hearings suggested that decisions might be affected by the time since the judges' last break, although later researchers argued that the pattern could be explained by the order in which cases were scheduled. This debate illustrates how difficult it is to separate real bias from other factors. Even so, many courts now use training programmes to make judges aware of unconscious bias.","Group discussion also shapes jury decisions. In deliberation, a confident, talkative juror may influence others, and members in the minority sometimes give in simply to end the disagreement. To reduce this risk, courts give clear instructions about the standard of proof and sometimes require unanimous agreement, so that every view must be addressed. Some systems allow majority verdicts to prevent a single juror from blocking a decision.","Various reforms aim to improve the quality of decisions. Written guidelines for sentencing narrow the range of punishment for similar offences. Judges may be required to explain their reasoning in detail, which encourages careful thinking. Some courts provide jurors with notes, written summaries and plain language instructions. None of these measures can remove human judgement entirely, nor should they. The goal is not to turn courts into machines, but to ensure that human decisions are as fair, consistent and transparent as possible, so that citizens can trust the outcome even when they disagree with it."],"gloss":[{"w":"jury","ar":"هيئة محلفين"},{"w":"verdict","ar":"حكم، قرار الإدانة أو البراءة"},{"w":"testimony","ar":"شهادة"},{"w":"leniently","ar":"بتساهل"},{"w":"parole","ar":"إفراج مشروط"},{"w":"unconscious","ar":"لا واعٍ"},{"w":"deliberation","ar":"مداولة"},{"w":"unanimous","ar":"بالإجماع"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Influences on decisions that are unrelated to the facts","Why witnesses forget names","The training of professional judges","How juries are chosen"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"A judge is usually expected to give written reasons for a decision.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: يُتوقع من القاضي تقديم أسباب مكتوبة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Members of a jury are selected because of their legal training.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: يُختارون عشوائياً من المواطنين العاديين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Trials with a jury are more expensive than trials with a judge alone.","a":"NG","why":"لا يتناول النص التكلفة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Judges with more years of experience are less affected by unconscious bias.","a":"NG","why":"لا يذكر النص أثر سنوات الخبرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do supporters of juries say?","o":["Jurors understand technical evidence better than judges.","Ordinary people bring common sense and increase public trust.","Juries are faster than judges.","Juries give written reasons."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: المواطنون يجلبون الحس السليم ويزيدون ثقة الجمهور."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did later researchers question the parole hearing study?","o":["The judges refused to be studied.","The pattern might be explained by how cases were ordered.","The study was too long.","The data were lost."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: قد يفسّره ترتيب جدولة القضايا."},{"k":"gap","q":"A jury usually delivers only a ___.","a":["verdict"],"why":"الفقرة 2: حكماً فقط دون أسباب."},{"k":"gap","q":"Courts sometimes require ___ agreement so that every view must be addressed.","a":["unanimous"],"why":"الفقرة 5: اتفاق بالإجماع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the aim of the reforms described in the last paragraph?","o":["To replace human judgement with machines","To make human decisions fairer and more consistent","To reduce the number of trials","To remove juries"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: جعل القرارات البشرية أكثر عدلاً واتساقاً."}],"sumAr":"يبيّن النص الفرق بين القضاة والمحلفين وكيف تؤثر عوامل نفسية لا علاقة لها بالوقائع في قراراتهم. ويعرض إصلاحات تهدف إلى جعل الأحكام أكثر عدلاً وشفافية."},{"id":"r-b2-24","l":"B2","title":"Automation and the Future of Work","topic":"technology","paras":["Machines have replaced human labour for centuries, from the mechanical looms of the eighteenth century to the factory robots of the twentieth. What is different today is the range of tasks that can be automated. Advances in artificial intelligence mean that software can now read documents, recognise images and answer questions, abilities once thought to require human intelligence. As a result, even highly educated professionals are starting to ask how their own work might change.","Economists have tried to estimate how many jobs are at risk. Their conclusions differ widely, partly because they analyse tasks rather than whole occupations. Most jobs combine routine tasks, such as filling in forms, with tasks that require judgement, creativity or personal contact. A job is more vulnerable when most of its tasks are routine and predictable, whether these are physical, as in assembly lines, or mental, as in data entry.","History offers some reassurance. When earlier technologies removed jobs, they also created new ones, often in industries that had not existed before. Few people in 1900 could have imagined careers in software or air traffic control. Automation can also lower prices and raise incomes, leading to higher demand for goods and services, and therefore more work. However, the transition can be painful. New jobs may appear in different places, or require different skills, from those that disappeared.","This creates a challenge for education. Workers whose tasks are automated need opportunities to retrain, yet adult training is often underfunded. Many experts argue that schools should place greater emphasis on skills that machines find difficult, such as critical thinking, teamwork and communication, as well as on basic digital literacy. Because the pace of change is rapid, the ability to keep learning throughout life may be more valuable than any single qualification.","There is also a debate about who benefits. If companies use machines to produce more with fewer employees, profits may flow to the owners of technology rather than to workers. Some economists propose measures such as higher taxes on capital, wage support, or shorter working weeks to share gains more widely. Others warn that heavy regulation might discourage innovation and slow growth. Both sides, however, accept that doing nothing would leave many workers behind. There is no consensus about which approach would work best.","Looking ahead, many specialists expect collaboration rather than complete replacement in most fields. A doctor may use software to scan images but still discuss treatment with the patient; a lawyer may use a program to search documents but still decide the strategy. In such roles, technology handles repetitive parts and people provide judgement, empathy and responsibility. The future of work will depend not only on what machines can do, but also on the choices that societies make about how to use them."],"gloss":[{"w":"automate","ar":"يؤتمت، يجعله آلياً"},{"w":"loom","ar":"نول نسيج"},{"w":"routine","ar":"روتيني"},{"w":"vulnerable","ar":"عرضة للخطر"},{"w":"reassurance","ar":"طمأنة"},{"w":"retrain","ar":"يعيد التدريب"},{"w":"literacy","ar":"إلمام، معرفة أساسية"},{"w":"consensus","ar":"إجماع"}],"qs":[{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["The need for new skills and lifelong learning","Why factories are closing","The history of mechanical looms","How to pay lower taxes"],"a":0},{"k":"tfng","q":"Earlier technologies created new kinds of jobs as well as removing old ones.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: أوجدت وظائف جديدة في صناعات جديدة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Economists agree on exactly how many jobs are at risk from automation.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: تقديراتهم تختلف كثيراً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Robots are cheaper than human workers in most factories.","a":"NG","why":"لا يتناول النص التكلفة المقارنة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Shorter working weeks have already been adopted in many countries.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكرها كمقترح فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do estimates of job losses differ?","o":["Economists study tasks, not whole occupations.","Governments hide data.","Robots are too new.","Few jobs are routine."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: لأنهم يحللون المهام لا المهن كاملة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do many specialists expect in most fields?","o":["Total replacement of workers","Collaboration between people and technology","Fewer doctors and lawyers","The end of education"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: تعاون لا إحلال كامل."},{"k":"gap","q":"A job is more vulnerable when most of its tasks are routine and ___.","a":["predictable"],"why":"الفقرة 2: روتينية ويمكن التنبؤ بها."},{"k":"gap","q":"Adult training is often ___.","a":["underfunded"],"why":"الفقرة 4: التدريب يعاني من نقص التمويل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do some economists suggest to share the gains of automation?","o":["Higher taxes on capital","Banning all machines","Raising prices","Stopping education"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: ضرائب أعلى على رأس المال."}],"sumAr":"يناقش النص أثر الأتمتة والذكاء الاصطناعي في الوظائف، وكيف أن التاريخ يبعث على بعض الطمأنينة. ويؤكد أهمية التعليم المستمر وتوزيع المكاسب، وتوقع تعاون الإنسان والآلة."},{"id":"r-c1-01","l":"C1","title":"The Adult Brain and the Second Language","topic":"science","paras":["For decades, it was widely assumed that the window for acquiring a language closes in early adolescence, after which learners are condemned to a heavy accent and halting grammar. This 'critical period' hypothesis drew support from studies of children who migrated abroad and from the everyday observation that young children seem to absorb new languages almost effortlessly. Yet the evidence concerning adults is considerably less bleak than popular belief suggests, and a growing number of researchers now argue that the notion of a sharp biological cut-off is, at best, an oversimplification.","Brain-imaging studies offer a first corrective. When adults learn a second language, they recruit largely the same network of regions as native speakers, but with revealing differences in efficiency. Beginners show heightened activity in frontal areas associated with effort and self-control, whereas proficient speakers rely more heavily on specialised regions in the temporal lobe, where words are stored and recognised. Over months of practice, activity gradually shifts from the former to the latter, and in some studies the density of grey matter in language-related areas increased measurably among intensive students. The adult brain, in other words, remains remarkably plastic.","Adults also possess advantages that children lack. They can grasp explicit rules, use their first language as a scaffold, and deploy deliberate strategies such as spaced repetition or the systematic comparison of grammatical structures. In classroom settings, adults and older teenagers frequently outpace young children during the first year of study precisely because they exploit these analytical tools. The child's superiority emerges only in the long term, and chiefly in two areas: pronunciation and the intuitive handling of subtle grammatical patterns that even advanced adult learners find difficult to master.","One explanation for this long-term difference concerns exposure rather than biology. Children who are immersed in a new country accumulate thousands of hours of meaningful interaction, are corrected gently by patient relatives and teachers, and suffer little social embarrassment when they make mistakes. Adults, by contrast, tend to spend their working days in their first language and use the new one only briefly, often in transactional exchanges with strangers. When researchers compare adults and children who have received equivalent amounts of rich input, the gap narrows considerably, although it rarely disappears altogether.","Not everyone accepts the exposure account. Sceptics point to the strikingly consistent finding that even highly motivated adults who have lived abroad for decades seldom lose a foreign accent entirely. They argue that sensitivity to speech sounds declines early in life, as neural circuits specialise in the sounds of the native language, making it harder to perceive distinctions that the first language ignores. Defenders of the exposure view reply that a small minority of adults do achieve native-like pronunciation, something a strict biological limit ought to forbid. The dispute remains unresolved, not least because it is almost impossible to measure a lifetime of exposure accurately.","Whatever the final verdict, several lines of research converge on practical advice. Sleep appears to consolidate newly learnt vocabulary; retrieving words from memory strengthens them far more than rereading a list; and meeting a word in several contexts fixes its meaning more securely than meeting it once. Emotional factors matter as well. Learners who regard errors as information rather than failure tend to speak more, and therefore improve more quickly. Some commentators have gone further, claiming that bilingualism delays the onset of dementia, but the evidence for this is mixed, and such claims should be treated with caution until larger studies are completed. For adult learners with limited time, the practical message is that short, frequent sessions of active recall are likely to be more productive than occasional marathon study days.","The overall picture, then, is one of gradual change rather than a sudden closing door. Age certainly influences the route a learner takes and, perhaps, the final destination, but motivation, quality of input and sheer time on task exert at least as much influence. Adults who treat their analytical maturity as an asset, rather than an excuse for abandoning the attempt, are likely to progress further than the old hypothesis would predict."],"gloss":[{"w":"hypothesis","ar":"فرضية"},{"w":"plastic","ar":"مرِن، قابل للتشكّل"},{"w":"scaffold","ar":"دعامة، سند مؤقت"},{"w":"outpace","ar":"يتفوّق في السرعة على"},{"w":"consolidate","ar":"يرسّخ، يثبّت"},{"w":"sceptic","ar":"متشكّك"},{"w":"converge","ar":"تلتقي، تتقارب"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Researchers are now unanimous that adult language learning is not limited by any biological factor.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4 تعرض المتشككين، والنزاع ما زال دون حسم؛ فليس هناك إجماع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Proficient speakers depend more than beginners on specialised areas of the temporal lobe.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: المتقنون يعتمدون أكثر على مناطق الفص الصدغي المتخصصة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Adults who study two foreign languages at once progress more slowly than those who study only one.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتحدث عن تعلّم لغتين في وقت واحد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Adults remember new vocabulary better when they study in the morning than in the evening.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 5 تذكر النوم والاسترجاع والسياق، لا وقت الدراسة."},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["A biological argument against the exposure explanation","Why adults make quicker early progress","The role of sleep in memory","Changes in grey matter with age"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Adults can use their first language as a ___ when they study new grammar.","a":["scaffold"],"why":"الفقرة 2: يستخدمون لغتهم الأولى كدعامة (scaffold)."},{"k":"gap","q":"Children immersed in a new country suffer little social ___ when they make mistakes.","a":["embarrassment"],"why":"الفقرة 3: الأطفال لا يشعرون بإحراج اجتماعي كبير عند الخطأ."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the passage, adults often learn faster than children in the first year because they","o":["receive more exposure to the language","make use of analytical tools","have more flexible neural circuits"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: يتفوقون لأنهم يستغلون الأدوات التحليلية كالقواعد الصريحة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do sceptics of the exposure account use as their main evidence?","o":["Children receive less input than adults do.","Pronunciation cannot be measured reliably.","Determined adults who live abroad for decades still keep a foreign accent."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: يستشهدون ببقاء اللكنة الأجنبية لدى كبار حتى بعد عقود."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's attitude to the claim that bilingualism delays dementia is","o":["cautious, pending further research","strongly supportive","entirely dismissive"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: الأدلة متباينة ويجب التعامل مع الادعاء بحذر."}],"sumAr":"يناقش النص فرضية الفترة الحرجة ويبيّن أن دماغ البالغ لا يزال مرِنًا وأن للكبار مزايا تحليلية، مع بقاء الخلاف حول سبب تفوّق الأطفال في النطق على المدى الطويل."},{"id":"r-c1-02","l":"C1","title":"The Paradox of Choice","topic":"society","paras":["Few ideas are as firmly embedded in modern economic thinking as the belief that more choice is always better. If a shopper dislikes one option, the reasoning goes, she may simply ignore it; an additional alternative can therefore make her no worse off and may make her better off. This assumption underpins the way supermarkets, telecom providers and online platforms organise their offerings. Over the past two decades, however, a body of psychological research has challenged it, suggesting that an abundance of options can leave people less satisfied, less decisive and, in some cases, less likely to act at all.","The classic demonstrations are simple. In experiments of this type, one group of participants is shown a small selection of products while another sees a much larger range of the same goods. The group with the larger range frequently reports greater enjoyment of the browsing, yet is less likely to make a purchase and, when it does, expresses weaker confidence in its decision. Similar patterns have been reported with pension funds, where employees confronted with dozens of schemes sometimes fail to enrol in any of them, thereby forgoing the very benefit the choice was meant to provide.","Several mechanisms have been proposed to explain this. The first is cognitive overload: comparing many options across many attributes demands more mental effort than people can comfortably invest. The second concerns opportunity cost. Every option selected means a dozen forgone, and the more attractive alternatives there are, the more vividly one imagines what has been lost. The third is escalating expectations. When only three varieties are available, a mediocre result is easily attributed to limited supply; when hundreds are available, disappointment feels like a personal failure, and regret follows. Some researchers add a fourth factor, social comparison: knowing that others have chosen differently makes a person doubt her own selection.","Individual temperament appears to play a part. Psychologists distinguish between 'maximisers', who search exhaustively for the best possible option, and 'satisficers', who are content with one that meets their requirements. Maximisers often achieve objectively better outcomes, such as higher starting salaries, but they tend to report lower satisfaction and more frequent regret. The satisficer's strategy, which looks modest, may therefore be the more rational one if the goal is wellbeing rather than optimisation.","The paradox has not gone unchallenged. Attempts to replicate the early experiments have produced inconsistent results, and a major review of the literature concluded that the average effect of larger assortments is close to zero. Closer inspection suggests that the effect depends on circumstances. Choice overload seems most likely when the options are difficult to compare, when the chooser has no clear preference to begin with, and when time is short. A shopper who knows precisely what she wants, or an expert who can instantly rule out most of the alternatives, is largely immune. Critics also note that laboratory choices carry few consequences, which may exaggerate the problem.","Policy debates have been shaped by these findings, sometimes unhelpfully. Some commentators draw the conclusion that governments and employers should simply reduce the number of options available. Yet restricting choice carries its own dangers, since people value autonomy and resent being steered, particularly in matters concerning health or money. A more defensible response is to improve how choices are presented: sensible default options for those who do not wish to decide, clear categories that allow quick filtering, and comparison tools that highlight the few attributes that really matter. Such measures respect the individual's freedom while sparing her the burden of examining every alternative personally.","The most plausible reading of the evidence is thus neither that choice is a blessing nor that it is a curse. It is a resource whose value depends on how well people are equipped to use it. Designers who curate options thoughtfully, and consumers who learn to set their criteria before they begin browsing, are likely to gain the benefits of variety without paying its psychological price."],"gloss":[{"w":"embedded","ar":"راسخ، متجذّر"},{"w":"abundance","ar":"وفرة"},{"w":"forgo","ar":"يتخلّى عن، يفوّت"},{"w":"escalating","ar":"متصاعد"},{"w":"temperament","ar":"طبع، مزاج"},{"w":"replicate","ar":"يكرّر (تجربة) للتحقق"},{"w":"immune","ar":"محصَّن، غير متأثر"},{"w":"curate","ar":"ينتقي ويُنظّم بعناية"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Traditional economic theory assumes that adding an extra option can never make a consumer worse off.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 0: خيار إضافي لا يجعلها أسوأ حالًا حسب الافتراض التقليدي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Maximisers tend to feel less satisfied with their decisions than satisficers do.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: المعظِّمون يبلغون عن رضا أقل وندم أكثر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Shoppers in rural areas are more affected by choice overload than shoppers in cities.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين الريف والمدينة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A review of the research found that large assortments harm decision-making in every situation.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: المراجعة وجدت أن الأثر المتوسط قريب من الصفر ويتوقف على الظروف."},{"k":"head","para":5,"o":["The risks of removing choice and better ways of presenting it","Why pension schemes fail","The psychology of regret","How supermarkets arrange products"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Choosing one option means giving up many others, which is known as ___ cost.","a":["opportunity"],"why":"الفقرة 2: الثاني هو تكلفة الفرصة (opportunity cost)."},{"k":"gap","q":"People who are content with an option that meets their requirements are called ___.","a":["satisficers"],"why":"الفقرة 3: الذين يقتنعون بما يلبي متطلباتهم هم satisficers."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the writer mention pension funds?","o":["To illustrate that too many schemes can prevent people from enrolling","To show that employees dislike saving money","To argue that governments should abolish pensions"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 1: كثرة الخطط قد تدفع الموظفين إلى عدم الاشتراك في أي منها."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the passage, choice overload is least likely to affect","o":["people who are short of time","experts who can quickly eliminate most options","those comparing very similar products"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: الخبير الذي يستبعد معظم البدائل فورًا محصَّن إلى حد كبير."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's overall conclusion?","o":["Choice is harmful and should be limited.","Choice is always beneficial to consumers.","The value of choice depends on how well it is managed and used."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 6: الخيار مورد تتوقف قيمته على حسن استخدامه وتنظيمه."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص فكرة أن كثرة الخيارات قد تُنقص الرضا وتؤخر القرار، ثم يبيّن أن الأثر يتوقف على الظروف، ويخلص إلى أن حسن تقديم الخيارات أهم من تقليلها."},{"id":"r-c1-03","l":"C1","title":"Transparency and Trust in Public Institutions","topic":"government","paras":["It seems almost self-evident that openness breeds trust. Citizens who can see how a ministry spends its budget, how a contract was awarded or how a licence was granted will, one assumes, feel more confident that those in power are acting honestly. This belief has inspired a generation of reforms: freedom-of-information laws, public registers of officials' assets, open-data portals and live broadcasts of council meetings. Yet the empirical record is more ambiguous than the slogan implies, and understanding why is essential for anyone designing anti-corruption policy. Whether openness truly delivers the trust it promises is therefore a question of evidence, not of faith.","The case for transparency rests on two arguments. The first is deterrence. Officials who know that their decisions may be scrutinised are less likely to favour relatives, accept bribes or conceal errors. The second is accountability: published information gives journalists, auditors and ordinary voters the raw material they need to challenge poor decisions and to reward good ones. Where these mechanisms operate, studies have linked greater openness to lower levels of petty corruption and to more efficient public spending. In principle, therefore, sunlight is the cheapest and least intrusive of all anti-corruption instruments, since it asks officials to change nothing except their willingness to be seen.","Nevertheless, a puzzling finding has emerged in several countries: increased disclosure is sometimes followed by a fall, rather than a rise, in public confidence. One explanation is that transparency exposes imperfection. Before disclosure, citizens may hold a vaguely positive image of an institution; afterwards, they are confronted with a steady stream of mistakes, delays and minor scandals that were previously hidden. The institution has not necessarily become worse, but it now looks worse. Critics sometimes describe this as the price of honesty, and argue that trust built on ignorance was never genuine in the first place.","A second difficulty is the quality of the information released. Publishing thousands of pages of unsorted spreadsheets technically satisfies a duty of openness, but it may leave the public no wiser. Researchers have coined the term 'opacity by overload' for situations in which relevant facts are buried beneath irrelevant ones. In such cases, disclosure benefits only the well-resourced, such as large media organisations or professional lobbyists, who can afford to analyse it, and may even widen the gap between ordinary citizens and the state.","A third objection concerns behaviour. When every exchange may be recorded and published, officials have an incentive to avoid candour. Frank discussion of difficult trade-offs migrates from the meeting room to informal conversations, and recorded sessions become theatrical performances in which the real decisions have already been made. Some scholars therefore distinguish between genuine transparency, which clarifies how decisions are reached, and performative transparency, which merely creates an impression of openness. Defenders of disclosure counter that such evasion is itself a sign of reform's effect, since it shows that officials fear exposure, and that the remedy is better design, not less openness.","What, then, determines whether disclosure strengthens trust? The evidence points to three conditions. Information must be comprehensible and relevant, which requires summary formats and plain language. It must be interpreted by intermediaries, including independent media, audit offices and civil-society groups, who can turn data into judgements. Above all, it must be linked to consequences. If revealing misconduct leads to no sanction, citizens quickly conclude that exposure is pointless, and cynicism deepens. Transparency without enforcement merely informs people of their own powerlessness. Publication alone changes little unless somebody is both able and willing to act on it.","The lesson is not that governments should retreat from openness; the alternative, secrecy, is far more likely to nurture suspicion and abuse. Rather, transparency should be understood as a necessary but insufficient ingredient. It works best as part of a system in which information flows to capable watchdogs and in which wrongdoing carries a real cost. Only then can openness be expected to earn the trust it is so often assumed to guarantee. Reformers who forget this risk replacing one form of disappointment with another."],"gloss":[{"w":"self-evident","ar":"بديهي"},{"w":"deterrence","ar":"ردع"},{"w":"scrutinise","ar":"يدقّق في، يفحص بإمعان"},{"w":"candour","ar":"صراحة"},{"w":"performative","ar":"شكلي، استعراضي"},{"w":"comprehensible","ar":"مفهوم، سهل الإدراك"},{"w":"cynicism","ar":"تشكّك ساخر، فقدان الثقة"},{"w":"watchdog","ar":"جهة رقابية"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer believes that secrecy is a safer approach for governments than disclosure.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 6: السرية أكثر احتمالًا لإنتاج الشك والفساد، فالكاتب لا يؤيدها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In some countries, more disclosure has been followed by lower public confidence.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: وُجد في عدة دول انخفاض الثقة بعد زيادة الإفصاح."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Freedom-of-information laws are more effective in large countries than in small ones.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين الدول الكبيرة والصغيرة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Large media organisations are better placed than ordinary citizens to benefit from unsorted data.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: الجهات الغنية بالموارد وحدها تستطيع تحليل البيانات الضخمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most officials who are filmed during meetings choose to resign from their posts.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 4 تتحدث عن تجنب الصراحة، لا عن الاستقالة."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Data released in forms the public cannot use","The cost of public broadcasting","How lobbyists influence officials","Why journalists distrust governments"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Researchers use the term 'opacity by ___' when important facts are buried under irrelevant ones.","a":["overload"],"why":"الفقرة 3: opacity by overload أي الغموض بسبب الإفراط في المعلومات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Transparency that only creates an impression of openness is called ___ transparency.","a":["performative"],"why":"الفقرة 4: الشفافية الشكلية تسمى performative transparency."},{"k":"mc","q":"How do defenders of disclosure respond to the problem of officials avoiding candour?","o":["They accept that disclosure should be reduced.","They claim it shows reform is working and call for better design.","They say that recorded meetings are always honest."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: يرون التهرب دليلًا على خشية المسؤولين ويطالبون بتصميم أفضل."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the writer, why may disclosure reduce trust?","o":["Citizens see flaws that were previously hidden.","Institutions deliberately publish false data.","Journalists exaggerate minor mistakes."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 2: الإفصاح يكشف الأخطاء التي كانت مخفية فيبدو المؤسسة أسوأ."}],"sumAr":"يبيّن النص أن الشفافية لا تُنتج الثقة تلقائيًا، فقد تكشف العيوب أو تغرق الناس بالبيانات أو تصبح شكلية، وتنجح فقط عندما تكون مفهومة وتفسّرها جهات رقابية وتقترن بعقوبات."},{"id":"r-c1-04","l":"C1","title":"Predicting Crime: The Ethics of Algorithms in Criminal Justice","topic":"law","paras":["Courts have always had to make predictions. A judge deciding whether to release a defendant on bail must estimate whether that person will return for trial or commit another offence in the meantime; a parole board must guess how a prisoner will behave once released. Traditionally, such judgements rested on experience and intuition. In a growing number of jurisdictions, however, they are now informed by statistical tools that convert factors such as age, criminal record and employment history into a numerical risk score. Supporters regard this as a long-overdue step towards rational decision-making. Critics fear that it risks automating injustice.","The case in favour begins with an uncomfortable truth about human judgement: it is noisy. Studies of sentencing have found that similar defendants can receive strikingly different outcomes depending on which judge hears the case, and even on the time of day or the decision-maker's mood. An algorithm, by contrast, applies the same formula every time, and its performance can be measured and audited. In some pilot schemes, the use of risk scores has been associated with a reduction in pre-trial detention without any increase in offending, suggesting that the tools can sometimes make the system both fairer and cheaper.","The most serious objection concerns the data on which such tools are trained. Historical records reflect policing patterns rather than offending itself. If officers patrol one neighbourhood far more intensively than another, more arrests will be recorded there, and residents will therefore appear riskier to the model, even if the underlying rates of wrongdoing are identical. The resulting scores may then justify still heavier patrols, creating a feedback loop in which the algorithm appears to confirm the very prejudice it has inherited. Arrest figures, in short, measure enforcement as much as behaviour.","Technical fixes are complicated by a deeper problem. Mathematicians have demonstrated that several intuitive definitions of fairness cannot all be satisfied simultaneously when groups have different recorded base rates of re-arrest. A tool can be equally accurate for two groups and yet wrongly label members of one group as dangerous more often than members of the other. Which kind of error matters more is not a technical question but a moral and political one, and it ought to be answered openly by legislators and the public, rather than quietly by software engineers.","A further difficulty is opacity. Many risk assessment tools are commercial products whose internal workings are protected as trade secrets. A defendant who is denied bail partly because of a score he cannot examine is, in effect, unable to challenge a piece of evidence used against him, which sits uneasily with the principle of due process. Defenders of the tools respond that judges are not fully transparent either. Yet a judge can be asked to give reasons and can be overturned on appeal; an unexplained number is far harder to contest.","The standard safeguard is to keep a human being in charge, with the score serving merely as advice. This is less reassuring than it sounds. Psychologists have documented automation bias, the tendency to defer to a numerical output, especially when the decision-maker is overworked or fears criticism for overriding it. Moreover, a label such as 'high risk' describes the proportion of people with a similar profile who reoffended, not what a particular individual will do. The apparent precision of a number can disguise how uncertain the underlying prediction really is.","It would be a mistake to demand either a complete ban or uncritical adoption. The fairest comparison is not between an algorithm and a flawless judge, but between an algorithm and the imperfect system that exists. Even so, certain conditions seem indispensable: independent validation before deployment, public disclosure of the methods used, the right of defendants to examine and contest their scores, and regular re-testing for discriminatory effects. A tool that cannot satisfy these requirements has no place in a courtroom, however accurate it claims to be."],"gloss":[{"w":"jurisdiction","ar":"نطاق قضائي، ولاية قضائية"},{"w":"audit","ar":"يدقّق، يراجع رسميًا"},{"w":"feedback loop","ar":"حلقة تغذية راجعة"},{"w":"opacity","ar":"غموض، انعدام الشفافية"},{"w":"due process","ar":"الإجراءات القانونية الواجبة"},{"w":"overturn","ar":"ينقض (حكمًا)"},{"w":"indispensable","ar":"لا غنى عنه"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Human judges give consistent decisions regardless of who they are or when they sit.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: الأحكام البشرية 'مشوَّشة' وتختلف حسب القاضي والوقت والمزاج."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Intensive policing of one area can make its residents appear more dangerous to a prediction model.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: كثرة الدوريات تعني اعتقالات أكثر فيبدو السكان أخطر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most defendants say they would prefer to be assessed by an algorithm rather than by a judge.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر آراء المتهمين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Software companies have been fined for refusing to reveal how their tools work.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 4 تذكر الأسرار التجارية، لكن لا تذكر غرامات."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Why fairness cannot be defined in a single way","The cost of software development","How judges are trained","The accuracy of arrest data"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Historical records reflect policing patterns rather than actual ___.","a":["offending"],"why":"الفقرة 2: السجلات تعكس أنماط الشرطة لا السلوك الإجرامي الفعلي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Companies often protect their risk assessment methods by calling them ___.","a":["trade secrets"],"why":"الفقرة 4: طرق التقييم محمية بوصفها أسرارًا تجارية."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer suggest about the 'feedback loop' described in paragraph 2?","o":["It proves that algorithms are deliberately biased.","It can make a model seem to confirm a prejudice it inherited.","It only occurs in wealthy neighbourhoods."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: الخوارزمية تبدو وكأنها تؤكد التحيز الذي ورثته من البيانات."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the passage, a 'high risk' label","o":["predicts precisely what an individual will do","reflects the behaviour of people with a similar profile","is produced only by human judges"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: التصنيف يصف نسبة من أعادوا الجريمة من ذوي الملف المشابه."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's conclusion is that algorithmic tools","o":["should be banned from courtrooms altogether","should be adopted without further examination","are acceptable only if they meet strict conditions of openness and testing"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 6: لا بد من شروط مثل التحقق المستقل والإفصاح وحق الطعن."}],"sumAr":"يناقش النص استخدام أدوات التنبؤ بالخطورة في المحاكم: مزاياها في الاتساق، ومخاطر التحيز المتوارث من البيانات، وتعارض معايير العدالة، وغموض الخوارزميات، ويدعو إلى شروط صارمة للمساءلة."},{"id":"r-c1-05","l":"C1","title":"Measuring Time: From Shadows to Atoms","topic":"science","paras":["Time appears to be the most objective of quantities, flowing at the same rate for everyone, everywhere. Yet the history of how humans have measured it suggests something rather different. The hours, minutes and seconds that structure modern life are not natural facts but human constructions, shaped at each stage by the practical needs of the societies that devised them. To follow the development of the clock is therefore to follow changes in trade, religion, industry and government.","The earliest timekeepers relied on the sky. Ancient civilisations observed the shifting length and direction of shadows, and built sundials to divide the day into portions. These devices were useless at night and on cloudy days, so water clocks, in which liquid dripped at a roughly steady rate from one vessel to another, were developed as a supplement. Both systems usually divided daylight into twelve parts, which meant that an 'hour' in summer was considerably longer than an hour in winter. Such flexibility is hard for modern minds to accept, but it suited agricultural communities whose lives followed the sun.","The mechanical clock, which appeared in medieval Europe, changed this arrangement. Installed in monasteries and then in town towers, clocks sounded bells that coordinated prayer, markets and civic duties. Over time, equal hours, which did not vary by season, became the norm. Some historians argue that the public clock fostered a new discipline in which labour was measured by the hour and punctuality became a virtue. Others consider this claim exaggerated, pointing out that wage labour, factories and urban growth did at least as much to teach people to value time as the devices themselves.","Accuracy improved dramatically with the introduction of the pendulum in the seventeenth century, which reduced the typical error of a good clock from several minutes a day to a matter of seconds. The impetus was largely commercial and military. Sailors could calculate latitude from the stars, but finding longitude required knowing the exact time at a reference point, and an error of a few minutes could place a ship many kilometres from its true position. The development of a reliable marine chronometer, able to keep time despite the motion and temperature changes of a long voyage, thus had enormous economic value and saved many lives.","Even then, there was no single 'correct' time. Each town set its clocks by the local position of the sun, so noon in one city arrived minutes before noon in another a short distance to the west. This was tolerable when travel was slow. The arrival of railways made it chaotic, since timetables required shared hours across hundreds of kilometres. Companies adopted standard time for their own purposes, and many communities initially resisted, regarding the change as an outside interference with local custom. International agreement on time zones came later in the nineteenth century, a reminder that deciding what time it is has always been a political act as well as a scientific one.","The twentieth century brought a shift from mechanical to physical oscillations. Quartz crystals, which vibrate at a very stable frequency when an electric current passes through them, made cheap and accurate watches possible. Atomic clocks went further, using the regular radiation emitted by caesium atoms as their standard, and since the late 1960s the official second has been defined in precisely this way. Such devices are accurate to within a tiny fraction of a second over millions of years, and satellite navigation, financial trading and telecommunications networks depend on them. Paradoxically, the rotation of the Earth itself is slightly irregular, so occasional leap seconds are inserted into official time to keep it in step with the day.","Each stage in this story answered a specific social demand: farmers needed the seasons, merchants and navigators needed accuracy, railways needed uniformity, and digital networks need synchronisation. The lesson is that precision is never pursued for its own sake. As our instruments become ever more exact, it is worth remembering that the question of what time it is has been, and will remain, partly a matter of human agreement."],"gloss":[{"w":"construction","ar":"بناء، شيء مصطنع"},{"w":"supplement","ar":"مكمِّل، إضافة"},{"w":"punctuality","ar":"الالتزام بالمواعيد"},{"w":"impetus","ar":"دافع، حافز"},{"w":"chronometer","ar":"ساعة بحرية دقيقة"},{"w":"uniformity","ar":"توحيد، تماثل"},{"w":"oscillation","ar":"تذبذب"},{"w":"synchronisation","ar":"تزامن، مزامنة"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Water clocks were designed to be used only during daylight hours.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: الساعات المائية طُوّرت لتعمل في الليل والأيام الغائمة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The spread of town clocks encouraged the replacement of seasonal hours with equal ones.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: مع الوقت أصبحت الساعات المتساوية هي المعيار."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The first sundials were built in ancient Egypt.","a":"NG","why":"النص يذكر 'الحضارات القديمة' دون تحديد الأصل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The marine chronometer was cheaper to produce than earlier navigation instruments.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 3 تتحدث عن أهميتها لا عن تكلفة إنتاجها."},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Railways and the need for standard time","The invention of the pendulum","Why sundials were abandoned","The measurement of latitude"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Railways required shared ___ across hundreds of kilometres.","a":["timetables"],"why":"الفقرة 4: السكك الحديدية تطلبت جداول زمنية موحدة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Atomic clocks use the radiation emitted by ___ atoms.","a":["caesium"],"why":"الفقرة 5: الساعات الذرية تستخدم إشعاع ذرات السيزيوم."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's view of the claim that clocks created work discipline?","o":["It is entirely correct.","It is probably overstated, since other factors mattered as much.","It has never been seriously debated."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 2: يرى آخرون أن الادعاء مبالغ فيه لأن العمل المأجور والمصانع أثّرت أيضًا."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are leap seconds added to official time?","o":["Atomic clocks gradually run slow.","Satellites require extra synchronisation.","The Earth's rotation is not perfectly regular."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: دوران الأرض غير منتظم قليلًا فتُضاف ثوانٍ كبيسة."},{"k":"mc","q":"The passage as a whole suggests that advances in timekeeping were mainly","o":["a response to social and economic needs","the result of accidental discoveries","driven by scientific curiosity alone"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 6: كل مرحلة جاءت استجابة لطلب اجتماعي محدد."}],"sumAr":"يتتبع النص تاريخ قياس الزمن من الظلال والساعات المائية إلى الساعات الميكانيكية والبندول والذرية، ويبيّن أن كل تطور لبّى حاجة اجتماعية أو اقتصادية، وأن تحديد الوقت قرار بشري أيضًا."},{"id":"r-c1-06","l":"C1","title":"Antibiotic Resistance: Borrowed Time","topic":"health","paras":["Few discoveries have saved as many lives as antibiotics. Routine surgery, cancer chemotherapy, organ transplants and the safe delivery of premature babies all depend on the ability to treat bacterial infections that would otherwise be fatal. It is therefore alarming that the effectiveness of these drugs is declining. Antibiotic resistance, the capacity of bacteria to survive medicines designed to kill them, is neither new nor unnatural; it is an inevitable consequence of evolution. What is new is the speed at which human behaviour has accelerated it.","The underlying mechanism is natural selection. When a population of bacteria is exposed to an antibiotic, susceptible individuals die, while any with a chance mutation that protects them survive and multiply. Their descendants then dominate the population. Complicating matters, bacteria can also exchange genes directly, even between different species, so that a resistance trait that arises in one organism can spread rapidly through an entire microbial community. Every course of antibiotics, however justified, therefore exerts selective pressure, and no use of these drugs is entirely free of cost.","Several practices have intensified the problem. Antibiotics are frequently prescribed for viral illnesses such as colds, against which they are powerless, often because patients expect treatment or because doctors cannot quickly confirm the cause. Patients sometimes stop taking a course once they feel better, leaving the hardiest bacteria alive. Agriculture is another major contributor: in many countries, healthy livestock receive antibiotics routinely, to prevent disease in crowded conditions or to promote growth. Hospitals, meanwhile, bring together large numbers of vulnerable patients and heavy drug use, creating ideal conditions for resistant strains to emerge and spread.","Public campaigns often place the blame on individual prescribers and patients, but this framing is contested. Doctors working under severe time pressure, without access to rapid diagnostic tests, may prescribe 'just in case'. In regions with poor sanitation, infections spread easily, which raises the demand for treatment in the first place. Moreover, in many low-income countries the greater problem is not overuse but lack of access: far more people die because they cannot obtain an effective antibiotic than because the drug has failed. Policies that restrict consumption without improving access could therefore do more harm than good.","A further difficulty lies in the development pipeline. Very few new classes of antibiotics have reached the market in recent decades. The reason is largely economic. A new antibiotic that works against resistant infections will, and should, be used sparingly and held in reserve, which means that its manufacturer sells little of it. Medicines for chronic conditions, taken daily for years, offer a far better return on investment, and several large companies have abandoned antibiotic research altogether. Some economists propose a subscription model, under which governments pay a fixed annual fee for access to a drug regardless of how much is used, thereby separating company revenue from sales volume.","Alternatives are being explored, though none is a complete substitute. Bacteriophages, viruses that attack bacteria, can be remarkably precise, but each must be matched to the particular bacterium causing an infection, which demands rapid diagnosis and extensive testing. Vaccines reduce infections and therefore the need for antibiotics; rapid diagnostic tools help doctors to distinguish bacterial from viral illness within minutes. Each of these measures is promising, yet they are most effective when deployed together with better hygiene and careful stewardship of existing drugs.","The most realistic conclusion is that resistance can be slowed but not abolished. No single remedy, whether a new drug, a stricter prescribing rule or a change in farming practice, will be sufficient. Progress requires a combination of measures: sensible use of existing medicines, investment in sanitation and vaccination, and financial reforms that make the search for new antibiotics worthwhile. Without them, humanity may find that it has been living on borrowed time, and that the era in which a simple infection was easily cured turns out to have been a brief interlude."],"gloss":[{"w":"susceptible","ar":"قابل للتأثر، غير مقاوم"},{"w":"mutation","ar":"طفرة"},{"w":"hardiest","ar":"الأشد صلابة"},{"w":"livestock","ar":"الماشية"},{"w":"pipeline","ar":"مسار التطوير (المنتجات قيد الإعداد)"},{"w":"bacteriophage","ar":"عاثية، فيروس يهاجم البكتيريا"},{"w":"stewardship","ar":"إدارة رشيدة، ترشيد الاستخدام"},{"w":"interlude","ar":"فترة عابرة"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Antibiotic resistance occurs only because of human misuse of medicines.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 0: المقاومة نتيجة طبيعية للتطور، والبشر سرّعوها فقط."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Bacteria belonging to different species can pass resistance traits to one another.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: البكتيريا تتبادل الجينات حتى بين الأنواع المختلفة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Resistant infections are more common in private hospitals than in public ones.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين المستشفيات الخاصة والعامة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Treatment with bacteriophages has now been approved for general use in most countries.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 5 لا تذكر موافقات رسمية على العلاج بالعاثيات."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Why blaming individuals is too simple","How hospitals reduce infection","The history of animal farming","Why doctors dislike diagnostic tests"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Hospitals bring together large numbers of ___ patients and heavy drug use.","a":["vulnerable"],"why":"الفقرة 2: المستشفيات تجمع مرضى ضعفاء واستخدامًا كثيفًا للأدوية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Under a ___ model, governments pay a fixed fee for access to a drug.","a":["subscription"],"why":"الفقرة 4: نموذج الاشتراك يدفع فيه الحكومات رسمًا ثابتًا."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why have several large companies abandoned antibiotic research?","o":["New antibiotics are too easy to copy.","Governments have banned further research.","Sparingly used drugs bring poor returns compared with long-term medicines."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: المضاد الجديد يُدَّخر فيبيع قليلًا، فالعائد ضعيف."},{"k":"mc","q":"What point does the writer make about low-income countries?","o":["Lack of access to effective antibiotics can be a bigger threat than overuse.","Resistance does not exist there.","Farmers there use fewer drugs than hospitals do."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 3: عدد الوفيات بسبب نقص الوصول أكبر من فشل الدواء."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's final view is that","o":["resistance will soon disappear","a single new drug will solve the problem","several measures must be combined to slow resistance"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 6: لا علاج واحد يكفي؛ يلزم مزيج من الإجراءات."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص آلية مقاومة المضادات الحيوية وأسبابها في الطب والزراعة، ويعرض صعوبات اقتصادية في تطوير أدوية جديدة وبدائل مثل العاثيات واللقاحات، ويخلص إلى ضرورة الجمع بين عدة إجراءات."},{"id":"r-c1-07","l":"C1","title":"Can Money Buy Happiness? What Economists Found","topic":"society","paras":["Whether money can buy happiness is among the oldest questions in popular wisdom, and for much of the twentieth century economists avoided it, regarding feelings as too subjective to measure. That reluctance has faded. Large surveys now ask people in dozens of countries to rate their satisfaction with life on a numerical scale, and the answers can be compared with income, age, health and employment. The results do not support the cynic who claims that wealth is irrelevant, nor the materialist who assumes that more is always better. They reveal a pattern that is considerably more interesting than either slogan.","Two findings appear consistently. First, in any given country at any given time, richer people report higher average satisfaction than poorer ones. Second, comparing countries, citizens of wealthy nations are on average more satisfied than those of poor nations. Taken together, these results seem to confirm the common-sense view. Yet a third observation complicates it. When economists tracked individual countries over several decades, they found that average happiness often rose only slightly, or not at all, even though national income had increased several times over. This puzzle, first described by an economist in the 1970s, is known as the Easterlin paradox.","Two main explanations have been offered. The first is relative income. Satisfaction depends not only on how much people earn but on how that amount compares with those around them. If everyone's income rises together, nobody's relative position improves, and the expected gain in well-being fails to materialise. The second is hedonic adaptation: people become accustomed to improved circumstances, and their aspirations rise to match. A larger flat or a faster car delivers a brief lift, after which it simply becomes the new normal, a phenomenon sometimes called the hedonic treadmill.","A heated debate concerns whether the benefits of money eventually level off. Some influential studies suggested that, beyond a certain income, additional earnings no longer made people feel happier. More recent analyses based on much larger samples reported no such ceiling: satisfaction continued to increase as income rose, although each extra unit of money produced a smaller gain than the one before. Part of the disagreement stems from a distinction between two kinds of well-being. Emotional well-being, the quality of day-to-day mood, may plateau relatively early, whereas life evaluation, the overall judgement people make about how their lives are going, tends to keep rising with income.","The effect of money is also unevenly distributed. It is strongest at low incomes, where extra earnings reduce insecurity, allow families to afford medical care and relieve the constant stress of making ends meet. Beyond the point at which basic needs are secure, the relationship weakens and other factors assume greater importance: health, stable relationships, a sense of control over one's time and trust in the surrounding community. In other words, money appears to matter chiefly as a protection against misery, and less as a source of joy.","Research on spending offers another insight. Experiments suggest that people derive longer-lasting satisfaction from spending on experiences, such as travel or shared meals, than on possessions, partly because experiences generate memories and bring people together. Spending on others tends to produce a greater lift than spending on oneself, and paying to avoid disliked chores can increase satisfaction by freeing up time. These findings should be treated with some caution, however: many experiments involve small samples, mostly drawn from wealthy countries, and the effects they report are usually modest.","What, then, can be concluded? Money matters, but its influence is neither simple nor unlimited. It reduces hardship, improves satisfaction with life, and allows people to purchase some of the things that really do make them happier. Yet it cannot compensate for poor health or isolation, and its benefits are diluted by comparison and adaptation. For policymakers, the implication is that reducing insecurity and inequality may do more for national well-being than the pursuit of growth alone."],"gloss":[{"w":"cynic","ar":"متشائم ساخر"},{"w":"materialist","ar":"مادّي، مهتم بالمال والممتلكات"},{"w":"paradox","ar":"مفارقة"},{"w":"aspiration","ar":"طموح"},{"w":"plateau","ar":"يستقر عند مستوى ثابت"},{"w":"ceiling","ar":"سقف، حد أعلى"},{"w":"dilute","ar":"يُضعف، يخفّف"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Within a single country, richer people tend to report lower satisfaction than poorer people.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: الأغنياء يبلغون في المتوسط عن رضا أعلى داخل البلد الواحد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Average happiness in some countries has risen far less than national income over several decades.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: السعادة ارتفعت قليلًا أو لم ترتفع رغم تضاعف الدخل الوطني."},{"k":"tfng","q":"People who win a very large sum of money remain happier than before for the rest of their lives.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتناول الفائزين بمبالغ كبيرة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Women report higher life satisfaction than men who earn the same income.","a":"NG","why":"لا مقارنة بين الجنسين في النص."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Does the benefit of higher income ever level off?","Why surveys are unreliable","The cost of buying experiences","Differences between rich and poor nations"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"People become accustomed to better circumstances, a process known as hedonic ___.","a":["adaptation"],"why":"الفقرة 2: التكيّف المتعة hedonic adaptation يفسّر المفارقة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Spending on ___ tends to produce a greater lift than spending on oneself.","a":["others"],"why":"الفقرة 5: الإنفاق على الآخرين يعطي رضا أكبر."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the passage, the dispute over a satisfaction ceiling is partly explained by","o":["errors in the surveys","the difference between daily mood and overall life evaluation","variations in the price of goods"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: المزاج اليومي قد يستقر مبكرًا بينما التقييم العام يواصل الارتفاع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer suggest about the effect of money at higher incomes?","o":["It disappears completely.","It remains the most important influence.","It weakens as other factors become more important."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 4: بعد تأمين الاحتياجات الأساسية تزداد أهمية الصحة والعلاقات."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's attitude to the research on spending is","o":["cautiously interested","completely sceptical","enthusiastic and uncritical"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: النتائج مفيدة لكن يجب التعامل معها بحذر بسبب صغر العينات."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص ما وجده الاقتصاديون عن المال والسعادة: الغنى يرتبط بالرضا، لكن مفارقة إيسترلين والمقارنة الاجتماعية والتكيّف تحدّ من الأثر، وطريقة الإنفاق مهمة، والتأمين ضد الفقر والعزلة أهم من النمو وحده."},{"id":"r-c1-08","l":"C1","title":"Mining the Deep Sea: Opportunity or Risk?","topic":"environment","paras":["Four or five kilometres below the surface of the ocean, vast plains of the seabed are strewn with lumps of rock the size of potatoes. These polymetallic nodules, formed over millions of years, contain nickel, copper, manganese and cobalt, metals that are essential for the batteries used in electric vehicles and for renewable energy systems. As demand for such materials grows, a number of companies and governments have turned their attention to the ocean floor. Because most of the relevant area lies beyond national borders, mining there is supposed to be regulated by an international authority, whose rules are still being written.","Proponents advance a straightforward argument. Land-based mining is far from harmless: it clears forests, displaces communities, consumes enormous quantities of water and leaves behind toxic waste. The supply of certain metals is also concentrated in a handful of countries, which makes the global transition to clean energy vulnerable to political disruption. Nodules, by contrast, lie loose on the seabed. Collecting them requires no tunnelling, no removal of topsoil and no tailings dams, and the ore may be richer in valuable metals than much of what remains on land.","Opponents begin from a different premise: that the deep sea is poorly understood and slow to recover. Most of the species that live there have never been described by scientists, and many are adapted to extremely stable, cold and dark conditions in which growth is measured in centuries. The nodules themselves provide the only hard surface on which some organisms can attach. Removing them is therefore equivalent to removing the habitat. Small-scale experiments, in which the seabed was disturbed decades ago, have shown that the tracks remain visible and that the animal life within them has still not returned to its original abundance.","Even more worrying, in the view of many biologists, are the plumes. Collecting machines stir up clouds of fine sediment, which may drift for considerable distances before settling and smothering creatures that feed by filtering the water. Wastewater from processing ships, if discharged at the surface or at mid-depth, could carry particles and dissolved metals through layers of the ocean where fish and other animals live. Noise and artificial light add further disturbance. The scale of these effects is unknown, because no commercial-scale operation has yet taken place, and scientific predictions rely heavily on modelling.","Supporters of mining answer that the comparison should be with the real alternative, not with an untouched ocean. If the world needs metals, they say, they must come from somewhere, and the damage caused by land mines is certain and well documented. Critics respond that this assumption is itself questionable. Recycling can recover a growing share of battery metals, manufacturers are developing battery chemistries that use less cobalt or none at all, and forecasts of demand are notoriously uncertain. If the metals prove to be unnecessary, the environmental cost of seabed mining would have been incurred for nothing.","The governance question is equally awkward. The international authority is charged both with promoting the exploitation of seabed resources, which are described as the common heritage of humankind, and with protecting the marine environment. Critics see an obvious conflict of interest. There are also unresolved questions about how revenue should be shared, especially with developing countries that cannot afford the technology. Some governments and scientists have called for a moratorium until baseline surveys are completed; others argue that a firm regulatory framework, developed alongside carefully monitored pilot projects, is preferable to an indefinite delay.","The strongest argument for caution is the irreversibility of potential damage. Where evidence is incomplete and recovery would take centuries, if it occurred at all, the burden of proof should rest on those who wish to proceed rather than on those who urge patience. This does not mean that the deep sea must remain untouched for ever. It does mean that a decision of such magnitude should follow, not precede, the research that would allow its consequences to be understood."],"gloss":[{"w":"nodule","ar":"عقيدة معدنية، كتلة صخرية صغيرة"},{"w":"proponent","ar":"مؤيّد، مناصر"},{"w":"tailings","ar":"مخلفات التعدين"},{"w":"plume","ar":"سحابة (من الرواسب)"},{"w":"sediment","ar":"رواسب"},{"w":"moratorium","ar":"وقف مؤقت (اختياري أو رسمي)"},{"w":"irreversibility","ar":"عدم القابلية للعكس"},{"w":"burden of proof","ar":"عبء الإثبات"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Collecting nodules from the seabed requires tunnelling similar to that used in land-based mines.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: جمع العقيدات لا يتطلب حفر أنفاق أو إزالة التربة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Experimental disturbance of the seabed has left effects that were still visible decades later.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: آثار المسارات ما زالت ظاهرة والحياة لم تعد كما كانت."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Nodules on the seabed contain more cobalt than any deposit found on land.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 1 تقول إن الخام قد يكون أغنى، دون مقارنة بالكوبالت وحده."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Deep-sea mining would create more jobs than land-based mining.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يتناول فرص العمل."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Clouds of sediment and other forms of pollution","The history of the nodules","Costs of recycling batteries","How land mines are cleaned up"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Machines stir up clouds of fine ___ that may smother filter-feeding creatures.","a":["sediment"],"why":"الفقرة 3: الآلات تثير سحبًا من الرواسب الدقيقة (sediment)."},{"k":"gap","q":"Manufacturers are developing battery ___ that use less cobalt.","a":["chemistries"],"why":"الفقرة 4: تُطوَّر كيمياء بطاريات تستخدم كوبالت أقل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the weakness of the proponents' argument, according to critics?","o":["It ignores the damage done by land mines.","It assumes that the metals will definitely be needed.","It overestimates the cost of recycling."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 4: الطلب غير مؤكد، فقد لا تلزم هذه المعادن أصلًا."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do critics see a conflict of interest in the international authority?","o":["It is owned by mining companies.","It receives money from developing countries.","It is responsible for both promoting mining and protecting the environment."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: الهيئة مكلّفة بالترويج للاستغلال وبحماية البيئة معًا."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's final position is that","o":["decisions on mining should wait for adequate research","mining should be banned for ever","mining should start immediately in all areas"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 6: القرار يجب أن يأتي بعد البحث، وعبء الإثبات على المؤيدين."}],"sumAr":"يقارن النص بين حجج مؤيدي تعدين قاع البحار، كتقليل أضرار المناجم البرية، ومخاوف المعارضين من تدمير بيئات بطيئة التعافي وسحب الرواسب وتضارب مصالح الهيئة المنظمة، ويدعو إلى إجراء البحوث أولًا."},{"id":"r-c1-09","l":"C1","title":"How the Design of Cities Shapes Behaviour","topic":"city","paras":["A city is not merely a backdrop against which life takes place; it is an environment that quietly rewards some actions and discourages others. Planners rarely compel anyone to do anything. Instead, they make certain choices easy, such as walking to a shop or lingering in a square, and others awkward, such as crossing a six-lane road on foot. Because these influences operate below the level of conscious decision, they are easy to overlook. Yet there is growing evidence that the layout of streets, the location of buildings and the design of public furniture affect health, safety and social life in ways that residents rarely notice.","Consider mobility. In compact neighbourhoods with short blocks, mixed uses and shops, schools and workplaces within a few minutes' walk, residents tend to travel on foot or by bicycle for everyday errands. In districts organised around wide roads, large car parks and strict separation of housing from services, the car becomes close to compulsory, even for trivial journeys. Public-health researchers link such car-dependent layouts to lower levels of physical activity and higher rates of obesity and related illness, although the size of the effect remains debated. Where pavements are narrow, crossings are infrequent and distances between destinations are long, walking becomes not only unpleasant but, for children and older people, genuinely unsafe.","Safety provides a second illustration. One influential urban writer argued that streets are safest when they are watched by many people with a legitimate reason to be there, a principle summarised as 'eyes on the street'. Windows that overlook pavements, shops that open at different hours and homes with front doors facing the road all increase natural surveillance. Isolated underpasses, blank walls and poorly lit car parks do the opposite. Some studies of lighting upgrades and the greening of neglected spaces have reported falls in recorded crime, though critics caution that offending may simply move elsewhere.","Design can also be used to restrict behaviour deliberately. Benches divided by armrests prevent anyone from lying down, and sloping ledges or studs discourage people from sitting or sleeping in particular places. Defenders describe such features as legitimate tools for keeping spaces usable by everyone. Opponents call them hostile architecture, arguing that they do not solve the problems they target but merely push vulnerable people out of sight, and that they treat citizens as threats rather than as members of a community. The debate illustrates that the design of the physical environment is never entirely neutral.","It would be a mistake, however, to exaggerate the power of design. Critics of what they call environmental determinism point to the problem of self-selection: people who enjoy walking may choose to live in walkable areas, so that differences in behaviour between neighbourhoods partly reflect who lives there rather than how the streets are built. Studies that take residents' preferences into account generally find smaller effects than simple comparisons suggest, though the effects rarely vanish altogether. Design, in other words, appears to influence behaviour at the margin, without overriding the choices people make for their own reasons.","The same caution applies to social life. Parks, squares and markets create opportunities for chance encounters between people who would not otherwise meet, and these contacts are thought to build trust. But a handsome plaza can stand empty if it is exposed to traffic noise, lacks shade or has no reason to attract visitors. Events, maintenance and a sense of local ownership often matter as much as the physical form. Architecture can provide a stage, but it cannot write the performance. Residents who help to plant, program or manage a space tend to look after it far better than those who merely receive it.","The sensible conclusion is that urban design shapes possibilities, not destinies. This carries a responsibility. Since planners inevitably encourage some behaviour and discourage other behaviour, they should be explicit about their intentions and open to public scrutiny. A city whose residents have been consulted about the habits it is meant to encourage is likely to be both more legitimate and more successful than one whose influence remains hidden in concrete and asphalt."],"gloss":[{"w":"backdrop","ar":"خلفية"},{"w":"compulsory","ar":"إلزامي"},{"w":"surveillance","ar":"مراقبة"},{"w":"hostile architecture","ar":"عمارة طاردة (تمنع الجلوس والمبيت)"},{"w":"determinism","ar":"حتمية"},{"w":"self-selection","ar":"الاختيار الذاتي (اختيار الناس لمكان يناسب ميولهم)"},{"w":"legitimate","ar":"مشروع، شرعي"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer argues that urban design forces residents to behave in particular ways.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 0 والخاتمة: التصميم يُسهّل أو يُصعّب لا يُجبر، ويشكّل الإمكانات لا المصائر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Studies that allow for residents' preferences still tend to find some effect of design on behaviour.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: التأثير أصغر لكنه نادرًا ما يختفي تمامًا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Young people are more influenced by the design of their neighbourhood than older people.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن بين الفئات العمرية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most cities have banned the use of sloping ledges to discourage sleeping.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 3 لا تذكر حظرًا لهذه الحواف."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Design that deliberately restricts behaviour","The benefits of shared benches","How to lower crime with lighting","Why plazas stand empty"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"An influential urban writer summarised her principle of natural surveillance as 'eyes on the ___'.","a":["street"],"why":"الفقرة 2: مبدأ 'عيون على الشارع' (eyes on the street)."},{"k":"gap","q":"Differences between neighbourhoods may partly result from ___, as people choose areas that suit them.","a":["self-selection"],"why":"الفقرة 4: الاختيار الذاتي يفسّر جزءًا من الفروق."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main reason why design influences are 'easy to overlook'?","o":["They are kept secret by planners.","They operate without people consciously deciding.","They only affect small areas."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 0: هذه التأثيرات تعمل دون مستوى القرار الواعي."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the writer mention a handsome but empty plaza?","o":["To show that good architecture always attracts visitors","To criticise the high cost of public spaces","To show that physical design alone cannot guarantee social life"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: الفعاليات والصيانة والانتماء المحلي لا تقل أهمية عن الشكل."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer suggests that planners should","o":["be open about the behaviour their designs aim to encourage","avoid influencing behaviour altogether","leave decisions entirely to engineers"],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 6: على المخططين الشفافية بشأن نواياهم وقبول التدقيق العام."}],"sumAr":"يوضح النص كيف تؤثر تصاميم المدن في المشي والأمان والحياة الاجتماعية، ويناقش العمارة الطاردة وحجة الاختيار الذاتي، ويخلص إلى أن التصميم يوسّع الإمكانات لا يفرض المصير وأن على المخططين أن يكونوا شفافين."},{"id":"r-c1-10","l":"C1","title":"Chasing Stolen Wealth Across Borders","topic":"anticorruption","paras":["When a public official diverts money from a state treasury, the theft is only the first stage of a longer problem. Funds taken from school, hospital or road budgets rarely stay where they were stolen. They are moved, often within days, through a chain of accounts, companies and properties in several jurisdictions, so that by the time an investigation begins the trail may cross five or six borders. Recovering those assets, rather than merely convicting the thief, has therefore become a distinct and demanding branch of law enforcement, one that tests the cooperation of states that have little else in common.","The first obstacle is concealment. Those who launder stolen funds seldom hold them in their own names. Instead they rely on shell companies, which exist on paper only, and on nominees, ordinary individuals who agree to appear as the owners of record. A single villa on a foreign coast may belong to a company registered in one territory, itself owned by a trust in another, whose beneficiaries are recorded in neither. Investigators must peel back each layer, and each layer may lie within the legal reach of a different state that has no obligation to help. Registers of beneficial ownership, which list the real people who ultimately control a company, were designed to shorten this task, but their value depends on whether the information is verified and on who is permitted to read it.","The second obstacle is legal. The traditional route is mutual legal assistance, in which one state asks another to gather evidence or freeze property. Requests must satisfy the formal requirements of the receiving country, are often translated, and may take years to answer. Meanwhile the money can be moved again. Many jurisdictions also insist on a criminal conviction before assets can be confiscated, which is impossible if the suspect has died, fled or remains in power. Some countries have therefore developed non-conviction-based forfeiture, which targets the property rather than the person and requires a court to be persuaded only that the assets probably derive from crime. Supporters regard this as a pragmatic remedy; critics warn that it lowers the standard of proof against owners who have not been found guilty of anything.","A third difficulty concerns what happens once the money is recovered. Returning funds to a government that was itself implicated in their theft would seem to defeat the purpose of the exercise. Several recent arrangements have therefore placed recovered sums under independent supervision, directing them towards projects that civil society groups can monitor, such as vaccination programmes or rural electrification. Such conditions are sometimes denounced as an affront to sovereignty, since they imply that the victim state cannot be trusted with its own property. Defenders reply that sovereignty belongs to a country's citizens, not to whoever happens to control its treasury at a given moment.","Practitioners disagree about where the greatest improvement lies. One camp argues that the priority is prevention: transparent public procurement, asset declarations by officials and prompt reporting of suspicious transactions by banks make large thefts harder to hide in the first place. A second camp contends that prevention, however admirable, does nothing for the sums already abroad, and that investment should go into specialist units able to follow money in real time. The two positions are less opposed than they sound, since the data produced by preventive systems is precisely what such units need, but budgets are finite and the emphasis a government chooses reveals a good deal about what it actually wants.","Technology has complicated rather than simplified the picture. Analytical software can scan millions of transactions for patterns that a human clerk would miss, and leaked archives have occasionally exposed entire networks. Yet digital currencies allow value to cross borders in minutes without passing through a regulated bank, and some are designed to obscure the sender. It would be a mistake, however, to assume that criminals hold the advantage. Public blockchains keep a permanent record, and several seizures have succeeded precisely because a transaction made years earlier could be reconstructed.","What emerges is a picture of partial progress. Recovery rates remain low in comparison with the amounts believed to have been stolen, and the process is slow, costly and uncertain. Nevertheless, each completed case establishes precedents and trains investigators, while the growing expectation that stolen wealth can be pursued may alter the calculations of those who contemplate theft. The deterrent effect, which is difficult to measure, may ultimately matter more than the sums returned."],"gloss":[{"w":"shell company","ar":"شركة وهمية (صورية)"},{"w":"nominee","ar":"شخص واجهة (مالك اسمي)"},{"w":"beneficial ownership","ar":"الملكية المنتفعة (المالك الفعلي)"},{"w":"forfeiture","ar":"مصادرة"},{"w":"sovereignty","ar":"السيادة"},{"w":"deterrent","ar":"رادع"},{"w":"procurement","ar":"المشتريات (الصفقات) العمومية"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Some legal systems cannot confiscate stolen assets if the suspect has died before being convicted.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: اشتراط الإدانة يجعل المصادرة مستحيلة إذا توفي المشتبه به."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer believes that registers of beneficial ownership have completely solved the problem of concealment.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: قيمتها تتوقف على التحقق من المعلومات وعلى من يحق له الاطلاع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most shell companies used to launder public money are registered in a small number of island territories.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر أماكن تسجيل أغلب الشركات الوهمية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Independent supervision of recovered funds has reduced the number of future thefts.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 4 تصف الإشراف المستقل دون أي ذكر لأثره على السرقات المستقبلية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer considers the preventive and the investigative approaches to be basically compatible.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: الموقفان أقل تعارضاً مما يبدوان، فبيانات الوقاية تفيد الوحدات."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Why courts hesitate to freeze foreign accounts","Controversy over how recovered money is spent","The cost of translating legal requests","How victim states punish former officials"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Shell companies, unlike genuine businesses, exist on ___ only.","a":["paper"],"why":"الفقرة 2: الشركات الوهمية موجودة على الورق فقط."},{"k":"gap","q":"Non-conviction-based forfeiture targets the property rather than the ___.","a":["person"],"why":"الفقرة 3: تستهدف الممتلكات لا الشخص."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's view of digital currencies?","o":["They have given criminals a decisive advantage.","They are impossible for investigators to study.","They can leave a lasting record that helps investigators.","They are used mainly by regulated banks."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 6: السجلات العامة دائمة، وقد أُعيد بناء معاملات قديمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main point of the final paragraph?","o":["Recovery of stolen assets has largely failed and should be abandoned.","The possible deterrent effect of recovery efforts may be their greatest benefit.","Investigators should concentrate on training rather than precedents.","Low recovery rates are mainly caused by inaccurate estimates."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 7: أثر الردع قد يفوق قيمة المبالغ المستردة."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص صعوبات تتبع الأموال العامة المسروقة عبر الحدود، من إخفاء الملكية والعقبات القانونية إلى مصير الأموال المستردة. ويخلص إلى أن التقدم جزئي، وأن أثر الردع قد يكون أهم من المبالغ المستردة."},{"id":"r-c1-11","l":"C1","title":"Can Scientific Results Be Repeated?","topic":"science","paras":["In recent years, a series of attempts to repeat published experiments has unsettled several fields. Teams of researchers, working independently, set out to re-run studies whose findings had been widely cited, and in a troubling proportion of cases they obtained smaller effects or none at all. The phrase reproducibility crisis entered the vocabulary, and it has been used so freely that its meaning has blurred. Before asking how serious the problem is, it is worth establishing what a failure to reproduce actually shows.","The first step is to separate two ideas that are often confused. A result is reproducible when another analyst, given the same data and the same code, arrives at the same numbers. It is replicable when a fresh study, with new participants or materials, finds the same pattern. A failed replication does not automatically mean that the original claim was false. The new sample may differ in age, culture or season; the equipment may be calibrated differently; a crucial condition may have gone unrecorded. Equally, a successful replication can mislead if both studies share the same hidden flaw. The evidence is informative, but it is never a verdict. Careful researchers therefore report exactly how a repetition differed from the original.","Why, then, do so many published findings fail to hold? Statistical weakness is the most frequently cited cause. Studies with small samples produce unstable estimates, and researchers who test many hypotheses on the same data will find some patterns that look significant purely by chance. The problem is aggravated by the habits of publication. Journals prefer novel, positive results, so experiments that show no effect often remain unpublished, a phenomenon known as the file-drawer problem. Researchers respond rationally to incentives: careers advance on the number of striking papers produced, not on the number of careful checks carried out.","Reformers have proposed a range of remedies. Under pre-registration, a team states its hypotheses and its analysis plan before collecting any data, which removes the temptation to adjust the analysis once results appear. In the format known as registered reports, a journal agrees to publish a paper on the strength of its method before the results are known, so that a null finding is as publishable as a dramatic one. Others emphasise open data and shared code, allowing anyone to check the calculations, and large collaborative projects in which many laboratories run the same protocol. Early signs suggest that such measures raise the proportion of findings that survive repetition.","These reforms have their critics, who raise objections more subtle than simple resistance to change. Rigid pre-registration, they argue, can discourage exploratory work, in which unexpected patterns suggest new questions; some of the most productive discoveries began as surprises. The administrative burden also falls unevenly, penalising small teams without technical support. Above all, a single standard may not suit every discipline. A laboratory experiment can be run again next week, but a survey of a rare habitat, or an observation of an unrepeatable astronomical event, cannot. Demanding identical repetition in such fields misunderstands what their evidence is.","A more optimistic reading holds that the whole episode demonstrates science working as intended, since the problem was detected by scientists themselves. There is truth in this, but it should not be pressed too far. Self-correction is neither automatic nor quick. Weak findings can persist for decades in textbooks, and in medicine or education they may shape practice long before anyone checks them. Correction happens only when someone is paid, praised or at least permitted to do the checking, and that is a matter of institutional design rather than of scientific character.","Perhaps the most useful lesson is therefore a modest one. The goal should not be perfect reproducibility, which no empirical field has ever possessed, but calibrated confidence: a habit of treating any single study as provisional, reporting uncertainty openly, and giving weight to results that several independent teams have obtained. Achieving that habit will require funders and universities to reward verification as generously as they now reward discovery."],"gloss":[{"w":"replicable","ar":"قابل للتكرار (إعادة الإنتاج)"},{"w":"calibrated","ar":"مُعايَر (مضبوط)"},{"w":"aggravated","ar":"تفاقمت (زاد سوءاً)"},{"w":"null finding","ar":"نتيجة صفرية (لا أثر)"},{"w":"exploratory","ar":"استكشافي"},{"w":"provisional","ar":"مؤقت (غير نهائي)"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"A failed replication does not necessarily prove that the original study was wrong.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: الفشل لا يعني تلقائياً أن الادعاء الأصلي خاطئ."},{"k":"tfng","q":"According to the writer, studies that find no effect are about as likely to be published as studies that find one.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: المجلات تفضل النتائج الإيجابية الجديدة، فتبقى النتائج الصفرية غير منشورة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Medical researchers are more likely than physicists to withhold their data from other teams.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص بين الأطباء والفيزيائيين في إتاحة البيانات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Pre-registration requires researchers to decide how they will analyse their data before it is collected.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: يحددون الفرضيات وخطة التحليل قبل جمع أي بيانات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The reforms described have already doubled the number of findings that survive repetition.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 4 تذكر مؤشرات مبكرة فقط دون أي نسبة محددة."},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Why exploratory research is more reliable than planned research","Objections to a single standard for every field","The cost of building large laboratories","How small teams outperform large ones"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Testing many hypotheses on the same data makes it likely that some will look significant by ___.","a":["chance"],"why":"الفقرة 3: أنماط تبدو دالة إحصائياً بمحض الصدفة."},{"k":"gap","q":"In registered reports, a journal agrees to publish on the strength of the ___ before results are known.","a":["method"],"why":"الفقرة 4: تلتزم المجلة بالنشر اعتماداً على المنهج قبل معرفة النتائج."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer suggest about the phrase reproducibility crisis?","o":["It describes the situation accurately.","It has been used so loosely that it lacks clarity.","It was invented by critics of science.","It applies only to medicine."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 1: استُعملت العبارة بحرية حتى اختلط معناها."},{"k":"mc","q":"The writer's attitude to the claim that the episode shows science correcting itself is that it","o":["is entirely mistaken.","is accurate but easily exaggerated.","should be ignored by funders.","applies only to textbooks."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 6: فيه حقيقة لكن لا ينبغي المبالغة فيه."}],"sumAr":"يميّز النص بين إعادة إنتاج النتائج وتكرار الدراسات، ويعرض أسباب ضعف بعض النتائج المنشورة والإصلاحات المقترحة والانتقادات الموجهة إليها. ويخلص إلى أن الهدف هو ثقة معايَرة بالنتائج، تدعمها حوافز تكافئ التحقق."},{"id":"r-c1-12","l":"C1","title":"Opening the Black Box: Explainable AI","topic":"ai","paras":["Automated systems now help to decide who receives a loan, which job applicants are interviewed and which patients are treated first. Many of the most accurate of these systems are built from layers of mathematical operations so numerous that even their designers cannot say, in ordinary language, why a particular input produced a particular output. Such models are commonly called black boxes. The field of explainable artificial intelligence has grown up in response, with the aim of making the reasoning of automated systems intelligible to the people who depend on them or are affected by them.","Three motives drive the field, and they do not always coincide. The first is accountability. A person refused a mortgage has a reasonable claim to know why, and a legal right to contest a decision is worthless if nobody can say what the decision rested on. The second is debugging. Explanations can reveal that a model has learned a shortcut: a program trained to recognise skin disease, for example, might have quietly learned to treat the presence of a ruler in the photograph as a sign of illness, because doctors tended to measure the worrying cases. The third is trust, though this is the most slippery of the three, for trust that is not earned by good performance is merely a feeling.","Broadly, two strategies exist. The first is to use models that are interpretable by design, such as short decision lists or scoring tables that a person can read from top to bottom. The second is to keep a complex model and add a post hoc explanation, a separate technique that highlights which parts of the input mattered most, or that states a counterfactual, such as the claim that the loan would have been approved had the applicant's income been somewhat higher. Counterfactuals are especially popular because they tell the affected person what could be done differently.","Sceptics point out a weakness in the second strategy. A post hoc explanation is itself a model of the original model, and it may describe its behaviour inaccurately. An explanation can be persuasive without being faithful, offering a tidy story that has little to do with the real computation. In high-stakes settings such as criminal justice or medicine, this critique concludes, we should not try to explain black boxes at all but should avoid them, relying on transparent models whose workings can be audited directly.","Defenders of complex models answer that the premise of this argument, that simpler models are nearly as accurate, holds for some kinds of data but not for others. With images, speech and free text, deep models outperform simple ones by a wide margin, and insisting on simplicity could cost lives in diagnosis. Still, the sceptics have a point that is often overlooked: for the structured, tabular data that dominates lending and administration, carefully designed simple models frequently come close to their complex rivals, which suggests that the supposed trade-off between accuracy and interpretability is sometimes exaggerated.","A further complication lies with the human recipient. An explanation is addressed to someone, and what satisfies a regulator will not help a patient. Experimental studies have found that people may place more confidence in a system that supplies reasons, even when the reasons are flawed. If so, explanation can make users less critical rather than more, the opposite of what was intended. The design of explanations therefore needs the methods of psychology as much as those of computer science. An explanation tested only on its authors, rather than on its intended readers, tells us very little about whether it works.","It follows that explanation should never be treated as a substitute for testing. Whatever technique is used, systems that affect people's lives still need independent audits, monitoring of their real-world outcomes and routes of appeal. The most productive question is perhaps not whether a model can be explained but what a particular explanation is for, who will read it, and what they will be able to do with it."],"gloss":[{"w":"intelligible","ar":"مفهوم (قابل للفهم)"},{"w":"accountability","ar":"المساءلة"},{"w":"counterfactual","ar":"افتراض مخالف للواقع"},{"w":"faithful","ar":"أمين (مطابق للأصل)"},{"w":"audit","ar":"تدقيق (مراجعة)"},{"w":"slippery","ar":"زلق (غامض)"},{"w":"outperform","ar":"يتفوق على"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"The designers of some highly accurate models are unable to explain simply why a given result was produced.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: حتى المصممون لا يستطيعون تفسير سبب مخرج معين بلغة عادية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer regards trust as the most reliable of the three motives for explainable AI.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: الثقة أكثر الدوافع غموضاً، وهي مجرد شعور إن لم تُكتسب بالأداء."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The skin-disease program described was withdrawn from use after its shortcut was discovered.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 2 تذكر المثال دون أي حديث عن سحب البرنامج."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Counterfactual explanations are popular because they suggest what an affected person might change.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 3: تخبر المتضرر بما يمكن فعله بشكل مختلف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Regulators in most countries now require that every automated decision be accompanied by a counterfactual explanation.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر شيئاً عن متطلبات الجهات التنظيمية في الدول."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["Why complex models are always more accurate","The case against explaining black boxes after the event","How loans are approved by human officers","Legal rights of job applicants"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Interpretable models, such as short decision lists, are described as being interpretable by ___.","a":["design"],"why":"الفقرة 3: نماذج قابلة للتفسير بحكم تصميمها."},{"k":"gap","q":"A post hoc explanation may be persuasive without being ___.","a":["faithful"],"why":"الفقرة 4: قد يكون التفسير مقنعاً دون أن يكون أميناً للحساب الحقيقي."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer say about simple models and tabular data?","o":["They are always far less accurate than deep models.","They are unsuitable for lending decisions.","They often perform almost as well as complex models.","They cannot be audited."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 5: النماذج البسيطة غالباً ما تقارب النماذج المعقدة في البيانات الجدولية."},{"k":"mc","q":"What concern about explanations does the sixth paragraph raise?","o":["They may make users less critical of the system.","They are too expensive to produce.","They are written only for regulators.","They slow down computers."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 6: قد يزيد التفسير ثقة المستخدمين حتى لو كانت الأسباب معيبة."}],"sumAr":"يناقش النص مسعى جعل قرارات الذكاء الاصطناعي مفهومة، ودوافعه وطرقه، ثم انتقادات التفسير اللاحق وحجج المدافعين عن النماذج المعقدة. ويخلص إلى أن التفسير لا يغني عن الاختبار والتدقيق المستقل."},{"id":"r-c1-13","l":"C1","title":"Why Did Humans Start to Write?","topic":"history","paras":["Writing is among the very few inventions that historians believe humans made more than once, quite independently. Most scholars accept that it arose separately in at least three regions of the ancient world, and perhaps more. Because the earliest examples were produced by communities with no knowledge of one another, they offer a rare opportunity to ask what pressures lead a society to create a system of written signs. The answer that emerges is far less romantic than many people expect.","The popular assumption is that writing began as a way of preserving speech: prayers, stories, songs or the words of rulers. The surviving evidence points elsewhere. The oldest large collections of written material from Mesopotamia are not poems but records of grain, livestock, textiles and labour. Tablets list how many sheep were delivered to a temple, how many workers received rations, and who owed what to whom. Literature appears much later, once the technology had already existed for several centuries. In this sense, writing began as a tool of accounting, and its first readers were administrators rather than poets.","The reasons are not hard to find. As settlements grew into cities, the quantity of goods passing through the hands of temples and palaces exceeded what any official could remember. Disputes over deliveries and debts needed a record that did not depend on a single person's memory or honesty. Some researchers argue that small clay tokens, used for centuries to represent units of goods, were the direct ancestors of the earliest signs, though this theory is disputed, and not every region shows such a progression. What is clear is that early writing was bound up with taxation, obligation and control; it was, among other things, an instrument of power.","A system that only counts goods, however, is not yet a means of expressing language. The decisive step was the use of what scholars call the rebus principle, in which a sign standing for an object is borrowed to represent its sound. In English, a drawing of a bee and a drawing of a leaf could be combined to suggest the word belief. Once signs could stand for sounds, names, verbs and abstract ideas became writable, and the system could grow beyond the inventory. Over time, the pictures became simplified into abstract strokes, which were faster to produce and which have long since ceased to resemble the things they once depicted.","Whether such early systems deserve the name of writing is itself debated. A narrow definition requires that a system be able to record any spoken sentence, which excludes knotted cords, notched sticks and many other devices used to store numbers and simple messages. A broader definition counts any conventional marks that convey meaning to a trained reader. The disagreement is not merely semantic, for the answer determines how many times writing was invented and, consequently, how special the achievement was. Neither position is plainly wrong; they answer different questions.","The consequences of writing were considerable, but they did not all arrive at once. Laws could be fixed and consulted rather than recalled, and agreements could be checked years after the people who made them had died. Some thinkers in antiquity feared that the new technology would weaken memory and create the appearance of wisdom without its substance. Yet for a long time reading and writing remained the skills of a small class of trained scribes, so that the first effect of literacy was to concentrate knowledge, not to spread it. Widespread literacy is a recent development in historical terms.","A final caution concerns the evidence itself. Clay tablets survive burial remarkably well, while papyrus, bark, wood and cloth decay. The earliest written records we possess may therefore be those made on the most durable materials, not the first ones made. Where a civilisation wrote on perishable surfaces, its earliest experiments may have vanished entirely. Any confident account of how and why writing began should be read with this limitation in mind: it describes what has been preserved, which is not necessarily what happened."],"gloss":[{"w":"administrator","ar":"موظف إداري"},{"w":"obligation","ar":"التزام"},{"w":"rebus","ar":"رمز صوتي (استعمال الصورة لصوتها)"},{"w":"scribe","ar":"كاتب (ناسخ)"},{"w":"perishable","ar":"قابل للتلف"},{"w":"depict","ar":"يصوّر (يمثّل)"},{"w":"antiquity","ar":"العصور القديمة"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"The oldest large collections of written material from Mesopotamia were mainly poetic works.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: أقدم المجموعات الكبيرة سجلات للحبوب والماشية والعمل لا قصائد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"All researchers agree that clay tokens were the direct ancestors of the earliest written signs.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 3: هذه النظرية محل خلاف ولا تنطبق على كل منطقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Early writing in Mesopotamia was invented by priests rather than by merchants.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يحدد هوية من اخترع الكتابة، فقط أن قراءها الأوائل إداريون."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that the number of independent inventions of writing depends on how writing is defined.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: التعريف يحدد عدد مرات اختراع الكتابة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In the early period, widespread literacy made knowledge more evenly shared.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 6: الأمية الواسعة سادت، وتركزت المعرفة في فئة صغيرة من الكتّاب."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["How signs came to represent sounds as well as objects","The influence of poets on early officials","Why pictures were abandoned by every civilisation","The role of tokens in taxation"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"The earliest written records were produced by administrators, not by ___.","a":["poets"],"why":"الفقرة 2: قراؤها الأوائل إداريون لا شعراء."},{"k":"gap","q":"Papyrus, bark, wood and cloth all ___ over time, unlike clay.","a":["decay"],"why":"الفقرة 7: هذه المواد تتحلل بينما ينجو الطين من الدفن."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer imply about early writing in the third paragraph?","o":["It was mainly designed to entertain rulers.","It was closely linked to systems of authority and obligation.","It was developed to replace spoken languages.","It could be read by almost everyone."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: ارتبطت الكتابة بالضرائب والالتزام والسيطرة، أي بأداة سلطة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's main caution in the last paragraph?","o":["Clay tablets have been wrongly dated.","Accounts of origins may reflect what survived, not what occurred.","Perishable materials were never used for writing.","Early scribes deliberately destroyed their records."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 7: الرواية تصف ما حُفظ وليس بالضرورة ما حدث."}],"sumAr":"يبيّن النص أن الكتابة نشأت في بداياتها أداةً للمحاسبة والإدارة لا للأدب، ثم تطورت حين صارت الرموز تمثل الأصوات. ويحذّر من أن الأدلة الباقية، وخاصة الطين، قد لا تعكس كل ما حدث فعلاً."},{"id":"r-c1-14","l":"C1","title":"Nudging Citizens: Behavioural Insights in Policy","topic":"government","paras":["For much of the twentieth century, public policy assumed that citizens respond to two things: rules and prices. If a government wished to reduce smoking, it banned it in public places or raised the tax. In recent decades, a third instrument has gained favour. Behavioural insights, drawn from psychology and economics, suggest that people are also strongly influenced by how a choice is presented, and that small changes in design can shift behaviour without forbidding anything or altering anyone's financial incentives. Governments in many countries have established small teams to apply this idea, popularly known as nudging.","The central observation is that human beings do not behave like the perfectly calculating decision-makers of simple economic models. They are busy, distracted and prone to inertia. The effect of defaults illustrates this well. When employees are enrolled in a pension scheme automatically, and must complete a form to leave it, far more of them end up saving than when they must complete a form to join. Nobody has been compelled to do anything, and everyone remains free to choose otherwise; the only thing that has changed is which option requires effort.","Other applications are equally modest. A tax authority might add a sentence to a reminder letter noting that most citizens in the recipient's area have already paid. A hospital might simplify an appointment form, or send a text message on the morning of a booking. A canteen might place fruit at eye level and sweets on a lower shelf. What these measures share is low cost and, above all, testability: because each can be compared with the old version in a randomised trial, advocates claim that behavioural policy is more evidence-based than much conventional policy.","The most common objection is ethical. If a government deliberately steers behaviour by exploiting the quirks of human psychology, critics say, it is manipulating its citizens, however benevolent its aims. Defenders respond that choices must be presented in some way, so that there is no neutral design to which governments could retreat; a form with a hidden default is as much an architecture as one with a helpful default. On this view, the real question is not whether to influence but who does so and for what purpose. One practical safeguard that has been suggested is a publicity test: a nudge is acceptable only if the authorities could disclose exactly how it works without weakening its effect.","A second line of criticism is empirical. Effects measured in small experiments are frequently weaker when a programme is extended to a whole country, and some fade as people grow accustomed to the prompt. Studies with striking results are more likely to be published than those with ordinary ones, which may flatter the field's record. More fundamentally, some critics argue that nudges are attractive to governments precisely because they are cheap, and that they can become a way of appearing to act on problems such as obesity or pollution which really require taxation or regulation. They accuse the approach of treating social problems as the failings of individuals.","Advocates do not deny that nudges have limits. Most describe them as a complement to law and spending, not a replacement, and point out that an inexpensive measure which works modestly is still better than none. Context also matters. A default that succeeds where citizens trust their institutions may fail where trust is low; a message highlighting that others pay their taxes may backfire if people know that many do not. For countries seeking to strengthen integrity in public service, this is an important warning: behavioural tools cannot substitute for institutions that deserve to be trusted.","Perhaps the fairest conclusion is that behavioural insights are a useful addition to the policymaker's toolkit, not a revolution. They are most convincing when used openly, tested honestly and reported fully, including the failures. Used in that way, they can make public services easier to use at little expense. Used as a substitute for difficult decisions, they invite exactly the scepticism that their critics express."],"gloss":[{"w":"inertia","ar":"جمود (ميل للبقاء على الحال)"},{"w":"default","ar":"الخيار الافتراضي"},{"w":"randomised trial","ar":"تجربة عشوائية"},{"w":"benevolent","ar":"حسن النية"},{"w":"safeguard","ar":"ضمانة (إجراء وقائي)"},{"w":"backfire","ar":"يأتي بنتيجة عكسية"},{"w":"complement","ar":"مكمّل"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Behavioural insights seek to change behaviour mainly by altering the prices people pay.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 1: تغيّر السلوك دون تغيير الحوافز المالية، عبر تصميم الخيار."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Automatic enrolment in a pension scheme leaves employees free to leave it.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: لم يُجبر أحد، والجميع يبقى حراً في الاختيار."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Governments that use nudging have reduced the cost of public healthcare.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر أي أثر على تكلفة الرعاية الصحية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Defenders claim that a government can avoid influencing choices by designing forms neutrally.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: لا يوجد تصميم محايد يمكن أن تلجأ إليه الحكومات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Nudges tested in small experiments always work equally well on a national scale.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: الأثر كثيراً ما يضعف عند التوسع إلى البلد كله."},{"k":"head","para":4,"o":["Doubts about how well nudges actually work","The history of taxation in modern states","Why citizens dislike official letters","The success of hospital reminders"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Behavioural policy is claimed to be more evidence-based because each measure can be compared with the old one in a randomised ___.","a":["trial"],"why":"الفقرة 3: إمكانية الاختبار في تجربة عشوائية."},{"k":"gap","q":"The suggested publicity test asks whether a nudge could be disclosed without weakening its ___.","a":["effect"],"why":"الفقرة 4: يُقبل التوجيه إن أمكن الكشف عن آليته دون إضعاف أثره."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do some critics think governments favour nudges?","o":["They are cheap and may replace harder measures.","They are always supported by voters.","They are required by international law.","They are more profitable than taxes."],"a":0,"why":"الفقرة 5: جذابة لأنها رخيصة وقد تحل محل الضرائب والتنظيم."},{"k":"mc","q":"What warning does the writer draw for countries strengthening integrity in public service?","o":["Behavioural tools are too expensive for them.","Nudges work equally everywhere.","Behavioural tools cannot replace trustworthy institutions.","Reminder letters should be abandoned."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 6: الأدوات السلوكية لا تحل محل مؤسسات جديرة بالثقة."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص فكرة الدفع السلوكي في السياسات العامة وأمثلتها، ثم يعرض الاعتراضات الأخلاقية والتجريبية عليها وردود المدافعين. ويخلص إلى أنها أداة مفيدة مكمّلة، لا بديلاً عن القرارات الصعبة ولا عن المؤسسات الجديرة بالثقة."},{"id":"r-c1-15","l":"C1","title":"Digital Evidence on Trial","topic":"law","paras":["A generation ago, a trial turned on documents, objects and the memories of witnesses. Today it is just as likely to depend on text messages, location records, photographs taken with phones and footage from street cameras. This digital evidence is often more detailed than anything a witness can recall, and it can establish who was where at what time with a precision that earlier courts could not have imagined. But the rules by which courts decide what to admit and how much to believe were written for a world of paper, and adapting them has proved harder than it first appeared.","The first problem is authenticity. A printed page can be examined for signs of forgery, but a file has no obvious physical features, and a screenshot of a conversation can be created in seconds by anyone with basic editing software. Courts therefore look beyond the content to the surrounding data: the metadata recording when a file was created and changed, the unique digital fingerprint known as a hash value, which alters if even a single character is modified, and the chain of custody, the documented record of everyone who handled the material. Gaps in that chain do not always make evidence inadmissible, but they give the opposing side a powerful argument that it should carry little weight.","A second difficulty is that digital material is fragile and plentiful at the same time. Simply opening a file or switching on a seized phone can change what it contains, so forensic examiners work from exact copies made with devices that prevent any alteration of the original. Meanwhile, the volume of data on a single phone may run to millions of items, far more than any lawyer can read. Much of it now sits not on the device but on servers abroad, which raises the same cross-border questions of jurisdiction and cooperation that complicate so many modern investigations.","Fabrication has become a problem of a different order with the arrival of convincing synthetic images, audio and video. The danger is usually described as the courtroom acceptance of a fake. Some legal scholars suggest that an equally serious danger runs the other way: once people know that anything can be forged, a guilty party may claim that genuine footage is artificial, and ordinary jurors may find it hard to disprove. This reversal has been called the liar's dividend. Courts are responding by calling on technical experts, but expert analysis costs money, and the party with the greater resources can afford the better challenge.","Fairness raises further questions. Investigators increasingly use specialised software to extract or analyse data, and the makers of such tools often treat their methods as commercial secrets. A defendant who cannot examine how a result was produced is poorly placed to challenge it, which strains the principle that both sides should have a realistic opportunity to contest the case against them. Some legal systems also begin from a presumption that computers operate correctly unless shown otherwise, a rule that critics consider unrealistic, since it places on the defendant the burden of proving a fault in a system he has never seen.","Privacy is the third pressure point. Searching a house, in the traditional view, discloses only what is in its rooms. A modern phone, by contrast, holds correspondence, health records, financial details and a record of its owner's movements, so that a search of it may reveal a whole life. Many courts have concluded that the old standards for warrants are insufficient and have required that searches be limited to material relevant to the suspected offence, though how such limits can be enforced in practice remains uncertain.","It would be easy to conclude that digital evidence demands entirely new principles of law. A more persuasive view is that the established principles of relevance, reliability and fairness remain sound, but that applying them now requires judges, lawyers and juries with a basic technical literacy. The task is less to rewrite the rules than to ensure that those who apply them understand what they are looking at."],"gloss":[{"w":"authenticity","ar":"الأصالة (صحة النسبة)"},{"w":"metadata","ar":"البيانات الوصفية"},{"w":"chain of custody","ar":"سلسلة الحيازة (تتبع المحرز)"},{"w":"inadmissible","ar":"غير مقبول (كدليل)"},{"w":"jurisdiction","ar":"الاختصاص القضائي"},{"w":"synthetic","ar":"مصطنع"},{"w":"warrant","ar":"إذن قضائي (بالتفتيش)"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Digital evidence can sometimes show a person's location more precisely than witnesses can.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: يحدد من كان أين ومتى بدقة لم تعرفها المحاكم السابقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A gap in the chain of custody always makes digital evidence inadmissible.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: الفجوات لا تجعل الدليل غير مقبول دائماً، لكنها تضعف وزنه."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Most deepfake videos presented in court have been created by foreign governments.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يذكر مصدر المقاطع المزيفة المقدمة أمام المحاكم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Some scholars believe that a guilty person might wrongly describe real footage as fake.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: قد يدّعي المذنب أن اللقطات الحقيقية مصطنعة (عائد الكاذب)."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer approves of the rule that computers are presumed to work correctly.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: يرى النقاد أن القاعدة غير واقعية، والكاتب يعرضها بوصفها مشكلة."},{"k":"head","para":5,"o":["Difficulties of searching a phone compared with a house","Why police prefer paper records","The decline of warrants in modern courts","How phones record health data"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"A hash value changes if even a single ___ in the file is modified.","a":["character"],"why":"الفقرة 2: بصمة التجزئة تتغير إذا عُدّل حرف واحد."},{"k":"gap","q":"Forensic examiners work from exact ___ so that the original is not altered.","a":["copies"],"why":"الفقرة 3: يعملون على نسخ مطابقة تمنع تغيير الأصل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the writer mention commercial secrets in the fifth paragraph?","o":["To show that investigators work too slowly","To suggest that defendants may be unable to test how a result was obtained","To argue that software should be banned in court","To explain why phones are expensive"],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: عدم القدرة على فحص طريقة النتيجة تُضعف حق الدفاع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's conclusion about legal principles?","o":["They are obsolete and must be replaced.","They are sound but need technically literate users.","They apply only to paper documents.","They have been improved by synthetic media."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 7: المبادئ سليمة لكن تطبيقها يحتاج ثقافة تقنية أساسية."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص تحديات الأدلة الرقمية أمام المحاكم: التوثق من الأصالة، وهشاشة البيانات وكثرتها، والتزييف العميق، وعدالة الفحص، والخصوصية. ويرى أن المبادئ القانونية القائمة صالحة، لكنها تحتاج قضاة ومحامين يفهمون التقنية."},{"id":"r-c1-16","l":"C1","title":"Learning to Live with a Changing Climate","topic":"environment","paras":["For many years, discussion of climate change concentrated almost exclusively on mitigation, the effort to reduce the emissions that drive warming. Talk of adaptation, the adjustment of societies to conditions that have already changed or are certain to change, was often regarded with suspicion, as if planning for the consequences amounted to surrendering the fight to prevent them. That attitude has faded. Even under the most ambitious reductions in emissions, some further change is already locked in by the gases in the atmosphere, and communities from river deltas to mountain villages are being forced to respond whether or not they have been given the means to do so.","Adaptation takes many forms, which can be grouped broadly into three kinds. The most visible is hard infrastructure: sea walls, larger drains, raised roads and reservoirs. A second kind works with natural systems, as when coastal mangroves are restored to absorb the energy of waves, wetlands are protected to hold floodwater, or trees are planted to cool crowded districts. The third is institutional and behavioural: early-warning systems, flexible insurance, revised building codes, and changes in what farmers plant and when. Often the least conspicuous of these measures turn out to be the most cost-effective.","No single solution suits every place. A technique that protects a low-lying city may be irrelevant to a dry upland region where the difficulty is too little water, not too much. This is one reason that local knowledge matters. Farmers who have observed shifting rainfall for decades often hold detailed understanding of which crops and planting dates still work, and programmes that begin by consulting them tend to fare better than those imposed from outside. It does not follow that traditional methods are always sufficient; when conditions move beyond anything experienced in living memory, past experience becomes a less reliable guide.","The most serious risk in adaptation planning is maladaptation, meaning an action that reduces vulnerability in the short term but increases it elsewhere or later. Widespread air conditioning cools buildings, but the additional electricity it requires may add to emissions and strain power grids during the very heatwaves it is meant to endure. A sea wall may protect one town while accelerating erosion at its neighbour. Irrigation can rescue a failing harvest while draining the underground reserves on which future harvests depend. Such outcomes are seldom intended; they arise because the effects of a measure are judged over too small an area or too short a period.","Questions of fairness are inseparable from the subject. The people most exposed to climate hazards are frequently those who have contributed least to the problem and who possess the fewest resources to respond. Financing is a further obstacle. The benefits of adaptation consist of harm that does not occur, which is hard to measure and does not generate revenue, so private investors tend to prefer projects that deliver visible returns. As a result, funding for adaptation has consistently lagged behind funding for mitigation, a gap that many governments of vulnerable countries regard as unjust.","There are also limits to what adaptation can achieve. Some losses cannot be offset by engineering or planning: a coral reef that has bleached, a way of life tied to a particular shoreline, or a sacred or ancestral landscape. Managed retreat, in which communities are helped to relocate from places that can no longer be protected, is therefore a delicate subject. Planners may regard it as the rational response to rising seas, but residents often feel that it asks them to give up not merely property but identity. Treating relocation as a technical matter risks overlooking why people resist it.","What, then, does good adaptation look like? Increasingly, specialists favour approaches that assume uncertainty rather than ignore it, building flexibility into plans so that they can be adjusted as conditions become clearer. A flood defence designed so that it can be raised later is preferable to one built to a single prediction. Adaptation and mitigation, in this view, are not rivals for the same resources but two halves of a single strategy: the less warming is allowed to occur, the smaller and cheaper the task of adjusting to it will be."],"gloss":[{"w":"mitigation","ar":"التخفيف (من الانبعاثات)"},{"w":"adaptation","ar":"التكيّف"},{"w":"maladaptation","ar":"التكيّف الخاطئ"},{"w":"conspicuous","ar":"بارز (ملفت)"},{"w":"vulnerability","ar":"الهشاشة (قابلية التضرر)"},{"w":"retreat","ar":"انسحاب (تراجع)"},{"w":"erosion","ar":"تآكل (تعرية)"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Adaptation was at one time viewed by some people as a sign of giving up on prevention.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 1: كان يُنظر إليه بريبة كأنه استسلام في معركة الوقاية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that the most visible adaptation measures are always the cheapest.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: أقل الإجراءات وضوحاً هي غالباً الأكثر جدوى من حيث التكلفة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Mangrove restoration has protected more coastal cities than sea walls have.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يقارن عدد المدن التي حمتها المانغروف والجدران البحرية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Maladaptation usually results from a deliberate decision to harm neighbouring communities.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: النتائج نادراً ما تكون مقصودة، وتنشأ من تقييم ضيق النطاق."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Funding for adaptation has generally been lower than funding for mitigation.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: تمويل التكيّف ظل متأخراً عن تمويل التخفيف."},{"k":"head","para":3,"o":["When protective measures cause new problems","The advantages of air conditioning in cities","How neighbouring towns cooperate on defences","Farmers who ignore the weather"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"Farmers who have watched rainfall change often know which crops and planting ___ still work.","a":["dates"],"why":"الفقرة 3: يعرفون المحاصيل ومواعيد الزراعة التي لا تزال ناجحة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The benefits of adaptation consist of ___ that does not occur.","a":["harm"],"why":"الفقرة 5: فوائد التكيّف هي ضرر لا يقع، يصعب قياسه."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the writer say that traditional methods may not be enough?","o":["Farmers do not trust local knowledge.","Conditions may move beyond anything experienced before.","Traditional methods are too expensive.","Governments forbid their use."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: حين تتجاوز الظروف الخبرة المعروفة تصبح التجارب السابقة أقل موثوقية."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the final paragraph, adaptation and mitigation are","o":["competing for the same limited resources.","two parts of a single strategy.","unrelated to one another.","equally unpredictable."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 7: ليسا متنافسين بل نصفان لاستراتيجية واحدة."}],"sumAr":"يعرض النص أنواع التكيّف مع تغير المناخ، ومخاطر التكيّف الخاطئ، وقضايا العدالة والتمويل، وحدود ما يمكن التكيّف معه. ويرى أن الخطط الناجحة مرنة وتعمل مع التخفيف من الانبعاثات لا ضده."},{"id":"r-c1-17","l":"C1","title":"The Economy of Attention","topic":"media","paras":["Every era has its scarce resource. In agricultural societies it was land; in industrial ones, capital and fuel. Some economists now argue that in a world where information is overabundant the scarcest resource is human attention. A message or a video costs almost nothing to produce and distribute, but each person has only a limited number of waking hours in which to notice it. The idea that attention can be treated as something to be competed for, captured and sold has given rise to the expression attention economy, and to a lively dispute about whether the arrangement serves the people whose attention is at stake.","The business logic is simple. Many of the most widely used digital services cost the user nothing. They are paid for by advertisers, who in turn pay according to how long people stay and how often they return. Success is therefore measured in engagement: minutes spent, items viewed, notifications opened. Designers have responded with features that are now familiar, such as feeds that never end, automatic playback of the next video, and alerts timed to bring people back. Many of these draw on research into variable rewards, the finding that an uncertain payoff holds attention more firmly than a predictable one.","Critics see in this a systematic exploitation of psychological weakness. They argue that products engineered to maximise time spent are bound to undermine concentration, interfere with sleep and encourage comparison with others. A more structural concern is that content provoking strong emotion tends to hold attention longer than content that informs calmly, so that systems optimised for engagement may amplify outrage and promote division without anyone having intended it. On this reading, the problem lies not in the wickedness of individual companies but in the incentive built into the model.","Others consider the alarm exaggerated. Each new medium, from the printed novel to television, was accused in its day of corroding the minds of its audience, and the predictions were largely mistaken. Users, they point out, gain real benefits from free access to knowledge, communication and entertainment, and most appear to decide for themselves how to spend their time. The strongest version of this defence also notes that the term addiction is sometimes used loosely, and that treating a habit as a disease can shift responsibility away from the person who has it.","The research evidence is more mixed than either side usually admits. Many studies link heavy use with poorer wellbeing, but most are correlational, and the direction of cause is unclear: people who are already lonely or anxious may spend longer online, rather than the other way round. Findings also differ according to the manner of use. Active communication with friends appears to carry different consequences from passive scrolling through strangers' posts. Averages conceal wide variation, and a measure that helps one group may do little for another. Honest reading of the evidence supports caution, not certainty.","Policy proposals reflect these uncertainties. Some would regulate design directly, requiring that automatic playback be switched off by default, or that young users be offered time limits. Others focus on transparency, demanding that platforms explain how their recommendation systems rank content and allow independent researchers to examine them. A third group favours paid, advertisement-free alternatives that would change the incentive itself. Each proposal draws objections. Defaults can be dismissed as paternalistic, transparency is difficult to enforce, and subscription models may leave poorer users with the more intrusive option.","Beneath the argument lies a question about who ought to control attention. In a conventional market, a firm succeeds by offering something the customer wants. In an attention market, the customer is partly the product, and the firm may succeed by encouraging behaviour that the user would, on reflection, prefer to avoid. That distinction, between what people choose in the moment and what they would endorse afterwards, may prove more useful for judging the system than any single statistic about screen time."],"gloss":[{"w":"scarce","ar":"نادر (شحيح)"},{"w":"engagement","ar":"التفاعل (الانشغال)"},{"w":"amplify","ar":"يضخّم"},{"w":"correlational","ar":"ارتباطي (قائم على الترابط)"},{"w":"paternalistic","ar":"أبوي (وصائي)"},{"w":"intrusive","ar":"متطفّل (مُقحم)"},{"w":"corrode","ar":"يُفسد (يُتلف تدريجياً)"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"Digital services that are free to users are usually financed by advertisers.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: تموّلها جهات الإعلان التي تدفع بحسب مدة بقاء الناس."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer states that every new medium has in the end proved harmful to its audience.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 4: التنبؤات بإفساد العقول كانت في الغالب خاطئة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Younger users spend more time on automatic playback features than older users do.","a":"NG","why":"لا يقارن النص بين استعمال الصغار والكبار لهذه الميزة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer suggests that the evidence on heavy use and poor wellbeing does not clearly show what causes what.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 5: معظم الدراسات ارتباطية واتجاه السبب غير واضح."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Subscription alternatives have been shown to reduce the time people spend online.","a":"NG","why":"الفقرة 6 تقترحها فقط ولا تذكر أي أثر مثبت."},{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["The argument that engagement-driven design damages users and public debate","Why advertisers prefer calm content","How television harmed earlier audiences","The benefits of sleep for concentration"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"An uncertain payoff holds attention more firmly than a ___ one.","a":["predictable"],"why":"الفقرة 2: المكافأة غير المؤكدة تشدّ الانتباه أكثر من المتوقعة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Active communication with friends may have different consequences from ___ scrolling.","a":["passive"],"why":"الفقرة 5: التواصل النشط يختلف أثره عن التصفح السلبي."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the writer's attitude to the evidence about technology and wellbeing?","o":["It proves that heavy use is harmful.","It supports caution rather than certainty.","It shows that there is no problem.","It is too old to be useful."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 5: القراءة الأمينة للأدلة تدعم الحذر لا اليقين."},{"k":"mc","q":"What point is made in the final paragraph?","o":["Customers always benefit from attention markets.","The distinction between momentary choice and considered preference is useful.","Screen time statistics are the best judgement tool.","Firms never intend to hold attention."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 7: التمييز بين اختيار اللحظة وما يقرّه المرء لاحقاً أنفع من أي إحصاء."}],"sumAr":"يشرح النص اقتصاد الانتباه وكيف تصمَّم المنتجات الرقمية لزيادة التفاعل، ويعرض انتقادات الخبراء وردود المدافعين. ويبيّن أن الأدلة مختلطة وأن السياسات المقترحة تواجه اعتراضات، وأن المعيار الأفضل هو ما يقرّه المستخدم بعد التفكير."},{"id":"r-c1-18","l":"C1","title":"What Counts as Proof?","topic":"science","paras":["Scientists and lawyers both claim to reach conclusions by weighing evidence, and in everyday speech the word proof is used for the result of either process. Yet the two professions use the word very differently, and the difference is not merely a matter of vocabulary. It reflects distinct purposes, distinct tolerances for error, and distinct ideas of when it is acceptable to stop asking questions. Looking at them side by side shows how much the meaning of proof depends on what a decision is for.","In the natural sciences, proof in the strict sense is rare; it belongs mainly to mathematics. Empirical theories are supported by evidence, but they are never conclusively demonstrated, since a new observation may always contradict them. Philosophers of science have therefore stressed that a good theory is one that could, in principle, be shown wrong and has so far survived attempts to do so. Confidence grows when independent methods, applied by different teams, converge on the same conclusion. Statistical thresholds help to decide when a pattern is unlikely to be an accident, but they are conventions, not laws of nature, and sensible scientists treat them accordingly.","A court cannot suspend judgement. A case must be decided, and decided within a reasonable period, so the law has developed explicit standards of proof. In a civil dispute, the claimant generally need only show that their version is more probable than not. In a criminal trial, the prosecution must establish guilt beyond reasonable doubt. These standards are not scientific measures but moral choices. They express a view about which mistake is worse: convicting an innocent person is judged more serious than acquitting a guilty one, and the demand for proof is raised to reflect that judgement.","The two systems also differ in what they permit as evidence. Science welcomes every relevant observation, and an inconvenient result is as valuable as a convenient one. Law, by contrast, excludes certain material for reasons unconnected with its truth. Evidence obtained by unlawful means may be inadmissible, hearsay is usually restricted, and some communications, such as those between a client and a lawyer, are protected. These rules sometimes keep reliable information from the decision-maker, but they serve values that the legal system rates above accuracy in the individual case, such as the integrity of the process and the protection of rights.","Friction arises where the two meet, notably in expert testimony. Courts have attempted to screen scientific evidence by asking whether a technique has been tested, whether its error rate is known and whether it is accepted in the relevant field. Yet some forensic methods were used for decades before such questions were put to them, and when scrutiny came, it showed that certain claims of certainty could not be supported. Scientists are also uneasy when the absence of proof of harm is treated by a court as proof of safety, or when a tentative finding is presented to a jury as settled fact.","Statistics provides a further source of misunderstanding. Suppose a trace found at a crime scene matches a suspect, and the chance of a random person matching is one in a million. It is tempting to conclude that there is only a one-in-a-million chance that the suspect is innocent. That reasoning confuses two different probabilities, an error often called the prosecutor's fallacy. In a city of ten million people, roughly ten innocent residents would be expected to match as well, so the match alone says little unless other evidence points to the accused. Judges and juries are rarely trained to detect such slips, and eloquent experts may not warn them.","Each discipline could learn from the other. Law might borrow from science a greater readiness to admit uncertainty and to revise settled practices when they are shown to be unreliable. Science might borrow from law the habit of stating in advance how much evidence is enough and who must supply it. Proof, in the end, is not a single thing that evidence either possesses or lacks. It is a judgement about sufficiency, made against a background of purposes and values, and it is wise to ask which standard is being applied before accepting that something has been proved."],"gloss":[{"w":"tolerance","ar":"درجة التسامح (تحمّل)"},{"w":"empirical","ar":"تجريبي"},{"w":"converge","ar":"تتقارب (تلتقي)"},{"w":"acquit","ar":"يُبرّئ"},{"w":"hearsay","ar":"شهادة السماع"},{"w":"forensic","ar":"جنائي (قضائي علمي)"},{"w":"fallacy","ar":"مغالطة"}],"qs":[{"k":"tfng","q":"According to the writer, proof in the strict sense is found mainly in mathematics rather than in empirical science.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 2: الإثبات بالمعنى الدقيق نادر في العلوم الطبيعية ويخص الرياضيات."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The writer says that statistical thresholds in science are natural laws.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 2: هي اتفاقات وليست قوانين طبيعة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Standards of proof in criminal trials are higher in countries with a jury system.","a":"NG","why":"النص لا يربط مستوى الإثبات بوجود هيئة المحلفين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The law sometimes excludes reliable evidence in order to protect other values.","a":"T","why":"الفقرة 4: قواعد الاستبعاد قد تحجب معلومات موثوقة لحماية قيم أخرى."},{"k":"tfng","q":"All forensic methods have been shown to be unreliable.","a":"F","why":"الفقرة 5: بعض الطرق فقط تبيّن أن ادعاءات اليقين فيها غير مدعومة."},{"k":"head","para":2,"o":["Why courts need standards of proof and what they express","The failure of lawyers to understand mathematics","How scientists decide to stop work","Why civil cases are more important than criminal ones"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"In a criminal trial, the prosecution must establish guilt beyond reasonable ___.","a":["doubt"],"why":"الفقرة 3: معيار الإثبات الجنائي هو ما وراء الشك المعقول."},{"k":"gap","q":"Confusing two different probabilities is often called the prosecutor's ___.","a":["fallacy"],"why":"الفقرة 6: الخلط بين احتمالين يُسمى مغالطة الادعاء."},{"k":"mc","q":"According to the third paragraph, standards of proof in law are","o":["measurements taken from laboratory studies.","moral choices about which error is worse.","designed to make convictions easier.","identical in civil and criminal cases."],"a":1,"why":"الفقرة 3: هي اختيارات أخلاقية حول أيّ الخطأين أسوأ."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the writer conclude about the meaning of proof?","o":["It is the same in science and in law.","It should be removed from legal vocabulary.","It is a judgement about sufficiency that depends on purposes and values.","It is easier to achieve in law than in mathematics."],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة 7: الإثبات حكم على الكفاية في ضوء الأغراض والقيم."}],"sumAr":"يقارن النص بين معنى الإثبات في العلم وفي القانون: في العلم الدعم تدريجي وقابل للمراجعة، وفي القانون معايير صريحة تعبّر عن خيارات أخلاقية. ويخلص إلى أن الإثبات حكم على الكفاية يعتمد على الغرض، وأن كلاً منهما يستفيد من الآخر."}]);
Fasih.add("listening",[{"id":"l-a2-01","l":"A2","title":"Booking a table for a family lunch","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, Garden Cafe. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Hello. I would like to book a table for Sunday lunch, please."},{"s":"A","t":"Of course. How many people will there be?"},{"s":"B","t":"Six people. We are four adults and two children."},{"s":"A","t":"And what time would you like to come?"},{"s":"B","t":"At half past twelve, if possible."},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, that is fine. We have a nice table by the window. Can I have your name, please?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, it is Karim Benali. Karim is K, A, R, I, M. Benali is B, E, N, A, L, I."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, Mr Benali. Do you need anything special?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, one high chair, please. My niece is only one year old."},{"s":"A","t":"No problem. I will write that down. We will see you on Sunday."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you very much. Goodbye."}],"gloss":[{"w":"high chair","ar":"كرسي طفل مرتفع"},{"w":"book a table","ar":"احجز طاولة"},{"w":"by the window","ar":"بجانب النافذة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which day does the family want to come?","o":["Saturday","Sunday","Friday"],"a":1,"why":"قال: Sunday lunch."},{"k":"gap","q":"How many people will come? ___","a":["6","six"],"why":"قال: Six people."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time is the booking?","o":["12.00","12.30","1.30"],"a":1,"why":"قال: half past twelve أي 12:30."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where is the table?","o":["Near the door","In the garden","By the window"],"a":2,"why":"ذكرت الموظفة طاولة بجانب النافذة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The family name is ___.","a":["Benali"],"why":"تهجئة الاسم: B, E, N, A, L, I."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The family needs a high chair for a baby.","a":"T","why":"طلب كرسياً مرتفعاً لابنة أخته الصغيرة."}]},{"id":"l-a2-02","l":"A2","title":"Asking for directions to the station","part":1,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Excuse me. Can you help me, please? I am looking for the train station."},{"s":"B","t":"Sure. It is not far from here. Do you see the big bank on the corner?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, I do."},{"s":"B","t":"Go past the bank and turn left at the traffic lights."},{"s":"A","t":"Turn left at the lights. Okay."},{"s":"B","t":"Then walk along Park Street for about five minutes. You will see a small library on your right."},{"s":"A","t":"A library on my right. Good."},{"s":"B","t":"The station is just after the library, opposite the bus stop."},{"s":"A","t":"Is it better to walk or to take a bus?"},{"s":"B","t":"Walk. The bus is slow at this time, and it costs two dollars."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you so much for your help."},{"s":"B","t":"You are welcome. Have a nice day!"}],"gloss":[{"w":"traffic lights","ar":"إشارة المرور"},{"w":"opposite","ar":"مقابل"},{"w":"on your right","ar":"على يمينك"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is on the corner near the speaker?","o":["A bank","A library","A bus stop"],"a":0,"why":"قال: the big bank on the corner."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where must the man turn left?","o":["At the bank","At the traffic lights","At the library"],"a":1,"why":"قال: turn left at the traffic lights."},{"k":"gap","q":"He walks along Park Street for about ___ minutes.","a":["5","five"],"why":"قال: for about five minutes."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where is the library?","o":["On the left","On the right","Opposite the bank"],"a":1,"why":"ستجد مكتبة على يمينك."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The station is opposite the bus stop.","a":"T","why":"قال: opposite the bus stop."},{"k":"gap","q":"The bus costs ___ dollars.","a":["2","two"],"why":"قال: it costs two dollars."}]},{"id":"l-a2-03","l":"A2","title":"A voicemail from a friend about the weekend","part":2,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hi Sara, it's Tom. Sorry, I missed your call. I'm calling about the weekend."},{"s":"A","t":"On Saturday morning, I'm going to play football with my brother. The match starts at ten o'clock, so I will be free after lunch."},{"s":"A","t":"Do you want to go to the new market in the afternoon? It opens at two. I want to buy a birthday present for my mother."},{"s":"A","t":"After that, we can have a coffee. My cousin Lina works in a small cafe near the market, and she can give us a cake for free."},{"s":"A","t":"On Sunday, I have to help my father in the garden, but I'm free in the evening. Maybe we can watch a film at my house."},{"s":"A","t":"Please call me back before Friday night. My number is the same as always. Bye for now!"}],"gloss":[{"w":"voicemail","ar":"رسالة صوتية"},{"w":"for free","ar":"مجاناً"},{"w":"birthday present","ar":"هدية عيد ميلاد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What will Tom do on Saturday morning?","o":["Play football","Go to the market","Work in a cafe"],"a":0,"why":"سيلعب كرة القدم مع أخيه."},{"k":"gap","q":"The football match starts at ___ o'clock.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"قال: starts at ten o'clock."},{"k":"gap","q":"Tom wants to buy a birthday present for his ___.","a":["mother"],"why":"قال: for my mother."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time does the market open?","o":["At ten","At two","At six"],"a":1,"why":"قال: It opens at two."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Tom's cousin works in a cafe.","a":"T","why":"قال: my cousin Lina works in a small cafe."},{"k":"mc","q":"When is Tom free on Sunday?","o":["In the morning","In the afternoon","In the evening"],"a":2,"why":"يساعد والده ثم يكون متفرغاً مساءً."}]},{"id":"l-a2-04","l":"A2","title":"At the pharmacy","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon. Can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, please. I have a bad headache and a sore throat."},{"s":"A","t":"How long have you had them?"},{"s":"B","t":"Since yesterday morning. I also feel a little tired."},{"s":"A","t":"Do you have a fever?"},{"s":"B","t":"No, I don't think so."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. I can give you some tablets for the headache. Take one tablet after breakfast and one after dinner."},{"s":"B","t":"How many days should I take them?"},{"s":"A","t":"For three days. And these sweets are good for your throat. Drink lots of warm water, too."},{"s":"B","t":"How much is everything?"},{"s":"A","t":"The tablets are six pounds and the sweets are three pounds. That is nine pounds in total."},{"s":"B","t":"Here you are. Thank you."},{"s":"A","t":"If you still feel ill on Friday, please see a doctor."}],"gloss":[{"w":"headache","ar":"صداع"},{"w":"sore throat","ar":"التهاب الحلق"},{"w":"fever","ar":"حمى"},{"w":"tablet","ar":"قرص دواء"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What problems does the customer have?","o":["A headache and a sore throat","A cough and a fever","A broken arm"],"a":0,"why":"ذكر صداعاً والتهاب حلق."},{"k":"mc","q":"Since when does he feel ill?","o":["Since this morning","Since yesterday morning","Since Monday"],"a":1,"why":"قال: Since yesterday morning."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The customer has a fever.","a":"F","why":"قال: No, I don't think so."},{"k":"gap","q":"He must take the tablets for ___ days.","a":["3","three"],"why":"قالت: For three days."},{"k":"gap","q":"The total price is ___ pounds.","a":["9","nine"],"why":"ستة زائد ثلاثة تساوي تسعة جنيهات."},{"k":"mc","q":"When should he see a doctor?","o":["On Wednesday","On Friday","Today"],"a":1,"why":"إذا بقي مريضاً يوم الجمعة."}]},{"id":"l-a2-05","l":"A2","title":"Buying a train ticket","part":1,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Hi. I need a ticket to Boston, please."},{"s":"A","t":"One way or return?"},{"s":"B","t":"Return, please. I want to come back on the same day."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. The next train leaves at eleven forty from platform four."},{"s":"B","t":"Platform four. Is it a fast train?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, it takes two hours. There is also a slower train at noon, but it takes three hours."},{"s":"B","t":"I will take the fast one, please. How much is it?"},{"s":"A","t":"A return ticket is thirty-eight dollars. Are you a student?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, I am. Here is my student card."},{"s":"A","t":"Great. With the card, you pay thirty dollars."},{"s":"B","t":"Wonderful. Can I pay by card?"},{"s":"A","t":"Of course. Please put it in the machine."}],"gloss":[{"w":"return ticket","ar":"تذكرة ذهاب وإياب"},{"w":"platform","ar":"رصيف القطار"},{"w":"one way","ar":"ذهاب فقط"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The man wants a ticket to ___.","a":["Boston"],"why":"قال: a ticket to Boston."},{"k":"mc","q":"What kind of ticket does he buy?","o":["One way","Return","Weekly"],"a":1,"why":"قال: Return, please."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time does the fast train leave?","o":["11.04","11.40","12.00"],"a":1,"why":"قالت: eleven forty."},{"k":"gap","q":"The train leaves from platform ___.","a":["4","four"],"why":"قالت: platform four."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long is the fast train journey?","o":["Two hours","Three hours","Four hours"],"a":0,"why":"القطار السريع يستغرق ساعتين."},{"k":"gap","q":"With the student card the ticket costs ___ dollars.","a":["30","thirty"],"why":"بالبطاقة الطلابية يدفع ثلاثين دولاراً."}]},{"id":"l-a2-06","l":"A2","title":"A tour guide welcomes visitors","part":2,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the Old Castle. My name is Mia, and I will be your guide today."},{"s":"A","t":"The tour takes about ninety minutes. First, we will walk around the garden. Then we will go inside to see the great hall and the kitchen."},{"s":"A","t":"Please stay with the group, and do not touch the old furniture. You can take photos, but please do not use a flash."},{"s":"A","t":"At half past eleven, we will have a short break. There is a small shop near the exit, and you can buy water and postcards there."},{"s":"A","t":"The castle is about seven hundred years old. A king built it to protect the town, and later a family lived here for three hundred years."},{"s":"A","t":"Does anyone have a question? No? Then please follow me."}],"gloss":[{"w":"guide","ar":"مرشد سياحي"},{"w":"flash","ar":"فلاش الكاميرا"},{"w":"exit","ar":"مخرج"},{"w":"furniture","ar":"أثاث"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The guide's name is ___.","a":["Mia"],"why":"قالت: My name is Mia."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long is the tour?","o":["About 60 minutes","About 90 minutes","About two hours"],"a":1,"why":"قالت: about ninety minutes."},{"k":"mc","q":"What will the group see first?","o":["The kitchen","The great hall","The garden"],"a":2,"why":"أولاً يتجولون حول الحديقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Visitors can use a flash for photos.","a":"F","why":"قالت: please do not use a flash."},{"k":"gap","q":"The break is at half past ___.","a":["11","eleven"],"why":"الاستراحة عند الحادية عشرة والنصف."},{"k":"mc","q":"How old is the castle?","o":["About 300 years","About 700 years","About 90 years"],"a":1,"why":"عمر القلعة نحو سبعمئة سنة."}]},{"id":"l-a2-07","l":"A2","title":"Joining a sports club","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello. Welcome to City Sports Club. Are you a new member?"},{"s":"B","t":"Not yet. I would like to join, please. I want to learn swimming."},{"s":"A","t":"Great. We have swimming lessons on Monday and Wednesday evenings, from six to seven."},{"s":"B","t":"Is that for beginners?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, it is. The teacher is very friendly. His name is Mr Green."},{"s":"B","t":"How much does it cost?"},{"s":"A","t":"Membership is twenty pounds a month. If you pay for a year, you get two months free."},{"s":"B","t":"That is a good price. Do I need to bring anything?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes. Bring a towel, a swimming cap and a bottle of water. We have lockers, but you need a one pound coin."},{"s":"B","t":"Okay. Can I try one lesson first?"},{"s":"A","t":"Of course. Come this Monday, and the first lesson is free."}],"gloss":[{"w":"member","ar":"عضو"},{"w":"beginner","ar":"مبتدئ"},{"w":"towel","ar":"منشفة"},{"w":"locker","ar":"خزانة صغيرة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does the man want to learn?","o":["Tennis","Swimming","Football"],"a":1,"why":"قال: I want to learn swimming."},{"k":"mc","q":"When are the lessons?","o":["Monday and Wednesday","Tuesday and Thursday","Every weekend"],"a":0,"why":"الإثنين والأربعاء مساءً."},{"k":"gap","q":"The teacher's name is Mr ___.","a":["Green"],"why":"قالت: His name is Mr Green."},{"k":"gap","q":"Membership costs ___ pounds a month.","a":["20","twenty"],"why":"قالت: twenty pounds a month."},{"k":"tfng","q":"He must bring a towel and a swimming cap.","a":"T","why":"طلبت منه منشفة وقبعة سباحة وزجاجة ماء."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is free?","o":["The towel","The first lesson","The locker"],"a":1,"why":"الدرس الأول مجاني."}]},{"id":"l-a2-08","l":"A2","title":"A weather report","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, and here is the weather for the week. Today will be cloudy in the north, with some rain in the afternoon. The temperature will be around twelve degrees."},{"s":"A","t":"In the south, it will be warm and sunny. It will reach twenty-two degrees, so it is a good day for the beach."},{"s":"A","t":"Tomorrow, strong winds will come from the west. Please be careful if you are driving near the coast."},{"s":"A","t":"On Wednesday, it will be very cold at night, and there may be fog on the roads early in the morning."},{"s":"A","t":"From Thursday, the weather will get better. We will see more sun, and the rain will stop."},{"s":"A","t":"By the weekend, it will be warm and dry everywhere, with a high of twenty degrees. Enjoy your week!"}],"gloss":[{"w":"temperature","ar":"درجة الحرارة"},{"w":"fog","ar":"ضباب"},{"w":"coast","ar":"ساحل"},{"w":"strong winds","ar":"رياح قوية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the weather in the north today?","o":["Sunny","Cloudy with rain","Foggy"],"a":1,"why":"قال: cloudy in the north with some rain."},{"k":"gap","q":"The temperature in the south will reach ___ degrees.","a":["22","twenty-two","twenty two"],"why":"قال: twenty-two degrees."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Tomorrow, the wind will come from the east.","a":"F","why":"الرياح ستأتي من الغرب."},{"k":"mc","q":"What may happen early on Wednesday morning?","o":["Snow","Fog","Strong rain"],"a":1,"why":"قال: there may be fog on the roads."},{"k":"gap","q":"The weather will get better from ___.","a":["Thursday"],"why":"قال: From Thursday."},{"k":"mc","q":"What will the weekend be like?","o":["Warm and dry","Cold and windy","Wet and cloudy"],"a":0,"why":"قال: warm and dry everywhere."}]},{"id":"l-a2-09","l":"A2","title":"Renting a flat","part":1,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello, Sunrise Homes. Can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. I saw your advert for a flat in Green Street. Is it still free?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, it is. It has two bedrooms, a small kitchen and a balcony."},{"s":"B","t":"How much is the rent?"},{"s":"A","t":"It is four hundred and fifty dollars a month, and the water is included."},{"s":"B","t":"What about the internet?"},{"s":"A","t":"You pay for that yourself. It is about twenty dollars a month."},{"s":"B","t":"Is the flat near a bus stop?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, the bus stop is two minutes from the door. The supermarket is also very close."},{"s":"B","t":"Good. Can I see the flat tomorrow?"},{"s":"A","t":"Of course. Come at four o'clock. You must bring your passport or identity card."},{"s":"B","t":"Okay. My name is Yusuf. Thank you for your help."}],"gloss":[{"w":"advert","ar":"إعلان"},{"w":"rent","ar":"إيجار"},{"w":"balcony","ar":"شرفة"},{"w":"included","ar":"مشمول"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where is the flat?","o":["Sunrise Road","Green Street","Park Avenue"],"a":1,"why":"قال: a flat in Green Street."},{"k":"gap","q":"The flat has ___ bedrooms.","a":["2","two"],"why":"قالت: two bedrooms."},{"k":"gap","q":"The monthly rent is ___ dollars.","a":["450","four hundred and fifty","four hundred fifty"],"why":"قالت: four hundred and fifty dollars."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is included in the rent?","o":["The internet","The water","The bus ticket"],"a":1,"why":"الماء مشمول في الإيجار."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The bus stop is far from the flat.","a":"F","why":"محطة الحافلة على بعد دقيقتين من الباب."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time is the visit?","o":["At two","At three","At four"],"a":2,"why":"قالت: Come at four o'clock."}]},{"id":"l-a2-10","l":"A2","title":"A teacher explains the week's timetable","part":2,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, class. Before we start, here is our timetable for this week."},{"s":"A","t":"On Monday, we have grammar at nine o'clock and reading at eleven."},{"s":"A","t":"On Tuesday, there is no class in the morning because I have a meeting. We will meet at two in the afternoon for speaking practice."},{"s":"A","t":"On Wednesday, we have a computer lesson in room twelve, not in our usual room. Please remember to bring headphones."},{"s":"A","t":"On Thursday, there is a short test. It is about the words from units five and six, so please study them at home."},{"s":"A","t":"On Friday, we will go to the library for an hour. We finish early, at twelve, so you can go home for lunch."},{"s":"A","t":"Please write this in your notebooks. Are there any questions?"}],"gloss":[{"w":"timetable","ar":"جدول الدروس"},{"w":"headphones","ar":"سماعات الأذن"},{"w":"test","ar":"اختبار"},{"w":"meeting","ar":"اجتماع"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is at nine o'clock on Monday?","o":["Reading","Grammar","Speaking"],"a":1,"why":"قالت: grammar at nine o'clock."},{"k":"tfng","q":"There is a morning class on Tuesday.","a":"F","why":"لا يوجد درس صباحاً لأن المعلمة لديها اجتماع."},{"k":"gap","q":"The computer lesson is in room ___.","a":["12","twelve"],"why":"قالت: room twelve."},{"k":"mc","q":"What must students bring on Wednesday?","o":["A dictionary","Headphones","A laptop"],"a":1,"why":"طلبت منهم إحضار سماعات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the test about?","o":["Units one and two","Units five and six","Units nine and ten"],"a":1,"why":"الاختبار عن الوحدتين خمسة وستة."},{"k":"gap","q":"On Friday, the class finishes at ___.","a":["12","twelve"],"why":"قالت: We finish early, at twelve."}]},{"id":"l-a2-11","l":"A2","title":"At the post office","part":1,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Next, please. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Hello. I want to send this parcel to Canada."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me see. Please put it on the scale. It is two kilos."},{"s":"B","t":"How long will it take?"},{"s":"A","t":"By air, it takes about five days. By sea, it takes a month."},{"s":"B","t":"By air, please. How much is it?"},{"s":"A","t":"Eighteen dollars. Do you want to add insurance? It is only three dollars more."},{"s":"B","t":"No, thank you. It is just some books for my sister."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. Please fill in this form with her address and your phone number."},{"s":"B","t":"Can I also buy two stamps for letters?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes. Stamps are one dollar each."},{"s":"B","t":"Here is a twenty-dollar bill. Thank you."},{"s":"A","t":"Your change is two dollars after the parcel. Have a good day."}],"gloss":[{"w":"parcel","ar":"طرد"},{"w":"scale","ar":"ميزان"},{"w":"insurance","ar":"تأمين"},{"w":"stamp","ar":"طابع بريدي"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where does the man want to send the parcel?","o":["To Canada","To Spain","To Italy"],"a":0,"why":"قال: to Canada."},{"k":"gap","q":"The parcel weighs ___ kilos.","a":["2","two"],"why":"قال الموظف: It is two kilos."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long does air mail take?","o":["Two days","About five days","A month"],"a":1,"why":"بالجو نحو خمسة أيام."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The man buys insurance.","a":"F","why":"قال: No, thank you."},{"k":"gap","q":"What is inside the parcel? ___","a":["books","some books"],"why":"قال: just some books for my sister."},{"k":"mc","q":"How much is one stamp?","o":["One dollar","Two dollars","Three dollars"],"a":0,"why":"قال: one dollar each."}]},{"id":"l-a2-12","l":"A2","title":"A shop announcement","part":2,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Attention, please. Good afternoon, shoppers, and welcome to Fresh Mart."},{"s":"A","t":"This week, we have some special offers. Apples are only two dollars a kilo, and a loaf of brown bread costs one dollar fifty."},{"s":"A","t":"On the first floor, all winter coats are half price. Please visit the clothes department near the escalator."},{"s":"A","t":"Today only, children can enjoy a free ice cream from the cafe on the ground floor. Please ask the staff at the desk."},{"s":"A","t":"We also have a message for the owner of a blue car, number plate four four seven. Your lights are on. Please come back to your car."},{"s":"A","t":"Fresh Mart closes at nine o'clock tonight. Thank you for shopping with us."}],"gloss":[{"w":"special offer","ar":"عرض خاص"},{"w":"half price","ar":"نصف السعر"},{"w":"escalator","ar":"سلم متحرك"},{"w":"staff","ar":"الموظفون"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How much are apples this week?","o":["One dollar a kilo","Two dollars a kilo","Three dollars a kilo"],"a":1,"why":"التفاح بدولارين للكيلو."},{"k":"gap","q":"A loaf of brown bread costs one dollar ___.","a":["50","fifty"],"why":"قال: one dollar fifty."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is half price?","o":["Shoes","Winter coats","Toys"],"a":1,"why":"المعاطف الشتوية بنصف السعر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The free ice cream is for adults.","a":"F","why":"الآيس كريم المجاني للأطفال."},{"k":"gap","q":"The blue car's number plate is ___.","a":["447","four four seven"],"why":"رقم اللوحة أربعة أربعة سبعة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time does the shop close?","o":["At eight","At nine","At ten"],"a":1,"why":"قال: closes at nine o'clock."}]},{"id":"l-a2-13","l":"A2","title":"A phone call to a hotel","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good evening, Lake View Hotel. Anna speaking."},{"s":"B","t":"Hello. I have a reservation for tomorrow, but I need to change it."},{"s":"A","t":"Certainly. What is your name, please?"},{"s":"B","t":"It is Hassan Omar."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, Mr Omar. You have a single room for two nights, from the fifth of May."},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. Can I arrive one day later, on the sixth, and stay for three nights?"},{"s":"A","t":"Let me check. Yes, that is possible. The price is the same, ninety pounds a night."},{"s":"B","t":"Is breakfast included?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, breakfast is from seven to ten in the dining room."},{"s":"B","t":"Perfect. Also, I will arrive late, at about eleven at night. Is that a problem?"},{"s":"A","t":"No, the reception is open all night."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you very much for your help."}],"gloss":[{"w":"reservation","ar":"حجز"},{"w":"single room","ar":"غرفة فردية"},{"w":"reception","ar":"الاستقبال"},{"w":"arrive","ar":"يصل"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The guest's family name is ___.","a":["Omar"],"why":"قالت: Mr Omar."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was the first booking?","o":["Two nights","Three nights","One night"],"a":0,"why":"الحجز الأصلي ليلتان."},{"k":"mc","q":"What new date will he arrive?","o":["The fifth of May","The sixth of May","The seventh of May"],"a":1,"why":"يريد الوصول في السادس."},{"k":"gap","q":"The room costs ___ pounds a night.","a":["90","ninety"],"why":"قالت: ninety pounds a night."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Breakfast is included in the price.","a":"T","why":"قالت: Yes, breakfast is from seven to ten."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time will he arrive?","o":["About nine","About ten","About eleven at night"],"a":2,"why":"سيصل حوالي الحادية عشرة ليلاً."}]},{"id":"l-a2-14","l":"A2","title":"Talking about last holiday","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Last summer, my family and I went to Turkey for two weeks. We flew from Algiers to Istanbul, and the flight took about four hours."},{"s":"A","t":"We stayed in a small hotel near the sea. The rooms were clean, and the people there were very kind. Every morning, we ate breakfast on the roof and watched the boats."},{"s":"A","t":"In the first week, we visited the old market and a famous museum. My brother bought a leather jacket, and I bought some sweets for my friends."},{"s":"A","t":"In the second week, we took a boat trip to an island. The water was warm, and we swam every day."},{"s":"A","t":"The only bad thing was the weather on the last day. It rained all morning, so we stayed in the hotel and played cards."},{"s":"A","t":"It was a great holiday, and I want to go again next year."}],"gloss":[{"w":"flight","ar":"رحلة جوية"},{"w":"island","ar":"جزيرة"},{"w":"leather jacket","ar":"سترة جلدية"},{"w":"boat trip","ar":"رحلة بالقارب"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How long was the holiday?","o":["One week","Two weeks","Three weeks"],"a":1,"why":"قال: for two weeks."},{"k":"gap","q":"The flight took about ___ hours.","a":["4","four"],"why":"قال: about four hours."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where did they eat breakfast?","o":["In the dining room","On the roof","On the beach"],"a":1,"why":"كانوا يتناولون الفطور على السطح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did the brother buy?","o":["A leather jacket","Some sweets","A museum ticket"],"a":0,"why":"اشترى أخوه سترة جلدية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"They took a boat trip in the first week.","a":"F","why":"الرحلة بالقارب كانت في الأسبوع الثاني."},{"k":"gap","q":"On the last day, they played ___.","a":["cards"],"why":"بسبب المطر لعبوا الورق."}]},{"id":"l-b1-01","l":"B1","title":"Registering for an English course","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Welcome to Bridge Language School. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Good morning. I would like to register for an English course, please."},{"s":"A","t":"Certainly. Have you studied English before?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, I studied it at school for six years, but I haven't practised much since then. I think my level is intermediate."},{"s":"A","t":"We will check that with a short test. We have two types of course."},{"s":"A","t":"The evening course meets three times a week, and the intensive course meets every morning."},{"s":"B","t":"I work in the afternoons, so the intensive course would suit me better. When does it start?"},{"s":"A","t":"The next one starts on the third of October and lasts ten weeks. Each lesson is two and a half hours."},{"s":"B","t":"How much does it cost?"},{"s":"A","t":"It is five hundred and eighty pounds, and that includes the books. If you register this week, you get ten percent off."},{"s":"B","t":"That sounds good. What do I need to do now?"},{"s":"A","t":"Please fill in this form and bring a passport photo tomorrow. The placement test is on Friday at nine thirty, in room fourteen."},{"s":"B","t":"Can I pay in two parts?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes. You can pay half now and half in the first week of the course."},{"s":"B","t":"Perfect. Thank you for your help."}],"gloss":[{"w":"register","ar":"يسجّل"},{"w":"intensive","ar":"مكثّف"},{"w":"placement test","ar":"اختبار تحديد المستوى"},{"w":"intermediate","ar":"متوسط"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the student choose the intensive course?","o":["It is cheaper","He works in the afternoons","It has fewer students"],"a":1,"why":"قال إنه يعمل بعد الظهر فتناسبه الدورة الصباحية."},{"k":"mc","q":"How often does the evening course meet?","o":["Every day","Twice a week","Three times a week"],"a":2,"why":"دورة المساء ثلاث مرات أسبوعياً."},{"k":"gap","q":"The next course starts on the ___ of October.","a":["3rd","third","3"],"why":"تبدأ الدورة في الثالث من أكتوبر."},{"k":"gap","q":"The course costs ___ pounds.","a":["580","five hundred and eighty","five hundred eighty"],"why":"السعر خمسمئة وثمانون جنيهاً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The price includes the books.","a":"T","why":"قالت: that includes the books."},{"k":"mc","q":"When is the placement test?","o":["On Friday morning","On Friday afternoon","Tomorrow evening"],"a":0,"why":"الاختبار يوم الجمعة الساعة التاسعة والنصف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"He must pay the full price today.","a":"F","why":"يمكنه دفع النصف الآن والنصف لاحقاً."}]},{"id":"l-b1-02","l":"B1","title":"A job interview for a part-time job","part":1,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Thank you for coming in, Layla. Please sit down. Can you tell me a little about yourself?"},{"s":"B","t":"Of course. I am a second-year student, and I study business. I am looking for a part-time job because I want some experience and a little extra money."},{"s":"A","t":"Why do you want to work in our bookshop?"},{"s":"B","t":"I love reading, and I enjoy talking to people. Last summer, I worked in a clothes shop, so I know how to help customers."},{"s":"A","t":"That is useful. What was the hardest part of that job?"},{"s":"B","t":"Probably the busy weekends. There were long lines, but I learned to stay calm and work quickly."},{"s":"A","t":"Good. We need someone for twelve hours a week. Could you work on Saturdays?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, I could work all day Saturday. I have classes from Monday to Thursday, but I am free on Friday afternoon, too."},{"s":"A","t":"Excellent. The pay is eleven dollars an hour. You would start with a one-week training period."},{"s":"B","t":"That sounds fine. Will I work with a team?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes. There are four of us, and we all share the tasks. If you are successful, I will call you by Wednesday."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you. I look forward to hearing from you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"part-time","ar":"بدوام جزئي"},{"w":"experience","ar":"خبرة"},{"w":"training period","ar":"فترة تدريب"},{"w":"customer","ar":"زبون"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does Layla study?","o":["Business","Literature","Law"],"a":0,"why":"قالت: I study business."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where did she work last summer?","o":["In a bookshop","In a clothes shop","In a cafe"],"a":1,"why":"عملت في متجر ملابس."},{"k":"tfng","q":"She found quiet days the hardest part of her last job.","a":"F","why":"الأصعب كان عطلات نهاية الأسبوع المزدحمة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The job is for ___ hours a week.","a":["12","twelve"],"why":"قال: twelve hours a week."},{"k":"mc","q":"When is she free besides Saturday?","o":["Monday morning","Friday afternoon","Thursday evening"],"a":1,"why":"قالت إنها متفرغة بعد ظهر الجمعة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The pay is ___ dollars an hour.","a":["11","eleven"],"why":"الأجر أحد عشر دولاراً."},{"k":"gap","q":"The manager will call by ___.","a":["Wednesday"],"why":"قال: I will call you by Wednesday."}]},{"id":"l-b1-03","l":"B1","title":"A radio item about a new city park","part":2,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, and welcome to City Radio. Our top story today is the opening of Riverside Park, the biggest new green space in our city for thirty years."},{"s":"A","t":"The park opens this Saturday at ten o'clock. It covers about twenty hectares along the river, in a place where an old factory stood until 2015."},{"s":"A","t":"The city council spent four years planning the project."},{"s":"A","t":"It planted more than two thousand trees, and it built a lake, three playgrounds and a ten-kilometre path for cyclists and walkers."},{"s":"A","t":"There is also a small cafe, which is run by local students, and a library of outdoor books for children."},{"s":"A","t":"The council says that the park will reduce traffic noise and make the air cleaner in nearby streets."},{"s":"A","t":"Entry is free, but cars are not allowed inside."},{"s":"A","t":"Visitors can leave their cars at the car park on Mill Road, which costs two pounds for the whole day. Bicycles can be rented at the main gate."},{"s":"A","t":"On opening day, there will be music from eleven, and a free picnic for families at one o'clock."},{"s":"A","t":"Remember to bring a hat, because there is not much shade yet. The young trees will need a few years to grow."},{"s":"A","t":"That was the news. Now, here is the traffic."}],"gloss":[{"w":"hectare","ar":"هكتار"},{"w":"council","ar":"مجلس البلدية"},{"w":"playground","ar":"ملعب أطفال"},{"w":"shade","ar":"ظل"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The park opens this ___.","a":["Saturday"],"why":"ستفتتح الحديقة يوم السبت."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was on the land before the park?","o":["A hospital","An old factory","A market"],"a":1,"why":"كان في المكان مصنع قديم حتى عام 2015."},{"k":"gap","q":"The council planted more than ___ trees.","a":["2000","two thousand","2,000"],"why":"زُرع أكثر من ألفي شجرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who runs the cafe?","o":["The council","Local students","A big company"],"a":1,"why":"المقهى يديره طلاب محليون."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Cars are allowed inside the park.","a":"F","why":"السيارات غير مسموحة داخل الحديقة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The car park costs ___ pounds for the day.","a":["2","two"],"why":"موقف السيارات بجنيهين لليوم."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why should visitors bring a hat?","o":["It may rain","There is little shade","The sun is very hot in winter"],"a":1,"why":"الأشجار صغيرة ولا ظل كافياً بعد."}]},{"id":"l-b1-04","l":"B1","title":"Two students plan a presentation","part":3,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hi, Nadia. Shall we decide how to organise our presentation on renewable energy? We have only ten minutes."},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. I think we should start with a short introduction, and then talk about solar power and wind power."},{"s":"A","t":"Good idea. I can do the solar part, because I read a lot about it for my last essay. Can you take wind power?"},{"s":"B","t":"Sure. But I would like to add a slide about costs, because many people think that renewable energy is too expensive."},{"s":"A","t":"Right. Maybe we can show a simple graph. Do you know where we can find some reliable data?"},{"s":"B","t":"The library has a report from the energy ministry. I borrowed it last week, and I can scan the main table."},{"s":"A","t":"Perfect. How many slides should we have?"},{"s":"B","t":"About eight. If we have more, we will speak too quickly."},{"s":"A","t":"Agreed. Let's practise on Thursday afternoon. Professor Hall is free at four, so we could ask him for some feedback."},{"s":"B","t":"Good. I will send him an email tonight. Shall I also book a room with a projector?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, please. And let's finish with a question for the audience. It makes people think."},{"s":"B","t":"Great. I will write one tomorrow."}],"gloss":[{"w":"renewable energy","ar":"طاقة متجددة"},{"w":"reliable","ar":"موثوق"},{"w":"projector","ar":"جهاز عرض"},{"w":"audience","ar":"الجمهور"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the presentation about?","o":["Renewable energy","Public transport","Water pollution"],"a":0,"why":"عن الطاقة المتجددة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The presentation lasts ___ minutes.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"لديهما عشر دقائق فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who will talk about wind power?","o":["The man","Nadia","Professor Hall"],"a":1,"why":"نادية ستتولى طاقة الرياح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What extra slide does Nadia want?","o":["A slide about costs","A slide about history","A slide about jobs"],"a":0,"why":"تريد شريحة عن التكاليف."},{"k":"gap","q":"They plan to use about ___ slides.","a":["8","eight"],"why":"قالت: About eight."},{"k":"tfng","q":"They will practise on Thursday morning.","a":"F","why":"سيتدربان بعد الظهر الساعة الرابعة يوم الخميس."},{"k":"mc","q":"How will they end the presentation?","o":["With a video","With a question for the audience","With a summary slide"],"a":1,"why":"سينهيان بسؤال للجمهور."}]},{"id":"l-b1-05","l":"B1","title":"Reporting a lost bag","part":1,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Lost property office. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Hello. I lost my bag on the train this afternoon, and I am very worried."},{"s":"A","t":"I am sorry to hear that. Can you describe it, please?"},{"s":"B","t":"It is a black backpack, quite big, with a red label on the front. It also has a broken zip on the side pocket."},{"s":"A","t":"What was inside?"},{"s":"B","t":"My laptop, a blue notebook, a pair of glasses and a bottle of water. Most importantly, my charger was in there."},{"s":"A","t":"Which train were you on?"},{"s":"B","t":"The two forty-five from Chicago to Detroit. I sat in coach three, next to the window, and I got off at about five."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. Let me write that down. What is your name and phone number?"},{"s":"B","t":"Daniel Ross. That is R, O, S, S. My number is five five five, three two zero, seven seven one."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, Mr Ross. Is there anything valuable in the bag, like money or documents?"},{"s":"B","t":"No money, thank goodness, but my university card is in the front pocket."},{"s":"A","t":"I will note that on the form. We will search the train tonight. If we find the bag, we will phone you tomorrow morning."},{"s":"B","t":"How long do you keep lost bags?"},{"s":"A","t":"For thirty days. After that, we give them to charity."}],"gloss":[{"w":"lost property","ar":"المفقودات"},{"w":"backpack","ar":"حقيبة ظهر"},{"w":"zip","ar":"سحّاب"},{"w":"charity","ar":"جمعية خيرية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where did the man lose his bag?","o":["On a bus","On a train","At the airport"],"a":1,"why":"فقدها في القطار."},{"k":"gap","q":"The label on the front is ___.","a":["red"],"why":"قال: a red label on the front."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is wrong with the bag?","o":["A broken zip","A hole","A missing strap"],"a":0,"why":"السحّاب في الجيب الجانبي مكسور."},{"k":"gap","q":"The train left at ___.","a":["2.45","2:45","two forty-five","two forty five","245"],"why":"القطار انطلق الساعة 2:45."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The man sat in coach five.","a":"F","why":"جلس في العربة الثالثة."},{"k":"gap","q":"His surname is spelt R, O, S, S: ___.","a":["Ross"],"why":"تهجئة الاسم R, O, S, S."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long does the office keep lost bags?","o":["Seven days","Fourteen days","Thirty days"],"a":2,"why":"تحتفظ بها لمدة ثلاثين يوماً."}]},{"id":"l-b1-06","l":"B1","title":"A talk on healthy sleep","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon, everyone. Today I would like to talk about sleep, and why it is so important for students."},{"s":"A","t":"Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep every night. When we sleep, our brain stores new information, and our body repairs itself."},{"s":"A","t":"If we do not sleep enough, we find it difficult to concentrate, and we feel more stressed."},{"s":"A","t":"So, what can we do to sleep better?"},{"s":"A","t":"My first tip is to go to bed and get up at the same time every day, even at the weekend."},{"s":"A","t":"This helps the body to build a routine."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, avoid screens for at least one hour before bed. The light from phones and laptops tells the brain that it is still daytime."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, be careful with caffeine."},{"s":"A","t":"Coffee, tea and energy drinks can stay in the body for up to eight hours, so it is better not to drink them after three o'clock in the afternoon."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, keep your bedroom cool, dark and quiet. A temperature of about eighteen degrees is ideal."},{"s":"A","t":"Many students believe that they can catch up on sleep at the weekend, but research shows that this does not work well. A regular routine is much better."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you for listening."}],"gloss":[{"w":"concentrate","ar":"يركّز"},{"w":"routine","ar":"روتين"},{"w":"caffeine","ar":"كافيين"},{"w":"stressed","ar":"متوتر"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"Most adults need seven to ___ hours of sleep.","a":["9","nine"],"why":"يحتاج البالغون من سبع إلى تسع ساعات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the brain do during sleep?","o":["It stops working","It stores new information","It makes more energy"],"a":1,"why":"الدماغ يخزّن المعلومات الجديدة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the first tip?","o":["Sleep at the same times every day","Sleep late at weekends","Take a short nap"],"a":0,"why":"أول نصيحة: نفس موعد النوم والاستيقاظ يومياً."},{"k":"gap","q":"Avoid screens for at least ___ hour before bed.","a":["1","one","an","one hour"],"why":"تجنب الشاشات ساعة قبل النوم."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Caffeine can stay in the body for up to eight hours.","a":"T","why":"قال: up to eight hours."},{"k":"mc","q":"After what time should students avoid coffee?","o":["Noon","Three o'clock","Eight o'clock"],"a":1,"why":"لا قهوة بعد الثالثة عصراً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Research shows that catching up on sleep at weekends works very well.","a":"F","why":"قال إن هذا لا يعمل جيداً."}]},{"id":"l-b1-07","l":"B1","title":"Booking a study room at the library","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. University Library, information desk."},{"s":"B","t":"Hello. I would like to book a study room for a group project, please."},{"s":"A","t":"Of course. How many students will there be?"},{"s":"B","t":"Five of us. We need a room for about three hours."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me see. The small rooms take four people, so you will need a large room. Those have a screen and a whiteboard."},{"s":"B","t":"That would be perfect. Is anything free on Tuesday?"},{"s":"A","t":"On Tuesday, there is a room free from ten until twelve, and again from three until six."},{"s":"B","t":"Could we have the afternoon one? We have classes in the morning."},{"s":"A","t":"Yes. But the maximum booking is two hours at a time, so I can give you three until five."},{"s":"B","t":"That is fine. Is there a way to extend it if no one else needs the room?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes. Come to the desk before five, and we will check. Can I have a name and a student number?"},{"s":"B","t":"Ahmed Saidi, S, A, I, D, I. My number is two zero two three, four four one."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, Ahmed. It is Room Nine on the second floor. Please remember that you can't eat in the rooms, but you can bring water."},{"s":"B","t":"Understood. Thank you very much."}],"gloss":[{"w":"whiteboard","ar":"سبّورة بيضاء"},{"w":"extend","ar":"يمدّد"},{"w":"maximum","ar":"الحد الأقصى"},{"w":"group project","ar":"مشروع جماعي"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The group has ___ students.","a":["5","five"],"why":"قال: Five of us."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why can't they use a small room?","o":["It has no screen","It is for four people","It is too expensive"],"a":1,"why":"الغرف الصغيرة تتسع لأربعة فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which day do they book?","o":["Monday","Tuesday","Thursday"],"a":1,"why":"يحجزون يوم الثلاثاء."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time is the booking?","o":["10 to 12","3 to 5","3 to 6"],"a":1,"why":"الحد الأقصى ساعتان فيحجزون من الثالثة إلى الخامسة."},{"k":"gap","q":"They have Room ___ on the second floor.","a":["9","nine"],"why":"قالت: It is Room Nine."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Students can bring water into the rooms.","a":"T","why":"قالت: you can bring water."},{"k":"tfng","q":"They must speak to the desk before the booking ends to extend it.","a":"T","why":"عليه الحضور إلى المكتب قبل الخامسة."}]},{"id":"l-b1-08","l":"B1","title":"A museum audio guide","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Welcome to the City Science Museum audio guide. Please press the green button when you want to hear the next section."},{"s":"A","t":"You are now standing in the Hall of Inventions. Straight ahead, you can see a large glass case."},{"s":"A","t":"Inside is a copy of the first mechanical clock made in this region, around the year thirteen hundred."},{"s":"A","t":"Notice how big it is. The clockmaker used heavy weights and a system of wheels, and the clock only showed the hour."},{"s":"A","t":"It did not have a minute hand."},{"s":"A","t":"On your left, there is a model of an early printing machine. Before this invention, people copied books by hand, and a single book could take months."},{"s":"A","t":"With the machine, a workshop could print hundreds of pages in a day, and knowledge spread faster than ever before."},{"s":"A","t":"Please do not touch the models, but children can try the interactive screens near the window. They show how the machine worked."},{"s":"A","t":"When you are ready, go through the blue door at the end of the hall to the Energy Gallery. The audio guide will start automatically."},{"s":"A","t":"The cafe is on the ground floor, and the museum closes at five thirty."}],"gloss":[{"w":"invention","ar":"اختراع"},{"w":"weights","ar":"أثقال"},{"w":"workshop","ar":"ورشة"},{"w":"gallery","ar":"قاعة عرض"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What should visitors press to hear the next section?","o":["The red button","The green button","The blue button"],"a":1,"why":"اضغط الزر الأخضر."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is inside the large glass case?","o":["A printing machine","A copy of an early clock","A computer"],"a":1,"why":"نسخة من أول ساعة ميكانيكية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The old clock had a minute hand.","a":"F","why":"الساعة كانت تُظهر الساعات فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where is the model of the printing machine?","o":["On the left","On the right","Behind the clock"],"a":0,"why":"قال: On your left."},{"k":"gap","q":"Before printing, copying a book could take ___.","a":["months"],"why":"نسخ كتاب واحد قد يستغرق شهوراً."},{"k":"gap","q":"Visitors go through the ___ door to the Energy Gallery.","a":["blue"],"why":"الباب الأزرق في نهاية القاعة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The museum closes at ___.","a":["5.30","5:30","five thirty","half past five"],"why":"يغلق الخامسة والنصف."}]},{"id":"l-b1-09","l":"B1","title":"An interview with a young lawyer","part":3,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Today, I am talking to Samira, a young lawyer who works for a legal aid centre. Samira, why did you choose law?"},{"s":"B","t":"When I was fifteen, my uncle lost his job unfairly, and nobody explained his rights to him."},{"s":"B","t":"I decided then that I wanted to help people understand the law."},{"s":"A","t":"What does a normal day look like?"},{"s":"B","t":"There is no normal day, really. In the morning, I usually meet clients who cannot afford a private lawyer."},{"s":"B","t":"Many of them have problems with rent, work contracts or family issues."},{"s":"A","t":"Is it a stressful job?"},{"s":"B","t":"Sometimes, yes. There is a lot of paperwork, and some cases take months. But when a family keeps their home because of my work, it is very rewarding."},{"s":"A","t":"What skills are most important?"},{"s":"B","t":"Listening, first of all. People often need to tell their story before you can help them. Then, clear writing, because a letter to a court must be precise."},{"s":"A","t":"Do you use technology?"},{"s":"B","t":"More and more. We use software to find old cases quickly, which saves hours every week."},{"s":"A","t":"What is your advice for law students?"},{"s":"B","t":"Do an internship during your studies. You learn more in one month in a real office than in a whole semester of reading."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, Samira."}],"gloss":[{"w":"legal aid","ar":"مساعدة قانونية"},{"w":"rights","ar":"حقوق"},{"w":"rewarding","ar":"مجزٍ"},{"w":"internship","ar":"تدريب عملي"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why did Samira decide to study law?","o":["Her uncle lost his job unfairly","Her father was a judge","She liked debates"],"a":0,"why":"فقد عمها وظيفته ظلماً."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who are Samira's clients?","o":["Rich companies","People who cannot afford a private lawyer","Only students"],"a":1,"why":"عملاؤها لا يستطيعون دفع أتعاب محامٍ خاص."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Her job is never stressful.","a":"F","why":"قالت: Sometimes, yes."},{"k":"gap","q":"She says the most important skill is ___.","a":["listening"],"why":"قالت: Listening, first of all."},{"k":"mc","q":"How does software help the centre?","o":["It writes letters","It finds old cases quickly","It meets clients"],"a":1,"why":"يجد القضايا القديمة بسرعة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Her advice is to do an ___ during your studies.","a":["internship"],"why":"نصحت بالتدريب العملي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Samira says reading for a semester teaches more than a month in an office.","a":"F","why":"قالت العكس تماماً."}]},{"id":"l-b1-10","l":"B1","title":"A podcast about saving money","part":2,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello and welcome to Smart Student, the podcast about making life easier on a small budget."},{"s":"A","t":"I am your host, and today I have four simple ideas for saving money."},{"s":"A","t":"The first idea is to track your spending. For one month, write down everything you buy, even a small coffee."},{"s":"A","t":"Most people are surprised to find that these little purchases add up to about thirty or forty pounds a month."},{"s":"A","t":"The second idea is to plan your meals."},{"s":"A","t":"When you cook at home and carry your lunch, you can easily save fifty pounds a month compared with eating out."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, use your student card. Many shops, cinemas and train companies give discounts of ten to twenty percent, but a lot of students forget to ask."},{"s":"A","t":"My fourth idea is to save first, and spend later. On the day you receive your money, move a small amount, perhaps ten percent, to a separate account."},{"s":"A","t":"If you don't see it, you won't spend it."},{"s":"A","t":"Remember, you don't have to change your life in one day. Start with one idea this week, and add another next month."},{"s":"A","t":"That is all for today. Next time, we will talk about buying second-hand books. Goodbye!"}],"gloss":[{"w":"budget","ar":"ميزانية"},{"w":"track","ar":"يتتبّع"},{"w":"discount","ar":"خصم"},{"w":"second-hand","ar":"مستعمل"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The host has ___ ideas for saving money.","a":["4","four"],"why":"قال: four simple ideas."},{"k":"mc","q":"What should listeners write down?","o":["Their plans","Everything they buy","Their marks"],"a":1,"why":"يدوّنون كل ما يشترونه."},{"k":"gap","q":"Small purchases can add up to about 30 or ___ pounds a month.","a":["40","forty"],"why":"ثلاثين أو أربعين جنيهاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"How much can cooking at home save?","o":["About 15 pounds","About 50 pounds","About 100 pounds"],"a":1,"why":"حوالي خمسين جنيهاً شهرياً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Student card discounts are usually between 10 and 20 percent.","a":"T","why":"قال: ten to twenty percent."},{"k":"mc","q":"What should you do on the day you receive money?","o":["Spend it on bills","Move a small amount to another account","Lend it to a friend"],"a":1,"why":"حوّل مبلغاً صغيراً إلى حساب منفصل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is next week's topic?","o":["Cheap flights","Second-hand books","Student jobs"],"a":1,"why":"الحلقة القادمة عن الكتب المستعملة."}]},{"id":"l-b1-11","l":"B1","title":"Complaining politely about an internet connection","part":1,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Customer service, Mark speaking. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Hello, Mark. I am calling about my internet connection. I am afraid it has been very slow for the last two weeks."},{"s":"A","t":"I am sorry to hear that. Can I have your account number?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, it is four eight one, nine two six."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you. Let me look. Which package do you have?"},{"s":"B","t":"The fifty megabit package. But in the evening, I can hardly watch a video, and my online classes keep stopping."},{"s":"A","t":"I see. Have you tried restarting the router?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, I turned it off for a minute several times, and I also moved it into the living room. It didn't help."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. I can see some problems on your line. I will send an engineer to check it."},{"s":"B","t":"When could he come? I am at university in the daytime."},{"s":"A","t":"We have a free slot on Thursday between five and seven in the evening."},{"s":"B","t":"That works for me. Will I have to pay for the visit?"},{"s":"A","t":"No, it is free, because the fault is on our side. And I will give you a ten dollar credit on your next bill."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you. I appreciate your help."}],"gloss":[{"w":"connection","ar":"اتصال"},{"w":"router","ar":"جهاز التوجيه"},{"w":"engineer","ar":"فني"},{"w":"credit","ar":"رصيد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How long has the connection been slow?","o":["Two days","Two weeks","Two months"],"a":1,"why":"منذ أسبوعين."},{"k":"gap","q":"The customer has the ___ megabit package.","a":["50","fifty"],"why":"باقة خمسين ميغابت."},{"k":"mc","q":"What problem does he have in the evening?","o":["No connection at all","Videos and online classes stop","The router is hot"],"a":1,"why":"لا يستطيع مشاهدة فيديو والدروس تتوقف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"He moved the router to the living room.","a":"T","why":"قال إنه نقله إلى غرفة المعيشة."},{"k":"mc","q":"When can the engineer come?","o":["Thursday evening","Friday morning","Monday afternoon"],"a":0,"why":"يوم الخميس بين الخامسة والسابعة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The customer must pay for the engineer's visit.","a":"F","why":"الزيارة مجانية لأن العطل من جهة الشركة."},{"k":"gap","q":"He will get a ___ dollar credit.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"رصيد عشرة دولارات."}]},{"id":"l-b1-12","l":"B1","title":"A short lecture: how the internet works","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Today, I would like to give you a simple picture of how the internet works."},{"s":"A","t":"First, the internet is not one machine."},{"s":"A","t":"It is a huge network of millions of computers, phones and servers that are connected with each other by cables, wireless signals and satellites."},{"s":"A","t":"Most of the data, about ninety-five percent, travels through cables under the sea."},{"s":"A","t":"When you open a web page, your computer sends a request. Think of it as a letter with an address."},{"s":"A","t":"This address is called an IP address, and every device has one."},{"s":"A","t":"The request does not travel in one piece. Instead, it is cut into small packets."},{"s":"A","t":"Each packet can take a different route, like cars choosing different roads to the same city."},{"s":"A","t":"At the end, your computer puts the packets back in the correct order."},{"s":"A","t":"Servers are computers that store web pages and send them to you when you ask."},{"s":"A","t":"Large companies keep thousands of servers in big buildings called data centres, which need a lot of electricity and cooling."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, a word about names. People remember words, not numbers, so a system called the domain name system works like a phone book."},{"s":"A","t":"It changes a name like a university website into the correct IP address."},{"s":"A","t":"Next week, we will discuss internet security."}],"gloss":[{"w":"network","ar":"شبكة"},{"w":"packet","ar":"حزمة بيانات"},{"w":"server","ar":"خادم"},{"w":"data centre","ar":"مركز بيانات"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the internet, according to the lecturer?","o":["One large machine","A network of many connected devices","A kind of software"],"a":1,"why":"شبكة ضخمة من أجهزة كثيرة متصلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"About ___ percent of data travels through undersea cables.","a":["95","ninety-five","ninety five"],"why":"قال: about ninety-five percent."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does an IP address resemble?","o":["A telephone number","The address on a letter","A password"],"a":1,"why":"شبّهه بعنوان على رسالة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A request travels as one big piece.","a":"F","why":"يُقسَّم الطلب إلى حزم صغيرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do data centres need a lot of?","o":["Water and paper","Electricity and cooling","Workers and cars"],"a":1,"why":"تحتاج كهرباء وتبريداً."},{"k":"gap","q":"The domain name system works like a ___.","a":["phone book","phonebook","telephone book"],"why":"يعمل مثل دليل الهاتف."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is next week's topic?","o":["Internet security","Social media","Programming"],"a":0,"why":"الأسبوع القادم عن أمن الإنترنت."}]},{"id":"l-b1-13","l":"B1","title":"Two colleagues organise a meeting","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hi, Omar. Do you have a minute? We need to organise the team meeting for next week."},{"s":"B","t":"Of course. The last one was on a Monday, but several people couldn't come. How about Wednesday?"},{"s":"A","t":"Wednesday is better for me, too. Shall we say ten o'clock in the morning?"},{"s":"B","t":"Ten is fine. But I have to leave at eleven thirty for a visit to a client, so the meeting should not be longer than ninety minutes."},{"s":"A","t":"Right. I think an hour will be enough. Which room should we use?"},{"s":"B","t":"The big meeting room is already booked. We could use the small one on the third floor, but it only has eight chairs."},{"s":"A","t":"There are nine of us, so that is a problem. Perhaps we can borrow a chair from the office next door."},{"s":"B","t":"Good idea. And what is on the agenda?"},{"s":"A","t":"First, the budget for next year. Then, the new customer service system. And finally, the holiday plan for the summer."},{"s":"B","t":"Should we send the agenda in advance?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, I will email it tomorrow morning. Could you bring the printed budget report? Ten copies, please."},{"s":"B","t":"No problem. Do we need to order coffee and biscuits?"},{"s":"A","t":"Just coffee, I think. I will ask the kitchen."}],"gloss":[{"w":"agenda","ar":"جدول الأعمال"},{"w":"client","ar":"عميل"},{"w":"in advance","ar":"مسبقاً"},{"w":"budget","ar":"ميزانية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which day do they choose?","o":["Monday","Wednesday","Friday"],"a":1,"why":"اختارا يوم الأربعاء."},{"k":"gap","q":"The meeting starts at ___ o'clock.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"يبدأ عند العاشرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why can't the meeting be very long?","o":["Omar has a client visit","The room is booked","There are no chairs"],"a":0,"why":"عمر يغادر الحادية عشرة والنصف لزيارة عميل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the problem with the small room?","o":["It has no screen","It has only eight chairs","It is too cold"],"a":1,"why":"فيها ثمانية كراسي فقط والمشاركون تسعة."},{"k":"gap","q":"There are ___ people in the team.","a":["9","nine"],"why":"قالت: There are nine of us."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The last item on the agenda is the holiday plan.","a":"T","why":"آخر بند هو خطة الإجازة الصيفية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Omar will bring ___ copies of the budget report.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"عشر نسخ."}]},{"id":"l-b1-14","l":"B1","title":"A guide to a university campus","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello, new students, and welcome to Hillside University. My name is Jake, and I am a third-year student. Let me show you the most important places on campus."},{"s":"A","t":"We are standing in front of the Main Building. This is where you will find the registration office, which is open from nine to four, Monday to Friday."},{"s":"A","t":"You need to go there to collect your student card."},{"s":"A","t":"Behind it, you can see the Central Library. It has four floors, and the top floor is silent. During exams, it stays open twenty-four hours a day."},{"s":"A","t":"On the right, the glass building is the Science Centre, with laboratories and lecture halls. Most first-year science classes are held here."},{"s":"A","t":"If you walk down the hill, you will reach the Student Hub. It has a cafe, a small shop and a room where you can relax between classes."},{"s":"A","t":"A lunch there costs about five dollars."},{"s":"A","t":"The sports hall is next to the car park. Membership is free for students, and it has a gym and a swimming pool."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, remember that the campus bus runs every ten minutes from the main gate to the student housing. It is free if you show your card."},{"s":"A","t":"Now, let's go and see the library."}],"gloss":[{"w":"campus","ar":"الحرم الجامعي"},{"w":"registration","ar":"التسجيل"},{"w":"laboratory","ar":"مختبر"},{"w":"student housing","ar":"سكن الطلاب"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Who is Jake?","o":["A teacher","A third-year student","A librarian"],"a":1,"why":"قال إنه طالب في السنة الثالثة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do new students go to the registration office?","o":["To pay fees","To collect their student card","To book a room"],"a":1,"why":"لاستلام بطاقة الطالب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The whole library is silent.","a":"F","why":"الطابق العلوي فقط هادئ تماماً."},{"k":"gap","q":"During exams, the library is open ___ hours a day.","a":["24","twenty-four","twenty four"],"why":"يبقى مفتوحاً أربعاً وعشرين ساعة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where are most first-year science classes?","o":["The Main Building","The Science Centre","The Student Hub"],"a":1,"why":"في المركز العلمي."},{"k":"gap","q":"A lunch at the Student Hub costs about ___ dollars.","a":["5","five"],"why":"نحو خمسة دولارات."},{"k":"mc","q":"How often does the campus bus run?","o":["Every five minutes","Every ten minutes","Every half hour"],"a":1,"why":"كل عشر دقائق."}]},{"id":"l-b1-15","l":"B1","title":"A talk on transparency in local government","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon. Today, I would like to talk about transparency in local government, and why it matters for ordinary citizens."},{"s":"A","t":"Transparency means that the public can see how decisions are made and how public money is spent."},{"s":"A","t":"In a transparent town, the council publishes its budget, its contracts and the minutes of its meetings, so that anyone can read them."},{"s":"A","t":"Why is this important? There are three main reasons. First, transparency helps to prevent corruption."},{"s":"A","t":"When officials know that people can check their decisions, they are less likely to misuse public money."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, it builds trust. Citizens are more willing to pay taxes and to follow rules when they understand what happens to their money."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, it improves services."},{"s":"A","t":"When residents can see which roads are repaired and which schools are built, they can say what they need, and the council can plan better."},{"s":"A","t":"How can a town become more transparent? Some councils publish contracts on a website, where citizens can search them."},{"s":"A","t":"Others hold open meetings, or let people ask questions online. A simple measure is to publish every contract above a certain value, for example ten thousand pounds."},{"s":"A","t":"Of course, transparency is not enough on its own. People also need to know their rights, and the authorities must answer their questions within a reasonable time."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"transparency","ar":"شفافية"},{"w":"minutes","ar":"محضر الاجتماع"},{"w":"contract","ar":"عقد"},{"w":"misuse","ar":"يسيء استخدام"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does transparency mean in the talk?","o":["Spending less money","Public access to how decisions are made and money is spent","Hiring more officials"],"a":1,"why":"أن يرى الناس كيف تُتخذ القرارات وتُنفق الأموال."},{"k":"gap","q":"The council publishes its budget, its contracts and the ___ of its meetings.","a":["minutes"],"why":"تنشر محاضر الاجتماعات."},{"k":"gap","q":"There are ___ main reasons why transparency is important.","a":["3","three"],"why":"قال: three main reasons."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the second reason?","o":["It builds trust","It saves time","It reduces taxes"],"a":0,"why":"السبب الثاني هو بناء الثقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Residents who see which roads are repaired can say what they need.","a":"T","why":"هذا ذُكر ضمن تحسين الخدمات."},{"k":"gap","q":"An example is to publish every contract above ___ pounds.","a":["10000","10,000","ten thousand","10 000"],"why":"قال: ten thousand pounds."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The speaker thinks transparency alone is enough.","a":"F","why":"قال إنها وحدها لا تكفي."}]},{"id":"l-b1-16","l":"B1","title":"A phone call to a bank about a new card","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, Northern Bank. This is Helen. How can I help?"},{"s":"B","t":"Good morning. I would like to order a new debit card, please. My old one stopped working last week."},{"s":"A","t":"I am sorry about that. For security, I need to ask a few questions. Can you give me your full name and date of birth?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. It is Rami Haddad, and my birth date is the twelfth of March, nineteen ninety-eight."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you. And the first line of your address?"},{"s":"B","t":"Fourteen Elm Road."},{"s":"A","t":"That is correct. Did you lose the card, or is it just damaged?"},{"s":"B","t":"It is damaged. The chip is broken, so the machines don't read it."},{"s":"A","t":"In that case, there is no charge for the replacement. The new card will arrive in five working days."},{"s":"B","t":"Can I collect it from the branch instead? I am often out during the day."},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, you can. Which branch is nearest to you?"},{"s":"B","t":"The one in Market Street."},{"s":"A","t":"I will arrange that. Please remember to bring your passport when you collect the card."},{"s":"B","t":"Will I keep the same PIN?"},{"s":"A","t":"No, you will get a new one by post, in a separate letter."}],"gloss":[{"w":"debit card","ar":"بطاقة مصرفية"},{"w":"replacement","ar":"بديل"},{"w":"branch","ar":"فرع"},{"w":"PIN","ar":"الرقم السري"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why does the customer need a new card?","o":["He lost it","It is damaged","It was stolen"],"a":1,"why":"الشريحة مكسورة."},{"k":"gap","q":"His surname is ___.","a":["Haddad"],"why":"الاسم Rami Haddad."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is his date of birth?","o":["12 March 1998","13 March 1989","2 December 1998"],"a":0,"why":"ولد في الثاني عشر من مارس 1998."},{"k":"gap","q":"The first line of his address is ___ Elm Road.","a":["14","fourteen"],"why":"قال: Fourteen Elm Road."},{"k":"tfng","q":"He has to pay for the replacement.","a":"F","why":"لا رسوم على الاستبدال."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long does the new card take to arrive?","o":["Two working days","Five working days","Ten working days"],"a":1,"why":"خمسة أيام عمل."},{"k":"mc","q":"How will he get his new PIN?","o":["By post","By phone","At the branch"],"a":0,"why":"سيصله بالبريد في رسالة منفصلة."}]},{"id":"l-b1-17","l":"B1","title":"Students discuss a survey","part":3,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Okay, we have collected all the answers. Let's look at the results of our survey about student transport. Maya, how many people replied?"},{"s":"B","t":"One hundred and twenty. That is more than we expected. We only planned for a hundred."},{"s":"C","t":"And what were the main findings, Maya?"},{"s":"B","t":"Well, forty-five percent of the students come to campus by bus. Thirty percent walk, and only fifteen percent come by car."},{"s":"C","t":"I am surprised that so few use bicycles."},{"s":"B","t":"Only ten percent do. Many said that the roads aren't safe, and there is nowhere to park a bike."},{"s":"A","t":"That could be an interesting recommendation. We could suggest more bicycle racks near the library."},{"s":"C","t":"Yes. What about the bus? Did people complain?"},{"s":"B","t":"Quite a lot. Sixty students said that the buses are too crowded in the morning, and some mentioned that they wait more than twenty minutes."},{"s":"A","t":"Then we should include a graph about waiting times. Leo, can you make it?"},{"s":"C","t":"Sure, I will do it tonight. When is the report due?"},{"s":"A","t":"Next Friday. So let's meet again on Monday to check everything."},{"s":"B","t":"I will write the introduction and the conclusion by then."}],"gloss":[{"w":"survey","ar":"استبيان"},{"w":"findings","ar":"النتائج"},{"w":"recommendation","ar":"توصية"},{"w":"crowded","ar":"مزدحم"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"How many students answered the survey? ___","a":["120","one hundred and twenty","one hundred twenty","a hundred and twenty"],"why":"أجاب مئة وعشرون طالباً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What percentage of students come by bus?","o":["30 percent","45 percent","15 percent"],"a":1,"why":"خمسة وأربعون بالمئة بالحافلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Only ___ percent of students use bicycles.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"عشرة بالمئة فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do they suggest to help cyclists?","o":["More bicycle racks","A new road","Cheaper bicycles"],"a":0,"why":"اقتراح مزيد من حوامل الدراجات قرب المكتبة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Sixty students said the buses are too crowded in the morning.","a":"T","why":"قالت مايا: ستون طالباً اشتكوا من ازدحام الحافلات صباحاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What will Leo make?","o":["A graph about waiting times","The conclusion","A poster"],"a":0,"why":"ليو سيعدّ رسماً بيانياً عن أوقات الانتظار."},{"k":"mc","q":"When do they meet again?","o":["Friday","Monday","Tonight"],"a":1,"why":"سيلتقون يوم الاثنين."}]},{"id":"l-b1-18","l":"B1","title":"A short lecture: what artificial intelligence is","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, everyone. In today's lecture, I would like to explain what artificial intelligence is, and what it is not."},{"s":"A","t":"Artificial intelligence, or AI, is the ability of a computer system to do tasks that normally need human thinking."},{"s":"A","t":"For example, it can recognise a face in a photo, translate a sentence, or suggest a film that you might like."},{"s":"A","t":"Most AI today is based on machine learning. In traditional programming, a person writes exact rules."},{"s":"A","t":"In machine learning, the computer receives thousands or millions of examples and finds the patterns by itself."},{"s":"A","t":"If you show it a million photos of cats, it learns what a cat looks like."},{"s":"A","t":"That means that the quality of the data is very important. If the examples are poor or unfair, the system will make poor or unfair decisions."},{"s":"A","t":"This problem is called bias."},{"s":"A","t":"Today, AI is used in many fields. In medicine, it helps doctors to read scans. In law, it can search thousands of documents in minutes."},{"s":"A","t":"In banks, it can spot unusual payments that may be fraud."},{"s":"A","t":"However, AI does not understand the world like a person. It does not have feelings, and it can make mistakes with great confidence."},{"s":"A","t":"So, humans must check important decisions."},{"s":"A","t":"In the next lecture, we will look at some ethical questions. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"artificial intelligence","ar":"ذكاء اصطناعي"},{"w":"pattern","ar":"نمط"},{"w":"bias","ar":"تحيّز"},{"w":"fraud","ar":"احتيال"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How does the lecturer define AI?","o":["A computer system doing tasks that need human thinking","A robot with feelings","A type of internet"],"a":0,"why":"نظام حاسوبي يؤدي مهام تحتاج تفكيراً بشرياً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What happens in machine learning?","o":["A person writes every rule","The computer finds patterns in examples","The computer copies a human"],"a":1,"why":"الحاسوب يجد الأنماط من أمثلة كثيرة."},{"k":"gap","q":"If you show a computer a million photos of ___, it learns what they look like.","a":["cats"],"why":"استخدم مثال القطط."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is bias?","o":["Unfair decisions caused by poor data","A fast computer","A kind of photo"],"a":0,"why":"قرارات غير عادلة بسبب بيانات سيئة."},{"k":"gap","q":"In medicine, AI helps doctors to read ___.","a":["scans"],"why":"يساعد الأطباء على قراءة الصور الطبية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"AI can search thousands of legal documents in minutes.","a":"T","why":"قال ذلك عن مجال القانون."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says AI understands the world like a person.","a":"F","why":"قال إنه لا يفهم العالم مثل الإنسان."}]},{"id":"l-b1-19","l":"B1","title":"Arranging a doctor's appointment","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, Oakfield Medical Centre. How can I help you?"},{"s":"B","t":"Hello. I would like to make an appointment with Doctor Evans, please. I have had a bad cough for ten days, and it is not getting better."},{"s":"A","t":"I am sorry to hear that. Are you a patient with us?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. My name is Salma Karim. That is S, A, L, M, A, and K, A, R, I, M."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, Salma. Doctor Evans is very busy this week. The first appointment is on Thursday at two fifteen."},{"s":"B","t":"Thursday is a problem for me, because I have an exam in the afternoon. Is there anything earlier?"},{"s":"A","t":"There is one free time tomorrow at nine forty, but it is with Doctor Brown, not Doctor Evans."},{"s":"B","t":"That is fine. I only want to see a doctor soon."},{"s":"A","t":"Do you have a high temperature or trouble breathing?"},{"s":"B","t":"No, but I wake up at night because of the cough."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. Please arrive ten minutes early, and bring your medical card. If it gets worse before tomorrow, call us at once or go to the emergency department."},{"s":"B","t":"I will. Thank you."},{"s":"A","t":"You're welcome. See you tomorrow, Salma."}],"gloss":[{"w":"appointment","ar":"موعد"},{"w":"cough","ar":"سعال"},{"w":"patient","ar":"مريض"},{"w":"emergency","ar":"طوارئ"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The patient has had a cough for ___ days.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"قالت: for ten days."},{"k":"mc","q":"When is the first appointment with Doctor Evans?","o":["Tomorrow at 9.40","Thursday at 2.15","Friday at 9.15"],"a":1,"why":"أقرب موعد الخميس الساعة 2:15."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Salma chooses the Thursday appointment.","a":"F","why":"لديها امتحان بعد الظهر يوم الخميس."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who will she see?","o":["Doctor Evans","Doctor Brown","A nurse"],"a":1,"why":"ستقابل الدكتور براون غداً."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does she wake up at night?","o":["Because of the cough","Because of a headache","Because of a fever"],"a":0,"why":"بسبب السعال."},{"k":"gap","q":"She must arrive ___ minutes early.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"عليها الوصول قبل عشر دقائق."},{"k":"gap","q":"She should bring her ___ card.","a":["medical"],"why":"تحضر بطاقتها الطبية."}]},{"id":"l-b1-20","l":"B1","title":"A volunteer coordinator explains tasks","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello, everyone, and thank you for volunteering for the Community Food Day. My name is Rachel, and I am the volunteer coordinator."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me explain what you will be doing."},{"s":"A","t":"The event takes place this Saturday at the community hall, from eight in the morning until four in the afternoon."},{"s":"A","t":"Please arrive by seven thirty so that we can give you a short briefing."},{"s":"A","t":"There are four main jobs. The first team will unload the trucks and sort the food into boxes."},{"s":"A","t":"This work is heavy, so we need strong volunteers, and please wear comfortable shoes."},{"s":"A","t":"The second team will work at the registration table. They will welcome families and write down their names and the number of people in each household."},{"s":"A","t":"The third team will pack the boxes. Each box should include rice, pasta, oil, canned vegetables and one fruit item. A checklist will be on the wall."},{"s":"A","t":"The fourth team will help elderly people to carry their boxes to their cars or to the bus stop."},{"s":"A","t":"Lunch will be provided at noon, and you will have a thirty-minute break. Everyone will receive a green T-shirt."},{"s":"A","t":"Please wear it all day, so people can recognise you."},{"s":"A","t":"If you can't come, please call me on Thursday at the latest. Thank you, and see you on Saturday."}],"gloss":[{"w":"volunteer","ar":"متطوع"},{"w":"briefing","ar":"إحاطة موجزة"},{"w":"household","ar":"أسرة"},{"w":"elderly","ar":"كبار السن"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The volunteer coordinator's name is ___.","a":["Rachel"],"why":"قالت: My name is Rachel."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time should volunteers arrive?","o":["At 7.30","At 8.00","At 8.30"],"a":0,"why":"الوصول قبل السابعة والنصف."},{"k":"mc","q":"What will the first team do?","o":["Unload trucks and sort food","Pack boxes","Welcome families"],"a":0,"why":"تفريغ الشاحنات وفرز الطعام."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the registration team write down?","o":["Phone numbers","Names and the number of people in each household","Car numbers"],"a":1,"why":"أسماء الأسر وعدد أفرادها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Each box contains meat and fruit juice.","a":"F","why":"الصندوق فيه أرز ومعكرونة وزيت وخضار معلبة وثمرة فاكهة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Lunch is at ___.","a":["noon","12","twelve","12.00","12:00"],"why":"الغداء عند الظهر."},{"k":"mc","q":"What colour is the T-shirt?","o":["Blue","Green","White"],"a":1,"why":"القميص أخضر."}]},{"id":"l-b1-21","l":"B1","title":"A short lecture: how a trial works","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello, and welcome to this introduction to the law. Today, I will explain, step by step, how a criminal trial works."},{"s":"A","t":"A trial begins when the prosecutor presents the charge. The prosecutor represents the state, and must prove that the accused person is guilty."},{"s":"A","t":"In most legal systems, the accused is considered innocent until this is proved."},{"s":"A","t":"Next, the defence lawyer speaks. This lawyer protects the rights of the accused, and may ask the court to reject weak evidence."},{"s":"A","t":"After that, both sides call witnesses. A witness is a person who saw something or knows something about the case."},{"s":"A","t":"Each witness gives evidence under oath, which means that he or she promises to tell the truth."},{"s":"A","t":"Then the lawyers can question the witness, and this is called cross-examination."},{"s":"A","t":"Experts may also be invited. For example, a doctor may explain an injury, or a specialist may explain bank records."},{"s":"A","t":"When all the evidence has been heard, each side gives a closing speech. Finally, the judge, or in some systems a jury of ordinary citizens, decides."},{"s":"A","t":"If the person is found guilty, the judge decides on the punishment. If not, the accused goes free."},{"s":"A","t":"The accused may appeal to a higher court if he or she believes that there was a mistake."},{"s":"A","t":"In the next lecture, we will study appeals in more detail."}],"gloss":[{"w":"prosecutor","ar":"المدّعي العام"},{"w":"witness","ar":"شاهد"},{"w":"oath","ar":"قَسَم"},{"w":"appeal","ar":"استئناف"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Who must prove that the accused is guilty?","o":["The defence lawyer","The prosecutor","The witness"],"a":1,"why":"المدعي العام هو من يثبت الذنب."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The accused is considered innocent until proved guilty.","a":"T","why":"هذا هو المبدأ في معظم النظم."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the job of the defence lawyer?","o":["To protect the rights of the accused","To decide the punishment","To explain bank records"],"a":0,"why":"يحمي حقوق المتهم."},{"k":"gap","q":"A witness gives evidence under ___.","a":["oath"],"why":"الشاهد يدلي بشهادته تحت القسم."},{"k":"gap","q":"Questioning a witness is called ___-examination.","a":["cross"],"why":"يُسمى استجواب الشهود cross-examination."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who decides the verdict in some systems?","o":["A jury","A police officer","A teacher"],"a":0,"why":"في بعض النظم هيئة محلفين."},{"k":"mc","q":"What may the accused do if he thinks there was a mistake?","o":["Appeal to a higher court","Leave the court","Call the police"],"a":0,"why":"يستأنف أمام محكمة أعلى."}]},{"id":"l-b1-22","l":"B1","title":"Two friends compare phones","part":1,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hey, Yasmin, is that a new phone? It looks different from your old one."},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, I bought it last month. My old one was four years old, and the battery was terrible. It only lasted half a day."},{"s":"A","t":"How is the new one?"},{"s":"B","t":"Much better. The battery lasts almost two days, and the camera is excellent. Look at this photo of the sea."},{"s":"A","t":"Wow, it is really clear. I have the same brand, but mine takes poor pictures at night."},{"s":"B","t":"How old is yours?"},{"s":"A","t":"Two years. The screen is cracked, as you can see, and it is getting slower and slower."},{"s":"B","t":"Why don't you change it? Are the new models very expensive?"},{"s":"A","t":"Mine would be about six hundred pounds, which is too much for me. I am thinking about buying a second-hand one for two hundred."},{"s":"B","t":"That is a good idea, but be careful. Make sure that the battery is still in good condition and that the phone is not locked to one network."},{"s":"A","t":"Thanks, I will remember that. How much was yours?"},{"s":"B","t":"It cost four hundred and fifty, but I paid in monthly instalments, so it didn't feel so expensive."},{"s":"A","t":"Maybe I should do the same."}],"gloss":[{"w":"battery","ar":"بطارية"},{"w":"cracked","ar":"مشقوق"},{"w":"second-hand","ar":"مستعمل"},{"w":"instalment","ar":"قسط"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why did Yasmin change her phone?","o":["The battery was terrible","The screen broke","She lost it"],"a":0,"why":"البطارية كانت سيئة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Her new battery lasts almost ___ days.","a":["2","two"],"why":"تدوم نحو يومين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Her friend's camera works well at night.","a":"F","why":"صور صديقها سيئة في الليل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is wrong with the friend's phone?","o":["The screen is cracked and it is slow","It has no camera","It is too heavy"],"a":0,"why":"الشاشة مشقوقة والهاتف بطيء."},{"k":"gap","q":"A new model would cost about ___ pounds.","a":["600","six hundred"],"why":"قال: about six hundred pounds."},{"k":"mc","q":"What advice does Yasmin give about second-hand phones?","o":["Check the battery and that it is not locked","Buy the cheapest","Buy only in shops"],"a":0,"why":"تأكد من البطارية وأنه غير مقفل على شبكة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Yasmin's phone cost ___ pounds.","a":["450","four hundred and fifty","four hundred fifty"],"why":"سعره أربعمئة وخمسون جنيهاً."}]},{"id":"l-b2-01","l":"B2","title":"Welcome talk for new university students","part":2,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Hartley University. My name is Dr Nora Finch, and I am the head of student support."},{"s":"A","t":"I know many of you are nervous today, so I will keep this short and practical."},{"s":"A","t":"I will talk about four things: your cards, the library, your timetable, and where to find help."},{"s":"A","t":"First, your student cards. Some of you have already queued at the main office, but that is not where you collect them."},{"s":"A","t":"Cards are issued at the Student Hub, in the old chapel building."},{"s":"A","t":"Please bring your passport or national identity card, and the letter confirming your place."},{"s":"A","t":"The photo will be taken on the spot, so there is no need to bring one."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, the library. It opens at eight in the morning and closes at ten at night on weekdays. At weekends it closes at six."},{"s":"A","t":"Sorry, I should say five on Sundays, because the staff finish early."},{"s":"A","t":"During the exam period in May, the ground floor stays open all night. Please do not bring hot food inside, but drinks with a lid are fine."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, your timetable. It appears in your online account from Friday. Do not worry if two classes seem to overlap."},{"s":"A","t":"This happens in the first week because some groups are still being finalised, and you will receive an email by Monday evening with the corrected version."},{"s":"A","t":"Fourth, getting help. Many students wait until they are in real difficulty before asking. Please do not do that."},{"s":"A","t":"Our advisers can help with money problems, health, housing and study skills."},{"s":"A","t":"You can book an appointment online, or simply walk in between ten and one on Tuesdays and Thursdays."},{"s":"A","t":"There is also a free writing clinic, which I strongly recommend if you have never written an academic essay."},{"s":"A","t":"One last thing. Tomorrow at two o'clock there will be a campus tour starting outside the library."},{"s":"A","t":"Actually, we have moved it to half past two, because the sports hall needs the earlier slot."},{"s":"A","t":"The tour lasts about forty minutes, and you will finish at the student café, where your first coffee is free."},{"s":"A","t":"Enjoy your first week, and remember that every student here started exactly where you are now."}],"gloss":[{"w":"issue","ar":"يُصدر / يمنح"},{"w":"timetable","ar":"جدول الدروس"},{"w":"overlap","ar":"يتداخل"},{"w":"finalise","ar":"يضع الصيغة النهائية"},{"w":"walk in","ar":"يأتي دون موعد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Where can students collect their cards?","o":["At the main office","At the Student Hub","At the library"],"a":1,"why":"البطاقات تُصدر في مركز الطلبة وليس في المكتب الرئيسي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Students must bring a passport or national ___ card.","a":["identity","ID"],"why":"سمعنا: passport or national identity card."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Students need to bring their own photo for the card.","a":"F","why":"المتحدثة قالت إن الصورة تُلتقط في المكان."},{"k":"mc","q":"When does the library close on Sundays?","o":["At ten at night","At six in the evening","At five in the evening"],"a":2,"why":"صحّحت المتحدثة نفسها: الخامسة أيام الأحد."},{"k":"gap","q":"During exams in May, the ground floor of the library stays open ___.","a":["all night"],"why":"الطابق الأرضي يبقى مفتوحاً طوال الليل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Overlapping classes in the first week will be fixed in a corrected timetable.","a":"T","why":"سيصل بريد إلكتروني بالجدول المصحح مساء الاثنين."},{"k":"mc","q":"When can students walk in to see an adviser without an appointment?","o":["Tuesdays and Thursdays","Mondays and Fridays","Every weekday"],"a":0,"why":"الإرشاد دون موعد: الثلاثاء والخميس من العاشرة إلى الواحدة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What time does the campus tour start?","o":["Two o'clock","Three o'clock","Half past two"],"a":2,"why":"تم تأخير الجولة إلى الثانية والنصف."}]},{"id":"l-b2-02","l":"B2","title":"A community recycling scheme","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"This is the Tuesday morning community report, and today I am in the town of Millbrook, where a small recycling scheme has become a big success."},{"s":"A","t":"The scheme began eighteen months ago with just four hundred households. Today more than two thousand three hundred families take part, which is roughly half the town."},{"s":"A","t":"The idea is simple. Each home has three bins. Blue is for paper and cardboard, green is for glass, and yellow is for cans and hard plastics."},{"s":"A","t":"Food scraps go into a small brown container collected every week."},{"s":"A","t":"Organiser Maria Lopez told me the early months were difficult."},{"s":"A","t":"Nearly a third of what people put in the bins could not be recycled at all, because it was dirty or in the wrong bin."},{"s":"A","t":"To fix that, the scheme trained forty volunteers, called street champions. Each one visits a few neighbours, explains the rules, and answers questions over a cup of tea."},{"s":"A","t":"The result is striking. The share of unusable waste has fallen from thirty percent to just nine percent."},{"s":"A","t":"There is also a reward system. Households collect points for every bag they recycle correctly."},{"s":"A","t":"At first the plan was to pay cash, but residents voted instead for vouchers to spend in local shops, and that has helped small businesses on the high street."},{"s":"A","t":"Money is being saved, too. Because less rubbish goes to landfill, the town pays lower fees, and the council says it saved about sixty thousand dollars last year."},{"s":"A","t":"All of that has gone into a new playground by the river."},{"s":"A","t":"Of course, not everyone is happy. Some residents complain that thin plastic film, like the wrapping on magazines, is still not accepted."},{"s":"A","t":"Maria says the machines at the sorting plant simply cannot handle it, but she is searching for a company that can."},{"s":"A","t":"The next step is the apartment blocks, which have shared bins and so have been harder to include."},{"s":"A","t":"Maria hopes to start in the spring, with the first building on Oak Street."},{"s":"A","t":"Reporting from Millbrook, I'm Jenna Walsh. Back to the studio."}],"gloss":[{"w":"scheme","ar":"مشروع / برنامج"},{"w":"landfill","ar":"مكب النفايات"},{"w":"household","ar":"أسرة / منزل"},{"w":"voucher","ar":"قسيمة شراء"},{"w":"scraps","ar":"بقايا"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"When did the scheme begin?","o":["Twelve months ago","Two years ago","Eighteen months ago"],"a":2,"why":"بدأ المشروع قبل ثمانية عشر شهراً."},{"k":"gap","q":"The yellow bin is for cans and hard ___.","a":["plastics","plastic"],"why":"الحاوية الصفراء للعلب والبلاستيك الصلب."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why were the early months difficult?","o":["Collections were often late","Too much waste was dirty or in the wrong bin","Not enough people had bins"],"a":1,"why":"قرابة الثلث لم يكن قابلاً لإعادة التدوير."},{"k":"gap","q":"The scheme trained ___ volunteers to visit neighbours.","a":["40","forty"],"why":"تم تدريب أربعين متطوعاً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Unusable waste is now less than a tenth of the total.","a":"T","why":"انخفضت النسبة من ثلاثين إلى تسعة بالمئة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did residents choose as a reward?","o":["Vouchers for local shops","Cash payments","Lower bin fees"],"a":0,"why":"صوّت السكان للقسائم بدلاً من المال."},{"k":"gap","q":"The savings paid for a new ___ by the river.","a":["playground"],"why":"وُجّهت الوفورات لملعب أطفال جديد."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Apartment blocks are already part of the scheme.","a":"F","why":"العمارات هي الخطوة التالية في الربيع."}]},{"id":"l-b2-03","l":"B2","title":"Introducing a science museum","part":2,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello, everyone, and welcome to the Riverside Science Museum. My name is Tom, and for the next forty-five minutes I will be your guide."},{"s":"A","t":"Before we begin, a few practical points. Large bags must go in the lockers by the entrance."},{"s":"A","t":"You need a two-dollar coin, but do not worry, you get it back when you open the locker again."},{"s":"A","t":"Photography is welcome everywhere except the planetarium. You may use your phone, but please switch off the flash, because it can damage some of our older exhibits."},{"s":"A","t":"Let us start here in the entrance hall. The tall swinging weight above you is a pendulum, nine metres long."},{"s":"A","t":"As the Earth turns, the pendulum slowly knocks down the small wooden blocks on the floor, one about every fifteen minutes."},{"s":"A","t":"Behind me is the Energy Hall on the ground floor."},{"s":"A","t":"You can pedal a bicycle to light up a model city, and you will quickly discover how hard it is to power even one street."},{"s":"A","t":"On the first floor you will find the Human Body gallery. The most popular exhibit is a giant model of the heart that you can walk through."},{"s":"A","t":"Visitors often tell me they never realised how loud it is inside."},{"s":"A","t":"The second floor has two attractions. One is the Space gallery, with a real meteorite you may touch."},{"s":"A","t":"The other is the planetarium, where there are two shows each day, at eleven fifteen and again at half past twelve."},{"s":"A","t":"Sorry, I mean at twelve forty-five, since we changed the schedule this month."},{"s":"A","t":"Children under ten must be with an adult in the experiment lab, which is also on the second floor."},{"s":"A","t":"Today's experiment is making slime, which is always a favourite."},{"s":"A","t":"When you are tired, head to the café on the top floor."},{"s":"A","t":"It has wonderful views of the river, but it closes at four, an hour before the museum itself."},{"s":"A","t":"The lifts are at the back of the building, past the gift shop. If you have any questions, please just ask me."},{"s":"A","t":"Now, if you would follow me to the Energy Hall."}],"gloss":[{"w":"exhibit","ar":"معروضة"},{"w":"pendulum","ar":"بندول"},{"w":"meteorite","ar":"نيزك"},{"w":"planetarium","ar":"قبة سماوية"},{"w":"locker","ar":"خزانة صغيرة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How long will the tour last?","o":["One hour","Thirty minutes","Forty-five minutes"],"a":2,"why":"المرشد قال خمساً وأربعين دقيقة."},{"k":"gap","q":"To use a locker you need a coin worth ___ dollars.","a":["2","two"],"why":"عملة بدولارين تُسترد لاحقاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why should visitors switch off the flash?","o":["It disturbs people in the planetarium","It uses too much battery","It can damage older exhibits"],"a":2,"why":"الفلاش قد يضر بالمعروضات القديمة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The pendulum in the entrance hall is ___ metres long.","a":["9","nine"],"why":"طول البندول تسعة أمتار."},{"k":"mc","q":"What can visitors do in the Energy Hall?","o":["Pedal a bicycle to light a model city","Walk through a model heart","Touch a real meteorite"],"a":0,"why":"في قاعة الطاقة تُشغَّل مدينة مصغرة بالدراجة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The giant model of the heart is on the second floor.","a":"F","why":"نموذج القلب في الطابق الأول."},{"k":"gap","q":"The second planetarium show starts at ___.","a":["12.45","12:45","twelve forty-five","12.45pm"],"why":"صحّح المرشد الوقت إلى الثانية عشرة وخمس وأربعين."},{"k":"mc","q":"When does the café close?","o":["Five o'clock","Half past four","Four o'clock"],"a":2,"why":"المقهى يُغلق في الرابعة، قبل المتحف بساعة."}]},{"id":"l-b2-04","l":"B2","title":"A new working from home policy","part":2,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon, everyone. Thank you for joining this call. I want to explain our new working from home policy, which begins on the first of March."},{"s":"A","t":"First, the background. For two years we have allowed home working on a case by case basis."},{"s":"A","t":"That led to confusion and, frankly, some unfairness, so we are replacing it with one clear policy."},{"s":"A","t":"The basic rule is this. Everyone may work from home for up to two days a week. The other three days are spent in the office."},{"s":"A","t":"To make sure teams can meet, Tuesdays and Thursdays are fixed office days for everybody."},{"s":"A","t":"We first considered Mondays and Thursdays, but the data showed far fewer people travel in on Mondays."},{"s":"A","t":"When you work from home, you should be online and reachable between ten and four. Outside these core hours, you can organise your day as you wish."},{"s":"A","t":"Now, equipment. The company will provide a laptop and a second screen, and you may take them home."},{"s":"A","t":"We will also pay a one-off allowance of a hundred and fifty pounds towards a proper chair or desk. You will need to send us the receipt."},{"s":"A","t":"We did discuss paying part of your broadband bill. In the end, finance decided it was too complicated to administer, so that will not be included."},{"s":"A","t":"Please remember security. Always use the company VPN, and avoid working on confidential files in cafés or on public wifi."},{"s":"A","t":"To apply, log in to the HR portal and choose your two days. Your line manager has five working days to approve or comment."},{"s":"A","t":"Most requests will be accepted, unless the nature of your job needs you on site, as with the reception team and the laboratory staff."},{"s":"A","t":"New employees can use the policy only after their three-month probation period ends."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, this is a six-month trial. In the fourth month we will send a short survey, and we will decide in September whether to make the policy permanent."},{"s":"A","t":"If you have questions, email me or visit the HR page. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"policy","ar":"سياسة / نظام"},{"w":"allowance","ar":"بدل مالي"},{"w":"probation","ar":"فترة التجربة"},{"w":"confidential","ar":"سري"},{"w":"permanent","ar":"دائم"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How many days a week may staff work from home?","o":["One","Two","Three"],"a":1,"why":"حتى يومين في الأسبوع."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which days are fixed office days?","o":["Mondays and Thursdays","Wednesdays and Fridays","Tuesdays and Thursdays"],"a":2,"why":"الاثنين تم النظر فيه ثم استُبعد."},{"k":"gap","q":"When working at home, staff must be online between ten and ___.","a":["four","4"],"why":"ساعات العمل الأساسية من العاشرة إلى الرابعة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The one-off allowance for a chair or desk is ___ pounds.","a":["150","a hundred and fifty","one hundred and fifty"],"why":"البدل مئة وخمسون جنيهاً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The company will pay part of each employee's broadband bill.","a":"F","why":"نوقشت الفكرة لكن تم رفضها."},{"k":"mc","q":"How long does a line manager have to respond to a request?","o":["Two days","Five working days","One week"],"a":1,"why":"للمدير خمسة أيام عمل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Some jobs, such as reception work, may need people to be on site.","a":"T","why":"طبيعة بعض الوظائف تتطلب الحضور."},{"k":"mc","q":"When will the company decide whether the policy is permanent?","o":["In March","In the fourth month","In September"],"a":2,"why":"القرار في سبتمبر بعد تجربة ستة أشهر."}]},{"id":"l-b2-05","l":"B2","title":"A talk for legal advice centre volunteers","part":2,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Welcome, everyone, and thank you for volunteering at the Eastside Legal Advice Centre. I am Rachel Moore, the volunteer coordinator."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me begin with the most important point. Volunteers do not give legal advice, not even simple advice, and not even if you are a law student."},{"s":"A","t":"Only our qualified lawyers do that. Your role is to help people reach them."},{"s":"A","t":"In practice, you will greet clients at reception, check their identity, and complete an intake form with their basic details and the nature of their problem."},{"s":"A","t":"You will also tell them how long they are likely to wait, which is usually about forty minutes."},{"s":"A","t":"About forty percent of our clients come with housing problems, such as unfair rent increases or a landlord refusing to repair something."},{"s":"A","t":"The next biggest group, around a quarter, have employment problems, mostly unpaid wages."},{"s":"A","t":"Many clients are frightened or embarrassed when they arrive, so please be patient. If someone becomes very upset or angry, do not argue."},{"s":"A","t":"Stay calm and call your supervisor."},{"s":"A","t":"Confidentiality is essential. Before your first shift you will sign an agreement."},{"s":"A","t":"You must never discuss a client's situation outside the centre, even without using their name, and you must not put private details in emails."},{"s":"A","t":"We also help people who do not speak English well."},{"s":"A","t":"We have a telephone interpreting service for more than thirty languages, so please never ask a family member or child to translate."},{"s":"A","t":"Now, the practical details. The centre is open Monday to Thursday from nine to five, and we run a free clinic on Saturday mornings."},{"s":"A","t":"Each shift lasts four hours."},{"s":"A","t":"We ask volunteers to commit to at least six months. I originally planned three, but training takes time, and clients benefit from familiar faces."},{"s":"A","t":"Training consists of two evening sessions. The first is next Tuesday at six o'clock in the meeting room upstairs."},{"s":"A","t":"You will receive an identity badge then, so there is no need to dress formally. Smart casual is fine."},{"s":"A","t":"Any questions before we begin the first session?"}],"gloss":[{"w":"client","ar":"عميل / مراجع"},{"w":"intake form","ar":"استمارة استقبال"},{"w":"confidentiality","ar":"السرية"},{"w":"landlord","ar":"مالك العقار"},{"w":"commit","ar":"يلتزم"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which task are volunteers allowed to do?","o":["Give simple advice","Complete intake forms","Speak for clients in court"],"a":1,"why":"المتطوعون لا يقدمون نصيحة قانونية، بل يملؤون الاستمارات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Clients usually wait about ___ minutes.","a":["40","forty"],"why":"زمن الانتظار المعتاد حوالي أربعين دقيقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which problem is the most common among clients?","o":["Employment problems","Family disputes","Housing problems"],"a":2,"why":"حوالي أربعين بالمئة مشاكل سكن."},{"k":"tfng","q":"If a client becomes angry, a volunteer should call a supervisor.","a":"T","why":"يجب الهدوء واستدعاء المشرف وعدم الجدال."},{"k":"gap","q":"The telephone interpreting service covers more than ___ languages.","a":["30","thirty"],"why":"أكثر من ثلاثين لغة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A volunteer may ask a client's child to translate if necessary.","a":"F","why":"طلبت المتحدثة ألا يُطلب ذلك من أحد أفراد الأسرة أو الطفل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the minimum time volunteers must commit to?","o":["Six months","Three months","One year"],"a":0,"why":"غيّرت المتحدثة الخطة الأصلية من ثلاثة أشهر إلى ستة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The first training session is on Tuesday at ___ o'clock.","a":["6","six"],"why":"الدورة الأولى الثلاثاء الساعة السادسة."}]},{"id":"l-b2-06","l":"B2","title":"Changes to a city transport network","part":2,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Attention, all passengers. This is an announcement from City Transit about important changes to our network, which start on the first Monday of next month."},{"s":"A","t":"Let us begin with the buses. Route twelve will no longer run along Station Road, because the road will be closed for repairs for about five months."},{"s":"A","t":"Instead, it will pass through Market Square, and the journey will take around six minutes longer."},{"s":"A","t":"Route seven, which many of you know, will be withdrawn completely."},{"s":"A","t":"Passengers who used it should take the new route twenty-seven, which follows almost the same path but also stops at the central library."},{"s":"A","t":"Now the trams. Line B will be extended by three stops, so that it finally reaches the university hospital."},{"s":"A","t":"Trams will run every ten minutes during the day, instead of every twelve."},{"s":"A","t":"These changes should connect the hospital with the city centre, which residents have requested for many years, and they should also make daily journeys faster."},{"s":"A","t":"At night, we are improving the service for people who work late or go out in the evening."},{"s":"A","t":"On Fridays and Saturdays, night buses will run every thirty minutes instead of every hour. Please note that this applies only to those two nights."},{"s":"A","t":"Turning to fares. A single ticket stays at two dollars fifty, so there is no change there."},{"s":"A","t":"However, the day pass will rise from seven dollars to eight dollars, the first increase in four years."},{"s":"A","t":"There is good news for students. The discount card will now give thirty percent off all tickets."},{"s":"A","t":"We had announced twenty-five percent last month, but the city council agreed to a larger reduction."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, cycling. Bicycle hire stations will open at twenty tram stops, and the first thirty minutes are free for card holders."},{"s":"A","t":"To plan your journey, update our app, which shows the new routes from today."},{"s":"A","t":"Information desks at the central station will also be open until nine every evening this week, and staff will be happy to help. Thank you for listening."}],"gloss":[{"w":"route","ar":"خط سير"},{"w":"withdraw","ar":"يُلغي / يسحب"},{"w":"extend","ar":"يمدّد"},{"w":"fare","ar":"أجرة"},{"w":"reduction","ar":"تخفيض"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why is route twelve changing its path?","o":["A road will be closed for repairs","There are fewer passengers","A new tram line is being built"],"a":0,"why":"طريق المحطة سيُغلق للإصلاح نحو خمسة أشهر."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which route replaces route seven?","o":["Route twelve","Route twenty-seven","Line B"],"a":1,"why":"الخط السابع ألغي وحلّ محله الخط السابع والعشرون."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Line B will reach the university hospital.","a":"T","why":"سيُمدّد الخط بثلاث محطات."},{"k":"gap","q":"On Fridays and Saturdays, night buses will run every ___ minutes.","a":["30","thirty"],"why":"كل نصف ساعة بدل كل ساعة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The price of a single ticket will go up.","a":"F","why":"التذكرة المفردة تبقى بسعر دولارين وخمسين سنتاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What will the new price of the day pass be?","o":["Seven dollars","Two dollars fifty","Eight dollars"],"a":2,"why":"سيرتفع من سبعة إلى ثمانية دولارات."},{"k":"mc","q":"How much discount will students get?","o":["Thirty percent","Twenty-five percent","Fifteen percent"],"a":0,"why":"وافق المجلس على تخفيض أكبر من الإعلان السابق."},{"k":"gap","q":"Bicycle hire stations will open at ___ tram stops.","a":["20","twenty"],"why":"عشرون محطة ترام."}]},{"id":"l-b2-07","l":"B2","title":"Discussing a research proposal","part":3,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Come in, both of you. I have read your draft proposal on social media use and sleep, and I think it has real potential."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you, Dr Hall. We were not sure about the title. Is it too long?"},{"s":"A","t":"The title is fine. My concern is the research question. At the moment you ask whether social media harms students' health, which is far too broad."},{"s":"C","t":"So we should focus on sleep only?"},{"s":"A","t":"Exactly, Ben. Something like, how does evening social media use affect the sleep of first-year students? That is specific and measurable."},{"s":"B","t":"That makes sense. We planned to survey two hundred students. Is that enough?"},{"s":"A","t":"More than enough for a project this size. A hundred and twenty would be realistic, and you would still have time to analyse the data properly."},{"s":"C","t":"Okay. I suggested using wearable sleep trackers, but Layla thought they would cost too much."},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, they were about eighty pounds each. Instead, we would use a questionnaire and a two-week sleep diary."},{"s":"A","t":"A sensible compromise. Diaries are not perfect, because people forget, but they are acceptable for an undergraduate study. Just make sure you explain the limitation."},{"s":"C","t":"Do we need ethical approval?"},{"s":"A","t":"Definitely, and before you collect any data. The committee usually takes three weeks, so submit the form as soon as you can."},{"s":"B","t":"And the literature review? How many studies do we need?"},{"s":"A","t":"At least ten, published in the last five years. Layla, you mentioned some research on teenagers."},{"s":"A","t":"That is useful as background, but your main sources should be about university students."},{"s":"B","t":"I will look for more this weekend."},{"s":"A","t":"Good. Your topic matters to many students, and the university may even use your findings, so please take the work seriously. Now, who will write which section?"},{"s":"C","t":"I can write the methodology, and Layla will do the introduction."},{"s":"A","t":"Fine. The final proposal is due next Friday at noon. Please send it to me by email on Thursday evening, so I can check it first."},{"s":"B","t":"We will do that. Thank you very much."}],"gloss":[{"w":"proposal","ar":"مقترح بحثي"},{"w":"sample","ar":"عيّنة"},{"w":"ethical approval","ar":"الموافقة الأخلاقية"},{"w":"limitation","ar":"قيد / نقطة ضعف"},{"w":"literature review","ar":"مراجعة الأدبيات"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the tutor's main concern about the draft?","o":["The title is too long","The research question is too broad","There are too few references"],"a":1,"why":"السؤال البحثي واسع جداً."},{"k":"gap","q":"The tutor wants the study to focus on students' ___.","a":["sleep"],"why":"اقترح التركيز على النوم فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"How many students does the tutor think is a realistic sample?","o":["Eighty students","Two hundred students","A hundred and twenty students"],"a":2,"why":"العدد الواقعي في رأيه مئة وعشرون."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do the students decide not to use wearable trackers?","o":["They are too expensive","They are not accurate","Students would refuse to wear them"],"a":0,"why":"كلّفة الجهاز حوالي ثمانين جنيهاً."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The tutor says the students should explain the limits of sleep diaries.","a":"T","why":"طلب شرح القيد لأن الناس ينسون."},{"k":"gap","q":"The ethics committee usually takes about ___ weeks.","a":["3","three"],"why":"اللجنة تحتاج عادة ثلاثة أسابيع."},{"k":"gap","q":"The literature review needs at least ___ studies.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"عشر دراسات على الأقل من السنوات الخمس الأخيرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who will write the methodology section?","o":["Ben","Layla","The tutor"],"a":0,"why":"المتحدث الثالث (بن) سيكتب المنهجية."}]},{"id":"l-b2-08","l":"B2","title":"Comparing survey methods","part":3,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"So, Jake, we need to decide how to collect the data for our library project. I made a list of three methods."},{"s":"B","t":"Great. Start with the online questionnaire, because that is what I expected we would choose."},{"s":"A","t":"The main advantages are cost and speed. We could build it for free and get answers within days. The disadvantage is the response rate."},{"s":"A","t":"My cousin ran one at work and only about ten percent replied."},{"s":"B","t":"And the people who reply are often the ones with strong opinions, so the results might not represent everyone."},{"s":"A","t":"Right, that is called self-selection. Second option, telephone interviews. Response is much better, perhaps forty-five percent, and the interviewer can explain unclear questions."},{"s":"B","t":"But phone calls cost money, and these days many people never answer a number they do not know. We only have three hundred dollars in the budget."},{"s":"A","t":"True. Third, face-to-face surveys outside the library. We would get rich, detailed answers, and people can show us things on a map or a list."},{"s":"B","t":"That sounds the most useful, but it takes much longer. Each interview would last at least fifteen minutes, and we would stand outside for hours."},{"s":"A","t":"There is also the risk of interviewer bias. People may give polite answers because someone is looking at them."},{"s":"B","t":"Good point. What if we combine methods? The online questionnaire could be our main source, and we add twenty face-to-face interviews to understand the reasons behind the numbers."},{"s":"A","t":"I like that. We could offer a prize draw to encourage people to answer the questionnaire. A fifty dollar voucher, perhaps."},{"s":"B","t":"Hmm, I am not sure. A prize might attract people who do not use the library at all, and they would just click through quickly."},{"s":"B","t":"Maybe we should simply ask the library to advertise it on its website."},{"s":"A","t":"Fair enough. Let us do that instead. And before we launch, we should test the questions with ten friends."},{"s":"B","t":"Yes, a pilot study. If anything confuses them, we will rewrite it."},{"s":"A","t":"Perfect. I will draft the questionnaire tonight, and you contact the library manager."}],"gloss":[{"w":"response rate","ar":"نسبة الاستجابة"},{"w":"self-selection","ar":"الاختيار الذاتي"},{"w":"bias","ar":"تحيّز"},{"w":"pilot study","ar":"دراسة تجريبية"},{"w":"budget","ar":"ميزانية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What are the main advantages of an online questionnaire?","o":["Cost and speed","A high response rate","Very detailed answers"],"a":0,"why":"رخيص وسريع لكن نسبة الاستجابة ضعيفة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Only about ___ percent of people answered the online survey at the cousin's work.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"نسبة الرد حوالي عشرة بالمئة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the problem with self-selection?","o":["Replies come mainly from people with strong opinions","The questions are too long","It costs too much"],"a":0,"why":"المجيبون غالباً أصحاب آراء قوية فلا تمثل النتائج الجميع."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do they doubt telephone interviews?","o":["Response rates are very low","They cost money and many people ignore unknown numbers","Interviewers cannot explain questions"],"a":1,"why":"التكلفة وعدم الرد على أرقام مجهولة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The budget for the project is ___ dollars.","a":["300","three hundred"],"why":"الميزانية ثلاثمئة دولار."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Face-to-face interviews may lead people to give polite answers.","a":"T","why":"هذا هو تحيّز القائم بالمقابلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"They decide to add ___ face-to-face interviews.","a":["20","twenty"],"why":"عشرون مقابلة لفهم الأسباب وراء الأرقام."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do they decide about the prize draw?","o":["Offer a fifty dollar voucher","Ask the library to advertise the survey instead","Offer a prize only to library members"],"a":1,"why":"رفضا الجائزة وفضّلا الإعلان في موقع المكتبة."}]},{"id":"l-b2-09","l":"B2","title":"Planning a presentation on renewable energy","part":3,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Right, let us plan our renewable energy presentation. We have twelve minutes in total, and then three minutes for questions."},{"s":"B","t":"Should we cover every type of renewable energy?"},{"s":"A","t":"No, that would be too much. I suggest solar, wind, and then storage, because storage is the biggest challenge."},{"s":"C","t":"What about nuclear power? Some people call it clean energy."},{"s":"A","t":"It is low carbon, but it is not renewable, and our brief says renewable sources only. So we leave it out."},{"s":"C","t":"Fair. I will mention it in one sentence at most if someone asks."},{"s":"B","t":"How should we divide the work? I would like to take wind, because I read a good report about offshore wind farms."},{"s":"A","t":"Perfect, Sam. I will do the introduction and solar. Priya, can you take storage and the conclusion?"},{"s":"C","t":"Sure. I can explain batteries and why the sun does not always shine when we need electricity."},{"s":"B","t":"For visuals, I think we need a map showing the main wind farm areas. Pictures work better than lots of words."},{"s":"A","t":"Agreed. For solar, I want a graph showing how the price of panels has fallen over the last ten years. It is a very clear trend."},{"s":"C","t":"And a case study might help. There is a village that put solar panels on the school roof and now saves money on its electricity bills."},{"s":"A","t":"Nice. Keep the case study to one slide. The professor said no more than ten slides, with very little text."},{"s":"B","t":"So that is three slides each, plus a title slide."},{"s":"A","t":"Exactly. When can we practise? I am free on Thursday afternoon."},{"s":"C","t":"I have a lab until three, but after four o'clock would be good."},{"s":"B","t":"Same for me. Shall we book a room in the library?"},{"s":"A","t":"I will do it online tonight. Our presentation is on Monday at nine thirty, so we need at least one full rehearsal."},{"s":"C","t":"And we should time ourselves. Last time my group ran two minutes over, and we lost marks."},{"s":"A","t":"Good advice. Let us send our slides to each other by Wednesday evening."}],"gloss":[{"w":"renewable","ar":"متجدد"},{"w":"storage","ar":"تخزين"},{"w":"brief","ar":"التعليمات / الموجز"},{"w":"case study","ar":"دراسة حالة"},{"w":"rehearsal","ar":"بروفة"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"The presentation must last ___ minutes in total.","a":["12","twelve"],"why":"اثنتا عشرة دقيقة ثم ثلاث دقائق للأسئلة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do they leave out nuclear power?","o":["It is too expensive","It is not renewable","It is too difficult to explain"],"a":1,"why":"التعليمات تطلب مصادر متجددة فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which topic will Sam take?","o":["Wind","Solar","Storage"],"a":0,"why":"سام يريد أن يتناول طاقة الرياح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What visual will be used for the wind section?","o":["A graph","A photograph","A map"],"a":2,"why":"خريطة لأهم مناطق مزارع الرياح."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The solar graph will show that panel prices have risen.","a":"F","why":"الرسم يوضح أن الأسعار انخفضت خلال عشر سنوات."},{"k":"gap","q":"The professor allows no more than ___ slides.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"عشر شرائح كحد أقصى."},{"k":"mc","q":"When will the group rehearse?","o":["Thursday morning","Thursday after four o'clock","Wednesday evening"],"a":1,"why":"أحدهم لديه مختبر حتى الثالثة فاتفقوا بعد الرابعة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Priya says a group of hers lost marks for going over time.","a":"T","why":"تأخرت مجموعتها دقيقتين فخسرت درجات."}]},{"id":"l-b2-10","l":"B2","title":"Feedback on a first essay draft","part":3,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Thanks for coming, Karim. I have read your draft essay on whether public transport should be free, and overall I was impressed."},{"s":"B","t":"Really? I was worried it was a mess."},{"s":"A","t":"Not at all. The structure is clear and your examples are vivid, especially the one about the city that made buses free for pensioners."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you. What should I improve?"},{"s":"A","t":"Let us start with the introduction. It is nearly four hundred words, which is almost a third of the whole essay. Also, there is no clear thesis statement."},{"s":"B","t":"I thought the thesis was in the last sentence."},{"s":"A","t":"It is hinted at, but a reader should know your position immediately. Try one clear sentence, then move on."},{"s":"B","t":"Okay. And the body paragraphs?"},{"s":"A","t":"Paragraphs one, two and four work well. Paragraph three is about the cost of building roads, which is interesting but not really relevant, so I suggest deleting it."},{"s":"B","t":"That would also help with the length. I counted fourteen hundred words, and the limit is twelve hundred."},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, so you need to cut about two hundred words. Sorry, I said three hundred earlier, but two hundred is correct, since the references do not count."},{"s":"B","t":"Right. What about my sources?"},{"s":"A","t":"This is the weakest point. Several of your sources are blogs and news websites. For a university essay, you should use academic journals or official reports."},{"s":"B","t":"Where do I find those?"},{"s":"A","t":"Start with the library database. If you search for urban transport policy, you will find plenty. The librarian can also show you how."},{"s":"B","t":"Is it all right to write I think in the essay?"},{"s":"A","t":"Better to avoid it. Use phrases such as it can be argued that or the evidence suggests. It sounds more academic."},{"s":"B","t":"Understood. And the conclusion?"},{"s":"A","t":"It repeated your main points well, but it added a new argument about air pollution. A conclusion should never introduce new ideas."},{"s":"B","t":"I will fix that. When is the final deadline?"},{"s":"A","t":"Wednesday of next week, at five. Come back before then if you want me to look at the new introduction."}],"gloss":[{"w":"draft","ar":"مسودة"},{"w":"thesis statement","ar":"عبارة الأطروحة"},{"w":"relevant","ar":"ذو صلة"},{"w":"academic journal","ar":"مجلة علمية"},{"w":"conclusion","ar":"خاتمة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does the tutor praise about the essay?","o":["Clear structure and vivid examples","Correct grammar","The number of sources"],"a":0,"why":"أثنى على وضوح البناء وحيوية الأمثلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The introduction is nearly ___ words long.","a":["400","four hundred"],"why":"المقدمة قرابة أربعمئة كلمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is wrong with the introduction apart from its length?","o":["It contains many mistakes","It has no clear thesis statement","It is not interesting"],"a":1,"why":"لا توجد عبارة أطروحة واضحة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which paragraph does the tutor suggest deleting?","o":["Paragraph four","Paragraph one","Paragraph three"],"a":2,"why":"الفقرة الثالثة عن تكلفة بناء الطرق غير ذات صلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"Karim needs to cut about ___ words.","a":["200","two hundred"],"why":"صحّح المدرّس رقمه من ثلاثمئة إلى مئتين."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the problem with Karim's sources?","o":["Several are blogs and news websites","There are too few of them","They are too old"],"a":0,"why":"عليه استخدام المجلات الأكاديمية والتقارير الرسمية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The tutor recommends using the phrase I think in academic essays.","a":"F","why":"نصح بتجنبها واستعمال عبارات مثل it can be argued that."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is wrong with the conclusion?","o":["It is too long","It introduces a new argument","It does not repeat the main points"],"a":1,"why":"أضافت حجة جديدة عن تلوث الهواء."}]},{"id":"l-b2-11","l":"B2","title":"Preparing a mock trial","part":3,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Let us go through your preparation for Thursday's mock trial. Elena, you represent the prosecution, and Marcus, you are for the defence. Where are you now?"},{"s":"B","t":"I have the case file. Our defendant is accused of stealing a bicycle from the rack outside the engineering building."},{"s":"C","t":"And our case is that he was in the library at the time."},{"s":"A","t":"Marcus, what evidence do you have for that?"},{"s":"C","t":"The library entry system records that his card was used at seven fifty and again at nine ten. The theft happened at about eight forty."},{"s":"A","t":"Good. Elena, who will you call as witnesses?"},{"s":"B","t":"First, the owner of the bicycle, to explain what was stolen and how much it was worth."},{"s":"B","t":"Second, the security guard, who saw someone in a grey jacket near the rack. I also wanted to call the cleaner, who found the broken lock."},{"s":"A","t":"How long is the trial? We have only forty minutes in total."},{"s":"B","t":"Then I will leave out the cleaner. Two witnesses are enough."},{"s":"A","t":"Wise. Remember, you must prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt. The burden is on you, Elena, not on Marcus."},{"s":"C","t":"So my job is to create doubt. The guard was thirty metres away, it was dark, and the camera footage is blurred."},{"s":"A","t":"Exactly, and you can raise that in cross-examination. There you may ask leading questions, such as, is it true that the light was poor?"},{"s":"A","t":"But you may only ask questions. Do not make speeches."},{"s":"C","t":"Understood. How long are the opening statements?"},{"s":"A","t":"Three minutes each. Keep them simple. Tell the jury what you will prove, and do not argue yet."},{"s":"B","t":"Who is on the jury?"},{"s":"A","t":"Six classmates. Their names will be drawn from a hat. They will not have read the case file, so explain everything clearly."},{"s":"C","t":"Where and when is the trial?"},{"s":"A","t":"Thursday at two o'clock in Room Four of the law building. Arrive fifteen minutes early to set up the room."},{"s":"B","t":"Should we wear formal clothes?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, please. Jackets or smart shirts. It helps you take the role seriously."}],"gloss":[{"w":"prosecution","ar":"الادعاء"},{"w":"defence","ar":"الدفاع"},{"w":"burden of proof","ar":"عبء الإثبات"},{"w":"cross-examination","ar":"الاستجواب المضاد"},{"w":"jury","ar":"هيئة المحلفين"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the defendant accused of?","o":["Stealing a bicycle","Breaking a window","Taking books from the library"],"a":0,"why":"المتهم متَّهم بسرقة دراجة من حامل الدراجات."},{"k":"gap","q":"The defendant's library card was first used at ___.","a":["7.50","7:50","seven fifty","ten to eight"],"why":"سجل المكتبة: السابعة وخمسون دقيقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which witness will Elena decide not to call?","o":["The security guard","The bicycle owner","The cleaner"],"a":2,"why":"بسبب ضيق الوقت استغنت عن عاملة النظافة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The whole trial can last only ___ minutes.","a":["40","forty"],"why":"الوقت الكلي أربعون دقيقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Marcus must prove that the defendant is innocent.","a":"F","why":"عبء الإثبات على الادعاء وليس على الدفاع."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which point will Marcus use against the security guard's evidence?","o":["He is a friend of the owner","He was thirty metres away in the dark","He has changed his story"],"a":1,"why":"كان بعيداً ثلاثين متراً والظلام دامس."},{"k":"gap","q":"Each opening statement lasts ___ minutes.","a":["3","three"],"why":"ثلاث دقائق لكل طرف."},{"k":"mc","q":"How will the jury members be chosen?","o":["Volunteers put their names forward","The professor chooses them","Names are drawn from a hat"],"a":2,"why":"تُسحب الأسماء من قبعة."}]},{"id":"l-b2-12","l":"B2","title":"Choosing a master's thesis topic","part":3,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Noor, thanks for sending your list of ideas. Let us talk them through. You have three options, correct?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. First, whistleblower protection laws. Second, corruption risks in public procurement. And third, using artificial intelligence to detect fraud in public spending."},{"s":"A","t":"What attracts you to the third one?"},{"s":"B","t":"Honestly, it connects my master's with the doctorate I want to do later. But I am worried about the data."},{"s":"A","t":"You are right to worry. Many governments publish spending data only in summary form, and a thesis needs detailed records."},{"s":"A","t":"Without them you would spend months searching rather than analysing."},{"s":"B","t":"What about whistleblower laws? There is a lot written on that."},{"s":"A","t":"That is the problem. It is a crowded field."},{"s":"A","t":"You would have to find something new to say, and examiners will expect you to compare several countries, which takes time."},{"s":"B","t":"So procurement looks like the middle path?"},{"s":"A","t":"It could be. There are open contract databases, and some include three years of detailed records."},{"s":"A","t":"You could look for warning signs, such as contracts awarded with only one bidder."},{"s":"B","t":"Could I still use some AI there?"},{"s":"A","t":"In a small way, yes. Simple statistical tools could highlight unusual contracts. But I would not promise too much."},{"s":"A","t":"Remember, the thesis is only twelve thousand words, and you have eight months."},{"s":"B","t":"Right. How narrow should I go?"},{"s":"A","t":"Very narrow. Perhaps one sector, such as road construction, in one region. A narrow study done well is better than a broad one done badly."},{"s":"B","t":"That makes sense. Initially, I thought of including hospitals as well."},{"s":"A","t":"Leave hospitals for the doctorate. Now, what do you need to do next?"},{"s":"B","t":"Write a one-page outline with my research question and the data source."},{"s":"A","t":"Exactly. Please also ask the librarian about access to the contract database, because some sites need a university login and approval can take several days."},{"s":"A","t":"Send the outline within three weeks. Let us meet next Wednesday at ten to check your progress."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you, Dr Kemp. I feel much clearer now."}],"gloss":[{"w":"whistleblower","ar":"مُبلِّغ عن المخالفات"},{"w":"procurement","ar":"المشتريات العامة"},{"w":"bidder","ar":"مقدّم عطاء"},{"w":"thesis","ar":"أطروحة"},{"w":"outline","ar":"مخطط أولي"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why is the AI topic attractive to Noor?","o":["The data is easy to find","It links to her future doctorate","It is the supervisor's speciality"],"a":1,"why":"يربط الماجستير بالدكتوراه التي تريدها لاحقاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main problem with the AI topic?","o":["The software is expensive","The time is too short","Detailed data is often not published"],"a":2,"why":"الحكومات تنشر غالباً بيانات ملخصة فقط."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Dr Kemp thinks the whistleblower topic is already heavily researched.","a":"T","why":"وصفه بأنه مجال مزدحم."},{"k":"gap","q":"One warning sign in contracts is having only one ___.","a":["bidder"],"why":"عقود مُنحت بمتقدم واحد فقط."},{"k":"gap","q":"The thesis must be ___ words long.","a":["12000","12,000","twelve thousand"],"why":"الأطروحة اثنا عشر ألف كلمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"How much time does Noor have for the thesis?","o":["Eight months","Six months","Twelve months"],"a":0,"why":"لديها ثمانية أشهر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Dr Kemp advises Noor to include hospitals in her study.","a":"F","why":"قال أن تترك المستشفيات للدكتوراه."},{"k":"gap","q":"Noor must send an outline within ___ weeks.","a":["3","three"],"why":"خلال ثلاثة أسابيع."}]},{"id":"l-b2-13","l":"B2","title":"How memory works","part":4,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Today I want to explain how human memory works, and why some study methods work far better than others."},{"s":"A","t":"Psychologists usually divide memory into three stages. The first is encoding, when information enters the brain."},{"s":"A","t":"The second is storage, and the third is retrieval, when we bring it back."},{"s":"A","t":"Let us begin with short-term memory. For decades, textbooks said it holds about seven items."},{"s":"A","t":"More recent research suggests the real figure is closer to four, if we count meaningful chunks."},{"s":"A","t":"What is a chunk? Think of a phone number. If you try to remember eleven separate digits, you will probably fail."},{"s":"A","t":"But if you group them into three or four blocks, the task becomes much easier."},{"s":"A","t":"Information in short-term memory fades after roughly twenty seconds unless we repeat it or connect it to something we already know. Connection is the key idea here."},{"s":"A","t":"A fact attached to an existing memory is much more likely to survive."},{"s":"A","t":"Long-term memory, by contrast, seems to have no practical limit. The challenge is not storage but retrieval."},{"s":"A","t":"You may know a name perfectly well and still fail to recall it, because you lack the right cue."},{"s":"A","t":"Cues can be surprising. Smell is a famous example. Many people report that the scent of a certain soap suddenly brings back a whole childhood afternoon."},{"s":"A","t":"Now, forgetting. In the 1880s, a German psychologist tested himself on lists of nonsense syllables."},{"s":"A","t":"He found that forgetting is fastest soon after learning: most of the material was lost within the first day."},{"s":"A","t":"This is why cramming the night before an exam is unwise. A better method is spaced repetition."},{"s":"A","t":"Review the material after one day, then after a week, then after a month, and each review will make the memory stronger."},{"s":"A","t":"Sleep also matters. During sleep, the brain replays the day's experiences and moves important information into long-term storage."},{"s":"A","t":"Students who sleep well after studying usually remember more than those who stay up all night."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, a practical tip. Many students re-read their notes again and again, but this gives a false feeling of knowing."},{"s":"A","t":"Testing yourself with questions is much more effective, even if it feels harder."},{"s":"A","t":"So, to summarise, use chunks, create connections, space your reviews, sleep well, and test yourself. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"encoding","ar":"ترميز"},{"w":"retrieval","ar":"استرجاع"},{"w":"chunk","ar":"كتلة معلومات"},{"w":"cue","ar":"مفتاح تذكّر"},{"w":"cramming","ar":"الحشو قبل الامتحان"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What are the three stages of memory?","o":["Seeing, hearing and thinking","Learning, sleeping and forgetting","Encoding, storage and retrieval"],"a":2,"why":"المراحل: الترميز والتخزين والاسترجاع."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does more recent research suggest about short-term memory?","o":["It holds about seven items","It holds about four chunks","It holds about twenty items"],"a":1,"why":"الأبحاث الحديثة تقترح قرابة أربع كتل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Without repetition, short-term information fades after about ___ seconds.","a":["20","twenty"],"why":"تتلاشى بعد نحو عشرين ثانية."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main challenge of long-term memory?","o":["Retrieval","Storage space","Speed of learning"],"a":0,"why":"المشكلة في الاسترجاع لا في التخزين."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says smells can bring back childhood memories.","a":"T","why":"ذكر رائحة الصابون مثالاً."},{"k":"gap","q":"In the old experiment, most material was lost within the first ___.","a":["day","one day"],"why":"فُقد معظم المادة خلال اليوم الأول."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which review schedule does the lecturer recommend?","o":["Every hour for a day","Once, on the night before the exam","After a day, a week and a month"],"a":2,"why":"المراجعة المتباعدة بعد يوم ثم أسبوع ثم شهر."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says re-reading notes is the most effective way to study.","a":"F","why":"يعطي إحساساً زائفاً بالمعرفة، والاختبار الذاتي أفضل."}]},{"id":"l-b2-14","l":"B2","title":"The design of cities","part":4,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon. Today's lecture is about the design of cities, and about one simple question: who is a city built for?"},{"s":"A","t":"For most of history, cities grew without a master plan. Medieval towns developed around a market or a river crossing, and their streets were narrow and winding."},{"s":"A","t":"This looks chaotic on a map, but it has advantages."},{"s":"A","t":"In hot climates, narrow lanes create shade, and old city centres in many countries are still pleasant to walk in during summer."},{"s":"A","t":"Planned cities are also very old. Ancient builders often used a grid, with straight streets crossing at right angles."},{"s":"A","t":"A grid is easy to build, easy to measure, and easy to navigate, which is why many cities in the nineteenth century copied it."},{"s":"A","t":"The biggest change came in the twentieth century with the car. Planners introduced zoning, which means separating areas by purpose."},{"s":"A","t":"Homes went in one zone, offices in another, and shops in a third. Highways connected them."},{"s":"A","t":"The result was long commutes. In some cities, workers spend more than an hour travelling each way."},{"s":"A","t":"Traffic jams, noise and air pollution followed, and streets stopped being places where people met and became channels for vehicles."},{"s":"A","t":"Today many designers are trying to reverse this trend. A popular idea is the fifteen-minute city."},{"s":"A","t":"In it, most daily needs, such as school, shops, a clinic and a park, are within fifteen minutes on foot or by bicycle."},{"s":"A","t":"To achieve this, planners promote mixed-use buildings, for example apartments above a bakery and a small office. They also convert car lanes into cycle paths and wider pavements."},{"s":"A","t":"Green space is another priority. Trees and parks make a city cooler in summer, because shade and evaporation can lower local temperatures by several degrees."},{"s":"A","t":"They also improve mental health and give children somewhere safe to play."},{"s":"A","t":"Of course, there are criticisms. First, rebuilding an existing city is very expensive."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, when a neighbourhood becomes more attractive, prices often rise, and poorer residents may be forced to leave. This is called gentrification."},{"s":"A","t":"So what lessons can we draw? Firstly, good design puts people before vehicles. Secondly, good design must also protect the people who already live there."},{"s":"A","t":"Next week, we will look at a case study of a city that tried to do both."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you, and please read chapter five before then."}],"gloss":[{"w":"zoning","ar":"تقسيم المناطق"},{"w":"commute","ar":"التنقل اليومي للعمل"},{"w":"mixed-use","ar":"متعدد الاستعمالات"},{"w":"gentrification","ar":"التحسين العمراني الذي يُهجّر السكان"},{"w":"pavement","ar":"رصيف"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is one advantage of narrow winding streets in hot climates?","o":["They are easier to navigate","They provide shade","They allow more traffic"],"a":1,"why":"الأزقة الضيقة تعطي ظلاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did many nineteenth-century cities copy the grid plan?","o":["It gave more shade","It was cheaper than roads","It was easy to build, measure and navigate"],"a":2,"why":"الشبكة سهلة البناء والقياس والتنقل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Zoning means separating areas by ___.","a":["purpose"],"why":"فصل المناطق حسب الغرض."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was a major result of zoning?","o":["Long commutes","More public squares","Smaller cities"],"a":0,"why":"أدى إلى رحلات عمل طويلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"In a fifteen-minute city, daily needs are within ___ minutes on foot or by bicycle.","a":["15","fifteen"],"why":"خمس عشرة دقيقة سيراً أو بالدراجة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Planners sometimes convert car lanes into cycle paths.","a":"T","why":"من وسائل تحقيق مدينة الخمس عشرة دقيقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does gentrification mean in the lecture?","o":["Building new highways around a city","Poorer residents being forced out as prices rise","Planting trees in neighbourhoods"],"a":1,"why":"ارتفاع الأسعار يدفع الأقل دخلاً إلى المغادرة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says rebuilding an existing city is cheap.","a":"F","why":"قال إنها مكلفة جداً."}]},{"id":"l-b2-15","l":"B2","title":"A short history of courts and written law","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Today I want to give you a short history of courts and written law, and to explain why writing the law down was such an important step."},{"s":"A","t":"Before writing, rules existed as custom, memorised and passed on by elders. The problem is obvious."},{"s":"A","t":"Memory changes, and powerful people can claim that the custom says whatever suits them."},{"s":"A","t":"Some of the earliest written laws come from Mesopotamia, about four thousand years ago."},{"s":"A","t":"One famous collection was carved on a tall stone pillar and placed in a public place, so that people could see what the rules were."},{"s":"A","t":"Many of its provisions set fixed penalties, which limited private revenge."},{"s":"A","t":"A similar idea appears in ancient Rome. Around four hundred and fifty years before the modern era, Roman citizens demanded that the law be displayed in the forum."},{"s":"A","t":"The result was known as the Twelve Tables."},{"s":"A","t":"Much later, in the sixth century, the emperor Justinian ordered scholars to collect and organise Roman law into a single code, and that work influenced European legal thinking for centuries."},{"s":"A","t":"Now let us move to medieval England. After the Norman conquest, royal judges began to travel around the country to hear cases."},{"s":"A","t":"When they met, they compared decisions, and over time the rulings became a shared body of rules."},{"s":"A","t":"This is the origin of common law, which relies heavily on precedent, meaning earlier decisions in similar cases."},{"s":"A","t":"England also developed the jury, a group of ordinary citizens who decide the facts."},{"s":"A","t":"In 1215, a charter forced on the king stated that no free man could be imprisoned without a lawful judgment."},{"s":"A","t":"This was not a modern bill of rights, but it established that even rulers were bound by law."},{"s":"A","t":"Meanwhile, much of continental Europe followed the Roman tradition. In 1804, France published a civil code, written clearly so that ordinary people could read it."},{"s":"A","t":"This approach, called civil law, puts the main rules in written codes, and judges apply them rather than building on earlier cases."},{"s":"A","t":"Today these two traditions have moved closer together. Common law countries have many statutes, and civil law courts also pay attention to earlier decisions."},{"s":"A","t":"Why does this matter? Three features run through this history: laws that are written, laws that are public, and courts that give reasons."},{"s":"A","t":"Together, they make it hard for officials to act in secret, which is why they are central to the fight against corruption."},{"s":"A","t":"In the next lecture, we will look at how judges are appointed. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"custom","ar":"عُرف"},{"w":"penalty","ar":"عقوبة"},{"w":"precedent","ar":"سابقة قضائية"},{"w":"jury","ar":"هيئة محلفين"},{"w":"statute","ar":"قانون مكتوب"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why was law based on custom a problem?","o":["There were not enough judges","It was too long to read","Memory changes and powerful people could twist it"],"a":2,"why":"الذاكرة تتغير والأقوياء يفسرونها لصالحهم."},{"k":"gap","q":"The earliest written laws are about ___ years old.","a":["4000","four thousand","4,000"],"why":"قبل نحو أربعة آلاف سنة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Where was the famous Mesopotamian collection placed?","o":["In a temple library","On a pillar in a public place","In the king's palace"],"a":1,"why":"نُحتت على عمود حجري في مكان عام."},{"k":"gap","q":"The Twelve Tables were displayed in the ___.","a":["forum"],"why":"عُرضت في الساحة العامة (الفورم)."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did Justinian do?","o":["He wrote the Twelve Tables","He introduced travelling judges","He had Roman law collected into one code"],"a":2,"why":"أمر بجمع القانون الروماني في مدونة واحدة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does common law rely on most?","o":["Earlier decisions","Written codes only","Advice from elders"],"a":0,"why":"يعتمد على السوابق القضائية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The charter of 1215 was a modern bill of rights.","a":"F","why":"لم تكن وثيقة حقوق حديثة، لكنها قيّدت الحكام بالقانون."},{"k":"gap","q":"France published its civil code in ___.","a":["1804"],"why":"نُشرت المدونة المدنية الفرنسية عام 1804."}]},{"id":"l-b2-16","l":"B2","title":"What transparency means in government","part":4,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Welcome back. Today we turn to a word that appears in almost every discussion of good government: transparency. What does it actually mean?"},{"s":"A","t":"In simple terms, transparency means that the public can see how decisions are made, how money is spent, and who is responsible."},{"s":"A","t":"The idea is that officials behave better when they know people are watching."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me break the concept into three parts. The first is the right to request information."},{"s":"A","t":"Many countries now have freedom of information laws, which allow citizens to ask for official documents."},{"s":"A","t":"The earliest such law was passed in Sweden in the eighteenth century, though most others appeared after nineteen sixty."},{"s":"A","t":"The second part is proactive publication. Instead of waiting for requests, governments publish budgets, contracts and meeting records on their own initiative."},{"s":"A","t":"This saves citizens the trouble of asking, and it saves officials the trouble of answering."},{"s":"A","t":"The third part is participation. Transparency is more useful when people can attend public meetings, comment on draft laws and report concerns."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me give an example. Imagine a city that decides to publish every contract worth more than ten thousand dollars."},{"s":"A","t":"Within months, a local journalist notices that three different schools paid very different prices for identical desks."},{"s":"A","t":"Questions are asked, the supplier is changed, and the city saves money. Nobody needed to prove corruption; simply seeing the information was enough."},{"s":"A","t":"However, transparency has limits. Some information must stay confidential, such as personal medical records or details of national security."},{"s":"A","t":"The challenge is to decide where to draw the line, and the usual principle is that secrecy needs a clear reason, while openness needs none."},{"s":"A","t":"There is a second limitation. Publishing a huge number of documents is not the same as being open."},{"s":"A","t":"If the files are in a confusing format or buried on a website nobody can find, citizens learn nothing. Critics call this a data dump."},{"s":"A","t":"Real transparency means information that is easy to find, to read and to compare."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, remember that transparency alone does not punish anyone. It must be connected to accountability, which means there are consequences when officials misuse their power."},{"s":"A","t":"Without that, people may see the problem clearly but feel unable to change it."},{"s":"A","t":"In our next session, we will look at the tools that connect transparency to accountability. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"transparency","ar":"الشفافية"},{"w":"proactive","ar":"استباقي"},{"w":"accountability","ar":"المساءلة"},{"w":"confidential","ar":"سري"},{"w":"supplier","ar":"مورّد"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"According to the lecturer, why does transparency improve behaviour?","o":["Officials are paid more","Officials know people are watching","Laws become shorter"],"a":1,"why":"الفكرة أن الموظفين يتصرفون أفضل حين يعرفون أنهم مراقَبون."},{"k":"gap","q":"The earliest freedom of information law was passed in ___.","a":["Sweden"],"why":"أقدم قانون لحرية المعلومات في السويد."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does proactive publication mean?","o":["Citizens write to officials for documents","Journalists find secret documents","Governments publish information on their own initiative"],"a":2,"why":"الحكومة تنشر دون انتظار الطلبات."},{"k":"gap","q":"In the example, the city publishes every contract worth more than ___ dollars.","a":["10,000","10000","ten thousand"],"why":"العقود التي تتجاوز عشرة آلاف دولار."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did the journalist discover?","o":["Schools paid very different prices for identical desks","A supplier had stolen money","Some contracts were missing"],"a":0,"why":"ثلاث مدارس دفعت أسعاراً مختلفة جداً لمكاتب متطابقة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says secrecy needs a clear reason.","a":"T","why":"المبدأ: السرية تحتاج مبرراً واضحاً أما الانفتاح فلا."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is a data dump?","o":["Deleting old files","Publishing huge numbers of documents that are hard to use","A report leaked to journalists"],"a":1,"why":"نشر كمّ هائل من الملفات يصعب فهمه أو إيجاده."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says transparency by itself punishes officials who misuse power.","a":"F","why":"الشفافية وحدها لا تعاقب، يلزم ربطها بالمساءلة."}]},{"id":"l-b2-17","l":"B2","title":"How machines learn from data","part":4,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Today's topic is how machines learn from data, a subject that sounds mysterious but rests on a few simple ideas."},{"s":"A","t":"Let us start with a comparison. In traditional programming, a person writes the rules. If an email contains the words free prize, mark it as spam."},{"s":"A","t":"The computer follows the rules exactly, but it only catches what the programmer thought of."},{"s":"A","t":"Machine learning reverses this."},{"s":"A","t":"Instead of writing rules, we give the computer thousands of examples, each labelled as spam or not spam, and a learning algorithm looks for patterns that separate the two groups."},{"s":"A","t":"The result is called a model."},{"s":"A","t":"This approach is known as supervised learning, because the examples come with correct answers, like a teacher marking homework."},{"s":"A","t":"It is the most common type used today, for tasks such as recognising faces or predicting house prices."},{"s":"A","t":"A crucial step is testing. Researchers usually keep about twenty percent of their data hidden during training."},{"s":"A","t":"After training, they show the model this unseen data and measure how often it is right."},{"s":"A","t":"If they tested on the same examples used for learning, the results would be misleadingly good."},{"s":"A","t":"That brings us to overfitting. An overfitted model has memorised the training examples, including their accidental details, instead of learning general patterns."},{"s":"A","t":"It is like a student who memorises last year's exam paper and then fails when the questions change."},{"s":"A","t":"The second type is unsupervised learning. Here there are no labels, and the algorithm simply searches for natural groups."},{"s":"A","t":"A shop might use it to discover that its customers fall into four distinct kinds of buyer, without anyone telling the computer what to look for."},{"s":"A","t":"The third type is reinforcement learning. The system tries actions and receives rewards or penalties, so it improves through trial and error."},{"s":"A","t":"This is how programs have learned to play complex board games, sometimes playing millions of games against themselves."},{"s":"A","t":"Now an important warning. A model learns whatever patterns are in its data, including unfair ones."},{"s":"A","t":"Suppose a company trains a hiring tool on the records of past employees, and most of those employees were men."},{"s":"A","t":"The tool may learn to prefer men, not because anyone intended it, but because the data reflected the past."},{"s":"A","t":"This is why data quality matters more than data quantity."},{"s":"A","t":"And it is why people who work in law and public policy need to understand these systems well enough to question them."},{"s":"A","t":"Next time, we will look at how to audit an algorithm. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"algorithm","ar":"خوارزمية"},{"w":"label","ar":"تصنيف / وسم"},{"w":"overfitting","ar":"فرط التخصيص"},{"w":"reinforcement","ar":"تعزيز"},{"w":"audit","ar":"تدقيق / مراجعة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the weakness of traditional programming for spam?","o":["The computer ignores the rules","It only catches what the programmer thought of","It is too slow"],"a":1,"why":"القواعد تلتقط فقط ما فكّر به المبرمج."},{"k":"gap","q":"The result of the learning process is called a ___.","a":["model"],"why":"النتيجة تسمى نموذجاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is it called supervised learning?","o":["A teacher watches the computer","The process is very slow","The examples come with correct answers"],"a":2,"why":"الأمثلة مصحوبة بالإجابات الصحيحة كأن معلماً يصحح."},{"k":"gap","q":"Researchers usually keep about ___ percent of data hidden for testing.","a":["20","twenty"],"why":"حوالي عشرين بالمئة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Overfitting is compared to which kind of student?","o":["A tired student","One who memorises last year's exam paper","A student with no books"],"a":1,"why":"يحفظ ورقة امتحان العام الماضي ويفشل عند تغيّر الأسئلة."},{"k":"gap","q":"A shop might find that its customers fall into ___ kinds of buyer.","a":["4","four"],"why":"أربعة أنواع من المشترين."},{"k":"mc","q":"How does reinforcement learning improve?","o":["From labelled examples","By finding natural groups","By trial and error with rewards"],"a":2,"why":"يتعلم بالتجربة والخطأ والمكافآت."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says the quantity of data matters more than its quality.","a":"F","why":"قال إن جودة البيانات أهم من كميتها."}]},{"id":"l-b2-18","l":"B2","title":"Why some languages die","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello everyone. Today we are going to ask why some languages die, and whether anything can be done about it."},{"s":"A","t":"There are roughly seven thousand languages in the world today."},{"s":"A","t":"Linguists estimate that around forty percent are endangered, and many experts think about half may disappear by the end of this century."},{"s":"A","t":"First, what does it mean for a language to die? It does not happen with a sudden disaster."},{"s":"A","t":"A language is usually considered dead when its last fluent speaker passes away."},{"s":"A","t":"But the real turning point comes much earlier, when parents stop passing the language on to their children."},{"s":"A","t":"Why do parents make that choice? The most common reason is economic."},{"s":"A","t":"If good jobs and university courses require a dominant language, families naturally want their children to master it."},{"s":"A","t":"They are not rejecting their heritage; they are trying to give their children opportunities."},{"s":"A","t":"Migration is a second factor. When young people move from villages to big cities, they live and work among speakers of other languages."},{"s":"A","t":"Their own language is heard only at home, and then less and less."},{"s":"A","t":"Schools and media play a role as well."},{"s":"A","t":"If lessons, television and the internet are all in one national language, a small language has no place in modern life."},{"s":"A","t":"Sometimes there is also social pressure, so that speakers feel embarrassed to use their language in public."},{"s":"A","t":"Interestingly, the number of speakers is not the only measure of health. A language with a million speakers can be in danger if no children are learning it."},{"s":"A","t":"A language with only a few thousand speakers can survive if the community uses it daily."},{"s":"A","t":"Why should we care?"},{"s":"A","t":"Each language holds a unique store of knowledge, for example names for local plants, traditional methods of navigation or farming, and stories that exist nowhere else."},{"s":"A","t":"When the language disappears, much of that knowledge goes with it."},{"s":"A","t":"There are encouraging examples. In Wales, the Welsh language has been supported through schools and television, and the number of young speakers has risen."},{"s":"A","t":"In New Zealand, communities created preschools where small children learn Maori entirely through the language."},{"s":"A","t":"Modern technology helps too, since speakers can record words and make learning apps cheaply."},{"s":"A","t":"The lesson is that revival is possible, but it needs children, and it needs the community to want it. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"endangered","ar":"مهدَّد بالانقراض"},{"w":"fluent","ar":"طليق"},{"w":"heritage","ar":"تراث"},{"w":"migration","ar":"هجرة"},{"w":"revival","ar":"إحياء"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"There are roughly ___ languages in the world today.","a":["7000","seven thousand","7,000"],"why":"نحو سبعة آلاف لغة."},{"k":"mc","q":"When is a language usually considered dead?","o":["When no one writes it","When a government bans it","When its last fluent speaker passes away"],"a":2,"why":"تُعدّ ميتة بوفاة آخر متحدث بطلاقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the real turning point for a language?","o":["Parents stop passing it on to children","The last speaker dies","A dictionary is published"],"a":0,"why":"النقطة الفاصلة حين يتوقف الآباء عن نقلها."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do parents often choose a dominant language for their children?","o":["They reject their heritage","They want their children to have opportunities","The law forces them"],"a":1,"why":"السبب اقتصادي: وظائف وجامعات تتطلب اللغة السائدة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Moving from villages to big cities can reduce the use of a small language.","a":"T","why":"في المدن يُسمع الصغير منها في البيت فقط ثم يقل."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A language with a million speakers cannot be in danger.","a":"F","why":"قد تكون في خطر إن لم يتعلمها الأطفال."},{"k":"gap","q":"In New Zealand, communities created ___ where small children learn Maori.","a":["preschools"],"why":"رياض أطفال تُدرَّس فيها اللغة بالكامل."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the lesson of the lecture?","o":["Technology alone can save languages","Revival needs children and the community's wish","Languages should only be recorded"],"a":1,"why":"الإحياء ممكن لكنه يحتاج أطفالاً ورغبة المجتمع."}]},{"id":"l-b2-19","l":"B2","title":"The economics of water","part":4,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Today we are going to look at the economics of water, which raises a puzzle that economists have discussed for hundreds of years."},{"s":"A","t":"The puzzle is this. Water is essential for life, yet in most places it is very cheap. Diamonds are not essential, yet they are extremely expensive. Why?"},{"s":"A","t":"The traditional answer is that price depends not on how useful something is overall, but on how scarce it is at the moment of buying."},{"s":"A","t":"Where water is plentiful, the next litre has little value."},{"s":"A","t":"But water is becoming scarce. About ninety-seven percent of the planet's water is salty, and most of the fresh water is frozen or underground."},{"s":"A","t":"The share that is easy to reach is tiny."},{"s":"A","t":"Agriculture is the biggest user. Around seventy percent of the fresh water taken from rivers and wells worldwide goes to irrigation."},{"s":"A","t":"This means that small improvements in farming can save more water than large changes in households."},{"s":"A","t":"One problem is that farmers and households often pay prices that are far below the true cost of delivering water."},{"s":"A","t":"Governments keep prices low for good reasons, to protect poorer people, but the effect is waste, because nobody has a reason to save."},{"s":"A","t":"One solution is tiered pricing. The first portion of water, enough for drinking, cooking and washing, is cheap. Further portions cost more per litre."},{"s":"A","t":"In this way, essential use is protected while heavy use is discouraged."},{"s":"A","t":"A second issue is leakage. In some cities, as much as a third of the treated water is lost through broken pipes before it reaches a tap."},{"s":"A","t":"Repairing them is often cheaper than finding new sources, but repairs are invisible and politicians prefer to cut ribbons on new dams."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, there is the idea of virtual water, the water used to produce goods."},{"s":"A","t":"Producing one kilogram of beef takes thousands of litres, whereas a kilogram of wheat takes far fewer. When a dry country imports food, it is effectively importing water."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, fairness. In many places, poor households without a connection buy water from vendors and pay several times more per litre than wealthy neighbours who have pipes."},{"s":"A","t":"Solving this requires investment in networks, not simply lower prices."},{"s":"A","t":"To conclude, water is an economic problem as well as an environmental one. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"scarce","ar":"شحيح / نادر"},{"w":"irrigation","ar":"ري"},{"w":"leakage","ar":"تسرّب"},{"w":"tiered pricing","ar":"التسعير المتدرج"},{"w":"vendor","ar":"بائع"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"According to the traditional answer, what mainly determines price?","o":["How useful something is overall","How scarce it is when buying","The cost of bottles"],"a":1,"why":"السعر يتحدد بالندرة لحظة الشراء."},{"k":"gap","q":"About ___ percent of the planet's water is salty.","a":["97","ninety-seven","ninety seven"],"why":"سبعة وتسعون بالمئة مالحة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sector uses the most fresh water?","o":["Households","Industry","Agriculture"],"a":2,"why":"الزراعة والري نحو سبعين بالمئة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says low water prices encourage people to save water.","a":"F","why":"الأسعار المنخفضة تسبب الهدر لأن لا أحد لديه سبب للتوفير."},{"k":"gap","q":"In tiered pricing, the first portion of water is ___.","a":["cheap"],"why":"الجزء الأول رخيص ثم يزداد السعر."},{"k":"gap","q":"In some cities, as much as a ___ of treated water is lost through leaks.","a":["third","1/3"],"why":"يضيع حتى ثلث المياه المعالجة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why are pipe repairs often neglected?","o":["Repairing pipes costs more than new dams","Repairs are invisible, so politicians prefer new projects","The leaks are very small"],"a":1,"why":"الإصلاحات غير مرئية فيفضّل السياسيون المشاريع الجديدة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Poor households without a connection may pay more per litre than wealthy ones.","a":"T","why":"يشترون من الباعة بأسعار أعلى بأضعاف."}]},{"id":"l-b2-20","l":"B2","title":"How scientists test a new medicine","part":4,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon. Today I want to explain how scientists test a new medicine before it reaches the pharmacy, a process that often takes ten years or more."},{"s":"A","t":"It begins in the laboratory. Researchers test a promising substance on cells and then on animals, to see whether it seems to work and whether it is toxic."},{"s":"A","t":"Most substances fail at this early stage, and only a few move forward."},{"s":"A","t":"The next step is human testing, which is divided into phases. In phase one, a small group of volunteers, usually between twenty and one hundred, receive the medicine."},{"s":"A","t":"The main question is safety. Researchers find the highest dose that people can tolerate without serious side effects."},{"s":"A","t":"In phase two, the medicine is given to a few hundred patients who actually have the illness."},{"s":"A","t":"Now the question is whether it works, and what dose is best."},{"s":"A","t":"Phase three is the largest and most expensive. Thousands of patients take part, often in many countries."},{"s":"A","t":"The new medicine is compared either with the best existing treatment or with a placebo, which is a harmless pill with no active ingredient."},{"s":"A","t":"Why use a placebo? Because simply believing you are being treated can make you feel better."},{"s":"A","t":"To separate that effect from the medicine itself, patients are assigned to groups at random, by a computer, so that the groups are similar."},{"s":"A","t":"In the best trials, the design is double-blind. This means neither the patients nor the doctors know who is receiving the real medicine until the trial is finished."},{"s":"A","t":"Otherwise, doctors might unconsciously treat or judge the groups differently."},{"s":"A","t":"When phase three is complete, the company sends all the results to a regulatory agency."},{"s":"A","t":"Independent experts examine the data, and if the benefits clearly outweigh the risks, the medicine is approved."},{"s":"A","t":"But testing does not stop there."},{"s":"A","t":"In phase four, doctors continue to monitor patients after approval, because rare side effects may appear only when hundreds of thousands of people use the medicine."},{"s":"A","t":"One more point, which is important for public trust."},{"s":"A","t":"In many countries, companies must register a trial before it begins and must report the results, even if the medicine failed."},{"s":"A","t":"Without this rule, disappointing results could simply be hidden, and doctors would see only the success stories."},{"s":"A","t":"To summarise, good testing needs several phases, random groups, blind design and honest reporting. Thank you."}],"gloss":[{"w":"trial","ar":"تجربة سريرية"},{"w":"dose","ar":"جرعة"},{"w":"placebo","ar":"دواء وهمي"},{"w":"side effect","ar":"أثر جانبي"},{"w":"regulatory agency","ar":"جهة تنظيمية"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"Testing a new medicine often takes ___ years or more.","a":["10","ten"],"why":"العملية تستغرق عشر سنوات أو أكثر."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main question in phase one?","o":["Does the medicine work?","Is the medicine safe?","How much will it cost?"],"a":1,"why":"المرحلة الأولى للسلامة وإيجاد أعلى جرعة محتملة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who takes part in phase two?","o":["Healthy volunteers","Thousands of patients in many countries","A few hundred patients with the illness"],"a":2,"why":"مئات قليلة من المرضى بالمرض نفسه."},{"k":"gap","q":"A placebo is a harmless ___ with no active ingredient.","a":["pill"],"why":"حبة غير ضارة بلا مادة فعالة."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In phase three, doctors decide which patients go into which group.","a":"F","why":"التوزيع عشوائي بواسطة الحاسوب."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does double-blind mean?","o":["Neither patients nor doctors know who gets the real medicine","Only patients do not know","Results are hidden from regulators"],"a":0,"why":"لا المريض ولا الطبيب يعرف حتى انتهاء التجربة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does phase four exist?","o":["The company needs advertising data","Rare side effects may appear only when very many people use it","Earlier phases were not completed"],"a":1,"why":"قد تظهر آثار نادرة عند استخدام مئات الآلاف."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In many countries, companies must report trial results even if the medicine failed.","a":"T","why":"يجب تسجيل التجربة والإبلاغ عن نتائجها وإن فشل الدواء."}]},{"id":"l-c1-01","l":"C1","title":"A research plan on artificial intelligence in courts","part":3,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Come in, Samira. I have read your draft proposal on artificial intelligence in courts, and I would like to go through it section by section."},{"s":"B","t":"Thank you. I was a little worried that the scope is too wide, to be honest."},{"s":"A","t":"It is, but not in the way you think. You say you will study risk assessment tools, translation software and case allocation systems. That is three projects, not one."},{"s":"B","t":"I included all three because each affects how fair a trial feels to ordinary people."},{"s":"A","t":"I understand, but a doctoral committee wants depth. I suggest you keep risk assessment tools and drop the other two, at least for the main study."},{"s":"B","t":"Could I mention translation software as a future direction in the conclusion?"},{"s":"A","t":"Certainly, a short paragraph would be fine. Now, your methods. You plan to interview forty judges. How did you arrive at that number?"},{"s":"B","t":"Mostly from similar studies. But I realise judges are very busy, so I might only reach fifteen."},{"s":"A","t":"Fifteen is realistic, and it is enough if you combine it with court observation. Have you thought about ethics approval?"},{"s":"B","t":"I have started the form. The difficulty is that some courts will not allow recordings, so I would rely on handwritten notes."},{"s":"A","t":"Then please say so clearly. Reviewers dislike surprises, and handwritten notes affect how you can quote people."},{"s":"B","t":"I will add a section on that. I also wanted to ask about the data. A vendor has offered me access to its risk scores for one region."},{"s":"A","t":"That is interesting, but be careful. If the company controls what you see, your independence could be questioned."},{"s":"A","t":"Ask for a written agreement that gives you the right to publish whatever you find."},{"s":"B","t":"Even if the findings are critical of the product?"},{"s":"A","t":"Especially then. Without that clause, I would not sign anything."},{"s":"B","t":"Understood. My other worry is the timeline. I have given myself six months for fieldwork."},{"s":"A","t":"That is too optimistic. Gaining access to courts takes time, so plan for nine months, and begin the first contacts while you are still writing the proposal."},{"s":"B","t":"That would push the writing stage into the third year."},{"s":"A","t":"It would, but a third year for writing is normal. What matters is that the first chapter is nearly finished by the end of year one."},{"s":"B","t":"I could draft the literature review during the access negotiations."},{"s":"A","t":"Exactly. And on that note, your reading list is strong on law, but I see almost nothing from computer science."},{"s":"B","t":"I was afraid I would not understand it."},{"s":"A","t":"You do not need to follow the mathematics. You need to know how these tools are trained and tested, because your argument depends on that."},{"s":"A","t":"I will send you two accessible articles."},{"s":"B","t":"That would be a great help. Should I rewrite the introduction as well?"},{"s":"A","t":"Yes, it is the weakest part. At the moment it begins with a general statement about technology changing society, which tells the reader nothing."},{"s":"A","t":"Start with a real dilemma a judge faces."},{"s":"B","t":"Like deciding whether to follow a score that disagrees with their own judgement."},{"s":"A","t":"Precisely. Send me a new version by the end of the month, and we will meet again in the first week of December."},{"s":"B","t":"I will. Thank you, Dr Hale, this has been really useful."}],"gloss":[{"w":"scope","ar":"نطاق البحث"},{"w":"fieldwork","ar":"العمل الميداني"},{"w":"vendor","ar":"الشركة المزوِّدة"},{"w":"ethics approval","ar":"الموافقة الأخلاقية"},{"w":"literature review","ar":"مراجعة الأدبيات"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the supervisor's main criticism of the proposal at the start?","o":["It lacks a clear research question.","It combines several projects in one study.","It ignores how fair trials feel to people."],"a":1,"why":"ذكرت أنها ثلاثة مشاريع لا مشروع واحد، والنطاق أوسع مما ينبغي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The supervisor refuses to let Samira mention translation software anywhere in the thesis.","a":"F","why":"وافقت على فقرة قصيرة عنها في الخاتمة كاتجاه مستقبلي."},{"k":"gap","q":"Samira thinks she may only manage to interview ___ judges.","a":["fifteen","15"],"why":"الأربعون خطتها الأصلية، لكنها قد تصل إلى خمسة عشر فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"What difficulty does Samira expect with some courts?","o":["They will not give interview times.","They will refuse ethics approval.","They will not permit recording."],"a":2,"why":"قالت إن بعض المحاكم لا تسمح بالتسجيل فستعتمد على ملاحظات مكتوبة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the supervisor advise about the vendor's offer?","o":["Insist on a written right to publish the findings.","Reject any cooperation with a company.","Accept it only if the product is praised."],"a":0,"why":"نصحت بعقد مكتوب يضمن حق النشر حتى لو كانت النتائج ناقدة."},{"k":"gap","q":"The supervisor says Samira should plan for ___ months of fieldwork.","a":["nine","9"],"why":"رأت أن ستة أشهر تفاؤل زائد وطلبت تسعة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the supervisor say about Samira's reading list?","o":["It contains too many old sources.","It has hardly any technical sources.","It needs more law cases."],"a":1,"why":"القائمة قوية في القانون وتكاد تخلو من علوم الحاسوب."},{"k":"mc","q":"How does the supervisor want the introduction to begin?","o":["With a broad statement about technology.","With a summary of the findings.","With a concrete problem a judge faces."],"a":2,"why":"طلبت أن تبدأ بمعضلة حقيقية يواجهها القاضي بدل عبارة عامة."}]},{"id":"l-c1-02","l":"C1","title":"Two researchers disagree about interview data","part":3,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Marcus, I have finished the first pass through the interview transcripts, and I think the picture is clearer than we expected."},{"s":"B","t":"Go on. I have only managed to read about half of them, so tell me what you found."},{"s":"A","t":"Well, out of the thirty-two interviews, twenty-six people said they stopped contacting the council after a first bad experience. That is the central theme for me."},{"s":"B","t":"Hold on. Twenty-six sounds large, but how many of those came from the two districts where we recruited through the community centre?"},{"s":"A","t":"Fourteen, I think. Why does that matter?"},{"s":"B","t":"Because people who go to a community centre are often there to complain. We may be hearing from the most frustrated residents, not the typical ones."},{"s":"A","t":"I see the point, but the other twelve came from the market stalls and the library, and they said the same thing."},{"s":"B","t":"Twelve out of eighteen from those places is still high, I admit. But I would not write that most residents distrust the council."},{"s":"B","t":"I would write that most of our participants did."},{"s":"A","t":"That is a fair distinction, although I find it rather cautious."},{"s":"B","t":"Reviewers will not find it cautious. They will find it correct."},{"s":"A","t":"Fine. Let us move on to coding. I have been coding line by line and letting the themes emerge, which gave me nine categories."},{"s":"B","t":"Nine is too many. We agreed at the start to use the framework from the pilot, with four categories: access, fairness, speed and respect."},{"s":"A","t":"The pilot framework missed something important. Several people talked about not understanding the forms, which fits none of your four."},{"s":"B","t":"It fits access, surely. If you cannot understand the form, you cannot reach the service."},{"s":"A","t":"Not exactly. Access is about opening hours and distance. This is about language and design."},{"s":"B","t":"Then add a fifth category called clarity, and merge your other categories into the framework."},{"s":"A","t":"That seems like a reasonable compromise. I would still keep a short list of the minor themes in an appendix."},{"s":"B","t":"Fine, as long as they do not appear in the main findings."},{"s":"A","t":"Another thing. Four interviews were conducted in Arabic and translated by Leila. Should we ask someone else to check them?"},{"s":"B","t":"I think so. Not because I doubt her, but because translation changes tone, and our whole analysis is about tone."},{"s":"A","t":"She has offered to do the back-translation herself."},{"s":"B","t":"That would not be independent. Let us ask the university language centre. It should take them about two weeks."},{"s":"A","t":"Agreed. One last point, the quotes. I would like to use the strongest ones in the abstract."},{"s":"B","t":"Use one at most. An abstract full of quotations looks like a news article, not a study."},{"s":"A","t":"All right, one quote. I will draft the findings section tonight and send it to you tomorrow morning."},{"s":"B","t":"Good. And I will finish reading the rest of the transcripts, so that I can argue with you properly."},{"s":"A","t":"Please do. I suspect you will find even more people who gave up on the forms."},{"s":"B","t":"If I do, that is one more reason for the new clarity category."}],"gloss":[{"w":"transcript","ar":"نص مكتوب للمقابلة"},{"w":"coding","ar":"ترميز البيانات النوعية"},{"w":"theme","ar":"محور، موضوع متكرر"},{"w":"back-translation","ar":"الترجمة العكسية"},{"w":"appendix","ar":"ملحق"}],"qs":[{"k":"gap","q":"According to Dana, ___ participants stopped contacting the council after one bad experience.","a":["twenty-six","twenty six","26"],"why":"قالت إن ستة وعشرين من اثنين وثلاثين توقفوا عن التواصل."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does Marcus question the recruitment through the community centre?","o":["The centre refused to share names.","The people there may be unusually dissatisfied.","The centre is too far from the council."],"a":1,"why":"يرى أن مرتادي المركز قد يكونون الأكثر شكوى لا الأكثر تمثيلا."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do they agree to write in the report?","o":["Most residents distrust the council.","The council is trusted in two districts.","Most of the participants distrusted the council."],"a":2,"why":"اتفقا على أن الأغلبية بين المشاركين لا بين السكان عموما."},{"k":"mc","q":"How did Dana arrive at nine categories?","o":["She coded line by line without a fixed framework.","She added new questions to the pilot.","She combined the four pilot categories."],"a":0,"why":"قالت إنها رمّزت سطرا سطرا وتركت المحاور تظهر من تلقاء نفسها."},{"k":"tfng","q":"They decide to add a fifth category for difficulty with understanding forms.","a":"T","why":"اقترح ماركوس فئة وضوح خامسة ووافقت دانا على الحل الوسط."},{"k":"mc","q":"Who will check the translated interviews?","o":["Leila, using back-translation.","Marcus, after reading the transcripts.","Staff from the university language centre."],"a":2,"why":"رفض ماركوس أن تراجعها ليلى بنفسها واقترح مركز اللغات."},{"k":"gap","q":"The language centre should need about ___ weeks for the check.","a":["two","2"],"why":"قال ماركوس إن العمل سيستغرق نحو أسبوعين."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Marcus say about quotations in the abstract?","o":["Only a single one should be used.","None should be used.","The strongest three should be used."],"a":0,"why":"قال استعمل اقتباسا واحدا على الأكثر."}]},{"id":"l-c1-03","l":"C1","title":"Evaluating sources for a literature review","part":3,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Right, we have six sources on public procurement for the literature review and we need to decide which to keep. Tom, you start with the government report."},{"s":"B","t":"I like it because it has the most recent figures, but it was written by the very department that runs the system, so I would treat the numbers carefully."},{"s":"C","t":"I agree it is biased, but we could still use it as evidence of what officials claim, as long as we say so."},{"s":"A","t":"Good idea. What about the consultant's blog?"},{"s":"B","t":"It is well written and very persuasive, yet there is no method and no data, and the author sells software to procurement offices."},{"s":"C","t":"I would drop it. Though I did notice it links to a study, so we can follow that link and read the original."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay, so the blog goes, but the study it cites might stay. Now the article from two thousand and nine."},{"s":"B","t":"It is the most cited paper on our list, over four thousand citations I think."},{"s":"C","t":"Cited a lot, but it is fifteen years old. Procurement rules in most countries have changed since then, particularly after online bidding became compulsory."},{"s":"A","t":"I would keep it for the background section, because it defined the key terms everyone uses. For the main argument, we need newer work."},{"s":"B","t":"Fine with me. The preprint, then. It has not been through peer review yet."},{"s":"C","t":"But the authors are from a reputable university and the sample is huge, covering nine countries."},{"s":"A","t":"I am uneasy about it. The conclusions could change after review. How about we include it but mark it clearly as a preprint?"},{"s":"C","t":"That is what I would do too. Tom?"},{"s":"B","t":"I would still be cautious, although I can live with that, provided we do not build a whole section on it."},{"s":"A","t":"Agreed. Next is the think tank report, which argues that publishing all contracts reduces corruption."},{"s":"B","t":"It reads like a campaign document. Every example supports the same conclusion, and nothing in it is critical of the idea."},{"s":"C","t":"I would not throw it out completely. It has a useful table comparing disclosure laws in twelve countries, which we cannot find elsewhere."},{"s":"A","t":"Then we use the table and cite the report carefully, without repeating its conclusions."},{"s":"B","t":"Good compromise. What about the last one, the case study of the hospital in the north?"},{"s":"C","t":"It is only one hospital, so we cannot generalise. But the detail is wonderful, because it shows how a bribe actually passes through the paperwork."},{"s":"A","t":"Then it works as an illustration, not as proof. I will put it in the discussion."},{"s":"B","t":"Let us write that down. Who is doing the summary table?"},{"s":"C","t":"I can do it by Thursday, if you send me the reference list tonight."},{"s":"A","t":"I will send it within the hour. And Tom, could you email Dr Nolan and ask whether a preprint is acceptable in a literature review?"},{"s":"B","t":"Sure, I will do that straight after this meeting."},{"s":"C","t":"Brilliant. Then we only need to agree on a reading order for the group presentation."}],"gloss":[{"w":"procurement","ar":"المشتريات العمومية"},{"w":"preprint","ar":"نسخة بحثية قبل التحكيم"},{"w":"peer review","ar":"التحكيم العلمي"},{"w":"generalise","ar":"يعمّم النتائج"},{"w":"illustration","ar":"مثال توضيحي"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why does Tom want to treat the government report's figures carefully?","o":["The department that produced it runs the system.","The figures are several years out of date.","The report has no clear method."],"a":0,"why":"أشار إلى أن التقرير كتبته الإدارة نفسها التي تشغّل النظام."},{"k":"gap","q":"Tom says the consultant's blog has no data and no ___.","a":["method"],"why":"قال إنه بلا منهج وبلا بيانات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What do the students decide about the article from two thousand and nine?","o":["Remove it because it is old.","Use it as the basis of the main argument.","Keep it for the background section."],"a":2,"why":"قررت أن تبقيه للخلفية لأنه عرّف المصطلحات الأساسية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Tom is completely comfortable with using the preprint from the start.","a":"F","why":"بقي حذرا ووافق فقط بشرط ألا يُبنى عليه قسم كامل."},{"k":"gap","q":"The think tank report contains a table comparing disclosure laws in ___ countries.","a":["twelve","12"],"why":"التسعة هي عدد دول ورقة ما قبل التحكيم، والجدول يشمل اثنتي عشرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"How will the group use the hospital case study?","o":["As an example, not as proof.","As proof that bribery is common.","As the main source for the conclusion."],"a":0,"why":"حالة مستشفى واحد لا تعمَّم، فتصلح للتوضيح فقط."},{"k":"gap","q":"Jess promises to finish the summary table by ___.","a":["Thursday"],"why":"قالت إنها تنجزه بحلول يوم الخميس."},{"k":"mc","q":"What will Tom do after the meeting?","o":["Send the reference list to Jess.","Ask the lecturer about using preprints.","Write the section on the think tank."],"a":1,"why":"طُلب منه أن يراسل الدكتور نولان بشأن قبول الورقة قبل التحكيم."}]},{"id":"l-c1-04","l":"C1","title":"Seminar: protecting whistleblowers","part":3,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Today we are looking at whistleblower protection. Omar, you read the case study. Can you start us off?"},{"s":"B","t":"Yes. It concerns an accountant in a regional hospital who noticed that invoices for cleaning services were being paid twice."},{"s":"B","t":"She reported it internally, and within months her contract was not renewed."},{"s":"C","t":"Which is the classic pattern, isn't it? The employee does the right thing and the employer finds another reason to remove them."},{"s":"A","t":"That is what we need to examine. Hannah, in your essay you argued that the law should shift the burden of proof. Explain that."},{"s":"C","t":"Normally, the worker must prove that the dismissal was connected to the report. That is almost impossible. I argued that the employer should have to prove it was not."},{"s":"B","t":"I see the logic, but isn't it unfair to employers? Anyone about to be dismissed for poor performance could simply file a report and claim protection."},{"s":"C","t":"That is the standard objection, but the evidence from countries that have tried it suggests such abuse is rare. Most people fear reporting, not exploit it."},{"s":"A","t":"Omar, are you convinced?"},{"s":"B","t":"Partly. I would accept the reversed burden if the report was made in good faith and through an official channel, but not if the employee went straight to the press."},{"s":"C","t":"I disagree with that. Official channels are often the very place where the problem is hidden. If a manager is part of the fraud, an internal report just warns him."},{"s":"A","t":"That raises the question of external reporting. Most laws set out three tiers: first the employer, then a regulator, then the public. What is the thinking behind that?"},{"s":"B","t":"To give organisations a chance to fix problems before they become scandals, and to protect people from false accusations in the media."},{"s":"C","t":"But the tiers can also delay everything. By the time someone reaches the third tier, the evidence may have disappeared."},{"s":"A","t":"Good. Let me add a point from the case study. The accountant had no lawyer, and she could not afford one. Does that matter?"},{"s":"B","t":"A great deal. Rights that exist on paper are useless if a person cannot afford to use them."},{"s":"B","t":"I think free legal advice is more important than any change in the burden of proof."},{"s":"C","t":"I would put them on the same level, to be fair. Though I accept that without advice, most people never reach a tribunal."},{"s":"A","t":"What about financial rewards? Some systems give whistleblowers a percentage of the money recovered."},{"s":"B","t":"I am against that. It changes the motive, and defence lawyers will say the witness is only in it for the money."},{"s":"C","t":"I used to think so too, but the sums recovered are huge, and rewards do make people come forward."},{"s":"C","t":"I think a small reward is acceptable, as long as the report is checked independently."},{"s":"A","t":"We are almost out of time. Let me ask each of you for one recommendation for the hospital's board, in a sentence."},{"s":"B","t":"Set up an anonymous reporting line run by someone outside the hospital."},{"s":"C","t":"Promise in writing that nobody will be dismissed for six months after reporting, unless the hospital proves a separate reason."},{"s":"A","t":"Thank you both. For next week, please read the two statutes on the reading list and bring one example of how each protects a worker."},{"s":"B","t":"Will the examples need to come from real cases, or can they be hypothetical?"},{"s":"A","t":"Real cases, please. They are much easier to discuss."}],"gloss":[{"w":"whistleblower","ar":"المبلّغ عن المخالفات"},{"w":"burden of proof","ar":"عبء الإثبات"},{"w":"in good faith","ar":"بحسن نية"},{"w":"tribunal","ar":"محكمة، هيئة قضائية"},{"w":"statute","ar":"قانون، تشريع"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What happened to the accountant in the case study?","o":["She was dismissed immediately after reporting.","She was paid twice for her work.","Her contract was not renewed after she reported internally."],"a":2,"why":"أبلغت داخليا ثم لم يُجدَّد عقدها بعد أشهر."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Hannah propose about the burden of proof?","o":["The employer should prove the dismissal was unconnected.","The worker should prove the report was honest.","The tribunal should collect the evidence itself."],"a":0,"why":"اقترحت أن يُثبت صاحب العمل أن الفصل لا علاقة له بالتبليغ."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is Omar's objection to this idea?","o":["Reporting is too costly for workers.","Employees facing dismissal might use reports to protect themselves.","Employers would stop hiring accountants."],"a":1,"why":"خشي أن يقدّم من سيُفصل بلاغا ليحتمي به."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Hannah says that misuse of such protection is common in countries that have introduced it.","a":"F","why":"قالت إن إساءة الاستعمال نادرة، فالناس يخافون من التبليغ."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why does Hannah reject Omar's condition about official channels?","o":["Managers may be involved in the wrongdoing.","The press is more reliable than regulators.","Official channels take too many months."],"a":0,"why":"إن كان المدير متورطا فالبلاغ الداخلي ينبّهه فقط."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Omar consider the most important improvement?","o":["A reward for whistleblowers.","A change in the burden of proof.","Free legal advice."],"a":2,"why":"رأى أن الاستشارة القانونية المجانية أهم من تغيير عبء الإثبات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is Hannah's view on financial rewards now?","o":["She opposes them completely.","She accepts small ones if the report is verified.","She wants rewards to be as large as possible."],"a":1,"why":"غيّرت رأيها وقبلت مكافأة صغيرة بشرط التحقق المستقل."},{"k":"gap","q":"Hannah's recommendation protects staff from dismissal for ___ after reporting.","a":["six months","6 months"],"why":"اقترحت وعدا مكتوبا بعدم الفصل لمدة ستة أشهر."}]},{"id":"l-c1-05","l":"C1","title":"Feedback on a start-up pitch","part":3,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Thank you both for making time. I have just presented the pitch, and I would love your honest reaction."},{"s":"B","t":"I will start with the positive. The opening story about the bakery owner who lost a contract because he missed a deadline was excellent. I could picture him."},{"s":"C","t":"I agree, that was the strongest moment. My concern is what came next. You went straight into twelve slides of technology and the audience lost the thread."},{"s":"A","t":"I thought investors wanted to see that the technology is sound."},{"s":"C","t":"They do, but later. First they want to know who pays, how much, and why they will not leave."},{"s":"B","t":"Which brings me to pricing. You are charging forty pounds a month. For a business with five employees that sounds low, and low prices make people suspicious."},{"s":"C","t":"I disagree a little. For a very small business forty pounds is already a stretch. I would keep it but add a higher plan with extra features for larger companies."},{"s":"A","t":"We have discussed a premium tier, with automatic bid drafting."},{"s":"B","t":"Then put it on the slide. At the moment I only saw it in your notes."},{"s":"A","t":"Okay. What about the market size? I said there are two million small firms."},{"s":"B","t":"That is the number I would remove. You have not explained which of those firms actually bid for public contracts, and most never will."},{"s":"B","t":"Start with the ones that do, even if the number is smaller, perhaps three hundred thousand."},{"s":"C","t":"Exactly. A small, believable number beats a huge one that nobody trusts."},{"s":"A","t":"Hmm, I worry that three hundred thousand sounds too small to be exciting."},{"s":"C","t":"Investors are not looking for excitement at that point. They are looking for a plan to win the first ten thousand customers."},{"s":"A","t":"And I only have a hundred and twenty at the moment."},{"s":"B","t":"Which is a good start, as long as you say how many of them still pay after three months. Do you know?"},{"s":"A","t":"Around seventy percent."},{"s":"B","t":"That is a strong figure! Put it on the slide next to the bakery story. It proves the story is not an exception."},{"s":"C","t":"One more point, about the competition. You said you have none."},{"s":"A","t":"Well, nobody does exactly what we do."},{"s":"C","t":"Everybody has competitors, even if it is only spreadsheets and email. Name them, and explain why your product is better than a spreadsheet."},{"s":"B","t":"I would add a short live demonstration. Two minutes, showing a tender notice becoming a checklist."},{"s":"A","t":"I was afraid the internet would fail during the pitch."},{"s":"B","t":"Record it as a video, then. That way nothing can go wrong, and you still show the product working."},{"s":"A","t":"That makes sense. Should I cut anything to stay within ten minutes?"},{"s":"C","t":"Yes, the team slide. Keep one sentence about it, because your experience is already clear from the opening."},{"s":"A","t":"Right. I will rebuild it this weekend and send a new version on Monday."},{"s":"B","t":"Send it to both of us. And practise out loud, because your delivery was a little fast."}],"gloss":[{"w":"pitch","ar":"عرض ترويجي للمستثمرين"},{"w":"tier","ar":"مستوى، فئة"},{"w":"tender","ar":"مناقصة"},{"w":"competitor","ar":"منافس"},{"w":"market size","ar":"حجم السوق"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which part of the pitch did the mentors like most?","o":["The slides about the technology.","The pricing explanation.","The opening story about a small business owner."],"a":2,"why":"أشاد كلاهما بقصة صاحب المخبز في البداية."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is Ruth's main criticism of the structure?","o":["The technology section came too early and was too long.","The founder spoke for too long about her team.","The ending did not include any figures."],"a":0,"why":"انتقلت مباشرة إلى اثنتي عشرة شريحة تقنية فضاع الجمهور."},{"k":"gap","q":"The current subscription costs ___ pounds a month.","a":["forty","40"],"why":"ذكرت الباحثة أن السعر أربعون جنيها شهريا."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Ruth suggest about the price?","o":["Lower it for very small firms.","Keep it and add a more expensive plan.","Raise it immediately to avoid suspicion."],"a":1,"why":"خالفت غريغ واقترحت إبقاء السعر مع خطة أعلى."},{"k":"gap","q":"Greg suggests a starting market of about ___ firms.","a":["three hundred thousand","300000","300,000"],"why":"اقترح ثلاثمئة ألف شركة تشارك فعلا في العقود العمومية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Greg is impressed by the proportion of customers who are still paying after three months.","a":"T","why":"وصف نسبة السبعين بالمئة بأنها رقم قوي."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does Greg recommend instead of a live demonstration online?","o":["A printed handout.","A recorded video of the product.","A longer question session."],"a":1,"why":"خوفا من انقطاع الإنترنت اقترح تسجيل العرض بالفيديو."},{"k":"mc","q":"What should the founder do with the team slide?","o":["Move it to the end.","Add more photographs.","Reduce it to a single sentence."],"a":2,"why":"نصحتها روث بجملة واحدة لأن خبرتها واضحة من البداية."}]},{"id":"l-c1-06","l":"C1","title":"Why people trust or distrust institutions","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon, everyone. Today I want to look at a question that sits at the centre of public administration: why do people trust some institutions and distrust others?"},{"s":"A","t":"Researchers usually begin by separating two ideas that are often confused."},{"s":"A","t":"Trust is a belief about what an institution will do, while trustworthiness is a fact about whether it deserves that belief. The two do not always match."},{"s":"A","t":"A hospital can be excellent and still distrusted, and a careless agency can be trusted for years."},{"s":"A","t":"The first explanation is performance. People judge institutions by what they deliver: clean water, punctual trains, a pension that arrives on time."},{"s":"A","t":"Studies in several countries show that when services improve, trust rises, but slowly. The reverse is quicker."},{"s":"A","t":"A single visible failure, such as a bridge collapse, can erase years of gradual gains."},{"s":"A","t":"Psychologists call this negativity bias, and it explains why bad news travels so much further than good news."},{"s":"A","t":"The second explanation concerns process rather than results. Procedural fairness means that people accept an unwelcome decision if they believe it was reached honestly."},{"s":"A","t":"In one well-known type of experiment, participants who lost a dispute were more satisfied if they had explained their side than if they had won unheard."},{"s":"A","t":"So being listened to matters at least as much as the outcome."},{"s":"A","t":"The third explanation is transparency, and here the picture is more complicated than most reformers expect. It seems obvious that publishing information should increase trust. Often it does."},{"s":"A","t":"But when an institution publishes data that reveal mistakes, trust can fall in the short term."},{"s":"A","t":"The advantage appears later, because people begin to believe that problems will be found and corrected rather than hidden."},{"s":"A","t":"So transparency is a long-term investment, not a quick repair."},{"s":"A","t":"A fourth factor is closeness. People tend to trust local institutions more than national ones, partly because they can see the people who work there."},{"s":"A","t":"A town clerk is a neighbour; a ministry is an abstraction. Personal contact is usually, though not always, positive."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, we must consider history and culture. In societies where public officials have long taken bribes, citizens learn to expect corruption and behave accordingly."},{"s":"A","t":"They pay, because everyone pays. That behaviour then confirms the expectation, and a cycle forms. Breaking it is difficult, because trust cannot be ordered by law."},{"s":"A","t":"Reformers who succeed tend to do three things. They start with one visible service and fix it completely. They punish a few high-profile offenders fairly and openly."},{"s":"A","t":"And they keep going for years, as one campaign is never enough."},{"s":"A","t":"To summarise, trust is built slowly through performance, fairness, openness and personal contact, and it is lost quickly."},{"s":"A","t":"For your assignment, choose one institution you know well and explain which of these factors is the most important in its case."},{"s":"A","t":"Please submit it in two weeks, and keep it under one thousand five hundred words."}],"gloss":[{"w":"trustworthiness","ar":"جدارة بالثقة"},{"w":"negativity bias","ar":"الانحياز للسلبي"},{"w":"procedural fairness","ar":"عدالة الإجراءات"},{"w":"cycle","ar":"حلقة مفرغة، دورة"},{"w":"high-profile","ar":"بارز، مشهور"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How does the lecturer distinguish trust from trustworthiness?","o":["Trust is a belief, while trustworthiness is whether the belief is deserved.","Trust is earned by results, while trustworthiness comes from laws.","Trust belongs to citizens, while trustworthiness belongs to ministers."],"a":0,"why":"الثقة اعتقاد بما ستفعله المؤسسة، أما الجدارة بالثقة فحقيقة عن استحقاقها."},{"k":"mc","q":"What effect does one highly visible failure have on trust?","o":["It rises slowly afterwards.","It destroys gains built up over many years.","It affects only local institutions."],"a":1,"why":"فشل واحد ظاهر قد يمحو مكاسب سنوات، وهذا هو التحيز السلبي."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Participants who lost a dispute were happier if they had been allowed to present their case.","a":"T","why":"أشار إلى أن من أتيح له شرح موقفه كان أكثر رضا حتى مع الخسارة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What can happen when an institution publishes data that reveal its mistakes?","o":["Trust grows at once.","Trust falls at first but may recover later.","Trust stays exactly the same."],"a":1,"why":"قد تنخفض الثقة قصيرا المدى ثم تزداد حين يصدق الناس أن المشكلات تُصحَّح."},{"k":"gap","q":"People often trust ___ institutions more than national ones.","a":["local"],"why":"ذكر أن الناس يثقون بالمؤسسات المحلية أكثر لأنهم يرون العاملين فيها."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why is it hard to break the cycle in societies with long-standing bribery?","o":["Officials refuse any investigation.","Laws against bribery do not exist.","Paying bribes confirms people's expectations."],"a":2,"why":"الدفع يؤكد التوقع فتتكرر الدائرة، والثقة لا تُفرض بقانون."},{"k":"gap","q":"Successful reformers begin by fully fixing one visible ___.","a":["service"],"why":"قال إنهم يبدؤون بخدمة واحدة ظاهرة ويصلحونها تماما."},{"k":"gap","q":"The students' assignment must be shorter than ___ words.","a":["1500","1,500","one thousand five hundred"],"why":"قال أبقوه أقل من ألف وخمسمئة كلمة، والأسبوعان موعد التسليم."}]},{"id":"l-c1-07","l":"C1","title":"Bias in human and machine decisions","part":4,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Welcome back. Last week we asked whether machines can be fair."},{"s":"A","t":"This week I want to put that question next to an uncomfortable fact: human decision makers are not especially fair either. Any honest comparison has to start there."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me begin with people. Decades of research show that human judgement is affected by things that have nothing to do with the case."},{"s":"A","t":"A well-known study of parole hearings reported that decisions varied with the time since the judges' last break."},{"s":"A","t":"I should say that later researchers argued the pattern might partly come from the order in which cases were scheduled, so we should treat the original claim cautiously."},{"s":"A","t":"Even so, the broader finding is solid: the same person can reach different conclusions about identical cases on different days."},{"s":"A","t":"Psychologists call this noise, and distinguish it from bias. Bias is a consistent push in one direction. Noise is random scatter."},{"s":"A","t":"A scale that always reads two kilos too heavy is biased; a scale that gives a different number each time is noisy. Human systems suffer from both."},{"s":"A","t":"Now to machines. A common first reaction is that an algorithm is neutral because it has no feelings. That is mistaken."},{"s":"A","t":"A model learns from historical data, and if the data record past discrimination, the model can reproduce it."},{"s":"A","t":"A famous kind of example involves hiring tools trained on the résumés of previous successful employees."},{"s":"A","t":"If most of those employees were men, the system may learn to prefer features that correlate with being male, even when gender is never mentioned."},{"s":"A","t":"This is called proxy discrimination."},{"s":"A","t":"However, machines have one real advantage: consistency. Given the same input, a model returns the same output every time. So it removes noise, even if it keeps bias."},{"s":"A","t":"Furthermore, a model can be audited. We can test it with thousands of artificial cases, change one detail at a time, and see whether the result changes."},{"s":"A","t":"We cannot do this with a human judge, who will remember the experiment and may change behaviour."},{"s":"A","t":"There is a catch, though. Researchers have shown that there are several mathematical definitions of fairness, and in most real situations they cannot all be satisfied at once."},{"s":"A","t":"For example, you can demand that the error rate is the same for two groups, or that a given score means the same risk for both groups."},{"s":"A","t":"If the groups differ in the underlying rate of the event, you cannot have both. This does not mean that fairness is impossible."},{"s":"A","t":"It means that someone must choose which definition matters, and that choice is a moral and legal one, not a technical one."},{"s":"A","t":"So what is the practical conclusion? Most experts now favour what is called human oversight, but we have to be careful with that phrase."},{"s":"A","t":"Studies suggest that people supervising a system often follow its recommendation even when it is wrong, a habit known as automation bias."},{"s":"A","t":"Oversight works only if the human has time, training and the genuine power to disagree."},{"s":"A","t":"In short: people are noisy and biased, machines are consistent and biased, and the sensible question is not which is perfect but which can be examined, challenged and improved."},{"s":"A","t":"For next week, read the two articles on the course page, and think about which definition of fairness you would choose for a court."}],"gloss":[{"w":"parole","ar":"إفراج مشروط"},{"w":"noise","ar":"تشتت عشوائي"},{"w":"audit","ar":"يدقق، يفحص"},{"w":"proxy","ar":"بديل غير مباشر"},{"w":"oversight","ar":"إشراف ورقابة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the lecturer's starting point for comparing humans and machines?","o":["Machines are always more accurate than people.","People are not particularly fair decision makers either.","Algorithms have already replaced judges."],"a":1,"why":"بدأ بحقيقة غير مريحة: البشر أيضا ليسوا عادلين بشكل خاص."},{"k":"mc","q":"How does the lecturer treat the parole hearing study?","o":["As proof that judges are corrupt.","As a result that should be handled cautiously.","As a study that has been completely disproved."],"a":1,"why":"ذكر أن باحثين لاحقين فسّروا النمط جزئيا بترتيب الجلسات، فيجب الحذر."},{"k":"gap","q":"A scale that always reads two kilos too heavy is an example of ___.","a":["bias"],"why":"الانحياز دفع ثابت في اتجاه واحد، أما التشتت العشوائي فهو الضجيج."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why might a hiring tool disadvantage women?","o":["It was told to ignore gender completely.","It learns features linked to male employees in past data.","It cannot read women's résumés."],"a":1,"why":"تتعلم من بيانات سابقة فتفضّل سمات مرتبطة بالذكور دون ذكر الجنس."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A trained model may give a different answer to the same input on different days.","a":"F","why":"قال إن النموذج يعطي المخرجات نفسها في كل مرة، فيزيل الضجيج."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why can the different definitions of fairness not all be met together?","o":["The groups differ in the underlying rate of the event.","Computers cannot compare two groups.","Courts have not defined fairness."],"a":0,"why":"إذا اختلفت المعدلات الأساسية بين المجموعتين تعذّر تحقيق التعريفين معا."},{"k":"gap","q":"Following a system's wrong recommendation is called ___ bias.","a":["automation"],"why":"سمّى هذه العادة انحياز الأتمتة عند من يشرف على النظام."},{"k":"mc","q":"When does human oversight of a system work?","o":["When the person has time, training and real power to disagree.","When the person checks every case twice.","When the system explains its mathematics."],"a":0,"why":"الإشراف مفيد فقط إن توفر للمشرف الوقت والتدريب وقدرة حقيقية على الاعتراض."}]},{"id":"l-c1-08","l":"C1","title":"How corruption is measured","part":4,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Today's topic sounds simple: how do we measure corruption? The difficulty is obvious."},{"s":"A","t":"People who pay or accept bribes do not report them, so there is no direct count."},{"s":"A","t":"Researchers have therefore developed several indirect methods, and each has strengths and weaknesses."},{"s":"A","t":"The first and most famous approach is the perception index. These indices combine surveys of business people and country experts to produce a score."},{"s":"A","t":"They are popular because they cover almost every country and can be compared across years. But they measure what people think, not what happens."},{"s":"A","t":"And perceptions can lag behind reality: a country may clean up its customs service, yet continue to be seen as corrupt for years."},{"s":"A","t":"The second approach is experience surveys. Instead of asking what people believe, interviewers ask households whether they paid a bribe to any official during the previous twelve months."},{"s":"A","t":"This is closer to real behaviour. The weakness is honesty: people may be ashamed or afraid to answer, so the numbers are probably too low."},{"s":"A","t":"To reduce this problem, some researchers use a technique called a list experiment."},{"s":"A","t":"Half of the respondents see a list of four harmless activities and say how many they did."},{"s":"A","t":"The other half see the same list plus a fifth item, paying a bribe."},{"s":"A","t":"The difference in averages between the groups estimates the share of bribe payers, though nobody has to admit to anything."},{"s":"A","t":"A third family of methods uses hard data. Take public procurement."},{"s":"A","t":"Analysts can examine contracts and look for warning signs: only one bidder, a very short advertising period, or frequent changes to the price after signing."},{"s":"A","t":"None of these proves a crime, but a pattern of red flags points investigators to where they should look."},{"s":"A","t":"The huge advantage is that the data already exist and can be analysed every day. The disadvantage is that corrupt officials learn which signs are watched and adapt."},{"s":"A","t":"The fourth approach is more creative. Economists have estimated corruption by measuring leakage."},{"s":"A","t":"For example, if a government budget says that one hundred schools should receive textbooks, researchers visit the schools to check what arrived."},{"s":"A","t":"The gap between the budget and the delivery is the loss."},{"s":"A","t":"This is a good method for specific programmes, but it cannot show how much of the loss is theft and how much is simple waste or poor record keeping."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, some studies use natural experiments. When auditors visit a randomly chosen municipality, the amount of misused money they find can be compared with that in similar places."},{"s":"A","t":"Such studies show that audits do reduce misuse in the places they visit, and sometimes in neighbouring places too."},{"s":"A","t":"What should we conclude? No single measure is reliable on its own."},{"s":"A","t":"The best practice is to combine them, so that the weaknesses of one are covered by the strengths of another."},{"s":"A","t":"And whichever indicator you use, always ask what exactly it measures. Is it perception, experience or a statistical sign?"},{"s":"A","t":"For the next session, please bring an example of an indicator from a report you have read, and be ready to explain which of these approaches it belongs to."}],"gloss":[{"w":"perception","ar":"انطباع، إدراك"},{"w":"bidder","ar":"مقدِّم عطاء"},{"w":"red flag","ar":"مؤشر إنذار"},{"w":"leakage","ar":"تسرّب الأموال"},{"w":"municipality","ar":"بلدية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Why is there no direct count of corruption?","o":["Governments refuse to publish budgets.","Those involved do not report it.","Courts hide the statistics."],"a":1,"why":"من يدفع الرشوة أو يقبلها لا يبلّغ عنها، فلا عدّ مباشرا."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is a weakness of perception indices?","o":["They cover too few countries.","They cannot be compared across years.","They may be slow to reflect real improvements."],"a":2,"why":"قد تتأخر الانطباعات عن الواقع، فتبقى الدولة موصومة بعد الإصلاح."},{"k":"gap","q":"Experience surveys ask about bribes paid in the previous ___ months.","a":["twelve","12"],"why":"ذكر أن الأسئلة تخص الأشهر الاثني عشر السابقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the purpose of a list experiment?","o":["To count bribes in official records.","To let people answer without admitting a bribe directly.","To compare two lists of countries."],"a":1,"why":"الفرق في المتوسطات يقدّر نسبة دافعي الرشوة دون أن يعترف أحد."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which warning sign in public procurement is mentioned?","o":["A contract signed at night.","A foreign company winning.","Only one bidder."],"a":2,"why":"ذكر من العلامات: مزايد واحد، ومدة إعلان قصيرة جدا، وتغيير السعر بعد التوقيع."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The leakage method can show exactly how much of the loss is theft.","a":"F","why":"قال إنها لا تبيّن حصة السرقة من الهدر أو سوء التوثيق."},{"k":"gap","q":"Audits reduce the ___ of public money in the places they visit.","a":["misuse"],"why":"أوضحت دراسات التجارب الطبيعية أن المراجعة تقلل إساءة استعمال المال العام."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the lecturer conclude?","o":["Perception indices should be abandoned.","Several measures should be combined.","Experience surveys are the most reliable."],"a":1,"why":"خلص إلى أن لا مقياس وحده يكفي والأفضل الجمع بينها."}]},{"id":"l-c1-09","l":"C1","title":"The psychology of persuasion in the courtroom","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good evening."},{"s":"A","t":"Tonight we look at a question that lawyers have asked for centuries, and psychologists for about a hundred years: what makes a jury believe one story rather than another?"},{"s":"A","t":"Let me start with a common belief, which is that jurors weigh up each piece of evidence like accountants adding up numbers. Research suggests otherwise."},{"s":"A","t":"Most jurors build a story. They take the facts they hear and arrange them into an account of what happened, with a beginning, a motive and an ending."},{"s":"A","t":"The story that is most complete and consistent tends to win, even if some of its details are weaker than those in the competing version."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, order matters. Experiments with mock juries find a primacy effect: information heard first leaves a strong impression."},{"s":"A","t":"Yet there is also a recency effect, since the last thing heard is easiest to remember. Which dominates depends on the length of the trial."},{"s":"A","t":"In short trials, the final speech has more influence."},{"s":"A","t":"In long trials, where jurors hear weeks of evidence, the opening statement tends to shape how everything afterwards is understood."},{"s":"A","t":"So experienced lawyers use their openings to give jurors a frame."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, the way witnesses speak. Many people assume that a confident witness is an accurate one."},{"s":"A","t":"In fact, studies of eyewitnesses show only a weak connection between confidence and accuracy, particularly when the confidence is expressed long after the event."},{"s":"A","t":"Nevertheless, jurors are strongly influenced by confidence."},{"s":"A","t":"A witness who hesitates and says, I think, perhaps, may be completely right and still be believed less than one who speaks firmly and is wrong."},{"s":"A","t":"Fourth, the effect of expert witnesses. Jurors usually treat a scientist as especially credible, but credibility is not the same as clarity."},{"s":"A","t":"An expert who uses technical language may seem impressive at first yet be less persuasive than one who explains a result with an everyday comparison."},{"s":"A","t":"In several experiments, an expert who used a simple analogy produced greater understanding and also greater agreement."},{"s":"A","t":"Fifth, emotion. Photographs of injuries, for example, can make jurors angrier at the defendant."},{"s":"A","t":"Courts know this, so judges are expected to weigh how much a picture proves against how much it might inflame."},{"s":"A","t":"Interestingly, studies suggest that instructions to disregard something a juror has already heard often fail."},{"s":"A","t":"In some cases they make the information more memorable, a result known as the backfire effect, although the evidence for this is mixed."},{"s":"A","t":"What does this mean for justice? Not that persuasion is a trick. Clear stories, fair order and honest explanation help jurors understand a case."},{"s":"A","t":"The danger lies in techniques that exploit weaknesses rather than helping understanding. That is why many legal systems regulate what lawyers may say in closing arguments."},{"s":"A","t":"For the assignment, take a famous trial from any country, read the summary, and identify which of the five influences we discussed was most visible."},{"s":"A","t":"You have until the fifteenth of next month."}],"gloss":[{"w":"jury","ar":"هيئة المحلفين"},{"w":"primacy effect","ar":"أثر الأسبقية"},{"w":"eyewitness","ar":"شاهد عيان"},{"w":"analogy","ar":"تشبيه، قياس"},{"w":"inflame","ar":"يثير الغضب"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"According to the lecturer, how do most jurors handle evidence?","o":["They add up each item separately.","They arrange the facts into a story.","They follow the first witness."],"a":1,"why":"يبني معظم المحلفين رواية متماسكة من الوقائع لا حسابا لكل دليل."},{"k":"mc","q":"In very long trials, which part tends to shape understanding?","o":["The closing speech.","The final witness.","The opening statement."],"a":2,"why":"في المحاكمات الطويلة يشكّل البيان الافتتاحي فهم كل ما بعده."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Studies show that a witness's confidence is a very reliable sign of accuracy.","a":"F","why":"وجدت الدراسات صلة ضعيفة فقط بين الثقة والدقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What can happen to a witness who hesitates?","o":["They may be right but still believed less.","They are always found to be lying.","They are replaced by an expert."],"a":0,"why":"قد يكون الشاهد المتردد محقا ومع ذلك يُصدَّق أقل من شاهد حازم مخطئ."},{"k":"mc","q":"What makes an expert witness more persuasive in the experiments mentioned?","o":["A longer career.","More technical vocabulary.","An everyday comparison."],"a":2,"why":"الخبير الذي استعمل تشبيها بسيطا حقق فهما واتفاقا أكبر."},{"k":"gap","q":"The tendency of ignored information to become more memorable is the ___ effect.","a":["backfire"],"why":"سمّاه أثر الارتداد، مع أن الأدلة عليه متباينة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why must judges consider carefully whether to show certain photographs?","o":["They cost too much to print.","They might anger jurors more than they prove.","They are usually inaccurate."],"a":1,"why":"يوازن القاضي بين ما تثبته الصورة وما قد تثيره من غضب."},{"k":"gap","q":"The assignment is due on the ___ of next month.","a":["fifteenth","15th","15"],"why":"قال أمامكم حتى الخامس عشر من الشهر القادم."}]},{"id":"l-c1-10","l":"C1","title":"How the brain consolidates memory during sleep","part":4,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Most students believe that sleep is time off for the brain, a period when nothing useful happens."},{"s":"A","t":"Today I want to show you that the reverse is closer to the truth: sleep is when much of what you learned during the day is stored."},{"s":"A","t":"The key idea is memory consolidation, the process by which a fragile new memory becomes stable and long lasting."},{"s":"A","t":"When you learn something, a region deep in the brain called the hippocampus records it quickly. You can think of the hippocampus as a temporary notebook."},{"s":"A","t":"It writes fast but has limited space. The cortex, the outer layer of the brain, is more like a library."},{"s":"A","t":"It stores information for decades, but it learns slowly. Consolidation is the transfer of information from the notebook to the library, and it happens mainly while you sleep."},{"s":"A","t":"Sleep is not a single state. It has stages, and they appear in cycles of about ninety minutes."},{"s":"A","t":"Early in the night, most of the time is spent in deep sleep, sometimes called slow wave sleep because the brain's electrical activity forms long, slow waves."},{"s":"A","t":"Later in the night, rapid eye movement sleep, or REM, becomes longer. Researchers believe the stages play different roles."},{"s":"A","t":"Deep sleep seems especially important for facts and events, what psychologists call declarative memory."},{"s":"A","t":"During slow waves, the hippocampus replays the pattern of activity it showed during learning, in a compressed form, and sends it to the cortex."},{"s":"A","t":"Short bursts of fast activity, called sleep spindles, appear at the same time and seem to help with the transfer."},{"s":"A","t":"In one classic design, volunteers learned pairs of words in the evening."},{"s":"A","t":"Those who slept remembered considerably more pairs the next morning than those who stayed awake for the same period."},{"s":"A","t":"REM sleep, by contrast, seems to be more important for emotional memories and for skills. It may also help the brain to combine information in new ways."},{"s":"A","t":"Volunteers who were given a puzzle with a hidden shortcut were more likely to discover it after a night's sleep than after the same time awake."},{"s":"A","t":"I should mention that these studies involve small groups, and scientists are still debating how strongly each stage is tied to each type of memory."},{"s":"A","t":"What does this mean in practice? First, an all-night revision session before an exam may be counterproductive."},{"s":"A","t":"You may gain a few extra hours of study, but you lose the processing that makes the information stable."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, a short nap of around twenty minutes can improve alertness, while a longer nap of ninety minutes can include a full cycle and help consolidation."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, studying difficult material shortly before bedtime might give the brain more to work on."},{"s":"A","t":"The evidence for this is weaker, so I would not rely on it alone."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, a word of caution. Sleep does not replace practice. Consolidation strengthens what you have already learned; it does not create knowledge from nothing."},{"s":"A","t":"So the formula for good learning is simple: study with attention, sleep well, and review the next day."},{"s":"A","t":"For the next lecture, please keep a sleep diary for one week and note your study times. We will use them as a class data set."}],"gloss":[{"w":"consolidation","ar":"ترسيخ الذاكرة"},{"w":"hippocampus","ar":"الحُصين"},{"w":"cortex","ar":"القشرة الدماغية"},{"w":"sleep spindle","ar":"مغزل النوم"},{"w":"counterproductive","ar":"يأتي بنتيجة عكسية"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How does the lecturer compare the hippocampus?","o":["To a library with permanent shelves.","To a temporary notebook.","To a filing cabinet."],"a":1,"why":"شبّهه بدفتر مؤقت يكتب بسرعة ومساحته محدودة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the main feature of the cortex?","o":["It stores information for a long time but learns slowly.","It records new events very quickly.","It is active only during REM sleep."],"a":0,"why":"القشرة كمكتبة: تحفظ لعقود لكنها تتعلم ببطء."},{"k":"gap","q":"Sleep cycles last about ___ minutes.","a":["ninety","90"],"why":"ذكر أن الدورات تدوم نحو تسعين دقيقة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which kind of memory depends especially on deep sleep?","o":["Memories of skills only.","Facts and events.","Feelings about the past."],"a":1,"why":"النوم العميق مهم خاصة للحقائق والأحداث، أي الذاكرة التصريحية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"In the word pairs experiment, the volunteers who stayed awake remembered more pairs than those who slept.","a":"F","why":"من ناموا تذكروا أزواجا أكثر بكثير في الصباح."},{"k":"mc","q":"What did the puzzle experiment suggest about REM sleep?","o":["It helps people notice a hidden shortcut.","It stops emotional memories.","It replaces the need for practice."],"a":0,"why":"بعد النوم كان الأفراد أكثر اكتشافا للحل المختصر الخفي."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the lecturer say about studying difficult material just before bedtime?","o":["It is proven to double memory.","It is dangerous for the brain.","The evidence is weaker, so it should not be the only strategy."],"a":2,"why":"وصف الدليل بأنه أضعف فلا ينبغي الاعتماد عليه وحده."},{"k":"gap","q":"A nap of about ___ minutes can improve alertness.","a":["twenty","20"],"why":"القيلولة القصيرة نحو عشرين دقيقة تحسّن اليقظة."}]},{"id":"l-c1-11","l":"C1","title":"Cities and climate adaptation","part":4,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Hello, everyone. In earlier lectures we spent a lot of time on mitigation, which means reducing the emissions that cause climate change."},{"s":"A","t":"Today we turn to adaptation, which means preparing cities for the changes that are already under way."},{"s":"A","t":"The distinction matters, because the two require different decisions and often compete for the same budget."},{"s":"A","t":"Cities are particularly exposed for three reasons. First, they concentrate people and property, so one flood can affect hundreds of thousands of residents."},{"s":"A","t":"Second, they are built of materials such as concrete and asphalt that absorb heat, which creates the urban heat island effect."},{"s":"A","t":"On a summer night a dense city centre can be several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, many cities sit on rivers or coasts, because that is where trade began centuries ago."},{"s":"A","t":"Let us look at heat first. The most effective measures are surprisingly modest."},{"s":"A","t":"Planting trees along streets reduces surface temperature, and paler roofs reflect sunlight instead of absorbing it."},{"s":"A","t":"Some cities have opened cooling centres, public buildings with air conditioning, for people who cannot cool their homes."},{"s":"A","t":"The older population, and those living alone, are the most at risk."},{"s":"A","t":"The success of such a scheme depends on whether officials know who these people are and can reach them. A cooling centre nobody visits protects nobody."},{"s":"A","t":"Flooding is a different challenge. The traditional solution was to build bigger pipes and higher walls, an approach called grey infrastructure."},{"s":"A","t":"It works up to a point, but each wall has a limit, and when it is exceeded the damage can be severe."},{"s":"A","t":"Many planners now favour the sponge city idea. Instead of moving water away as fast as possible, the city is designed to hold it."},{"s":"A","t":"That means parks that can safely flood, pavements that let rain soak through, and roofs covered with plants."},{"s":"A","t":"These green solutions are often cheaper to maintain and offer other benefits, such as shade and wildlife."},{"s":"A","t":"However, they need space, and space in a crowded centre is expensive."},{"s":"A","t":"This leads to the hardest question, which is who pays and who benefits."},{"s":"A","t":"Studies from several cities show that wealthy districts tend to receive protection first, partly because they have more political influence and better data about their own needs."},{"s":"A","t":"Poorer districts often have worse drainage and fewer trees. So adaptation can widen inequality unless funding rules are explicit."},{"s":"A","t":"One approach is to rank projects by the number of vulnerable people protected per pound spent, rather than by the value of the property saved."},{"s":"A","t":"There is also the question of timing. Infrastructure lasts fifty years or more, so a decision made today must work in a climate we can only estimate."},{"s":"A","t":"Engineers therefore speak of flexible design: building a wall that can be raised later, instead of building the highest wall at once."},{"s":"A","t":"It costs slightly more at the start, but it avoids paying for protection that may never be needed."},{"s":"A","t":"To conclude, successful adaptation combines physical measures with social planning."},{"s":"A","t":"Cities that do best identify their most vulnerable residents, mix green and grey solutions, and review their plans regularly."},{"s":"A","t":"Your task this week is to choose a city you know and find out one measure it has taken against either heat or flooding."}],"gloss":[{"w":"mitigation","ar":"التخفيف من الانبعاثات"},{"w":"adaptation","ar":"التكيّف"},{"w":"urban heat island","ar":"الجزيرة الحرارية الحضرية"},{"w":"drainage","ar":"تصريف المياه"},{"w":"vulnerable","ar":"هش، معرّض للخطر"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"How does the lecturer define adaptation?","o":["Cutting the emissions that cause climate change.","Preparing cities for changes already happening.","Moving residents away from coastal areas."],"a":1,"why":"التكيف يعني تهيئة المدن للتغيرات الجارية، أما التخفيف فخفض الانبعاثات."},{"k":"gap","q":"Dense city centres create the urban heat ___ effect.","a":["island"],"why":"ذكر تأثير الجزيرة الحرارية الحضرية بسبب الإسمنت والإسفلت."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does the success of a cooling centre depend on?","o":["Reaching the people who are most at risk.","Having the newest air conditioning.","Opening only on summer nights."],"a":0,"why":"ينجح المركز إن عرف المسؤولون الفئات المعرضة للخطر ووصلوا إليها."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the limitation of grey infrastructure?","o":["It cannot be built near rivers.","It is always cheaper than green solutions.","Damage can be severe once a wall's limit is exceeded."],"a":2,"why":"لكل جدار حدّ، وإذا تجاوزه الماء قد يكون الضرر كبيرا."},{"k":"tfng","q":"The lecturer says green flood solutions are usually more expensive to maintain than grey ones.","a":"F","why":"قال إنها غالبا أرخص صيانة وتقدم فوائد أخرى كالظل، لكنها تحتاج مساحة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do wealthy districts often receive protection first?","o":["They pay higher taxes only.","They have more influence and better data.","They are always closer to rivers."],"a":1,"why":"لهم نفوذ سياسي أكبر وبيانات أفضل عن احتياجاتهم."},{"k":"mc","q":"How could funding rules make adaptation fairer?","o":["By ranking projects by vulnerable people protected per pound.","By choosing projects according to property value.","By giving each district equal money."],"a":0,"why":"اقترح ترتيب المشاريع بعدد الضعفاء المحميين لكل جنيه لا بقيمة العقارات."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the advantage of flexible design?","o":["The highest wall is built first.","Protection can be increased later if needed.","Maintenance is no longer necessary."],"a":1,"why":"يمكن رفع الجدار لاحقا فلا يُدفع ثمن حماية قد لا تلزم."}]},{"id":"l-c1-12","l":"C1","title":"The history of evidence","part":4,"accent":"AU","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Every legal system must answer one question: how can a court discover what really happened?"},{"s":"A","t":"Today we trace how the answer has changed, from oaths to forensic science."},{"s":"A","t":"In many early societies, a court relied less on what had happened than on who was speaking."},{"s":"A","t":"An accused person might swear an oath, and bring neighbours to swear that his oath was trustworthy."},{"s":"A","t":"These oath helpers had not seen the event; they vouched for his character. The logic was social: a person with many respectable supporters was assumed to be honest."},{"s":"A","t":"Over time, this approach was replaced by testimony. By the later middle ages, courts in many places had begun to question witnesses directly."},{"s":"A","t":"The idea of two eyewitnesses as a standard of proof appeared in several legal traditions. It seems a sensible rule, yet it had a weakness."},{"s":"A","t":"It made it almost impossible to convict anyone when a crime occurred in private, and it gave great power to anyone willing to lie, since two false witnesses were enough."},{"s":"A","t":"The next major change was the rise of documents. As trade expanded, written contracts, receipts and records became the best proof of what people had agreed."},{"s":"A","t":"Courts began to ask for the original paper and to check seals and signatures."},{"s":"A","t":"This created a new problem, forgery, and so the first experts in handwriting appeared in courtrooms."},{"s":"A","t":"In the nineteenth century, science entered the courtroom. Early forensic methods included the comparison of fingerprints and chemical tests for poison."},{"s":"A","t":"Fingerprints were attractive because no two people were thought to have the same pattern."},{"s":"A","t":"A print left at a scene was physical evidence that could not be bribed or frightened. Courts quickly came to value such material."},{"s":"A","t":"Indeed, some lawyers began to complain that jurors were too impressed by anything described as scientific."},{"s":"A","t":"That concern proved to be justified."},{"s":"A","t":"In the twentieth century, a number of forensic techniques, such as hair comparison and bite mark analysis, were accepted for decades before anyone tested how reliable they were."},{"s":"A","t":"When researchers finally measured error rates, several methods performed much worse than expected. Reviews by scientific bodies called for stricter validation."},{"s":"A","t":"Today many courts apply tests before admitting an expert opinion: has the method been tested, has it been reviewed by other scientists, and is its error rate known?"},{"s":"A","t":"The most recent revolution is genetic profiling, which began in the nineteen eighties."},{"s":"A","t":"It was the first method that could both identify a guilty person with extraordinary precision and prove the innocence of someone already in prison."},{"s":"A","t":"Hundreds of wrongful convictions around the world have been corrected as a result. But genetic evidence has also taught a lesson: even excellent evidence can be misunderstood."},{"s":"A","t":"A match statistic, explained badly, can mislead a jury as much as a weak fingerprint."},{"s":"A","t":"So the history of evidence is not simply a story of progress from superstition to science."},{"s":"A","t":"It is a story of changing trust: first in reputation, then in witnesses, then in documents, and now in laboratories. Each stage solved one problem and created another."},{"s":"A","t":"For next week, read the short article on the reading list about admissibility, and prepare two questions."}],"gloss":[{"w":"oath","ar":"يمين"},{"w":"vouch for","ar":"يزكّي، يشهد بصدق"},{"w":"forgery","ar":"تزوير"},{"w":"forensic","ar":"جنائي، علم الأدلة الجنائية"},{"w":"admissibility","ar":"مقبولية الدليل"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What was the role of oath helpers in early courts?","o":["They had watched the event.","They spoke for the character of the accused.","They examined documents."],"a":1,"why":"لم يشهدوا الواقعة بل زكّوا شخصية المتهم أمام المحكمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What was a weakness of the rule requiring two eyewitnesses?","o":["It could not be applied to written contracts.","It was too expensive for courts.","Private crimes were nearly impossible to prove."],"a":2,"why":"من الصعب إدانة أحد في جريمة وقعت سرا، ويكفي شاهدا زور."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why did handwriting experts appear in courtrooms?","o":["To deal with forged documents.","To teach judges to read.","To replace the use of seals."],"a":0,"why":"ازدياد الوثائق خلق مشكلة التزوير فظهر خبراء الخط."},{"k":"gap","q":"Fingerprints were valued as physical evidence that could not be ___ or frightened.","a":["bribed"],"why":"ذكر أن البصمة دليل مادي لا يمكن رشوته أو تخويفه."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Hair comparison was tested for reliability before courts began to accept it.","a":"F","why":"قُبلت هذه التقنيات عقودا قبل أن يختبر أحد مدى موثوقيتها."},{"k":"gap","q":"One question courts now ask is whether the method's ___ rate is known.","a":["error"],"why":"من أسئلة القبول: هل اختُبرت الطريقة وهل روجعت وهل معدل خطئها معروف."},{"k":"mc","q":"What made genetic profiling special?","o":["It was cheaper than fingerprints.","It could both identify offenders and clear prisoners.","It needed no expert to explain it."],"a":1,"why":"حدد المذنب بدقة وأثبت براءة سجناء، فصُحّحت مئات الإدانات الخاطئة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the lecturer's overall view of the history of evidence?","o":["A simple story of progress to science.","A story of decline in honesty.","A story of changing trust, each stage creating new problems."],"a":2,"why":"قال إنها ليست تقدما بسيطا بل تغيّر في موضع الثقة، وكل مرحلة حلّت مشكلة وخلقت أخرى."}]},{"id":"l-c1-13","l":"C1","title":"How language models produce text","part":4,"accent":"GB","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good afternoon. Language models now draft emails, summarise reports and answer questions, and people often describe them as if they understood language the way we do."},{"s":"A","t":"This lecture explains, in simple terms, what these systems actually do when they produce a sentence."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me start with the basic task. A language model is trained to predict the next piece of text."},{"s":"A","t":"Given the words, the cat sat on the, it estimates which word is most likely to come next. Mat is likely; refrigerator is much less so."},{"s":"A","t":"That is the entire training goal. Everything else, including the ability to answer questions, emerges from doing this prediction extremely well over enormous quantities of text."},{"s":"A","t":"The unit is not exactly a word. The text is first cut into tokens, which are short pieces, sometimes whole words, sometimes parts of a word."},{"s":"A","t":"A rare name might be split into three or four tokens, while a common word is usually a single one."},{"s":"A","t":"This explains why models sometimes make odd mistakes with spelling or counting letters: they never see the letters directly."},{"s":"A","t":"Next, how does the model decide? Inside it is a very large network of numbers, called parameters, which were adjusted during training."},{"s":"A","t":"Modern systems use a design called the transformer, whose key component is attention."},{"s":"A","t":"Attention allows the model, at each step, to look back at the earlier tokens and decide which ones matter most for the next prediction."},{"s":"A","t":"In the sentence, the lawyer told the client that she would win, attention helps the model work out who she refers to, based on patterns seen in training."},{"s":"A","t":"The output of the network is not one word but a probability for every possible token. Then comes a choice."},{"s":"A","t":"If the system always chose the most probable token, its text would be dull and repetitive. So a setting called temperature controls the randomness."},{"s":"A","t":"At a low temperature the model plays safe. At a high temperature it takes more risks, which can make the writing more creative but also less accurate."},{"s":"A","t":"This is why asking the same question twice can produce two different answers."},{"s":"A","t":"Training has two main stages. In the first, called pre-training, the model reads vast amounts of text and learns general patterns."},{"s":"A","t":"In the second, the model is refined using examples of good answers and feedback from human reviewers, who rate which responses are more helpful and honest."},{"s":"A","t":"This second stage is why a modern assistant behaves like a polite helper rather than simply continuing a sentence."},{"s":"A","t":"Now, an important limitation. Because the model is built to produce plausible text, it can generate statements that sound confident but are false. These are sometimes called hallucinations."},{"s":"A","t":"The model has no built-in way of checking facts against the world; it knows only patterns."},{"s":"A","t":"Systems connected to a search tool or a trusted database make fewer mistakes, but nothing removes the risk completely."},{"s":"A","t":"For this reason, anyone using a model for legal or academic work should verify every claim and every reference, especially names, dates and citations."},{"s":"A","t":"To sum up: a language model predicts text, one token at a time, using attention to consider the context, and a degree of randomness to avoid repetition."},{"s":"A","t":"It is a powerful tool for drafting and explaining, but it is not a source of truth."},{"s":"A","t":"In the seminar, bring one example of an answer from a model that you checked, and tell us what you found."}],"gloss":[{"w":"token","ar":"وحدة نصية"},{"w":"parameter","ar":"معامل في النموذج"},{"w":"attention","ar":"آلية الانتباه"},{"w":"plausible","ar":"معقول، مقنع"},{"w":"hallucination","ar":"هلوسة، معلومة مختلقة"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the basic training goal of a language model?","o":["To store facts in a database.","To predict the next piece of text.","To translate between languages."],"a":1,"why":"هدف التدريب كله هو التنبؤ بالجزء التالي من النص."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do models sometimes make mistakes when counting letters?","o":["They are trained only on short texts.","They work with tokens rather than individual letters.","They ignore rare words."],"a":1,"why":"النص يُقطّع إلى وحدات، فلا يرى النموذج الحروف مباشرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What does attention allow the model to do?","o":["Choose a random word.","Correct its own spelling.","Look back at earlier tokens to see which matter most."],"a":2,"why":"تتيح له مراجعة الرموز السابقة وتحديد الأهم للتنبؤ التالي."},{"k":"gap","q":"A setting that controls how random the output is is called ___.","a":["temperature"],"why":"الإعداد المسمى درجة الحرارة يتحكم في العشوائية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"A model always gives exactly the same answer when the same question is asked twice.","a":"F","why":"بسبب العشوائية قد يعطي السؤال نفسه جوابين مختلفين."},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the purpose of the second stage of training?","o":["To make the model behave like a helpful assistant using human feedback.","To add more text from the internet.","To remove all parameters."],"a":0,"why":"يُصقل النموذج بأمثلة جيدة وتقييم بشري فيتصرف كمساعد مهذب."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why can a model produce false statements that sound confident?","o":["It knows only patterns and cannot check facts itself.","It always copies from one source.","It refuses to use a search tool."],"a":0,"why":"يُنتج نصا معقولا لا حقيقة مؤكدة، وليس له وسيلة داخلية لفحص الوقائع."},{"k":"gap","q":"Users should check names, dates and ___ especially.","a":["citations"],"why":"أوصى بالتحقق من كل ادعاء ومرجع، خصوصا الأسماء والتواريخ والاستشهادات."}]},{"id":"l-c1-14","l":"C1","title":"The economics of innovation","part":4,"accent":"US","lines":[{"s":"A","t":"Good morning. Why do some economies grow steadily richer while others stagnate?"},{"s":"A","t":"Economists have long believed that the answer is largely innovation, meaning new ideas turned into products and methods."},{"s":"A","t":"Today I want to explain the economic logic of innovation, and why it is more puzzling than it first appears."},{"s":"A","t":"Let me begin with a feature that makes ideas different from most goods. If I eat a sandwich, you cannot eat it."},{"s":"A","t":"But if I use an idea, you can use it too, and the idea is not used up. Economists call this non-rivalry."},{"s":"A","t":"The result is that ideas are enormously valuable to society, since one discovery can benefit millions of people."},{"s":"A","t":"Yet they are hard for the creator to profit from, because others can copy them."},{"s":"A","t":"This creates the central problem of innovation policy."},{"s":"A","t":"If copying is easy, firms may not invest in research, since a competitor could imitate the product without paying the cost of developing it."},{"s":"A","t":"The traditional solution is the patent, which gives an inventor the exclusive right to use an invention for a fixed period, usually around twenty years."},{"s":"A","t":"In exchange, the inventor must publish how it works. Patents therefore reward invention while still spreading knowledge."},{"s":"A","t":"But patents are a compromise, not a perfect answer. During the period of protection, the owner can charge higher prices, so fewer people can afford the product."},{"s":"A","t":"Patents can also be used defensively, to block competitors rather than to protect real inventions."},{"s":"A","t":"Some economists argue that in fast-moving industries, such as software, patents slow progress, because every new product may infringe someone's existing patent."},{"s":"A","t":"There is a second challenge, which is risk. Most research projects fail."},{"s":"A","t":"A pharmaceutical company may test thousands of compounds to find one useful medicine, which can take over ten years."},{"s":"A","t":"Private investors are often reluctant to fund such uncertain work, particularly in the early stages. That is one reason why governments pay for basic research in universities."},{"s":"A","t":"The internet, satellite navigation and many medicines were first developed with public funding, although private firms later turned them into commercial products."},{"s":"A","t":"Third, there is the question of who actually innovates. It is tempting to imagine that large firms do most of it, since they have the money."},{"s":"A","t":"Evidence suggests a more mixed picture. Large firms are good at improving existing products step by step."},{"s":"A","t":"Newcomers, by contrast, are more likely to introduce radical changes, since they have nothing to lose from destroying the old way of doing things."},{"s":"A","t":"One famous economist called this creative destruction: new businesses replace old ones, causing losses and gains at the same time."},{"s":"A","t":"Finally, innovation affects people unevenly. A new technology may create skilled jobs while eliminating routine ones."},{"s":"A","t":"Overall employment may recover, but the individuals who lose work are not always the ones who find the new jobs."},{"s":"A","t":"This is why policies for retraining are considered an essential partner of innovation policy."},{"s":"A","t":"To summarise, innovation is valuable because ideas can be shared, difficult to finance because of risk and copying, and uneven in its effects on people."},{"s":"A","t":"Good policy supports research, protects inventors fairly, and helps workers to adapt."},{"s":"A","t":"For next week, find one recent invention and decide whether a patent was important to its success."}],"gloss":[{"w":"non-rivalry","ar":"عدم التنافسية في الاستعمال"},{"w":"patent","ar":"براءة اختراع"},{"w":"infringe","ar":"ينتهك حقا"},{"w":"radical","ar":"جذري"},{"w":"creative destruction","ar":"التدمير الخلّاق"}],"qs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What does non-rivalry mean for ideas?","o":["Using an idea does not prevent others from using it.","Ideas can only be owned by firms.","Ideas lose value when they are shared."],"a":0,"why":"استعمال فكرة لا يستنفدها، فيمكن للآخرين استعمالها أيضا."},{"k":"gap","q":"A patent usually protects an invention for about ___ years.","a":["twenty","20"],"why":"ذكر أن مدة الحماية المعتادة نحو عشرين عاما."},{"k":"mc","q":"What can happen when patents are used defensively?","o":["They reduce the price of products.","They block competitors rather than protect real inventions.","They force firms to share secrets."],"a":1,"why":"قد تُستعمل لمنع المنافسين لا لحماية اختراعات حقيقية."},{"k":"tfng","q":"Some economists think patents may slow progress in industries such as software.","a":"T","why":"قالوا إن كل منتج جديد قد ينتهك براءة قائمة فيتباطأ التقدم."},{"k":"mc","q":"Why do governments often fund basic research?","o":["Firms refuse to hire scientists.","Private investors are reluctant to back very uncertain projects.","Universities cannot publish otherwise."],"a":1,"why":"المشاريع البحثية محفوفة بالمخاطر ولا يتحمس المستثمرون الخواص لتمويلها مبكرا."},{"k":"gap","q":"Developing a new medicine can take more than ___ years.","a":["ten","10"],"why":"ذكر أن تطوير دواء قد يستغرق أكثر من عشر سنوات."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which firms are more likely to introduce radical changes?","o":["Large established firms.","Government laboratories.","Newcomers with little to lose."],"a":2,"why":"الشركات الجديدة ليس لديها ما تخسره من هدم الطريقة القديمة."},{"k":"mc","q":"What problem does the lecturer identify about job changes caused by innovation?","o":["Workers who lose jobs are not always those who get new ones.","New technology never creates jobs.","Employment falls permanently."],"a":0,"why":"قد يتعافى التشغيل عموما لكن المتضررين ليسوا دائما من يجد الوظائف الجديدة."}]}]);
Fasih.add("shadow",[{"id":"sh-a2-01","l":"A2","title":"At the airport","titleAr":"في المطار","lines":[{"en":"Could you tell me where the check-in desk is?","ar":"هل يمكنك أن تخبرني أين مكتب تسجيل الوصول؟"},{"en":"I would like a window seat, please.","ar":"أودّ مقعدًا بجانب النافذة، من فضلك."},{"en":"I have one suitcase and one small bag.","ar":"لدي حقيبة سفر واحدة وحقيبة صغيرة."},{"en":"Is my flight to Madrid on time?","ar":"هل رحلتي إلى مدريد في موعدها؟"},{"en":"Which gate should I go to?","ar":"إلى أي بوابة يجب أن أذهب؟"},{"en":"Boarding starts at half past six, doesn't it?","ar":"يبدأ الصعود إلى الطائرة في السادسة والنصف، أليس كذلك؟"},{"en":"Excuse me, where can I get a bottle of water?","ar":"عذرًا، من أين يمكنني شراء زجاجة ماء؟"},{"en":"I'm sorry, I think I lost my boarding pass.","ar":"آسف، أظن أنني أضعت بطاقة الصعود."},{"en":"Here is my passport and my ticket.","ar":"هذا جواز سفري وهذه تذكرتي."}]},{"id":"sh-a2-02","l":"A2","title":"In a taxi","titleAr":"في سيارة الأجرة","lines":[{"en":"Hello, could you take me to the train station?","ar":"مرحبًا، هل يمكنك أن توصلني إلى محطة القطار؟"},{"en":"How long will it take to get there?","ar":"كم من الوقت سيستغرق الوصول إلى هناك؟"},{"en":"Could you turn on the air conditioning, please?","ar":"هل يمكنك تشغيل المكيّف، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"I'm in a hurry, so please go the fastest way.","ar":"أنا مستعجل، لذا اسلك أسرع طريق من فضلك."},{"en":"Please stop next to the blue building.","ar":"من فضلك توقف بجانب المبنى الأزرق."},{"en":"How much is the fare to the airport?","ar":"كم أجرة الذهاب إلى المطار؟"},{"en":"Can you wait here for five minutes?","ar":"هل يمكنك الانتظار هنا خمس دقائق؟"},{"en":"Keep the change. Thank you very much.","ar":"احتفظ بالباقي. شكرًا جزيلًا."}]},{"id":"sh-a2-03","l":"A2","title":"At a hotel reception","titleAr":"في استقبال الفندق","lines":[{"en":"Hello, I have a reservation under the name Karim.","ar":"مرحبًا، لدي حجز باسم كريم."},{"en":"I'd like a quiet room for two nights.","ar":"أريد غرفة هادئة لمدة ليلتين."},{"en":"Does the price include breakfast?","ar":"هل يشمل السعر وجبة الفطور؟"},{"en":"What time is breakfast served in the morning?","ar":"في أي ساعة يُقدَّم الفطور صباحًا؟"},{"en":"Could you give me the Wi-Fi password, please?","ar":"هل يمكنك إعطائي كلمة مرور الواي فاي، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"The shower in my room isn't working properly.","ar":"الدش في غرفتي لا يعمل جيدًا."},{"en":"Could I have an extra towel and a pillow?","ar":"هل يمكنني الحصول على منشفة ووسادة إضافيتين؟"},{"en":"What time do I have to check out tomorrow?","ar":"في أي ساعة يجب أن أغادر الفندق غدًا؟"},{"en":"Can you call a taxi for me at seven?","ar":"هل يمكنك أن تطلب لي سيارة أجرة في السابعة؟"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-04","l":"A2","title":"Ordering food in a café","titleAr":"طلب الطعام في مقهى","lines":[{"en":"Hi, could I see the menu, please?","ar":"مرحبًا، هل يمكنني رؤية قائمة الطعام، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"I'll have a cheese sandwich and an orange juice.","ar":"سآخذ شطيرة جبن وعصير برتقال."},{"en":"Is the soup of the day vegetarian?","ar":"هل حساء اليوم نباتي؟"},{"en":"Could I have my tea with a little milk?","ar":"هل يمكنني الحصول على الشاي مع قليل من الحليب؟"},{"en":"What would you recommend for dessert?","ar":"ماذا تنصحني أن آخذ كحلوى؟"},{"en":"Excuse me, we ordered ten minutes ago.","ar":"عذرًا، لقد طلبنا قبل عشر دقائق."},{"en":"Can we have the bill, please?","ar":"هل يمكننا الحصول على الفاتورة، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"Could I pay by card, or only cash?","ar":"هل يمكنني الدفع بالبطاقة أم نقدًا فقط؟"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-05","l":"A2","title":"Shopping for clothes","titleAr":"شراء الملابس","lines":[{"en":"Excuse me, do you have this shirt in a larger size?","ar":"عذرًا، هل لديكم هذا القميص بمقاس أكبر؟"},{"en":"Where can I try these trousers on?","ar":"أين يمكنني تجربة هذا البنطال؟"},{"en":"It's a bit too tight around the shoulders.","ar":"إنه ضيق قليلًا عند الكتفين."},{"en":"Do you have it in a darker colour?","ar":"هل لديكم منه بلون أغمق؟"},{"en":"How much is this jacket after the discount?","ar":"كم سعر هذه السترة بعد التخفيض؟"},{"en":"That's a little expensive for me today.","ar":"هذا غالٍ قليلًا بالنسبة لي اليوم."},{"en":"I'll take the grey one, please.","ar":"سآخذ الرمادي، من فضلك."},{"en":"Can I return it if it doesn't fit?","ar":"هل يمكنني إرجاعه إن لم يكن مناسبًا؟"},{"en":"Do you have a receipt for the exchange?","ar":"هل لديك إيصال من أجل الاستبدال؟"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-06","l":"A2","title":"At the doctor's","titleAr":"عند الطبيب","lines":[{"en":"I've had a headache since yesterday morning.","ar":"أعاني من صداع منذ صباح أمس."},{"en":"I also have a sore throat and a cough.","ar":"وأعاني أيضًا من التهاب في الحلق وسعال."},{"en":"I don't have a fever, but I feel very tired.","ar":"ليست لدي حمى، لكنني أشعر بتعب شديد."},{"en":"I'm allergic to penicillin, so please be careful.","ar":"لدي حساسية من البنسلين، لذا انتبه من فضلك."},{"en":"How often should I take this medicine?","ar":"كم مرة يجب أن آخذ هذا الدواء؟"},{"en":"Should I take it before or after meals?","ar":"هل آخذه قبل الوجبات أم بعدها؟"},{"en":"Do I need to stay at home for a few days?","ar":"هل أحتاج إلى البقاء في البيت بضعة أيام؟"},{"en":"Can I have a medical certificate for my university?","ar":"هل يمكنني الحصول على شهادة طبية لجامعتي؟"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-07","l":"A2","title":"Meeting someone new","titleAr":"التعرّف على شخص جديد","lines":[{"en":"Hi, I don't think we've met. I'm Yassine.","ar":"مرحبًا، لا أظننا التقينا. أنا ياسين."},{"en":"Nice to meet you. Where are you from?","ar":"سررت بلقائك. من أين أنت؟"},{"en":"I'm from Algeria, but I live in Blida now.","ar":"أنا من الجزائر، لكنني أعيش في البليدة الآن."},{"en":"What do you do? Are you a student?","ar":"ماذا تعمل؟ هل أنت طالب؟"},{"en":"Yes, I'm studying medicine at the university.","ar":"نعم، أدرس الطب في الجامعة."},{"en":"How long have you lived in this city?","ar":"منذ متى تعيش في هذه المدينة؟"},{"en":"What do you like doing in your free time?","ar":"ماذا تحب أن تفعل في وقت فراغك؟"},{"en":"I really enjoy playing football with my friends.","ar":"أستمتع حقًا بلعب كرة القدم مع أصدقائي."},{"en":"It was great talking to you. See you soon!","ar":"كان من الرائع الحديث إليك. أراك قريبًا!"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-08","l":"A2","title":"Asking for help in the street","titleAr":"طلب المساعدة في الشارع","lines":[{"en":"Excuse me, could you help me, please?","ar":"عذرًا، هل يمكنك مساعدتي، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"I'm lost. Where is the nearest bus stop?","ar":"لقد تهت. أين أقرب محطة حافلات؟"},{"en":"Is the post office far from here?","ar":"هل مكتب البريد بعيد من هنا؟"},{"en":"Go straight and turn left at the traffic lights.","ar":"اذهب مباشرة ثم انعطف يسارًا عند إشارة المرور."},{"en":"Could you show me the way on my phone?","ar":"هل يمكنك أن تريني الطريق على هاتفي؟"},{"en":"Sorry, could you speak a little more slowly?","ar":"آسف، هل يمكنك التحدث ببطء أكثر قليلًا؟"},{"en":"Could you repeat that, please? I didn't understand.","ar":"هل يمكنك أن تعيد ذلك، من فضلك؟ لم أفهم."},{"en":"Thank you so much. You've been very kind.","ar":"شكرًا جزيلًا. لقد كنت لطيفًا جدًا."}]},{"id":"sh-a2-09","l":"A2","title":"On the phone","titleAr":"على الهاتف","lines":[{"en":"Hello, this is Sara speaking. Can I speak to Mr Amine?","ar":"مرحبًا، معك سارة. هل يمكنني التحدث إلى السيد أمين؟"},{"en":"I'm sorry, he's not here right now.","ar":"آسف، إنه غير موجود الآن."},{"en":"Could you ask him to call me back, please?","ar":"هل يمكنك أن تطلب منه الاتصال بي، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"My number is zero five five, one two three.","ar":"رقمي صفر خمسة خمسة، واحد اثنان ثلاثة."},{"en":"Sorry, the line is bad. Could you say that again?","ar":"آسف، الخط سيّئ. هل يمكنك إعادة ذلك؟"},{"en":"Can I leave a message for him?","ar":"هل يمكنني ترك رسالة له؟"},{"en":"I'm calling about the appointment on Thursday.","ar":"أتصل بخصوص الموعد يوم الخميس."},{"en":"Could you hold on a moment, please?","ar":"هل يمكنك الانتظار لحظة، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"Thanks for calling. Have a nice day!","ar":"شكرًا لاتصالك. أتمنى لك يومًا سعيدًا!"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-10","l":"A2","title":"At the university office","titleAr":"في مكتب الجامعة","lines":[{"en":"Good morning, I'd like to ask about my timetable.","ar":"صباح الخير، أودّ أن أسأل عن جدول حصصي."},{"en":"Where can I hand in this registration form?","ar":"أين يمكنني تسليم استمارة التسجيل هذه؟"},{"en":"Do I need to bring any other documents?","ar":"هل أحتاج إلى إحضار أي وثائق أخرى؟"},{"en":"When will the exam results be published?","ar":"متى ستُنشر نتائج الامتحانات؟"},{"en":"I missed the lecture because I was ill.","ar":"فاتتني المحاضرة لأنني كنت مريضًا."},{"en":"Is it possible to change my group?","ar":"هل من الممكن أن أغيّر فوجي؟"},{"en":"What time does the office close today?","ar":"في أي ساعة يُغلق المكتب اليوم؟"},{"en":"Could I get a certificate to prove I'm a student?","ar":"هل يمكنني الحصول على شهادة تثبت أنني طالب؟"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-11","l":"A2","title":"At the bank","titleAr":"في البنك","lines":[{"en":"I'd like to open a bank account, please.","ar":"أودّ فتح حساب بنكي، من فضلك."},{"en":"What documents do I need to bring with me?","ar":"ما الوثائق التي أحتاج إلى إحضارها معي؟"},{"en":"Is there a monthly fee for this account?","ar":"هل هناك رسوم شهرية لهذا الحساب؟"},{"en":"I want to withdraw two hundred euros, please.","ar":"أريد سحب مئتي يورو، من فضلك."},{"en":"Could you check my balance for me?","ar":"هل يمكنك التحقق من رصيدي من أجلي؟"},{"en":"My card doesn't work at the cash machine.","ar":"بطاقتي لا تعمل في جهاز الصراف الآلي."},{"en":"How long does an international transfer take?","ar":"كم يستغرق التحويل الدولي من الوقت؟"},{"en":"Can I make an appointment with an adviser?","ar":"هل يمكنني حجز موعد مع مستشار؟"}]},{"id":"sh-a2-12","l":"A2","title":"Talking about your day","titleAr":"الحديث عن يومك","lines":[{"en":"I woke up at six and had a quick breakfast.","ar":"استيقظت في السادسة وتناولت فطورًا سريعًا."},{"en":"Then I took the bus to the university.","ar":"ثم ركبت الحافلة إلى الجامعة."},{"en":"I had two lectures and a short meeting.","ar":"حضرت محاضرتين واجتماعًا قصيرًا."},{"en":"At lunchtime, I ate with my friends in the canteen.","ar":"في وقت الغداء أكلت مع أصدقائي في المطعم الجامعي."},{"en":"In the afternoon, I studied in the library for hours.","ar":"بعد الظهر درست في المكتبة لساعات."},{"en":"I was very tired, so I came home early.","ar":"كنت متعبًا جدًا، فعدت إلى البيت مبكرًا."},{"en":"After dinner, I called my parents and watched the news.","ar":"بعد العشاء اتصلت بوالديّ وشاهدت الأخبار."},{"en":"Tomorrow I have an exam, so I'll go to bed early.","ar":"غدًا لدي امتحان، لذا سأنام مبكرًا."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-01","l":"B1","title":"A job interview","titleAr":"مقابلة عمل","lines":[{"en":"Thank you for inviting me to this interview.","ar":"شكرًا لدعوتي إلى هذه المقابلة."},{"en":"I recently graduated, and I'm looking for my first professional position.","ar":"تخرجت حديثًا، وأبحث عن أول وظيفة مهنية لي."},{"en":"One of my main strengths is that I work well in a team.","ar":"من أهم نقاط قوتي أنني أعمل جيدًا ضمن فريق."},{"en":"In my last internship, I was responsible for organising the documents.","ar":"في تدريبي الأخير كنت مسؤولًا عن تنظيم الوثائق."},{"en":"I sometimes spend too long on details, but I'm working on it.","ar":"أحيانًا أقضي وقتًا طويلًا في التفاصيل، لكنني أعمل على ذلك."},{"en":"I'm particularly interested in this role because it involves research.","ar":"أنا مهتم بهذا المنصب خصوصًا لأنه يتضمن البحث."},{"en":"Could you tell me more about the training you offer?","ar":"هل يمكنك إخباري أكثر عن التدريب الذي تقدمونه؟"},{"en":"Where do you see this department in five years?","ar":"أين ترى هذا القسم بعد خمس سنوات؟"},{"en":"I would be very happy to join your team.","ar":"سأكون سعيدًا جدًا بالانضمام إلى فريقكم."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-02","l":"B1","title":"Giving your opinion politely","titleAr":"إبداء الرأي بأدب","lines":[{"en":"In my opinion, public services should be more transparent.","ar":"في رأيي، ينبغي أن تكون الخدمات العامة أكثر شفافية."},{"en":"As far as I'm concerned, education is the best investment.","ar":"بالنسبة لي، التعليم هو أفضل استثمار."},{"en":"I tend to think that online classes can be very useful.","ar":"أميل إلى الاعتقاد بأن الدروس عبر الإنترنت قد تكون مفيدة جدًا."},{"en":"I see what you mean, but I look at it differently.","ar":"أفهم ما تقصده، لكنني أنظر إلى الأمر بشكل مختلف."},{"en":"That's an interesting point, although I'm not completely sure about it.","ar":"هذه نقطة مثيرة للاهتمام، رغم أنني لست متأكدًا منها تمامًا."},{"en":"Perhaps we could look at the problem from another angle.","ar":"ربما يمكننا النظر إلى المشكلة من زاوية أخرى."},{"en":"I may be wrong, but I think the figures are too low.","ar":"قد أكون مخطئًا، لكنني أعتقد أن الأرقام منخفضة جدًا."},{"en":"Would you mind if I added something to that?","ar":"هل تمانع إن أضفت شيئًا إلى ذلك؟"}]},{"id":"sh-b1-03","l":"B1","title":"Explaining a problem to customer service","titleAr":"شرح مشكلة لخدمة العملاء","lines":[{"en":"I'm calling because my order hasn't arrived yet.","ar":"أتصل لأن طلبي لم يصل بعد."},{"en":"I placed it two weeks ago, and I still haven't received anything.","ar":"قدّمته منذ أسبوعين، ولم أستلم شيئًا حتى الآن."},{"en":"The tracking page says it was delivered, but it wasn't.","ar":"تقول صفحة التتبع إنه سُلّم، لكن ذلك لم يحدث."},{"en":"I've already sent two emails, but nobody replied.","ar":"لقد أرسلت بريدين إلكترونيين بالفعل، لكن لم يردّ أحد."},{"en":"Could you check what happened to my parcel, please?","ar":"هل يمكنك أن تتحقق مما حدث لطردي، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"I would prefer a refund rather than a replacement.","ar":"أفضّل استرداد المبلغ على الاستبدال."},{"en":"How long will it take for the money to reach my account?","ar":"كم سيستغرق وصول المال إلى حسابي؟"},{"en":"Could you confirm the details in writing, please?","ar":"هل يمكنك تأكيد التفاصيل كتابةً، من فضلك؟"},{"en":"I'd like to speak to a manager if this isn't solved.","ar":"أودّ التحدث إلى المدير إن لم تُحلّ المشكلة."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-04","l":"B1","title":"Describing your studies","titleAr":"وصف دراستك","lines":[{"en":"I'm doing a master's degree in transparency and anti-corruption.","ar":"أدرس الماجستير في الشفافية ومكافحة الفساد."},{"en":"The course covers law, public administration and research methods.","ar":"تغطي الدراسة القانون والإدارة العامة ومناهج البحث."},{"en":"I chose this field because I believe in fair public institutions.","ar":"اخترت هذا المجال لأنني أؤمن بمؤسسات عامة عادلة."},{"en":"This semester, I'm writing a paper on public procurement.","ar":"في هذا الفصل أكتب بحثًا عن الصفقات العمومية."},{"en":"What I enjoy most is analysing real cases.","ar":"أكثر ما أستمتع به هو تحليل الحالات الواقعية."},{"en":"The hardest part is reading long articles in English.","ar":"أصعب جزء هو قراءة المقالات الطويلة بالإنجليزية."},{"en":"I have to hand in my dissertation by next June.","ar":"يجب أن أسلّم مذكرتي بحلول يونيو القادم."},{"en":"After my master's, I'd like to do a PhD abroad.","ar":"بعد الماجستير أودّ إجراء دكتوراه في الخارج."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-05","l":"B1","title":"Small talk at a conference","titleAr":"حديث عابر في مؤتمر","lines":[{"en":"Hello, is this your first time at this conference?","ar":"مرحبًا، هل هذه أول مرة لك في هذا المؤتمر؟"},{"en":"Yes, it is. I came here to learn from other researchers.","ar":"نعم، إنها كذلك. جئت لأتعلم من باحثين آخرين."},{"en":"What did you think of the opening speech this morning?","ar":"ما رأيك في الكلمة الافتتاحية هذا الصباح؟"},{"en":"I found it inspiring, especially the part about open data.","ar":"وجدتها ملهمة، خاصة الجزء المتعلق بالبيانات المفتوحة."},{"en":"Which sessions are you planning to attend this afternoon?","ar":"أي جلسات تخطط لحضورها بعد الظهر؟"},{"en":"Shall we grab a coffee before the next talk starts?","ar":"هل نتناول قهوة قبل أن يبدأ العرض التالي؟"},{"en":"I'd love to hear more about your research.","ar":"يسعدني أن أسمع المزيد عن بحثك."},{"en":"Here is my card. Let's keep in touch by email.","ar":"هذه بطاقتي. لنبقَ على تواصل عبر البريد الإلكتروني."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-06","l":"B1","title":"Asking questions after a lecture","titleAr":"طرح أسئلة بعد محاضرة","lines":[{"en":"Thank you for a very clear and interesting lecture.","ar":"شكرًا على محاضرة واضحة ومثيرة جدًا."},{"en":"Could I ask a question about the second part of your talk?","ar":"هل يمكنني طرح سؤال عن الجزء الثاني من محاضرتك؟"},{"en":"I didn't quite understand how you collected your data.","ar":"لم أفهم تمامًا كيف جمعتَ بياناتك."},{"en":"Could you explain that point in a little more detail?","ar":"هل يمكنك شرح تلك النقطة بتفصيل أكثر قليلًا؟"},{"en":"How reliable are the results, given the small sample?","ar":"ما مدى موثوقية النتائج، مع صغر العيّنة؟"},{"en":"Do you think this method could work in other countries?","ar":"هل تعتقد أن هذه الطريقة قد تنجح في دول أخرى؟"},{"en":"Would you recommend any books or articles on this topic?","ar":"هل توصي بكتب أو مقالات في هذا الموضوع؟"},{"en":"Thank you. That answers my question very well.","ar":"شكرًا. هذا يجيب عن سؤالي جيدًا."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-07","l":"B1","title":"Introducing a project idea","titleAr":"تقديم فكرة مشروع","lines":[{"en":"I'd like to present a short idea for a research project.","ar":"أودّ تقديم فكرة موجزة لمشروع بحثي."},{"en":"The project aims to find out why some reports are ignored.","ar":"يهدف المشروع إلى معرفة لماذا تُهمَل بعض التقارير."},{"en":"To begin with, we would collect public reports from several cities.","ar":"في البداية سنجمع تقارير عامة من عدة مدن."},{"en":"Then we would use software to compare them and find patterns.","ar":"ثم نستخدم برنامجًا لمقارنتها واكتشاف الأنماط."},{"en":"The main benefit is that it would save a lot of time.","ar":"الفائدة الرئيسية أنه سيوفر الكثير من الوقت."},{"en":"One possible difficulty is getting access to enough data.","ar":"من الصعوبات المحتملة الحصول على بيانات كافية."},{"en":"We expect to finish the first stage within six months.","ar":"نتوقع إنهاء المرحلة الأولى خلال ستة أشهر."},{"en":"I would really appreciate your feedback on this idea.","ar":"سأقدّر كثيرًا ملاحظاتكم على هذه الفكرة."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-08","l":"B1","title":"Agreeing and disagreeing in a meeting","titleAr":"الموافقة والاعتراض في اجتماع","lines":[{"en":"I completely agree with what Nadia just said.","ar":"أوافق تمامًا على ما قالته نادية للتو."},{"en":"That's exactly what I was thinking.","ar":"هذا بالضبط ما كنت أفكر فيه."},{"en":"I'm afraid I see it differently, because the budget is limited.","ar":"للأسف أرى الأمر بشكل مختلف، لأن الميزانية محدودة."},{"en":"I take your point, but it might be too risky.","ar":"أتفهّم وجهة نظرك، لكن قد يكون ذلك محفوفًا بالمخاطر."},{"en":"I'm not sure that would work in practice.","ar":"لست متأكدًا أن ذلك سينجح عمليًا."},{"en":"Could we compromise and test it on a small scale first?","ar":"هل يمكننا التوصل إلى حل وسط وتجربته على نطاق صغير أولًا؟"},{"en":"Let's vote on it so that everyone can have a say.","ar":"لنصوّت عليه كي يتمكن الجميع من إبداء رأيهم."},{"en":"So we all agree to meet again on Monday, don't we?","ar":"إذن نتفق جميعًا على اللقاء مجددًا يوم الإثنين، أليس كذلك؟"}]},{"id":"sh-b1-09","l":"B1","title":"Talking to a supervisor","titleAr":"التحدث إلى المشرف","lines":[{"en":"Thank you for seeing me. Do you have a few minutes?","ar":"شكرًا لأنك استقبلتني. هل لديك بضع دقائق؟"},{"en":"I've finished the first chapter, and I'd like your comments.","ar":"أنهيت الفصل الأول، وأودّ ملاحظاتك."},{"en":"I'm having trouble finding enough reliable sources.","ar":"أواجه صعوبة في العثور على مصادر موثوقة كافية."},{"en":"Do you think I should narrow down my research question?","ar":"هل تعتقد أنه ينبغي لي تضييق سؤال بحثي؟"},{"en":"I'm afraid I might not meet the deadline.","ar":"أخشى ألا ألتزم بالموعد النهائي."},{"en":"Would it be possible to have an extra week?","ar":"هل من الممكن الحصول على أسبوع إضافي؟"},{"en":"I'll send you the revised version by Friday.","ar":"سأرسل لك النسخة المعدّلة بحلول يوم الجمعة."},{"en":"Thank you for your advice. It was really helpful.","ar":"شكرًا على نصيحتك. كانت مفيدة حقًا."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-10","l":"B1","title":"A visa interview","titleAr":"مقابلة التأشيرة","lines":[{"en":"Good morning. I'm applying for a student visa.","ar":"صباح الخير. أتقدم بطلب تأشيرة طالب."},{"en":"I've been accepted onto a doctoral programme at a university.","ar":"قُبلت في برنامج دكتوراه في إحدى الجامعات."},{"en":"I'm going to study there for three years.","ar":"سأدرس هناك لمدة ثلاث سنوات."},{"en":"My scholarship will cover my tuition fees and living costs.","ar":"ستغطي منحتي الدراسية الرسوم ونفقات المعيشة."},{"en":"I have all the documents here, including my bank statements.","ar":"لدي جميع الوثائق هنا، بما فيها كشوف حساباتي البنكية."},{"en":"I plan to return to my country after I finish my studies.","ar":"أخطط للعودة إلى بلدي بعد إنهاء دراستي."},{"en":"My family lives in Algeria, and I'll visit them every summer.","ar":"تعيش عائلتي في الجزائر، وسأزورها كل صيف."},{"en":"How long will it take to process my application?","ar":"كم سيستغرق معالجة طلبي؟"}]},{"id":"sh-b1-11","l":"B1","title":"Describing a process","titleAr":"وصف عملية","lines":[{"en":"First of all, you need to create an online account.","ar":"أولًا، تحتاج إلى إنشاء حساب على الإنترنت."},{"en":"Next, you fill in the form with your personal details.","ar":"بعد ذلك تملأ الاستمارة ببياناتك الشخصية."},{"en":"After that, the documents are uploaded and checked by staff.","ar":"بعد ذلك تُرفع الوثائق ويراجعها الموظفون."},{"en":"If anything is missing, you will receive an email.","ar":"إذا كان هناك شيء ناقص فستصلك رسالة إلكترونية."},{"en":"Once the application is approved, you pay the fee online.","ar":"بمجرد الموافقة على الطلب تدفع الرسوم عبر الإنترنت."},{"en":"Then a confirmation letter is sent to your address.","ar":"ثم تُرسل رسالة تأكيد إلى عنوانك."},{"en":"Finally, you have to collect the certificate in person.","ar":"وأخيرًا عليك استلام الشهادة بنفسك."},{"en":"The whole process usually takes about two weeks.","ar":"تستغرق العملية كلها عادة نحو أسبوعين."}]},{"id":"sh-b1-12","l":"B1","title":"Telling a short story about yesterday","titleAr":"سرد قصة قصيرة عن أمس","lines":[{"en":"Yesterday I was on my way to the library when it started raining.","ar":"أمس كنت في طريقي إلى المكتبة عندما بدأ المطر."},{"en":"I hadn't brought an umbrella, so I ran into a small café.","ar":"لم أحضر مظلة، فركضت إلى مقهى صغير."},{"en":"While I was waiting, I met an old classmate there.","ar":"وبينما كنت أنتظر، قابلت زميلًا قديمًا في الدراسة هناك."},{"en":"We hadn't seen each other for almost five years.","ar":"لم نلتقِ منذ نحو خمس سنوات."},{"en":"We talked for two hours and completely forgot about the time.","ar":"تحدثنا لمدة ساعتين ونسينا الوقت تمامًا."},{"en":"When I finally looked at my watch, the library was closed.","ar":"وعندما نظرت أخيرًا إلى ساعتي، كانت المكتبة قد أُغلقت."},{"en":"In the end, I walked home feeling happy anyway.","ar":"في النهاية مشيت إلى البيت وأنا سعيد رغم ذلك."},{"en":"It turned out to be a better evening than I'd planned.","ar":"تبيّن أنها أمسية أفضل مما خططت له."}]}]);
Fasih.add("pron",[{"id":"pr-01","title":"/p/ and /b/","titleAr":"صوتا /p/ و /b/","ar":"في العربية لا يوجد صوت /p/، فيميل العربي إلى نطقه /b/ فيقول bark بدل park. لنطق /p/ أغلق شفتيك ثم افتحهما بدفعة هواء قوية دون اهتزاز الحنجرة؛ ضع ورقة أمام فمك فتتحرك مع /p/ ولا تتحرك كثيرًا مع /b/. أما /b/ ففيه اهتزاز في الحنجرة وهواء أقل.","pairs":[["park","bark"],["pill","bill"],["pack","back"],["pie","buy"],["pen","Ben"],["rope","robe"],["cap","cab"],["pear","bear"]],"sent":["Please put the paper bag on the big blue bench.","The baby bought a pink balloon in the park.","Peter's bike has a broken pedal.","I spoke to the public prosecutor about the problem."]},{"id":"pr-02","title":"/f/ and /v/","titleAr":"صوتا /f/ و /v/","ar":"العربية فيها /f/ لكن ليس فيها /v/، فيقول العربي fan بدل van. لنطق /v/ ضع أسنانك العليا على الشفة السفلى تمامًا كما في /f/ ثم شغّل الحنجرة (ضع يدك على رقبتك فتشعر بالاهتزاز). الخطأ الشائع نطق /v/ كأنه /f/ بلا اهتزاز.","pairs":[["fan","van"],["fail","veil"],["safe","save"],["fine","vine"],["leaf","leave"],["few","view"],["ferry","very"],["proof","prove"]],"sent":["Very few visitors visited the village in November.","Steve gave five votes to the new leader.","The fast ferry leaves from the river at five.","We have to prove that the evidence is valid."]},{"id":"pr-03","title":"Short i and e (sit, set)","titleAr":"صوتا i القصير و e (sit / set)","ar":"العربية فيها كسرة وفتحة لكنها لا تفرّق بين هذين الصوتين، فينطق العربي sit و set متقاربتين. صوت /ɪ/ في sit قصير ومسترخٍ والفم شبه مغلق واللسان مرتفع قليلًا. أما /e/ في set فيكون الفم أكثر انفتاحًا والفكّ ينزل قليلًا. الخطأ الشائع نطق الصوتين كما في الكسرة الطويلة أو الفتحة المائلة.","pairs":[["sit","set"],["bit","bet"],["pin","pen"],["lid","led"],["tin","ten"],["bill","bell"],["fill","fell"],["list","lest"]],"sent":["Ben put the pen in the tin box.","Let him sit and tell me the list.","The red bed is in the big hill house.","Ten men did the exercise in the gym."]},{"id":"pr-04","title":"Long ee and short i (sheep, ship)","titleAr":"صوتا ee الطويل و i القصير (sheep / ship)","ar":"في sheep الصوت /iː/ طويل: شدّ شفتيك كأنك تبتسم وأطل الصوت. في ship الصوت /ɪ/ قصير ومسترخٍ وبلا ابتسامة. الخطأ الشائع أن يطيل العربي الصوتين بالقدر نفسه أو يقصّرهما معًا، فيضيع الفرق في المعنى بين leave و live وبين seat و sit.","pairs":[["sheep","ship"],["seat","sit"],["leave","live"],["beat","bit"],["feel","fill"],["green","grin"],["heel","hill"],["reach","rich"]],"sent":["The sheep left the ship near the green beach.","Please sit in this seat and keep clean.","I feel ill, so I will leave the meeting.","He lives in a big city and eats cheap meat."]},{"id":"pr-05","title":"a in cat and u in cut","titleAr":"صوتا a في cat و u في cut","ar":"في cat الصوت /æ/ يُنطق بفم مفتوح كثيرًا وفكّ منخفض وشفتين ممدودتين قليلًا، مثل فتحة مفخّمة نحو الكسرة. في cut الصوت /ʌ/ قصير وفم نصف مفتوح واللسان في الوسط. العربي يخلط بينهما فيقول cat كأنها cut أو العكس، فتتغير الكلمة: hat وhut، bag وbug.","pairs":[["cat","cut"],["hat","hut"],["bag","bug"],["ran","run"],["cap","cup"],["match","much"],["lack","luck"],["ankle","uncle"]],"sent":["The cat ran up the hut at lunch.","My uncle has a black cap and a big bag.","The bus was full of bugs and fun noise.","Come and have a cup of tea, Dad."]},{"id":"pr-06","title":"o in not and o in note","titleAr":"صوتا o في not و o في note","ar":"في not الصوت /ɒ/ قصير بفم مستدير ومفتوح قليلًا. في note الصوت /əʊ/ مزدوج: يبدأ بصوت وسط ثم ينزلق نحو ضمّة خفيفة بشفتين مستديرتين. الخطأ الشائع أن ينطقه العربي كواو طويلة صافية /oː/ دون الانزلاق، أو أن يقصّر note فتصبح كأنها not.","pairs":[["not","note"],["hop","hope"],["cod","code"],["rob","robe"],["got","goat"],["cost","coast"],["pop","pope"],["shot","show"]],"sent":["I hope he got a note from the old doctor.","The goat is not on the road.","Tom wrote the code on a hot day.","Joe won't stop at the coast."]},{"id":"pr-07","title":"th in think vs s","titleAr":"صوت th في think مقابل s","ar":"صوت /θ/ غير موجود في العربية الفصحى المنطوقة عند كثيرين، فيحوّلونه إلى /s/ فيقولون sink بدل think. لنطقه أخرج طرف لسانك قليلًا بين الأسنان العليا والسفلى وانفخ الهواء بلطف دون اهتزاز الحنجرة. في /s/ يبقى اللسان خلف الأسنان. الخطأ هنا قد يغيّر المعنى: think وsink، thank وsank.","pairs":[["think","sink"],["thank","sank"],["thing","sing"],["thick","sick"],["path","pass"],["mouth","mouse"],["three","tree"],["thought","sought"]],"sent":["I think three thousand people thanked me.","Thank you for the thick path through the north.","The third month of the year is a month of growth.","He thought about the mouth of the river."]},{"id":"pr-08","title":"th in this vs z","titleAr":"صوت th في this مقابل z","ar":"صوت /ð/ هو النظير المجهور لـ /θ/، وهو يشبه ظاء/ذال العربية إلى حد ما لكن بطرف اللسان بين الأسنان بلطف. عادة ينطقه العربي /z/ أو /d/ فيقول zis أو dis. اجعل لسانك بين أسنانك وشغّل الحنجرة لتشعر بالاهتزاز. هذا الصوت يظهر كثيرًا في the وthis وthat وthey وwith.","pairs":[["this",""],["that",""],["they",""],["then",""],["breathe",""],["brother",""],["together",""],["weather",""]],"sent":["This is the other one that they bought.","My brother and father were there with them.","Then they breathe the clean air together.","I'd rather go there than stay here."]},{"id":"pr-09","title":"ch and sh","titleAr":"صوتا ch و sh","ar":"/tʃ/ في chip مركّب من توقف ثم احتكاك: ابدأ بلسان ملتصق بسقف الفم ثم أطلق الهواء بقوة. أما /ʃ/ في ship فاحتكاك مستمر فقط كالشين العربية. يخلط العربي أحيانًا فيقول sheap بدل cheap. العربية فيها شين لكن ليس فيها صوت ch كما في الإنجليزية، فتدرّب على بدء الصوت بتاء خفيفة.","pairs":[["chip","ship"],["cheap","sheep"],["chair","share"],["catch","cash"],["watch","wash"],["choose","shoes"],["March","marsh"],["much","mush"]],"sent":["She chose a cheap chair in the shop.","Which kitchen has fresh cheese and fish?","The teacher will check the church map in March.","I watch him wash his shoes each Thursday."]},{"id":"pr-10","title":"ng at the end of words","titleAr":"صوت ng في نهاية الكلمات","ar":"في الإنجليزية /ŋ/ صوت أنفي واحد في sing، يخرج من الأنف مع رفع مؤخرة اللسان نحو سقف الحلق الرخو، ولا يُسمع حرف g منفصل. الخطأ الشائع نطق n ثم g بوضوح فتصبح sing كأنها sin-g، أو الاكتفاء بـ n فتصبح sin. لا تُفصل النون عن الجيم.","pairs":[["sing","sin"],["thing","thin"],["king","kin"],["long","lawn"],["morning",""],["building",""],["meeting",""],["evening",""]],"sent":["I am running along the long morning road.","The king is singing in the building.","We are having a meeting this evening.","She was reading and writing something interesting."]},{"id":"pr-11","title":"Consonant clusters without adding a vowel","titleAr":"تجمّعات الحروف الساكنة دون إضافة حرف علة","ar":"العربية لا تبدأ الكلمة بحرفين ساكنين متتاليين، فيضيف العربي حركة قبلهما أو بينهما فيقول istreet بدل street، وisspring بدل spring، وaskid بدل asked. تدرّب على نطق الأصوات متتابعة بسرعة دون أي حرف علة، وابدأ بإطالة الصوت الأول: sssstreet. وفي asked انطق /skt/ دفعة واحدة.","pairs":[["street",""],["spring",""],["strong",""],["school",""],["asked",""],["scratch",""],["splash",""],["next",""]],"sent":["The street in spring is quiet and clean.","She asked the strict student to stop.","Strong winds struck the school on Sunday.","He is next in the queue to speak."]},{"id":"pr-12","title":"-ed endings","titleAr":"نهايات -ed","ar":"تُنطق -ed بثلاث طرق: /t/ بعد الأصوات المهموسة مثل worked وstopped؛ /d/ بعد المجهورة مثل played وlived؛ و/ɪd/ فقط بعد t أو d مثل wanted وneeded فتزيد مقطعًا. الخطأ الشائع نطق -ed دائمًا مقطعًا كاملًا أي worked كأنها work-ed.","pairs":[["worked",""],["stopped",""],["played",""],["lived",""],["wanted",""],["needed",""],["watched",""],["opened",""]],"sent":["He worked, stopped and watched the road.","She played and lived in a small village.","They wanted and needed more time.","We opened the door and looked inside."]},{"id":"pr-13","title":"-s endings","titleAr":"نهايات -s","ar":"تُنطق -s بثلاث طرق: /s/ بعد المهموسة مثل cats وbooks؛ /z/ بعد المجهورة والحروف العلة مثل dogs وplays؛ و/ɪz/ بعد أصوات الصفير مثل buses وwatches فتزيد مقطعًا. العربي ينطقها غالبًا /s/ دائمًا أو يحذفها فيقول he work بدل he works.","pairs":[["cats",""],["books",""],["dogs",""],["plays",""],["buses",""],["watches",""],["judges",""],["knows",""]],"sent":["He works and she watches the buses.","The dogs and cats sleep in the houses.","My brother plays and knows many games.","The judge reads books and writes notes."]},{"id":"pr-14","title":"Word stress (photograph, photographer)","titleAr":"النبر على الكلمة (photograph / photographer)","ar":"في الإنجليزية لكل كلمة مقطع منبور يُنطق أقوى وأطول وأعلى. في PHOtograph النبر على المقطع الأول، لكن في phoTOgrapher ينتقل إلى الثاني. العربي ينبر مقاطع الكلمة بالتساوي تقريبًا، فيضيع المعنى. قاعدة: كثير من الكلمات تغير نبرها حين تتغير النهاية مثل -ic وإضافة -er. ابحث عن النبر في المعجم وصفّق عند المقطع المنبور.","pairs":[["photograph","photographer"],["economy","economic"],["politics","political"],["music","musician"],["science","scientific"],["history","historical"],["analyse","analysis"],["able","ability"]],"sent":["The photographer took a photograph of the economy minister.","Every musician needs ability and a strong sense of music.","The scientific analysis of history is important.","I study politics, and the political situation is changing."]},{"id":"pr-15","title":"Weak forms and the schwa","titleAr":"الأشكال الضعيفة وصوت schwa","ar":"الصوت /ə/ (schwa) أكثر صوت شائعًا في الإنجليزية، وهو صوت قصير جدًا ومسترخٍ تنطقه الكلمات غير المنبورة مثل a وthe وto وof وand وcan. مثال: can في I can swim تنطق /kən/ لا /kæn/. العربي ينطق كل كلمة بوضوح ونبر كامل فتبدو جمله بطيئة وغير طبيعية. اجعل الكلمات الصغيرة سريعة وضعيفة والكلمات الرئيسية فقط قوية.","pairs":[["about",""],["banana",""],["support",""],["to",""],["of",""],["and",""],["can",""],["the",""]],"sent":["I can go to a shop and buy a banana.","Tom and Anna are tired of the weather.","She wants to talk about a problem at the office.","A cup of tea and a piece of cake, please."]},{"id":"pr-16","title":"Silent letters","titleAr":"الحروف الصامتة","ar":"في الإنجليزية حروف تُكتب ولا تُنطق، وهي تربك العربي لأنه اعتاد أن كل حرف يُنطق. من أمثلتها k في knife وknow، وw في write وwrong، وb في doubt وthumb، وl في walk وcould، وh في hour وhonest، وgh في night وthrough. تعلّم الكلمة نطقًا لا هجاءً، ولا تنطق الحرف الصامت حتى لا يخطئ المستمع.","pairs":[["knife",""],["know",""],["write",""],["wrong",""],["doubt",""],["walk",""],["hour",""],["night",""]],"sent":["I know the answer, so I don't doubt it.","He wrote the wrong word on the whiteboard.","We walk for an hour every night.","The honest knight left his knife outside."]}]);
Fasih.add("sp1",[{"id":"sp1-01","l":"B1","topic":"Hometown","qs":[{"q":"Where is your hometown, and what is it famous for?","a":"My hometown is Tissemsilt, a quiet city in the highlands of Algeria. It's known for its forests and cool winters.","tip":"اذكر المكان ثم معلومة مميزة واحدة، ولا تكتف بالاسم."},{"q":"Do you like living in your hometown?","a":"Yes, mostly. It's peaceful and everyone knows each other, although there are fewer job opportunities than in big cities.","tip":"أعطِ رأيًا ثم سببًا ثم تحفظًا صغيرًا ليبدو الجواب طبيعيًا."},{"q":"What changes have you seen in your hometown in recent years?","a":"A lot of new roads and shops have been built. The centre is busier now, and young people use the internet much more.","tip":"استعمل المضارع التام أو الماضي للحديث عن التغيّر."},{"q":"Would you recommend your hometown to visitors?","a":"Definitely. They could walk in the nearby woods and try the local food, and the people are very welcoming.","tip":"يمكنك استعمال could وwould لتقديم اقتراح مهذب."}]},{"id":"sp1-02","l":"B1","topic":"Studies","qs":[{"q":"What are you studying at the moment?","a":"I'm doing a master's degree in transparency and anti-corruption. It's quite demanding, but I find it very interesting.","tip":"لا تذكر اسم التخصص فقط، أضف شعورك تجاهه."},{"q":"Why did you choose this subject?","a":"I wanted to understand how public institutions can be fairer. I also think it will give me useful skills for research.","tip":"أجب بـ because أو I wanted to لتعطي سببًا واضحًا."},{"q":"What is the most difficult part of your studies?","a":"Reading long academic articles in English is the hardest part. I often need to read them twice to understand the main idea.","tip":"تحدث عن صعوبة حقيقية ثم اذكر كيف تتعامل معها."},{"q":"How do you usually study for exams?","a":"I make short summaries and test myself with questions. I study in the library because it's quiet and I can concentrate there.","tip":"اذكر عادتين محددتين لتبدو إجابتك غنية."}]},{"id":"sp1-03","l":"B1","topic":"Work","qs":[{"q":"What kind of work do you do?","a":"I work with technical documents for construction projects. I write and organise reports, so accuracy is very important.","tip":"صف عملك بجملة بسيطة ثم اذكر ما يهم فيه."},{"q":"What do you enjoy most about your job?","a":"I enjoy solving problems and seeing a project move forward. It's also satisfying when a long document is finally finished.","tip":"استخدم enjoy مع اسم أو -ing."},{"q":"Is there anything you dislike about your work?","a":"Sometimes the deadlines are very tight, which can be stressful. However, it teaches me to plan my time carefully.","tip":"بعد ذكر السلبية أضف However لتنهي بنبرة إيجابية."},{"q":"What job would you like to do in the future?","a":"I'd like to become a researcher in the field of law and technology. I hope to work at a university abroad one day.","tip":"استعمل I'd like to وI hope to للحديث عن المستقبل."}]},{"id":"sp1-04","l":"B1","topic":"Family","qs":[{"q":"Tell me about your family.","a":"There are six of us in my family: my parents, two brothers and a sister. We're very close and we have dinner together every Friday.","tip":"اذكر العدد ثم تفصيلًا شخصيًا صغيرًا."},{"q":"Who are you closest to in your family?","a":"I'm closest to my mother. She always listens to me and gives me good advice when I'm worried.","tip":"الجملة الثانية تشرح السبب، وهذا ما يريده الممتحن."},{"q":"Do you spend much time with your family?","a":"Yes, whenever I can. Because of my studies I'm often busy, but we talk on the phone almost every day.","tip":"whenever I can عبارة مفيدة وطبيعية."},{"q":"Do you think family is important?","a":"Of course. Family gives you support when things are difficult, and it teaches you values that stay with you for life.","tip":"اربط الرأي بسبب ومثال عام."}]},{"id":"sp1-05","l":"B1","topic":"Daily routine","qs":[{"q":"What is your daily routine like?","a":"I get up at about six, have breakfast and go to the university. In the evening I study for a few hours and then go to bed.","tip":"استخدم كلمات الترتيب مثل then وafter that."},{"q":"Which part of the day do you like best?","a":"I like the evening best because it's calm. I can read or study without being interrupted.","tip":"اذكر وقتًا محددًا وسببه."},{"q":"Do you have the same routine at the weekend?","a":"Not really. At the weekend I sleep later and meet friends, so my days are much more relaxed.","tip":"Not really طريقة طبيعية للنفي الخفيف."},{"q":"Would you like to change anything in your routine?","a":"Yes, I'd like to do some exercise every morning. At the moment I don't have enough time, but I'm trying to plan better.","tip":"استخدم الشرط أو I'd like to للحديث عن تغيير."}]},{"id":"sp1-06","l":"B1","topic":"Food","qs":[{"q":"What kinds of food do you like?","a":"I love traditional Algerian dishes like couscous, but I also enjoy fresh salads and grilled fish.","tip":"أعط مثالين محددين بدل قول I like everything."},{"q":"Do you prefer eating at home or in restaurants?","a":"I prefer eating at home because the food is healthier and cheaper. Still, I sometimes go out with friends for a change.","tip":"prefer مع because ثم Still للتوازن."},{"q":"Can you cook?","a":"I can cook a few simple dishes, such as rice with vegetables. I'd like to learn more because cooking is a useful skill.","tip":"اذكر ما تستطيع فعله بدقة بدل نعم أو لا."},{"q":"Has your diet changed in recent years?","a":"Yes, I eat less sugar than before. I try to have more fruit and drink more water, because I care about my health.","tip":"استخدم المضارع التام أو المقارنة مثل less than."}]},{"id":"sp1-07","l":"B1","topic":"Weather","qs":[{"q":"What is the weather like in your region?","a":"Winters are cold and sometimes snowy, while summers are hot and dry. Spring is the nicest season, in my opinion.","tip":"while مفيدة للمقارنة بين فصلين."},{"q":"What kind of weather do you like?","a":"I like mild, sunny weather because I can go out and walk. I don't enjoy very hot days, as they make me tired.","tip":"اذكر سببًا لما تحبه ولما لا تحبه."},{"q":"Does the weather affect your mood?","a":"Yes, definitely. When it's grey and rainy, I feel lazy, but sunshine gives me more energy.","tip":"استعمل when لتصف علاقة السبب بالنتيجة."},{"q":"Has the weather changed where you live?","a":"I think summers are hotter than they used to be. People also say that there is less rain, which worries farmers.","tip":"used to تناسب الحديث عن الماضي مقابل الحاضر."}]},{"id":"sp1-08","l":"B1","topic":"Hobbies","qs":[{"q":"What do you like doing in your free time?","a":"I like reading and walking in the open air. Recently I've also started learning how to edit videos.","tip":"اذكر هوايتين، وواحدة منهما حديثة تعطي حيوية."},{"q":"How did you become interested in that hobby?","a":"A friend showed me some videos, and I wanted to try it myself. After a few weeks, I was hooked.","tip":"be hooked تعبير عامي مقبول يعني أُغرمت به."},{"q":"Do you prefer hobbies you do alone or with others?","a":"It depends. Reading is better alone, but I enjoy football much more with friends because it's more fun.","tip":"It depends تمنحك وقتًا للتفكير وتفتح جوابًا متوازنًا."},{"q":"Is there a hobby you would like to try?","a":"I'd love to try photography. I think it would help me see ordinary places in a new way.","tip":"استعمل would للتعبير عن أمنية."}]},{"id":"sp1-09","l":"B1","topic":"Sport","qs":[{"q":"Do you play any sports?","a":"I play football once a week with my friends. I'm not very skilled, but it's great exercise and a lot of fun.","tip":"اعترف بمستواك بصدق، فهذا طبيعي ومقبول."},{"q":"What sports are popular in your country?","a":"Football is by far the most popular. People of all ages watch matches, and kids play in the streets after school.","tip":"by far للتأكيد على الأكثر شعبية."},{"q":"Do you think sport is important for young people?","a":"Yes, because it keeps them healthy and teaches teamwork. It also gives them something positive to do instead of staying on their phones.","tip":"سببان للإجابة أفضل من سبب واحد."},{"q":"Would you like to try a new sport?","a":"I'd like to try swimming. It's good for the whole body, and I've never had proper lessons.","tip":"اذكر سببًا وحقيقة شخصية عن تجربتك السابقة."}]},{"id":"sp1-10","l":"B1","topic":"Reading","qs":[{"q":"Do you enjoy reading?","a":"Yes, I read almost every day, mostly articles and non-fiction books. I find it relaxing and it improves my vocabulary.","tip":"اذكر ما تقرأ ولماذا تقرأ."},{"q":"What kind of books do you like?","a":"I prefer books about history and science because they explain how the world works. Now and then I read a good novel, too.","tip":"Now and then تعبير طبيعي بمعنى أحيانًا."},{"q":"Do you prefer paper books or e-books?","a":"I prefer e-books because I can carry hundreds of them on my phone. However, paper books are better for long, quiet reading.","tip":"قارن بين الخيارين بوضوح باستعمال However."},{"q":"Did you read a lot when you were a child?","a":"Not much, to be honest. We didn't have many books at home, but I started reading more when I went to university.","tip":"to be honest عبارة مفيدة وتجعل الجواب صادقًا."}]},{"id":"sp1-11","l":"B1","topic":"Travel","qs":[{"q":"Do you like travelling?","a":"Yes, very much. I enjoy discovering new places and trying different food, but I don't travel as often as I'd like.","tip":"as often as I'd like مقارنة طبيعية جدًا."},{"q":"What was the best trip you have ever taken?","a":"I once visited the north coast with my family. The sea was beautiful, and we stayed in a small village for a week.","tip":"استعمل الماضي البسيط لتروي رحلة محددة."},{"q":"Do you prefer travelling alone or with other people?","a":"I prefer travelling with friends because it's safer and more fun. On the other hand, travelling alone lets you decide everything yourself.","tip":"On the other hand لإظهار الوجه الآخر."},{"q":"Which country would you like to visit in the future?","a":"I'd love to visit Japan. I'm interested in its technology and its culture, and I hear that the cities are very clean.","tip":"I hear that طريقة لطيفة لنقل معلومة سمعتها."}]},{"id":"sp1-12","l":"B1","topic":"Technology","qs":[{"q":"What technology do you use every day?","a":"I use my phone and laptop all day long. I use them for studying, communicating and finding information.","tip":"اذكر شيئين ثم الغرض من استعمالهما."},{"q":"Has technology made your life easier?","a":"Yes, in many ways. I can access books and lessons online, so I don't always need to go to the library.","tip":"in many ways تسمح بذكر مثال بعدها."},{"q":"Is there any technology you find annoying?","a":"Notifications annoy me a lot. They interrupt my work, so I often turn my phone off when I study.","tip":"annoy فعل جيد، واذكر الحل الذي تتبعه."},{"q":"How do you think technology will change education?","a":"I think students will use more artificial intelligence to practise and get feedback. Still, teachers will remain important to guide them.","tip":"لا بأس بالتوقع باستعمال will مع I think."}]},{"id":"sp1-13","l":"B1","topic":"Friends","qs":[{"q":"Do you have many friends?","a":"I have a few close friends and many acquaintances. I met most of my close friends at university.","tip":"فرّق بين close friends وacquaintances لتظهر مفردات أدق."},{"q":"What do you usually do with your friends?","a":"We often study together, play football or just have tea and talk. It's a good way to relax after a busy week.","tip":"اذكر ثلاثة أنشطة بسيطة."},{"q":"What makes a good friend?","a":"A good friend is honest and loyal, and listens when you have a problem. Trust is the most important thing for me.","tip":"استعمل صفات مثل honest وloyal وreliable."},{"q":"Do you keep in touch with old friends?","a":"Yes, mostly by messages and sometimes by video calls. We try to meet up whenever I go back to my hometown.","tip":"keep in touch وmeet up عبارتان مفيدتان."}]},{"id":"sp1-14","l":"B1","topic":"Shopping","qs":[{"q":"Do you enjoy shopping?","a":"Not really. I find it tiring to walk around crowded shops, so I only go when I need something specific.","tip":"اشرح رأيك السلبي بلطف دون مبالغة."},{"q":"Do you prefer shopping online or in shops?","a":"I usually shop online because it saves time and prices are easy to compare. For clothes, though, I prefer trying things on in a shop.","tip":"though في نهاية الجملة طبيعية جدًا في الكلام."},{"q":"What was the last thing you bought?","a":"I bought a new pair of headphones last week. They have good sound, and they help me concentrate while I study.","tip":"الماضي البسيط مع الزمن last week."},{"q":"Do you usually spend a lot of money when you shop?","a":"No, I try to stick to a budget. I make a list before I go out so that I don't buy things I don't need.","tip":"stick to a budget وso that جملتان مفيدتان."}]},{"id":"sp1-15","l":"B1","topic":"Transport","qs":[{"q":"How do you usually get to the university?","a":"I usually take the bus because it's cheap and direct. When the weather is nice, I sometimes walk.","tip":"اذكر الوسيلة والسبب ثم استثناءً."},{"q":"What is public transport like in your city?","a":"It's quite basic. Buses are fairly cheap, but they aren't always on time and they get crowded in the morning.","tip":"fairly وquite تخفف الحكم وتجعله واقعيًا."},{"q":"Do you prefer travelling by car or by train?","a":"I prefer the train because I can read or work during the journey. A car is more flexible, though, especially for short trips.","tip":"اربط تفضيلك بشيء تفعله أثناء الرحلة."},{"q":"How could transport in your area be improved?","a":"There should be more frequent buses and better bus stops. A tram or a train line would also reduce traffic.","tip":"should وwould تناسبان الاقتراحات."}]},{"id":"sp1-16","l":"B1","topic":"Sleep","qs":[{"q":"How many hours do you usually sleep?","a":"I usually sleep about seven hours. During exams, I sleep less, but I try to catch up at the weekend.","tip":"catch up عبارة تعني التعويض."},{"q":"Do you have any problems sleeping?","a":"Sometimes I find it hard to fall asleep if I've used my phone late. I'm trying to read a book instead.","tip":"fall asleep تُستعمل بدل sleep عند الحديث عن بدء النوم."},{"q":"Do you ever take naps during the day?","a":"Yes, a short nap after lunch helps me feel fresh again. I try not to sleep for more than twenty minutes.","tip":"take a nap تعبير شائع."},{"q":"Why do you think sleep is important?","a":"Without enough sleep, it's difficult to concentrate or remember things. It also affects your health and your mood.","tip":"اذكر أثرين: العقل والصحة."}]},{"id":"sp1-17","l":"B1","topic":"Mornings","qs":[{"q":"Are you a morning person?","a":"Not really. I'm more productive in the evening, but I'm trying to get up earlier so that I can study in the quiet hours.","tip":"morning person تعني شخصًا نشيطًا في الصباح."},{"q":"What do you usually do in the morning?","a":"I have a light breakfast, check my messages and then leave for the university. I never skip breakfast because it gives me energy.","tip":"استخدم never مع المضارع البسيط للعادات."},{"q":"What time do you normally get up?","a":"I get up at about six on weekdays, but at the weekend, I stay in bed until eight.","tip":"about لتقريب الأرقام."},{"q":"Do you think getting up early is a good habit?","a":"I do. You have more time to plan your day, and the morning is a good time to study when your mind is fresh.","tip":"I do جواب مختصر ثم سبب."}]},{"id":"sp1-18","l":"B1","topic":"Languages","qs":[{"q":"What languages do you speak?","a":"I speak Arabic and French, and I'm learning English. I'd like to improve my English because I need it for my research.","tip":"اذكر لغاتك ثم الدافع للتعلم."},{"q":"Why are you learning English?","a":"I need it to read academic papers and to study abroad. English is also the language of most international research.","tip":"اذكر سببين: الدراسة والبحث."},{"q":"What do you find difficult about learning a language?","a":"Speaking is the hardest part for me. I know many words, but I'm sometimes nervous about making mistakes.","tip":"اعترف بصعوبة محددة وليس بشكل عام."},{"q":"How do you practise your English?","a":"I read articles, listen to podcasts and repeat sentences aloud. I also try to write short texts every day.","tip":"اذكر ثلاثة أنشطة بصيغة مضارع."}]},{"id":"sp1-19","l":"B1","topic":"The internet","qs":[{"q":"How often do you use the internet?","a":"I use it every day, for several hours. Most of the time, it's for study, but I also watch videos and talk to friends.","tip":"for several hours وmost of the time تعبيرات للتكرار."},{"q":"What do you mostly use the internet for?","a":"Mostly for research and learning. I look up articles, watch online lectures and sometimes read the news.","tip":"look up عبارة فعلية تعني بحث عن معلومة."},{"q":"Do you think the internet has any disadvantages?","a":"Yes, there's a lot of false information and it can waste a lot of time. People also need to protect their personal data.","tip":"اذكر عيبين ثم لا تطل."},{"q":"How did people live before the internet?","a":"They used books, libraries and letters. Life was slower, but finding information took much more effort.","tip":"استعمل الماضي البسيط لوصف الماضي."}]},{"id":"sp1-20","l":"B1","topic":"Your home","qs":[{"q":"Do you live in a house or an apartment?","a":"I live in an apartment on the third floor. It has three rooms and a small balcony.","tip":"صف البيت بعدد الغرف وميزة واحدة."},{"q":"What is your favourite room in your home?","a":"My favourite room is my study. It has a big desk and a window, and it's the quietest place in the house.","tip":"اذكر الغرفة ثم أسباب تحبها."},{"q":"Would you like to change anything about your home?","a":"I'd like a bigger desk and more shelves for my books. A garden would be wonderful, but it's not possible in an apartment.","tip":"الشرط الثاني تعبير جيد عن الأمنية."},{"q":"Do you want to stay there in the future?","a":"For now, yes, but I might move abroad for my studies. Then I'd probably rent a small flat near the university.","tip":"might وprobably تعبران عن عدم اليقين."}]},{"id":"sp1-21","l":"B1","topic":"Weekends","qs":[{"q":"What do you usually do at weekends?","a":"I usually relax and catch up with my family. On Fridays, we have a big lunch together, and in the evening I see my friends.","tip":"اذكر نشاطين ثم زمنًا محددًا."},{"q":"Do you prefer to be busy or relaxed at the weekend?","a":"A bit of both. I like to study for a couple of hours in the morning, and after that I want to relax.","tip":"A bit of both إجابة ذكية عندما يكون الخياران صحيحين."},{"q":"What did you do last weekend?","a":"I went to the market with my mother and then watched a football match with my brother. It was a simple but enjoyable weekend.","tip":"استعمل الماضي البسيط وكلمات الربط then."},{"q":"What would be your perfect weekend?","a":"A perfect weekend would be a short trip to the mountains with friends, followed by a good meal. No work and no alarm clock!","tip":"would be لوصف الحلم أو المثال الأعلى."}]},{"id":"sp1-22","l":"B1","topic":"Nature","qs":[{"q":"Do you like spending time in nature?","a":"Yes, I do. Walking in the forest clears my mind and helps me forget about my work for a while.","tip":"clear your mind عبارة طبيعية."},{"q":"Are there any natural places near where you live?","a":"There's a beautiful forest of cedar trees about thirty minutes from my city. In spring, many families go there for picnics.","tip":"استعمل there is لتقديم مكان."},{"q":"Do you think people care enough about nature?","a":"Not enough, in my opinion. There's a lot of rubbish in public places, and people often waste water.","tip":"not enough تعبير مهذب عن النقد."},{"q":"What can ordinary people do to protect the environment?","a":"They can recycle, use less plastic and save water and electricity. Planting trees together is also a great idea.","tip":"can مع قائمة من الحلول العملية."}]},{"id":"sp1-23","l":"B1","topic":"Holidays","qs":[{"q":"What do you usually do on holidays?","a":"I usually visit my relatives and spend time at home. If I have a longer break, I go on a short trip with my friends.","tip":"If مع المضارع البسيط لحالة محتملة."},{"q":"What is your favourite holiday or festival?","a":"I love Eid because the whole family gets together. We share special food, and the children get gifts.","tip":"اذكر مناسبة عامة محايدة وسببًا اجتماعيًا."},{"q":"Do you prefer holidays by the sea or in the mountains?","a":"The sea, definitely. I love swimming and relaxing on the beach, although the mountains are cooler in summer.","tip":"definitely لتقوية الاختيار."},{"q":"Where did you go on your last holiday?","a":"I went to the coast with my family last summer. We rented a small flat near the beach and stayed for five days.","tip":"الماضي البسيط وتفاصيل صغيرة: كم يومًا وأين."}]},{"id":"sp1-24","l":"B1","topic":"Future plans","qs":[{"q":"What are you going to do after you finish your studies?","a":"I'm going to continue with a PhD abroad. After that, I'd like to work in research and teaching.","tip":"be going to للخطط المحددة."},{"q":"Where do you see yourself in ten years?","a":"I hope to be a researcher at a university, working on projects that help make public institutions fairer.","tip":"I hope to be + صفة أو مهنة صيغة مفيدة."},{"q":"Is there anything you want to achieve this year?","a":"I want to improve my English enough to take the IELTS exam. I also plan to publish my first research article.","tip":"want to وplan to بصيغتين مختلفتين."},{"q":"What could stop you from achieving your goals?","a":"Money could be a problem, and so could lack of time. But I'm determined to find scholarships and organise my time well.","tip":"could للاحتمال وdetermined لإظهار العزم."}]}]);
Fasih.add("sp2",[{"id":"sp2-01","l":"B1","card":"Describe a teacher who taught you something useful.","pts":["who the teacher was","what subject or skill they taught you","how they taught it","and explain why it was useful to you"],"model":"I'd like to talk about my English teacher at secondary school. She was a calm woman in her forties, and everybody in the class respected her. She taught us English, but she also taught us how to study properly, which was the most useful lesson of all.\n\nAs for her method, she never just gave us the answers. Instead, she asked us to work in small groups and explain the grammar to each other. At first I felt shy, but after a few weeks I started to enjoy it. She also made us keep a notebook for new words, and every Thursday she tested five of them. It sounds simple, but it really worked.\n\nThe reason this was so useful is that I still use the same habits today. When I read an article about law or technology, I write down the new words and review them at the weekend. Thanks to her, I can now read English papers for my studies without feeling lost.\n\nTo sum up, she did not only teach me a language. She taught me how to learn, and that is something I will keep for the rest of my life. I am really grateful to her.","phr":["I'd like to talk about...","As for her method,","The reason this was so useful is that...","Thanks to her,","To sum up,"],"tipAr":"ابدأ بجملة تقديم واضحة ثم اتبع النقاط الأربع بالترتيب، واستخدم أدوات الربط مثل As for وThe reason لتبدو إجابتك منظمة."},{"id":"sp2-02","l":"B1","card":"Describe a relative you enjoy spending time with.","pts":["who the person is","how often you see them","what you usually do together","and explain why you enjoy their company"],"model":"I'm going to describe my uncle, my father's younger brother. He is about forty-five and works as a civil engineer in a nearby city. He is tall, funny and always wears a big smile.\n\nWe don't live in the same town, so I see him about once a month, usually at the weekend. During the holidays, we meet more often because he comes to stay at my grandparents' house, and the whole family gathers there.\n\nWhen we are together, we usually do a few simple things. We go for a long walk in the morning, then we cook lunch together. He is a really good cook, and he always lets me help. In the evening, we sometimes play board games with my cousins, and he never lets me win!\n\nThe reason I enjoy his company is that he listens carefully. When I was worried about my exams last year, he did not tell me what to do. He just asked questions and helped me find my own answer. Also, he has a great sense of humour, so I never feel bored with him.\n\nIn short, he is like an older brother to me, and I always look forward to our next meeting.","phr":["I'm going to describe...","We don't live in the same town, so...","When we are together,","The reason I enjoy... is that...","In short,"],"tipAr":"لا تذكر الاسم الحقيقي للشخص؛ يكفي أن تقول my uncle أو my cousin، وركّز على التفاصيل الحية مثل الأنشطة والمواقف."},{"id":"sp2-03","l":"B1","card":"Describe a city you have visited and liked.","pts":["where the city is","when you went there","what you did there","and explain why you liked it"],"model":"I'd like to describe Constantine, a beautiful city in the north-east of Algeria. It is famous for its high bridges and its deep valley, and it is about four hours from my hometown by car.\n\nI went there two years ago, in spring, with three university friends. We had just finished our exams, so we wanted a short trip to relax. The weather was perfect, not too hot and not too cold.\n\nOnce we arrived, we left our bags at a small hotel and went straight to the old town. We walked across one of the long bridges, took a lot of photos and visited a museum. After that, we tried some local sweets in a tiny shop near the market. In the evening, we took a cable car and watched the lights of the city from above. It was unforgettable.\n\nThe reason I liked it so much is that it feels different from anywhere else I know. The buildings are old, but the people are modern and friendly. Everything was also quite cheap, which is important for students.\n\nTo be honest, I would love to go back one day, perhaps with my family, because there is still so much more to see.","phr":["I'd like to describe...","I went there... with...","Once we arrived,","After that,","To be honest,"],"tipAr":"استخدم الماضي البسيط عند الحديث عن الرحلة (went, visited, tried) وتجنب الخلط بينه وبين المضارع."},{"id":"sp2-04","l":"B1","card":"Describe a quiet place where you like to go.","pts":["where the place is","how often you go there","what you do there","and explain why it is quiet and special for you"],"model":"The quiet place I'd like to talk about is the public library in my city. It is a big old building near the town hall, with high windows and long wooden tables.\n\nI go there about three times a week, mostly in the afternoon after my classes. During exam periods, I sometimes spend the whole day there, with only a short break for lunch.\n\nWhen I'm there, I usually find a seat near the window on the second floor. First, I put my phone on silent and take out my notebook. Then I read for my law course or practise English with some articles. Sometimes I just sit and think about my plans for the future. It's a good place to organise my ideas.\n\nThe reason it feels so special is that everybody respects the silence. Nobody talks loudly, and there are no noisy cars, because the library is in a small street. Also, the smell of old books makes me feel calm and focused. At home, it is harder to concentrate because there are always visitors and a television in the living room.\n\nAll in all, it's my second home, and I believe I study better there than anywhere else.","phr":["The quiet place I'd like to talk about is...","I go there about... times a week","First,... Then...","The reason it feels so special is that...","All in all,"],"tipAr":"استعمل كلمات الترتيب First وThen وAlso لتربط الأفكار، فهذا يمنحك سلاسة أكثر في الكلام."},{"id":"sp2-05","l":"B1","card":"Describe a book you read and enjoyed.","pts":["what the book was","when you read it","what it was about","and explain why you enjoyed it"],"model":"I'm going to talk about a short novel about a young man who wants to become a judge in a small village. I read it last summer, during the long holidays, when I had a lot of free time.\n\nThe story is about a student from a poor family who works in a shop in the morning and studies at night. He sees that some people in his village receive help from officials only because they know them. He feels this is unfair, so he decides to study law and make things fairer. The story follows him for about ten years, and at the end he finally gets his dream job.\n\nI enjoyed the book for several reasons. First, the language was simple, so I could read it quickly. Second, the main character is very realistic. He makes mistakes, gets discouraged and even wants to give up at one point, but he keeps trying. That made me feel I could do the same with my own goals.\n\nAlso, the book made me think about justice and honesty in everyday life, which is an interest of mine.\n\nIn conclusion, it was a lovely and inspiring story, and I would recommend it to any student.","phr":["I'm going to talk about...","The story is about...","I enjoyed the book for several reasons.","That made me feel...","In conclusion,"],"tipAr":"عند وصف كتاب استعمل المضارع لتلخيص القصة (The story is about...) والماضي لما فعلته أنت (I read it)."},{"id":"sp2-06","l":"B1","card":"Describe a gift you gave to someone.","pts":["what the gift was","who you gave it to","why you chose it","and explain how the person felt about it"],"model":"I'd like to talk about a gift I gave to my mother last year for her birthday. It was a small digital photo frame, and I filled it with more than a hundred family pictures.\n\nI chose it for a few reasons. My mother loves looking at old photos, but they were all in boxes in the cupboard, and she never had time to take them out. Also, I did not have much money as a student, so I needed something useful but not too expensive. Then I remembered the frame in a shop and thought it would be perfect.\n\nBefore her birthday, I asked my sister to help me. We scanned the old photos in secret and put them in the frame. It took us two evenings, and we had to be careful because my mother was always at home.\n\nWhen she opened the box, she was very surprised. At first she did not say anything, and then her eyes filled with tears. She said it was the best gift she had ever received, and she put it in the living room straight away.\n\nTo be honest, her reaction made me happier than any gift I have ever received myself.","phr":["I'd like to talk about...","I chose it for a few reasons.","Also,","When she opened the box,","To be honest,"],"tipAr":"قصة صغيرة عن ردّة فعل الشخص تجعل إجابتك أكثر حيوية، فأضف دائماً مشهداً واحداً يمكن تخيّله."},{"id":"sp2-07","l":"B1","card":"Describe a celebration you enjoyed.","pts":["what the celebration was","who was there","what happened during it","and explain why you enjoyed it"],"model":"I'm going to talk about my cousin's wedding, which took place two summers ago in my grandfather's village. It was the biggest family celebration I have ever attended.\n\nAlmost eighty people came, including uncles, aunts, neighbours and friends from other towns. I was happy to meet relatives I had not seen for years. The whole house was decorated with lights and flowers, and there was a long table in the garden.\n\nDuring the party, a lot of things happened. First, we all had a big meal together, with traditional dishes that my aunts had cooked for two days. After that, some young men played music, and the children ran around and danced. Later in the evening, my grandfather made a short speech, and everybody clapped. I helped to serve tea and sweets, so I was busy but also very cheerful.\n\nThe reason I enjoyed it so much is that the whole family was together in one place. In everyday life, we are always busy with our studies and jobs, so we rarely see each other. That night, everybody was relaxed and laughing.\n\nIn short, it was a warm, noisy and happy evening that I will remember for a long time.","phr":["I'm going to talk about...","took place","During the party,","Later in the evening,","The reason I enjoyed it so much is that..."],"tipAr":"لا تكتفِ بقول It was nice؛ صِف ما رأيت وسمعت (الأضواء، الموسيقى، الطعام) لتجعل إجابتك أغنى."},{"id":"sp2-08","l":"B1","card":"Describe your first day at university or at a new school.","pts":["when it was","where you went","what happened that day","and explain how you felt about it"],"model":"I'd like to describe my first day at university, which was four years ago in September. I had just finished high school, and I was both excited and nervous.\n\nThe university is in a larger city, about two hours from my home. I left my house very early with my father, because I did not want to be late. When we arrived, the campus was full of students, and I could not find my faculty. In the end, a friendly older student showed me the way.\n\nDuring the day, I attended an introduction lecture in a huge hall with more than two hundred students. After that, we received our timetables and met our group leader. At lunchtime, I sat alone for a few minutes, but then a boy next to me started a conversation. We discovered that we both liked technology, and we have been friends ever since.\n\nAs for my feelings, in the morning I was a little afraid because everything was new and big. However, by the evening I felt much more confident and happy.\n\nLooking back, that day was the start of an important chapter in my life, and I'm glad I stayed positive.","phr":["I'd like to describe...","In the end,","During the day,","As for my feelings,","Looking back,"],"tipAr":"في هذه البطاقة عبّر عن مشاعرك بكلمات متنوعة مثل nervous وexcited وconfident بدل تكرار happy."},{"id":"sp2-09","l":"B1","card":"Describe a time when you helped someone.","pts":["who you helped","when it happened","how you helped them","and explain how you felt afterwards"],"model":"I'd like to talk about a time when I helped an elderly neighbour. It happened about a year ago, in winter, when I was coming back from the university.\n\nHe is a kind man in his seventies who lives alone on the first floor of our building. That evening, I saw him standing outside the door with a lot of shopping bags, and he looked very tired. The lift was broken, and he could not carry the bags up the stairs.\n\nSo I offered to help. I took the heavy bags and walked slowly with him to his flat. After that, I put the food in his kitchen and checked that he was fine. He wanted to give me some money, but I refused politely. Then he asked me to have a cup of tea with him, and we talked for half an hour about his life as a young teacher.\n\nAfterwards, I felt very pleased with myself. It was a small thing to do, but it made a big difference for him. Also, I learned that older people often just need someone to talk to.\n\nSince then, I say hello to him every day, and sometimes I do his shopping on Saturdays.","phr":["I'd like to talk about a time when...","It happened about... ago","So I offered to help.","Afterwards,","Since then,"],"tipAr":"اجعل القصة مرتبة زمنياً: ماذا حدث أولاً ثم بعد ذلك، واختم بما شعرت به وما تعلمته."},{"id":"sp2-10","l":"B1","card":"Describe a goal you would like to achieve in the future.","pts":["what the goal is","when you want to achieve it","what you need to do to reach it","and explain why it is important to you"],"model":"I'm going to talk about my main goal, which is to continue my studies abroad and become a researcher. I would like to do a doctorate in a good university and work on how technology can help to make public services fairer.\n\nI hope to start in about three years, once I finish my master's degree. It's a long way, but I think it is realistic if I stay serious.\n\nTo reach this goal, I need to do several things. First, I have to improve my English, especially my speaking and academic writing, so I practise every day. Second, I want to publish a few articles to show that I can do real research. Finally, I need to save some money for application fees and travel, so I do small online jobs when I have free time.\n\nThe reason it is so important to me is that I want to do something useful for my country and for other people. I also believe that education is the best way to change my life. Many young people in my region have talent, but they do not always have the chance to show it.\n\nIn short, this goal gives me energy every morning, and I will not give up easily.","phr":["I'm going to talk about my main goal, which is to...","I hope to start in...","To reach this goal, I need to...","First,... Second,... Finally,...","The reason it is so important to me is that..."],"tipAr":"عند الحديث عن الأهداف استخدم I would like to وI hope to وI plan to بدلاً من تكرار I want to."},{"id":"sp2-11","l":"B2","card":"Describe a colleague or fellow student you work well with.","pts":["who the person is","how you met","what kind of work you do together","and explain why you work well together"],"model":"I'd like to talk about a fellow student from my master's programme, whom I'll call my study partner. We both research topics connected to law and public administration, and we've been working together for about a year.\n\nWe met in the first week of the course, when the lecturer put us in a pair for a presentation on public procurement. At that point we hardly knew each other, but the presentation went so well that we decided to keep collaborating.\n\nNowadays, we share a lot of tasks. We read the same articles and then compare our notes. She is better at structuring arguments, whereas I tend to look for data and examples, so we cover each other's weaknesses. Before every deadline, we also read each other's drafts and give honest feedback.\n\nWhat makes us such a good team is mainly trust. If one of us doesn't understand something, we say so immediately without feeling embarrassed. On top of that, we have similar standards: neither of us enjoys leaving things until the last minute.\n\nI think this partnership has improved my work considerably. I used to be quite stubborn about my ideas, but now I'm more open to criticism. All in all, it's the kind of cooperation I hope to find in my future career as well.","phr":["we hardly knew each other","whereas I tend to...","cover each other's weaknesses","On top of that,","What makes us such a good team is..."],"tipAr":"استخدم whereas وtend to لتُظهر مقارنة بين شخصيتين بأسلوب أكثر نضجاً، فهذا يرفع درجة التنوع في القواعد."},{"id":"sp2-12","l":"B2","card":"Describe a person you admire for what they have achieved.","pts":["who the person is","what they have achieved","how you learned about them","and explain why you admire them"],"model":"The person I'd like to describe is a scientist from my region who built her career in medical research despite very limited resources. I don't know her personally, but I've followed her story for several years.\n\nShe grew up in a small town without a proper laboratory, and she had to study in a second language. Even so, she won a scholarship, completed her doctorate abroad and later returned to open a small research unit at a public university. Her team has developed a low-cost test that helps clinics detect a common disease earlier.\n\nI first heard about her in a documentary that a lecturer recommended in my first year. Afterwards, I read several interviews in which she spoke openly about failure, which I found very refreshing.\n\nThe reason I admire her is twofold. First, she was extremely persistent: she was rejected many times before her work was accepted. Second, she chose to share her knowledge rather than keep it for herself, training dozens of young researchers for free.\n\nHer example shows me that talent alone is not enough; what really matters is patience and a sense of responsibility towards others. That's why she has become a kind of role model in my own academic journey.","phr":["despite very limited resources","Even so,","The reason I admire her is twofold.","rather than keep it for herself","a kind of role model"],"tipAr":"في وصف شخص تُعجب به، قدّم سببين واضحين (First... Second...) فهذا يمنح إجابتك بنية قوية ويسهّل عليك الاسترسال."},{"id":"sp2-13","l":"B2","card":"Describe a market or shopping place you have visited.","pts":["where it is","when you went there","what you saw and bought","and explain what you liked or disliked about it"],"model":"I'd like to describe the weekly market in a small town near my hometown. It takes place every Friday in a large open square, and people come from all the surrounding villages to buy and sell.\n\nI went there last spring with my aunt, who wanted to buy vegetables and some fabric. We arrived quite early, at around eight o'clock, because she said the best products disappear first.\n\nThe moment we got there, I was struck by the colours and the noise. There were stalls with piles of oranges, fresh herbs and olives, and sellers were shouting prices to attract customers. Further along, there were stands for spices, second-hand books and even old tools. In the end, I bought a pair of leather shoes and a small bag of dates, and my aunt got a very good price for her fabric after some friendly bargaining.\n\nWhat I liked most was the atmosphere. Everyone seemed to know each other, and there was a real sense of community. On the other hand, it was extremely crowded, and I had to watch my bag carefully. There was also quite a lot of rubbish on the ground by midday.\n\nOverall, it was a lively and authentic experience, and I would happily go again.","phr":["takes place","I was struck by...","Further along,","a real sense of community","On the other hand,"],"tipAr":"البطاقة تطلب ما أعجبك وما لم يعجبك؛ لا تنسَ ذكر جانب سلبي واحد باستعمال On the other hand لتبدو إجابتك متوازنة."},{"id":"sp2-14","l":"B2","card":"Describe a building you find interesting.","pts":["where the building is","what it looks like","what it is used for","and explain why you find it interesting"],"model":"The building I'd like to talk about is the old courthouse in the centre of my city. It was built during the early twentieth century, and it stands right next to the main square, so almost everyone passes by it every day.\n\nIn terms of appearance, it is quite impressive. It has a wide stone staircase, tall columns at the entrance and a large clock above the main door. The walls are a pale yellow colour, and the windows are decorated with iron patterns. Inside, there is a big hall with a high ceiling, which gives you the impression that justice is something serious and important.\n\nToday, it is still used as a court, mainly for civil cases. A few years ago, I visited it with my class to watch a hearing, and that was the first time I saw a real trial.\n\nThe reason it fascinates me is that it combines history with something I'm really passionate about. When I stood in the hall, I tried to imagine all the people who had come there over the decades, hoping for a fair decision. It made me realise how much responsibility lawyers and judges carry.\n\nTo sum up, it's more than a beautiful structure; for me it is a symbol of the values I want to work for.","phr":["In terms of appearance,","it gives you the impression that...","is still used as...","The reason it fascinates me is that...","more than a beautiful structure"],"tipAr":"ابدأ بالوصف المادي (الشكل واللون) ثم انتقل إلى الوظيفة ثم الأثر الشخصي؛ هذا ترتيب مريح للمستمع."},{"id":"sp2-15","l":"B2","card":"Describe a useful tool or piece of technology that you use regularly.","pts":["what it is","how you started using it","what you use it for","and explain why it is useful to you"],"model":"The piece of technology I rely on most is my laptop, and more specifically, a note-taking application that I run on it. I use it every single day for my studies and my research.\n\nI started using it about two years ago. A classmate showed it to me when I was complaining that my paper notes were completely disorganised. At first I was sceptical because I had always preferred writing by hand, but after a week I was hooked.\n\nNowadays, I use it for almost everything. I store my lecture notes, link ideas between different articles and keep a list of sources for my thesis. I can also search for any word in seconds, which is incredible when you have hundreds of pages. Besides, it works offline, which is essential where the internet connection is not always stable.\n\nThe main reason it's so useful is that it saves me a huge amount of time and reduces stress. Before, I would waste half an hour looking for a single reference. Now I find it in a few seconds.\n\nHaving said that, I try not to depend on it too much. I still write down my most important thoughts by hand, because I remember them better that way.","phr":["I rely on...","I was hooked.","Nowadays,","Besides,","Having said that,"],"tipAr":"عبارة Having said that مفيدة جداً لإضافة تحفّظ بسيط بعد رأي إيجابي، وتُظهر قدرتك على التفكير المتوازن."},{"id":"sp2-16","l":"B2","card":"Describe a competition or contest you took part in.","pts":["what the competition was","when and where it took place","what you had to do","and explain how you felt about the result"],"model":"I'd like to talk about a university debate competition that I took part in two years ago. It was organised by the faculty of law and included teams from five different universities.\n\nIt took place over a weekend in a big lecture hall in the capital. My team consisted of three students, and I was the second speaker. Before the event, we had prepared for almost a month, meeting every evening after class.\n\nThe format was quite demanding. We were given the topic, whether public officials should declare their wealth, only fifteen minutes before each round, and we had to prepare arguments for one side, even if we personally disagreed. In the final round, I had to respond to the other team's points on the spot, which was extremely stressful.\n\nAs for the result, we came second. Naturally, I was a bit disappointed at first because we had been so close to winning. However, after a day or two, I realised how much I had gained. My public speaking improved enormously, and I learnt to listen carefully to opponents instead of just waiting for my turn.\n\nLooking back, I'm proud of the effort, and I'm planning to take part again next year.","phr":["took part in","consisted of","on the spot","we came second","Looking back,"],"tipAr":"عند الحديث عن نتيجة غير مثالية، اذكر شعورك الأول ثم ما استفدته؛ هذه التفاصيل تُظهر نضجاً في الإجابة."},{"id":"sp2-17","l":"B2","card":"Describe a memorable journey you made.","pts":["where you went","who you travelled with","what happened on the way","and explain why the journey was memorable"],"model":"I'd like to describe a long train journey I took across the south of the country with two close friends. It happened in the summer after our second year at university, and we were heading to the desert region to see some historical sites.\n\nWe set off early in the morning, and the trip was supposed to take about ten hours. Unfortunately, the train broke down in the middle of nowhere, and we had to wait for almost three hours in the heat. At first we were quite irritated, but then some other passengers shared their food and tea with us, and the atmosphere changed completely. We ended up singing and chatting with strangers.\n\nWhen we finally arrived, it was already dark. We went outside and looked up at the sky, and I had never seen so many stars in my life. The next day, we visited ancient rock paintings, which was the highlight of the trip.\n\nThe reason this journey stays in my memory is that things didn't go according to plan, yet it turned out to be better than expected. It taught me that unexpected problems can sometimes create the best stories, and that kindness from strangers can make a long wait feel short.","phr":["set off","was supposed to take...","broke down","ended up + verb-ing","didn't go according to plan"],"tipAr":"الجمل مثل was supposed to وended up تضيف مستوى لغوياً أعلى؛ حاول استعمال اثنتين منها على الأقل."},{"id":"sp2-18","l":"B2","card":"Describe a difficult decision you made.","pts":["what the decision was","when you had to make it","how you made up your mind","and explain whether you think it was the right decision"],"model":"The decision I'd like to describe is choosing to continue with a master's degree in a field that was not my original specialty. I had studied accounting and finance, so most people expected me to look for a job in a bank or an accounting firm.\n\nI had to decide about a year ago, just after graduating. At that time, I'd received a small job offer, and the salary was reasonable. However, I had become more and more interested in transparency and the fight against corruption, and I knew that a master's in this area would give me a chance to do research.\n\nTo make up my mind, I did several things. First, I wrote a list of advantages and disadvantages. Then I talked to two lecturers and an older cousin who works in an office. In the end, my own curiosity decided it: I realised that I would regret it more if I did not try.\n\nLooking back, I'm convinced it was the right choice, even though it hasn't always been easy. Money has been tight, and I sometimes doubt myself. Nevertheless, I'm learning things that truly motivate me, and I've found a clear direction for my future. That feeling is worth more than a quick salary.","phr":["I had to decide...","make up my mind","In the end,","even though it hasn't always been easy","Nevertheless,"],"tipAr":"عند الحديث عن قرار، اذكر الخيارات التي كانت أمامك ثم كيف رجّحت أحدها، وأنهِ بتقييم صريح فيه إيجابيات وصعوبات."},{"id":"sp2-19","l":"B2","card":"Describe a skill you learnt and are proud of.","pts":["what the skill is","how you learnt it","what difficulties you faced","and explain why you are proud of it"],"model":"The skill I'm most proud of is programming, and in particular, writing small tools that automate repetitive tasks. I started learning it about three years ago, mostly because I was curious about how software works.\n\nI learnt mainly by myself. I followed free video courses in the evenings and then tried to build small projects, like a script that sorts my files or one that checks a document for repeated sentences. Occasionally, I asked for advice in online forums, and people were surprisingly helpful.\n\nThat said, it wasn't always smooth. For the first few months, my programs kept crashing, and I couldn't understand the error messages. Once, I spent an entire weekend looking for a mistake, and it turned out to be a missing comma. I nearly gave up several times, especially during exams.\n\nWhat makes me proud is that I've kept going despite those problems. Today, I can build useful tools that save me hours of work, and I even helped a friend organise the data for his thesis. Beyond that, it has changed how I think: I now break big problems into small steps.\n\nIn short, it's a skill that has opened many doors for me, and I'm still improving it every week.","phr":["I'm particularly proud of...","mostly because...","That said,","it turned out to be...","Beyond that,"],"tipAr":"لا بأس أن تذكر الصعوبات والأخطاء الصغيرة؛ فهي تجعل القصة طبيعية وتمنحك مفردات إضافية مثل crash وgive up."},{"id":"sp2-20","l":"B2","card":"Describe a mistake you made and what you learnt from it.","pts":["what the mistake was","when and where it happened","how you realised it was a mistake","and explain what you learnt from it"],"model":"I'd like to talk about a mistake I made while preparing an important assignment last year. I was asked to write a short report for a seminar, and I trusted a website that looked very professional without checking who had written it.\n\nIt happened about two weeks before the deadline, in the university library. I collected several statistics from that site and included them in my report without verifying them anywhere else. I was in a hurry, so I convinced myself that it must be correct.\n\nI realised my error during the presentation. A classmate raised her hand and politely asked about my source. When I looked for it, I found that the website didn't mention where the numbers came from. Afterwards, the lecturer told me that two of them were completely outdated. I felt extremely embarrassed.\n\nFortunately, I learnt a great deal from this experience. Firstly, I now always check at least two reliable sources before using any figure. Secondly, I begin my assignments earlier so that I'm not tempted to rush. Finally, I understood that admitting a mistake openly is far better than hiding it.\n\nIn a way, I'm almost grateful it happened, because it made me a more careful researcher.","phr":["I trusted... without checking","It happened about... before the deadline","I realised my error during...","I learnt a great deal from...","In a way,"],"tipAr":"ختم الإجابة بما تعلمته هو الجزء الأهم في هذه البطاقة؛ خصّص له ثلث الوقت تقريباً واستعمل Firstly وSecondly وFinally."},{"id":"sp2-21","l":"B2","card":"Describe a friend or acquaintance who is good at something.","pts":["who the person is","what they are good at","how they developed this ability","and explain how it has affected you"],"model":"I'd like to talk about an old school friend who is remarkably good at drawing. We've known each other since primary school, and although we now study different subjects, we still meet regularly.\n\nHe can draw almost anything: portraits, buildings, even complicated maps from memory. If you give him a pen and a piece of paper, he'll produce something impressive within minutes.\n\nAs far as I know, he developed this ability through a lot of practice. When he was about ten, his uncle gave him a sketchbook, and since then he has drawn every day. He also watched online tutorials and copied the work of artists he liked. According to him, the secret is to do a little every day rather than a lot once a week.\n\nHis talent has influenced me in a couple of ways. Firstly, he designed the logo for a small project of mine, and it looked far more professional than anything I could have done. Secondly, and more importantly, he has shown me what regular practice can achieve. After watching him, I decided to set aside thirty minutes every morning to improve my writing in English.\n\nI suppose that's the best thing about having talented friends: they quietly push you to become better yourself.","phr":["As far as I know,","through a lot of practice","According to him,","in a couple of ways","I suppose that..."],"tipAr":"عبارات التخفيف مثل As far as I know وI suppose تجعل كلامك طبيعياً وتحميك من تقديم معلومات مؤكدة لا تعرفها."},{"id":"sp2-22","l":"B2","card":"Describe a historical place you have visited.","pts":["where the place is","when and why you visited it","what you saw there","and explain what you learnt from the visit"],"model":"The historical place I'd like to describe is an ancient Roman site not far from my hometown, with stone streets, broken columns and a large open-air theatre. It is one of the best-preserved sites in the region.\n\nI visited it about three years ago on a school trip organised by our history teacher. The purpose was to help us understand how people lived in the area two thousand years ago, and, to be honest, I wasn't expecting much. I thought it would be a boring day of listening to explanations.\n\nHowever, as soon as I entered the theatre, my opinion changed. We could see the rows of seats curving upwards, and the guide explained that the acoustics were so good that someone speaking on the stage could be heard at the very top without shouting. We also walked along the main road, where you can still see the marks of cart wheels in the stones, and visited the remains of public baths.\n\nWhat I learnt is that ancient societies were far more organised than we usually imagine. They had water systems, laws about trade and even public buildings for justice. It made me realise that many of the ideas we discuss today, such as fair rules for everyone, have very deep roots.\n\nThe visit really sparked my interest in history, and I've been back twice since.","phr":["one of the best-preserved sites","to be honest, I wasn't expecting much","as soon as I entered","far more organised than we usually imagine","sparked my interest in..."],"tipAr":"يمكنك ربط المكان التاريخي باهتماماتك (القانون، العدالة) فهذا يجعل إجابتك شخصية ومميزة."},{"id":"sp2-23","l":"B2","card":"Describe a park or green space in your area.","pts":["where it is","what it looks like","what people do there","and explain how important it is for the local community"],"model":"I'd like to describe the main public garden in my town, which lies at the foot of a small hill, about ten minutes' walk from the town centre. It was opened some thirty years ago and has been improved several times since.\n\nIt's quite a large space with tall palm trees, long paths and plenty of benches. There is a small fountain in the middle, surrounded by flower beds, and at the far end there is a playground for children. In spring, the whole garden fills with colour and the smell of orange blossom.\n\nPeople use it in many different ways. Early in the morning, you'll see older men walking slowly and chatting about the news. Around five o'clock, families arrive with their children, and teenagers play football on the grass. Some students, like me, go there with a book to revise for exams in the shade.\n\nI believe it plays an essential role in the community. In a crowded town with many tall buildings, it offers a place to breathe and relax. It also brings people of different ages together, which doesn't happen very often elsewhere. Moreover, it's free, so everyone can enjoy it regardless of their income.\n\nFor all these reasons, I think local authorities should invest even more in places like this.","phr":["lies at the foot of...","plenty of...","surrounded by...","plays an essential role in...","regardless of their income"],"tipAr":"عند الحديث عن أهمية مكان للمجتمع اذكر فائدتين على الأقل: صحية واجتماعية، فهذا يعطي عمقاً للنقطة الأخيرة."},{"id":"sp2-24","l":"B2","card":"Describe an article or film that changed the way you think about something.","pts":["what it was","when and where you came across it","what it was about","and explain how it changed your opinion"],"model":"I'd like to talk about a documentary on how public money is spent, which I watched about a year and a half ago. A lecturer recommended it, and I watched it one evening in my room on my laptop.\n\nThe film follows several journalists who investigate why a hospital in a poor district never received the equipment that had been paid for. Step by step, they trace the money through contracts, offices and companies, and they discover that the paperwork was changed along the way. What impressed me was that they relied on documents and data rather than rumours.\n\nBefore watching it, I used to think that corruption was something obvious, like someone openly taking an envelope. However, the documentary showed me that it's often hidden in complicated procedures that most people never read. That completely changed my perspective. I realised that transparency is not only about punishing people; it's also about designing systems where it's hard to cheat in the first place.\n\nAs a result, I became more interested in how technology and open data can help to detect problems early. In fact, it was one of the main reasons I chose my current field of study.\n\nSo, in a way, a single film shaped the direction of my education.","phr":["Step by step,","relied on... rather than...","I used to think that...","That completely changed my perspective.","As a result,"],"tipAr":"عبارة I used to think... However, ... أسلوب ممتاز لوصف تغيّر الرأي، وتتكرر كثيراً في أسئلة الجزء الثاني."},{"id":"sp2-25","l":"B2","card":"Describe a gift you received that was important to you.","pts":["what the gift was","who gave it to you","when you received it","and explain why it was important to you"],"model":"The gift I'd like to talk about is a second-hand laptop that my older brother gave me when I started university. It wasn't new, and the keyboard had a few worn keys, but it meant the world to me.\n\nHe handed it to me about four years ago, on the evening before I left for the dormitory. We were sitting in the living room, and he said, I bought this with my first salary, and now it's your turn to use it well. I remember the moment clearly because he was always shy about showing his feelings.\n\nAt that time, my family couldn't afford a new computer, so I had been worried about how I would do my assignments. This laptop solved that problem immediately. I used it to write essays, to attend online lectures and, later on, to learn programming and start my research.\n\nIts importance for me is both practical and emotional. Practically, without it I would have struggled much more during my first years. Emotionally, it reminds me that someone believed in me before I had achieved anything.\n\nEven though I've since bought a better machine, I still keep the old one in my drawer, and I will probably never throw it away.","phr":["it meant the world to me","He handed it to me...","couldn't afford","both practical and emotional","Even though..."],"tipAr":"تفاصيل صغيرة مثل جملة قالها الشخص أو مكان تسليم الهدية تجعل القصة مقنعة وتساعدك على الاسترسال."},{"id":"sp2-26","l":"B2","card":"Describe an important event in your life that you celebrated.","pts":["what the event was","who celebrated it with you","what you did","and explain why it was important"],"model":"The event I'd like to describe is my graduation from my bachelor's degree. It took place in July, a little over a year ago, and it was without doubt one of the proudest days of my life.\n\nThe ceremony was held in the university's main hall, and my parents, my two sisters and a couple of close friends came to support me. My mother had bought a new dress for the occasion, and my father wore his best suit, which he normally keeps for weddings.\n\nDuring the ceremony, we all wore long gowns and square hats, and each of us was called to the stage to receive a certificate. When my name was announced, I could hear my sisters clapping loudly from the back. Afterwards, we took dozens of photos in the garden, and the whole family went to a restaurant for a late lunch.\n\nThe reason this day mattered so much is that it represented years of effort, late nights and sacrifices, not just mine but also my family's. I was the first person in my family to finish a university degree, so for my parents it was a dream come true.\n\nLooking back, I think that day gave me the motivation I needed to continue studying, and I still keep the certificate on my desk.","phr":["without doubt","held in...","I was called to the stage","represented years of effort","a dream come true"],"tipAr":"أعطِ تفاصيل حسية (الملابس، التصفيق، الصور) ثم اشرح الأهمية العاطفية؛ هذا يجمع بين الوصف والتعليل."},{"id":"sp2-27","l":"B2","card":"Describe a time when you had to convince someone to do something.","pts":["who you wanted to convince","what you wanted them to do","how you convinced them","and explain how you felt about the result"],"model":"I'd like to talk about the time I convinced my father to let me study away from home. He had always preferred that I stay in my hometown, so when I told him I'd like to enrol in a university in another city, he was not pleased.\n\nIt happened about five years ago, just after I'd passed my final school exams. He was worried about the cost, and he also felt that I was too young to live alone.\n\nTo persuade him, I prepared carefully. First, I made a simple budget, showing that the expenses were manageable if I shared a room with other students. Then I asked my uncle, whom my father respects a lot, to talk to him. Finally, I promised to call every evening and visit every other weekend. I spoke calmly and listened to his fears instead of arguing.\n\nAfter about a week, he agreed on one condition: that I would study seriously. I was overjoyed, but also a little nervous, because suddenly I had a big responsibility to live up to.\n\nLooking back, I'm happy I handled it patiently. If I had simply insisted, we might have ended up with a bad atmosphere at home. Instead, he became one of my strongest supporters.","phr":["to persuade him","First,... Then... Finally,...","instead of arguing","on one condition","live up to"],"tipAr":"عند وصف الإقناع اذكر الخطوات (أعددتُ، طلبتُ مساعدة، وعدتُ) ثم النتيجة؛ ولا تنسَ الحديث عن مشاعرك في النهاية."},{"id":"sp2-28","l":"B2","card":"Describe a project you would like to start in the future.","pts":["what the project is","who would be involved","what you would need to do","and explain why you would like to do it"],"model":"One project I'd really love to start is a free online platform where ordinary people can check how public money is spent in their town. It would show things like road repairs, school buildings and local contracts in a simple, easy-to-read format.\n\nI would begin with a small team of three or four people: a developer, a designer and a researcher who understands public procurement. Ideally, I would also involve a lawyer to make sure that everything we publish is accurate and legal. Later, we could invite volunteers from universities to help us collect data.\n\nIn terms of what I'd have to do, the first step would be to learn more about open data and which documents are publicly available. After that, I'd build a basic version of the website and test it in one town only. If it worked, we could gradually extend it to other regions. Of course, I'd also need some funding, so I'd apply for small grants.\n\nThe reason I'm so keen on this idea is that many people feel powerless when they don't know where the money goes. If information were easier to understand, citizens could ask better questions.\n\nI realise it's an ambitious plan, but I think it's well worth trying, and I've already started taking notes.","phr":["One project I'd really love to start is...","Ideally,","In terms of what I'd have to do,","The reason I'm so keen on... is that...","well worth trying"],"tipAr":"في الحديث عن المستقبل استعمل would وcould وif + الماضي، مثل If information were easier to understand..."},{"id":"sp2-29","l":"B2","card":"Describe a job you would like to do in the future.","pts":["what the job is","what the work involves","what skills or qualifications you would need","and explain why you would like this job"],"model":"The job I'd really like to do one day is university lecturer and researcher in the field of law and technology. I would like to study how artificial intelligence can be used in courts and in analysing legal cases, and also teach young students about it.\n\nAs for the work itself, it would involve three main things. First, I'd carry out research and publish articles. Second, I would prepare and give lectures, and finally I'd supervise students who are writing their own theses. There would also be conferences, where I could meet other researchers from different countries.\n\nIn terms of qualifications, I'd need a doctorate, ideally from a well-known university abroad. I would also need strong academic writing skills, a good level of English and, in my case, a basic understanding of programming so I can follow the technical side. On top of that, patience is essential, because research can be slow.\n\nThe reason this job appeals to me is that it combines my three main interests: law, technology and learning. Moreover, I believe good research can help societies become fairer and more efficient.\n\nIt's a long road, but I'm working towards it step by step.","phr":["The job I'd really like to do one day is...","As for the work itself,","it would involve...","In terms of qualifications,","The reason this job appeals to me is that..."],"tipAr":"استعمل In terms of وAs for لتنظيم الإجابة حول النقاط الأربع، فهي تُظهر لغة أكاديمية مناسبة لمستوى B2."},{"id":"sp2-30","l":"B2","card":"Describe a law or rule you think should be introduced.","pts":["what the law or rule would be","where it should be introduced","how it would work","and explain how it would benefit people"],"model":"If I could introduce a new law, it would be one that requires every public institution to publish its main contracts and budgets online in a simple format. I think it should apply in my own country, but it could work anywhere.\n\nThe way it would work is quite simple. Every ministry, city council and public company would have to upload basic information within thirty days: who received the contract, how much it cost, and when the work should be finished. Citizens could search the information for free, and an independent office would check that institutions respect the deadline. Those who failed to publish would face a fine.\n\nI believe that the benefits would be considerable. Firstly, it would make it harder to hide wasteful or dishonest decisions, because anyone, including journalists and students, could examine the data. Secondly, honest companies would have a fairer chance to compete, since they'd know that the process is visible to everyone. Finally, people would trust public institutions more, which is essential for any healthy society.\n\nOf course, there could be difficulties at the beginning, such as a lack of training or technical problems. Nevertheless, I'm convinced that the long-term advantages would outweigh them.\n\nIn short, openness is one of the simplest and cheapest ways to improve public life.","phr":["If I could introduce a new law, it would be...","The way it would work is...","would face a fine","Firstly,... Secondly,... Finally,...","the long-term advantages would outweigh them"],"tipAr":"في أسئلة القوانين استعمل الجملة الشرطية الثانية (If I could..., it would...) ثم اشرح آلية التطبيق والفائدة."},{"id":"sp2-31","l":"C1","card":"Describe a teacher or mentor who had a lasting influence on your way of thinking.","pts":["who the person is","how you came to know them","what ideas or habits they passed on to you","and explain how they have shaped the way you think today"],"model":"The person who springs to mind is a lecturer in constitutional law who supervised my undergraduate dissertation. I came across him quite by chance: I had gone to his office simply to ask for a reading list, and he ended up questioning my topic for almost an hour.\n\nWhat set him apart was his insistence on precision. He would circle a vague word in my draft and write, What exactly do you mean? At first I found it exasperating, but gradually I understood that unclear language usually conceals unclear thinking. He also taught me to argue against myself, that is, to look for the strongest objection to my own position before anyone else could.\n\nThese habits have profoundly shaped the way I work. Whenever I form an opinion about a policy, I now ask myself what evidence would prove me wrong. Likewise, I try to define my terms before debating them, which has saved me from many pointless arguments.\n\nIf I'm honest, he was not an easy person to impress, and I rarely received praise. Yet that very strictness made his occasional approval meaningful. Looking back, I'd say he gave me something more valuable than knowledge: a method of questioning, which I intend to carry into my doctoral research.","phr":["springs to mind","What set him apart was...","conceals","Whenever I form an opinion...","a method of questioning"],"tipAr":"في مستوى C1 اربط الأفكار بعلاقات سببية (gradually I understood) واستعمل تعبيرات مثل What set him apart was لإبراز الفكرة."},{"id":"sp2-32","l":"C1","card":"Describe a person who has made a positive difference in your community.","pts":["who the person is","what they have done","how people in the community have reacted","and explain what impact their work has had"],"model":"I'd like to describe a retired pharmacist in my neighbourhood who set up a free homework club for children in a rented room above a bakery. I'm not sure he realises how much influence he has had, because he's remarkably modest.\n\nIt started roughly six years ago, when he noticed that several local children were falling behind at school because their parents couldn't afford private lessons. Rather than complaining, he simply asked retired teachers and university students, including me for a while, to volunteer a couple of evenings a week. Today the club welcomes around sixty children, and it has expanded to include basic computer skills.\n\nAs for the reaction, it has been overwhelmingly positive. Parents bring pastries and tea, local shops donate stationery and the municipality has finally offered a larger room. Not everyone was supportive at the outset, though; some neighbours worried about noise, but they came round once they saw the results.\n\nIn terms of impact, the most visible change is that many of those children now pass their exams and aspire to go to university. Less obvious, but equally important, is the sense of solidarity it has created. It demonstrates that a single determined person can make the entire neighbourhood feel more capable.","phr":["Rather than complaining,","fall behind","As for the reaction,","came round","aspire to"],"tipAr":"لا تُكثر الأوصاف العامة؛ استخدم أرقاماً تقريبية وأمثلة (ستون طفلاً، غرفة فوق مخبز) لتجعل الصورة واضحة ومقنعة."},{"id":"sp2-33","l":"C1","card":"Describe a place that has changed significantly over the years.","pts":["where the place is","what it was like in the past","how and why it has changed","and explain how you feel about these changes"],"model":"The place I have in mind is the neighbourhood around my grandparents' house, on the edge of my hometown. When I was a child, it was a quiet area of orchards and dirt roads, where you could hear roosters in the morning and cross the street without looking.\n\nOver the last fifteen years, it has been transformed almost beyond recognition. Most of the orchards have been replaced by apartment blocks, the roads have been paved, and a small shopping centre now stands where we used to play football. The main driver of this change has been population growth: as the town expanded, land became too valuable for farming.\n\nMy feelings about it are decidedly mixed. On the one hand, I can't deny the benefits. There is a clinic within walking distance, buses run regularly, and young families have somewhere affordable to live. On the other hand, I miss the stillness and the strong sense of neighbourliness. People used to know each other by name; now many don't even greet their neighbours.\n\nWhat troubles me most is that the changes were largely unplanned. Had the authorities consulted residents and protected a few green areas, the development might have been more balanced. Nevertheless, I accept that places evolve, and I try to appreciate what the area now offers.","phr":["almost beyond recognition","The main driver of this change has been...","decidedly mixed","On the one hand... On the other hand...","Had the authorities consulted..., ..."],"tipAr":"الجملة الشرطية المعكوسة Had the authorities consulted... تضيف قيمة نحوية عالية؛ جرّب إدخال واحدة منها في إجابتك."},{"id":"sp2-34","l":"C1","card":"Describe a piece of technology that you think has changed society.","pts":["what the technology is","how it works or what it does","how people use it in daily life","and explain what effects it has had, both good and bad"],"model":"The technology I'd like to discuss is the smartphone, which I would argue has altered society more profoundly than almost any other invention of my lifetime. Essentially, it's a small computer, a camera and a connection to the internet, all in one device that fits in a pocket.\n\nPeople use it for far more than calls. They pay bills, follow the news, find directions, attend lessons and even file official requests to public offices. In rural areas of my country, many families first accessed the internet through a phone rather than a computer, which tells you a lot about its reach.\n\nThe benefits are considerable. Information that was once available only to a privileged few is now at everyone's fingertips, and citizens can document events instantly, which strengthens accountability. Small businesses can also reach customers without renting a shop.\n\nThat said, there are serious downsides. Many people struggle to concentrate for long periods, and misinformation spreads rapidly. Furthermore, constant notifications can erode genuine face-to-face conversation; you often see friends sitting together in silence, each staring at a screen.\n\nOn balance, I regard it as a powerful tool whose value depends largely on how deliberately we use it, and I try to switch mine off when I study.","phr":["I would argue that...","tells you a lot about...","at everyone's fingertips","That said,","On balance,"],"tipAr":"البطاقة تطلب الإيجابيات والسلبيات معاً؛ خصّص فقرة لكل منهما واختم بعبارة On balance لتُظهر رأيك."},{"id":"sp2-35","l":"C1","card":"Describe a time when you had to work under pressure to meet a deadline.","pts":["what you had to do","why the deadline was tight","how you managed the pressure","and explain what you learnt about yourself"],"model":"I recall a week last winter when I had to submit a full research proposal for a scholarship application. The call closed on a Friday at noon, but because of a misunderstanding about the date, I only realised on Monday that the deadline was so close.\n\nThe proposal needed a clear research question, a literature review, a methodology and a timeline, and I had barely started. On top of that, I had two exams that same week, so time was extremely scarce.\n\nTo cope, I drew up a strict schedule. I divided each day into blocks: mornings for revising, afternoons for writing and evenings for editing. I also switched off my phone and asked a friend to read my drafts, because by Thursday I could no longer see my own mistakes. Admittedly, I slept very little, and there was a moment when I seriously considered withdrawing.\n\nIn the end, I submitted it with twenty minutes to spare. As for what I learnt, I discovered that I perform better under structure than under panic, and that asking for help is a strength rather than a weakness. At the same time, I promised myself never to rely on last-minute effort again, and I now set my own deadlines a week early.","phr":["I recall a week when...","On top of that,","drew up a strict schedule","with twenty minutes to spare","a strength rather than a weakness"],"tipAr":"اذكر ما تعلمته عن نفسك بصيغة ملموسة (أعمل أفضل بنظام واضح)، ثم أضف قراراً عملياً اتخذته بعدها."},{"id":"sp2-36","l":"C1","card":"Describe an occasion when you had to make a choice between two options.","pts":["what the two options were","what influenced your thinking","which option you chose","and explain whether you are satisfied with your choice"],"model":"The occasion that comes to mind was when I had to choose between two scholarships for a short summer programme. One was in a technology company's training centre, focusing on data analysis; the other was at a law school, where the course dealt with human rights and anti-corruption.\n\nBoth were attractive for different reasons. The first would have given me practical skills that employers value, and the second matched my academic interests more closely. What complicated things was that I couldn't accept both, because the dates overlapped.\n\nI was influenced by three factors. Firstly, I asked myself which experience would be harder to obtain later. I could learn data analysis through online courses, but a specialised legal programme, with experts and a network of students, was a rare opportunity. Secondly, I spoke to a lecturer, who advised me to follow my real curiosity. Thirdly, I simply imagined myself a year later and asked which choice I'd regret not making.\n\nIn the end, I opted for the law school, and I'm largely satisfied. The programme gave me contacts and ideas that still feed my research. Admittedly, I still wonder what the other path would have offered, yet I've since taught myself the basics of data analysis anyway.","phr":["The occasion that comes to mind was...","What complicated things was that...","Firstly,... Secondly,... Thirdly,...","I opted for...","would have offered"],"tipAr":"استعمل صيغة would have + اسم الفاعل الثالث (would have given) للحديث عن الخيار الذي لم تأخذه، فهي علامة على إتقان أعلى."},{"id":"sp2-37","l":"C1","card":"Describe an experience that tested your patience.","pts":["what the experience was","when and where it happened","how you reacted","and explain what the experience taught you"],"model":"I'd like to talk about an experience that tested my patience to the limit: waiting an entire day at a government office to renew an official document. It happened about two years ago, and I had assumed it would take no more than an hour.\n\nI arrived at eight in the morning to find a queue already stretching out of the door. Inside, there was only one open counter, the computer system kept freezing, and nobody could tell us how long it would take. Every few minutes, an employee would reappear to say that we had to be patient, which, unsurprisingly, did little to calm people down.\n\nAs for my reaction, I started off quite irritated and checked the time constantly. Eventually, I realised that getting angry wasn't helping anyone, least of all me. So I took out a book and began talking to the man beside me, a retired driver who had been through this many times. His calm attitude was contagious.\n\nI finally received my document at around four o'clock. What the experience taught me is that frustration is often a choice, and that the real issue lies in poorly designed procedures. In fact, it strengthened my conviction that public services should be simpler and more efficient.","phr":["tested my patience to the limit","did little to calm people down","least of all me","His calm attitude was contagious.","the real issue lies in..."],"tipAr":"يمكنك تحويل موقف بسيط (طابور في مكتب) إلى فكرة أعمق ترتبط باهتماماتك، وهذا يضيف عمقاً لإجابتك دون تكلف."},{"id":"sp2-38","l":"C1","card":"Describe a goal you set yourself that required long-term commitment.","pts":["what the goal was","how you planned to achieve it","what obstacles you encountered","and explain what keeps you motivated"],"model":"The goal I've been pursuing for the past two years is to build an international research record, so that I can eventually be admitted to a doctoral programme abroad. It's a long-term undertaking, and it requires consistency more than brilliance.\n\nMy plan has three strands. First, I read and write for at least two hours every day, regardless of how tired I feel. Second, I'm working on a number of articles, aiming to submit them to academic journals. Third, I'm gradually improving my English to a level where I can present my ideas confidently at conferences.\n\nNaturally, I've encountered obstacles. Money is a constant concern, and as an independent researcher without an institution behind me, I sometimes struggle to access journals or find reviewers. There have also been moments of doubt, particularly after receiving rejection emails, which can be quite discouraging.\n\nWhat keeps me going is a combination of curiosity and purpose. I'm genuinely fascinated by the questions I'm exploring, and I believe my work could one day contribute to fairer institutions. Moreover, I remind myself that every rejection contains feedback I can use.\n\nBy keeping the goal in view and celebrating small progress, I've found that the journey itself has become rewarding.","phr":["pursuing a goal","requires consistency more than brilliance","My plan has three strands.","a constant concern","every rejection contains feedback"],"tipAr":"الأهداف طويلة المدى تحتاج الحاضر التام المستمر (I've been pursuing) فاستعمله لتُظهر استمرارية الجهد حتى اليوم."},{"id":"sp2-39","l":"C1","card":"Describe a social or environmental problem you would like to help solve.","pts":["what the problem is","who it affects","what could be done about it","and explain why you personally care about it"],"model":"The problem I feel strongly about is the lack of transparency in the way public contracts are awarded. It is not a flashy issue, yet it silently affects the quality of schools, roads and hospitals in many countries, including mine.\n\nIt affects ordinary citizens first and foremost, particularly those who rely on public services because they cannot pay for private alternatives. When money is wasted or diverted, they are the ones who suffer from overcrowded classrooms and poorly equipped clinics. Honest businesses are affected too, since they lose out to those with connections.\n\nAs for solutions, I'd suggest a multi-pronged approach. Governments could publish all tenders and results online in an accessible format. Independent bodies should be given the authority and resources to audit them. Technology can also play a role: for instance, software could flag unusual patterns, such as the same company always winning, so that investigators know where to look. Ultimately, though, none of it works without informed citizens and protected whistle-blowers.\n\nPersonally, I care deeply about this because I've seen how a poorly built school can shape a child's prospects. It also connects directly to my studies, which gives me the sense that I'm preparing for work that truly matters.","phr":["first and foremost","lose out to","a multi-pronged approach","play a role","Ultimately, though,"],"tipAr":"اختر مشكلة تعرفها جيداً من دراستك؛ المعرفة الحقيقية بالموضوع تتيح لك مفردات دقيقة وحججاً مقنعة."},{"id":"sp2-40","l":"C1","card":"Describe an idea you have that you believe could improve the way people learn.","pts":["what the idea is","how it would work in practice","what resources it would need","and explain why you believe it would be effective"],"model":"The idea I'd like to describe is an offline learning assistant for students in areas with unreliable internet. It would be an app, installed once, containing structured lessons, exercises and speaking practice, along with feedback that does not depend on a connection.\n\nIn practice, a learner would follow a short daily plan, say, twenty minutes of vocabulary, a listening task and a few sentences to repeat aloud. The app would record how often each word has been reviewed and bring back the ones that the learner tends to forget, using a technique called spaced repetition. Because everything would run on the phone, it could be used on a bus, in a village or during a power cut.\n\nAs for resources, we would need a small team to prepare high-quality content, a developer and, ideally, teachers to check the material. Costs could be kept low, since there are no servers to maintain.\n\nI believe it would be effective for two reasons. Firstly, regular short sessions are proven to help memory far more than occasional long ones. Secondly, it removes the barrier of cost and connectivity, which excludes so many talented learners.\n\nUltimately, I see it as a modest way of making education more equal, and I'm already experimenting with a prototype.","phr":["in practice","a technique called...","tends to forget","Ultimately,","a modest way of making... more equal"],"tipAr":"عندما تشرح فكرة جديدة قدّم مثالاً ملموساً عن يوم المتعلّم (twenty minutes of vocabulary...) فهذا يجعلها واقعية ومقنعة."}]);
Fasih.add("sp3",[{"id":"sp3-01","l":"B2","topic":"Education","qs":[{"q":"Why do some students find it hard to stay motivated at school?","a":"Well, I'd say it's mostly because lessons can feel disconnected from real life. If pupils can't see why algebra matters, they switch off. For example, my cousin hated maths until a teacher showed him how engineers use it to design bridges. After that, he started working harder. So relevance is the key, I think."},{"q":"Do you think exams are the best way to measure what a student knows?","a":"Not really, to be honest. Exams test memory under pressure, which isn't the same as understanding. A student might panic and forget everything. That said, they're fair and simple to organise. On the whole, I'd prefer a mix, such as exams plus projects, so that different strengths can show up."},{"q":"How important is the teacher's personality in the classroom?","a":"I'd say it's very important. A friendly, patient teacher makes students feel safe to ask questions, and that boosts learning. I remember a geography teacher who told funny stories about every country. We all paid attention and remembered the facts. Knowledge matters, of course, but personality is what keeps the lesson alive."},{"q":"Should schools teach practical skills like cooking or managing money?","a":"Yes, definitely. Many young adults leave school knowing history dates but not how to budget a monthly salary. Teaching basic finance would prevent a lot of stress later, for instance with credit cards or rent. On the whole, I think schools should prepare pupils for life, not only for exams, so these lessons would be really valuable."}]},{"id":"sp3-02","l":"B2","topic":"Technology","qs":[{"q":"How has technology changed the way people communicate?","a":"Well, it has made communication much faster and cheaper. Twenty years ago, you waited weeks for a letter from abroad; now you can video-call your brother in seconds. That said, I think conversations have become shallower. People send short messages instead of talking properly. So we gained speed, but perhaps we lost some depth."},{"q":"Do you think people depend too much on their phones?","a":"Yes, I'd say so. Many people check their phone every few minutes without even noticing. At restaurants, you often see a whole family looking at screens instead of talking. It's convenient, of course, but this habit can harm concentration and relationships. On the whole, we should set limits, like keeping phones away during meals."},{"q":"Is it a good idea for young children to use tablets?","a":"In moderation, yes. Educational apps can teach letters and numbers in a fun way. However, too much screen time can affect sleep and attention. For example, a neighbour's child now struggles to sit still for a story. I'd say parents should choose the content carefully and limit use to about an hour daily."},{"q":"What new technology would you like to see in the next ten years?","a":"I'd love to see cheap, reliable translation devices that work offline. Imagine travelling anywhere and speaking naturally with local people. This would help students, tourists and workers alike. For instance, a patient could explain symptoms to a foreign doctor easily. On the whole, I believe such tools would bring people closer together."}]},{"id":"sp3-03","l":"B2","topic":"Work","qs":[{"q":"What makes a job satisfying, in your opinion?","a":"Well, I'd say feeling useful matters most. A good salary is important, but if you feel your work helps people, you go home happier. For example, a nurse I know earns an average wage, yet she loves her job because patients thank her every day. So purpose, respect and fair pay together create satisfaction."},{"q":"Do you think people should change jobs often?","a":"It depends. Changing jobs can teach you new skills and raise your salary. However, moving too often may look unreliable to employers. On the whole, I'd say staying three to five years is sensible: long enough to learn properly, short enough to keep growing. My uncle did exactly that and built a strong career."},{"q":"Is working from home better than working in an office?","a":"Each has advantages. At home, you save time on travelling and can concentrate quietly. That said, you miss the chance to chat and solve problems quickly with colleagues. A friend of mine felt lonely after a year at home. I think a hybrid system, two days at home and three in the office, works best."},{"q":"How can employers keep their staff happy?","a":"Well, fair pay is the starting point, but it isn't everything. Employers should listen to staff and offer chances to learn new skills. For example, a company that pays for training courses shows it values its people. Also, flexible hours help parents. On the whole, respect keeps employees loyal more than money alone."}]},{"id":"sp3-04","l":"B2","topic":"The environment","qs":[{"q":"Who should be responsible for protecting the environment, individuals or governments?","a":"I'd say both, but governments carry the bigger responsibility. Individuals can recycle or save water, which helps, but only governments can pass laws that force factories to stop polluting. For instance, a ban on plastic bags changed habits in my city overnight. So people should act, yet strong rules make the real difference."},{"q":"Why do some people not care about environmental problems?","a":"Well, probably because the damage often feels distant. If your street is clean, a melting glacier seems unimportant. Also, daily problems like rent and prices take priority. That said, when people see the effects locally, such as a polluted river, they start caring. So I think education and visible examples are really needed."},{"q":"Do you think it is realistic to ask people to use less energy?","a":"Yes, if it's made easy and rewarding. Telling people to sacrifice comfort rarely works. However, offering cheaper bills for energy-saving lamps or good insulation does. For example, my neighbours installed solar panels once the government gave a discount. On the whole, small incentives work better than strict warnings."},{"q":"How can cities become greener?","a":"Well, planting more trees and creating parks would be a simple start. Cities could also encourage cycling with safe lanes and reduce car traffic in the centre. For example, some European cities have closed central streets to cars, and the air became cleaner. I'd say green spaces also make residents healthier and happier."}]},{"id":"sp3-05","l":"B2","topic":"Cities","qs":[{"q":"Why do so many people move from villages to big cities?","a":"Mainly for jobs and opportunities. Cities offer better universities, hospitals and a wider choice of work. For example, many young people from small towns in my country go to the capital to find a good position. That said, they often face high rents. On the whole, hope for a better future drives the move."},{"q":"What are the main problems of living in a large city?","a":"Well, I'd say traffic and housing costs come first. Commuting can take two hours a day, which is exhausting. Also, noise and pollution affect health. A friend of mine pays half his salary on a tiny flat. So, although cities are exciting, life there can be stressful and expensive."},{"q":"Do you think old buildings should be protected or replaced with modern ones?","a":"I'd say the important ones should be protected, because they tell the story of a city. Tourists visit old streets, not glass towers. That said, if a building is dangerous or empty, replacing it makes sense. For example, an old market near me became a lovely library. So, a balance is best."},{"q":"How can a city make life better for its elderly residents?","a":"Well, simple things help a lot, such as benches in the street, ramps and clear signs. Cheap buses and nearby clinics matter too. For instance, my grandmother stopped going out when the pavement near her house was broken. After repairs, she walked to the shop again. On the whole, small details change older people's independence."}]},{"id":"sp3-06","l":"B2","topic":"Health","qs":[{"q":"Why do many people find it difficult to lead a healthy lifestyle?","a":"Well, I'd say it's largely about time and habit. People work long hours, so they grab fast food and skip exercise. Also, unhealthy snacks are cheap and everywhere. For example, my colleague wants to lose weight but eats lunch at his desk every day. So changing routine, not knowledge, is the real challenge."},{"q":"Is prevention more important than treatment?","a":"Definitely, in my view. Preventing illness is cheaper and less painful than curing it. For example, regular check-ups can detect high blood pressure before it causes a heart attack. That said, we obviously still need good hospitals. On the whole, governments should spend more on health education and screening programmes."},{"q":"Do you think mental health is taken seriously enough?","a":"Not yet, I'd say. Many people still feel ashamed to admit they are anxious or depressed, so they suffer silently. A friend of mine waited two years before speaking to a specialist. That said, awareness is growing. On the whole, schools and workplaces should talk about it openly to remove the stigma."},{"q":"Should unhealthy food be taxed?","a":"I think it could help, to be honest. A higher price on sugary drinks makes people think twice before buying them, and the money could fund hospitals. That said, the tax must not hurt poor families. For instance, healthy options should become cheaper at the same time. On the whole, it's worth trying."}]},{"id":"sp3-07","l":"B2","topic":"Family","qs":[{"q":"How has family life changed in the last few decades?","a":"Well, families are generally smaller now, and many parents both work. Fifty years ago, grandparents often lived in the same house, but today relatives may be in different cities. For example, my aunt raises her children alone because her family lives far away. So, we have more independence but less daily support."},{"q":"Is it better for children to be brought up in a big family?","a":"I'd say there are real benefits. Children learn to share, negotiate and help each other. For instance, in my family the older kids look after the younger ones, which teaches responsibility. That said, parents of large families have less time and money for each child. On the whole, it depends on the circumstances."},{"q":"What can parents do to stay close to their teenage children?","a":"Well, the key is listening without judging. Teenagers close up if every conversation becomes a lecture. Parents could share activities, like cooking or watching a match together. For example, my friend's father took him fishing every weekend, and they still talk openly today. On the whole, regular time together builds trust."},{"q":"Do you think grandparents should help raise grandchildren?","a":"Yes, within limits. Grandparents offer love, patience and stories that parents sometimes lack time for. For instance, my grandfather taught me proverbs and how to play chess. That said, the parents must stay in charge of the main rules. On the whole, it's a wonderful bond if everyone respects each other's role."}]},{"id":"sp3-08","l":"B2","topic":"Media","qs":[{"q":"Do you think people trust the news less nowadays?","a":"Yes, I'd say so. With social media, anyone can publish a story, and false information spreads quickly. For example, I recently saw a fake report about a food shortage that made people panic in the shops. That said, reliable newspapers still exist. On the whole, readers must check the source before believing a headline."},{"q":"How does advertising influence what people buy?","a":"Well, it influences us more than we admit. Adverts create the feeling that we need a product, often by linking it to happiness. For instance, a sports shoe advert shows a smiling champion, so people imagine they will run faster too. So I'd say we should ask ourselves whether we truly need the item."},{"q":"Should there be limits on what children can watch on screens?","a":"Yes, I think so. Some programmes show violence or unrealistic lifestyles that confuse young minds. Parents can use filters and choose suitable channels. For example, my sister lets her son watch nature documentaries but not action films. That said, banning everything doesn't work; discussing what they see is also important."},{"q":"Is it a good idea for famous people to share their private lives online?","a":"Not always. It can make them feel closer to their fans, but it also removes their privacy and pushes them to behave for attention. For example, some celebrities post every meal and then face harsh criticism. On the whole, I'd say keeping a part of life private is healthier."}]},{"id":"sp3-09","l":"B2","topic":"Travel","qs":[{"q":"Why do people enjoy travelling to foreign countries?","a":"Well, I think curiosity is the main reason. People want to see new landscapes, taste different food and experience another way of life. For instance, a friend of mine returned from Turkey full of stories about its markets and history. That said, travelling also helps us relax and escape routine for a while."},{"q":"Do you think tourism is good for local communities?","a":"Mostly yes, because it creates jobs in hotels, restaurants and transport. However, it can raise prices and damage nature. For example, a quiet fishing village I visited became crowded and expensive after it became famous. On the whole, tourism helps if the number of visitors is managed carefully."},{"q":"Is it better to travel with a group or alone?","a":"I'd say it depends on your personality. In a group, you share costs and feel safe, which is great for a first trip. That said, travelling alone gives complete freedom to choose your schedule and meet local people. Personally, I'd prefer a small group of friends, so there's company and flexibility."},{"q":"How might travel change in the future?","a":"Well, I expect it to become more eco-friendly. Airlines are already testing cleaner fuels, and trains are improving. Also, virtual reality may let people preview a destination before booking. For instance, you could walk through a hotel from your sofa. On the whole, I think travelling will be easier, but perhaps more expensive."}]},{"id":"sp3-10","l":"B2","topic":"Money","qs":[{"q":"Do you think young people today are good at managing money?","a":"Not always, I'd say. Online shopping and easy payments make spending effortless, so many young people run out of money before the end of the month. For example, my cousin bought a new phone on credit and then struggled with the instalments. That said, some are quite smart and save regularly."},{"q":"Is it important to save money for the future?","a":"Absolutely. Life is unpredictable, and savings give you security if you lose your job or face a medical emergency. For instance, my neighbour could repair his flooded kitchen without borrowing because he had saved for years. On the whole, even small amounts every month make a big difference."},{"q":"Does having more money make people happier?","a":"Up to a point, yes. Money removes worries about food, rent and healthcare, which obviously improves life. However, beyond that, relationships and health matter more. For example, a wealthy relative of mine works constantly and seems exhausted. So I'd say money helps, but it can't replace time with loved ones."},{"q":"Should rich people pay higher taxes?","a":"Generally, I think yes, because they can afford it and society benefits from better schools and hospitals. That said, the system must be fair and transparent, so people trust where the money goes. For example, citizens accept taxes more willingly when they see new roads. On the whole, fairness and openness matter most."}]},{"id":"sp3-11","l":"B2","topic":"Sport","qs":[{"q":"Why is sport good for children?","a":"Well, it keeps them healthy and teaches them teamwork. When a child plays football, he learns to cooperate, accept defeat and keep trying. For example, my nephew was shy until he joined a basketball club; now he has many friends. That said, sport should be fun, not a source of pressure."},{"q":"Should governments spend money on building sports facilities?","a":"I'd say yes, because accessible pitches and pools encourage people to be active, which reduces health costs later. For instance, a free public track in my town is full every evening. That said, the money must also reach schools and hospitals. On the whole, it's a sensible investment if planned well."},{"q":"Do you think professional athletes are paid too much?","a":"Well, it's a difficult question. Their careers are short and demanding, and clubs earn huge profits from them. However, compared with nurses or teachers, the gap seems unfair. For example, one footballer may earn in a week what a teacher earns in ten years. I'd say society's priorities are a little distorted."},{"q":"How does watching sport bring people together?","a":"It creates a shared emotion. Strangers hug when their team scores, and for ninety minutes differences disappear. For instance, whole neighbourhoods here gather in cafés to watch big matches and talk for days afterwards. That said, rivalry can sometimes turn aggressive. On the whole, though, I think it unites more than it divides."}]},{"id":"sp3-12","l":"B2","topic":"Culture","qs":[{"q":"Why is it important to preserve traditional customs?","a":"Well, traditions connect us to our roots and give a community its identity. If we lose them, we lose part of our history. For example, the way my grandmother prepares couscous for celebrations brings the whole family together. That said, customs can evolve; they don't have to stay frozen in time."},{"q":"Do you think globalisation is making all cultures similar?","a":"To some extent, yes. You can find the same fast-food chains and films in almost every country. However, local cultures still survive, and some even gain pride by comparison. For example, young people in my area are returning to traditional music in modern styles. So I'd say cultures are mixing, not disappearing."},{"q":"Should museums be free for everyone?","a":"I'd say yes, or at least very cheap. Culture shouldn't depend on income. When museums are free, schoolchildren and families visit more often and learn naturally. For instance, a free museum day in my city attracted thousands. That said, museums need funding, so governments should cover the cost."},{"q":"How can young people be encouraged to take an interest in their heritage?","a":"Well, I think they respond to experiences rather than lectures. School trips to historical sites, storytelling events and short videos on social media can all work. For example, a local festival with crafts workshops attracted many teenagers in my town. So making heritage lively and interactive is the key."}]},{"id":"sp3-13","l":"B2","topic":"Science","qs":[{"q":"Why is scientific research important for society?","a":"Well, it solves real problems and improves daily life. Vaccines, clean water systems and mobile phones all began as research. For instance, studying viruses allowed doctors to stop deadly diseases. That said, results often take years, so governments must be patient. On the whole, I'd say research is the engine of progress."},{"q":"Should scientists be allowed to study anything they want?","a":"Mostly, yes, but with ethical limits. Freedom leads to discoveries nobody predicted. However, some experiments, such as those that harm people, must be controlled by committees. For example, medical trials need patient consent. So I'd say curiosity is valuable, but safety and respect for human beings come first."},{"q":"How can schools make science more interesting?","a":"I'd say through experiments and real-life examples. Reading about chemistry is dull, but seeing a colour change in a flask is exciting. For instance, my teacher once built a small volcano, and we all remembered the lesson. So hands-on activities, visits to labs and good questions make science memorable."},{"q":"Do you think people trust scientists enough?","a":"Not always, I'm afraid. Misleading posts online often make people doubt experts, especially about health. For example, some refused vaccines because of rumours. That said, scientists should explain their work in simple language. On the whole, trust grows when researchers are open and honest about uncertainty."}]},{"id":"sp3-14","l":"B2","topic":"Language learning","qs":[{"q":"Why is it difficult for adults to learn a new language?","a":"Well, adults have less free time and often feel embarrassed about making mistakes. Children just play and absorb the language. For example, my friend moved abroad at thirty and avoided speaking for months because he feared errors. That said, adults understand grammar better, so with regular practice they can progress."},{"q":"Is it necessary to live in a country to learn its language well?","a":"Not necessarily, although it helps. Living there forces you to use the language daily. However, today you can chat with natives online and watch films with subtitles. For instance, I know someone who reached a high level without leaving home. On the whole, motivation matters more than location."},{"q":"Should all children learn a foreign language at primary school?","a":"I think so, because young brains pick up sounds easily. Early learning also opens minds to other cultures. For example, children in some countries learn two languages from the age of six and speak confidently later. That said, lessons must be playful, with songs and games, not endless grammar tables."},{"q":"Will translation apps make language learning unnecessary?","a":"I doubt it. Apps are useful for quick help, but they can't capture tone, humour or cultural meaning. Moreover, speaking directly to someone builds a real relationship. For example, a business partner trusts you more if you try their language. So I'd say apps will assist us, not replace us."}]},{"id":"sp3-15","l":"B2","topic":"The law and justice","qs":[{"q":"Why is it important for everyone to obey the law?","a":"Well, laws protect people's rights and keep society orderly. If everyone chose which rules to follow, there would be chaos. For example, imagine the roads if nobody respected traffic lights. That said, laws must be fair, otherwise people lose respect for them. So obeying and improving the law go together."},{"q":"Do you think punishments are effective in reducing crime?","a":"Partly. Clear punishments discourage some offenders, especially for serious crimes. However, they don't solve root causes like poverty or lack of education. For instance, a young man with no job may be tempted by crime whatever the penalty. I'd say prevention through opportunities works better alongside fair sentences."},{"q":"Should young offenders be treated differently from adults?","a":"Yes, I'd say so. Young people are still developing and can change with proper guidance. Sending them to adult prisons may make them worse. For example, a programme that placed teenagers in training workshops reduced repeat offences in one region. That said, serious crimes still need appropriate consequences."},{"q":"Why do some people lose trust in the justice system?","a":"Mainly because of delays and unequal treatment. If a case takes years, or the powerful seem to escape punishment, ordinary citizens feel hopeless. For instance, a neighbour waited six years for a simple property ruling. On the whole, speed, transparency and equality are essential to rebuild confidence."}]},{"id":"sp3-16","l":"B2","topic":"Honesty and corruption","qs":[{"q":"Why do some people take bribes?","a":"Well, I think it's a mix of greed, low salaries and weak controls. When someone believes they won't be caught, temptation grows. Also, if bribery seems normal, people copy it. For example, an official on a tiny wage might accept money to speed up a document. That said, it's still wrong and harms everybody."},{"q":"How does corruption affect ordinary citizens?","a":"It makes life more expensive and unfair. Money meant for hospitals or roads disappears, so services get worse. For instance, a bridge built with cheap materials because of dishonest contracts can collapse. On the whole, honest people pay the price, and trust in public institutions falls."},{"q":"Is honesty always the best policy?","a":"Generally, yes, because trust takes years to build and seconds to lose. A truthful person earns respect. That said, there are rare situations where kindness matters, such as not criticising a friend's cooking too harshly. For example, I'd always be honest in business, but gentle in personal matters."},{"q":"What can be done to reduce corruption?","a":"Well, transparency is the best tool. When contracts, budgets and decisions are published, it's harder to hide dishonest deals. Also, protecting whistleblowers encourages people to speak up. For example, a public website listing all tenders lets citizens check prices. On the whole, strong laws plus an informed public make a big difference."}]},{"id":"sp3-17","l":"B2","topic":"Artificial intelligence","qs":[{"q":"In what ways can artificial intelligence help in daily life?","a":"Well, it can save time on routine tasks. For example, a navigation app suggests the fastest route, and a translation tool helps you read a foreign website instantly. In healthcare, it can help doctors spot problems in scans. On the whole, it's like a helpful assistant, as long as humans make the final decisions."},{"q":"Do you think AI will take away many jobs?","a":"Some, yes, especially repetitive ones, such as data entry. However, new jobs will appear, like AI trainers and technicians. History shows this pattern: when machines arrived in factories, people moved to different roles. That said, workers will need retraining, so governments should support them with courses and guidance."},{"q":"Can we trust decisions made by AI?","a":"With caution, I'd say. AI learns from data, and if the data is biased, the decisions will be too. For instance, a hiring programme once favoured one group simply because past employees were similar. So humans should check important decisions and explain how the system reached them."},{"q":"Should children be taught about AI at school?","a":"Yes, I believe so. They will grow up surrounded by it, so they should understand what it can and can't do. For example, a lesson on spotting a fake image created by AI would be very useful. That said, basic skills like reading and critical thinking must remain the foundation."}]},{"id":"sp3-18","l":"B2","topic":"Privacy","qs":[{"q":"Why is privacy important to people?","a":"Well, it gives us freedom to be ourselves without being judged or watched. If everyone could read your messages, you'd feel uncomfortable. Privacy also protects us from fraud. For example, if a stranger knows your address and bank details, they can cause real harm. So it's a basic part of dignity and safety."},{"q":"Do you think people share too much personal information online?","a":"Yes, quite often. People post photos of their homes, holidays and children without thinking who might see them. For instance, announcing a long trip online can tell thieves your house is empty. That said, sharing is fun. On the whole, we should check privacy settings and think before posting."},{"q":"Is it acceptable for companies to collect data about their customers?","a":"Only if they are open about it and customers agree. Data can improve services, for example by suggesting useful products. However, secretly selling it is wrong. I'd say clear rules and fines are necessary. On the whole, people should be able to see and delete the information held about them."},{"q":"Should the government be allowed to use cameras in public places?","a":"I'd say yes in busy areas, because cameras can help to prevent and solve crimes. For instance, footage once helped police find a stolen car quickly. That said, there must be strict rules about who views the recordings and for how long. Otherwise, it becomes surveillance rather than protection."}]},{"id":"sp3-19","l":"C1","topic":"Leadership","qs":[{"q":"To what extent are good leaders born rather than made?","a":"I'd argue that leadership is largely developed. Some people have natural charisma, certainly, but it isn't enough on its own. Skills such as listening, delegating and staying calm under pressure can be trained. For example, many successful managers started out as shy employees and improved through mentoring. So talent may start the journey, yet experience shapes the leader."},{"q":"How does leadership in a small team differ from leadership in a large organisation?","a":"Well, in a small team, a leader can know everyone personally and adjust quickly. In a large organisation, communication becomes more formal, and a leader relies on managers to carry the vision. That said, the principle is the same: people must trust you. For instance, a clear mission statement helps thousands of staff move in one direction."},{"q":"Is it more important for a leader to be respected or liked?","a":"Ideally both, but if I had to choose, I'd say respected. A leader who is merely popular may avoid hard decisions, whereas a respected one acts fairly even when it is unpopular. For example, a headteacher who enforces rules consistently eventually earns affection too. So respect tends to produce genuine, lasting goodwill."},{"q":"What might happen to a country whose leaders lack integrity?","a":"The consequences could be severe. Public money may be misused, institutions weakened, and citizens could lose faith in any official promise. Over time, talented people might emigrate, and investors would hesitate. For instance, projects awarded through favouritism are often poorly built. Ultimately, integrity at the top sets the moral tone for the whole society."}]},{"id":"sp3-20","l":"C1","topic":"Young people","qs":[{"q":"What are the biggest pressures facing young people today compared with a generation ago?","a":"I'd say the combination of economic uncertainty and constant online comparison. Previously, a degree almost guaranteed a stable job; now graduates may wait years for one. Meanwhile, social media encourages them to measure their lives against polished images. For instance, many young people feel they are falling behind simply because a peer posts a success story."},{"q":"Do you think young people are less patient than older generations?","a":"Perhaps, although it's a generalisation. Technology has taught them to expect instant answers, from messages to deliveries. That said, many young people show remarkable patience when learning skills like coding or music. So it may be less about age and more about the environment, which rewards speed in certain areas."},{"q":"How can societies make better use of young people's energy and ideas?","a":"Well, by giving them real responsibility rather than token roles. Youth councils, paid internships and start-up funding would allow fresh ideas to be tested. For example, a student-designed recycling scheme could be piloted in a district. On the whole, if we treat young people as partners, society benefits from their creativity."},{"q":"Would it be a good idea to lower the voting age?","a":"It's debatable. Supporters argue that decisions about education and climate affect the young most, so they deserve a voice. Critics worry about maturity. On balance, I'd lean towards allowing it, provided schools teach civic education first. For example, pupils who debate local issues in class would arguably be well prepared."}]},{"id":"sp3-21","l":"C1","topic":"Old people","qs":[{"q":"In what ways can older people contribute to society after they retire?","a":"Well, they hold a wealth of experience that is often wasted. Retired engineers could mentor apprentices, and former teachers might run reading clubs. For example, a retired accountant in my neighbourhood helps small shops with their books for free. On the whole, society gains skills, and the elderly gain purpose, which benefits their health too."},{"q":"Is it the family's responsibility or the state's to look after elderly people?","a":"I'd say it should be shared. Families provide love and daily presence, which no institution can replace. However, caring for an ill parent can be exhausting and costly, so the state should offer support such as nursing visits or pensions. That said, relying solely on the state risks loneliness. A partnership works best."},{"q":"How might ageing populations affect the economy?","a":"They could put pressure on pensions and healthcare, since fewer workers support more retirees. For instance, some countries are already raising the retirement age. That said, older consumers also create demand for new services, like specialised care and leisure. So, with sensible planning, an ageing population need not be a disaster, merely a challenge."},{"q":"Do you think older people and young people have enough contact with each other today?","a":"Probably not. Housing patterns and busy schedules separate generations, so each group knows little about the other. Yet contact is valuable: young people gain wisdom, and the elderly feel included. For example, a school that invited residents of a care home to read with pupils saw both groups benefit. Such projects should be more common."}]},{"id":"sp3-22","l":"C1","topic":"Consumerism","qs":[{"q":"Why do people buy things they do not really need?","a":"I think it's driven by emotion rather than logic. Shopping gives a quick sense of reward, and advertising convinces us that a new item will improve our status or mood. For example, many people upgrade a perfectly good phone just because a newer model appears. In the end, the satisfaction fades quickly, which keeps the cycle going."},{"q":"To what extent is consumerism harmful to the environment?","a":"Considerably, I'd argue. Producing and transporting goods consumes energy, and discarded items create mountains of waste. Fast fashion is a clear example: clothes worn only a few times end up in landfills. That said, consumers have power; if they buy durable products and repair them, companies would adapt. So responsibility is shared."},{"q":"Could a society ever be happy without constant economic growth?","a":"It's conceivable, though it would require a change in values. Happiness depends more on health, community and security than on ever-increasing consumption. For instance, some surveys suggest people in modest but stable communities report high satisfaction. However, without growth, creating jobs becomes harder. So a balance between prosperity and sufficiency seems the most realistic goal."},{"q":"Will people buy fewer physical goods in the future?","a":"Possibly. Younger people increasingly prefer experiences, like travel or concerts, and services such as streaming or car sharing over ownership. For example, many city dwellers no longer buy cars at all. That said, essentials will always be purchased. I'd expect a shift toward quality, rental and second-hand markets rather than a sudden drop."}]},{"id":"sp3-23","l":"C1","topic":"Transport","qs":[{"q":"What would persuade more people to use public transport instead of private cars?","a":"Reliability and convenience above all. If buses come on time and reach where people need to go, many would leave their cars at home. Lower fares and safe, clean vehicles would also help. For instance, a new tram line in a nearby city cut car use noticeably. Without good service, appeals to save the planet fall flat."},{"q":"How might self-driving vehicles change cities?","a":"They could reduce accidents and free up parking space, since cars could drop passengers and move on. Elderly and disabled people might gain independence. That said, there are risks: job losses for drivers and possible traffic increases, as travelling becomes effortless. So planners must prepare regulations early rather than react after problems appear."},{"q":"Should governments invest more in railways than in motorways?","a":"I'd lean towards railways, as they carry more people with less pollution and fewer accidents. For example, a high-speed link can replace short flights entirely. However, in sparsely populated regions, roads remain essential. So the answer depends on geography, but overall, trains offer a more sustainable long-term investment."},{"q":"Is it fair to charge drivers a fee for entering city centres?","a":"I think it can be fair if the money improves alternatives. Drivers contribute to congestion and pollution, so paying seems logical, much like a user fee. That said, it may burden low-income workers who have no other option. Therefore, discounts or better buses should accompany the charge, as some European cities have shown."}]},{"id":"sp3-24","l":"C1","topic":"Volunteering","qs":[{"q":"Why do some people dedicate their free time to volunteering?","a":"Mostly because it gives them meaning. Helping others creates a sense of belonging and achievement that a paid job might not. For example, a retired nurse I know teaches first aid to teenagers every weekend and says it keeps her energised. Some also hope to gain skills, but the emotional reward usually comes first."},{"q":"Should volunteering be compulsory for students?","a":"I'm somewhat doubtful. Compulsion may spoil the spirit of the activity, since people help because they want to. On the other hand, a short required programme could expose young people to experiences they'd never choose themselves, and some might continue willingly. For instance, a pupil who was forced to clean a park later joined a conservation group."},{"q":"Can volunteers replace paid professionals?","a":"Not really, and they shouldn't. Volunteers are valuable for extra support, such as visiting lonely residents, but complex tasks like medical treatment need trained, accountable staff. If governments rely too heavily on volunteers, it may become a way of avoiding proper funding. So I'd see them as a complement, not a substitute."},{"q":"What benefits can companies gain from encouraging employees to volunteer?","a":"Quite a few. Staff who volunteer develop teamwork and leadership skills, and often return more motivated. The company also improves its public image. For example, a firm that gave employees one paid day a year to help local schools noticed stronger loyalty. In short, it's good for the community and for business."}]},{"id":"sp3-25","l":"C1","topic":"Success","qs":[{"q":"How should success be measured, in your view?","a":"I'd say it's personal rather than universal. Wealth and status are easy to count, but they don't reflect satisfaction. A person who earns a modest salary yet feels respected and calm might be more successful than a stressed millionaire. So I'd define success as living in line with your values and goals."},{"q":"Does failure play a necessary role in achieving success?","a":"Largely, yes. Failure shows us what doesn't work and builds resilience. For example, many scientists run hundreds of unsuccessful experiments before a breakthrough. That said, repeated failure without reflection is just discouraging. The crucial point is to analyse mistakes and adjust. In that sense, failure is a teacher, provided we're willing to listen."},{"q":"Is talent more important than hard work?","a":"I'd argue hard work matters more in the long run. Talent gives a head start, but without discipline it fades. For instance, a gifted student who never revises may be overtaken by an average one who studies steadily. That said, the ideal combination is both. Consistency, however, is what distinguishes those who finish from those who don't."},{"q":"Do you think society puts too much pressure on people to succeed?","a":"To some degree, yes. Social media and family expectations can make people feel that ordinary lives are failures. This pressure may cause anxiety, especially among students. For example, some young people choose careers they dislike just to please others. A healthier attitude would celebrate different paths and small, steady progress."}]},{"id":"sp3-26","l":"C1","topic":"Decision-making","qs":[{"q":"What factors make it difficult for people to make good decisions?","a":"Emotions, lack of information and time pressure are the main ones. When we're anxious or excited, we may overlook risks. Also, too many options can paralyse us. For example, someone choosing a university course might panic at the sheer number of possibilities and simply copy a friend. Calm reflection and good advice usually improve decisions."},{"q":"Is it better to make decisions quickly or to take plenty of time?","a":"It depends on the stakes. For small matters, like what to eat, overthinking wastes energy. For major ones, such as buying a house, rushing can be costly. That said, waiting forever is also a decision, often a bad one. I'd suggest gathering key facts, setting a deadline, and then committing."},{"q":"Should important decisions be made by individuals or by groups?","a":"Both have merits. Groups bring different perspectives and reduce individual blind spots. However, they can be slow, and members sometimes agree just to avoid conflict. For instance, a committee might approve a poor plan because nobody wants to disagree. I'd say groups should discuss, but one accountable person should finally decide."},{"q":"How might artificial intelligence influence the way we make decisions?","a":"It could improve them by analysing large amounts of data quickly, for example in medical diagnosis or financial planning. However, there's a danger that people stop thinking critically and accept the machine's suggestion blindly. So, in my opinion, AI should inform decisions, while responsibility remains with humans who can weigh values and context."}]},{"id":"sp3-27","l":"C1","topic":"The internet","qs":[{"q":"In what ways has the internet changed the way people learn?","a":"Profoundly. Anyone with a connection can now access lectures, books and tutorials for free. For example, I could learn a programming language from videos without attending a class. That said, the abundance of information makes it harder to judge quality. So skills like evaluating sources have become just as important as the content itself."},{"q":"Do you think the internet has made people more informed or more misinformed?","a":"Honestly, both. Reliable information is easier to find than ever, yet rumours spread faster than corrections. A false claim about a medicine can reach millions within hours. Therefore, I'd argue that the internet amplifies whatever habits users already have: careful readers become better informed, while careless ones become more confused."},{"q":"Should there be stricter regulation of what appears online?","a":"I think some regulation is necessary, especially against fraud, hate speech and content harmful to children. However, excessive control risks limiting free expression and could be misused to silence criticism. Ideally, rules should be clear, transparent and decided democratically. For instance, platforms could be required to label sources and remove illegal material promptly."},{"q":"What might happen to society if the internet became unavailable for a week?","a":"It would be chaotic at first. Banking, transport systems, hospitals and communication all rely on it. People would feel isolated, and many businesses would lose money. On the other hand, some might rediscover face-to-face conversation. Overall, the experience would reveal how deeply dependent we are, and how fragile our systems are."}]},{"id":"sp3-28","l":"C1","topic":"Universities","qs":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of a university: preparing students for jobs or promoting knowledge?","a":"Ideally both, though I'd stress knowledge. Job skills can change quickly, whereas the ability to think critically and research remains useful for decades. For instance, a graduate trained in analysis can adapt to many roles. That said, universities should cooperate with employers so that students aren't left unprepared for the labour market."},{"q":"Should university education be free for everybody?","a":"There's a strong argument for it, since education benefits the whole society, and fees can exclude talented students. On the other hand, free tuition requires heavy public spending, which might reduce quality if poorly managed. A compromise could be low fees combined with grants for those in need. Transparency about spending is essential either way."},{"q":"Are online degrees as valuable as traditional ones?","a":"They can be, if the institution is credible. Online study offers flexibility for working adults and lowers costs. However, it lacks daily interaction, labs and networking, which are important for many subjects. For example, medicine can't be learnt properly through a screen. So I'd say it depends on the subject and the student's discipline."},{"q":"How could universities contribute more to their local communities?","a":"They could share expertise through free workshops, advice clinics and partnerships with local businesses. For instance, a law faculty might offer legal guidance to people who can't afford a lawyer, while students gain practical experience. This would build trust and show that universities are not isolated towers but active, useful neighbours."}]},{"id":"sp3-29","l":"C1","topic":"Public services","qs":[{"q":"What makes a public service efficient and trustworthy?","a":"Clear procedures, trained staff and openness. When citizens know what documents are required and how long the process will take, frustration drops. Also, being able to complain and see action builds trust. For example, an office that publishes waiting times and processes applications online avoids favouritism. Ultimately, accountability is what keeps standards high."},{"q":"Should essential services like water and electricity be run by the state or by private companies?","a":"Both models can work, but because these services are essential, strong public oversight is vital. Private firms may bring efficiency and investment, yet may prioritise profit over fairness. State management can ensure access for all but risks waste. In my view, what matters most is transparent contracts and independent regulators."},{"q":"How can technology improve the way public services are delivered?","a":"By reducing queues and paperwork. Online platforms allow people to renew documents from home, and digital records cut human discretion, which lowers opportunities for bribery. For example, e-payment of fines removes the need to hand cash to an officer. That said, those without internet access must still be served in person."},{"q":"Do you think public servants should be paid more to prevent dishonesty?","a":"Decent salaries certainly reduce temptation, since someone struggling to feed a family may be vulnerable. However, higher pay alone doesn't guarantee integrity. It must be combined with monitoring, clear penalties and a culture of ethics. In other words, it's a necessary condition, but it isn't a sufficient one."}]},{"id":"sp3-30","l":"C1","topic":"The future","qs":[{"q":"How do you think daily life will be different in fifty years?","a":"I'd speculate that it will be more automated and more connected. Many routine tasks, from cleaning to scheduling, could be handled by machines, leaving people time for creative work. Healthcare may become personalised and preventive. That said, we may face new challenges such as water shortages, so adaptation will be as important as invention."},{"q":"Are you optimistic or pessimistic about the future of the planet?","a":"Cautiously optimistic. Renewable energy is getting cheaper, and awareness is spreading, particularly among young people. However, progress is uneven, and time is limited. If governments and companies act decisively, we could avoid the worst outcomes. So the future isn't fixed; it depends on choices made in the next two decades."},{"q":"What skills will be most valuable for the next generation?","a":"I'd argue adaptability, critical thinking and communication. Technical knowledge may become outdated quickly, but the ability to learn new things and judge information remains vital. For example, a person who can evaluate an AI-generated report is more useful than one who merely memorises. Emotional intelligence will also matter when working with diverse teams."},{"q":"Should governments plan for the distant future or concentrate on present problems?","a":"Both, though it's tempting to focus on the present because elections are short-term. Yet issues like climate change, pensions and education need long-term strategies. For instance, planting forests today benefits those who aren't yet born. So I'd say wise governments balance urgent needs with investments that pay off over decades."}]}]);
Fasih.add("wsent",[{"id":"ws-a2-01","l":"A2","task":"Write three sentences about your daily routine. Use: usually, never, sometimes.","taskAr":"اكتب ثلاث جمل عن روتينك اليومي. استعمل: usually وnever وsometimes.","frame":["I usually ...","I never ...","Sometimes I ..."],"model":"I usually get up at six o'clock. I never drink coffee after lunch. Sometimes I walk to the library in the evening.","check":["هل كتبت ثلاث جمل كاملة؟","هل بدأت كل جملة بحرف كبير وأنهيتها بنقطة؟","هل وضعت usually وnever قبل الفعل الرئيسي؟","هل استعملت المضارع البسيط؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-02","l":"A2","task":"Write three or four sentences about your family. Say who they are and what they do.","taskAr":"اكتب ثلاث أو أربع جمل عن عائلتك. اذكر من هم وماذا يعملون.","frame":["There are ... people in my family.","My father is ...","My sister ..."],"model":"There are five people in my family. My father is a teacher and my mother is a nurse. My brother is a student. He plays football every Friday.","check":["هل استعملت There are وis/are بشكل صحيح؟","هل أضفت s إلى الفعل مع he وshe؟","هل ذكرت مهنة لكل شخص؟","هل أنهيت كل جملة بنقطة؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-03","l":"A2","task":"Describe your studies in four sentences. Say what you study, where, and what you like about it.","taskAr":"صف دراستك في أربع جمل. قل ماذا تدرس وأين وما الذي يعجبك فيها.","frame":["I study ... at ...","I am in my ... year.","My favourite subject is ...","I like it because ..."],"model":"I study law at a university in Algeria. I am in my second year. My favourite subject is public law. I like it because it teaches me about justice and fair rules.","check":["هل كتبت I study وليس I am study؟","هل استعملت because لذكر السبب؟","هل كتبت اسم المادة بوضوح؟","هل فيها أربع جمل على الأقل؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-04","l":"A2","task":"Write four sentences about your best friend. Say what he or she looks like and what he or she is like.","taskAr":"اكتب أربع جمل عن أعز أصدقائك. صف شكله وشخصيته.","frame":["My best friend is called ...","He/She is ... and has ...","He/She is very ...","We often ..."],"model":"My best friend is called Karim. He is tall and has short black hair. He is very kind and funny. We often study together in the library on Saturdays.","check":["هل استعملت is مع he وshe؟","هل وصفت الشكل والشخصية معاً؟","هل كتبت الاسم بحرف كبير؟","هل استعملت often قبل الفعل؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-05","l":"A2","task":"Write about your town or city in about 40 words. Say where it is and what people can do there.","taskAr":"اكتب عن مدينتك أو بلدتك في نحو 40 كلمة. قل أين تقع وماذا يستطيع الناس أن يفعلوا فيها.","frame":["My town is in ...","There is a ... and there are ...","People can ...","I like it because ..."],"model":"My town is in the north of Algeria. There is a big market and there are two small parks. People can walk by the river in the evening. I like my town because it is quiet and the people are friendly.","check":["هل استعملت There is مع المفرد وThere are مع الجمع؟","هل استعملت can + فعل بدون to؟","هل كتبت الأسماء الخاصة بحرف كبير؟","هل ذكرت سبباً مع because؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-06","l":"A2","task":"Write about what you did yesterday in about 40 words. Use at least four past simple verbs.","taskAr":"اكتب عما فعلته أمس في نحو 40 كلمة. استعمل أربعة أفعال على الأقل في الماضي البسيط.","frame":["Yesterday I woke up at ...","In the morning I ...","Then I ...","In the evening I ..."],"model":"Yesterday I woke up at seven. In the morning I went to the university and I attended two lectures. Then I had lunch with my friends. In the evening I studied English for an hour and watched a short film.","check":["هل كل الأفعال في الماضي (went, had, watched)؟","هل استعملت did not مع الفعل الأصلي إن نفيت؟","هل رتبت الأحداث بـ then وin the evening؟","هل كتبت Yesterday في بداية الجملة أو نهايتها؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-07","l":"A2","task":"Write about your plans for next weekend in about 35 words. Use going to and will.","taskAr":"اكتب عن خططك لعطلة نهاية الأسبوع القادمة في نحو 35 كلمة. استعمل going to وwill.","frame":["Next weekend I am going to ...","On Saturday I will ...","I am also going to ...","I hope ..."],"model":"Next weekend I am going to visit my grandparents. On Saturday I will help my grandfather in his garden. In the evening we are going to cook dinner together. I hope the weather will be nice.","check":["هل استعملت am going to للخطط؟","هل استعملت will للتوقع أو القرار اللحظي؟","هل جاء بعد going to فعل في صيغته الأصلية؟","هل ذكرت اليوم أو الوقت؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-08","l":"A2","task":"Write a short email to a friend. Invite him or her to your house on Friday. Say the time and what you will do.","taskAr":"اكتب رسالة قصيرة لصديق تدعوه فيها إلى بيتك يوم الجمعة. اذكر الوقت وماذا ستفعلان.","frame":["Hi ...,","Would you like to come to my house on ...?","We can ...","Please tell me ...","Best wishes,"],"model":"Hi Samir,\nWould you like to come to my house on Friday? Please come at four o'clock. We can drink tea, play cards and watch the match together. My brother will also be here. Please tell me if you can come.\nBest wishes,\nAmine","check":["هل بدأت بتحية وانتهيت بخاتمة؟","هل استعملت Would you like to للدعوة المهذبة؟","هل ذكرت اليوم والساعة؟","هل طلبت منه الرد؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-09","l":"A2","task":"Describe your room or your home in about 50 words. Say what there is and where things are.","taskAr":"صف غرفتك أو بيتك في نحو 50 كلمة. قل ما الموجود وأين توجد الأشياء.","frame":["My room is ...","There is a ... next to ...","The ... is on/under ...","My favourite thing is ..."],"model":"My room is small but comfortable. There is a bed next to the window and a desk opposite the door. The lamp is on the desk and my books are on the shelf above it. My favourite thing is the big window because I can see the mountains from it.","check":["هل استعملت حروف الجر in وon وnext to وunder؟","هل استعملت there is وthere are صحيحاً؟","هل وضعت الصفة قبل الاسم (small room)؟","هل ذكرت شيئاً مفضلاً مع السبب؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-10","l":"A2","task":"Write about your favourite food in about 40 words. Say what it is, how it is made and when you eat it.","taskAr":"اكتب عن طعامك المفضل في نحو 40 كلمة. قل ما هو وكيف يُحضَّر ومتى تأكله.","frame":["My favourite food is ...","It is made with ...","You need ...","I usually eat it ..."],"model":"My favourite food is couscous with vegetables. It is made with semolina, carrots, potatoes and a little meat. My mother cooks it every Friday and the whole family eats together. I like it because it is warm, tasty and healthy.","check":["هل استعملت some وa little وa lot of مع غير المعدود؟","هل استعملت is made with للمكونات؟","هل ذكرت وقت تناوله؟","هل أضفت رأيك مع because؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-11","l":"A2","task":"Write about a job you would like to do in about 40 words. Say what the job is and why you want it.","taskAr":"اكتب عن وظيفة تود أن تعمل بها في نحو 40 كلمة. قل ما هي ولماذا تريدها.","frame":["I would like to be a ...","A ... works in ...","The job is ... because ...","In the future I want to ..."],"model":"I would like to be a lawyer. A lawyer works in an office and in court. The job is important because lawyers help people who are in trouble. In the future I want to work for a company that protects workers' rights.","check":["هل استعملت I would like to be + مهنة؟","هل استعملت a قبل المهنة المفردة؟","هل ذكرت سببين؟","هل استعملت want to + فعل؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-12","l":"A2","task":"Explain in simple English what transparency means. Write about 50 words and give one example.","taskAr":"اشرح بإنجليزية بسيطة معنى الشفافية (transparency). اكتب نحو 50 كلمة وأعطِ مثالاً واحداً.","frame":["Transparency means ...","For example, ...","When a government is transparent, ...","This is important because ..."],"model":"Transparency means that an organisation is open and honest about what it does. For example, a city publishes how it spends its money on the internet. When a government is transparent, citizens can check the information and ask questions. This is important because it helps to stop corruption and builds trust.","check":["هل شرحت الكلمة بجملة بسيطة؟","هل استعملت For example قبل المثال؟","هل استعملت الجملة الشرطية When ... مع المضارع؟","هل ذكرت لماذا الشفافية مهمة؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-13","l":"A2","task":"Write a short formal email to your professor. Ask for a meeting about your project. Write about 50 words.","taskAr":"اكتب رسالة رسمية قصيرة إلى أستاذك تطلب فيها موعداً لمناقشة مشروعك. اكتب نحو 50 كلمة.","frame":["Dear Professor ...,","I am writing to ask ...","Would it be possible to ...?","I am free on ...","Yours sincerely,"],"model":"Dear Professor Hadi,\nI am writing to ask for a meeting about my research project. Would it be possible to see you this week? I am free on Tuesday and Wednesday afternoon. I would like to discuss my title and my plan. Thank you for your time.\nYours sincerely,\nNadia Benali","check":["هل بدأت بـ Dear Professor وانتهيت بـ Yours sincerely؟","هل ذكرت سبب الرسالة في الجملة الأولى؟","هل استعملت Would it be possible للطلب المهذب؟","هل اقترحت أوقاتاً محددة؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-14","l":"A2","task":"Look at a park on a sunny Sunday. Describe what people are doing. Write about 50 words using the present continuous.","taskAr":"تخيل حديقة في يوم أحد مشمس. صف ما يفعله الناس الآن. اكتب نحو 50 كلمة بالمضارع المستمر.","frame":["It is a sunny day in the park.","A man is ...","Two children are ...","Some people are ..."],"model":"It is a sunny Sunday in the park. A man is reading a newspaper under a tree. Two children are playing with a red ball. Some people are walking their dogs and a woman is selling ice cream. Everyone looks happy and the park is full of life.","check":["هل استعملت am/is/are + فعل-ing؟","هل كتبت is مع المفرد وare مع الجمع؟","هل صفت ما يحدث الآن لا العادة؟","هل كتبت 55 كلمة تقريباً؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-15","l":"A2","task":"Write about a holiday you enjoyed in about 55 words. Say where you went, who with, what you did and what you thought.","taskAr":"اكتب عن عطلة استمتعت بها في نحو 55 كلمة. قل أين ذهبت ومع من وماذا فعلت ورأيك فيها.","frame":["Last summer I went to ... with ...","We stayed in ...","Every day we ...","It was ... because ..."],"model":"Last summer I went to the seaside with my cousins. We stayed in a small hotel near the beach. Every day we swam in the sea and played football on the sand. In the evening we ate fresh fish. It was a wonderful holiday because the weather was hot and everybody was relaxed.","check":["هل الأفعال كلها في الماضي (went, stayed, ate)؟","هل استعملت was/were للوصف؟","هل ذكرت مع من ذهبت؟","هل ختمت برأيك في العطلة؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-16","l":"A2","task":"Compare two cities or two phones in about 50 words. Use comparative adjectives and at least one superlative.","taskAr":"قارن بين مدينتين أو هاتفين في نحو 50 كلمة. استعمل صيغ المقارنة وصيغة تفضيل واحدة على الأقل.","frame":["... is bigger than ...","... is more ... than ...","... is cheaper/older ...","In my opinion, the best ... is ..."],"model":"Algiers is bigger than Blida and it has more cars. Blida is quieter and cheaper than Algiers. The buses in Algiers are more crowded, but there are more things to do there. Blida is smaller, but it is closer to the mountains. In my opinion, the best city for students is Blida.","check":["هل استعملت er مع الصفات القصيرة وmore مع الطويلة؟","هل استعملت than بعد الصفة المقارنة؟","هل استعملت the best أو the biggest؟","هل أعطيت رأيك في النهاية؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-17","l":"A2","task":"Describe a simple project idea in about 60 words. Say what it is, who it helps and what you need.","taskAr":"صف فكرة مشروع بسيط في نحو 60 كلمة. قل ما هو ومن يستفيد منه وماذا تحتاج.","frame":["My project idea is ...","It is for ...","It can help people to ...","I need ... and ...","First, I will ..."],"model":"My project idea is a small phone app for students. It is for people who want to find cheap books near their university. Students can write the name of a book and see the nearest shop and the price. I need a computer, a designer and some money. First, I will ask fifty students if they would use it.","check":["هل ذكرت الفكرة في الجملة الأولى؟","هل استعملت who في وصف الأشخاص؟","هل ذكرت ما تحتاجه؟","هل ذكرت خطوتك الأولى بـ First, I will؟"]},{"id":"ws-a2-18","l":"A2","task":"Write a short paragraph about why you are learning English. Write 60 to 70 words and include a goal for the future.","taskAr":"اكتب فقرة قصيرة عن سبب تعلمك الإنجليزية. اكتب 60 إلى 70 كلمة وضمّنها هدفاً للمستقبل.","frame":["I am learning English because ...","First, ...","Second, ...","In the future, I want to ...","I study ... every day."],"model":"I am learning English because it is the language of science and international work. First, I need it to read research articles in my field. Second, I want to speak with people from other countries. In the future, I want to study abroad and write my own research. I study English for one hour every day, and I am getting better slowly.","check":["هل بدأت بجملة موضوع واضحة فيها because؟","هل رتبت الأسباب بـ First وSecond؟","هل ذكرت هدفاً بـ In the future, I want to؟","هل أنهيت الفقرة بجملة ختامية؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-01","l":"B1","task":"Write one paragraph (about 80 words) about your life as a student. Describe your studies, your timetable and one difficulty.","taskAr":"اكتب فقرة واحدة (نحو 80 كلمة) عن حياتك كطالب. صف دراستك وجدولك وصعوبة واحدة تواجهها.","frame":["I am currently studying ... at ...","My timetable is ...","One of the biggest difficulties is ...","To deal with it, I ..."],"model":"I am currently studying transparency and anti-corruption at a university in Algeria. My timetable is quite busy: I have lectures on Monday, Tuesday and Thursday, and I spend the other days reading and writing. I really enjoy the subject because it deals with real problems in society. One of the biggest difficulties is that most of the best articles are in English. To deal with it, I read one short article every morning and write down ten new words.","check":["هل فيها جملة موضوع تعرّف بدراستك؟","هل ذكرت صعوبة واحدة وحلاً لها؟","هل استعملت المضارع المستمر مع currently والبسيط مع العادات؟","هل ربطت الأفكار بـ because وTo deal with؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-02","l":"B1","task":"Write an informal email (about 85 words) to a friend about a trip you took. Say where you went, what you saw and what you liked best.","taskAr":"اكتب رسالة ودية (نحو 85 كلمة) لصديق عن رحلة قمت بها. قل أين ذهبت وماذا رأيت وما أعجبك أكثر.","frame":["Hi ...,","I'm writing to tell you about ...","The best part was ...","I wish you could have ...","Write soon,"],"model":"Hi Yacine,\nHow are you? I'm writing to tell you about my trip to the desert last week. We travelled by bus for six hours and then rode camels to the camp. The best part was the night sky: I had never seen so many stars! We also ate bread baked in the sand, which was delicious. It was very cold at night, but the blankets were warm. I wish you could have come with us. Let's plan another trip together soon.\nWrite soon,\nOmar","check":["هل الأسلوب ودي (I'm, Let's, How are you)؟","هل استعملت الماضي البسيط لسرد الرحلة؟","هل ذكرت أفضل جزء بوضوح؟","هل ختمت بجملة تدعو للرد أو للقاء؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-03","l":"B1","task":"Describe a place you know well (a town, a market or a library) in one paragraph of about 90 words. Use your senses.","taskAr":"صف مكاناً تعرفه جيداً (بلدة أو سوق أو مكتبة) في فقرة من نحو 90 كلمة. استعمل الحواس.","frame":["... is a place where ...","When you walk in, you can see ...","You can hear ... and smell ...","What makes it special is ..."],"model":"The old market in my town is a place where everyone meets on Friday morning. When you walk in, you can see tables full of oranges, olives and bright scarves. You can hear the sellers shouting their prices, and you can smell fresh bread and mint tea. The streets are narrow and crowded, but nobody seems to be in a hurry. What makes it special is the friendly atmosphere: people stop to talk, to laugh and to help each other. For me, it is the heart of the town.","check":["هل استعملت ثلاث حواس على الأقل؟","هل استعملت جملة وصل مثل a place where؟","هل استعملت صفات دقيقة (narrow, crowded, fresh)؟","هل ختمت بإحساسك الشخصي؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-04","l":"B1","task":"Explain what transparency means in public life. Write one paragraph (about 80 words) with a definition, an example and a reason why it matters.","taskAr":"اشرح معنى الشفافية في الحياة العامة. اكتب فقرة (نحو 80 كلمة) فيها تعريف ومثال وسبب أهميتها.","frame":["Transparency can be defined as ...","A clear example is ...","Because of this, citizens can ...","That is why transparency matters: ..."],"model":"Transparency can be defined as the practice of making decisions, money and rules open to the public. A clear example is a municipality that publishes its budget and every contract it signs. Because of this, citizens can compare the promises with the real results, and journalists can ask questions. When information is hidden, mistakes and dishonest behaviour are much harder to discover. That is why transparency matters: it protects public money, encourages honest officials and builds trust between people and their institutions.","check":["هل بدأت بتعريف واضح؟","هل أعطيت مثالاً ملموساً يمكن تخيّله؟","هل ربطت النتيجة بـ Because of this؟","هل ختمت بجملة تلخص الأهمية؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-05","l":"B1","task":"Write a formal email (about 95 words) to a professor. Ask for an extension for an assignment and explain the reason politely.","taskAr":"اكتب رسالة رسمية (نحو 95 كلمة) إلى أستاذ تطلب فيها تمديد موعد تسليم واجب، واشرح السبب بلطف.","frame":["Dear Professor ...,","I am writing regarding ...","Unfortunately, ...","I would be grateful if ...","I apologise for any inconvenience."],"model":"Dear Professor Mansouri,\nI am writing regarding the assignment for your course on public administration, which is due on 5 December. Unfortunately, I have been ill for the past week and I have not been able to finish the final section. I have already completed the research and most of the writing. I would be grateful if you could allow me to submit it on 10 December. I can send you my plan today if you would like to see it. I apologise for any inconvenience and thank you for your understanding.\nYours sincerely,\nSofiane Cherif","check":["هل ذكرت اسم المقرر وموعد التسليم؟","هل شرحت السبب باختصار ولباقة؟","هل استعملت I would be grateful if بدل صيغة الأمر؟","هل خلت الرسالة من الاختصارات مثل I'm وcan't؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-06","l":"B1","task":"Give your opinion: should cities publish how they spend public money online? Write one paragraph (about 100 words) with two reasons and an example.","taskAr":"أبدِ رأيك: هل ينبغي أن تنشر المدن كيف تنفق المال العام على الإنترنت؟ اكتب فقرة (نحو 100 كلمات) فيها سببان ومثال.","frame":["In my opinion, ...","The first reason is that ...","Another reason is ...","For example, ...","For these reasons, ..."],"model":"In my opinion, cities should publish how they spend public money online. The first reason is that citizens have the right to know what happens to the taxes they pay. Another reason is that open information makes it harder to hide waste. For example, if a city publishes the price of every new road, people can easily see when one road costs twice as much as another. Some officials may say that this takes too much time. However, modern websites make it simple and cheap. For these reasons, I believe that publishing spending is a good rule for every city.","check":["هل صرّحت برأيك في الجملة الأولى؟","هل لكل سبب جملة مستقلة؟","هل أعطيت مثالاً بعبارة For example؟","هل ذكرت رأياً مخالفاً وردّيت عليه بـ However؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-07","l":"B1","task":"Write a short story (about 95 words) that begins: \"Last Tuesday, I found a wallet on the bus.\" Use past simple and past continuous.","taskAr":"اكتب قصة قصيرة (نحو 95 كلمات) تبدأ بالجملة: \"Last Tuesday, I found a wallet on the bus.\" استعمل الماضي البسيط والماضي المستمر.","frame":["Last Tuesday, I found a wallet on the bus.","While I was ..., I noticed ...","At first, I ...","Then I decided to ...","In the end, ..."],"model":"Last Tuesday, I found a wallet on the bus. While I was reading, I noticed it under the seat. At first, I wanted to keep it because I was short of money. Then I opened it and saw a student card with a photo of a young girl. I decided to take it to the university office. When I arrived, a girl was crying at the desk. She had lost her card and her bus pass. In the end, she thanked me with a big smile, and I felt proud of my choice.","check":["هل بدأت بالجملة المعطاة بالضبط؟","هل استعملت while + الماضي المستمر للخلفية؟","هل رتبت الأحداث بـ At first وThen وIn the end؟","هل فيها مشاعر أو قرار واضح؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-08","l":"B1","task":"Describe this chart in words (about 95 words). A line chart shows the number of students (in thousands) at an online course: 2018 = 10, 2020 = 30, 2022 = 55, 2024 = 60.","taskAr":"صف هذا الرسم البياني بالكلمات (نحو 95 كلمة). رسم خطي يبيّن عدد الطلاب (بالآلاف) في دورة إلكترونية: 2018 = 10، 2020 = 30، 2022 = 55، 2024 = 60.","frame":["The line chart shows ...","In 2018, the number was ...","Between ... and ..., it rose sharply to ...","After that, ... increased slowly.","Overall, ..."],"model":"The line chart shows how many students joined an online course between 2018 and 2024. The numbers are given in thousands. In 2018, only 10 thousand students took the course. Between 2018 and 2020, the number tripled and reached 30 thousand. It then continued to rise sharply and hit 55 thousand in 2022. After that, the increase was much slower: by 2024, the figure had risen by only 5 thousand, to 60 thousand. Overall, the course became six times more popular in six years, although growth slowed down at the end of the period.","check":["هل بدأت بجملة تقول ما يبيّنه الرسم؟","هل استعملت أفعال الاتجاه (rose, tripled, reached)؟","هل ذكرت الأرقام والسنوات بدقة؟","هل ختمت بجملة Overall تلخص الاتجاه العام؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-09","l":"B1","task":"Write a summary (about 95 words) of this text: \"Many cities now use smart bins that tell workers when they are full. Trucks only visit full bins, so they drive less. As a result, cities spend less on fuel, the air is cleaner and the streets are tidier. However, the bins are expensive to buy, and small towns often cannot afford them.\"","taskAr":"اكتب ملخصاً (نحو 95 كلمة) لهذا النص: \"Many cities now use smart bins that tell workers when they are full. Trucks only visit full bins, so they drive less. As a result, cities spend less on fuel, the air is cleaner and the streets are tidier. However, the bins are expensive to buy, and small towns often cannot afford them.\"","frame":["The text explains that ...","According to the text, ...","As a result, ...","However, the text also says that ..."],"model":"The text explains that some cities now use smart bins to manage their rubbish. According to the text, the bins send a message to workers when they are full, so trucks only collect from the bins that need emptying. As a result, cities use less fuel, there is less pollution and the streets stay cleaner. However, the text also says that the bins are expensive, which means that small towns may not be able to buy them. In short, smart bins are useful for large cities, but cost is still a problem for smaller ones.","check":["هل استعملت كلماتك الخاصة بدل نسخ الجمل؟","هل ذكرت الفكرة الرئيسية والنتيجة والمشكلة؟","هل خلت من رأيك الشخصي؟","هل استعملت عبارات مثل According to the text؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-10","l":"B1","task":"Describe a project idea to improve your town (about 105 words). Explain the problem, your solution, who will help and the result you expect.","taskAr":"صف فكرة مشروع لتحسين بلدتك (نحو 105 كلمة). اشرح المشكلة والحل ومن سيساعد والنتيجة المتوقعة.","frame":["The main problem in my town is ...","My idea is to ...","The project would be run by ...","We could get help from ...","If it works, ..."],"model":"The main problem in my town is that people do not know how to report broken streetlights and holes in the road. My idea is to create a simple website and phone number where residents can send a photo and the place of the problem. The project would be run by a small team of volunteer students. We could get help from the municipality, which could fix the most urgent problems first. Every month, we would publish a list of the problems that were reported and the ones that were solved. If it works, the streets will be safer, and people will trust their local government more.","check":["هل ذكرت المشكلة قبل الحل؟","هل استعملت would وcould للحديث عن مشروع مقترح؟","هل ذكرت من سينفّذ ومن سيساعد؟","هل ختمت بنتيجة باستعمال If it works؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-11","l":"B1","task":"Write about 105 words: do you prefer studying online or in a classroom? Give reasons and a short example from your own experience.","taskAr":"اكتب نحو 105 كلمة: هل تفضّل الدراسة عبر الإنترنت أم في القاعة؟ اعرض الأسباب ومثالاً قصيراً من تجربتك.","frame":["I prefer ... to ...","There are two main reasons for this.","Firstly, ...","On the other hand, ...","Personally, ..."],"model":"I prefer studying in a classroom to studying online. There are two main reasons for this. Firstly, it is easier to concentrate when I am sitting with other students. At home, my phone and my family often distract me. Secondly, I can ask questions immediately and hear the questions of others. On the other hand, online lessons save time and travel money, and I can watch a difficult lecture twice. Last year, I followed a statistics course online, and I had to read the same page three times because nobody could explain it to me. Personally, I think the best choice is a mix of both.","check":["هل ذكرت تفضيلك في أول جملة؟","هل رتبت الأسباب بـ Firstly وSecondly؟","هل أعطيت الجانب الآخر بـ On the other hand؟","هل في المثال تفصيل من تجربتك؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-12","l":"B1","task":"Write a complaint email (about 110 words) to an online shop. You received a damaged product. Explain what happened and what you want.","taskAr":"اكتب رسالة شكوى (نحو 110 كلمة) إلى متجر إلكتروني. وصلك منتج تالف. اشرح ما حدث وما الذي تطلبه.","frame":["Dear Sir or Madam,","I am writing to complain about ...","When the parcel arrived, ...","I have attached ...","I would like you to ...","I look forward to hearing from you."],"model":"Dear Sir or Madam,\nI am writing to complain about a laptop bag that I ordered from your website on 3 November (order number 4821). When the parcel arrived last Monday, the zip was broken and there was a large hole in one corner. I have attached two photos that show the damage. The bag was not cheap, and I needed it for my studies. I would like you to send me a new bag or to give me my money back, whichever is easier for you. I would also like to know how long this will take. I look forward to hearing from you.\nYours faithfully,\nHassan Rahmani","check":["هل ذكرت رقم الطلب وتاريخه؟","هل وصفت المشكلة بدقة وبنبرة مهذبة؟","هل حددت ما تطلبه بوضوح؟","هل انتهيت بـ Yours faithfully لأنك لا تعرف الاسم؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-13","l":"B1","task":"Describe a person you admire (about 135 words). Say who the person is, what he or she did and why you admire him or her.","taskAr":"صف شخصاً تُعجب به (نحو 135 كلمة). قل من هو وماذا فعل ولماذا تُعجب به.","frame":["The person I admire most is ...","He/She is a ... who ...","One thing he/she did was ...","What I admire most is ...","He/She has taught me that ..."],"model":"The person I admire most is my old maths teacher, Mrs Amel. She is a woman who never gave up on her students. One thing she did was to stay at school every afternoon for free, so that weaker students could ask questions. I was one of them. At the beginning, I could not solve even the easiest problems, but she explained everything again and again with patience and a smile. Her classroom was always calm and tidy, and she remembered the name of every student. What I admire most is that she treated everybody equally, rich or poor. She has taught me that hard work and kindness can change a person's life. Today I try to help my younger cousins in the same way, and I hope to become a teacher like her one day.","check":["هل ذكرت من هو الشخص ولماذا تعجب به؟","هل استعملت who في جملة وصل واحدة على الأقل؟","هل ذكرت فعلاً محدداً قام به؟","هل ختمت بما تعلمته منه؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-14","l":"B1","task":"Write a story (about 130 words) about a day when everything went wrong. Use the past perfect at least twice.","taskAr":"اكتب قصة (نحو 130 كلمة) عن يوم سارت فيه الأمور كلها بشكل سيئ. استعمل الماضي التام مرتين على الأقل.","frame":["It was the worst day of ...","I had forgotten to ... so ...","By the time I arrived, ...","Just then, ...","Luckily, ..."],"model":"It was the worst day of the term. I woke up late because my alarm had not rung, and I missed the bus. I ran to the station, but by the time I arrived, the next bus had already left. When I finally reached the university, the exam had started and the door was closed. Just then, I realised that I had left my pen and my student card at home. I was so upset that I sat on the steps and almost cried. Luckily, the professor came out, listened to me and allowed me to take the exam in the afternoon. That evening I bought two alarm clocks and went to bed early. I learnt that a bad day is easier to forget when you laugh about it.","check":["هل استعملت had + اسم المفعول لحدث سابق؟","هل رتبت الأحداث ترتيباً زمنياً واضحاً؟","هل استعملت by the time وjust then وluckily؟","هل للقصة نهاية؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-15","l":"B1","task":"Describe this bar chart (about 120 words). It shows how 100 students travel to university: bus 40, walking 25, car 20, bicycle 10, train 5.","taskAr":"صف هذا الرسم بالأعمدة (نحو 120 كلمة). يبيّن كيف يصل 100 طالب إلى الجامعة: الحافلة 40، المشي 25، السيارة 20، الدراجة 10، القطار 5.","frame":["The bar chart illustrates ...","The most popular way is ..., with ...","The second most common ...","By contrast, ...","In summary, ..."],"model":"The bar chart illustrates how 100 students travel to university. The most popular way is by bus, with 40 students, which is exactly two fifths of the total. The second most common way is walking, chosen by 25 students. A smaller group of 20 students come by car, while only 10 students use a bicycle. By contrast, the train is the least popular option, with just 5 students. It is interesting that the number of students who walk is higher than the number who drive. In summary, public transport and walking together account for about two thirds of all journeys, and private transport is less common. If the university built a safer bicycle path, more students might choose to cycle.","check":["هل بدأت بجملة تقدم الرسم (illustrates)؟","هل رتبت الأرقام من الأكبر إلى الأصغر؟","هل استعملت لغة المقارنة (most, least, higher than)؟","هل ختمت بـ In summary تعطي الاتجاه العام؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-16","l":"B1","task":"Write about the advantages and disadvantages of using AI tools for study (about 130 words).","taskAr":"اكتب عن مزايا وعيوب استعمال أدوات الذكاء الاصطناعي في الدراسة (نحو 130 كلمة).","frame":["AI tools are becoming ...","One advantage is that ...","Another benefit is ...","However, there are also disadvantages.","To sum up, ..."],"model":"AI tools are becoming very common among students. One advantage is that they can explain difficult ideas in simple words at any time of the day. Another benefit is that they help students check grammar and organise their notes, which saves a lot of time. However, there are also disadvantages. Firstly, AI sometimes gives wrong information in a confident voice, so students must always check important facts. Secondly, if students copy answers without thinking, they stop learning and may even break university rules. For example, a student who asks AI to write a whole essay will not improve his own writing. To sum up, AI is a useful assistant, but it should never replace thinking. Students who use it carefully, and who check every answer, will gain the most.","check":["هل ذكرت ميزتين وعيبين؟","هل استعملت عبارات الإضافة والتناقض (Another, However)؟","هل أعطيت مثالاً واحداً على الأقل؟","هل ختمت بخلاصة متوازنة؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-17","l":"B1","task":"Write an email (about 120 words) applying for a short internship at a research centre. Introduce yourself, explain your skills and say why you are interested.","taskAr":"اكتب رسالة (نحو 120 كلمة) للتقدم إلى تدريب قصير في مركز بحثي. عرّف بنفسك واذكر مهاراتك وسبب اهتمامك.","frame":["Dear Dr ...,","I am writing to apply for ...","I am currently ...","I have experience in ...","I am particularly interested in ... because ...","I have attached ..."],"model":"Dear Dr Khelifi,\nI am writing to apply for the two-month internship at your research centre, which I saw advertised on your website. I am currently a master's student in transparency and anti-corruption, and I have a bachelor's degree in accounting and finance. I have experience in reading financial reports, organising data in spreadsheets and writing short research summaries in English. I am particularly interested in your work on public procurement because I would like to understand how contracts can be checked more easily. I am a careful and reliable worker, and I learn quickly. I have attached my CV and a short writing sample. I would be very happy to discuss my application at any time.\nYours sincerely,\nAbdel Rahman Belkacem","check":["هل ذكرت الوظيفة وأين رأيت الإعلان؟","هل ذكرت مؤهلاتك ومهارتين على الأقل؟","هل شرحت سبب اهتمامك بالمركز تحديداً؟","هل ذكرت المرفقات وأنهيت بأسلوب رسمي؟"]},{"id":"ws-b1-18","l":"B1","task":"Write a summary (about 125 words) of this text: \"Corruption does not only waste money. When officials take bribes, roads are built badly, medicines are not delivered and honest companies lose contracts. Over time, citizens stop trusting the state and many talented young people leave the country. Experts say that open budgets, independent courts and free media are the best ways to reduce corruption.\" Add one sentence of your own opinion.","taskAr":"اكتب ملخصاً (نحو 125 كلمة) لهذا النص: \"Corruption does not only waste money. When officials take bribes, roads are built badly, medicines are not delivered and honest companies lose contracts. Over time, citizens stop trusting the state and many talented young people leave the country. Experts say that open budgets, independent courts and free media are the best ways to reduce corruption.\" أضف جملة واحدة برأيك الشخصي.","frame":["The text argues that ...","First, it explains that ...","It also mentions that ...","As a solution, experts suggest ...","In my opinion, ..."],"model":"The text argues that corruption is harmful in many ways, and not only because it wastes money. First, it explains that when officials accept bribes, public services become worse. For example, roads are badly built and medicines may never reach the people who need them. It also mentions that honest companies lose their chance to win contracts. In the long term, people lose trust in the state, and many talented young people decide to leave their country. As a solution, experts suggest three main measures: open budgets, independent courts and free media. In my opinion, the first step should be open budgets, because it is the easiest for citizens to understand and to check. Without public information, the other measures cannot work well.","check":["هل لخّصت الأفكار الرئيسية بكلماتك؟","هل ذكرت الأسباب والنتائج والحلول بالترتيب؟","هل فصلت رأيك عن الملخص بعبارة In my opinion؟","هل التزمت بعدد الكلمات المطلوب؟"]}]);
Fasih.add("wlesson",[{"id":"wl-01","l":"A2","title":"Capital letters and punctuation","titleAr":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم","bodyAr":"في العربية لا توجد حروف كبيرة وصغيرة، لذلك ينسى كثير من الطلاب هذه القاعدة المهمة في الإنجليزية. تذكّر ثلاثة أشياء بسيطة.\n**1. الحرف الكبير** نكتبه في بداية كل جملة، وفي أسماء الأشخاص والمدن والبلدان والأيام والأشهر واللغات (Monday, Algeria, English)، وكذلك مع الضمير **I** دائماً.\n**2. النقطة (.)** نضعها في نهاية الجملة الخبرية. وعلامة الاستفهام (?) في نهاية السؤال.\n**3. الفاصلة (,)** نضعها بين عناصر القائمة (I bought apples, bread and milk) وبعد كلمات البداية مثل Yesterday, وقبل but إذا ربطت بها جملتين كاملتين.\nلاحظ أن علامة الترقيم تلتصق بالكلمة التي قبلها ثم تأتي مسافة: Hello, my name is Sara. لا نضع مسافة قبل النقطة أو الفاصلة.\nنصيحة: قبل أن تسلّم أي نص، اقرأه مرة أخيرة وابحث عن ثلاثة أشياء فقط: حرف كبير في أول كل جملة، ونقطة في آخرها، وحرف I كبير.","examples":[{"bad":"i live in algiers and i like it","good":"I live in Algiers, and I like it.","ar":"الحرف الكبير في I وفي Algiers وفي بداية الجملة، والنقطة في النهاية."},{"bad":"Where do you live.","good":"Where do you live?","ar":"الجملة سؤال فتنتهي بعلامة استفهام لا بنقطة."},{"bad":"On monday I visit my friends in oran.","good":"On Monday I visit my friends in Oran.","ar":"أيام الأسبوع وأسماء المدن تُكتب بحرف كبير."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which word needs a capital letter? \"My brother lives in france.\"","o":["brother","lives","france"],"a":2,"why":"أسماء البلدان تُكتب بحرف كبير."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which word needs a capital letter? \"Do you speak english?\"","o":["Do","you","english"],"a":2,"why":"أسماء اللغات تُكتب بحرف كبير."},{"k":"gap","q":"I bought apples___ bread and milk. (write the punctuation mark)","a":[","],"why":"نفصل بين عناصر القائمة بفاصلة."},{"k":"fix","q":"Yesterday i went to the market.","bad":"i","good":["I"],"why":"ضمير المتكلم I يُكتب دائماً بحرف كبير."},{"k":"fix","q":"What is your name.","bad":"What is your name.","good":["What is your name?"],"why":"السؤال ينتهي بعلامة استفهام."},{"k":"order","a":"My friend lives in Constantine.","ar":"صديقي يسكن في قسنطينة."}]},{"id":"wl-02","l":"A2","title":"Joining ideas: and, but, so, because","titleAr":"ربط الأفكار: and وbut وso وbecause","bodyAr":"الجمل القصيرة جداً تبدو مملة، لكن الجمل الطويلة بلا ربط تصبح فوضى. أربع كلمات بسيطة تكفي لتبدأ.\n**and** للإضافة: I like tea and I like coffee.\n**but** للتعارض: I like tea, but I don't like coffee.\n**so** للنتيجة: I was tired, so I went to bed. (السبب أولاً ثم النتيجة)\n**because** للسبب: I went to bed because I was tired. (النتيجة أولاً ثم السبب)\nانتبه إلى الفرق: بعد **so** تأتي النتيجة، وبعد **because** يأتي السبب. وبعد because لا نقول so في الجملة نفسها (لا نكتب: Because I was tired, so I slept).\nقاعدة الفاصلة: نضع فاصلة قبل but وso عندما تربطان بين جملتين كاملتين. أما مع and فالفاصلة غير ضرورية في الجمل القصيرة.\nتدريب مفيد: خذ جملتين من يومك واربطهما بكلمة مختلفة كل مرة وانظر كيف يتغير المعنى.","examples":[{"bad":"I was hungry, because I ate a sandwich.","good":"I was hungry, so I ate a sandwich.","ar":"الجوع هو السبب والأكل هو النتيجة، لذلك نستعمل so."},{"bad":"Because it was raining, so we stayed at home.","good":"Because it was raining, we stayed at home.","ar":"لا نجمع because وso في جملة واحدة؛ نستعمل واحدة فقط."},{"bad":"I like football and my brother doesn't.","good":"I like football, but my brother doesn't.","ar":"الفكرتان متعارضتان فنستعمل but لا and."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"She was ill, ___ she stayed at home.","o":["because","so","but"],"a":1,"why":"المرض سبب والبقاء في البيت نتيجة، فنستعمل so."},{"k":"mc","q":"I stayed at home ___ I was ill.","o":["but","so","because"],"a":2,"why":"الجملة الثانية تعطي السبب، فنستعمل because."},{"k":"mc","q":"The film was long, ___ it was interesting.","o":["but","so","and because"],"a":0,"why":"الفكرتان متعارضتان (طويل / ممتع) فنستعمل but."},{"k":"gap","q":"I have an exam tomorrow, ___ I am studying tonight.","a":["so"],"why":"الامتحان سبب والدراسة نتيجة، فنستعمل so."},{"k":"fix","q":"He studied hard because he passed the exam.","bad":"because","good":["so"],"why":"النجاح نتيجة للدراسة، لذلك نستعمل so وليس because."},{"k":"order","a":"I like tea, but I don't like coffee.","ar":"أحب الشاي لكنني لا أحب القهوة."}]},{"id":"wl-03","l":"A2","title":"The parts of a paragraph","titleAr":"أجزاء الفقرة","bodyAr":"الفقرة الجيدة تشبه بيتاً صغيراً: لها مدخل وغرف وباب أخير. وفي الإنجليزية تتكون من ثلاثة أجزاء.\n**1. جملة الموضوع (topic sentence):** أول جملة، وتقول الفكرة الرئيسية للفقرة. مثال: My town is a good place for students.\n**2. جمل الدعم (supporting sentences):** من جملتين إلى أربع، وتشرح الفكرة بأسباب أو أمثلة أو تفاصيل. مثال: There is a big library. Rents are cheap. The buses are frequent.\n**3. الجملة الختامية (closing sentence):** تعيد الفكرة بكلمات مختلفة أو تعطي خلاصة. مثال: That is why I recommend it.\nكل جمل الدعم يجب أن تتحدث عن الفكرة نفسها فقط. إذا ظهرت فكرة جديدة فابدأ فقرة جديدة.\nاستعمل كلمات تنظيم مثل First, Also, For example, In short لتساعد القارئ على متابعة أفكارك.\nقبل الكتابة اسأل نفسك: ما فكرتي الرئيسية في جملة واحدة؟ ثم اكتبها أولاً.","examples":[{"bad":"I like my town. My brother has a car. I play football.","good":"My town is a good place for students. There is a big library. Rents are cheap.","ar":"الجملة الأولى في المثال الجيد تعلن الفكرة، والباقي يدعمها."},{"bad":"There are many parks. In short, it is a green city.","good":"My city is green. There are many parks. In short, it is a green city.","ar":"الفقرة تحتاج جملة موضوع في البداية قبل الدعم والخاتمة."},{"bad":"My school is great. The teachers are kind. My father works in a bank.","good":"My school is great. The teachers are kind. The library is big.","ar":"الجملة عن عمل الأب لا تدعم فكرة المدرسة."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What is the job of a topic sentence?","o":["It gives the main idea.","It gives an example.","It ends the paragraph."],"a":0,"why":"جملة الموضوع تعرض الفكرة الرئيسية وتأتي أولاً."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sentence is the best closing sentence?","o":["I have a brother.","In short, exercise is good for your health.","Football is popular."],"a":1,"why":"الجملة الختامية تلخص الفكرة وغالباً تبدأ بعبارة مثل In short."},{"k":"mc","q":"Choose the best topic sentence for a paragraph about the library.","o":["The library is the best place to study in my university.","I went there yesterday.","Books are made of paper."],"a":0,"why":"جملة الموضوع تقدم الفكرة التي ستدعمها الجمل التالية."},{"k":"gap","q":"A supporting sentence gives a reason or an ___.","a":["example"],"why":"جمل الدعم تعطي أسباباً أو أمثلة."},{"k":"fix","q":"My town is quiet. There is a park. My cousin likes pizza.","bad":"My cousin likes pizza.","good":["The people are friendly."],"why":"الجملة الأخيرة خارج الموضوع؛ استبدلها بجملة تدعم فكرة البلدة."},{"k":"order","a":"My town is a good place to live.","ar":"بلدتي مكان جيد للعيش."}]},{"id":"wl-04","l":"A2","title":"Common mistakes of Arabic speakers","titleAr":"أشهر أخطاء الكتابة عند الناطقين بالعربية","bodyAr":"هناك أربعة أخطاء يقع فيها العرب كثيراً، وسببها أن العربية تعمل بطريقة مختلفة.\n**1. الجمل الطويلة المربوطة بـ and:** في العربية نستعمل الواو كثيراً، أما الإنجليزية فتفضّل جملاً قصيرة تنتهي بنقطة. اجعل كل فكرة جملة.\n**2. نسيان is/are:** الجملة العربية الاسمية لا تحتاج فعلاً (الجو جميل)، لكن الإنجليزية تحتاج دائماً فعلاً: The weather is nice.\n**3. the الناقصة أو الزائدة:** نستعمل the مع شيء محدد معروف (the sun, the teacher)، ولا نستعملها مع الكلام العام (I like music, not the music). وللمفرد المعدود نحتاج a/an.\n**4. ترتيب الصفة:** الصفة في العربية بعد الاسم (بيت كبير)، وفي الإنجليزية قبله: a big house. وهي لا تتغير مع الجمع: two big houses.\nنصيحة: بعد الكتابة اسأل ثلاثة أسئلة: هل في كل جملة فعل؟ هل الصفة قبل الاسم؟ هل the في مكانها؟","examples":[{"bad":"My house big.","good":"My house is big.","ar":"الإنجليزية تحتاج فعل to be حتى مع الصفة."},{"bad":"I have a car red.","good":"I have a red car.","ar":"الصفة تأتي قبل الاسم في الإنجليزية."},{"bad":"I like the football.","good":"I like football.","ar":"مع الكلام العام عن شيء نحبه لا نستعمل the."},{"bad":"I woke up and I washed and I ate and I went to school and I met my friend.","good":"I woke up and washed. I ate breakfast and went to school. There I met my friend.","ar":"اقسم الجملة الطويلة إلى جمل قصيرة."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Choose the correct sentence.","o":["The students are happy.","The students happy.","The students is happy."],"a":0,"why":"نحتاج are مع الجمع."},{"k":"mc","q":"Choose the correct sentence.","o":["She is a teacher kind.","She is kind a teacher.","She is a kind teacher."],"a":2,"why":"الصفة تأتي قبل الاسم."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sentence is correct?","o":["I love the nature.","I love nature.","I love a nature."],"a":1,"why":"عندما نتحدث عن الطبيعة بشكل عام لا نضع the."},{"k":"gap","q":"Algeria ___ a big country.","a":["is"],"why":"الجملة تحتاج فعل is حتى لو لم تحتج العربية إلى فعل."},{"k":"fix","q":"My teacher very nice.","bad":"My teacher very nice.","good":["My teacher is very nice."],"why":"الجملة تفتقد الفعل is."},{"k":"fix","q":"I saw a car blue near the school.","bad":"car blue","good":["blue car"],"why":"الصفة تأتي قبل الاسم."}]},{"id":"wl-05","l":"A2","title":"Writing a clear email","titleAr":"كتابة رسالة إلكترونية واضحة","bodyAr":"الرسالة الواضحة تُقرأ بسرعة ويسهل الرد عليها. اتبع هذا الترتيب البسيط.\n**1. التحية:** Dear Mr Hadi, للرسمي وHi Sara, للودي.\n**2. سبب الرسالة في الجملة الأولى:** I am writing to ask about... أو I am writing to invite you to...\n**3. التفاصيل:** اكتب ما يحتاجه القارئ فقط: التاريخ والوقت والمكان والسؤال.\n**4. ما تريده من القارئ:** Please send me... أو Could you tell me...?\n**5. الخاتمة:** Thank you for your help. ثم Best wishes, للودي وYours sincerely, للرسمي مع اسمك.\nاجعل الموضوع (Subject) قصيراً ومحدداً مثل: Question about the exam time.\nلا تكتب بحروف كبيرة كلها، ولا تستعمل الاختصارات مثل plz أو u في الرسائل الرسمية.\nاقرأ رسالتك قبل الإرسال وتأكد أن القارئ يعرف ما المطلوب منه بعد ثلاثين ثانية.","examples":[{"bad":"Hi teacher, give me the exam date.","good":"Dear Mr Hadi, could you tell me the date of the exam, please?","ar":"في الرسالة الرسمية نبدأ بـ Dear ونطلب بأدب بـ could you ... please."},{"bad":"Subject: hello","good":"Subject: Question about the exam time","ar":"الموضوع المحدد يساعد القارئ على فهم الرسالة قبل فتحها."},{"bad":"Thanks, Ali","good":"Thank you for your help. Best wishes, Ali","ar":"الخاتمة المهذبة وعبارة التوقيع تجعل الرسالة كاملة."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which opening is best for a formal email to a teacher?","o":["Hey teacher,","Dear Mrs Amel,","Hi hi,"],"a":1,"why":"Dear + اللقب والاسم هو الأنسب في الرسالة الرسمية."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sentence states the reason for writing?","o":["I am writing to ask about the course.","See you soon.","I like my teacher."],"a":0,"why":"I am writing to ... تذكر سبب الرسالة مباشرة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which ending is formal?","o":["Bye bye,","Yours sincerely,","Cheers,"],"a":1,"why":"Yours sincerely, هي خاتمة الرسالة الرسمية."},{"k":"gap","q":"Could you ___ me the time of the meeting, please?","a":["tell"],"why":"Could you tell me ... طريقة مهذبة للسؤال."},{"k":"fix","q":"Dear Mr Hadi, send me the file.","bad":"send me the file.","good":["could you send me the file, please?","please send me the file."],"why":"الأمر المباشر غير مهذب في رسالة رسمية؛ أضف please أو could you."},{"k":"order","a":"I am writing to ask about the exam.","ar":"أكتب لأسأل عن الامتحان."}]},{"id":"wl-06","l":"B1","title":"Linking words: adding, contrasting, giving examples","titleAr":"كلمات الربط: الإضافة والتناقض والأمثلة","bodyAr":"الكتابة الجيدة لا تعرض أفكاراً متفرقة، بل تربطها. تعلّم ثلاث مجموعات.\n**للإضافة:** also, in addition, moreover, as well as. مثال: The course is cheap. In addition, it is online.\n**للتناقض:** but, however, although, whereas, on the other hand. مثال: Cars are fast. However, they cause pollution.\n**للأمثلة:** for example, for instance, such as. مثال: Many cities have problems, such as traffic and noise.\nانتبه إلى علامات الترقيم: كلمات مثل However وIn addition تأتي في بداية جملة جديدة وبعدها فاصلة، أو بعد فاصلة منقوطة (;). ولا نربط بها جملتين بفاصلة عادية فقط.\nأما although فتربط جملتين في جملة واحدة ولا يأتي بعدها but: Although it was cold, we went out.\nلا تفرط في استعمال الروابط؛ يكفي رابط واحد في كل انتقال حقيقي بين فكرتين.","examples":[{"bad":"The test was hard, however I passed.","good":"The test was hard. However, I passed.","ar":"However تبدأ جملة جديدة وبعدها فاصلة."},{"bad":"Although it was expensive, but I bought it.","good":"Although it was expensive, I bought it.","ar":"لا نستعمل although وbut معاً."},{"bad":"I like fruit, for example, apples are cheap.","good":"I like fruit, for example apples and oranges.","ar":"for example يقدم المثال نفسه وليس جملة جديدة غير مرتبطة."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Choose the best linker: Online lessons are flexible. ___, they save travel time.","o":["However","In addition","For example"],"a":1,"why":"الجملة الثانية تضيف ميزة، فنستعمل In addition."},{"k":"mc","q":"It rained all day. ___, we enjoyed the trip.","o":["Moreover","Nevertheless","For instance"],"a":1,"why":"هنا تناقض بين المطر والاستمتاع، فنحتاج رابط تناقض."},{"k":"mc","q":"Many fruits are rich in vitamins, ___ oranges and kiwis.","o":["such as","however","moreover"],"a":0,"why":"such as تُدخل أمثلة على الفاكهة."},{"k":"gap","q":"___ the road was closed, we arrived on time.","a":["Although","Even though"],"why":"الجملتان متناقضتان ونحتاج رابطاً يبدأ جملة تابعة."},{"k":"fix","q":"The hotel was cheap, however it was clean.","bad":"however","good":["but"],"why":"جملة واحدة بفاصلة قبل however غير صحيحة؛ استعمل but أو ابدأ جملة جديدة."},{"k":"order","a":"Moreover, the course is free for students.","ar":"علاوة على ذلك، الدورة مجانية للطلاب."}]},{"id":"wl-07","l":"B1","title":"Giving an opinion with reasons and an example","titleAr":"إبداء الرأي مع الأسباب والمثال","bodyAr":"الرأي بلا سبب يبدو ضعيفاً. استعمل هذا الهيكل الواضح.\n**1. الرأي:** In my opinion, ... / I believe that ... / I strongly agree that ...\n**2. السبب:** The main reason is that ... / This is because ...\n**3. المثال:** For example, ... / For instance, ...\n**4. الخلاصة:** For this reason, ... / That is why ...\nمثال كامل: In my opinion, public libraries should be free. The main reason is that books are expensive for many families. For example, a student who cannot buy a textbook can borrow it. That is why free libraries help education.\nحاول أن يكون المثال محدداً وسهل التخيل (a student, a small town) بدلاً من الكلام العام.\nويمكنك إظهار أنك تفكر في الرأي الآخر: Some people say ..., but I think ...\nتذكر أن الرأي لا يحتاج كلمة I think في كل جملة؛ جملة واحدة في البداية تكفي.","examples":[{"bad":"Libraries are good because they are good.","good":"Libraries are good because they give free access to books.","ar":"السبب يجب أن يضيف معلومة لا أن يكرر الرأي."},{"bad":"I think cities need parks. It is useful.","good":"I think cities need parks. The main reason is that people can relax and exercise there.","ar":"السبب يحتاج جملة كاملة تشرح لماذا."},{"bad":"Online study is better. Everybody knows it.","good":"In my opinion, online study is better. For example, I can watch a lecture again if I miss something.","ar":"نستبدل الادعاء العام بمثال شخصي محدد."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which phrase introduces an opinion?","o":["In addition,","For example,","In my opinion,"],"a":2,"why":"In my opinion تُستعمل للتصريح بالرأي."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sentence gives a reason?","o":["I like cars.","The main reason is that parking is expensive.","See you tomorrow."],"a":1,"why":"The main reason is that ... تقدم السبب."},{"k":"mc","q":"Choose the best example: Reading helps learners. ___","o":["For example, I learnt ten new words from one short article.","Reading is reading.","Books exist."],"a":0,"why":"المثال المحدد يدعم الفكرة ويسهل تذكرها."},{"k":"gap","q":"In my ___, school uniforms are a good idea.","a":["opinion","view"],"why":"عبارة In my opinion أو In my view تُستعمل للتصريح بالرأي."},{"k":"fix","q":"I believe that homework is useful because it is useful.","bad":"it is useful","good":["it helps students practise","it helps students to practise"],"why":"استبدل السبب المكرر بسبب حقيقي يشرح الفكرة."},{"k":"trans","q":"Many people think that cycling is healthy. I agree.","start":"In my opinion,","a":["In my opinion, cycling is healthy."],"why":"نبدأ بـ In my opinion ثم نكتب الرأي نفسه."}]},{"id":"wl-08","l":"B1","title":"Avoiding repetition: pronouns and synonyms","titleAr":"تجنب التكرار: الضمائر والمرادفات","bodyAr":"تكرار الكلمة نفسها يجعل الفقرة ثقيلة. هناك ثلاث طرق سهلة لتجنب ذلك.\n**1. الضمائر:** he, she, it, they, this. بدل: The teacher is kind. The teacher helps us. اكتب: The teacher is kind. She helps us.\n**2. المرادفات:** كلمات قريبة في المعنى: big / large، buy / purchase، problem / difficulty، important / essential.\n**3. كلمات عامة تلخص الفكرة:** this problem, this idea, these changes. مثال: The city built new roads. These changes reduced traffic.\nانتبه: يجب أن يكون واضحاً إلى ماذا يشير الضمير. إذا كانت هناك امرأتان في الجملة فلا تكتب she بلا توضيح.\nكذلك لا تستبدل كل كلمة بمرادف؛ فالمصطلحات الأساسية (مثل transparency) من الأفضل أن تبقى كما هي حتى لا يختلط المعنى.\nاقرأ فقرتك وضع خطاً تحت أي كلمة ظهرت ثلاث مرات، ثم غيّر مرتين منها.","examples":[{"bad":"My brother has a car. My brother's car is old. My brother wants a new car.","good":"My brother has an old car. He wants a new one.","ar":"نستعمل ضميراً وكلمة one لتجنب التكرار."},{"bad":"The report is long. The report is difficult.","good":"The report is long. It is also difficult.","ar":"الضمير it يحل محل the report."},{"bad":"The city has a big problem. The problem is traffic.","good":"The city has a serious problem. This problem is traffic.","ar":"نستعمل مرادفاً للصفة ونشير بـ This للفكرة السابقة."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Replace the repeated words: Mina is a doctor. Mina works in a hospital.","o":["Mina she works in a hospital.","She works in a hospital.","Her work in a hospital."],"a":1,"why":"الضمير She يحل محل Mina."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which word is a synonym of 'big'?","o":["quiet","tiny","large"],"a":2,"why":"large بمعنى big أي كبير."},{"k":"mc","q":"The company hired new workers. ___ decision saved money.","o":["This","Those","Them"],"a":0,"why":"This decision تلخص الفكرة السابقة."},{"k":"gap","q":"My parents live in Oran. ___ visit us every summer.","a":["They"],"why":"They يحل محل My parents."},{"k":"fix","q":"The library is big. The library it is quiet.","bad":"library it","good":["library"],"why":"لا نكرر الفاعل بالاسم والضمير معاً."},{"k":"trans","q":"Sara bought a laptop. Sara uses the laptop every day.","start":"Sara bought a laptop and","a":["Sara bought a laptop and uses it every day."],"why":"نحذف تكرار Sara ونستعمل it بدل the laptop."}]},{"id":"wl-09","l":"B1","title":"Formal and informal style","titleAr":"الأسلوب الرسمي وغير الرسمي","bodyAr":"الأسلوب هو اختيار الكلمات المناسبة للقارئ. اكتب بأسلوب غير رسمي مع صديق، وبأسلوب رسمي مع أستاذ أو مؤسسة أو في بحث.\n**في الأسلوب الرسمي:**\n- لا نستعمل الاختصارات: I am وليس I'm، cannot وليس can't.\n- نستعمل أفعالاً أطول: ask → enquire، get → receive، need → require، help → assist.\n- نستعمل عبارات مهذبة: I would be grateful if you could ...\n- لا نستعمل كلمات عامية مثل guys وkids وawesome.\n**في الأسلوب غير الرسمي:** يمكنك استعمال الاختصارات والجمل القصيرة وعلامة التعجب: Thanks a lot! See you soon.\nقارن: Informal: Can you send me the file? Formal: Could you please send me the file?\nاختر أسلوباً واحداً وثبّته في النص كله. خلط الأسلوبين يجعل الكلام غريباً: لا تبدأ بـ Dear Professor ثم تقول Hey, thanks!\nعند الشك، اختر الأسلوب الرسمي الأبسط.","examples":[{"bad":"Hi Professor, I can't come to class, sorry.","good":"Dear Professor, I am unable to attend class. I apologise.","ar":"الرسالة للأستاذ تحتاج أسلوباً رسمياً بلا اختصارات."},{"bad":"Can you help me, guys?","good":"Could you please assist me?","ar":"كلمة guys غير رسمية والسؤال بـ could أكثر أدباً."},{"bad":"I need the form ASAP!","good":"I would be grateful if you could send me the form soon.","ar":"في الأسلوب الرسمي نستعمل صيغة مهذبة بدل الأمر."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"Which sentence is formal?","o":["I'd like to ask about the course.","I would like to enquire about the course.","Tell me about the course."],"a":1,"why":"الصيغة الكاملة وكلمة enquire رسميتان."},{"k":"mc","q":"Choose the informal ending.","o":["Yours faithfully,","Yours sincerely,","See you soon!"],"a":2,"why":"See you soon! عبارة ودية غير رسمية."},{"k":"mc","q":"A more formal word for 'get' in 'I got your letter' is:","o":["receive","grab","find"],"a":0,"why":"received أكثر رسمية من got."},{"k":"gap","q":"___ you please send me the report?","a":["Could","Would"],"why":"Could أو Would تجعل الطلب مهذباً."},{"k":"fix","q":"Dear Dr Hadi, I can't attend the meeting.","bad":"can't","good":["cannot","am unable to"],"why":"الأسلوب الرسمي لا يستعمل الاختصار."},{"k":"trans","q":"Can you help me with my homework?","start":"Could you","a":["Could you please help me with my homework?","Could you help me with my homework?"],"why":"Could you أكثر تهذيباً من Can you."}]},{"id":"wl-10","l":"B1","title":"From paragraph to essay","titleAr":"من الفقرة إلى المقال","bodyAr":"المقال هو عدة فقرات مترابطة حول فكرة واحدة. له ثلاثة أقسام.\n**1. المقدمة (Introduction):** فقرة قصيرة تعرّف الموضوع وتعطي رأيك أو خطة المقال. مثال: Many students use phones in class. In this essay, I will explain why this is a problem.\n**2. الجسم (Body):** فقرة أو فقرتان أو ثلاث، كل فقرة لها جملة موضوع وفكرة واحدة مع دعم ومثال.\n**3. الخاتمة (Conclusion):** فقرة قصيرة تلخص الأفكار وتعيد الرأي بكلمات مختلفة. تبدأ بـ In conclusion, أو To sum up, ولا نضيف فيها فكرة جديدة.\nيمكن أن يكون المقال البسيط من أربع فقرات: مقدمة، فقرتان في الجسم، خاتمة.\nاستعمل روابط بين الفقرات مثل Firstly وSecondly وIn addition وHowever لكي ينتقل القارئ بسهولة.\nقبل الكتابة سجّل خطة من ثلاثة أسطر: رأيي، السبب الأول، السبب الثاني. ثم اكتب المقال حول هذه الخطة.","examples":[{"bad":"Phones are bad. I hate them. The end.","good":"Phones in class cause problems. In this essay, I will explain two main reasons.","ar":"المقدمة الجيدة تعرّف الموضوع وتعد القارئ بخطة."},{"bad":"Also, phones are good for maps.","good":"In conclusion, phones should be limited in class.","ar":"الخاتمة تلخص الرأي ولا تضيف فكرة جديدة."},{"bad":"Paragraph 2: Many things. Paragraph 3: More things.","good":"Paragraph 2: Phones distract students. Paragraph 3: Phones reduce talking between students.","ar":"كل فقرة في الجسم تعالج فكرة واحدة محددة."}],"exs":[{"k":"mc","q":"What belongs in a conclusion?","o":["A long example","A completely new argument","A summary of the main ideas"],"a":2,"why":"الخاتمة تلخص الأفكار ولا تأتي بفكرة جديدة."},{"k":"mc","q":"Which sentence is the best introduction?","o":["Nowadays, many students use AI tools. This essay discusses whether this is helpful.","I have a cat.","There are many things in the world and I like them."],"a":0,"why":"المقدمة تعرض الموضوع وهدف المقال."},{"k":"mc","q":"A body paragraph should have","o":["five different ideas","one main idea with support","only a question"],"a":1,"why":"كل فقرة في الجسم لها فكرة رئيسية واحدة مع دعم."},{"k":"gap","q":"___ conclusion, online learning has more advantages than disadvantages.","a":["In"],"why":"عبارة In conclusion تبدأ الخاتمة."},{"k":"fix","q":"I will present my opinion in introduction.","bad":"in introduction","good":["in the introduction"],"why":"نقول in the introduction مع the لأن المقصود مقدمة هذا المقال."},{"k":"order","a":"In conclusion, cities should publish their budgets.","ar":"في الخلاصة، ينبغي للمدن أن تنشر ميزانياتها."}]}]);
Fasih.add("w2",[{"id":"w2-01","l":"B1","type":"opinion","topic":"education","prompt":"Some people think that primary school children should not be given homework. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وبيّن موقفك بوضوح (أوافق إلى حد كبير). الفقرة الأولى: السبب الأول مع مثال (الأطفال متعبون بعد اليوم الدراسي). الفقرة الثانية: السبب الثاني مع مثال (اللعب والأسرة يعلّمان مهارات أخرى)، ثم اذكر باختصار الرأي المخالف. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك في جملتين.","ideas":["Young children are tired after a long school day","Play and sleep help the brain to learn","Homework can make children hate learning","Parents who are busy cannot always help","A little reading at home can build good habits"],"vocab":["homework","concentrate","free time","a good habit","under pressure","as a result","take part in","responsibility"],"model":"I largely agree that primary school children should not be given homework. Young children spend most of the day in class, and they need their evenings for rest, play and time with their families.\n\nThe first reason is that small children get tired quickly. After six hours of lessons, a seven-year-old cannot concentrate well on more written work. When parents force a child to sit at a table, he often starts to see learning as a punishment. For example, a girl who cries over her maths sheet every night may come to hate the subject, even if she was good at it. Free time, by contrast, gives children the energy they need for the next school day.\n\nAnother reason is that play teaches skills which worksheets cannot. When children play football with their neighbours or help to cook dinner, they learn teamwork, patience and responsibility. Reading a story with a parent for fifteen minutes can also do more for a child's language than a page of copied words. These activities are not formal homework, but they help children to grow in a natural way.\n\nHowever, I accept that a little homework can be useful. It shows parents what their children are learning, and it may help older pupils to form the habit of studying before they start secondary school. For this reason, I would not ban it completely for children aged ten and eleven. Short tasks, such as reading for ten minutes, would be enough.\n\nIn conclusion, homework for very young children does more harm than good, because it makes them tired and takes away valuable time for play and family life. Schools should keep it very light in the later primary years and avoid it for the youngest pupils, so that children can enjoy learning instead of fearing it.","notesAr":"1) الموقف واضح من الجملة الأولى (largely agree) ويتكرر في الخاتمة دون نسخ حرفي. 2) كل فقرة رئيسية فيها سبب ثم مثال مرسوم بالذهن (الفتاة وورقة الحساب، كرة القدم مع الجيران). 3) الفقرة الرابعة تعترف بالرأي المخالف بإيجاز، وهذا يدل على تفكير متوازن ويرفع درجة التماسك والمنطق."},{"id":"w2-02","l":"B1","type":"discuss","topic":"family","prompt":"Some young people prefer to live with their parents after they finish their studies. Others think it is better to live alone. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر الرأيين وأعلن أنك ستناقشهما. الفقرة الأولى: مزايا العيش مع الأهل (توفير المال، الدعم العائلي) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: مزايا العيش وحيدا (الاستقلال، تحمل المسؤولية) مع مثال. ثم عبّر عن رأيك الشخصي في فقرة قصيرة. الخاتمة: لخّص الرأيين وموقفك.","ideas":["Living at home saves money on rent and food","Family can give emotional and practical support","Living alone teaches young people to be independent","Some jobs are far from the family home","Privacy and freedom are important for adults"],"vocab":["independent","rent","bills","support","privacy","responsible","move out","manage money"]},{"id":"w2-03","l":"B1","type":"problem","topic":"health","prompt":"In many countries, more and more children eat fast food and do very little exercise. What are the causes of this problem and what can be done to solve it?","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف المشكلة باختصار وبيّن أنك ستذكر الأسباب والحلول. الفقرة الأولى: سببان أو ثلاثة مع مثال لكل واحد (الإعلانات، انشغال الأهل، الألعاب الإلكترونية). الفقرة الثانية: حلان أو ثلاثة مع مثال (وجبات صحية في المدارس، رياضة يومية، توعية الأهل). الخاتمة: لخّص الأسباب والحلول.","ideas":["Busy parents buy cheap, quick meals","Adverts make fast food look attractive","Children spend hours on phones and games","Schools could serve healthy lunches","Daily sport lessons should be compulsory","Parents can cook with their children"],"vocab":["fast food","obesity","unhealthy","exercise","advertisement","balanced diet","habit","cut down on"]},{"id":"w2-04","l":"B1","type":"advdis","topic":"work","prompt":"More and more people now work from home instead of going to an office. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وحدد رأيك (المزايا أكبر). الفقرة الأولى: المزايا مع مثال (توفير الوقت والمال، المرونة). الفقرة الثانية: العيوب مع مثال (الوحدة، صعوبة الفصل بين العمل والحياة)، ثم اذكر كيف يمكن تقليلها. الخاتمة: لخّص الميزان وأكد رأيك.","ideas":["Workers save time and money on travel","Flexible hours suit parents and people with health problems","Companies can rent smaller offices","Some workers feel lonely at home","It is hard to switch off after work","New staff learn faster in an office"],"vocab":["commute","flexible","colleague","office","distraction","productive","lonely","switch off"],"model":"In my opinion, the advantages of working from home are greater than the disadvantages, although the situation is not perfect for everyone.\n\nThe main advantage is the time and money that workers save. Many employees spend two hours a day on crowded buses or in traffic, and they also pay for fuel or tickets. A man who works from his living room can start earlier, finish on time and use the saved hours to rest or to be with his children. Companies benefit as well, because they can rent smaller offices and pay lower bills. This is why several large firms now allow their staff to work at home two or three days a week.\n\nAnother benefit is flexibility. People with young children or health problems can arrange their day around their needs. For example, a mother who is a translator can work while her baby sleeps and send the finished text in the evening. In this way, talented people do not have to leave their jobs.\n\nOn the other hand, there are real drawbacks. Workers at home can feel lonely, because they miss the small talk and the support of colleagues. It can also be hard to separate work from private life; some people answer emails late at night and never really switch off. Moreover, young employees learn faster when they sit next to experienced workers and can ask questions at once.\n\nNevertheless, I believe these problems can be solved. Companies can organise regular meetings in the office, and workers can set fixed hours and choose a quiet corner for their job.\n\nIn conclusion, working from home saves time and money and gives people more freedom, so the benefits outweigh the costs, provided that employers and employees manage it carefully.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تجيب عن السؤال مباشرة (الميزة أكبر) ثم يبقى الموقف ثابتا حتى النهاية. 2) الجمل الرابطة طبيعية (On the other hand, Moreover, Nevertheless) دون عبارات محفوظة مبتذلة. 3) العيوب لم تُهمل: ذُكرت ثم عُولجت بحلول عملية، فتبدو الحجة متوازنة ومقنعة."},{"id":"w2-05","l":"B1","type":"twopart","topic":"transport","prompt":"In many cities, a lot of people travel to work by car instead of using buses or trains. Why do you think this happens? Is this a positive or a negative development?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأعلن أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: أجب عن السؤال الأول (لماذا؟) بسببين مع مثال (راحة السيارة، ضعف المواصلات العامة). الفقرة الثانية: أجب عن السؤال الثاني بوضوح (سلبي في رأيي) مع مثال (ازدحام وتلوث). الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين.","ideas":["Cars are comfortable and always available","Public transport can be slow, crowded or late","Many workplaces are far from stations","Too many cars cause traffic jams and pollution","Parking costs time and money"],"vocab":["traffic jam","public transport","convenient","pollution","fuel","timetable","reliable","in a hurry"]},{"id":"w2-06","l":"B1","type":"opinion","topic":"environment","prompt":"Some people believe that the government should make recycling compulsory by law. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وبيّن موقفك. الفقرة الأولى: سبب أول مع مثال (القانون يغيّر السلوك بسرعة كما في حزام الأمان). الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثان أو اعتراض مع رد (الغرامات قد تكون ظالمة، لذا نحتاج حاويات سهلة وتوعية). الخاتمة: أكّد موقفك.","ideas":["Laws make people change their habits quickly","Recycling saves energy and raw materials","Fines may be unfair for poor families","Clear bins in every street make recycling easy","Schools can teach children to sort rubbish"],"vocab":["recycle","rubbish","landfill","fine","compulsory","raw materials","sort","waste"]},{"id":"w2-07","l":"B1","type":"discuss","topic":"crime and justice","prompt":"Some people think that criminals who commit small crimes should do community work instead of going to prison. Others believe that prison is the best punishment. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر الرأيين وأعلن أنك ستناقشهما ثم تعطي رأيك. الفقرة الأولى: حجج مؤيدي السجن (الردع، حماية المجتمع) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: حجج مؤيدي الخدمة المجتمعية (التكلفة، تجنب الاحتكاك بالمجرمين الخطرين) مع مثال. فقرة قصيرة لرأيك الشخصي. الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Prison protects society from dangerous people","Fear of prison may stop other people from breaking the law","Prison is expensive for the state","Minor offenders may learn bad habits from serious criminals","Community work helps repair the damage"],"vocab":["offender","sentence","community work","punishment","deter","victim","first-time offender","break the law"],"model":"People disagree about the best way to punish those who commit minor crimes. Some believe that community work is better, while others think that prison is the only real punishment. I will discuss both views before giving my opinion.\n\nThose who support prison argue that it protects society and sends a strong message. A person who steals or damages property and then spends a few weeks behind bars is likely to think twice before doing it again. Other people, seeing this, may also decide not to break the law. In addition, victims often feel that justice has been done when the offender is clearly punished.\n\nHowever, there are good reasons to prefer community work for small crimes. First, prison is expensive; the state pays for food, guards and buildings, and this money could be spent on schools or hospitals. Second, prison can make things worse. A young man who is sent there for stealing a phone may meet serious criminals and learn bad habits from them. When he comes out, he may have no job and no friends, so he returns to crime. By contrast, a person who must clean parks or help in a care home for two hundred hours repairs some of the damage and stays in contact with his family and employer.\n\nIn my opinion, community work is the better choice for first-time offenders who have committed small, non-violent crimes. It is cheaper, it helps the community directly and it gives people a chance to change. However, prison should remain for dangerous people, such as those who attack others, because the public must be kept safe.\n\nIn conclusion, although prison has a strong deterrent effect, community work is more useful and fairer for minor offences, and I support it in those cases.","notesAr":"1) المقال يغطي الرأيين بوضوح في فقرتين منفصلتين ثم يعطي الرأي الشخصي في فقرة مستقلة، وهذا ما يطلبه السؤال. 2) الأمثلة محسوسة (شاب سرق هاتفا، تنظيف الحدائق، دار رعاية). 3) الرأي مشروط بدقة (الجرائم الصغيرة وغير العنيفة فقط)، فيبدو ناضجا وغير متطرف."},{"id":"w2-08","l":"B1","type":"opinion","topic":"work","prompt":"Some people think that employees should work only four days a week instead of five. Do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الفكرة وبيّن موقفك. الفقرة الأولى: سبب أول مع مثال (الراحة ترفع الإنتاجية). الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثان مع مثال (وقت أكثر للأسرة والتعلم)، مع الإشارة إلى مشكلة محتملة وحلها (بعض الوظائف تحتاج دواما كاملا). الخاتمة: أكّد رأيك.","ideas":["Rested workers make fewer mistakes","A longer weekend gives time for family and hobbies","Some jobs, such as hospitals, need staff every day","Employees may work longer hours on four days","Companies may save on electricity and office costs"],"vocab":["employee","working week","productive","stress","weekend","schedule","salary","well-being"]},{"id":"w2-09","l":"B1","type":"problem","topic":"cities","prompt":"Many city centres are very noisy and crowded. What problems does this cause for the people who live there, and what can be done about it?","planAr":"المقدمة: صف المشكلة باختصار وأعلن بنية المقال. الفقرة الأولى: المشكلات مع مثال (قلة النوم، التوتر، صعوبة التنقل). الفقرة الثانية: الحلول مع مثال (مناطق للمشاة، تحديد ساعات الشاحنات، مساحات خضراء). الخاتمة: لخّص المشكلات وأفضل حل.","ideas":["Noise makes it hard to sleep and study","Crowded streets are stressful and unsafe","Air pollution harms health","Pedestrian zones can reduce traffic","More parks give people quiet places","Lorries could deliver only at night or early morning"],"vocab":["crowded","noise pollution","residents","pedestrian","stress","green space","ban","public transport"]},{"id":"w2-10","l":"B1","type":"advdis","topic":"media","prompt":"Nowadays many people get their news from social media instead of newspapers or television. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وحدد رأيك (العيوب أكبر قليلا). الفقرة الأولى: المزايا مع مثال (السرعة، المجانية، صور من موقع الحدث). الفقرة الثانية: العيوب مع مثال (الإشاعات، رؤية ما نحب فقط). الخاتمة: لخّص وقدّم نصيحة عملية.","ideas":["News arrives within minutes","It is free and easy to use","Ordinary people can post photos from the scene","False stories spread very fast","Apps show us only what we already like","Short headlines hide the full story"],"vocab":["headline","rumour","reliable source","up to date","spread","fact-check","opinion","subscription"],"model":"Social media has become the main source of news for millions of people. In my view, this trend has both benefits and dangers, but the disadvantages are slightly greater.\n\nThe biggest advantage is speed and convenience. When something important happens, such as a storm or a road accident, people can read about it within minutes on their phones. They do not have to wait for the evening bulletin or the next morning's newspaper. Social media is also free and easy to use, so someone who cannot afford a newspaper subscription can still stay informed. In addition, ordinary people can post photos and videos from the scene, which gives us a fuller picture of events.\n\nHowever, the disadvantages are serious. The main problem is false information. Anyone can post a story without checking the facts, and shocking stories spread faster than careful ones. For example, a false rumour that a local bridge was closed could send thousands of drivers onto other roads and cause a huge traffic jam. Newspapers and television channels, by contrast, usually employ editors who check facts before they publish.\n\nAnother problem is that social media shows us only what we like. The apps choose posts that match our interests, so people rarely see other opinions. Over time, this can make them narrow-minded and less willing to listen to others. Moreover, people who read only short headlines may not understand the full story.\n\nTo sum up, social media gives fast, free and personal news, but it also spreads rumours and limits our view of the world. In my opinion, the risks are greater, so people should use it together with trusted sources, such as established newspapers, and check any surprising story before sharing it.","notesAr":"1) الإجابة مباشرة: المزايا والعيوب مذكورة والحكم النهائي واضح (العيوب أكبر). 2) المثال (إشاعة إغلاق الجسر) سهل التخيل ويربط السبب بنتيجة ملموسة. 3) الخاتمة تضيف توصية عملية بدل تكرار الكلمات نفسها."},{"id":"w2-11","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"government and society","prompt":"Senior public officials should be legally required to publish details of their income and property every year. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الفكرة وصرّح بموقفك (أوافق بشدة أو بتحفظ). الفقرة الأولى: حجة الشفافية والردع مع مثال (مقارنة الثروة بالراتب تكشف الإثراء غير المشروع). الفقرة الثانية: الرد على الاعتراضات (الخصوصية والأمن) مع حل وسط (نشر مع حماية البيانات الشخصية الحساسة). الخاتمة: أكّد موقفك.","ideas":["Public officials are paid by taxpayers and should be accountable","Open records make illegal enrichment easier to detect","Journalists and citizens can compare wealth with salary","Privacy and safety concerns can be met by limited details","Honest officials have nothing to fear","Trust in institutions grows when rules apply to everyone"],"vocab":["accountable","transparency","public official","asset declaration","illicit enrichment","conflict of interest","disclose","integrity"]},{"id":"w2-12","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"education","prompt":"Some people believe that university education should be free for all students. Others think that students should pay for their own studies. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اعرض الرأيين وأعلن نهجك. الفقرة الأولى: حجج التعليم المجاني (تكافؤ الفرص، حاجة الدولة إلى مهنيين) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: حجج دفع الرسوم (عبء الميزانية، الجدية والتحفيز) مع مثال. ثم رأيك (مثلا: مجاني مع نظام قروض أو منح). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Free tuition gives talented poor students a fair chance","Society benefits from more doctors, engineers and teachers","Public budgets are limited and universities need funding","Students who pay may take their studies more seriously","Grants and income-based loans can balance both sides"],"vocab":["tuition fees","scholarship","equal opportunity","graduate","public funding","burden","student loan","qualification"]},{"id":"w2-13","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"law and public integrity","prompt":"In many countries, public money is lost because officials accept bribes when awarding contracts for building projects. What are the causes of this problem, and what measures could be taken to solve it?","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف المشكلة وأعلن أنك ستعرض الأسباب ثم الحلول. الفقرة الأولى: الأسباب مع أمثلة (غياب الشفافية في المناقصات، تدني الرواتب، ضعف العقوبات). الفقرة الثانية: الحلول مع أمثلة (نشر بيانات العقود إلكترونيا، رقابة مستقلة، عقوبات رادعة، حماية المبلّغين). الخاتمة: اربط الحلول بالأسباب.","ideas":["Secret tendering lets officials favour one company","Very low salaries can push clerks towards bribes","Weak punishment makes the risk seem small","Publishing contracts online allows public checking","Independent auditors should report to parliament","Whistleblowers need legal protection"],"vocab":["bribery","tender","audit","transparency","whistleblower","deterrent","public contract","inflated costs"],"model":"Corruption in public contracts drains billions from budgets that should pay for schools, roads and hospitals. This essay will examine the main causes and propose practical solutions.\n\nOne important cause is the lack of transparency in the way contracts are awarded. When tenders are decided behind closed doors, an official can favour a company that offers him a private payment, and nobody outside the room can see it. Low pay is a second cause. A clerk who earns barely enough to feed his family may see a bribe as the only way to survive, especially when everyone around him seems to do the same. Finally, weak punishment encourages the habit; if officials who are caught only lose their post and keep their wealth, the risk seems small compared with the reward.\n\nSeveral measures could address these causes. First, governments should publish every stage of the tender online, including the list of bidders, the criteria and the final price. When citizens, journalists and rival companies can compare the figures, a suspiciously expensive bridge is much harder to hide. Some cities have already placed contract data on open websites, and local reporters have used them to question inflated costs.\n\nSecond, independent audit bodies should have the power to check projects and report directly to parliament, not to the ministers they are examining. Third, courts must apply real penalties, including prison and the return of stolen money, to officials and the companies that paid them. Fair salaries and protection for staff who report wrongdoing would also reduce the pressure to take part in it.\n\nIn conclusion, contract corruption grows where decisions are secret, salaries are low and punishment is weak. Open data, independent auditing, firm sentences and better protection for honest staff would together make bribery far more difficult and restore public trust.","notesAr":"1) البنية تطابق السؤال تماما: فقرة للأسباب وفقرتان للحلول، وكل حل مرتبط بسبب مذكور سابقا (الشفافية، الرواتب، العقوبات). 2) المثال المحسوس (جسر مكلف بشكل مشبوه، صحفيون محليون) يجعل الفكرة قابلة للتصور. 3) مفردات الموضوع دقيقة (tenders, audit bodies, return of stolen money) دون حشو."},{"id":"w2-14","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"science","prompt":"Governments spend large sums of money on space exploration. Why do they do this? Do you think this money could be better spent on other things?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأعلن أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: لماذا تنفق الحكومات؟ (الاكتشافات العلمية، التقنيات المشتقة كالأقمار الاصطناعية، المكانة الوطنية) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: هل يمكن إنفاقه بشكل أفضل؟ قدّم رأيك مع مثال (الصحة والتعليم أولى، لكن بعض الإنفاق مبرر). الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين.","ideas":["Space research leads to useful technology such as satellites","Weather forecasts and navigation depend on space systems","Countries want scientific prestige and cooperation","Hunger and disease are urgent problems on Earth","A small share of the budget can support both goals"],"vocab":["space exploration","satellite","budget","prestige","scientific breakthrough","priority","spin-off technology","invest in"]},{"id":"w2-15","l":"B2","type":"advdis","topic":"law and technology","prompt":"Some courts are beginning to use computer programs to help judges review evidence and suggest sentences. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وحدد رأيك (مثلا: المزايا أكبر بشرط بقاء القرار بيد القاضي). الفقرة الأولى: المزايا مع مثال (السرعة، الاتساق في الأحكام، تقليل التحيز البشري). الفقرة الثانية: العيوب مع مثال (تحيز البيانات، غموض الخوارزمية، ضعف المساءلة) وكيف يمكن تقليلها. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك.","ideas":["Programs can review thousands of documents quickly","Consistent advice may reduce unfair differences between sentences","Data used to train a program may contain old bias","Defendants must be able to challenge how a result was reached","A human judge must stay responsible for the final decision"],"vocab":["algorithm","bias","consistent","sentence","defendant","evidence","accountability","due process"]},{"id":"w2-16","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"crime and justice","prompt":"The main purpose of prison should be to prepare offenders for a useful life after release, not to punish them. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة العبارة وصرّح بموقفك (أوافق إلى حد كبير). الفقرة الأولى: حجة التأهيل مع مثال (التدريب المهني يقلل العودة إلى الجريمة). الفقرة الثانية: الظروف القاسية لا تصلح الناس، ثم اعترف بدور العقوبة وفق الجرائم الخطيرة. الخاتمة: أكّد موقفك بصياغة جديدة.","ideas":["Most prisoners return to society one day","Job training reduces reoffending","Harsh conditions can make people angrier","Loss of freedom is already a punishment","Dangerous offenders must be kept away from the public"],"vocab":["rehabilitation","reoffend","inmate","custody","sentence","vocational training","deterrent","reintegrate"],"model":"I agree to a large extent that prisons should concentrate on preparing offenders for life after release, although I do not think punishment can be ignored completely.\n\nThe strongest argument for rehabilitation is that most prisoners will eventually return to society. A man who serves five years for theft and leaves with no skills, no job and a criminal record is very likely to steal again. Prisons that offer education and training reduce this risk. For instance, an inmate who learns carpentry or computer repair can apply for a real job afterwards, and a steady salary gives him a reason to stay away from crime. Every person who does not reoffend means fewer victims and lower costs for the state, so rehabilitation protects the public better than long, empty sentences.\n\nIn addition, harsh conditions often make people angrier rather than wiser. Overcrowded cells and constant idleness can turn a minor offender into a bitter one. Programmes in which prisoners study, work and receive counselling for anger or addiction treat the causes of their behaviour instead of just locking it away. Several countries that have invested in such programmes report that fewer former prisoners return to custody.\n\nHowever, I do not believe that punishment has no role. Losing one's freedom is itself a punishment, and it shows victims and the public that serious crimes have consequences. Dangerous offenders must also be kept apart from society for as long as they are a threat. Even so, prisons should offer them a way back.\n\nIn conclusion, the primary goal of imprisonment should be to change behaviour, because that is the surest way to make communities safer. Punishment through the loss of liberty is necessary, but it should be the starting point of a prisoner's journey back to society, not its final purpose.","notesAr":"1) الموقف مدروس: موافقة قوية مع تحفظ محدد (الجرائم الخطيرة)، وهذا أفضل من الموافقة المطلقة. 2) الحجة الأولى فيها سلسلة منطقية واضحة: مهارة، ثم وظيفة، ثم راتب، ثم ابتعاد عن الجريمة. 3) الخاتمة تعيد الفكرة بصياغة جديدة وصورة ختامية (بداية رحلة العودة) بدل تكرار الجمل."},{"id":"w2-17","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"health","prompt":"Some people think that governments should do more to improve public health, for example by taxing unhealthy food. Others believe that people should take responsibility for their own health. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اعرض الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: دور الحكومة مع مثال (ضرائب على المشروبات المحلاة، قيود على الإعلانات، فحوص مجانية). الفقرة الثانية: مسؤولية الفرد مع مثال (الغذاء والرياضة قرارات شخصية، والتدخل قد يقيّد الحرية). ثم رأيك (توازن بين الاثنين). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Taxes on sugary drinks can reduce consumption","Poor families may lack healthy, affordable food","Public campaigns and clear food labels help people choose","Adults should be free to make personal choices","Healthy habits begin at home and in school"],"vocab":["public health","tax","obesity","regulate","personal responsibility","preventive","label","healthcare costs"]},{"id":"w2-18","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"environment","prompt":"Huge amounts of plastic waste end up in rivers and seas around the world. What are the main causes of this problem, and what can governments and individuals do to reduce it?","planAr":"المقدمة: وضّح خطورة المشكلة وأعلن البنية. الفقرة الأولى: الأسباب مع أمثلة (الاستخدام الواحد، ضعف جمع النفايات، التصريف من المصانع). الفقرة الثانية: ما تفعله الحكومات (حظر الأكياس، مسؤولية المنتجين، أنظمة إعادة التدوير) وما يفعله الأفراد (أكياس قابلة لإعادة الاستعمال). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Single-use packaging is cheap and everywhere","Poor waste collection lets rubbish blow into rivers","Fishing nets and factory waste add to the problem","Governments can ban or tax plastic bags","Producers should pay for collecting their packaging","Individuals can reuse bottles and bags"],"vocab":["single-use","marine life","waste collection","biodegradable","ban","landfill","pollute","packaging"]},{"id":"w2-19","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"media","prompt":"Many people share news stories online without checking whether they are true. Why do people do this? What can be done to reduce the spread of false information?","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الظاهرة وأعلن أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: لماذا يشارك الناس؟ (سهولة المشاركة، العاطفة، الثقة بالأصدقاء) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: الحلول مع مثال (التربية الإعلامية في المدارس، تعديل تصميم المنصات، نشر الحكومات معلومات دقيقة بسرعة). الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين.","ideas":["One tap shares a message with hundreds of people","Shocking stories make us react before we think","We trust stories posted by friends and relatives","Schools can teach media literacy","Platforms could ask users to read before sharing","Reliable information must be published quickly"],"vocab":["misinformation","media literacy","share","reliable source","viral","verify","impulse","platform"],"model":"People often forward startling stories within seconds of seeing them, and false information can travel across the world before experts have time to respond. There are clear reasons for this behaviour, and there are also practical ways to limit the damage.\n\nFirstly, sharing is quick and costs nothing. A single tap sends a message to hundreds of friends, so people act on impulse without stopping to think. Secondly, emotional stories attract attention. A post that makes us angry or frightened feels urgent, and we want to warn others. Finally, many people trust a story simply because a relative or close friend posted it, and they assume that someone else has already checked it. For example, a false message claiming that a common medicine was dangerous was passed on by thousands of worried parents who only wanted to protect their children.\n\nTo tackle the problem, schools should teach media literacy from an early age. Pupils could practise checking who wrote an article, whether other reliable sources report the same facts and whether a photo has been used before. Adults who learn these habits are less likely to be misled. In addition, social media companies could add a short pause before a post is shared, asking users whether they have read the article. Small delays like this can reduce thoughtless sharing. Platforms should also label disputed claims clearly and make corrections easy to see.\n\nGovernments can play a part as well by publishing accurate information quickly and openly, so that rumours have less space to grow. However, they must avoid censorship, which can damage public trust.\n\nIn conclusion, false stories spread because sharing is effortless, emotion beats caution and people trust their friends. Education, better platform design and transparent public information together offer the best chance of slowing them down.","notesAr":"1) كل سؤال له إجابة منفصلة ومنظمة: ثلاثة أسباب (Firstly, Secondly, Finally) ثم حلول متعددة، فلا يضيع أي جزء من المطلوب. 2) المثال (رسالة كاذبة عن دواء ينشرها أولياء أمور قلقون) عاطفي وسهل الحفظ. 3) تنبيه ذكي في النهاية: الحل لا يجب أن يتحول إلى رقابة، وهذا يدل على تفكير ناقد."},{"id":"w2-20","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"culture","prompt":"As the world becomes more connected, traditional cultures and local languages are disappearing, and this is a harmful development. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الفكرة وبيّن موقفك. الفقرة الأولى: لماذا هو ضار؟ (فقدان الهوية والمعرفة التقليدية) مع مثال (لغة محلية لا ينطقها إلا كبار السن). الفقرة الثانية: الجانب الآخر (الثقافات تتغير دائما، والتواصل يفيد) مع مثال، ثم حلول (تعليم اللغات المحلية، التوثيق الرقمي). الخاتمة: أكّد موقفك.","ideas":["Languages carry stories, medicine and local knowledge","Young people may prefer global brands and foreign languages","Cultures have always changed through contact","Technology can record songs, crafts and languages","Schools can teach local heritage"],"vocab":["heritage","tradition","globalisation","identity","endangered language","preserve","cultural diversity","adapt"]},{"id":"w2-21","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"work","prompt":"Some people prefer a secure job with a modest salary. Others would rather take a better-paid job that is less secure. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اعرض الرأيين وأعلن نهجك. الفقرة الأولى: مزايا الوظيفة المستقرة مع مثال (قرض سكن، استقرار الأسرة). الفقرة الثانية: مزايا الوظيفة الأعلى أجرا مع مثال (ادخار أسرع، خبرة أوسع). ثم رأيك (يعتمد على المرحلة العمرية والالتزامات). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["A steady income allows families to plan ahead","Secure jobs reduce stress and help in getting loans","Higher pay can help people save and invest","Risky jobs may teach new skills quickly","Young people without family duties can take more risks"],"vocab":["job security","salary","stable income","risk","career prospects","redundancy","benefits","long-term"]},{"id":"w2-22","l":"B2","type":"advdis","topic":"cities","prompt":"Many cities are now building very tall apartment blocks to house their growing populations. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وحدد رأيك (المزايا أكبر بشرط التخطيط الجيد). الفقرة الأولى: المزايا مع مثال (توفير الأرض، دعم النقل العام، خفض تكلفة السكن). الفقرة الثانية: العيوب مع مثال (العزلة، تعطل المصاعد، حجب الشمس) وكيف يخففها المخططون. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك.","ideas":["Towers use land efficiently and protect the countryside","Dense districts make public transport practical","More homes can reduce price pressure","Residents may feel isolated and children lack play space","Lifts are a problem for elderly and disabled people","Planners can add parks and limit height"],"vocab":["urban growth","high-rise","housing shortage","density","public transport","green space","planning","neighbourhood"],"model":"As populations rise, many cities are choosing to build upwards rather than outwards. I believe that tall apartment blocks bring more benefits than drawbacks, provided that they are planned carefully.\n\nThe main advantage is that they use land efficiently. A single tower can house hundreds of families on a plot that would hold only a few detached houses. This leaves space for parks, schools and farmland, and it stops the city from spreading into the countryside. Moreover, when many people live close together, public services become cheaper and more practical. A metro line or a bus route serves thousands of residents within a short walk, so fewer people need a car, and traffic and pollution fall.\n\nAnother benefit is lower housing costs. Building upwards allows developers to offer more homes on expensive land, which can make rents fall or at least stop them rising so fast. Young workers and small families then have a better chance of living near their jobs instead of travelling long distances every day.\n\nOn the other hand, there are disadvantages. Residents of high floors may feel isolated, because they rarely meet their neighbours, and children have less space to play outside. Lifts break down, which is a serious problem for elderly or disabled people. Large towers can also block sunlight and change the character of old neighbourhoods.\n\nYet these problems can be reduced. Planners can include shared gardens, playgrounds and small shops at ground level, and they can set limits on height near historic areas. Regular maintenance contracts for lifts should also be a legal requirement.\n\nIn conclusion, although tall buildings can affect community life and the appearance of a city, their efficient use of land, lower costs and support for public transport make them a sensible response to urban growth.","notesAr":"1) الحجة الأولى مبنية بتسلسل: كفاءة الأرض ثم خدمات أرخص ثم نقل عام ثم تلوث أقل. 2) العيوب تُذكر بأمثلة محسوسة (المصعد المعطل لكبار السن) ثم تُعالج بحلول، فلا يبدو الرأي منحازا. 3) الربط بين الجمل طبيعي (Moreover, On the other hand, Yet) والموقف مشروط (provided that they are planned carefully)."},{"id":"w2-23","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"law and public integrity","prompt":"Employees who report corruption or serious wrongdoing in their organisation should be protected by law from losing their jobs or being punished. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الفكرة وصرّح بموقفك (أوافق بقوة). الفقرة الأولى: الحماية ضرورية لأن المبلّغ يخاطر برزقه، ودون حماية يسكت الناس، مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: فوائد الحماية للمؤسسة والمجتمع، ثم الاعتراض (بلاغات كيدية) والرد عليه (التحقق والعقوبة على الكذب). الخاتمة: أكّد موقفك.","ideas":["Reporting wrongdoing can cost a person their job and safety","Without protection many employees stay silent","Early reports can save public money and even lives","False reports should be investigated and punished","Anonymous reporting channels build trust"],"vocab":["whistleblower","retaliation","misconduct","confidential","investigate","legal protection","report","accountability"]},{"id":"w2-24","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"society","prompt":"In many countries, large numbers of young people are leaving villages and small towns to live in big cities. What problems does this cause, and what can be done to encourage them to stay?","planAr":"المقدمة: صف الظاهرة وأعلن بنية المقال. الفقرة الأولى: المشكلات (شيخوخة القرى، إهمال الزراعة، ازدحام المدن وارتفاع الإيجارات) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: الحلول مع مثال (استثمار في الإنترنت والمدارس والمستشفيات الريفية، حوافز للمشاريع الصغيرة). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Villages lose workers and schools may close","Farmland is left unused","Big cities become crowded and rents rise","Better internet allows remote work in small towns","Rural hospitals and schools make families stay","Grants and cheap loans can help local businesses"],"vocab":["rural","urban migration","ageing population","infrastructure","incentive","job opportunities","overcrowding","local economy"]},{"id":"w2-25","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"family","prompt":"In many families today, parents and children spend very little time together. Why is this? What effect can it have on children?","planAr":"المقدمة: صف الظاهرة وأعلن أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: الأسباب مع أمثلة (ضغط العمل، الشاشات، جداول الأطفال المزدحمة). الفقرة الثانية: الآثار على الأطفال مع أمثلة (ضعف الشعور بالأمان، تأخر اكتشاف المشكلات، التعلم من الغرباء على الإنترنت). الخاتمة: لخّص مع توصية قصيرة.","ideas":["Both parents often work long hours","Each family member uses a separate screen","Children attend many after-school activities","Children may feel less secure and less valued","Problems such as bullying may be noticed too late","Shared meals support language and values"],"vocab":["quality time","screen time","bond","working hours","emotional support","values","generation gap","spend time with"],"model":"Busy work schedules and constant screens mean that many parents and children share a home but little else. There are several reasons for this situation, and the consequences for children can be serious.\n\nThe first reason is the pressure of work. In many households both parents have jobs, and long journeys and overtime leave them with little energy in the evening. A father who returns at eight o'clock may only have time to say goodnight. The second reason is technology. Even when the family is in the same room, each person often looks at a separate screen, so there is no conversation. In addition, children themselves are busier than before; after school they attend extra lessons and clubs, which fill the time that families once spent together at meals.\n\nThis lack of shared time can affect children in several ways. Firstly, they may feel less secure and less valued. A child who never talks to his parents about his day may keep problems to himself, such as bullying at school, and the parents may notice too late. Secondly, children learn language, manners and values partly by listening to adults at home. A girl who regularly cooks with her mother or discusses the news at dinner develops confidence in speaking and thinking, whereas a child who spends hours alone with a tablet may learn mostly from strangers online. Finally, weak family bonds can make teenagers more likely to look for approval from the wrong people.\n\nFortunately, small steps can help, such as a screen-free dinner or a weekly walk together.\n\nIn conclusion, work pressure, technology and crowded timetables reduce family time, and the result may be emotional distance and weaker development in children. Families should therefore protect a few regular moments every week for real conversation.","notesAr":"1) السؤالان مجابان بوضوح: الفقرة الأولى للأسباب والثانية للآثار، ولا يوجد خلط بينهما. 2) الأمثلة بسيطة وواقعية (أب يعود في الثامنة، فتاة تطبخ مع أمها)، وتقابل بين طفلين بجملة whereas. 3) الجملة القصيرة عن الحلول تُظهر وعيا بالمشكلة دون الخروج عن المطلوب."},{"id":"w2-26","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"transparency","prompt":"Some people believe that citizens should be able to see all government documents and decisions. Others think that some information must be kept secret. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اعرض الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: حجج الشفافية الكاملة مع مثال (نشر ميزانية البلدية يمنع الهدر). الفقرة الثانية: حجج السرية (الأمن، البيانات الشخصية، المفاوضات) مع مثال. ثم رأيك: الأصل هو الإتاحة والاستثناءات محدودة ومحددة بالقانون وخاضعة للمراجعة القضائية. الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Public money belongs to the public, so spending should be visible","Open records help journalists and citizens detect waste","Security plans and personal data need protection","Secret decisions can hide mistakes or corruption","Courts or independent bodies can review secrecy claims"],"vocab":["freedom of information","public record","confidential","national security","oversight","disclosure","privacy","exception"]},{"id":"w2-27","l":"B2","type":"advdis","topic":"science","prompt":"Scientists can now change the genes of crops to make them produce more food or resist disease. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال وحدد رأيك. الفقرة الأولى: المزايا مع مثال (محاصيل تقاوم الجفاف والآفات، غلة أكثر، أسعار أقل). الفقرة الثانية: العيوب مع مثال (مخاوف على التنوع الحيوي، اعتماد المزارعين على شركات البذور، الحاجة إلى اختبارات طويلة) وكيف تُعالج بالتنظيم. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك.","ideas":["Drought-resistant crops help farmers in dry regions","Higher yields can reduce food prices and hunger","Crops that resist pests need fewer chemicals","Wild plant varieties could be affected","Farmers may depend on a few seed companies","Strong testing and clear labels build trust"],"vocab":["genetically modified","crop yield","pest","drought-resistant","biodiversity","food security","regulation","long-term effects"]},{"id":"w2-28","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"society","prompt":"Every young person should be required to spend six months doing community service, such as helping in hospitals or cleaning public spaces. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الفكرة وصرّح بموقفك (أوافق بشروط). الفقرة الأولى: فائدة المجتمع مع مثال (متطوعون لتنظيف الشواطئ يوفرون ميزانية). الفقرة الثانية: فائدة الشباب مع مثال (الاحترام والعمل الجماعي والخبرة). ثم اذكر الاعتراض (الإلزام وحرية الفرد) وردّ عليه بالاختيار بين المشاريع. الخاتمة: أكّد موقفك.","ideas":["Hospitals and care homes need extra helping hands","Young people learn teamwork and respect","Service gives work experience to school leavers","Compulsion may limit personal freedom","Choice of projects and a small allowance make it fairer"],"vocab":["community service","volunteer","compulsory","teamwork","work experience","public services","allowance","exemption"],"model":"I agree that young people should be required to give some months to community service, because it benefits both society and the individual, though it must be organised fairly.\n\nFirstly, society gains from extra help in areas that are short of staff. Hospitals, care homes and environmental projects often depend on a few overworked employees. If thousands of school leavers spent six months serving meals to elderly patients, planting trees along rivers or helping at food banks, these services would improve at a low cost. For example, a town that uses volunteers to clean its beaches each summer can spend its budget on new equipment instead of wages.\n\nSecondly, the young people themselves learn lessons that a classroom rarely teaches. Working with people from different backgrounds develops patience, teamwork and respect. A student from a wealthy family who spends weeks helping homeless people is likely to understand social problems far better than one who only reads about them in a textbook. Employers also value reliability and communication, and a record of service shows both. This can help a young person who has no work experience to start a career.\n\nCritics argue that compulsion goes against personal freedom and that students may resent being forced to work without pay. This concern is reasonable. However, the scheme could give young people a choice of projects, such as teaching, environmental work or helping in clinics, and it could provide a modest allowance and a certificate of recognition. Exemptions for those with health problems or family duties would also be necessary.\n\nIn conclusion, a well-designed service programme can strengthen public services and help young people to grow into responsible adults. Provided that participants have a choice, are treated fairly and are supported financially, the benefits clearly outweigh the loss of a few months.","notesAr":"1) الموقف واضح ومشروط (though it must be organised fairly) ويتكرر في الخاتمة بالشرط نفسه. 2) كل فقرة رئيسية تبدأ بجملة موضوع واضحة (Firstly / Secondly) تتبعها فكرة ثم مثال. 3) فقرة الاعتراض تُظهر القدرة على تقييم الرأي المخالف والرد عليه باقتراحات عملية."},{"id":"w2-29","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"transport","prompt":"Some cities are considering making public transport free for all passengers. Why might governments want to do this? Do you think it is a good idea?","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الفكرة وأعلن أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: لماذا تريد الحكومات ذلك؟ (تقليل الازدحام والتلوث، مساعدة ذوي الدخل المحدود) مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: هل هي فكرة جيدة؟ قدّم رأيك مع مثال (التمويل صعب وقد يزيد الازدحام في الحافلات، فالأفضل خفض الأسعار أو المجانية لفئات محددة). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Free fares encourage drivers to leave their cars at home","Poorer residents can reach jobs and schools more easily","Less traffic means cleaner air","Governments lose ticket income and must find other funding","Buses may become overcrowded without more investment","Free travel for students and the elderly is a middle option"],"vocab":["fare","subsidy","congestion","emissions","public funding","commuter","overcrowded","low-income"]},{"id":"w2-30","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"science and technology","prompt":"Some people think that scientists should be free to research any subject they choose. Others believe that governments should set limits on scientific research. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: اعرض الرأيين وأعلن نهجك. الفقرة الأولى: حجة حرية البحث مع مثال (اكتشافات جاءت من فضول علمي غير موجّه، كالمضادات الحيوية). الفقرة الثانية: حجة وضع الحدود مع مثال (حماية المشاركين في التجارب، مخاطر التقنيات الخطرة). ثم رأيك (حرية مع ضوابط أخلاقية ولجان مستقلة). الخاتمة: لخّص.","ideas":["Many great discoveries began with simple curiosity","Strict control can slow progress and force scientists abroad","Some experiments could harm people or the environment","Ethics committees can approve or reject projects","Public debate helps decide which risks are acceptable"],"vocab":["ethics","research funding","ethics committee","breakthrough","regulation","curiosity","risk","informed consent"]},{"id":"w2-31","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"technology","prompt":"Some people believe that artificial intelligence will soon replace teachers in schools and universities.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["AI can mark work and give extra practice at any hour","teachers notice emotions and confusion that software misses","children learn values by watching adults","AI frees teachers for discussion and personal support","someone human must be accountable for what pupils learn"],"vocab":["to replace","routine tasks","personalised feedback","individual support","to be accountable for","a powerful tool","classroom management"],"model":"Artificial intelligence is already marking essays and answering students' questions, so it is tempting to think that human teachers will soon become unnecessary. I disagree with this view, although I accept that AI will change the way teachers work.\n\nThe main reason is that teaching involves much more than delivering information. A good teacher notices when a student is anxious, bored or quietly confused, and adjusts the lesson at once. For example, a maths teacher may see a pupil staring at the board and realise that the explanation went too fast, even though the pupil says nothing. A program can only react to the answers it receives, so it often misses these signals. In addition, children learn habits such as patience and respect by watching adults, and a screen cannot demonstrate them in the same way.\n\nOn the other hand, AI will certainly take over some routine tasks. It can mark hundreds of grammar exercises in seconds and give every learner extra practice at midnight, when no teacher is available. A student preparing for an exam, for instance, could ask a chatbot to explain a difficult formula five times without feeling embarrassed. However, this does not make teachers redundant. It frees them to spend their time on discussion, feedback and individual support, which are the most valuable parts of education. Parents and governments also want a real person to be responsible for what pupils are taught, and a company's software cannot fill that role.\n\nIn conclusion, AI is likely to become a powerful assistant in the classroom, but it is unlikely to replace the teacher. Schools should therefore train teachers to use these tools wisely instead of trying to do without them.","notesAr":"1) موقف واضح من أول جملة: \"I disagree ... although I accept\" تجمع الرأي والاعتراف بالرأي الآخر.\n2) كل فقرة رئيسية فيها سبب ثم مثال محدد يسهل تخيّله (المعلم الذي يرى التلميذ يحدّق في السبورة، الطالب الذي يسأل الروبوت عند منتصف الليل).\n3) الروابط طبيعية (In addition, On the other hand, However, therefore) والخاتمة تعيد الموقف دون أفكار جديدة."},{"id":"w2-32","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"globalisation","prompt":"Some people think that the spread of international brands and foreign films is damaging local traditions. Others believe it brings people closer together and enriches daily life.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["foreign brands can push out small local shops and crafts","young people may copy foreign habits and forget traditional customs","shared films, music and food create curiosity about other cultures","traditions can survive when communities are proud of them","tourism and the internet can promote local culture abroad"],"vocab":["local traditions","cultural identity","to be exposed to","international brands","mutual understanding","to preserve","homogeneous"]},{"id":"w2-33","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"urbanisation","prompt":"In many countries, young people are leaving villages to live in large cities, and the villages are becoming almost empty.\n\nWhat are the causes of this problem, and what measures could be taken to solve it?","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر المشكلة بإيجاز وأنك ستشرح أسبابها وحلولها. الفقرة الأولى: سببان للمشكلة مع مثال لكل سبب. الفقرة الثانية: حلان واقعيان مع بيان كيف يعالج كل منهما سبباً مذكوراً. الخاتمة: لخّص الأسباب والحلول.","ideas":["few jobs and low wages in rural areas","poorer schools, hospitals and internet access in villages","cities seem to offer excitement and better careers","provide grants or tax relief for rural businesses","improve transport and broadband so people can work remotely"],"vocab":["rural areas","to migrate","job opportunities","basic services","a rural exodus","incentives","broadband"]},{"id":"w2-34","l":"B2","type":"advdis","topic":"tourism","prompt":"In many countries, tourism has become the main source of income for coastal towns and historic cities.\n\nDo the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال واذكر هل المزايا أكبر من العيوب. الفقرة الأولى: أهم الميزات مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: أهم العيوب مع مثال، ثم بيّن لماذا تبقى أقل وزناً (أو أكبر). الخاتمة: كرّر حكمك النهائي بعبارات جديدة.","ideas":["tourism creates jobs for young people with little training","visitor spending produces tax revenue for roads and museums","housing becomes more expensive for local residents","litter, noise and pressure on water supplies","limits on tourist flats and entry fees can reduce the harm"],"vocab":["a source of income","to create employment","tax revenue","overcrowding","to afford housing","visitor numbers","sustainable tourism"],"model":"Tourism now supports many towns that once depended on fishing or farming. In my view, the benefits are greater than the drawbacks, provided that local authorities manage visitor numbers carefully.\n\nThe most important advantage is employment. Hotels, restaurants, taxi firms and souvenir shops all need staff, and many of these jobs are open to young people with little formal training. In a small coastal town, for example, a teenager can start as a waiter in the summer, learn some English and later open a café of his own. Tourist spending also reaches ordinary families directly, which is rarely true of income from heavy industry, and it gives governments the tax revenue to repair roads, museums and harbours.\n\nHowever, the disadvantages are real. Crowds raise the cost of housing, because owners find it more profitable to rent flats to visitors than to local residents. In some historic cities, shopkeepers complain that the old market has been replaced by cheap souvenir stalls and that families can no longer afford to live in the centre. Visitors also cause litter, noise and pressure on water supplies, especially in dry regions. Nevertheless, these problems can be reduced by sensible rules. Cities can limit the number of tourist flats, charge a small entry fee for the busiest sites and spend the income on cleaning and public transport. Where such measures exist, residents benefit from visitors instead of suffering because of them.\n\nIn conclusion, tourism brings jobs and public money that few other industries can match, and its negative effects can largely be controlled. For this reason, I believe that the advantages outweigh the disadvantages.","notesAr":"1) يحدد المقال حكمه فوراً (\"the benefits are greater ... provided that\") ويضع شرطاً واقعياً.\n2) فقرة الميزات وفقرة العيوب كل منهما بسبب ومثال ملموس (مراهق يبدأ نادلاً ثم يفتح مقهى؛ أصحاب المحلات والسوق القديم).\n3) فقرة العيوب تنتهي بحلول (Nevertheless ...) فيبقى الحكم النهائي مقنعاً، وتستعمل عبارات متنوعة مثل tax revenue و visitor numbers."},{"id":"w2-35","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"language","prompt":"Many languages around the world are disappearing because younger generations prefer to speak English or another major language.\n\nWhy is this happening? Is it a problem if a small language disappears?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأشر إلى أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: أجب عن السؤال الأول بسبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: أجب عن السؤال الثاني بموقف واضح مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين. تأكد أن كل سؤال أخذ جواباً مباشراً.","ideas":["jobs, universities and the internet reward major languages","parents want children to have better career chances","small languages carry stories, songs and local knowledge","once the last speakers die, the knowledge is lost","schools and media can keep minority languages alive"],"vocab":["minority language","to die out","native speaker","cultural heritage","career prospects","bilingual","to revitalise"]},{"id":"w2-36","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"law","prompt":"Some people argue that computer programs, not human judges, should decide simple legal cases such as traffic fines and unpaid bills.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["programs apply the same rule to everyone and never get tired","faster decisions reduce the huge backlog in courts","people want to explain their situation to a human","software can contain hidden errors or bias","a right of appeal to a human judge is essential"],"vocab":["a backlog of cases","to reach a verdict","impartial","an appeal","legal reasoning","bias","due process"]},{"id":"w2-37","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"law","prompt":"Some people think that judges should always follow strict sentencing rules set by the law. Others believe that judges should be free to decide each sentence according to the circumstances of the case.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["fixed rules give equal and predictable punishments","guidelines make it harder to hide favouritism or bribery","no rule can foresee every situation","a judge can hear the defendant and judge his character","published written reasons can combine both approaches"],"vocab":["to hand down a sentence","sentencing guidelines","discretion","fair trial","defendant","leniency","to review a decision"],"model":"Sentencing is one of the most visible decisions in any legal system, so it is not surprising that people disagree about how much freedom judges should have. I will discuss both positions before explaining why I favour a balanced approach.\n\nSupporters of strict rules argue that they make justice equal and predictable. If two people commit the same theft, they should receive similar punishments, whatever the judge's mood or background. Fixed guidelines also make it harder for corruption to go unnoticed, because any unusual sentence would be easy to spot. A judge who gave a very light penalty to a friend, for example, would have to explain why he ignored the official table of penalties, and a public explanation of that kind discourages favouritism and bribery.\n\nOn the other hand, no rule can anticipate every situation. A man who steals bread to feed his hungry children is not the same as a gang member who steals for profit, yet a rigid system might treat them identically. Judges can listen to witnesses, read reports about the defendant's character and see whether he truly regrets his actions. When they are allowed to use this knowledge, sentences are fairer and more likely to help offenders return to normal life. For instance, a young first-time offender who agrees to repay the victim and attend a training course may be better served by a community sentence than by a spell in prison.\n\nIn my opinion, the best solution is to give judges a range of penalties for each offence and to require written reasons whenever they go outside it. This keeps the equality that strict rules provide while leaving room for human judgement, and it allows higher courts to review decisions.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تعرض الرأيين وتعلن أن الموقف وسطي، فيعرف القارئ مسار المقال.\n2) كل رأي يُعرض بإنصاف مع سبب ومثال واضح (القاضي والصديق، سارق الخبز مقابل العصابة، المخالف الشاب والخدمة المجتمعية).\n3) الرأي الشخصي في الخاتمة عملي ومحدد (نطاق من العقوبات + تسبيب مكتوب) وليس عبارة عامة، مع مفردات قانونية دقيقة."},{"id":"w2-38","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"government","prompt":"Some people think that all government contracts, including the names of the companies and the amounts paid, should be published online for everyone to see.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["public money belongs to citizens, so they should see how it is spent","publication discourages bribery and favouritism","journalists and students can spot waste","some security or business details may need protection","simple, searchable websites matter more than huge files"],"vocab":["transparency","public contract","to scrutinise","taxpayers' money","to deter corruption","accountability","searchable database"]},{"id":"w2-39","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"corruption","prompt":"In some countries, ordinary people believe that they must pay small bribes to officials in order to receive basic public services.\n\nWhat are the causes of this problem, and what can be done to reduce it?","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر المشكلة بإيجاز وأنك ستشرح أسبابها وحلولها. الفقرة الأولى: سببان للمشكلة مع مثال لكل سبب. الفقرة الثانية: حلان واقعيان مع بيان كيف يعالج كل منهما سبباً مذكوراً. الخاتمة: لخّص الأسباب والحلول.","ideas":["low salaries and weak supervision of officials","complicated procedures and long queues create temptation","fear of punishment is low because cases are rarely investigated","online services reduce personal contact with officials","protection for people who report corruption and clear public information"],"vocab":["to offer a bribe","public servant","red tape","to turn a blind eye","a whistleblower","oversight","to prosecute"]},{"id":"w2-40","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"privacy","prompt":"Many apps and websites collect personal information such as users' locations and shopping habits.\n\nWhy do companies collect so much data? Is this a positive or negative development?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأشر إلى أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: أجب عن السؤال الأول بسبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: أجب عن السؤال الثاني بموقف واضح مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين. تأكد أن كل سؤال أخذ جواباً مباشراً.","ideas":["data makes advertising more profitable","data itself can be sold to other firms","personalised services are convenient","hacked databases lead to fraud","users rarely understand long terms and conditions"],"vocab":["personal data","to track","consent","terms and conditions","data breach","targeted advertising","to invade privacy"],"model":"Almost every free app now asks permission to track where we go and what we buy. This essay will explain why companies gather such information and argue that, in its present form, the development is more negative than positive.\n\nCompanies collect data mainly because it is profitable. Advertisers pay more for messages that reach the right person, and a business that knows a customer's habits can predict what he will want next. For example, a food-delivery app that notices someone orders pizza every Friday can send a discount code on Friday afternoon. Data can also be sold to other firms, so it has become a product in its own right, which gives companies a strong reason to collect as much as they can.\n\nAlthough personalised services can be convenient, I believe that the risks are greater. First, large databases attract criminals. When a company's system is hacked, millions of addresses and passwords may be stolen and used for fraud. Second, users rarely understand what they have agreed to, since the terms are long and written in complicated language. A person who allows a weather app to track his location may not realise that the information is passed to dozens of unknown companies. Over time, this loss of control can make people feel watched and less willing to search for sensitive information, such as health advice, or to express their opinions freely.\n\nIn conclusion, firms collect data because it earns money, but the practice currently harms privacy and security. Stricter laws that require clear consent and the deletion of unused data would make the development far more acceptable.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تجيب عن السؤالين صراحة (لماذا؟ ثم الحكم: سلبي أكثر منه إيجابي)، وهذا ما يطلبه نوع twopart.\n2) كل جواب في فقرة مستقلة بسبب ومثال قريب من الحياة (تطبيق التوصيل وبيتزا يوم الجمعة، تطبيق الطقس والموقع).\n3) يعترف المقال بفائدة الخدمات الشخصية (Although ...) ثم يرجّح الأخطار، وتنتهي الخاتمة بحل عملي مختصر."},{"id":"w2-41","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"research","prompt":"Some people think that universities should spend their money only on research that has a clear practical use, such as new medicines or cheaper energy.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["practical research solves urgent problems and justifies public funding","many great discoveries began as curiosity without a practical aim","basic research trains skilled scientists","it is impossible to predict which idea will become useful","a mixed budget protects both needs"],"vocab":["pure research","practical applications","to fund","a breakthrough","scientific curiosity","public funding","to benefit society"]},{"id":"w2-42","l":"B2","type":"advdis","topic":"ageing","prompt":"In many countries, people are living longer and the proportion of elderly citizens is rising.\n\nDo the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال واذكر هل المزايا أكبر من العيوب. الفقرة الأولى: أهم الميزات مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: أهم العيوب مع مثال، ثم بيّن لماذا تبقى أقل وزناً (أو أكبر). الخاتمة: كرّر حكمك النهائي بعبارات جديدة.","ideas":["older people share experience and often help with childcare","longer lives reflect better health care","pension and hospital costs rise for the state","fewer workers support more retired people","older workers can stay active and fill labour shortages"],"vocab":["life expectancy","an ageing population","pension system","retirement age","healthcare costs","workforce","dependency"]},{"id":"w2-43","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"energy","prompt":"Some people believe that governments should invest mainly in renewable energy such as solar and wind power. Others think that fossil fuels will remain necessary for many years and should receive most of the investment.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["fossil fuels give steady power in any weather","a sudden change could cost jobs and raise prices","renewables have very low running costs","clean energy protects health and the climate","batteries and stronger grids are needed first"],"vocab":["renewable energy","fossil fuels","power station","to reduce emissions","a power cut","storage","the transition"],"model":"Energy policy affects everything from electricity bills to the air we breathe. Some people want governments to put nearly all their money into solar and wind power, while others say that oil and gas cannot be replaced yet. I believe that renewable energy should receive the larger share, but not the whole of it.\n\nThose who defend fossil fuels point to reliability. A gas power station produces electricity day and night, in any weather, whereas solar panels stop working after sunset and wind turbines depend on the breeze. A hospital or a steel factory cannot accept sudden power cuts. In addition, many countries have built their economies around cheap fuel or fuel exports, and a rapid change could cost jobs and raise prices for poor families, for example in a region where thousands of workers depend on a single gas field.\n\nHowever, renewable sources are the better long-term investment. Their running costs are very low because sunshine and wind are free, and the price of solar panels has fallen sharply over the past decade. Moreover, they do not release the gases that heat the planet or the smoke that damages the lungs of children in crowded cities. A sunny country that builds large solar farms, for instance, can reduce its fuel imports and sell surplus electricity to its neighbours.\n\nIn conclusion, both views contain some truth, but investment should clearly favour clean energy. Until batteries become cheaper and power grids stronger, existing gas plants can fill the gaps when the sun is not shining, and governments should retrain energy workers for new jobs as the change takes place.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تعرض الرأيين وتعلن الموقف مبكراً (أكبر حصة للطاقة المتجددة وليس كلها).\n2) فقرة لكل رأي، وكل فقرة بسبب ومثال محسوس (المستشفى والمصنع، منطقة تعتمد على حقل غاز واحد، رئتا الأطفال في المدن المزدحمة).\n3) الخاتمة تحلّ التعارض بحل انتقالي واقعي بدلاً من تكرار الجمل، وتستعمل ربطاً جيداً (whereas, In addition, Moreover)."},{"id":"w2-44","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"consumerism","prompt":"Many people buy new phones, clothes and furniture long before the old ones stop working. Some think this habit is harmful to society and the environment.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["constant buying creates a mountain of waste","advertising makes people feel their things are outdated","people spend money they do not have","shops and factories provide jobs","repairing and reusing saves money and resources"],"vocab":["consumer culture","disposable","to throw away","impulse buying","advertising","landfill","second-hand"]},{"id":"w2-45","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"international aid","prompt":"Wealthy countries and charities send large amounts of financial aid to poorer countries, but in many cases the aid does not improve people's lives as much as expected.\n\nWhat are the causes of this problem, and what could be done to make aid more effective?","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر المشكلة بإيجاز وأنك ستشرح أسبابها وحلولها. الفقرة الأولى: سببان للمشكلة مع مثال لكل سبب. الفقرة الثانية: حلان واقعيان مع بيان كيف يعالج كل منهما سبباً مذكوراً. الخاتمة: لخّص الأسباب والحلول.","ideas":["money is lost through corruption or poor planning","projects ignore what local people really need","aid creates dependence instead of skills","donors and recipients should publish how money is spent","train local workers and let communities decide priorities"],"vocab":["financial aid","to depend on","to misuse funds","local communities","long-term development","donor","to monitor"]},{"id":"w2-46","l":"B2","type":"twopart","topic":"sport","prompt":"Professional footballers and other top athletes often earn far more than doctors or teachers.\n\nWhy is this the case? Is it fair?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأشر إلى أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: أجب عن السؤال الأول بسبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: أجب عن السؤال الثاني بموقف واضح مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين. تأكد أن كل سؤال أخذ جواباً مباشراً.","ideas":["sport is a huge entertainment business with advertising income","very few people have top-level talent","athletes' careers are short and risky","essential workers train for years and save lives","higher taxes on huge incomes could support public services"],"vocab":["professional athlete","salary","to generate profit","a short career","advertising revenue","essential workers","to tax"],"model":"Top athletes can earn in a single year what a nurse would earn in a lifetime. This essay will explain the reasons for this gap and argue that, although it is understandable in economic terms, it is not entirely fair.\n\nThe main reason is that sport is a huge entertainment business. Millions of people pay to watch a match or subscribe to a television channel, and advertisers pay enormous sums to appear in front of those viewers. A famous striker therefore brings in far more money for his club than the cost of his salary. In addition, very few people have the talent to play at the highest level. Because the supply of such players is tiny and clubs compete fiercely for them, wages rise. A teacher, by contrast, can be replaced by thousands of equally qualified people, so the market does not push her pay up in the same way.\n\nIs this fair? In terms of effort and risk, athletes do deserve a good income, as their careers are short and one injury can end them overnight. Nevertheless, I believe that the difference with essential workers is excessive. A doctor spends a decade in training and may save hundreds of lives, yet she may earn a fraction of a footballer's weekly wage. Such a gap suggests that society rewards popularity more than usefulness. Governments cannot fix market prices, but they could tax very high earnings more heavily and use the revenue to raise salaries in public hospitals and schools.\n\nIn conclusion, athletes are paid highly because they generate profit and are rare, but a fair society should not let the gap with teachers and doctors grow without limit.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تعد بالإجابة عن السؤالين (السبب ثم العدالة) وتحدد الموقف: مفهوم اقتصادياً لكنه غير عادل تماماً.\n2) السبب الاقتصادي مشروح بمنطق واضح (العرض القليل والطلب الضخم) مع مثال المهاجم والمعلمة.\n3) السؤال الثاني يُجاب بإنصاف (يستحقون دخلاً جيداً Nevertheless ...) ثم حل عملي (ضرائب أعلى لدعم المستشفيات والمدارس)."},{"id":"w2-47","l":"B2","type":"advdis","topic":"law","prompt":"Some courts are starting to use artificial intelligence to search through documents and suggest how similar cases were decided in the past.\n\nDo the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال واذكر هل المزايا أكبر من العيوب. الفقرة الأولى: أهم الميزات مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: أهم العيوب مع مثال، ثم بيّن لماذا تبقى أقل وزناً (أو أكبر). الخاتمة: كرّر حكمك النهائي بعبارات جديدة.","ideas":["AI reads thousands of pages in minutes","consistent decisions in similar cases","judges and lawyers save time and costs","AI may repeat bias hidden in old data","people need to understand how a suggestion was produced"],"vocab":["legal research","precedent","to speed up","consistency","algorithmic bias","transparency","human oversight"]},{"id":"w2-48","l":"B2","type":"discuss","topic":"art","prompt":"Some people think that governments should spend public money on museums, theatres and public art. Others believe that this money should be used for more essential services such as hospitals and roads.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["hospitals and roads meet urgent daily needs","taxpayers may resent funding what they never use","art builds identity and attracts visitors","free museums give children cultural experiences","a small, steady share can serve both aims"],"vocab":["public funding","cultural heritage","essential services","to attract visitors","creativity","a priority","a balanced budget"]},{"id":"w2-49","l":"B2","type":"opinion","topic":"economy","prompt":"Some economists argue that governments should support small local businesses rather than try to attract large foreign companies.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["local firms keep money circulating inside the community","foreign firms may send profits abroad","many small firms make an economy more resilient","foreign firms bring technology, skills and export markets","support through loans, training and lower costs"],"vocab":["small business","foreign investment","to boost the economy","profit","resilient","supplier","to create jobs"],"model":"Every government wants more jobs and higher incomes, and it must choose where to direct its support. I largely agree that small local businesses deserve greater attention than large foreign firms, although foreign investment still has a role.\n\nThe first reason is that local businesses keep money inside the community. A family bakery buys flour from nearby farmers, pays rent to a local landlord and employs neighbours, so each dinar earned circulates several times before it leaves the area. A foreign company, by contrast, often sends most of its profit abroad and imports its equipment and managers. In a small town, for example, a new factory owned by outsiders may employ many workers but leave little behind if it closes.\n\nThe second reason is resilience. An economy made of thousands of small firms is less likely to collapse when one of them fails, whereas a country that relies on a single multinational can suffer badly if that company moves to a cheaper location. Small firms are also more flexible: a tailor can start making face masks within a week when demand suddenly rises. Governments can help them with simple tools such as easier loans, lower administrative costs and training in accounting and online marketing. I do not believe, however, that foreign companies should be rejected. They bring technology and skills that local firms may lack, and they can open export markets, so the sensible approach is to welcome them on condition that they train local staff and buy materials from local suppliers.\n\nIn conclusion, supporting small businesses should be the priority because it spreads wealth and builds stability, but it should be combined with carefully managed foreign investment.","notesAr":"1) الموقف جزئي ودقيق (\"largely agree ... although\") وهذا أفضل من الموافقة المطلقة في السؤال من نوع to what extent.\n2) سبب أول ومثال (المخبز العائلي مقابل مصنع الغرباء)، وسبب ثانٍ ومثال (الخياط الذي ينتج الكمامات خلال أسبوع).\n3) يعالج الرأي المخالف داخل الفقرة الثانية (However ... on condition that) فتبدو الحجة ناضجة ومتوازنة."},{"id":"w2-50","l":"B2","type":"problem","topic":"transparency","prompt":"In many countries, citizens find it difficult to obtain information about how public money is spent, and as a result they trust their institutions less.\n\nWhat are the causes of this problem, and what measures could be taken to solve it?","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر المشكلة بإيجاز وأنك ستشرح أسبابها وحلولها. الفقرة الأولى: سببان للمشكلة مع مثال لكل سبب. الفقرة الثانية: حلان واقعيان مع بيان كيف يعالج كل منهما سبباً مذكوراً. الخاتمة: لخّص الأسباب والحلول.","ideas":["officials fear criticism and keep records secret","information is stored on paper or in confusing formats","no law forces institutions to answer requests","publish budgets and contracts online in simple form","create an independent body to enforce the right to information"],"vocab":["freedom of information","public records","to disclose","budget","to restore trust","an independent body","citizens"]},{"id":"w2-51","l":"C1","type":"discuss","topic":"ai","prompt":"Some people believe that images, music and stories created with the help of artificial intelligence cannot be regarded as real art. Others argue that such systems are simply a new tool, comparable to the camera in the nineteenth century.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["art traditionally expresses human experience and intention","AI recombines existing work and may copy artists without payment","the camera was also dismissed before it became an art form","the human choosing, directing and editing still shows creativity","clear labelling and fair payment for source artists"],"vocab":["to generate","authorship","originality","creative process","intellectual property","a medium","to blur the line between"]},{"id":"w2-52","l":"C1","type":"opinion","topic":"law","prompt":"Courts in some countries use software to suggest outcomes or to estimate the risk that a defendant will commit another offence. Some argue that the reasoning of such systems should be open to public inspection, even if technology companies must reveal commercial secrets.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["a verdict must be explained so that it can be challenged","hidden logic makes errors permanent","outside experts can detect discrimination developers overlook","companies have a legitimate interest in protecting their work","confidential audits can reconcile both interests"],"vocab":["algorithm","to reoffend","accountability","a trade secret","to scrutinise","an audit","discriminatory outcomes","the burden of proof"],"model":"Courts in several countries are experimenting with software that recommends outcomes or estimates the risk that a defendant will reoffend. I strongly agree that the reasoning behind such systems should be open to inspection, even if companies must disclose commercially sensitive details, because justice that cannot be examined is hard to describe as justice at all.\n\nThe first argument concerns accountability. A judge must explain a verdict in writing, and the losing party may challenge each step of that reasoning on appeal. If an algorithm influences the decision but its logic remains hidden, this safeguard disappears. Imagine a defendant refused bail because a program scored him as high-risk: without access to the formula, neither he nor his lawyer can discover whether the score rested on a mistaken record or on something irrelevant, such as his postcode. Secrecy therefore makes errors permanent.\n\nThe second argument is that scrutiny improves quality. Independent researchers who can test a system are able to detect discrimination that its developers overlooked, much as engineers inspect a bridge before the public is allowed to cross it. If a risk tool were found to label one neighbourhood's residents as dangerous far more often than another's, that pattern could be corrected only because outsiders had been able to analyse the results.\n\nAdmittedly, companies have a legitimate interest in protecting the investment that went into their products. Yet this concern can be accommodated. Auditors bound by confidentiality could examine the code, and firms that wish to sell to the state could be required to accept such audits as a condition of the contract.\n\nIn conclusion, when software helps to decide questions of liberty, its workings must be as open to challenge as any judge's reasoning, and commercial secrecy should not outweigh that principle.","notesAr":"1) الموقف قوي وواضح من الجملة الأولى (strongly agree) مع تعليل فلسفي موجز: العدالة التي لا تُفحص لا تُعد عدالة.\n2) الفقرتان الرئيسيتان: المساءلة (المتهم المحروم من الكفالة بسبب درجة لا يعرف أساسها) ثم تحسين الجودة (فحص الجسر قبل عبور الناس)، ومثالان محسوسان وتشبيه جيد.\n3) يرد على الاعتراض المحتمل (Admittedly ... Yet) بحل عملي هو التدقيق السري، وهذا يرفع المقال إلى مستوى C1 بمفردات مثل accountability و scrutiny و audit."},{"id":"w2-53","l":"C1","type":"twopart","topic":"globalisation","prompt":"In a connected world, young people in very different countries increasingly share the same music, fashion, language and ideas.\n\nWhat factors are driving this trend? Is it a loss for humanity as a whole?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأشر إلى أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: أجب عن السؤال الأول بسبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: أجب عن السؤال الثاني بموقف واضح مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين. تأكد أن كل سؤال أخذ جواباً مباشراً.","ideas":["social media and streaming platforms spread the same content","English as a common language for work and study","migration, travel and international education","local diversity of food, dialect and craft may fade","shared references can also support cooperation and understanding"],"vocab":["cultural convergence","to be driven by","homogenisation","cultural diversity","a lingua franca","to erode","cross-cultural exchange"]},{"id":"w2-54","l":"C1","type":"advdis","topic":"transparency","prompt":"Some governments now publish detailed data on public spending, court decisions and the assets of senior officials.\n\nDo the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال واذكر هل المزايا أكبر من العيوب. الفقرة الأولى: أهم الميزات مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: أهم العيوب مع مثال، ثم بيّن لماذا تبقى أقل وزناً (أو أكبر). الخاتمة: كرّر حكمك النهائي بعبارات جديدة.","ideas":["citizens and journalists can detect waste and conflicts of interest","officials behave more carefully when they know they are observed","raw data may be misread or used to spread false claims","personal privacy and security may be put at risk","clear formats, context and legal limits can reduce the harm"],"vocab":["open data","asset declaration","conflict of interest","to be scrutinised","privacy","disclosure","misinterpretation","public trust"]},{"id":"w2-55","l":"C1","type":"discuss","topic":"law","prompt":"Some people believe that the main purpose of prison should be to punish offenders. Others argue that it should be to rehabilitate them so that they can return to society.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["victims expect wrongdoing to carry a real consequence","punishment may deter others and reassure the public","most prisoners are released, so what they learn matters","training and education reduce reoffending and later costs","prison still protects the public from dangerous people"],"vocab":["rehabilitation","to deter","reoffending","a custodial sentence","to reintegrate","retribution","vocational training","inmate"],"model":"Few questions in criminal justice are as persistent as what prison is for. Some insist that it exists to punish wrongdoing, whereas others see it as a place to prepare offenders for a law-abiding life. In my view, rehabilitation should be the central aim, with punishment as its boundary rather than its purpose.\n\nAdvocates of punishment argue that justice requires wrongdoing to carry a price. Victims and their families often feel that a lenient sentence dismisses their suffering, and the prospect of serious consequences may deter others from offending. A person who has defrauded hundreds of pensioners, for instance, would find it hard to accept that a short course of counselling is an adequate response. Moreover, public confidence in the law depends partly on a sense that serious crimes do not go unanswered.\n\nNevertheless, everyday experience suggests that punishment alone seldom makes societies safer. Most prisoners are eventually released, and an inmate who has spent years without training, education or support is likely to return to the same environment with the same prospects. By contrast, a man who learns a trade in prison, such as welding or carpentry, has a realistic alternative to crime on release. Workshops and literacy classes cost money, but they are far cheaper than the repeated expense of arrests, trials and further imprisonment, to say nothing of the harm suffered by new victims.\n\nIn conclusion, punishment answers society's need for justice, but rehabilitation answers its need for safety. Loss of liberty will remain necessary for dangerous offenders; however, its role should be to protect the public while rehabilitation takes place, not to inflict suffering for its own sake. A system that prioritises the second goal will ultimately serve both.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تحدد موقفاً مصاغاً بدقة: التأهيل هو الهدف المركزي والعقاب حدّ له وليس غاية (boundary rather than purpose).\n2) كل رأي يعرض بإنصاف وبمثال ملموس (من احتال على مئات المتقاعدين، السجين الذي يتعلم اللحام أو النجارة) مع حجة اقتصادية تقوّي الرأي الشخصي.\n3) الخاتمة تتضمن تحفظاً صغيراً (السجن للخطرين) ثم جملة أخيرة تجمع الهدفين، مع ربط متنوع (whereas, Moreover, Nevertheless, By contrast)."},{"id":"w2-56","l":"C1","type":"problem","topic":"research","prompt":"Many researchers are under pressure to publish large numbers of papers quickly, and as a result there are growing concerns about weak, misleading or even invented research results.\n\nWhat are the causes of this problem, and what solutions can be suggested?","planAr":"المقدمة: اذكر المشكلة بإيجاز وأنك ستشرح أسبابها وحلولها. الفقرة الأولى: سببان للمشكلة مع مثال لكل سبب. الفقرة الثانية: حلان واقعيان مع بيان كيف يعالج كل منهما سبباً مذكوراً. الخاتمة: لخّص الأسباب والحلول.","ideas":["promotion and funding depend on the number of publications","journals prefer exciting positive results","peer reviewers are overworked and unpaid","reward quality, openness and replication instead of quantity","require researchers to share data and methods"],"vocab":["research integrity","peer review","to fabricate data","to replicate a study","publication pressure","misconduct","incentive","retraction"]},{"id":"w2-57","l":"C1","type":"opinion","topic":"economy","prompt":"Economic growth based on ever-increasing consumption cannot continue indefinitely. Some people therefore argue that governments should measure national success by citizens' wellbeing rather than by the size of the economy.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع بكلماتك واذكر موقفك بوضوح (أوافق / لا أوافق / أوافق جزئياً). الفقرة الأولى: سبب رئيسي مع مثال محدد. الفقرة الثانية: سبب ثانٍ مع مثال، أو اعتراف بالرأي المخالف ثم الرد عليه. الخاتمة: لخّص موقفك بجملتين دون أفكار جديدة.","ideas":["resources and the environment are limited","a higher national income does not always mean healthier or happier lives","wellbeing indicators include health, education, safety and free time","growth still funds hospitals, schools and technology","combine several indicators, not replace economic data"],"vocab":["gross domestic product","wellbeing","sustainable","a measure of success","depletion of resources","quality of life","indicator","to prioritise"]},{"id":"w2-58","l":"C1","type":"advdis","topic":"corruption","prompt":"Some countries pay a share of recovered money to citizens whose information leads to the conviction of corrupt officials.\n\nDo the advantages of this approach outweigh the disadvantages?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة السؤال واذكر هل المزايا أكبر من العيوب. الفقرة الأولى: أهم الميزات مع مثال. الفقرة الثانية: أهم العيوب مع مثال، ثم بيّن لماذا تبقى أقل وزناً (أو أكبر). الخاتمة: كرّر حكمك النهائي بعبارات جديدة.","ideas":["fear of retaliation stops many witnesses from speaking","insiders are often the only source of proof","the state may recover more money than it pays","false accusations driven by greed or rivalry","payment only after a verdict and penalties for false reports"],"vocab":["to report","reward","to expose","retaliation","incentive","false accusation","a conviction","safeguard"],"model":"A number of countries pay a share of recovered funds to citizens whose information leads to the conviction of corrupt officials. In my opinion, the benefits of such schemes outweigh their drawbacks, provided that they are carefully designed.\n\nThe principal advantage is that rewards overcome the strongest obstacle to reporting: fear. Someone who exposes a corrupt manager risks dismissal, social isolation and sometimes threats, so without a tangible incentive many witnesses prefer silence. A clerk who notices that contracts are always awarded to the same company, for example, may stay quiet because the personal cost is too high, yet a substantial payment can make the risk acceptable and allow him to start a new career elsewhere. Since corruption is hidden by nature, people on the inside are often the only ones who can provide proof, so encouraging them is highly effective. In successful cases, the state recovers far more than it pays out.\n\nOn the other hand, critics warn that financial rewards may encourage false accusations and turn colleagues into informers motivated by greed rather than principle. A rival might invent a claim to damage someone's reputation, wasting investigators' time and harming an innocent person. These dangers are real, but they can be limited. Payment can be made only after a court has reached a verdict, and those who knowingly make false reports can be punished under the law. Where such conditions apply, the incentive rewards truth rather than rumour.\n\nIn conclusion, although rewards carry a risk of abuse, strict safeguards can contain it, and the greater likelihood that serious corruption is exposed makes these schemes worthwhile.","notesAr":"1) المقدمة تعطي الحكم والشرط معاً (\"outweigh ... provided that\") فلا يبدو الموقف مطلقاً.\n2) فقرة الميزات تشرح سبباً عميقاً (الخوف) بمثال الموظف الذي يلاحظ العقود، وتضيف حجة اقتصادية (الدولة تسترد أكثر مما تدفع).\n3) فقرة العيوب تعرض أخطر اعتراض ثم ترد عليه بضمانات محددة (الدفع بعد الحكم، عقوبة البلاغ الكاذب)، وهذا يمثل تحليلاً متوازناً."},{"id":"w2-59","l":"C1","type":"twopart","topic":"ageing","prompt":"As populations grow older, some governments are considering raising the age at which people can receive a state pension.\n\nWhy is this considered necessary by some policymakers? What problems might it create?","planAr":"المقدمة: أعد صياغة الموضوع وأشر إلى أنك ستجيب عن السؤالين. الفقرة الأولى: أجب عن السؤال الأول بسبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: أجب عن السؤال الثاني بموقف واضح مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: لخّص الإجابتين. تأكد أن كل سؤال أخذ جواباً مباشراً.","ideas":["fewer workers support more retirees","people live longer and draw pensions for more years","heavy physical jobs are difficult after a certain age","poorer workers often have shorter, less healthy lives","flexible retirement and retraining may soften the effects"],"vocab":["pension","life expectancy","to contribute","retirement age","fiscal pressure","manual labour","inequality","flexible retirement"]},{"id":"w2-60","l":"C1","type":"discuss","topic":"international aid","prompt":"Some people believe that wealthy countries should give aid to poorer nations without any conditions. Others argue that aid should depend on reforms such as greater transparency and action against corruption.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","planAr":"المقدمة: عرّف الموضوع وبيّن أنك ستناقش الرأيين. الفقرة الأولى: الرأي الأول مع سبب ومثال. الفقرة الثانية: الرأي الثاني مع سبب ومثال. الخاتمة: اذكر رأيك الشخصي بوضوح مع سبب مختصر (يمكن ذكره أيضاً في المقدمة).","ideas":["conditions can be seen as interference and may delay urgent help","the poorest citizens should not suffer for their rulers' failures","unconditional money may be stolen or wasted","transparency conditions protect donors and help citizens follow the money","a mix of emergency help and conditional development support"],"vocab":["conditionality","sovereignty","development aid","emergency relief","to embezzle","reform","good governance","to hold to account"]}]);
Fasih.add("w1",[{"id":"w1-01","l":"B1","chart":"line","title":"Daily bus passengers in three cities, 2000-2020","unit":"thousand","x":["2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"series":[{"name":"City X","data":[40,45,52,60,70]},{"name":"City Y","data":[65,62,55,48,42]},{"name":"City Z","data":[30,38,50,58,64]}],"prompt":"The line graph below shows the number of people who used buses each day in three cities between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The line graph compares the number of passengers who used buses each day in three cities, City X, City Y and City Z, between 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, bus use rose in City X and City Z, whereas it fell in City Y. City Y began with the highest figure, but by 2020 it had the lowest, and City X ended as the busiest city.\n\nIn 2000, City Y had 65 thousand daily passengers, compared with 40 thousand in City X and only 30 thousand in City Z. Over the next ten years, the figure for City Y dropped steadily to 55 thousand, while City X climbed to 52 thousand and City Z rose sharply to 50 thousand.\n\nFrom 2010 onwards, the gap changed completely. City Y continued to decline and reached 42 thousand in 2020. By contrast, City X grew to 70 thousand, and City Z reached 64 thousand after passing City Y between 2010 and 2015. Both of the growing cities therefore finished well above City Y, which lost more than a third of its passengers.","keyAr":"المدينتان X وZ ارتفع عدد ركاب الحافلات فيهما، بينما انخفض في المدينة Y. بدأت Y بأعلى رقم (65 ألفًا) وانتهت بأدنى رقم (42 ألفًا)، وأصبحت X الأكثر ازدحامًا (70 ألفًا). تجاوزت Z المدينة Y بين 2010 و2015.","phr":["The line graph compares …","Overall, … rose, whereas … fell.","began with the highest figure","rose sharply to …","By contrast, …","after passing … between … and …"]},{"id":"w1-02","l":"B2","chart":"line","title":"Electricity from renewable sources in three countries, 2000-2020","unit":"%","x":["2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"series":[{"name":"Country A","data":[5,9,16,27,41]},{"name":"Country B","data":[22,25,26,28,29]},{"name":"Country C","data":[14,18,17,12,9]}],"prompt":"The graph below shows the percentage of electricity produced from renewable sources in three countries between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of electricity produced from renewable sources in three countries over a twenty-year period, from 2000 to 2020.\n\nOverall, the share of renewable power rose dramatically in Country A and edged up in Country B, whereas it fell in Country C after an early increase. Country A moved from the lowest figure to the highest.\n\nIn 2000, Country B led with 22%, followed by Country C on 14% and Country A on only 5%. Country A then grew steadily to 16% in 2010 and accelerated to 27% in 2015, before reaching a peak of 41% in 2020, overtaking Country B at some point between 2015 and 2020. Country B, meanwhile, increased only slightly, from 22% to 29%.\n\nCountry C followed a different pattern. Its figure climbed to a high of 18% in 2005 and stayed close to that level in 2010, at 17%, but then declined sharply to 12% in 2015 and just 9% by the end of the period, making it the lowest of the three.","keyAr":"ارتفعت حصة الطاقة المتجددة بقوة في الدولة A (من 5% إلى 41%) فصارت الأعلى، وزادت قليلًا في B (22% إلى 29%)، أما في C فارتفعت إلى 18% عام 2005 ثم هبطت إلى 9%. تجاوزت A الدولة B بين 2015 و2020.","phr":["illustrates the proportion of …","edged up","accelerated to …","reaching a peak of …","overtaking … at some point between …","declined sharply to …"]},{"id":"w1-03","l":"B2","chart":"line","title":"International students at three universities, 2014-2020","unit":"thousand","x":["2014","2015","2016","2017","2018","2019","2020"],"series":[{"name":"University P","data":[12,14,15,19,24,28,33]},{"name":"University Q","data":[25,26,28,27,25,22,20]},{"name":"University R","data":[8,10,13,17,19,20,21]}],"prompt":"The line graph below shows the number of international students enrolled at three universities between 2014 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The line graph shows how many international students were enrolled at three universities, P, Q and R, between 2014 and 2020.\n\nOverall, enrolment rose at Universities P and R, while it fell at University Q after peaking in 2016. University P recorded the biggest increase and ended with the highest number.\n\nIn 2014, University Q was the most popular, attracting 25 thousand international students, compared with 12 thousand at P and just 8 thousand at R. Q's figure edged up to a peak of 28 thousand in 2016 and then declined gradually, falling to 20 thousand by 2020.\n\nBy contrast, University P grew every year, rising slowly to 15 thousand in 2016 and then more quickly to reach 33 thousand in 2020. It overtook Q between 2018 and 2019, when its figure moved from 24 to 28 thousand. University R also increased steadily, from 8 thousand to 21 thousand, and it finally surpassed Q in 2020 by a narrow margin of one thousand.","keyAr":"ارتفع عدد الطلاب الدوليين في الجامعتين P وR وانخفض في Q بعد ذروتها (28 ألفًا) عام 2016. نمت P كل عام حتى 33 ألفًا وتجاوزت Q بين 2018 و2019، وتجاوزتها R عام 2020 بفارق ألف طالب.","phr":["was the most popular","peaked at …","declined gradually","grew every year","overtook … between … and …","by a narrow margin of …"]},{"id":"w1-04","l":"B2","chart":"line","title":"Weekly hours teenagers spent on four activities in Country D, 2000-2020","unit":"hours","x":["2000","2005","2010","2015","2020"],"series":[{"name":"Online","data":[3,7,12,18,24]},{"name":"Television","data":[20,19,16,11,7]},{"name":"Reading","data":[6,5,5,4,3]},{"name":"Sport","data":[8,8,9,9,10]}],"prompt":"The graph below shows the average number of hours per week that teenagers in Country D spent on four leisure activities between 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The graph compares the average number of hours per week that teenagers in Country D spent on four leisure activities between 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, time spent online increased dramatically, while time spent watching television fell sharply; reading and sport changed only slightly. By 2020, going online had replaced television as the most time-consuming activity.\n\nIn 2000, television dominated teenagers' free time at 20 hours a week, far more than sport (8 hours), reading (6 hours) or being online (just 3 hours). Over the following decade, television viewing fell gradually to 16 hours, whereas online use quadrupled to 12 hours.\n\nThe trends continued after 2010. Online time rose to 18 hours in 2015 and 24 hours in 2020, overtaking television between 2010 and 2015. Television, in contrast, dropped to just 7 hours by the end of the period. Reading declined slightly from 6 to 3 hours, and sport was the only other activity to grow, edging up from 8 to 10 hours.","keyAr":"ازداد الوقت على الإنترنت بشدة (من 3 إلى 24 ساعة أسبوعيًا) وانخفض وقت التلفاز بحدة (من 20 إلى 7)، بينما تغيّر القراءة والرياضة قليلًا. تجاوز الإنترنت التلفاز بين 2010 و2015.","phr":["increased dramatically","fell sharply","dominated teenagers' free time","quadrupled to …","in contrast","edging up from … to …"]},{"id":"w1-05","l":"B2","chart":"line","title":"Employees working from home in four industries, 2012-2022","unit":"%","x":["2012","2014","2016","2018","2020","2022"],"series":[{"name":"IT","data":[18,24,30,36,62,55]},{"name":"Finance","data":[10,13,17,21,48,40]},{"name":"Education","data":[6,7,8,9,30,22]},{"name":"Manufacturing","data":[3,3,4,4,9,8]}],"prompt":"The line graph below shows the percentage of employees who worked from home at least one day a week in four industries between 2012 and 2022. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The line graph presents the percentage of employees who worked from home at least one day a week in four industries between 2012 and 2022.\n\nOverall, home working became more common in every industry, with a dramatic jump in 2020 followed by a modest fall. IT had the highest percentage throughout the whole period, whereas manufacturing remained the lowest of the four.\n\nBetween 2012 and 2018, the rise was gradual. In IT, the figure grew from 18% to 36%, and in finance it roughly doubled, from 10% to 21%. Education and manufacturing stayed low, reaching only 9% and 4% respectively in 2018.\n\nAll four lines peaked in 2020. IT reached 62%, finance 48%, education 30% and manufacturing just 9%. The biggest proportional increase occurred in education, where the figure more than tripled from 9% to 30%. In 2022, each industry saw a decline, to 55% in IT, 40% in finance, 22% in education and 8% in manufacturing, but all remained far above their 2018 levels.","keyAr":"زاد العمل من المنزل في كل القطاعات مع قفزة كبيرة عام 2020 (IT 62%، المالية 48%، التعليم 30%، التصنيع 9%) ثم تراجع طفيف عام 2022. قطاع IT الأعلى دائمًا والتصنيع الأدنى دائمًا.","phr":["the percentage of employees who …","a dramatic jump in …","throughout the period","roughly doubled","more than tripled","remained far above …"]},{"id":"w1-06","l":"C1","chart":"line","title":"Annual production of four crops in Country E, 2010-2022","unit":"million tonnes","x":["2010","2012","2014","2016","2018","2020","2022"],"series":[{"name":"Wheat","data":[52,55,58,54,49,47,50]},{"name":"Rice","data":[38,41,43,47,52,55,57]},{"name":"Maize","data":[30,33,31,36,40,38,44]},{"name":"Barley","data":[20,19,21,18,17,19,16]}],"prompt":"The line graph below shows the annual production of four crops, in million tonnes, in Country E between 2010 and 2022. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The line graph shows the annual production of four crops, in million tonnes, in Country E from 2010 to 2022.\n\nOverall, rice was the only crop to grow in every period, and it replaced wheat as the leading crop. Maize also rose in the long term despite fluctuations, whereas wheat and barley ended below their 2010 levels.\n\nWheat began as the largest crop at 52 million tonnes and climbed to a peak of 58 million in 2014. It then fell steadily to 47 million in 2020, before recovering slightly to 50 million in 2022. Rice, starting from 38 million tonnes, increased in every period and passed wheat between 2016 and 2018, reaching 57 million by 2022.\n\nMaize and barley were the smaller crops. Maize output fluctuated, dipping from 33 to 31 million in 2014 and again from 40 to 38 million in 2020, but it still rose from 30 to 44 million overall. Barley remained the lowest throughout, hovering between 16 and 21 million tonnes, and it ended at its lowest point of 16 million.","keyAr":"الأرز هو المحصول الوحيد الذي ارتفع في كل الفترات (38 إلى 57 مليون طن) وحلّ محل القمح في الصدارة بين 2016 و2018. بلغ القمح ذروته 58 مليونًا عام 2014 ثم انخفض إلى 50. ارتفع الذرة رغم تذبذبه (30 إلى 44) وبقي الشعير الأدنى (16 مليونًا في النهاية).","phr":["in every period","replaced … as the leading …","climbed to a peak of …","recovering slightly to …","fluctuated","hovering between … and …"]},{"id":"w1-07","l":"B1","chart":"bar","title":"How people travel to work in two cities","unit":"%","x":["Car","Bus","Train","Bicycle","Walking"],"series":[{"name":"City A","data":[45,25,15,10,5]},{"name":"City B","data":[20,30,28,15,7]}],"prompt":"The bar chart below shows how people in two cities travel to work. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The bar chart compares how people in two cities, City A and City B, travel to work, using five different means of transport.\n\nOverall, the car was the most popular choice in City A, whereas the bus was the most common in City B. Public transport was clearly more important in City B, while walking was the least popular method in both cities.\n\nIn City A, 45% of workers drove to work, which was almost twice as many as those who took the bus (25%). Train travel accounted for 15% and cycling for 10%, and only 5% walked. In City B, by contrast, just 20% used a car, which was less than half the figure for City A.\n\nCity B's commuters preferred public transport: 30% went by bus and 28% by train, which was nearly double the train figure for City A. Cycling was also more popular in City B, at 15%, than in City A, at 10%, but walking was similar in the two cities, at 7% and 5% respectively.","keyAr":"السيارة الأكثر استخدامًا في المدينة A (45%) والحافلة في B (30%). النقل العام أهم في B (حافلة 30% وقطار 28%) والمشي الأقل في المدينتين (5% و7%). نسبة السيارة في B (20%) أقل من نصف نسبتها في A.","phr":["The bar chart compares …","the most popular choice","whereas …","almost twice as many as …","by contrast","respectively"]},{"id":"w1-08","l":"B2","chart":"bar","title":"Average monthly household spending in Country F, 2000 and 2020","unit":"US dollars","x":["Housing","Food","Transport","Healthcare","Leisure"],"series":[{"name":"2000","data":[420,310,180,90,120]},{"name":"2020","data":[780,350,260,210,240]}],"prompt":"The bar chart below shows the average monthly spending of households in Country F on five categories in 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The bar chart compares average monthly household spending on five categories in Country F in 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, spending rose in every category over the twenty years. Housing was by far the largest item in both years, while healthcare and leisure showed the fastest growth.\n\nIn 2000, households spent $420 a month on housing, followed by $310 on food, $180 on transport, $120 on leisure and just $90 on healthcare. By 2020, the housing figure had climbed to $780, which was nearly double its earlier level and more than twice as high as the next category, food, at $350.\n\nFood spending increased only slightly, by $40, whereas transport rose from $180 to $260. The most striking changes occurred in leisure and healthcare. Spending on leisure doubled to $240, and healthcare spending more than doubled, from $90 to $210, although it remained the lowest category in both years. As a result, total monthly spending across the five categories grew from $1,120 to $1,840.","keyAr":"ارتفع الإنفاق في كل الفئات بين 2000 و2020. السكن الأكبر دائمًا (من 420 إلى 780 دولارًا)، والغذاء زاد قليلًا (310 إلى 350). أسرع نمو كان في الترفيه (ضعف) والرعاية الصحية (من 90 إلى 210).","phr":["Overall, spending rose in every …","by far the largest","followed by …","nearly double its earlier level","increased only slightly","more than doubled"]},{"id":"w1-09","l":"B2","chart":"bar","title":"Graduates by subject and gender in Country G, 2022","unit":"thousand","x":["Engineering","Medicine","Law","Business","Arts","Education"],"series":[{"name":"Male","data":[42,18,15,30,9,6]},{"name":"Female","data":[14,26,19,29,17,22]}],"prompt":"The bar chart below shows the number of male and female graduates in six subject areas in Country G in 2022. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The bar chart shows the number of male and female graduates, in thousands, in six subject areas in Country G in 2022.\n\nOverall, women outnumbered men in four of the six subjects, with the clearest gender gaps in engineering and education. Engineering was the most popular subject among men, whereas business was the most popular among women.\n\nEngineering produced the largest number of male graduates, at 42 thousand, which was three times the female figure of 14 thousand. Business was the second most popular choice for men, with 30 thousand, and there the numbers were almost equal, since 29 thousand women also graduated. Law and medicine attracted fewer men, with 15 and 18 thousand respectively.\n\nWomen, by contrast, dominated education, with 22 thousand graduates against only 6 thousand men. Female graduates also outnumbered males in medicine (26 thousand compared with 18 thousand), law (19 and 15 thousand) and arts (17 and 9 thousand). In total, women accounted for 127 thousand graduates, compared with 120 thousand men.","keyAr":"تفوقت النساء عدديًا في أربعة من ستة تخصصات (الطب والقانون والفنون والتربية). الهندسة الأكثر شعبية بين الرجال (42 ألفًا، ثلاثة أضعاف النساء) والأعمال بين النساء (29 ألفًا). في الأعمال تقارب الرقمان (30 و29).","phr":["outnumbered","the clearest gender gap","three times the … figure","almost equal","dominated","compared with …"]},{"id":"w1-10","l":"B2","chart":"bar","title":"Visitors to four tourist attractions in a European country","unit":"thousand","x":["Museum","Castle","Botanical garden","Beach park"],"series":[{"name":"2010","data":[120,95,60,150]},{"name":"2015","data":[140,110,85,135]},{"name":"2020","data":[100,150,130,90]}],"prompt":"The bar chart below shows the number of visitors to four tourist attractions in a European country in 2010, 2015 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The bar chart gives information about the number of visitors, in thousands, to four tourist attractions in a European country in 2010, 2015 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the castle and the botanical garden attracted steadily more visitors over the decade, whereas the beach park lost popularity. In 2010 the beach park was the busiest site, but by 2020 the castle had taken the lead.\n\nIn 2010, the beach park received 150 thousand visitors, ahead of the museum with 120 thousand, the castle with 95 thousand and the botanical garden with 60 thousand. Five years later, the beach park fell to 135 thousand, while the museum, castle and garden all gained, reaching 140, 110 and 85 thousand respectively.\n\nThe changes were most dramatic by 2020. The castle's figure rose to 150 thousand, and the botanical garden more than doubled its 2010 total, to 130 thousand. In contrast, the beach park dropped to just 90 thousand, the lowest of the four, and the museum fell back to 100 thousand after its rise in 2015.","keyAr":"ازداد زوار القلعة والحديقة النباتية باستمرار (القلعة 95 إلى 150 ألفًا، الحديقة 60 إلى 130)، وانخفض زوار منتزه الشاطئ (150 إلى 90). كان المنتزه الأكثر ازدحامًا عام 2010 وأصبحت القلعة الأولى عام 2020. ارتفع المتحف ثم تراجع.","phr":["gives information about …","lost popularity","taken the lead","respectively","more than doubled","fell back to …"]},{"id":"w1-11","l":"B2","chart":"bar","title":"Electricity generated by five sources in two countries","unit":"TWh","x":["Coal","Gas","Nuclear","Hydropower","Wind and solar"],"series":[{"name":"Country G","data":[120,95,60,35,40]},{"name":"Country H","data":[30,70,85,110,55]}],"prompt":"The bar chart below shows the amount of electricity, in terawatt-hours (TWh), that two countries generated from five energy sources in one year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The bar chart compares the amount of electricity, in terawatt-hours (TWh), that two countries generated from five energy sources in one year.\n\nOverall, Country G relied mainly on fossil fuels, with coal and gas together providing most of its power, while Country H depended more on hydropower and nuclear energy. Coal was the biggest source in Country G but the smallest in Country H.\n\nCountry G produced 120 TWh from coal and 95 TWh from gas, a combined total of 215 TWh. By contrast, Country H generated only 30 TWh from coal, although its gas output, at 70 TWh, was not far below that of Country G.\n\nNuclear energy supplied 60 TWh in Country G and 85 TWh in Country H. The biggest difference was in hydropower: Country H produced 110 TWh, more than three times the figure for Country G, at 35 TWh. Wind and solar contributed 40 TWh in G and 55 TWh in H, making them the second smallest source in both countries.","keyAr":"اعتمدت الدولة G على الوقود الأحفوري (فحم 120 وغاز 95 تيراواط ساعة)، واعتمدت H على الطاقة المائية (110) والنووية (85). الفحم الأكبر في G والأصغر في H (30). المائية في H أكثر من ثلاثة أضعاف G.","phr":["relied mainly on …","together providing most of …","By contrast, …","a combined total of …","The biggest difference was in …","more than three times the figure for …"]},{"id":"w1-12","l":"C1","chart":"bar","title":"Adults taking part in six types of learning, by age group","unit":"%","x":["Online courses","Evening classes","Workplace training","Language study","Professional exams","Community workshops"],"series":[{"name":"Aged 18-29","data":[38,14,22,30,18,12]},{"name":"Aged 30-49","data":[26,18,41,19,24,15]},{"name":"Aged 50+","data":[11,16,15,8,6,27]}],"prompt":"The bar chart below shows the percentage of adults in three age groups who took part in six types of learning activity last year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The bar chart shows the percentage of adults in three age groups who took part in six types of learning activity last year.\n\nOverall, no single activity was the favourite of every age group. Younger adults were the most active online, those aged 30 to 49 mainly learned at work, and the oldest group led only in community workshops.\n\nOnline courses were taken by 38% of the 18-29 group, compared with 26% of those aged 30 to 49 and only 11% of those aged 50 and over. Language study showed a similar pattern, falling from 30% among the youngest group to 19% and then 8%.\n\nWorkplace training was the most common activity for the middle group, at 41%, well above the figures for the younger (22%) and older (15%) groups. This group also recorded the highest percentages for professional exams (24%) and evening classes (18%), although the differences in evening classes were small, ranging only between 14% and 18%. By contrast, 27% of people aged 50 and over attended community workshops, more than double the 12% of the youngest group.","keyAr":"لا يوجد نشاط مفضل لدى كل الفئات: الشباب الأكثر في الدورات الإلكترونية (38%) واللغات (30%)، وفئة 30-49 في التدريب المهني (41%) والامتحانات (24%)، وفئة 50+ تتصدر في الورش المجتمعية فقط (27%). الفروق في الدروس المسائية صغيرة.","phr":["no single activity was the favourite of …","led only in …","showed a similar pattern","well above the figures for …","ranging only between … and …","more than double the … of …"]},{"id":"w1-13","l":"B1","chart":"pie","title":"How a typical household in Country A spends its monthly income","unit":"%","x":["Housing","Food","Transport","Education","Leisure","Other"],"series":[{"name":"Household spending","data":[35,25,15,10,10,5]}],"prompt":"The pie chart below shows how a typical household in Country A divides its monthly income between six types of spending. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The pie chart shows how a typical household in Country A divides its monthly income between six types of spending.\n\nOverall, housing and food together took up the majority of the budget, whereas leisure and other items accounted for only a small share of the total, with transport falling in between.\n\nHousing was the largest category, at 35% of total income, followed by food at 25%. Together, these two basic needs accounted for 60% of all spending. Transport came next with 15%, which was 10 percentage points lower than the figure for food.\n\nEducation and leisure each took exactly 10% of the household budget, so families spent the same amount on learning as on free-time activities. The remaining 5% was spent on other items, making this the smallest category of all. In other words, housing was seven times as large as the final category, and it was more than three times the size of the share spent on either education or leisure.","keyAr":"السكن والغذاء معًا يستهلكان 60% من الدخل (35% و25%). يليهما النقل (15%)، ثم التعليم والترفيه بـ 10% لكل منهما، والبنود الأخرى الأصغر (5%). السكن سبعة أضعاف الفئة الأخيرة.","phr":["divides its income between …","accounted for … of the total","followed by …","Together, these … accounted for …","each took exactly …","the smallest category of all"]},{"id":"w1-14","l":"B2","chart":"pie","title":"Sources of electricity in Country B, 1990 and 2020","unit":"%","x":["Coal","Oil","Gas","Hydropower","Wind and solar"],"series":[{"name":"1990","data":[52,20,15,11,2]},{"name":"2020","data":[18,4,38,12,28]}],"prompt":"The two pie charts below show the sources of electricity in Country B in 1990 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The two pie charts compare the sources of electricity in Country B in 1990 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the country moved away from coal and oil towards gas and renewable energy. Coal was the dominant source in 1990, but by 2020 gas had taken over as the main one. Renewable sources, including hydropower, grew in importance, from 13% to 40% of the total.\n\nIn 1990, coal supplied just over half of the country's electricity, at 52%, followed by oil at 20% and gas at 15%. Hydropower provided 11%, while wind and solar power contributed a mere 2%, making them the smallest source.\n\nBy 2020 the picture had changed considerably. Coal's share had fallen to 18%, and oil had almost disappeared, at 4%. Gas, in contrast, rose to 38% and became the largest source, and wind and solar energy expanded dramatically from 2% to 28%. Hydropower remained relatively stable, rising by only one percentage point to 12%. Taken together, coal and oil provided 72% of electricity in 1990 but only 22% thirty years later.","keyAr":"تحولت الدولة B من الفحم والنفط إلى الغاز والطاقة المتجددة. كان الفحم المصدر الأول عام 1990 (52%) وأصبح الغاز الأول عام 2020 (38%). قفزت الرياح والشمس من 2% إلى 28% وبقيت المائية شبه ثابتة (11% إلى 12%).","phr":["moved away from … towards …","the dominant source","a mere 2%","had almost disappeared","expanded dramatically","remained relatively stable"]},{"id":"w1-15","l":"B2","chart":"pie","title":"Reasons for visiting City X, 2005 and 2020","unit":"%","x":["Leisure","Business","Visiting relatives","Education","Medical care"],"series":[{"name":"2005","data":[40,30,18,8,4]},{"name":"2020","data":[48,14,22,10,6]}],"prompt":"The pie charts below show the main reasons why people visited City X in 2005 and in 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The two pie charts show the main reasons why people visited City X in 2005 and in 2020.\n\nOverall, leisure was the leading reason in both years, and its share increased, while business travel declined sharply. Every other category grew slightly over the period. Taken together, leisure and business made up 70% of visits in 2005, but only 62% in 2020.\n\nIn 2005, 40% of visitors came for leisure, and business was a clear second at 30%. Visits to relatives made up 18% of the total, whereas education and medical care were much smaller, at 8% and 4% respectively.\n\nBy 2020, leisure had risen to 48%, which was almost half of all visits. The share of business visitors, however, more than halved, falling from 30% to 14%, and this placed it behind visits to relatives, which had grown to 22%. Education and medical care both increased a little, to 10% and 6% respectively, but they remained the least common reasons for coming to the city, as they had been fifteen years earlier.","keyAr":"بقيت السياحة الترفيهية السبب الأول في العامين (40% ثم 48%)، وانخفضت زيارات الأعمال بشدة (من 30% إلى 14%) فصارت خلف زيارة الأقارب (22%). ازداد التعليم والعلاج قليلًا (8% إلى 10% و4% إلى 6%) وبقيا الأقل.","phr":["the leading reason","a clear second","made up … of the total","more than halved","placed it behind …","the least common reasons"]},{"id":"w1-16","l":"C1","chart":"pie","title":"Sources of income of a university in Country H, 2000 and 2020","unit":"%","x":["Government grants","Student fees","Research contracts","Donations","Commercial activities","Investments"],"series":[{"name":"2000","data":[52,24,10,6,5,3]},{"name":"2020","data":[28,38,16,8,7,3]}],"prompt":"The pie charts below show where a university in Country H obtained its income in 2000 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The two pie charts illustrate where a university in Country H obtained its income in 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the university shifted from relying on state support to depending on its students. Government grants were the largest source in 2000, but by 2020 student fees had taken over. Most other sources grew modestly, and investments were the only one that did not change.\n\nIn 2000, more than half of the income, 52%, came from government grants, with student fees supplying 24%. Research contracts contributed 10%, donations 6% and commercial activities 5%, while investments provided only 3%.\n\nTwenty years later, government funding had dropped to 28%, whereas fees rose to 38% and became the principal source. Research contracts also grew, from 10% to 16%, and donations and commercial activities increased slightly, to 8% and 7% respectively. Investments stayed at 3%, which was the smallest share in both years. Together, government grants and student fees supplied 66% of the income in 2020, compared with 76% in 2000.","keyAr":"تحولت الجامعة من الاعتماد على المنح الحكومية (52% عام 2000 ثم 28%) إلى رسوم الطلاب (24% ثم 38%) التي أصبحت المصدر الأول. نمت عقود البحث (10% إلى 16%) وزادت التبرعات والأنشطة التجارية قليلًا، وبقيت الاستثمارات 3%.","phr":["obtained its income","shifted from … to …","took over","the principal source","increased slightly, to …","the smallest share in both years"]},{"id":"w1-17","l":"B1","chart":"table","title":"Average rainfall in three towns by season","unit":"mm","x":["Spring","Summer","Autumn","Winter"],"series":[{"name":"Town A","data":[60,20,75,110]},{"name":"Town B","data":[85,45,90,70]},{"name":"Town C","data":[30,5,25,40]}],"prompt":"The table below shows the average rainfall in three towns during the four seasons of the year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The table compares the average rainfall, in millimetres, in three towns during the four seasons of the year.\n\nOverall, Town B was the wettest town in total, while Town C was by far the driest. Summer was the driest season in all three towns.\n\nIn summer, Town A received 20 mm of rain, Town B 45 mm and Town C only 5 mm, which was the lowest figure in the table. Spring was wetter, with 60 mm in Town A, 85 mm in Town B and 30 mm in Town C.\n\nAutumn brought 75 mm to Town A and 90 mm to Town B, the highest autumn figure, while Town C received just 25 mm. In winter, Town A had the heaviest rainfall of all, at 110 mm, compared with 70 mm in Town B and 40 mm in Town C. Over the whole year, Town B recorded 290 mm in total, slightly more than Town A, with 265 mm, whereas Town C received only 100 mm.","keyAr":"كانت البلدة B الأمطر مجموعًا (290 ملم) والبلدة C الأجف بفارق كبير (100 ملم). الصيف أجف فصل في البلدات الثلاث (5 ملم في C). سجلت A أعلى رقم في الجدول شتاءً (110 ملم).","phr":["the wettest / the driest","by far the driest","the lowest figure in the table","compared with …","the heaviest rainfall of all","in total"]},{"id":"w1-18","l":"B2","chart":"table","title":"International visitors to five countries (millions)","unit":"million","x":["Country A","Country B","Country C","Country D","Country E"],"series":[{"name":"2000","data":[12,30,8,20,5]},{"name":"2010","data":[18,34,15,19,9]},{"name":"2020","data":[25,22,31,21,14]}],"prompt":"The table below gives information about the number of international visitors, in millions, to five countries in 2000, 2010 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The table gives information about the number of international visitors, in millions, to five countries in 2000, 2010 and 2020.\n\nOverall, visitor numbers rose in four of the five countries, with Country C showing the most remarkable growth. Country B, in contrast, was the only one to experience a decline, although it was the most visited country in the first two years.\n\nIn 2000, Country B attracted 30 million visitors, far more than Country D with 20 million, Country A with 12 million, Country C with 8 million and Country E with 5 million. By 2010, Country B had increased slightly to 34 million, while Countries A and C rose to 18 and 15 million respectively.\n\nBy 2020 the ranking had changed. Country C's total had almost quadrupled, to 31 million, making it the top destination, and Country A grew to 25 million. Country B fell to 22 million. Country D stayed almost flat at around 20 million throughout, and Country E, although it nearly tripled to 14 million, remained the least visited.","keyAr":"زاد عدد الزوار في أربع دول من خمس، وأكبر نمو في الدولة C (من 8 إلى 31 مليونًا) فصارت الأولى عام 2020. الدولة B الوحيدة التي تراجعت (من 34 إلى 22) بعد أن كانت الأولى. بقيت D شبه ثابتة وE الأقل (14).","phr":["gives information about …","the most remarkable growth","the only one to …","had almost quadrupled","stayed almost flat","remained the least visited"]},{"id":"w1-19","l":"B2","chart":"table","title":"Households owning four devices in five regions","unit":"%","x":["North","South","East","West","Central"],"series":[{"name":"Laptop","data":[78,55,66,82,38]},{"name":"Smartphone","data":[95,88,92,97,79]},{"name":"Tablet","data":[52,35,40,61,22]},{"name":"Smart TV","data":[60,41,72,58,30]}],"prompt":"The table below shows the percentage of households that owned four types of electronic device in five regions of a country. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The table shows the percentage of households owning four types of electronic device in five regions of a country.\n\nOverall, smartphones were by far the most widespread device in every region, while tablets were generally the least common. The West had the highest ownership levels for three of the four devices, whereas the Central region had the lowest for all four.\n\nSmartphone ownership ranged from 79% in the Central region to 97% in the West, and it exceeded 85% in every other region. Laptops were also popular in the West, at 82%, and in the North, at 78%, but only 38% of Central households had one.\n\nSmart TVs showed a different pattern: the East led with 72%, ahead of the North with 60% and the West with 58%, while the Central figure was only 30%. Tablets were owned by 61% of households in the West and 52% in the North, but by just 40% in the East, 35% in the South and 22% in the Central region.","keyAr":"الهواتف الذكية الأكثر انتشارًا في كل المناطق (79% إلى 97%) والأجهزة اللوحية الأقل عادةً. الغرب الأعلى في ثلاثة أجهزة والوسط الأدنى في الأربعة جميعها. تتصدر المنطقة الشرقية في التلفزيونات الذكية (72%).","phr":["by far the most widespread","generally the least common","ranged from … to …","exceeded …","showed a different pattern","ahead of …"]},{"id":"w1-20","l":"C1","chart":"table","title":"Graduates by subject area in Country J (thousands)","unit":"thousand","x":["Computer science","Engineering","Medicine","Law","Education","Business"],"series":[{"name":"2005","data":[4,22,8,11,20,15]},{"name":"2010","data":[9,25,10,13,18,19]},{"name":"2015","data":[18,27,12,12,15,24]},{"name":"2020","data":[31,26,15,10,13,28]}],"prompt":"The table below shows how many students graduated, in thousands, in six subject areas in Country J in 2005, 2010, 2015 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","model":"The table shows how many students graduated, in thousands, in six subject areas in Country J in 2005, 2010, 2015 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the number of graduates grew in computer science, business and medicine, whereas it declined in education and, after an early rise, in law. Engineering remained fairly stable and stayed among the largest fields, but computer science changed the most and became the biggest field by 2020.\n\nIn 2005, engineering led with 22 thousand graduates, followed by education with 20 thousand and business with 15 thousand, while computer science produced only 4 thousand. Over the next fifteen years, computer science expanded rapidly to 9, 18 and finally 31 thousand, overtaking engineering between 2015 and 2020. Business also grew steadily, from 15 to 28 thousand.\n\nEngineering fluctuated within a narrow range, peaking at 27 thousand in 2015 and ending at 26 thousand. Medicine rose gradually from 8 to 15 thousand. Law peaked at 13 thousand in 2010 and then fell to 10 thousand, while education showed a continuous decline, from 20 thousand in 2005 to 13 thousand in 2020.","keyAr":"نما عدد الخريجين في علوم الحاسوب (4 إلى 31 ألفًا) والأعمال (15 إلى 28) والطب (8 إلى 15)، وانخفض في التربية (20 إلى 13) والقانون بعد ذروته 13 عام 2010. بقيت الهندسة مستقرة نسبيًا (22 إلى 27) وتجاوزتها الحاسوب بين 2015 و2020.","phr":["grew in …, whereas it declined in …","changed the most","expanded rapidly","overtaking … between … and …","fluctuated within a narrow range","a continuous decline"]}]);
